const SEED_NOTES = [{"id": "note-solas-1974", "v": 4, "title": "SOLAS 1974", "category": "Maritime › IMO › Conventions", "tags": ["solas", "imo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>SOLAS 1974 — International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea</b></p><p><b>What is SOLAS</b></p><ul><li>Main IMO convention for merchant ship safety</li><li>Minimum standards: construction, equipment, operation</li><li>Flag State certifies · Port State Control (PSC) inspects</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1912 Titanic → SOLAS 1914 (never in force, WWI)</li><li>1929 · 1948 · 1960 (first under IMO, then IMCO)</li><li>1974: adopted 1 Nov 1974 · in force 25 May 1980 · tacit acceptance</li><li>1978 Protocol: tanker safety · in force 1 May 1981</li><li>1988 Protocol: HSSC (harmonized surveys) · in force 3 Feb 2000</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>International voyages</li><li>Passenger ships: &gt;12 passengers</li><li>Cargo ships: ≥500 GT (Ch IV and parts of Ch V: ≥300 GT)</li><li>Not covered (I/3): warships, troopships · cargo &lt;500 GT · non-mechanically propelled · wooden primitive · pleasure yachts not in trade · fishing vessels</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch I — General Provisions</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all SOLAS ships</li></ul><p><i>Part A — Application, definitions</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application: international voyages, flag State applies the rules</li><li>Reg 2 Definitions: passenger ship = &gt;12 passengers; international voyage; new/existing ship</li><li>Reg 3 Exceptions: warships, cargo &lt;500 GT, non-mechanically propelled, primitive wooden, yachts not in trade, fishing vessels</li><li>Reg 4 Exemptions: single voyage or novel ships — Exemption Certificate issued</li><li>Reg 5 Equivalents: alternative fitting allowed if at least as effective — reported to IMO</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Surveys and certificates</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Inspection and survey: by flag State officers or RO; Administration guarantees completeness</li><li>Reg 7 Passenger ships: initial · renewal every 12 months · additional after repairs</li><li>Reg 8 Cargo ship LSA &amp; equipment: initial · annual · periodical (within 3 months of 2nd or 3rd anniversary) · renewal</li><li>Reg 9 Cargo ship radio: initial · periodical · renewal</li><li>Reg 10 Cargo ship structure &amp; machinery: initial · intermediate · annual · renewal · 2 bottom inspections in 5 years (max 36 months apart)</li><li>Reg 11 Maintenance after survey: no changes without approval · report accidents/defects to Administration</li><li>Reg 12 Issue of certificates: Passenger Ship Safety · Cargo Ship Safety Construction / Equipment / Radio (or one Cargo Ship Safety Certificate)</li><li>Reg 13 Issue by another Government: on request of flag State</li><li>Reg 14 Duration and validity: passenger 12 months · cargo 5 years · extension max 3 months (to reach survey port) or 1 month (short voyages) · renewal within 3 months before expiry keeps expiry date</li><li>Reg 15 Forms of certificates &amp; Records of Equipment (Forms P, E, C, R)</li><li>Reg 16 Availability: certificates kept on board, readily available</li><li>Reg 17 Acceptance: certificates of other Contracting Governments accepted</li><li>Reg 18 Qualification: certificate may note conditions or limits</li><li>Reg 19 Control (PSC): check valid certificates · clear grounds → detailed inspection · may detain</li><li>Reg 20 Privileges: only ships with valid certificates</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Casualties</i></p><ul><li>Reg 21 Casualties: flag State investigates · findings to IMO</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch II-1 — Construction: Structure, Subdivision, Stability, Machinery, Electrical</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships and cargo ≥500 GT (mostly new ships)</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Definitions · Reg 3 Definitions for machinery/electrical parts</li></ul><p><i>Part A-1 — Structure</i></p><ul><li>3-1 Structure: class society rules or equivalent</li><li>3-2 Protective coatings (PSPC): ballast tanks ≥500 GT, double-side skin bulk carriers ≥150 m — MSC.215(82)</li><li>3-3 Safe access to tanker bows</li><li>3-4 Emergency towing: tankers ≥20,000 dwt both ends · towing procedure for all ships — MSC.256(84)</li><li>3-5 Asbestos: new installation banned (1 Jan 2011)</li><li>3-6 Means of access to cargo area spaces: oil tankers ≥500 GT, bulk carriers ≥20,000 GT — MSC.133(76) · Ship Structure Access Manual</li><li>3-7 Construction drawings kept on board and ashore</li><li>3-8 Towing and mooring equipment: design, inspection, maintenance (updated 1 Jan 2024)</li><li>3-9 Means of embarkation/disembarkation (gangways, accommodation ladders)</li><li>3-10 Goal-Based Standards: bulk carriers &amp; oil tankers ≥150 m · Ship Construction File — MSC.287(87)</li><li>3-11 Corrosion protection of cargo oil tanks: crude oil tankers ≥5,000 dwt</li><li>3-12 Noise: ≥1,600 GT, contracted ≥1 Jul 2014 — Noise Code MSC.337(91)</li><li>3-13 Lifting appliances &amp; anchor handling winches: design, test, inspect, maintain — MSC.532(107) · 1 Jan 2026</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Subdivision and stability</i></p><ul><li>Reg 4 General: probabilistic damage stability (SOLAS 2009)</li><li>Reg 5 Intact stability: inclining test on completion · lightship check every 5 years (passenger)</li><li>5-1 Stability information for the master: approved stability booklet, limiting KG/GM curves</li><li>Reg 6 Required subdivision index R · Reg 7 Attained index A (A ≥ R) · 7-1 factor p · 7-2 factor s · 7-3 permeability</li><li>Reg 8 Passenger ship special stability · 8-1 Flooding casualty: safe return to port / onboard stability computer or shore support</li><li>Reg 9 Double bottoms: passenger ships and cargo ships (not tankers)</li><li>Reg 10–12 Watertight bulkheads: construction, testing · collision bulkhead · peak &amp; machinery bulkheads</li><li>Reg 13 / 13-1 Openings in watertight bulkheads: passenger / cargo ships</li><li>Reg 15 / 15-1 Openings in shell plating · external openings (cargo)</li><li>Reg 16 / 16-1 Watertight doors, sidescuttles, decks, trunks: construction and initial tests</li><li>Reg 17 / 17-1 Internal watertight integrity above bulkhead deck · ro-ro passenger hull integrity</li><li>Reg 18 Subdivision load lines (passenger ships)</li><li>Reg 19 Damage control information: plans and booklet on the bridge</li><li>Reg 20 Loading of passenger ships: stability check before departure</li><li>Reg 21 Watertight doors (passenger): operated daily · drills weekly · inspected at least weekly</li><li>Reg 22 Prevention and control of water ingress: watertight doors closed at sea except when necessary · logbook entries</li><li>22-1 Flooding detection: passenger ships carrying ≥36 persons</li><li>Reg 23 Ro-ro passenger ships: bow doors, inner doors, indicators, TV monitoring</li><li>Reg 24 Water ingress control on cargo ships</li><li>Reg 25 Water level detectors: single-hold cargo ships (not bulk carriers)</li><li>25-1 Water level detectors: multiple-hold cargo ships (not bulk carriers/tankers) — MSC.482(103) · 1 Jan 2024</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Machinery</i></p><ul><li>Reg 26 General · Reg 27 Machinery: overspeed and safety devices</li><li>Reg 28 Means of going astern: enough astern power to stop</li><li>Reg 29 Steering gear: main and auxiliary · rudder hard over 35° to 30° other side in ≤28 s at max draught &amp; speed · auxiliary 15° to 15° in ≤60 s at half speed</li><li>Reg 30 Electric / electrohydraulic steering gear: alarms, indicators</li><li>Reg 31 Machinery controls · Reg 32 Steam boilers · Reg 33 Steam pipes · Reg 34 Air pressure systems</li><li>Reg 35 Machinery space ventilation · 35-1 Bilge pumping arrangements</li><li>Reg 37 Bridge–engine room communication (two independent means)</li><li>Reg 38 Engineer&#39;s alarm (audible in engineers&#39; accommodation)</li><li>Reg 39 Emergency installations (passenger ships): above bulkhead deck</li></ul><p><i>Part D — Electrical</i></p><ul><li>Reg 40 General · Reg 41 Main source of power and lighting</li><li>Reg 42 Emergency source — passenger ships: 36 h</li><li>42-1 Supplementary emergency lighting (ro-ro passenger ships)</li><li>Reg 43 Emergency source — cargo ships: 18 h · emergency lighting, VHF, fire pumps, steering</li><li>Reg 44 Starting of emergency generator: stored energy for ≥3 consecutive starts + second source for 3 more starts within 30 min (unless manual start)</li><li>Reg 45 Protection against shock, fire, electrical hazards</li></ul><p><i>Part E — Periodically unattended machinery spaces (UMS)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 46–54: fire detection, bilge/flooding alarms, bridge control of propulsion, alarms, safety systems, extra rules for passenger ships</li></ul><p><i>Part F — Alternative design</i></p><ul><li>Reg 55: alternative design and arrangements (engineering analysis approved by Administration)</li></ul><p><i>Part G — Low-flashpoint fuels</i></p><ul><li>Reg 56–57: ships using LNG and other low-flashpoint fuels → IGF Code MSC.391(95)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch II-2 — Fire Protection, Detection, Extinction</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all SOLAS ships (stricter for passenger ships and tankers)</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Fire safety objectives &amp; functional requirements · Reg 3 Definitions (A/B/C-class divisions)</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Prevention of fire and explosion</i></p><ul><li>Reg 4 Probability of ignition: oil fuel flashpoint ≥60°C · signed supplier declaration before bunkering (MSC.520(106), 1 Jan 2026) · tanker inert gas (≥8,000 dwt) · cargo tank venting</li><li>Reg 5 Fire growth potential: ventilation &amp; fuel shut-offs · fire-restricting materials</li><li>Reg 6 Smoke and toxicity: low-smoke paints, deck coverings</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Suppression of fire</i></p><ul><li>Reg 7 Detection and alarm: fixed detection in machinery &amp; accommodation · manual call points</li><li>Reg 8 Control of smoke spread</li><li>Reg 9 Containment of fire: A/B-class divisions · fire doors · main vertical zones (passenger)</li><li>Reg 10 Fire fighting: fire pumps · fire main · hydrants &amp; hoses · portable extinguishers · fixed systems · fire-fighter&#39;s outfits (cargo ships: at least 2) · EEBDs · two-way radios</li><li>Reg 11 Structural integrity</li></ul><p><i>Part D — Escape</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Notification of crew and passengers: general emergency alarm, public address</li><li>Reg 13 Means of escape: two escape routes · EEBDs · low-location lighting (passenger)</li></ul><p><i>Part E — Operational requirements</i></p><ul><li>Reg 14 Operational readiness &amp; maintenance: maintenance plan for fire equipment</li><li>Reg 15 Instructions, training and drills: fire drills · training manual · fire control plan on board</li><li>Reg 16 Operations: fire safety operational booklet</li></ul><p><i>Part F — Alternative design</i></p><ul><li>Reg 17</li></ul><p><i>Part G — Special requirements</i></p><ul><li>Reg 18 Helicopter facilities</li><li>Reg 19 Carriage of dangerous goods: Document of Compliance for DG</li><li>Reg 20 Vehicle, special category &amp; ro-ro spaces: fixed fire detection, fire patrol, video monitoring (MSC.555(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li><li>20-1 Vehicle carriers carrying hydrogen/CNG vehicles</li><li>Reg 21 Casualty threshold, safe return to port (passenger) · Reg 22 Systems to stay operational after fire · Reg 23 Safety centre (passenger)</li></ul><p><i>Codes and other changes</i></p><ul><li>FSS Code MSC.98(73) · FTP Code 2010 MSC.307(88)</li><li>PFOS foam banned: new ships from 1 Jan 2026, existing ships by first survey after</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch III — Life-Saving Appliances and Arrangements</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships and cargo ≥500 GT</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Exemptions · Reg 3 Definitions</li><li>Reg 4 Evaluation, testing and approval of LSA · Reg 5 Production tests</li></ul><p><i>Part B, Section I — All ships</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Communications: portable VHF · SARTs/AIS-SART · rocket parachute flares (12) · on-board communications · general alarm</li><li>Reg 7 Personal LSA: lifebuoys (with lights, smoke signals, line) · lifejacket for every person (+ children) · immersion suits</li><li>Reg 8 Muster list &amp; emergency instructions: posted in conspicuous places</li><li>Reg 9 Operating instructions: posters at survival craft</li><li>Reg 10 Manning of survival craft: officer or certificated person in charge</li><li>Reg 11 Muster and embarkation arrangements: lighting, embarkation ladders</li><li>Reg 12 Launching stations · Reg 13 Stowage of survival craft · Reg 14 Stowage of rescue boats · Reg 15 Stowage of MES</li><li>Reg 16 Survival craft launching &amp; recovery · Reg 17 Rescue boat launching &amp; recovery</li><li>17-1 Recovery of persons from the water: plans and procedures</li><li>Reg 18 Line-throwing appliances</li><li>Reg 19 Emergency training and drills:<ul><li>Abandon ship + fire drill: every crew member monthly · within 24 h of leaving port if &gt;25% crew new</li><li>Lifeboat launch with crew: every 3 months · free-fall: every 6 months (12 with simulated launch every 6)</li><li>Rescue boat launch: monthly if possible, at least every 3 months</li><li>Enclosed space entry &amp; rescue: every 2 months — MSC.350(92)</li><li>On-board training: within 2 weeks of joining</li></ul></li><li>Reg 20 Operational readiness, maintenance &amp; inspections:<ul><li>Weekly: visual check of survival craft, rescue boats &amp; launching appliances · engines run · general alarm tested</li><li>Monthly: checklist inspection · recorded in log</li><li>Liferafts, inflatable lifejackets, MES: serviced every 12 months (max 17)</li><li>Hydrostatic release units: serviced every 12 months (disposable: replaced at expiry)</li><li>Falls: turned end for end every ≤30 months · renewed every ≤5 years</li><li>Release gear: annual thorough exam · 5-yearly overhaul — MSC.402(96) · 1 Jan 2020</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Section II — Passenger ships (additional)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 21 Survival craft: lifeboats ≥50% each side + liferafts · rescue boat</li><li>Reg 22 Personal LSA: lifejackets +5% · children&#39;s and infant lifejackets</li><li>Reg 23 Embarkation · Reg 24 Stowage · Reg 25 Muster stations</li><li>Reg 26 Ro-ro passenger ships: MES / fast rescue boats · Reg 27 Information on passengers (count before departure)</li><li>Reg 28 Helicopter landing &amp; pick-up areas · Reg 29 Decision support system for master</li><li>Reg 30 Drills: abandon ship &amp; fire drill weekly · passenger muster before or on departure</li></ul><p><i>Section III — Cargo ships (additional)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 31 Survival craft: lifeboats each side 100% (or free-fall 100%) + liferafts 100% (each side if not easily moved)</li><li>Reg 32 Personal LSA: immersion suit for every person</li><li>Reg 33 Embarkation &amp; launching: free-fall launch test (MSC.485(103), 1 Jan 2024)</li></ul><p><i>Sections IV–V</i></p><ul><li>Reg 34 All LSA must meet the LSA Code — MSC.48(66)</li><li>Reg 35 Training manual &amp; on-board training aids</li><li>Reg 36 Instructions for on-board maintenance</li><li>Reg 37 Muster list &amp; emergency instructions: duties, signals, substitutes</li></ul><p><i>LSA Code change ahead</i></p><ul><li>New ventilation rules for lifeboats installed ≥1 Jan 2029 — MSC.535(107)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch IV — Radiocommunications (GMDSS)</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships and cargo ≥300 GT</li><li>Modernized GMDSS — MSC.496(105) · 1 Jan 2024</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Terms (sea areas A1–A4) · Reg 3 Exemptions</li><li>Reg 4 Functional requirements:<ul><li>Distress alerting ship-to-shore by 2 independent means</li><li>Receiving shore-to-ship alerts</li><li>Ship-to-ship alerting</li><li>SAR coordination and on-scene communications</li><li>Locating signals</li><li>Maritime safety information (MSI)</li><li>General radiocommunications</li><li>Bridge-to-bridge communications</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Part B — Undertakings by Contracting Governments</i></p><ul><li>Reg 5 Radio services ashore · 5-1 GMDSS identities (MMSI)</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Ship requirements</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Radio installations: protected, at conning position, with operating instructions</li><li>Reg 7 Equipment, all ships: VHF DSC (ch 70) &amp; voice (ch 16, 13) · SART/AIS-SART · NAVTEX · EGC (if needed) · EPIRB (406 MHz)</li><li>Reg 8 Sea area A1 · Reg 9 Sea areas A1+A2 (MF DSC) · Reg 10 Sea areas A1–A3 (recognized mobile satellite service or HF) · Reg 11 Sea areas A1–A4 (HF)</li><li>Reg 12 Watches: continuous DSC watch · VHF ch 16 (where practicable)</li><li>Reg 13 Sources of energy: reserve source (batteries) for all GMDSS equipment</li><li>Reg 14 Performance standards (type approved)</li><li>Reg 15 Maintenance: A1/A2 one method · A3/A4 two methods (duplication, shore-based, at-sea)</li><li>Reg 16 Radio personnel: GMDSS certificate holders (GOC / ROC)</li><li>Reg 17 Radio records: GMDSS logbook</li><li>Reg 18 Position updating: automatic position to DSC equipment</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch V — Safety of Navigation</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships, all voyages (some regs by size; warships excepted)</li></ul><p><i>Government services (Regs 1–13)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Definitions · Reg 3 Exemptions and equivalents</li><li>Reg 4 Navigational warnings · Reg 5 Meteorological services · Reg 6 Ice patrol</li><li>Reg 7 Search and rescue services · Reg 8 Life-saving signals · Reg 9 Hydrographic services</li><li>Reg 10 Ships&#39; routeing (TSS) · Reg 11 Ship reporting systems · Reg 12 VTS · Reg 13 Aids to navigation</li></ul><p><i>Ship requirements (Regs 14–35)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 14 Manning: Minimum Safe Manning Document · working language on board</li><li>Reg 15 Bridge design &amp; procedures: BRM principles, one-person operation support</li><li>Reg 16 Maintenance of equipment · Reg 17 Electromagnetic compatibility</li><li>Reg 18 Approval &amp; performance standards · annual VDR performance test</li><li>Reg 19 Carriage requirements:<ul><li>All ships: magnetic compass · charts / ECDIS · GNSS · BNWAS (≥150 GT, all passenger ships)</li><li>≥300 GT: echo sounder · 9 GHz radar · SDME · AIS (international)</li><li>≥500 GT: gyro compass · rudder angle &amp; RPM indicators · ATA</li><li>≥3,000 GT: second radar (3 GHz) · second ATA</li><li>≥10,000 GT: ARPA · heading/track control</li><li>≥50,000 GT: rate-of-turn indicator · SDME over ground</li><li>ECDIS: passenger ≥500 GT · tankers ≥3,000 GT · other cargo ≥10,000 GT (new ≥3,000 GT) · phased 2012–2018</li><li>Electronic inclinometer: containerships &amp; bulk carriers ≥3,000 GT built ≥1 Jan 2026</li><li>ECDIS S-100 standard — MSC.530(106)/Rev.1 · new installations mandatory 1 Jan 2029</li></ul></li><li>19-1 LRIT: passenger, cargo ≥300 GT, MODUs · 4 position reports a day — MSC.202(81)</li><li>Reg 20 VDR: passenger ships · cargo ≥3,000 GT (built ≥1 Jul 2002)</li><li>Reg 21 International Code of Signals &amp; IAMSAR Manual Vol III on board</li><li>Reg 22 Bridge visibility: sea view ≤2 ship lengths or 500 m (whichever less) obscured ahead · blind sectors ≤10° each, ≤20° total</li><li>Reg 23 Pilot transfer arrangements: pilot ladder, combination arrangements, officer supervising — MSC.572(110) + MSC.576(110) · new installations ≥1 Jan 2028 · existing at first survey ≥1 Jan 2029 · non-SOLAS/domestic ships by 1 Jan 2030</li><li>Reg 24 Heading/track control: manual steering available at once · test manual steering after long autopilot use and before special-caution areas</li><li>Reg 25 Steering gear: more than one power unit running in special-caution areas</li><li>Reg 26 Steering gear testing &amp; drills: tested within 12 h before departure · emergency steering drill at least every 3 months · logged</li><li>Reg 27 Nautical charts &amp; publications: adequate and up to date</li><li>Reg 28 Records of navigational activities · daily reporting to company</li><li>Reg 29 Life-saving signals table on board</li><li>Reg 30 Operational limitations (passenger ships)</li><li>Reg 31 Danger messages: dangerous ice, derelicts, tropical storms, wind force 10+ without warning, ice accretion · lost containers (2026)</li><li>Reg 32 Information required in danger messages · lost containers: position and number — MSC.550(108) · 1 Jan 2026</li><li>Reg 33 Distress: master must proceed with all speed to assist · log reason if unable</li><li>Reg 34 Safe navigation: voyage plan before sailing (appraisal, planning, execution, monitoring) — A.893(21)</li><li>34-1 Master&#39;s discretion: company cannot constrain safety decisions</li><li>Reg 35 Misuse of distress signals prohibited</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VI — Carriage of Cargoes and Oil Fuels</b></p><ul><li>Applies: cargo ships carrying solid cargoes (not liquids/gases in bulk)</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · 1-1 Definitions · 1-2 Solid bulk cargoes other than grain → IMSBC Code MSC.268(85)</li><li>Reg 2 Cargo information: shipper gives cargo details before loading (bulk: density, moisture, hazards)</li><li>Reg 3 Oxygen analysis &amp; gas detection instruments (+ training)</li><li>Reg 4 Use of pesticides (fumigation)</li><li>Reg 5 Stowage &amp; securing: Cargo Securing Manual (CSS Code A.714(17))</li><li>5-1 Safety data sheets: MARPOL Annex I cargoes and fuel oil</li><li>5-2 No blending of bulk liquid cargoes / production processes at sea</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Solid bulk cargoes</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Acceptability for shipment: moisture below TML (Group A)</li><li>Reg 7 Loading, unloading, stowage: loading plan agreed with terminal (BLU Code A.862(20)) · trimming</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Grain</i></p><ul><li>Reg 8 Definitions · Reg 9 Grain Code MSC.23(59): Document of Authorization · heel from grain shift ≤12° · GM ≥0.30 m (amended MSC.552(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VII — Carriage of Dangerous Goods</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships carrying DG</li></ul><p><i>Part A — Packaged form</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application → IMDG Code MSC.122(75) · Amdt 42-24 (MSC.556(108)) mandatory 1 Jan 2026</li><li>Reg 3 Carriage only per IMDG Code</li><li>Reg 4 Documents: DG declaration, container packing certificate, special list/manifest or stowage plan</li><li>Reg 5 Cargo Securing Manual</li><li>Reg 6 Reporting DG incidents (loss overboard) to nearest coastal State</li></ul><p><i>Part A-1 — Solid DG in bulk</i></p><ul><li>Reg 7 Definitions · 7-1 Application · 7-2 Documents · 7-3 Stowage &amp; segregation · 7-4 Reporting incidents</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Chemical tankers</i></p><ul><li>Reg 8–10: IBC Code MSC.4(48) · ship types 1, 2, 3 · Certificate of Fitness</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Gas carriers</i></p><ul><li>Reg 11–13: IGC Code MSC.5(48) · revised MSC.370(93) · ship types 1G–3G · Certificate of Fitness</li></ul><p><i>Part D — INF cargo</i></p><ul><li>Reg 14–16: INF Code MSC.88(71) · INF Class 1, 2, 3 ships</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VIII — Nuclear Ships</b></p><ul><li>Applies: nuclear-powered ships · Code A.491(XII)</li><li>Reg 1–12: reactor approval · radiation safety · safety assessment · operating manual · surveys · Nuclear Ship Safety Certificate · special control in ports · casualty reporting</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch IX — Management for the Safe Operation of Ships (ISM)</b></p><ul><li>Applies: 1 Jul 1998 — passenger ships, tankers, gas carriers, bulk carriers, cargo HSC ≥500 GT · 1 Jul 2002 — other cargo &amp; MODU ≥500 GT</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions (Company, DOC, SMC)</li><li>Reg 2 Application</li><li>Reg 3 Safety management: Company and ship comply with ISM Code A.741(18)</li><li>Reg 4 Certification: DOC (Company) · SMC (ship) · both 5 years</li><li>Reg 5 Maintenance of conditions</li><li>Reg 6 Verification and control: annual DOC verification · SMC intermediate between 2nd–3rd anniversary · Interim DOC 12 months · Interim SMC 6 months</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch X — High-Speed Craft</b></p><ul><li>Applies: HSC built ≥1 Jan 1996</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Requirements → HSC Code 1994 MSC.36(63) / 2000 MSC.97(73) · HSC Safety Certificate &amp; Permit to Operate</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XI-1 — Special Measures to Enhance Maritime Safety</b></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Recognized organizations: RO Code MSC.349(92)</li><li>Reg 2 Enhanced surveys: ESP Code 2011 A.1049(27) · bulk carriers &amp; oil tankers · close-up surveys, thickness measurement (firms audited, MSC.553(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li><li>2-1 Harmonized survey periods for cargo ships outside ESP</li><li>Reg 3 IMO number: passenger ≥100 GT, cargo ≥300 GT · permanently marked on hull / superstructure and inside</li><li>Reg 4 PSC on operational requirements: crew familiar with essential shipboard procedures</li><li>Reg 5 Continuous Synopsis Record (CSR): history of flag, owner, class, DOC/SMC/ISSC issuers</li><li>Reg 6 Marine casualty investigation: Casualty Investigation Code MSC.255(84)</li><li>Reg 7 Atmosphere testing instrument: O₂, flammable gases, H₂S, CO — 1 Jul 2016</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XI-2 — Special Measures to Enhance Maritime Security (ISPS)</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships, cargo ≥500 GT, MODUs (international) + port facilities</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application</li><li>Reg 3 Government obligations: set security levels</li><li>Reg 4 Company &amp; ship requirements: comply with ISPS Code (SOLAS/CONF.5/34) · Part A mandatory, Part B guidance</li><li>Reg 5 Company: master has info on who appoints crew and who employs the ship</li><li>Reg 6 Ship Security Alert System (SSAS): covert · from bridge + one other place · alerts company/authority only · no alarm on board</li><li>Reg 7 Threats to ships · Reg 8 Master&#39;s discretion for safety and security</li><li>Reg 9 Control &amp; compliance: ISSC check · info before entry · records of last 10 port calls</li><li>Reg 10 Port facilities: PFSA, PFSP, PFSO</li><li>Reg 11 Alternative security agreements · Reg 12 Equivalent arrangements · Reg 13 Communication of information to IMO</li></ul><p><i>ISPS Code key points</i></p><ul><li>Security levels 1, 2, 3</li><li>SSP, SSO, CSO, PFSO</li><li>ISSC valid 5 years</li><li>Drills every 3 months · exercises once per calendar year (≤18 months apart)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XII — Additional Safety Measures for Bulk Carriers</b></p><ul><li>Applies: bulk carriers</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Implementation schedule</li><li>Reg 4 Damage stability: ≥150 m, cargo density ≥1,000 kg/m³ — survive flooding of any one hold</li><li>Reg 5 Structural strength: withstand one-hold flooding</li><li>Reg 6 Structural &amp; other requirements · Reg 7 Survey &amp; maintenance</li><li>Reg 8 Compliance information: solid triangle mark on hull (≥150 m single-side skin, cargo ≥1,780 kg/m³)</li><li>Reg 9 Ships unable to meet Reg 4.3</li><li>Reg 10 Cargo density declaration by shipper (verified if 1,250–1,780 kg/m³)</li><li>Reg 11 Loading instrument: ≥150 m · also &lt;150 m built ≥1 Jul 2006</li><li>Reg 12 Water ingress alarms: holds, ballast &amp; dry spaces forward of collision bulkhead · pre-alarm and main alarm on bridge</li><li>Reg 13 Pumping systems: drain/pump forward ballast tanks &amp; dry spaces</li><li>Reg 14 Restrictions from sailing with any hold empty: single-side skin ≥150 m, cargo ≥1,780 kg/m³, after 10 years old · no hold &lt;10% of max allowable cargo (full load)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XIII — Verification of Compliance</b></p><ul><li>Applies: Contracting Governments</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Verification: III Code A.1070(28) · IMSAS audits since 1 Jan 2016</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XIV — Safety Measures for Ships Operating in Polar Waters</b></p><ul><li>Applies: SOLAS ships in Arctic / Antarctic waters</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Requirements: Polar Code Part I-A — MSC.385(94) · 1 Jan 2017 · Reg 4 Alternative design</li><li>Polar Ship Certificate · Categories A / B / C · Polar Water Operational Manual (PWOM)</li><li>From 1 Jan 2026: navigation &amp; voyage planning extended to fishing vessels ≥24 m, pleasure yachts ≥300 GT, cargo 300–499 GT — MSC.538(107)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XV — Safety Measures for Ships Carrying Industrial Personnel</b></p><ul><li>Applies: cargo ships &amp; HSC cargo craft ≥500 GT carrying &gt;12 industrial personnel · new ships ≥1 Jul 2024</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 General (counting IP) · Reg 3 Application (existing ships by first intermediate/renewal survey) · Reg 4 Other chapters · Reg 5 Requirements: IP Code MSC.527(106), certificate, surveys</li><li>Chapter adopted by MSC.521(106)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Who is Party (status)</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS 1974: 168 Contracting States · 98.91% of world tonnage</li><li>1978 Protocol: 123 States · 1988 Protocol (HSSC): 128 States</li><li>Almost every flag is Party; the few non-Parties are mostly landlocked or small States</li><li>Non-Party ships get <b>no more favourable treatment</b> (1988 Protocol Art I(3)): PSC inspects them to the same SOLAS standard and can restrict or detain them</li></ul><hr><p><b>Special Areas &amp; Routeing (SOLAS V/10–V/12)</b></p><ul><li>V/10 Ships&#39; routeing: Traffic Separation Schemes (TSS), Areas To Be Avoided (ATBA), deep-water routes — follow the ones IMO made mandatory</li><li>V/11 Mandatory ship reporting systems, e.g.:<ul><li>REEFREP — Torres Strait &amp; Great Barrier Reef inner route (MSC.52(66))</li><li>STRAITREP — Straits of Malacca &amp; Singapore (MSC.73(69), 1 Dec 1998): ≥300 GT, ≥50 m, any ship with hazardous cargo, all passenger ships</li><li>OUESSREP (Ushant) · Finisterre · WETREP (Western Europe PSSA) · CANREP (Canaries) · GALREP (Galápagos)</li></ul></li><li>V/12 Vessel Traffic Services (VTS): follow VTS where a coastal State sets them up</li><li>Polar waters (Ch XIV + Polar Code): an extra rulebook for Arctic &amp; Antarctic</li></ul><hr><p><b>USA — local rules on top of SOLAS (USCG)</b></p><ul><li>US is a SOLAS Party · USCG does PSC and enforces SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW for foreign ships</li><li>Notice of Arrival (33 CFR 160): ≥96 h before arrival (voyages &lt;96 h: before departure, ≥24 h before arrival) via eNOAD</li><li>33 CFR 164 Navigation safety (self-propelled ≥1,600 GT):<ul><li>Tests within 12 h before entering US waters: steering gear, internal comms &amp; alarms, standby generator, emergency batteries, main propulsion ahead/astern</li><li>Emergency steering drill logged within 48 h before entry (or every 3 months)</li><li>Corrected charts + US Coast Pilot, Light List, tide &amp; current tables (foreign equivalents allowed)</li><li>2nd independent radar ≥10,000 GT · gyro repeater, rudder angle indicator, echo sounder with recorder</li><li>AIS on at all times underway &amp; at anchor (§164.46)</li><li>Report failure of radar, AIS, gyro, echo sounder, steering gear to the COTP at once (§164.53)</li></ul></li><li>Security: MTSA rules (33 CFR 104) alongside ISPS · visitors&#39; ID checked and logged</li><li>Certificate of Compliance (COC) exam: foreign tank, gas &amp; passenger ships · full exam every 2 years<ul><li>Tankers: from May 2026 the annual mid-period exam can be credited by letter for good performers (no detention, violation or casualty in 3 years)</li></ul></li><li>Marine casualty: report immediately · CG-2692 within 5 days (46 CFR 4.05)</li><li>Crew: passport or ILO 185 seafarer ID; cash &gt;US$10,000 declared</li></ul><hr><p><b>Other regional rules</b></p><ul><li>EU: Paris MoU PSC (EU Directive 2009/16) · EU domestic passenger ships (Directive 2009/45)</li><li>PSC regimes: Paris MoU (Europe &amp; N. Atlantic) · Tokyo MoU (Asia-Pacific, incl. Philippines) · USCG · Indian Ocean, Black Sea, Mediterranean, Caribbean, Viña del Mar, Abuja, Riyadh MoUs</li><li>CIC 2026 (Paris + Tokyo MoU): <b>Cargo Securing</b> (SOLAS loading, stowage &amp; securing of cargo units) · 1 Sep–30 Nov 2026</li></ul><hr><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Chapter = the rule · Code = the detailed standard</li><li>PSC &amp; MARINA focus: certificates (Ch I), drills &amp; LSA (Ch III), GMDSS (Ch IV), navigation equipment &amp; voyage planning (Ch V), ISM (Ch IX), ISPS (Ch XI-2)</li><li>Watch: 2026 changes (lost containers, flashpoint, inclinometer, ro-ro fire, lifting appliances) · 2028–2029 (pilot ladders, S-100 ECDIS, lifeboat ventilation)</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Amendments to IMO instruments: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Amendments-to-IMO-instruments.aspx</li><li>IMO — Rules in force from 1 January 2026: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>ABS — SOLAS V/23 pilot transfer: https://ww2.eagle.org/en/rules-and-resources/regulatory-updates/SOLAS-Pilot-Transfer-Arrangements.html</li><li>Hong Kong Marine Dept — SOLAS Ch XV text: https://www.mardep.gov.hk/en/msnote/pdf/msin2312anx1.pdf</li><li>AMSA — IP Code MSC.527(106): https://www.amsa.gov.au/sites/default/files/2024-03/msc.527106.pdf</li><li>UK SI 2022/1218 Part 3 — UK law applying SOLAS XII bulk carrier rules: https://legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2022/1218/part/3/made/data.pdf</li><li>Bureau Veritas — Modernized GMDSS: https://marine-offshore.bureauveritas.com/newsroom/modernization-gmdss-entry-force-1-january-2024-amended-solas-chapter-iv</li><li>IRClass — MSC.402(96) lifeboat maintenance: https://www.irclass.org/technical-circulars/imo-resolution-msc-402-96-reg-maintenance-thorough-examination-operational-testing-overhaul-and-repair-of-lifeboats-including-free-fall-lifeboats/</li><li>West P&amp;I — SOLAS ECDIS &amp; BNWAS carriage schedule: https://www.westpandi.com/publications/news/solas-mandatory-requirements-for-ecdis-and-bnwas/</li><li>IMO — ESP Code A.1049(27): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/Documents/A%20-%20Assembly/1049(27).pdf</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (Parties &amp; tonnage): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status%20of%20IMO%20Treaties.pdf</li><li>RISK4SEA PSC wiki — ships of non-Parties, no more favourable treatment: https://risk4sea.com/psc-wiki-risk4sea/general-for-port-state-control/1-5-ships-of-non-parties/</li><li>Swedish Transport Agency — IMO mandatory ship reporting systems: https://transportstyrelsen.se/globalassets/global/sjofart/dokument/sjotrafik_dok/imo-documents-srs.pdf</li><li>MPA Singapore — STRAITREP (MSC.73(69)): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/docs/mpalibraries/circulars-and-notices/port-marine-circulars/-pc98-65</li><li>eCFR — 33 CFR Part 164 Navigation safety: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-33/chapter-I/subchapter-P/part-164</li><li>JD Supra — US arrival guide (NOA, casualty reports, crew ID): https://www.jdsupra.com/legalnews/navigating-u-s-arrival-a-guide-for-7727596/</li><li>USCG — performance-driven COC exam (May 2026): https://www.news.uscg.mil/maritime-commons/Article/4497186/coast-guard-announces-performance-driven-certificate-of-compliance-examination/</li><li>Paris MoU — CIC 2026 Cargo Securing: https://parismou.org/2026/08/concentrated-inspection-campaign-cargo-securing</li></ul>"}, {"id": "note-marpol-7378", "v": 3, "title": "MARPOL 73/78", "category": "Maritime › IMO › Conventions", "tags": ["marpol", "imo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>MARPOL 73/78 — International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships</b></p><p><b>What is MARPOL</b></p><ul><li>Main IMO convention against pollution from ships, operational and accidental</li><li>6 Annexes: oil, noxious liquids, packaged harmful substances, sewage, garbage, air</li><li>Flag State certifies (IOPP, NLS, ISPP, IAPP, IEE) · Port State Control inspects</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1954 OILPOL (oil only) · 1967 Torrey Canyon → new convention</li><li>1973: MARPOL adopted 2 Nov 1973</li><li>1978 Protocol: after 1976–77 tanker accidents · MARPOL 73/78 in force 2 Oct 1983</li><li>1997 Protocol: added Annex VI (air) · in force 19 May 2005</li><li>Annexes in force: I 2 Oct 1983 · II 6 Apr 1987 · III 1 Jul 1992 · IV 27 Sep 2003 · V 31 Dec 1988 · VI 19 May 2005</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships of Party flags (incl. hydrofoils, air-cushion, submersibles, floating craft, fixed/floating platforms)</li><li>Not covered: warships, naval auxiliaries, State ships on non-commercial service</li><li>Annex I &amp; II compulsory · Annexes III–VI optional for each Party</li></ul><p><b>Articles (main)</b></p><ul><li>Art 4 Violation: flag State sanctions; violation anywhere is prohibited</li><li>Art 5 Certificates &amp; inspection by Port States</li><li>Art 6 Detection of violations · Art 7 Undue delay to ships (compensation)</li><li>Art 8 Reports on incidents (Protocol I: report without delay)</li><li>Art 16 Amendments (tacit acceptance)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex I — Prevention of Pollution by Oil</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships · IOPP Certificate: oil tankers ≥150 GT, other ships ≥400 GT · 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1 — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions (oil, oily mixture, special area, en route)</li><li>Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Exemptions &amp; waivers · Reg 4 Exceptions (safety, damage) · Reg 5 Equivalents</li></ul><p><i>Ch 2 — Surveys and certification</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Surveys: initial, renewal, intermediate, annual, additional</li><li>Reg 7–9 IOPP Certificate &amp; Supplement (Form A other ships, Form B tankers)</li><li>Reg 10 Duration: 5 years</li><li>Reg 11 PSC on operational requirements</li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Machinery spaces of all ships</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Sludge tank: adequate capacity · no overboard connection</li><li>12A Oil fuel tank protection: aggregate fuel ≥600 m³, delivered ≥1 Aug 2010 — tanks away from shell</li><li>Reg 13 Standard discharge connection</li><li>Reg 14 Oil filtering equipment:<ul><li>≥400 GT: 15 ppm equipment</li><li>≥10,000 GT: 15 ppm alarm + automatic stop</li></ul></li><li>Reg 15 Discharge from machinery spaces:<ul><li>Outside special areas: en route · ≤15 ppm · OFE working · not from cargo pump-room bilges</li><li>Inside special areas: same (≥400 GT) · Antarctic: prohibited</li></ul></li><li>Reg 16 No oil in forepeak or collision-bulkhead tanks · ballast not in fuel tanks (≥4,000 GT)</li><li>Reg 17 Oil Record Book Part I (machinery operations) · kept 3 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 4 — Cargo area of oil tankers</i></p><ul><li>Reg 18 Segregated ballast tanks (SBT): crude ≥20,000 dwt, product ≥30,000 dwt</li><li>Reg 19 Double hull &amp; double bottom: tankers ≥5,000 dwt delivered ≥6 Jul 1996</li><li>Reg 20 Phase-out of single-hull tankers · Reg 21 Heavy grade oil in single-hull tankers</li><li>Reg 22 Pump-room bottom protection</li><li>Reg 23 Accidental oil outflow performance · Reg 24–26 Damage assumptions, outflow, tank size limits</li><li>Reg 27 Intact stability · Reg 28 Subdivision &amp; damage stability</li><li>Reg 29 Slop tanks · Reg 30 Pumping, piping, discharge arrangements</li><li>Reg 31 ODME (oil discharge monitoring) · Reg 32 Oil/water interface detector</li><li>Reg 33 Crude oil washing (COW): new crude tankers ≥20,000 dwt</li><li>Reg 34 Discharge from cargo area:<ul><li>Outside special areas: &gt;50 nm from land · en route · ≤30 L per nm · total ≤1/30,000 of last cargo · ODME &amp; slop tank working</li><li>Inside special areas: prohibited</li></ul></li><li>Reg 35 COW operations manual · Reg 36 Oil Record Book Part II (cargo/ballast operations)</li></ul><p><i>Ch 5–11</i></p><ul><li>Reg 37 SOPEP: tankers ≥150 GT, other ships ≥400 GT · tankers ≥5,000 dwt: shore-based damage stability programme</li><li>Reg 38 Reception facilities</li><li>Reg 39 Fixed/floating platforms</li><li>Reg 40–42 Ship-to-ship (STS) oil transfer: tankers ≥150 GT · STS Operations Plan · notify coastal State ≥48 h before</li><li>Reg 43 Antarctic: no heavy fuel oil carriage or use</li><li>Reg 43A Arctic HFO ban: from 1 Jul 2024 (waivers possible until 1 Jul 2029) — MEPC.329(76)</li><li>Reg 44 Verification of compliance (III Code)</li><li>Reg 46–47 Polar Code (Part II-A)</li></ul><p><i>Special areas</i></p><ul><li>Mediterranean · Baltic · Black Sea · Red Sea · Gulfs · Gulf of Aden · Antarctic · NW European waters · Oman area of Arabian Sea · Southern South African waters</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex II — Noxious Liquid Substances (NLS) in Bulk</b></p><ul><li>Applies: chemical tankers / ships carrying NLS in bulk · NLS Certificate 5 years (or IBC Certificate of Fitness)</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1–2 — General &amp; categorization</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Exceptions · Reg 4 Exemptions · Reg 5 Equivalents</li><li>Reg 6 Categories:<ul><li>X: major hazard → discharge prohibited</li><li>Y: hazard → limited discharge</li><li>Z: minor hazard → less strict limits</li><li>OS (other substances): not regulated</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Surveys and certification</i></p><ul><li>Reg 7 Chemical tankers: IBC/BCH certificate accepted</li><li>Reg 8 Surveys · Reg 9–10 NLS Certificate · 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 4 — Design and equipment</i></p><ul><li>Reg 11 Construction (IBC Code) · Reg 12 Pumping &amp; stripping:<ul><li>Residue ≤75 L per tank for X/Y/Z (ships after 2007)</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Ch 5 — Operational discharges</i></p><ul><li>Reg 13 Discharge conditions: en route ≥7 knots · below waterline · ≥12 nm from land · depth ≥25 m</li><li>Prewash before leaving unloading port: Category X · high-viscosity/solidifying Y (stricter since 2021 in NW European waters, Baltic, Norwegian &amp; North Seas)</li><li>Reg 14 Procedures &amp; Arrangements (P&amp;A) Manual</li><li>Reg 15 Cargo Record Book (kept 3 years)</li></ul><p><i>Ch 6–10</i></p><ul><li>Reg 16 PSC (surveyor endorses prewash in Cargo Record Book)</li><li>Reg 17 SMPEP (Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plan) for NLS</li><li>Reg 18 Reception facilities</li><li>Antarctic: no discharge · Polar Code Part II-A</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex III — Harmful Substances in Packaged Form</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships carrying marine pollutants (identified in IMDG Code)</li><li>Reg 1 Application (IMDG Code marine pollutants)</li><li>Reg 2 Packing: minimize hazard</li><li>Reg 3 Marking &amp; labelling: &quot;MARINE POLLUTANT&quot; mark, durable for 3 months in seawater</li><li>Reg 4 Documentation: shipping documents state &quot;marine pollutant&quot; · special list/manifest/stowage plan</li><li>Reg 5 Stowage: properly secured</li><li>Reg 6 Quantity limitations</li><li>Reg 7 Exceptions (jettison for safety)</li><li>Reg 8 PSC on operational requirements · Reg 9 Verification</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex IV — Sewage</b></p><ul><li>Applies: ships ≥400 GT, or &lt;400 GT certified for &gt;15 persons (international) · ISPP Certificate 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1–2 — General, surveys, certificate</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Exceptions</li><li>Reg 4 Surveys · Reg 5–8 ISPP Certificate · 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Equipment &amp; discharge</i></p><ul><li>Reg 9 Sewage systems: approved sewage treatment plant, OR comminuting &amp; disinfecting system, OR holding tank</li><li>Reg 10 Standard discharge connection</li><li>Reg 11 Discharge:<ul><li>Comminuted &amp; disinfected: &gt;3 nm from land</li><li>Untreated (from holding tank): &gt;12 nm · en route ≥4 knots · moderate rate</li><li>Approved treatment plant: anywhere (not visible solids, no discoloration)</li></ul></li><li>Baltic special area (passenger ships): sewage discharge prohibited unless special-area treatment plant · new ships 1 Jun 2019 · existing 1 Jun 2021 · route-exempt ships 1 Jun 2023</li></ul><p><i>Ch 4–6</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Reception facilities · 12bis Special area reception for passenger ships</li><li>Reg 13 PSC · Reg 14 Verification</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex V — Garbage</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships (incl. fixed/floating platforms)</li><li>Revised Annex V in force 1 Jan 2013: general prohibition, exceptions listed</li></ul><p><i>Regulations</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions (garbage, food wastes, cargo residues, operational wastes, e-waste)</li><li>Reg 2 Application</li><li>Reg 3 General prohibition: all discharge prohibited unless allowed · plastics never (incl. synthetic ropes, nets, plastic bags, incinerator ash from plastics)</li><li>Reg 4 Outside special areas:<ul><li>Food waste: &gt;3 nm if comminuted (≤25 mm) · &gt;12 nm if not comminuted</li><li>Cargo residues not harmful to the marine environment (HME): &gt;12 nm</li><li>Cleaning agents in wash water: if not HME</li><li>Animal carcasses: as far as possible from land</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5 Fixed/floating platforms: comminuted food waste &gt;12 nm only</li><li>Reg 6 Inside special areas: comminuted food waste &gt;12 nm · limited cargo-residue wash water · Antarctic: no poultry unless sterilized<ul><li>Special areas: Mediterranean, Baltic, Black Sea, Red Sea, Gulfs, North Sea, Antarctic, Wider Caribbean</li></ul></li><li>Reg 7 Exceptions: safety of ship/life · accidental loss (reasonable precautions) · loss of fishing gear reported</li><li>Reg 8 Reception facilities</li><li>Reg 9 PSC on operational requirements</li><li>Reg 10 Placards, Garbage Management Plan, records:<ul><li>Placards: ships ≥12 m</li><li>Garbage Management Plan: ≥100 GT or ≥15 persons</li><li>Garbage Record Book: ≥400 GT or ≥15 persons · and ships 100–399 GT from 1 May 2024 — MEPC.360(79) · kept 2 years</li><li>Part I (all ships): A plastics · B food waste · C domestic · D cooking oil · E incinerator ash · F operational waste · G animal carcasses · H fishing gear · I e-waste</li><li>Part II (solid bulk cargo ships): J cargo residues (non-HME) · K cargo residues (HME)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 11 Verification of compliance</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex VI — Prevention of Air Pollution from Ships</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships · IAPP Certificate: ≥400 GT · 5 years · IEE Certificate: ≥400 GT</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1–2 — General, surveys, certification</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Definitions · Reg 3 Exceptions &amp; exemptions · Reg 4 Equivalents (e.g. scrubbers)</li><li>Reg 5 Surveys · Reg 6–9 IAPP / IEE Certificates · Reg 10 PSC · Reg 11 Detection of violations</li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Control of emissions</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Ozone-depleting substances: no new installations (HCFCs banned from 1 Jan 2020) · ODS Record Book</li><li>Reg 13 NOx (diesel engines &gt;130 kW, EIAPP Certificate, NOx Technical Code 2008):<ul><li>Tier I: ships built ≥1 Jan 2000</li><li>Tier II: built ≥1 Jan 2011</li><li>Tier III: in NOx ECAs — North American &amp; US Caribbean (built ≥1 Jan 2016) · Baltic &amp; North Sea (built ≥1 Jan 2021) · Canadian Arctic (from 1 Mar 2026, keel laid ≥1 Jan 2025) · Norwegian Sea (contract ≥1 Mar 2026, or keel ≥1 Sep 2026, or delivery ≥1 Mar 2030)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 14 SOx &amp; PM:<ul><li>Global fuel sulphur ≤0.50% since 1 Jan 2020 · carriage ban of non-compliant fuel since 1 Mar 2020 (unless scrubber)</li><li>In SOx ECAs: ≤0.10% since 1 Jan 2015</li><li>SOx ECAs: Baltic · North Sea · North American · US Caribbean · Mediterranean (1 May 2025) · Canadian Arctic &amp; Norwegian Sea (1 Mar 2027) — MEPC.392(82)</li><li>Fuel changeover procedure &amp; log entries entering/leaving ECA</li></ul></li><li>Reg 15 VOC: tankers — VOC Management Plan</li><li>Reg 16 Shipboard incineration: type-approved incinerator (built ≥2000) · no PVC, no cargo residues of Annex I/II/III · not in port</li><li>Reg 17 Reception facilities</li><li>Reg 18 Fuel oil quality:<ul><li>Bunker Delivery Note (BDN): kept 3 years</li><li>Sealed MARPOL sample: kept ≥12 months</li><li>Non-availability → FONAR to flag &amp; port State</li><li>Fuel flashpoint and sulphur verified by sampling (in-use &amp; on-board samples)</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Ch 4 — Energy efficiency &amp; carbon intensity (revised MEPC.328(76), in force 1 Nov 2022)</i></p><ul><li>EEDI: new ships ≥400 GT (required index tightened in phases)</li><li>EEXI: existing ships ≥400 GT, from 1 Jan 2023</li><li>Reg 26 SEEMP: all ships ≥400 GT · now 3 parts — efficiency measures, fuel data collection, CII improvement plan</li><li>Reg 27 Fuel Oil Data Collection System (DCS): ≥5,000 GT · report yearly · Statement of Compliance · more detail from 1 Jan 2026 (MEPC.385(81))</li><li>Reg 28 CII rating: ≥5,000 GT · A–E yearly · D for 3 years or E once → corrective action plan<ul><li>Reduction vs 2019: 2027 13.626% · 2028 16.250% · 2029 18.875% · 2030 21.5% — MEPC.400(83)</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Pending</i></p><ul><li>IMO Net-Zero Framework (fuel standard + GHG pricing): approved MEPC 83 (Apr 2025) · not adopted at MEPC/ES.2 Oct 2025 (57–49 vote to adjourn 1 year) · session reconvenes 16–27 Nov 2026 · earliest entry into force 1 Mar 2028</li><li>North-East Atlantic ECA (SOx, NOx, PM; 0.10% S): also deferred in Oct 2025 · decision at Nov 2026 session</li><li>NOx Technical Code amendments: 1 Sep 2026 (MEPC.398(83)) · 1 Mar 2027 (MEPC.397(83))</li></ul><hr><p><b>Who is Party (status)</b></p><ul><li>Annexes I/II: 161 States (98.89% tonnage) · III: 151 · IV: 147 · V: 155 · VI: 105 (96.70%)</li><li>Annexes I &amp; II compulsory · III–VI optional, so a State can be Party to some Annexes only</li><li>Non-Party ships get <b>no more favourable treatment</b> (Art 5(4)): PSC applies MARPOL to them anyway</li><li>Related separate conventions: BWM 2004 (95 States, 92.41%) · AFS 2001 anti-fouling (96 States) · Hong Kong ship recycling (in force 26 Jun 2025)</li></ul><hr><p><b>USA — Party to Annexes I, II, III, V, VI · NOT Annex IV</b></p><ul><li>Law: Act to Prevent Pollution from Ships (APPS, 33 USC 1901) — false Oil Record Book or &quot;magic pipe&quot; = criminal case<ul><li>Annex I/II/V → 33 CFR 151, 155, 156, 157 · Annex III → hazmat rules (46 CFR 148, 49 CFR 171–176) · Annex VI → APPS + EPA engine rules (40 CFR)</li></ul></li><li>Sewage (no Annex IV): Clean Water Act — US-approved Marine Sanitation Device (33 CFR 159) · No-Discharge Zones</li><li>North American ECA (with Canada): 200 nm from coast · 0.10% S since 1 Jan 2015 · NOx Tier III for ships built ≥2016 · US Caribbean ECA (Puerto Rico, US Virgin Islands)</li><li>California (CARB): distillate fuel (MGO/MDO) within 24 nm of the California baseline · At-Berth rule: shore power or approved control at berth</li><li>Ballast water: US is <b>not</b> Party to BWM 2004 · own rules 33 CFR 151 Subparts C–D (up to 12 nm): USCG type-approved BWMS (D-2-like standard), US public water, shore discharge, or no discharge · BWM Plan, records &amp; reports</li><li>Vessel discharges: EPA Vessel General Permit (VGP) still applies until USCG finishes VIDA rules (EPA VIDA standards final Oct 2024; ships ≥79 ft; ballast, bilge, graywater, scrubber washwater, hull, coatings…)</li><li>OPA 90 oil spill rules:<ul><li>Vessel Response Plan (VRP): tankers, and non-tank ships ≥400 GT carrying oil fuel · Qualified Individual, OSRO, salvage &amp; firefighting contracts · drills</li><li>COFR (financial responsibility certificate) needed to enter US waters</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>EU — regional rules</b></p><ul><li>EU Sulphur Directive: 0.10% S at berth in EU ports (since 1 Jan 2010) · exempt &lt;2 h on timetable or on shore power · log changeover times</li><li>EU ETS (emissions trading) — ships ≥5,000 GT: surrender allowances for 40% of 2024 emissions, 70% for 2025, 100% from 2026 · 100% intra-EU voyages</li><li>FuelEU Maritime (from 1 Jan 2025, ≥5,000 GT): GHG intensity of energy −2% in 2025, rising to −80% by 2050<ul><li>Shore power: container &amp; passenger ships at TEN-T ports from 1 Jan 2030, all EEA ports with OPS from 2035 (stays &lt;2 h exempt)</li></ul></li><li>EU MRV: monitor, report, verify CO₂ (and other GHG) per voyage</li><li>Mediterranean ECA (MARPOL): 0.10% S from 1 May 2025</li></ul><hr><p><b>China — Domestic Emission Control Areas (DECA)</b></p><ul><li>Coastal DECA: 0.50% S since 1 Jan 2019 · ships without scrubber may not carry &gt;0.50% fuel since 1 Mar 2020</li><li>Inland rivers (Yangtze, Xijiang): 0.10% S since 1 Jan 2020</li><li>Hainan waters: 0.10% S since 1 Jan 2022</li><li>Scrubber washwater discharge banned in coastal DECA port areas, inland rivers and Bohai</li><li>Possible 0.10% for more coastal waters under review</li></ul><hr><p><b>Certificates &amp; Records (quick list)</b></p><ul><li>IOPP + Oil Record Book I/II · SOPEP</li><li>NLS Certificate + Cargo Record Book · P&amp;A Manual · SMPEP</li><li>ISPP · Garbage Management Plan + Garbage Record Book · placards</li><li>IAPP · EIAPP · IEE · SEEMP · DCS Statement of Compliance · ODS Record Book · BDNs + samples</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>MARPOL = what you may discharge, where, and how it is recorded</li><li>PSC focus: Oil Record Book entries vs. sounding/OWS, 15 ppm alarm, garbage records, fuel sulphur &amp; BDNs, ECA changeover logs, SEEMP/CII</li><li>&quot;Magic pipe&quot; or false ORB entries = criminal cases (heavy fines, jail) in many ports</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>Clyde &amp; Co — MARPOL Annex VI key developments 2026: https://www.clydeco.com/en/insights/2026/april/marpol-annex-vi-key-developments-for-2026</li><li>IMO — Amendments to IMO instruments (Mediterranean ECA, Arctic HFO ban): https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Amendments-to-IMO-instruments.aspx</li><li>RS Class — new ECAs Norwegian Sea &amp; Canadian Arctic: https://rs-class.org/en/news/general/marpol-amendments-entering-into-force-new-ecas-for-the-norwegian-sea-and-canadian-arctic-/</li><li>LISCR — MEPC 83 summary: https://www.liscr.com/marketing/liscr/media/liscr/online%20library/maritime/mepc-83-meeting-summary.pdf</li><li>IMO — EEXI and CII FAQ: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Pages/EEXI-CII-FAQ.aspx</li><li>DNV — Net-Zero Framework decision delayed: https://www.dnv.com/news/2025/decision-on-the-imo-net-zero-framework-delayed-for-one-year/</li><li>AMSA — Garbage Record Book: https://www.amsa.gov.au/marine-environment/marine-pollution/garbage-record-book</li><li>Lithuania TSA — MEPC.360(79) text: https://ltsa.lrv.lt/uploads/ltsa/documents/files/TJO/MEPC_360(79).pdf</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (Parties &amp; tonnage): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status%20of%20IMO%20Treaties.pdf</li><li>USCG — MARPOL: US Annexes and US laws: https://www.dco.uscg.mil/Our-Organization/Assistant-Commandant-for-Prevention-Policy-CG-5P/Inspections-Compliance-CG-5PC-/Commercial-Vessel-Compliance/MARPOL</li><li>Ship &amp; Bunker — North American ECA (200 nm): https://shipandbunker.com/news/am/377041-north-american-eca-comes-into-effect</li><li>CARB — Ocean-Going Vessel Fuel Regulation (24 nm): https://ww2.arb.ca.gov/our-work/programs/ocean-going-vessel-fuel-regulation</li><li>International Registries — US ballast water rules (US not Party to BWM): https://register-iri.com/wp-content/uploads/Update-on-United-States-Ballast-Water-Treatment-Requirements.pdf</li><li>Holland &amp; Knight — EPA VIDA final rule (Oct 2024): https://hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2024/10/epa-to-cruise-ships-cargo-ships-barges-tankers-tugs-and-tows</li><li>Skuld — US non-tank Vessel Response Plans: https://www.skuld.com/topics/environment/oil-pollution/america/us-nontank-vessel-response-plan-requirements/</li><li>American Club — EU 0.1% sulphur at berth: https://www.American-Club.com/files/files/MA_EU_Sulphur_Content.pdf</li><li>Kuehne+Nagel — EU ETS &amp; FuelEU Maritime: https://mykn.kuehne-nagel.com/news/article/eu-maritime-regulations-to-reduce-ghg-emissio-19-Dec-2024</li><li>Lloyd&#39;s Register — FuelEU shore power at berth: https://www.lr.org/en/services/statutory-compliance/fueleu-regulation/zero-emission-requirements-for-energy-used-at-berth/</li><li>DieselNet — China DECA sulphur rules: https://dieselnet.com/standards/cn/marine.php</li></ul>"}, {"id": "note-stcw-1978", "v": 2, "title": "STCW 1978", "category": "Maritime › IMO › Conventions", "tags": ["stcw", "imo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>STCW 1978 — International Convention on Standards of Training, Certification and Watchkeeping for Seafarers</b></p><p><b>What is STCW</b></p><ul><li>IMO convention for minimum training, certification &amp; watchkeeping standards for seafarers on seagoing ships</li><li>Convention (Articles) + Annex (Regulations, Ch I–VIII) + STCW Code<ul><li>Code Part A: mandatory (competence tables: function, competence, knowledge, assessment)</li><li>Code Part B: guidance</li></ul></li><li>Philippines: MARINA is the single STCW Administration (RA 10635, 2014) · MARINA STCW Office issues CoC/CoP</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1978: adopted 7 Jul 1978 · in force 28 Apr 1984 · first global standard for seafarers</li><li>1995 amendments: full revision · STCW Code created · White List (I/7) · in force 1 Feb 1997</li><li>2010 Manila amendments: adopted 25 Jun 2010 · in force 1 Jan 2012 · transition to 1 Jan 2017<ul><li>Rest hours tightened · alcohol limits · ECDIS, BRM/ERM, leadership &amp; teamwork</li><li>New grades: Able Seafarer Deck/Engine, ETO, ETR</li><li>Refresher training every 5 years · security training (VI/6)</li></ul></li><li>2016–2018: IGF Code ships (V/3) · Polar waters (V/4, in force 1 Jul 2018)</li><li>1 Jan 2026: harassment &amp; bullying (incl. sexual harassment &amp; assault) prevention added to basic &amp; officer training — MSC.560(108)</li><li>Comprehensive review (started MSC 105, 2022): 400+ gaps found · revised STCW targeted for MSC approval 2029–2030</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Seafarers serving on seagoing ships of Party flags</li><li>Not covered (Art III): warships, naval auxiliaries, State ships non-commercial · fishing vessels (see STCW-F 1995) · pleasure yachts not in trade · wooden ships of primitive build</li></ul><p><b>Articles (main)</b></p><ul><li>Art I General obligations · Art II Definitions · Art III Application</li><li>Art VI Certificates: issued after required training, service &amp; competence</li><li>Art VIII Dispensation: Master may be permitted to serve with a lower-grade officer — max 6 months, not for radio officer/master/chief engineer except emergency</li><li>Art IX Equivalents · Art X Control (PSC)</li><li>Art XII Amendments (tacit acceptance)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch I — General Provisions</b></p><ul><li>I/1 Definitions (CoC, CoP, documentary evidence, seagoing service)</li><li>I/2 Certificates &amp; endorsements:<ul><li>CoC (officers) needs flag State endorsement · original certificates carried on board</li></ul></li><li>I/3 Near-coastal voyages</li><li>I/4 Control procedures (PSC): check certificates, rest hours, watchkeeping competence — detain if deficiency endangers persons/ship/environment</li><li>I/5 National provisions (fraud, penalties)</li><li>I/6 Training &amp; assessment: qualified instructors &amp; assessors</li><li>I/7 Communication of information → &quot;White List&quot; of Parties giving full effect</li><li>I/8 Quality standards: QSS for training &amp; certification · independent evaluation every ≤5 years</li><li>I/9 Medical standards:<ul><li>Medical certificate max 2 years · under 18: max 1 year · colour vision max 6 years</li><li>Expiry during voyage: valid to next port with doctor, max 3 months</li></ul></li><li>I/10 Recognition of foreign certificates (endorsement of recognition)</li><li>I/11 Revalidation of CoC every 5 years:<ul><li>Sea service ≥12 months in last 5 years, or ≥3 months in last 6 months, or approved course/test</li></ul></li><li>I/12 Simulators: performance standards (radar/ARPA, ECDIS)</li><li>I/13 Trials · I/14 Company responsibilities:<ul><li>Seafarers certified &amp; familiarized · ship-specific familiarization before duties · effective communication language</li></ul></li><li>I/15 Transitional provisions</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch II — Master and Deck Department</b></p><ul><li>II/1 OOW (navigational watch) ≥500 GT:<ul><li>Age ≥18 · ≥12 months approved seagoing service within approved training (training record book) or ≥36 months</li><li>≥6 months bridge watchkeeping under master/qualified officer</li><li>GMDSS GOC/ROC · basic training VI/1</li></ul></li><li>II/2 Master &amp; Chief Mate:<ul><li>≥3,000 GT: CM — ≥12 months as OOW · Master — ≥36 months as OOW, or ≥24 months if ≥12 of them as CM</li><li>500–3,000 GT: CM — ≥12 months as OOW · Master — ≥36 months as OOW, or ≥24 months if ≥12 as CM</li></ul></li><li>II/3 OOW &amp; Master &lt;500 GT (near-coastal / not near-coastal)</li><li>II/4 Rating forming part of navigational watch (RFPNW):<ul><li>Age ≥16 · ≥6 months seagoing service, or ≥2 months with special training</li></ul></li><li>II/5 Able Seafarer Deck: age ≥18 · RFPNW + ≥18 months service, or ≥12 months with approved training</li></ul><p><b>Code A-II key competences (OOW)</b></p><ul><li>Plan &amp; conduct passage, position fixing · keep safe watch (COLREG, BRM)</li><li>Radar/ARPA &amp; ECDIS · respond to emergencies &amp; distress · IAMSAR · SMCP English</li><li>Cargo handling &amp; stowage · stability, trim &amp; stress · fire &amp; LSA · MARPOL &amp; leadership</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch III — Engine Department</b></p><ul><li>III/1 OOW engineering watch (≥750 kW): age ≥18 · ≥12 months workshop + sea service (approved training) · ≥30 months education &amp; training</li><li>III/2 Chief &amp; Second Engineer ≥3,000 kW:<ul><li>2/E: ≥12 months as assistant engineer/OOW</li><li>C/E: ≥36 months, or ≥24 months if ≥12 as 2/E</li></ul></li><li>III/3 Chief &amp; Second Engineer 750–3,000 kW:<ul><li>2/E: ≥12 months · C/E: ≥24 months, of which ≥12 as 2/E</li></ul></li><li>III/4 Rating forming part of engine watch: age ≥16 · ≥6 months service, or 2 months with training</li><li>III/5 Able Seafarer Engine</li><li>III/6 Electro-Technical Officer (ETO) · III/7 Electro-Technical Rating (ETR)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch IV — Radiocommunication &amp; Radio Operators</b></p><ul><li>IV/1 Application (GMDSS ships)</li><li>IV/2 GMDSS radio operators:<ul><li>GOC: sea areas A1–A4 · ROC: A1 only</li><li>Required for deck officers (OOW and above)</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch V — Special Training for Certain Ship Types</b></p><ul><li>V/1-1 Oil &amp; chemical tankers:<ul><li>Basic: cargo operation training</li><li>Advanced (Master, C/E, CM, 2/E, anyone in charge of cargo): ≥3 months service on that tanker type + course</li></ul></li><li>V/1-2 Liquefied gas tankers: basic + advanced (same idea)</li><li>V/2 Passenger ships: crowd management, safety training, crisis management &amp; human behaviour, passenger safety/cargo safety &amp; hull integrity (ro-ro)</li><li>V/3 Ships under IGF Code (gas/low-flashpoint fuels): basic + advanced</li><li>V/4 Polar waters: basic (Master, CM, OOW) + advanced (Master, CM)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VI — Emergency, Occupational Safety, Security, Medical Care, Survival</b></p><ul><li>VI/1 Basic Training (all seafarers with safety duties) — Code A-VI/1:<ul><li>Personal survival techniques (PST)</li><li>Fire prevention &amp; firefighting (FPFF)</li><li>Elementary first aid (EFA)</li><li>Personal safety &amp; social responsibilities (PSSR) — now incl. prevention of &amp; response to violence and harassment (Table A-VI/1-4, MSC.560(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li><li>Philippines: MARINA STCW Advisory 2025-40 / 2025-40A — BT-PSSR updating training for existing certificate holders</li><li>Refresher every 5 years: PST &amp; FPFF</li></ul></li><li>VI/1 Familiarization: safety familiarization before duties (all persons on board)</li><li>VI/2 Proficiency in survival craft &amp; rescue boats (PSCRB) · fast rescue boats · refresher 5 years</li><li>VI/3 Advanced firefighting · refresher 5 years</li><li>VI/4 Medical first aid (A-VI/4-1) · Medical care (A-VI/4-2, person in charge of medical care)</li><li>VI/5 Ship Security Officer (SSO)</li><li>VI/6 Security awareness (all) · designated security duties (crew with security tasks)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VII — Alternative Certification</b></p><ul><li>Function-based certificates (navigation, cargo, engineering, etc.) as alternative to Ch II/III</li><li>Same competence standards · rarely used</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VIII — Watchkeeping</b></p><ul><li>VIII/1 Fitness for duty:<ul><li>Rest: ≥10 h in any 24 h · ≥77 h in any 7 days</li><li>Max 2 rest periods, one ≥6 h · interval between rest periods ≤14 h</li><li>Exceptions: emergencies &amp; drills; Party may allow 70 h/7 days for ≤2 consecutive weeks</li><li>Drills: minimize rest disturbance</li><li>Watch schedules posted · rest-hour records kept &amp; endorsed</li><li>Alcohol: max 0.05% BAC or 0.25 mg/l in breath · drug &amp; alcohol abuse prevention</li></ul></li><li>VIII/2 Watchkeeping arrangements &amp; principles (Code A-VIII/2):<ul><li>Part 3-1 Navigational watch: lookout, master&#39;s standing orders, calling the master, handover, restricted visibility, pilot on board</li><li>Part 3-2 Engineering watch: UMS, handover, calling the C/E</li><li>Part 3-3 Radio watch</li><li>Part 4 Watch in port: deck &amp; engine · hazardous cargo</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>STCW = who is qualified to do what on board, and how rested they must be</li><li>Promotion path (≥3,000 GT): OOW → 12 months → CM → 12 months as CM (or 36 as OOW) → Master</li><li>PSC focus: valid CoC &amp; endorsements, CoPs, medical certificate, rest-hour records vs. log, watch schedules, familiarization records, language/communication</li><li>Keep track: 5-year CoC revalidation · 5-year refreshers (PST, FPFF, PSCRB, AFF) · 2-year medical</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — STCW Convention (dates of adoption, 1995 &amp; 2010 amendments): https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-standards-of-training,-certification-and-watchkeeping-for-seafarers-(stcw).aspx</li><li>IMO — Rules in force from 1 January 2026 (MSC.560(108)): https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>UK MCA — MGN 718: IMO amendment to PSSR (Table A-VI/1-4): https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-718-mf-2026-imo-amendment-to-pssr/mgn-718-mf-2026-imo-amendment-to-pssr</li><li>IMO — Comprehensive review of STCW FAQ: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/comprehensive-review-of-the-stcw-convention-and-code-faq.aspx</li><li>MARINA STCW Office — advisories: https://stcw.marina.gov.ph/marina-stcw-advisories/</li></ul>"}, {"id": "note-mlc-2006", "v": 2, "title": "MLC 2006 (ILO)", "category": "Maritime › ILO › Conventions", "tags": ["mlc", "ilo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>MLC 2006 — Maritime Labour Convention (ILO)</b></p><p><b>What is MLC</b></p><ul><li>ILO convention, the &quot;seafarers&#39; bill of rights&quot;: decent work &amp; living conditions on board</li><li>&quot;4th pillar&quot; of maritime law with SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW</li><li>Replaces 37 older ILO maritime Conventions and their Recommendations (68 instruments since 1920)</li><li>Structure: Articles → Regulations → Code (Part A mandatory Standards · Part B Guidelines)</li><li>5 Titles · &quot;No more favourable treatment&quot; (Art V.7): PSC applies it to ships of non-ratifying flags too</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Adopted 23 Feb 2006 (94th International Labour Conference, Geneva)</li><li>In force 20 Aug 2013 — after 30 ratifications covering ≥33% of world tonnage</li><li>Philippines ratified 20 Aug 2012 — the 30th registered ratification (with Russia the same day) → in force 20 Aug 2013</li><li>Amendments (via Special Tripartite Committee, Art XIII):<ul><li>2014: financial security for abandonment, death &amp; long-term disability · in force 18 Jan 2017</li><li>2016: harassment &amp; bullying guidance · MLC certificate extension (up to 5 months) · in force 8 Jan 2019</li><li>2018: wages &amp; SEA continue if held by pirates/armed robbers · in force 26 Dec 2020</li><li>2022: social connectivity, free food &amp; water, sized PPE, prompt repatriation · in force 23 Dec 2024</li><li>2025: &quot;key workers&quot;, shore leave, repatriation, harassment rules · adopted Apr 2025 · in force ~Dec 2027</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships, public or private, ordinarily engaged in commercial activities</li><li>Seafarer = any person employed, engaged or working on board in any capacity (incl. hotel staff)</li><li>Not covered: fishing vessels (ILO C188) · traditional ships (dhows, junks) · warships &amp; naval auxiliaries · ships only in inland/sheltered waters</li><li>Ships &lt;200 GT not on international voyages: flag State may apply some rules differently</li></ul><p><b>Articles (main)</b></p><ul><li>Art I General obligations · Art II Definitions &amp; scope</li><li>Art III Fundamental rights: freedom of association &amp; collective bargaining · no forced labour · no child labour · no discrimination</li><li>Art IV Seafarers&#39; employment &amp; social rights (safe workplace, fair terms, decent living conditions, health &amp; welfare)</li><li>Art V Implementation &amp; enforcement (flag + port State · no more favourable treatment)</li><li>Art VI Regulations &amp; Code (Part A mandatory, Part B guidance; &quot;substantially equivalent&quot;)</li><li>Art XIII Special Tripartite Committee (governments, shipowners, seafarers)</li><li>Art XIV–XV Amendments (Code: tacit acceptance)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 1 — Minimum Requirements to Work on a Ship</b></p><ul><li>Reg 1.1 Minimum age:<ul><li>≥16 years · ≥18 for night work and hazardous work</li><li>Night = ≥9 h, starting not later than midnight, ending not earlier than 5 am</li></ul></li><li>Reg 1.2 Medical certificate:<ul><li>Max 2 years · under 18: max 1 year · colour vision max 6 years</li><li>Expires at sea → valid until next port with doctor, max 3 months</li></ul></li><li>Reg 1.3 Training &amp; qualifications: trained/certified (STCW) · personal safety training</li><li>Reg 1.4 Recruitment &amp; placement:<ul><li>No fees charged to seafarers for placement</li><li>Licensed/regulated agencies (Philippines: DMW, formerly POEA) · agency protection system (insurance/compensation if agency fails)</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 2 — Conditions of Employment</b></p><ul><li>Reg 2.1 Seafarers&#39; Employment Agreement (SEA):<ul><li>Signed by seafarer &amp; shipowner (or representative) · copy on board · seafarer keeps original</li><li>Time to review &amp; get advice before signing · record of employment (no comment on quality of work)</li><li>Minimum notice for early termination: ≥7 days</li><li>2018: SEA continues while held by pirates</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.2 Wages:<ul><li>Paid at least monthly · monthly account (statement) · allotments to family · reasonable exchange-rate charges</li><li>2018: wages continue during piracy captivity</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.3 Hours of work &amp; rest (either system):<ul><li>Max work: 14 h in any 24 h · 72 h in any 7 days, OR</li><li>Min rest: 10 h in any 24 h · 77 h in any 7 days</li><li>Rest max 2 periods, one ≥6 h · interval ≤14 h</li><li>Table of shipboard working arrangements posted · records kept, signed by seafarer · emergencies override</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.4 Leave:<ul><li>Paid annual leave ≥2.5 calendar days per month of employment</li><li>Shore leave for health &amp; well-being (2025: no unjustified denial; key-worker access)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.5 Repatriation:<ul><li>Free repatriation: SEA expiry, termination, illness/injury, shipwreck, ship sale, piracy</li><li>Max service period on board &lt;12 months</li><li>2014: financial security for abandonment (Std A2.5.2) — 4 months&#39; wages, repatriation, essential needs (food, water, fuel, medical care)</li><li>2022: prompt repatriation, also when SEA expires abroad</li><li>2025: clearer repatriation duties incl. luggage allowance (30 kg)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.6 Compensation for ship loss or foundering: unemployment indemnity (max 2 months&#39; wages, Part B)</li><li>Reg 2.7 Manning levels: sufficient for safety, efficiency, fatigue (Safe Manning Document)</li><li>Reg 2.8 Career &amp; skill development, employment opportunities</li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 3 — Accommodation, Recreational Facilities, Food &amp; Catering</b></p><ul><li>Reg 3.1 Accommodation &amp; recreational facilities:<ul><li>Ships built ≥20 Aug 2013: cabin floor areas, headroom ≥203 cm, separate sleeping rooms, no cabins forward of collision bulkhead</li><li>Heating, ventilation, lighting, noise &amp; vibration limits · sanitary facilities · separate hospital if ≥15 seafarers on voyages &gt;3 days</li><li>Frequent inspections by master (record kept)</li><li>2022: social connectivity — internet access, reasonable cost (Reg 3.1 para 17)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 3.2 Food &amp; catering:<ul><li>Food &amp; drinking water free of charge · adequate quantity, nutrition, quality, variety (religion &amp; culture respected)</li><li>Ship&#39;s cook trained &amp; qualified · min age 18</li><li>Ships with &lt;10 crew: trained food handler instead of cook</li><li>Frequent documented inspections of food, water &amp; catering spaces</li><li>2022: nutritionally balanced food &amp; good quality water, free</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 4 — Health Protection, Medical Care, Welfare &amp; Social Security</b></p><ul><li>Reg 4.1 Medical care on board &amp; ashore:<ul><li>Free medical care, as close as possible to shore care · right to see doctor in port</li><li>Doctor required: ≥100 persons on international voyages &gt;3 days</li><li>No doctor: officer trained in medical care (STCW VI/4) · medicine chest · medical guide · radio medical advice</li><li>Standard medical report form</li><li>2022: prompt disembarkation of sick/injured · repatriation of remains</li></ul></li><li>Reg 4.2 Shipowners&#39; liability:<ul><li>Costs of sickness &amp; injury from start of duty to repatriation</li><li>Wages paid while sick/injured on board · ashore ≥16 weeks (national law)</li><li>Burial expenses</li><li>2014: financial security for contractual claims (death &amp; long-term disability)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 4.3 Health &amp; safety protection, accident prevention:<ul><li>Safety &amp; health policy, risk assessment · ship safety committee (≥5 seafarers)</li><li>Report &amp; investigate occupational accidents</li><li>2016 guidance: harassment &amp; bullying prevention</li><li>2022: PPE in proper sizes (incl. women) · deaths reported to ILO</li></ul></li><li>Reg 4.4 Access to shore-based welfare facilities (welfare boards, seafarer centres)</li><li>Reg 4.5 Social security: protection in at least 3 of 9 branches (medical care, sickness, unemployment, old age, employment injury, family, maternity, invalidity, survivors)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 5 — Compliance &amp; Enforcement</b></p><ul><li>Reg 5.1 Flag State:<ul><li>5.1.1 General principles: effective inspection &amp; certification system</li><li>5.1.2 Recognized Organizations (class societies) may do surveys</li><li>5.1.3 Maritime Labour Certificate &amp; DMLC:<ul><li>Required: ships ≥500 GT on international voyages (or between foreign ports)</li><li>MLC Certificate: 5 years · intermediate inspection between 2nd &amp; 3rd anniversary</li><li>Interim certificate: max 6 months (new ship, flag change, new shipowner)</li><li>DMLC Part I (flag State: national requirements) · Part II (shipowner: how they comply)</li><li>16 areas inspected: min age · medical cert · qualifications · SEA · recruitment &amp; placement · hours of work/rest · manning · accommodation · recreational facilities · food &amp; catering · health &amp; safety · medical care · on-board complaints · payment of wages · financial security for repatriation · financial security for shipowner liability</li><li>Certificates on board &amp; posted: MLC, DMLC, financial security certificates (2014)</li></ul></li><li>5.1.4 Inspection &amp; enforcement (inspectors&#39; powers, detention)</li><li>5.1.5 On-board complaint procedures: fair, quick, no victimization · complaint can go to master or ashore · copy of procedures given to seafarer</li><li>5.1.6 Marine casualties: official inquiry for serious casualties (2025: seafarers&#39; fair treatment in inquiries)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5.2 Port State:<ul><li>5.2.1 Inspections in port: check MLC &amp; DMLC · detailed inspection if documents missing/false, clear grounds, or complaint · detention for serious/repeated breaches</li><li>5.2.2 Onshore complaint handling</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5.3 Labour-supplying responsibilities: control of recruitment agencies &amp; social security for nationals (important for the Philippines)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>MLC = the seafarer&#39;s rights: contract, wages, rest, leave, repatriation, food, cabin, medical care, complaints</li><li>PSC &amp; ITF focus: SEA copies vs. crew list, wage records &amp; allotments, rest-hour records, MLC/DMLC &amp; financial security certificates, food &amp; water inspection records, complaint procedure, cabin condition</li><li>Abandonment: financial security must pay 4 months&#39; wages + repatriation — ILO/IMO abandonment database records cases</li><li>STCW &amp; MLC rest hours are the same (10 h / 77 h) — work-hour option exists only in MLC</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>ILO — MLC FAQ A44: amendments and their status (2014, 2016, 2018): https://faqmlc.ilo.org/en/?p=20318</li><li>ILO — 2022 amendments in force 23 Dec 2024: https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/new-important-set-amendments-mlc-2006-will-enter-force-23-december-2024</li><li>DNV — 2025 amendments (STC-5, Apr 2025; in force ~Dec 2027): https://www.dnv.com/news/2025/new-amendments-to-the-mlc-2006/</li><li>ILO — Philippines&#39; ratification, 30th, in force 20 Aug 2013: https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/philippines-ratification-marks-global-milestone-decent-work-seafarers</li><li>ILO — Russia &amp; Philippines ratifications (37 Conventions replaced): https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/milestone-ratifications-seafarers-labour-rights-charter</li><li>ILO — MLC 2006 text and preparatory reports: https://www.ilo.org/international-labour-standards/maritime-labour-convention-2006-0/text-and-preparatory-reports-maritime-labour-convention-2006</li></ul>"}];
// IMO conventions and codes (Obsidian notes in notes/imo, made by notes/imo-to-js.mjs).
const IMO_NOTES = [{"id":"note-imo-afs-2001","v":250363573,"title":"AFS 2001","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["afs","anti-fouling","tbt","cybutryne","hull-coating","certificate","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>AFS 2001 — International Convention on the Control of Harmful Anti-fouling Systems on Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>5 Oct 2001</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>17 Sep 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Organotin (TBT)</td><td>Not applied after 1 Jan 2003 · not borne on hulls after 1 Jan 2008 (or covered by a barrier coating)</td></tr><tr><td>Cybutryne</td><td>MEPC.331(76), in force 1 Jan 2023 · not applied or re-applied from 1 Jan 2023</td></tr><tr><td>Ships ≥ 400 GT, international voyages</td><td>International Anti-fouling System Certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Ships 24 m to &lt; 400 GT, international voyages</td><td>Declaration on Anti-fouling System</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is AFS</h2><ul><li>Parties must prohibit or restrict harmful anti-fouling systems on ships flying their flag, on ships operating under their authority, and on ships entering their ports, shipyards or offshore terminals (Art 3(1), Art 4(1))</li><li>Anti-fouling paints stop marine organisms attaching to hulls, which would slow ships and raise fuel use; the metallic compounds in them leach into seawater and can persist (IMO)</li><li>TBT caused deformities in oysters and sex changes in whelks (IMO)</li><li>The prohibited substances are in Annex 1, updated as needed; a technical group reviews proposals for other substances</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Open for signature 1 Feb 2002 – 31 Dec 2002 (Art 17)</li><li>Art 18: in force 12 months after at least 25 States, whose merchant fleets are at least 25% of world gross tonnage (met, giving 17 Sep 2008)</li><li><b>Cybutryne amendment MEPC.331(76)</b>: adopted 17 Jun 2021 · deemed accepted 1 Jul 2022 (Art 16(2))</li></ul><hr><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"afs-timeline\"><figcaption>Key dates of AFS 2001</figcaption></figure><h2>The Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Title / content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General obligations — give full effect to the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Definitions (10 terms)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Control of anti-fouling systems</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Control of waste materials</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Process for proposing amendments to Annex 1</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Technical groups</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Research and monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Surveys and certification</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Inspection of ships</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Violations</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Undue delay or detention of ships</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Settlement of disputes</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Relationship to international law of the sea (UNCLOS)</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Amendments</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Key articles</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 3(1)</b>: applies to ships flying a Party's flag, ships operating under a Party's authority, and ships entering a Party's port, shipyard or offshore terminal</li><li><b>Art 3(2)</b>: not warships, naval auxiliaries and other government ships on non-commercial service (Parties still ensure they act consistently where reasonable and practicable)</li><li><b>Art 3(3)</b>: Parties prevent non-Party ships from getting more favourable treatment</li><li><b>Art 4(2)</b>: a ship with a system that becomes controlled may keep it until its next scheduled renewal, but <b>not longer than 60 months after application</b>, unless the Committee finds exceptional circumstances</li><li><b>Art 5</b>: waste from applying or removing controlled systems is handled safely and in an environmentally sound way</li><li><b>Art 6</b>: a Party sends an initial proposal to IMO; the Committee decides if an in-depth review is needed; the proposing Party submits a comprehensive proposal; a technical group evaluates it; the Committee decides</li><li><b>Art 11 inspection</b>: (1) port inspection is limited to checking the certificate or declaration, and brief sampling that does not affect the system's integrity, structure or operation — sample processing time must not prevent the ship leaving; (2) a thorough inspection if there are clear grounds; (3) a Party may warn, detain, dismiss or exclude a ship in violation, and tells its Administration; (4) co-operation on enforcement</li><li><b>Art 12</b>: sanctions adequate in severity to discourage violations</li><li><b>Art 13</b>: a ship unduly detained or delayed is entitled to compensation</li><li><b>Art 16</b>: Annex amendment deemed accepted after 12 months unless more than one third of Parties object; generally in force six months after acceptance</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex 1 — Controls</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Substance</th><th>Control</th><th>Date</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Organotin compounds acting as biocides</td><td>Ships shall not apply or re-apply</td><td>1 Jan 2003</td></tr><tr><td>Organotin compounds acting as biocides</td><td>Ships shall either not bear such compounds on hulls or external surfaces, <b>or bear a coating that forms a barrier</b> to them</td><td>1 Jan 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Cybutryne</td><td>Ships shall not apply or re-apply anti-fouling systems containing cybutryne</td><td>1 Jan 2023</td></tr><tr><td>Cybutryne (existing)</td><td>Ships with cybutryne in the external coating layer must remove it or apply a barrier (sealer) coat at the next scheduled renewal after 1 Jan 2023, and not later than <b>60 months</b> after the last application</td><td>from 1 Jan 2023</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Organotin 1 Jan 2008 control: does not apply to fixed and floating platforms, FSUs and FPSOs built before 1 Jan 2003 that have not been in dry dock on or after that date</li><li>Cybutryne exceptions: fixed and floating platforms, FSUs and FPSOs built before 1 Jan 2023 and not dry-docked on or after that date · ships not on international voyages · ships under 400 GT on international voyages, if accepted by the coastal State(s)</li><li>Annexes 2 and 3: information needed in an initial proposal (five elements) and a comprehensive proposal (properties, environmental fate and effects, non-target organisms, human health, methods and peer review)</li></ul><h2>Annex 4 — Surveys and certification</h2><p><b>Regulation 1 — Surveys</b> (ships of 400 GT and above on international voyages, except platforms, FSUs and FPSOs)</p><ul><li>(a) an <b>initial survey</b> before the ship is put into service, or before a certificate is first issued</li><li>(b) a survey when the anti-fouling systems are <b>changed or replaced</b></li></ul><p><b>Regulation 2 — Certificate</b></p><ul><li>Issued after a successful survey; the issuing authority has full responsibility</li><li>Cybutryne amendment: for a ship with a system applied before the control took effect, the Administration issues a certificate not later than two years after entry into force of the control (MEPC.331(76) guidance: affected flagged ships obtain a valid certificate within 24 months)</li></ul><p><b>Regulation 3</b> — another Party may survey and certify at the Administration's request; no certificate to a ship of a non-Party State</p><p><b>Regulation 4 — Validity</b>: the certificate ceases to be valid if the anti-fouling system is changed without endorsement, or on transfer to another flag</p><p><b>Regulation 5 — Declaration</b>: ships of 24 m or more but under 400 GT on international voyages (except platforms, FSUs and FPSOs): the owner or authorized agent signs a <b>Declaration on Anti-fouling System</b>, with supporting documents</p><p><b>Certificate:</b> the International Anti-fouling System Certificate is supplemented by a <b>Record of Anti-fouling Systems</b>; Reg 1(4) allows survey and certification to be delegated to Recognized Organizations</p><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>At every dry dock with new hull paint: keep the paint documents, and get the Record of Anti-fouling Systems updated</blockquote><blockquote>Check the record shows no organotin (or a barrier coat) and no cybutryne (or a barrier coat)</blockquote><blockquote>A port State may check the certificate or declaration and take brief samples, without delaying the ship</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>AFS Convention articles (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-05/harmful-anti-fouling-ships.html</li><li>AFS Convention annexes (Australian Parliament, treaty text): https://aph.gov.au/~/media/wopapub/house/committee/jsct/march2003/treaties/Antifouling_text_pdf.ashx</li><li>MEPC.331(76) cybutryne amendment (Dutch treaty publication): https://zoek.officielebekendmakingen.nl/trb-2022-8.html</li><li>IMO — AFS Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-the-Control-of-Harmful-Anti-fouling-Systems-on-Ships-(AFS).aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-athens-2002","v":216000347,"title":"Athens 2002","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["athens","pal","passengers","luggage","liability","insurance","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Athens Convention 2002 (PAL)</h1><p><b>Athens Convention relating to the Carriage of Passengers and their Luggage by Sea, 1974, as amended by the 2002 Protocol</b></p><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subject</td><td>Carrier's liability for passengers' death or injury and for luggage</td></tr><tr><td>Convention</td><td>Adopted 13 Dec 1974 · in force 28 Apr 1987</td></tr><tr><td>2002 Protocol</td><td>Adopted 1 Nov 2002 · in force 23 Apr 2014</td></tr><tr><td>Main changes in 2002</td><td>Strict liability for shipping incidents, higher limits, compulsory insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Unit of account</td><td>SDR</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>History</h2><ul><li>The 1990 Protocol never entered into force.</li><li>A State that ratifies the 2002 Protocol must denounce the 1974 Convention and its 1976 and 1990 Protocols, if bound by them.</li><li>The Protocol lets States keep or set higher limits for carriers under their jurisdiction (Art 7).</li><li><b>IMO Reservation and Guidelines (Oct 2006):</b> adopted by the Legal Committee at its 92nd session to help States ratify. A State may limit war and terrorism liability to 250,000 SDR per passenger per occasion, or 340 million SDR per ship per occasion. The Guidelines say war cover should include war-related acts, capture or seizure, derelict weapons, terrorist or malicious acts, and confiscation or expropriation.</li></ul><h2>Application (Art 2)</h2><p>Applies to international carriage when:</p><ul><li>the ship flies the flag of, or is registered in, a State Party; or</li><li>the contract of carriage was made in a State Party; or</li><li>the place of departure or destination is in a State Party.</li></ul><p>It does not apply where another convention's mandatory civil liability regime for a different transport mode governs the carriage.</p><h2>Key definitions (Art 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Carrier</td><td>A person by or on behalf of whom a contract of carriage was concluded</td></tr><tr><td>Performing carrier</td><td>A person other than the carrier (owner, charterer or operator) who actually performs all or part of the carriage</td></tr><tr><td>Ship</td><td>A seagoing vessel only, not an air-cushion vehicle</td></tr><tr><td>Cabin luggage</td><td>Luggage in the passenger's cabin, or otherwise in the passenger's possession, custody or control</td></tr><tr><td>International carriage</td><td>Departure and destination in two different States, or in one State with a scheduled intermediate port of call in another</td></tr><tr><td>Shipping incident</td><td>Shipwreck, capsizing, collision or stranding; explosion or fire in the ship; or a defect in the ship</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Liability for death and personal injury</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"athens-liability\"><figcaption>Death and injury: liability tiers and compulsory insurance (Arts 3, 4bis, 7)</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shipping incident, up to 250,000 SDR per passenger per occasion</td><td>Carrier liable; it avoids this only by proving war, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, an exceptional natural phenomenon, or that a third party wholly caused it by an act or omission done with intent to cause the incident</td></tr><tr><td>Shipping incident, above 250,000 SDR</td><td>Carrier liable unless it proves it was without fault or neglect</td></tr><tr><td>Not a shipping incident</td><td>Carrier liable only for fault or neglect; the claimant must prove it</td></tr><tr><td>Overall limit (Art 7)</td><td>400,000 SDR per passenger per distinct occasion</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>A State Party may set a higher national limit, not lower than 400,000 SDR, and must inform the Secretary-General.</li><li>Contributory fault (Art 6): if the carrier proves the passenger's fault or neglect caused or contributed to the harm, the court may exonerate the carrier wholly or partly under its own law.</li></ul><h2>Luggage and vehicles (Art 8, per carriage)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Limit (SDR)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cabin luggage, per passenger</td><td>2,250</td></tr><tr><td>Vehicle, including luggage in or on it</td><td>12,700</td></tr><tr><td>Other luggage, per passenger</td><td>3,375</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Deductible:</b> the carrier and passenger may agree a deductible of up to 330 SDR for vehicle damage, and up to 149 SDR per passenger for other luggage.</li><li><b>Valuables (Art 5):</b> money, negotiable securities, gold, silverware, jewellery, works of art and other valuables are covered only if deposited with the carrier for safekeeping, and then up to the Art 8(3) limit unless a higher limit is agreed.</li><li>Cabin luggage: liability needs fault, and fault is presumed for shipping incidents. Other luggage: carrier liable unless it proves it was without fault.</li></ul><h2>Compulsory insurance (Art 4bis)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Which ships</td><td>Registered in a State Party and licensed to carry more than 12 passengers</td></tr><tr><td>Cover</td><td>Death and personal injury, at least 250,000 SDR per passenger per occasion</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Issued after the authority confirms compliance; shows ship, carrier, IMO number, type and duration of security, insurer and validity; <b>carried on board</b></td></tr><tr><td>Termination</td><td>Security cannot end early until 3 months after notice is given to the authorities</td></tr><tr><td>Direct action</td><td>Claims may be brought directly against the insurer; the 250,000 SDR limit applies even if the carrier cannot limit liability</td></tr><tr><td>Insurer's defences</td><td>May use the carrier's defences (except bankruptcy or winding up) and the assured's wilful misconduct</td></tr><tr><td>State duties</td><td>A flag State may not let a ship operate without a certificate; each Party must ensure insurance for such ships entering or leaving its ports</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Time limits and jurisdiction</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Time bar (Art 16)</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Starts</td><td>Injury: disembarkation. Death during carriage: when the passenger should have disembarked. Luggage: disembarkation, or when it should have occurred, whichever is later</td></tr><tr><td>Outer limit</td><td>5 years from disembarkation; or 3 years from when the claimant knew, or should have known, of the injury, loss or damage, if earlier</td></tr><tr><td>Extension</td><td>By the carrier's written declaration or a written agreement made after the cause of action arose</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction (Art 17)</td><td>Claimant's choice among: defendant's residence or principal place of business; departure or destination; claimant's residence (if defendant does business there); place of contract (same condition). After the incident the parties may agree another court or arbitration</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Strict liability for shipping incidents up to 250,000 SDR per passenger; up to 400,000 SDR unless the carrier proves no fault. Ships with more than 12 passengers must carry the insurance certificate on board. Claims can arrive up to 2 years later (5 at most), so record every passenger injury.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 22 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Consolidated Athens text (University of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/carriage-passangers-luggage-sea-consolidated.html</li><li>IMO — Athens Convention (PAL): https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Athens-Convention-relating-to-the-Carriage-of-Passengers-and-their-Luggage-by-Sea-(PAL).aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-bwm-2004","v":244418750,"title":"BWM 2004","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["bwm","ballast-water","d-1","d-2","bwms","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>BWM 2004 — International Convention for the Control and Management of Ships' Ballast Water and Sediments</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>13 Feb 2004</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>8 Sep 2017</td></tr><tr><td>Standards</td><td>D-1 exchange · D-2 performance</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>≥ 400 GT: International Ballast Water Management Certificate</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is BWM</h2><ul><li>Aim (Art 2): prevent, minimize and ultimately eliminate the transfer of harmful aquatic organisms and pathogens</li><li>Every ship carries a Ballast Water Management Plan and a Ballast Water Record Book</li><li>D-2 deadline: all ships by 8 Sep 2024 at the latest (see B-3)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Art 18: in force 12 months after 30 States representing 35% of world merchant tonnage (35% reached 8 Sep 2016)</li><li>MEPC.296(72) (amends A-1 and D-3) + MEPC.300(72): <b>BWMS Code</b> made mandatory (adopted MEPC 72, Apr 2018; in force Oct 2019); it superseded the G8 Guidelines from Oct 2019</li><li>MEPC.297(72): revised D-2 implementation schedule (Reg B-3) · MEPC.299(72): amended E-1 and E-5 (endorsement of additional surveys)</li><li>MEPC.325(75): <b>commissioning testing</b> made mandatory (amends Reg E-1 and the Appendix I certificate form) · adopted Nov 2020 · in force 1 Jun 2022</li><li>MEPC.369(80): new Appendix II (Ballast Water Record Book form) · MEPC.383(81): amends A-1 and B-2 to allow electronic record books · IMO listed them as expected in force Feb 2025 and 1 Oct 2025</li><li>Experience-building phase set up at MEPC 71 (Jul 2017, MEPC.290(71)) → data analysis report MEPC 78 (2022) → Convention Review Plan MEPC 80 (2023) → list of provisions to amend endorsed MEPC 81 (Mar 2024)</li><li>MEPC 84 (27 Apr–1 May 2026): approved the draft amendments from the Correspondence Group on the review of the Convention; entry into force to be decided at MEPC 85 so they take effect together with the BWMS Code. Direction: ships must prove the BWMS is working, maintained and meeting D-2, not just installed and type-approved</li><li>MEPC 84 also dealt with the 2026 G4 Guidelines (ballast water management and BWM Plans; they replace the earlier G4) and approved a unified interpretation of Reg D-3 (to go into BWM.2/Circ.66): systems approved under the 2016 G8 Guidelines are treated as equal to the BWMS Code and may still be installed</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><ul><li>Annex Regulations (A-, B-, D-, E- series) + Appendix I (form of the International BWM Certificate) + Appendix II (form of the Ballast Water Record Book)</li><li>Key IMO guidelines: G1 sediment reception facilities · G2 sampling · G3 equivalent compliance · G4 BWM plans (MEPC.127(53), amended; 2026 revision at MEPC 84) · G5 reception facilities · G6 ballast water exchange (2017, MEPC.288(71)) · G7 risk assessment under A-4 (2017, MEPC.289(71)) · G8 BWMS approval (2016, MEPC.279(70); superseded by the BWMS Code Oct 2019) · G9 active substances (MEPC.169(57)) · G10 prototype technology (MEPC.140(54)) · G11 exchange design and construction standards (MEPC.149(55)) · G12 sediment control design (MEPC.209(63)) · G13 additional measures incl. emergency situations (MEPC.161(56)) · G14 designation of exchange areas (MEPC.151(55))</li></ul><hr><h2>Section A — General provisions</h2><ul><li>A-1 Definitions</li><li>A-3 <b>Exceptions</b> — the standards do not apply to: uptake or discharge needed for the ship's safety in an emergency · accidental discharge or ingress from damage to the ship or equipment · operations to avoid or minimise pollution from the ship · uptake and discharge of the same ballast water on the high seas · discharge at the place where all the ballast water originated, if no unmanaged water from elsewhere was mixed in</li><li>A-4 <b>Exemptions</b> — for ships on voyages between specified locations (or in a defined area); max 5 years; only if the ballast water is not mixed outside the named locations; based on a risk assessment under G7</li><li>A-5 Equivalent compliance — for pleasure craft used solely for recreation or competition, and craft used primarily for search and rescue (G3: craft &lt; 50 m long with max ballast capacity of 8 m³)</li></ul><h2>Section B — Management and control requirements for ships</h2><ul><li>B-1 <b>Ballast Water Management Plan</b> (specific to the ship)</li><li>B-2 <b>Ballast Water Record Book</b> (kept on board at least 2 years, then under company control at least 3 more years)</li><li>B-3 Implementation schedule: D-2 is tied to the IOPP renewal survey (renewal survey after 8 Sep 2019 → D-2 by that survey, depending on the previous survey); ships without an IOPP renewal survey: date set by the flag State, not later than 8 Sep 2024; ships built on or after 8 Sep 2017: D-2 from delivery</li><li>B-4 <b>Ballast water exchange</b> (D-1): at least 200 nm from the nearest land and in water ≥ 200 m deep; if impossible, at least 50 nm from land and ≥ 200 m deep</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bwm-exchange-distance\"><figcaption>Reg B-4: ballast water exchange at least 200 nm from the nearest land in water at least 200 m deep; if that is not possible, at least 50 nm from land and at least 200 m deep. Drawn from the Convention text.</figcaption></figure><h2>Section D — Standards</h2><ul><li><b>D-1 Exchange standard</b>: at least 95% volumetric exchange (typically by pumping through 3 × the tank volume)</li><li><b>D-2 Performance standard</b> (discharge):<ul><li>&lt; 10 viable organisms per m³ of size ≥ 50 µm</li><li>&lt; 10 viable organisms per mL of size 10 to &lt; 50 µm</li><li>Toxicogenic *Vibrio cholerae* (O1 and O139) &lt; 1 cfu per 100 mL (or &lt; 1 cfu per g wet weight of zooplankton)</li><li>*Escherichia coli* &lt; 250 cfu per 100 mL</li><li>Intestinal enterococci &lt; 100 cfu per 100 mL</li></ul></li><li>D-3 Approval of BWMS: BWMS Code; systems using Active Substances need approval by IMO (G9 procedure)</li></ul><h2>Section E — Survey and certification</h2><ul><li>Ships ≥ 400 GT: <b>International Ballast Water Management Certificate</b>, valid up to 5 years (shows the expiry date)</li><li>Ships &lt; 400 GT: no survey/certificate requirement, but they must carry a Ballast Water Management Plan and a Record Book</li><li>Art 9: port State control officers may verify that the ship has a valid certificate</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Plan and Record Book on board; certificate if ≥ 400 GT</blockquote><blockquote>D-2 is the standard; D-1 exchange only follows the B-4 distance and depth rules</blockquote><blockquote>PSC can verify the certificate</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IRClass — MEPC 84 report: https://www.irclass.org/media/8526/mepc-84-report.pdf</li><li>Türk Loydu — MEPC 84 key outcomes: https://www.turkloydu.org/en-us/publications/articles/imo-marine-environment-protection-committee-mepc-84-key-regulatory-outcomes/</li><li>DNV — mandatory BWMS commissioning test from 1 Jun 2022: https://www.dnv.com/news/2022/mandatory-bwms-commissioning-test-required-from-1-june-2022-225958/</li><li>IMO — BWM Convention and Guidelines (G1–G14, amendments, EBP): https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/BWMConventionandGuidelines.aspx</li><li>IMO — MEPC 81 summary: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/MeetingSummaries/Pages/MEPC-81.aspx</li><li>IMO — Implementing the BWM Convention (D-2 numbers): https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/implementing-the-bwm-convention.aspx</li><li>IMO — FAQ implementing BWM: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Documents/FAQ%20-%20Implementing%20the%20Ballast%20Water%20Management%20Convention.pdf</li><li>UK gov — control and management of ballast water (exceptions, exemptions): https://www.gov.uk/control-and-management-of-ballast-water</li><li>IMO — BWM Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-for-the-Control-and-Management-of-Ships'-Ballast-Water-and-Sediments-(BWM).aspx</li><li>IMO — Resolution MEPC.300(72) BWMS Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MEPCDocuments/MEPC.300(72).pdf</li><li>Lloyd's Register — MEPC 84 summary report: https://www.lr.org/en/knowledge/regulatory-updates/imo-meetings-and-future-legislation/mepc-84-summary-report/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-bunkers-2001","v":31540509,"title":"Bunkers 2001","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["bunkers","bunker-oil","liability","insurance","certificate","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Bunkers 2001 — International Convention on Civil Liability for Bunker Oil Pollution Damage</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>23 Mar 2001</td></tr><tr><td>Opened for signature</td><td>1 Oct 2001 to 30 Sep 2002 (Art 12), then accession</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>21 Nov 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Entry-into-force rule</td><td>1 year after 18 States, including 5 with ships totalling at least 1 million GT (Art 14)</td></tr><tr><td>Liability</td><td>Strict, joint and several on all \"shipowners\" (Art 3)</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Ships over 1,000 GT (Art 7)</td></tr><tr><td>Time limits</td><td>3 years from the damage · 6 years from the incident (Art 8)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>19 Articles and an Annex (certificate model)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is Bunkers 2001</h2><ul><li>Compensation for people harmed by spills of <b>bunker oil</b>: oil carried as a ship's fuel</li><li>A standalone convention covering pollution damage only</li><li>Modelled on the 1969 Civil Liability Convention</li><li>Three tools: strict liability of the shipowner, compulsory insurance, and direct action against the insurer</li></ul><h2>Scope (Art 2)</h2><p>Applies only to:</p><ul><li>pollution damage in the territory, including the territorial sea, of a State Party, or in its exclusive economic zone (or equivalent zone up to 200 nautical miles from the baselines); and</li><li>preventive measures, <b>wherever taken</b>, to prevent or minimise such damage.</li></ul><h2>Key definitions (Art 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ship</td><td>Any seagoing vessel or seaborne craft, of any type</td></tr><tr><td>Shipowner</td><td>The owner (including the registered owner), bareboat charterer, manager and operator</td></tr><tr><td>Registered owner</td><td>The person registered as owner or, if unregistered, the owner. For a State ship run by a company registered as operator, that company</td></tr><tr><td>Bunker oil</td><td>Hydrocarbon mineral oil, including lubricating oil, used or intended for operating or propelling the ship, and its residues</td></tr><tr><td>Incident</td><td>An occurrence, or series with the same origin, that causes pollution damage or a grave and imminent threat of it</td></tr><tr><td>Preventive measures</td><td>Reasonable measures taken after an incident to prevent or minimise pollution damage</td></tr><tr><td>Pollution damage</td><td>(a) Loss or damage outside the ship from contamination by bunker oil escaping or discharged, wherever it occurs; environmental impairment compensation is limited to reasonable reinstatement costs. (b) The costs of preventive measures and further loss or damage they cause</td></tr><tr><td>Gross tonnage</td><td>Calculated under Annex I of the Tonnage Convention 1969</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Liability (Art 3)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bunkers-liability\"><figcaption>Who is liable, who can be sued and what insurance covers</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The shipowner at the time of the incident is liable for pollution damage from bunker oil on board or originating from the ship. For a series of occurrences, liability attaches to the owner at the first occurrence.</li><li>Where several persons are liable, liability is <b>joint and several</b>.</li><li>Claims against the shipowner may be made only under this Convention. The shipowner's independent rights of recourse are preserved.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>No liability if the shipowner proves</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>War, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, or an exceptional, inevitable and irresistible natural phenomenon</td></tr><tr><td>Damage wholly caused by a third party's intentional act or omission</td></tr><tr><td>Damage wholly caused by the negligence or wrongful act of a government authority responsible for lights or navigational aids</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>If the claimant's intentional act or negligence caused the damage wholly or partly, the shipowner may be wholly or partly exonerated towards that claimant.</li><li><b>Two or more ships (Art 5):</b> all shipowners concerned (unless exonerated) are jointly and severally liable for damage that is not reasonably separable.</li></ul><h2>Exclusions (Art 4)</h2><ul><li>Damage that is \"pollution damage\" under the CLC, whether or not CLC compensation is payable</li><li>Warships, naval auxiliaries and other State-owned or operated ships used only on non-commercial government service. A State may elect to apply the Convention to them.</li><li>For State-owned ships used commercially, the State may be sued in the Art 9 courts and must waive defences based on its sovereign status</li></ul><h2>Limitation (Art 6)</h2><ul><li>The shipowner, insurer and security provider keep the right to limit liability under any applicable national or international regime, <b>such as the 1976 LLMC Convention, as amended</b>.</li></ul><h2>Compulsory insurance (Art 7)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who</td><td>The registered owner of a ship over 1,000 GT registered in a State Party</td></tr><tr><td>Cover</td><td>Insurance or other financial security (e.g. a bank guarantee) for pollution liability up to the applicable limitation amount, never more than the LLMC 1976 amount, as amended</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Issued after the State authority checks compliance. The registry State issues or certifies it; for an unregistered ship, any State Party may</td></tr><tr><td>Contents</td><td>Ship name, distinctive number or letters and port of registry · registered owner's name and principal place of business · <b>IMO number</b> · type and duration of security · insurer or guarantor's name and principal place of business · validity period, not longer than the security</td></tr><tr><td>Language</td><td>The issuing State's official language; if that is not English, French or Spanish, a translation into one of them is needed</td></tr><tr><td>On board</td><td><b>Carried on board</b>; a copy is deposited with the registry authorities</td></tr><tr><td>Early termination</td><td>Security does not count if it can end, other than by expiry, within 3 months of notice of termination to the authorities, unless the certificate is surrendered or replaced in that period</td></tr><tr><td>Recognised bodies</td><td>A State may authorise a recognised organisation to issue certificates, but remains fully responsible for them</td></tr><tr><td>Mutual acceptance</td><td>Certificates issued by one Party are accepted by the others with equal force</td></tr><tr><td>Flag State</td><td>Must not allow a ship under its flag to operate without a certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Port State</td><td>Each Party must ensure by national law that the cover is in force for any ship over 1,000 GT, wherever registered, entering or leaving its ports or an offshore facility in its territorial sea</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic records</td><td>A State may notify that ships need not carry or produce the certificate in its ports, if the issuing State keeps electronic records open to all Parties</td></tr><tr><td>State-owned ships</td><td>No insurance needed, but the ship carries a registry-State certificate stating the ship is State-owned and liability is covered within the limit</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic trade</td><td>A State may declare Art 7 not applicable to ships operating only in its own territory and territorial sea</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Direct action (Art 7(10)):</b></p><ul><li>Claimants may sue the insurer or security provider directly.</li><li>The insurer may use the shipowner's defences (except bankruptcy or winding up), including Art 6 limitation, and the shipowner's <b>wilful misconduct</b>. No other defences.</li><li>Even if the owner cannot limit, the insurer may limit to the required insurance amount.</li><li>The insurer may require the shipowner to be joined.</li></ul><h2>Time limits (Art 8)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bunkers-timebar\"><figcaption>Action within 3 years of the damage, and never later than 6 years after the incident</figcaption></figure><h2>Jurisdiction and judgments (Arts 9–10)</h2><ul><li>Actions against the shipowner, insurer or security provider may be brought <b>only in the courts of States Parties</b> where the incident caused damage (territory, territorial sea or EEZ area), or where preventive measures were taken.</li><li>Each defendant must get reasonable notice. Each Party must ensure its courts can hear such claims.</li><li>A final, enforceable judgment from such a court is recognised in every Party, unless it was obtained by fraud or the defendant had no reasonable notice and fair chance to present its case. The merits cannot be reopened.</li></ul><h2>Final clauses</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11</td><td>Supersession: replaces earlier conventions only where they conflict</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval, accession</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>States with more than one legal system</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Denunciation: effective 1 year after deposit, or any longer period stated</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Revision or amendment: IMO convenes a conference at the request of one-third of the Parties</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Depositary: the IMO Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Transmission to the United Nations</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Languages: Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian, Spanish</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Ships over 1,000 GT carry the Bunkers certificate on board, and PSC checks it. The shipowner is strictly liable and the insurer can be sued directly. Claims are barred 3 years after the damage, and never later than 6 years after the incident. Cargo oil from tankers falls under the CLC, not Bunkers 2001.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Treaty text (Australian Parliament, JSCT): https://aphref.aph.gov.au/house/committee/jsct/28march2006/treaties/bunkers_text.pdf</li><li>IMO — Bunkers Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-Civil-Liability-for-Bunker-Oil-Pollution-Damage-(BUNKER).aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-clc-and-fund-1992","v":124204261,"title":"CLC and Fund 1992","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["clc","fund","iopc","oil-pollution","liability","compensation","tanker","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>CLC and Fund 1992 — Civil Liability Convention and Fund Convention for Oil Pollution Damage (with the 2003 Supplementary Fund)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Pays</th><th>Limit per incident (SDR)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 — CLC 1992</td><td>Shipowner (insured)</td><td>4.51 million up to 5,000 GT; +631 per GT above; max 89.77 million</td></tr><tr><td>2 — Fund 1992</td><td>Oil receivers</td><td>203 million in total (including CLC)</td></tr><tr><td>3 — Supplementary Fund 2003</td><td>Oil receivers (optional)</td><td>750 million in total</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is CLC / Fund</h2><ul><li><b>CLC 1992</b> (tier 1): International Convention on Civil Liability for Oil Pollution Damage — strict shipowner liability with compulsory insurance</li><li><b>Fund 1992</b>: International Convention on the Establishment of an International Fund for Compensation for Oil Pollution Damage — pays where full compensation cannot be obtained under the CLC (tier 2); financed by contributions from receivers of oil</li><li><b>Supplementary Fund Protocol 2003</b> (tier 3): optional</li><li>Covers pollution damage from <b>persistent oil</b> carried by ships constructed or adapted to carry oil in bulk — laden or unladen, including the bunker oil of such ships</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>CLC 1969: adopted 29 Nov 1969 · in force 19 Jun 1975</li><li>Fund 1971: adopted 18 Dec 1971 · in force 16 Oct 1978</li><li>1992 Protocols (CLC and Fund): adopted 27 Nov 1992 · in force 30 May 1996<ul><li>Extended coverage to the EEZ · preventive measures covered even without a spill, where there is a grave and imminent threat of pollution damage · environmental damage limited to the reasonable cost of reinstatement</li><li>States Parties to the 1992 CLC ceased to be Parties to CLC 1969 from 16 May 1998</li><li>CLC 1992 entry into force: 12 months after 10 States, including four each with at least 1 million units of gross tanker tonnage, deposited instruments (Protocol Art 13)</li></ul></li><li>Original 1992 limits (CLC 3 million SDR up to 5,000 GT + 420 SDR per extra GT, max 59.7 million; Fund 135 million in total) were raised by about 50% by the 2000 amendments, for incidents on or after 1 Nov 2003 (current limits in the table above)</li><li>2000 Protocol (adopted 27 Sep 2000, in force 27 Jun 2001) to end the Fund 1971 · Fund 1971 ceased to be in force 24 May 2002</li><li>Supplementary Fund Protocol: adopted 16 May 2003 · in force 3 Mar 2005</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>Pollution damage in the territory, territorial sea and EEZ of a Contracting State, and preventive measures wherever taken (CLC Art II)</li><li>\"Oil\" = any persistent hydrocarbon mineral oil such as crude oil, fuel oil, heavy diesel oil and lubricating oil, whether carried as cargo or in the bunkers of such a ship (CLC Art I)</li></ul><hr><h2>CLC 1992 — Tier 1 (shipowner)</h2><ul><li>The owner is liable for pollution damage caused by the ship as a result of the incident (Art III)</li><li>Exceptions: act of war or hostilities (and similar) · wholly caused by a third party's act done with intent to cause damage · wholly caused by the negligence or wrongful act of a government or authority responsible for maintaining lights or navigational aids</li><li>Channelling: no claim against the owner except under the Convention; no claim against listed persons such as crew, charterers and salvors, unless the damage resulted from their personal act or omission with intent, or recklessly and with knowledge that damage would probably result</li><li><b>Limits (incidents from 1 Nov 2003)</b>:<ul><li>Ship up to 5,000 GT: <b>4.51 million SDR</b></li><li>Above 5,000 GT: 4.51 million + <b>631 SDR per additional GT</b></li><li>Maximum: <b>89.77 million SDR</b></li></ul></li><li>The owner loses the right to limit if the damage resulted from his personal act or omission, committed with intent to cause such damage, or recklessly and with knowledge that such damage would probably result (Art V(2))</li><li><b>Compulsory insurance</b> for ships carrying <b>more than 2,000 tons of oil in bulk as cargo</b> (Art VII) · claims can be brought <b>directly against the insurer</b> or other provider of financial security (Art VII(8))</li><li>Time limits (Art VIII): action within 3 years from the date the damage occurred; never later than 6 years from the incident</li></ul><h2>Fund 1992 — Tier 2 (IOPC Fund)</h2><ul><li>Aim (Art 2): compensation to the extent that the protection under the CLC is inadequate</li><li>Pays any person suffering pollution damage where full compensation cannot be obtained under the CLC (Art 4)</li><li>Maximum per incident (including CLC): <b>203 million SDR</b>; a higher cap of 300.74 million SDR applies in certain conditions set in the Convention</li><li>Contributions (Art 10): paid by persons who received in total more than 150,000 tons of contributing oil (crude oil and fuel oil received after sea transport)</li></ul><h2>Supplementary Fund 2003 — Tier 3</h2><ul><li>Maximum payable for any one incident: <b>750 million SDR</b>, including amounts paid under the 1992 CLC and Fund</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"clc-fund-tiers\"><figcaption>Three tiers per incident in million SDR: CLC owner limit up to 89.77, Fund 1992 total 203, Supplementary Fund 2003 total 750. Bar lengths are to scale.</figcaption></figure><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Tankers carrying more than 2,000 tons of oil in bulk as cargo must have CLC insurance; the CLC covers the tanker's bunker oil as well as cargo oil</blockquote><blockquote>Persistent oil (crude, fuel oil, heavy diesel, lubricating oil) is covered</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IOPC Funds — Text of the Conventions: https://iopcfunds.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Text-of-Conventions_e.pdf</li><li>IMO — CLC: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-civil-liability-for-oil-pollution-damage-(clc).aspx</li><li>IMO — Fund: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-the-establishment-of-an-international-fund-for-compensation-for-oil-pollution-damage-%28fund%29.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-colreg-1972","v":28908248,"title":"COLREG 1972","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["colreg","rules-of-the-road","collision","lights","sound-signals","watchkeeping","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>COLREG 1972 — Convention on the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Done at London</td><td>20 Oct 1972</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>15 Jul 1977</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Articles I–IX · Rules 1–41 · Annexes I–IV</td></tr><tr><td>Parts</td><td>A General · B Steering and Sailing · C Lights and Shapes · D Sound and Light Signals · E Exemptions</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is COLREG</h2><ul><li>The \"rules of the road\" at sea: look-out, safe speed, risk of collision, action to avoid collision, steering and sailing rules, lights and shapes, sound and light signals, distress signals</li><li>The Convention (Articles I–IX) obliges Parties to give effect to the International Regulations (Rules 1–41 + Annexes I–IV) (Art I)</li><li>On entry into force the Regulations replaced the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea, 1960 (Art IV(5))</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Entry into force (Art IV): 12 months after at least 15 States whose merchant fleets make up at least 65% of the world fleet (vessels of 100 GT and over) became Parties</li><li>Art II: open for signature until 1 Jun 1973, then accession</li><li>Amendments (Art VI): proposed → two-thirds majority in the Maritime Safety Committee → two-thirds majority in the Assembly → in force on the date set by the Assembly unless more than one-third of Parties object (tacit acceptance)</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1981</td><td>A.464(12)</td><td>19 Nov 1981</td><td>1 Jun 1983</td></tr><tr><td>1987</td><td>A.626(15)</td><td>19 Nov 1987</td><td>19 Nov 1989</td></tr><tr><td>1989</td><td>A.678(16)</td><td>19 Oct 1989</td><td>19 Apr 1991</td></tr><tr><td>1993</td><td>A.736(18)</td><td>4 Nov 1993</td><td>4 Nov 1995</td></tr><tr><td>2001</td><td>A.910(22)</td><td>29 Nov 2001</td><td>29 Nov 2003</td></tr><tr><td>2007</td><td>A.1004(25)</td><td>29 Nov 2007</td><td>1 Dec 2009</td></tr><tr><td>2013</td><td>A.1085(28)</td><td>4 Dec 2013</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>1981: many rules, incl. Rules 1(c), 3(g), 10, 13(a), 22, 23, 24, 25, 27, 29, 30, 33, 34, 35, 36, 37, 38 and Annexes I and III</li><li>1987: Rules 1(e), 3(h), new 8(f), 10(a) and (c); Annex I; Annex IV new para 1(o)</li><li>1989: Rule 10(d) · 1993: Rule 26 (fishing vessel lights), Annexes I, II and IV</li><li>2001: Rule 3(a) and (m), Rule 8(a), Rule 18(f), Rule 23(c), Rule 31, Rule 33(a), Rule 35(i) (wing-in-ground craft), Annex I Sec 13, Annex III</li><li>2007: Annex IV (distress signals) revised</li><li>2013: new <b>Part F, Rules 39–41</b> (verification of compliance)</li><li>Note: the UK MCA amendment table gives 10 Dec 2013 for the 2013 amendments; the IMO resolution A.1085(28) itself states 4 Dec 2013</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>All vessels on the high seas and in all waters connected therewith navigable by seagoing vessels (Rule 1(a))</li><li>Rule 1(b): special rules for roadsteads, harbours, rivers, lakes or inland waterways connected with the high seas must conform as closely as possible to these Rules</li><li>Rule 1(c): a State may make special rules for additional station or signal lights, shapes or whistle signals for warships, vessels under convoy, fishing vessels fishing in a fleet — they must not be mistaken for any signal in the Rules</li><li>Rule 1(d): traffic separation schemes may be adopted by IMO</li><li>Rule 1(e): a vessel of special construction or purpose may comply as closely as practicable with the Rules on lights, shapes and signals, as determined by its Government</li></ul><hr><h2>Part A — General (Rules 1–3)</h2><ul><li>Rule 1 Application (above)</li><li>Rule 2 <b>Responsibility</b><ul><li>(a) nothing exonerates a vessel, owner, master or crew from the consequences of neglect to comply with the Rules or neglect of any precaution required by the ordinary practice of seamen or the special circumstances</li><li>(b) due regard to all dangers of navigation and collision and any special circumstances, including the limitations of the vessels involved, which may make a departure necessary to avoid <b>immediate danger</b></li></ul></li><li>Rule 3 General definitions:<ul><li>(a) vessel: every description of water craft, including non-displacement craft, WIG craft and seaplanes, used or capable of being used as a means of transportation on water</li><li>(b) power-driven vessel: propelled by machinery · (c) sailing vessel: under sail provided propelling machinery, if fitted, is not being used</li><li>(d) vessel engaged in fishing: fishing with nets, lines, trawls or other apparatus which restrict manoeuvrability (not trolling lines or other gear that do not restrict manoeuvrability)</li><li>(e) seaplane: any aircraft designed to manoeuvre on the water</li><li>(f) vessel not under command: through some exceptional circumstance unable to manoeuvre as required by the Rules because of some exceptional circumstance</li><li>(g) vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre: restricted by the nature of her work — includes vessels laying, servicing or picking up a navigation mark, submarine cable or pipeline; dredging, surveying or underwater operations; replenishing or transferring persons, provisions or cargo while underway; launching or recovering aircraft; mine clearance; towing operations that severely restrict the towing vessel and tow in their ability to deviate from their course</li><li>(h) vessel constrained by her draught: a power-driven vessel which, because of her draught in relation to the available depth and width of navigable water, is severely restricted in her ability to deviate from the course she is following</li><li>(i) underway: not at anchor, or made fast to the shore, or aground</li><li>(j) length and breadth: length overall and greatest breadth</li><li>(k) in sight of one another: only when one can be observed visually from the other</li></ul></li></ul><h2>Part B — Steering and sailing rules</h2><ul><li><b>Section I — Conduct of vessels in any condition of visibility (Rules 4–10)</b><ul><li>Rule 4 Application: Rules in Section I apply in any condition of visibility</li><li>Rule 5 <b>Look-out</b>: at all times by sight and hearing as well as by all available means appropriate in the prevailing circumstances and conditions, so as to make a full appraisal of the situation and of the risk of collision</li><li>Rule 6 <b>Safe speed</b>: at all times, so that proper and effective action can be taken to avoid collision and the vessel can be stopped within a distance appropriate to the circumstances<ul><li>(a) all vessels: visibility · traffic density · manoeuvrability (stopping distance and turning ability) · at night, background light · state of wind, sea and current, and proximity of navigational hazards · draught in relation to available depth of water</li><li>(b) vessels with operational radar: characteristics, efficiency and limitations of the radar · limitations imposed by the radar range scale in use · effect on radar detection of sea state, weather and other sources of interference · possibility that small vessels, ice and other floating objects may not be detected at adequate range · number, location and movement of vessels detected · more exact assessment of visibility possible when radar is used to determine the range of vessels or other objects in the vicinity</li></ul></li><li>Rule 7 <b>Risk of collision</b><ul><li>(a) all available means appropriate to the circumstances; if there is any doubt, such risk shall be deemed to exist</li><li>(b) proper use of radar equipment if fitted and operational, including long-range scanning to obtain early warning, and radar plotting or equivalent systematic observation of detected objects</li><li>(c) no assumptions on the basis of scanty information, especially scanty radar information</li><li>(d) in determining whether risk exists: (i) such risk shall be deemed to exist if the compass bearing of an approaching vessel does not appreciably change; (ii) such risk may sometimes exist even when an appreciable bearing change is evident, particularly when approaching a very large vessel or a tow or at close range</li></ul></li><li>Rule 8 <b>Action to avoid collision</b><ul><li>(a) if the circumstances of the case admit, positive, made in ample time and with due regard to the observance of good seamanship (and, since 2001, in compliance with the other Rules of Part B)</li><li>(b) any alteration of course and/or speed large enough to be readily apparent to another vessel observing visually or by radar; avoid a succession of small alterations</li><li>(c) if there is sufficient sea room, alteration of course alone may be the most effective action, provided it is made in good time, is substantial and does not result in another close-quarters situation</li><li>(d) action should result in passing at a safe distance; its effectiveness shall be carefully checked until the other vessel is finally past and clear</li><li>(e) if necessary to avoid collision or allow more time to assess the situation, slacken speed or take all way off by stopping or reversing the means of propulsion</li><li>(f) (i) a vessel required by these Rules not to impede the passage or safe passage of another vessel shall, when required by the circumstances of the case, take early action to allow sufficient sea room for the safe passage of the other vessel; (ii) this vessel is not relieved of the obligation if approaching so as to involve risk of collision, and shall have full regard to the actions which may be required by the Rules of Part B; (iii) when her passage is not to be impeded, the vessel remains fully obliged to comply with the Rules of Part B when the two vessels are approaching one another so as to involve risk of collision</li></ul></li><li>Rule 9 <b>Narrow channels</b><ul><li>(a) keep as near to the outer limit of the channel or fairway which lies on her starboard side as is safe and practicable</li><li>(b) a vessel of less than 20 m or a sailing vessel shall not impede the passage of a vessel which can safely navigate only within a narrow channel or fairway</li><li>(c) a vessel engaged in fishing shall not impede the passage of any other vessel navigating within a narrow channel or fairway</li><li>(d) shall not cross a narrow channel or fairway if such crossing impedes a vessel which can safely navigate only within it; the latter may use the Rule 34(d) doubt signal if in doubt about the intention of the crossing vessel</li><li>(e) overtaking: (i) where overtaking can take place only if the vessel to be overtaken has to take action to permit safe passing, the overtaking vessel indicates her intention by the appropriate signal of Rule 34(c)(i); the vessel to be overtaken, if in agreement, sounds the Rule 34(c)(ii) signal and takes steps to permit safe passing; if in doubt, she may sound the Rule 34(d) signals; (ii) this Rule does not relieve the overtaking vessel of her obligation under Rule 13</li><li>(f) a vessel nearing a bend or an area of a channel or fairway where other vessels may be obscured shall navigate with particular alertness and caution and sound the appropriate Rule 34(e) signal</li><li>(g) any vessel shall, if the circumstances of the case admit, avoid anchoring in a narrow channel</li></ul></li><li>Rule 10 <b>Traffic separation schemes</b><ul><li>(a) applies to schemes adopted by IMO; does not relieve any vessel of her obligation under any other Rule</li><li>(b) a vessel using a scheme shall: (i) proceed in the appropriate traffic lane in the general direction of traffic flow for that lane; (ii) so far as practicable keep clear of a traffic separation line or separation zone; (iii) normally join or leave a lane at the termination of the lane, but when joining or leaving from either side shall do so at as small an angle to the general direction of traffic flow as practicable</li><li>(c) so far as practicable avoid crossing traffic lanes, but if obliged to do so shall cross as nearly as practicable at right angles to the general direction of traffic flow</li><li>(d) (i) a vessel shall not use an inshore traffic zone when she can safely use the appropriate lane within the adjacent scheme; vessels of less than 20 m, sailing vessels and vessels engaged in fishing may use the inshore traffic zone at all times; (ii) notwithstanding (d)(i), a vessel may use an inshore traffic zone when en route to or from a port, offshore installation or structure, pilot station or any other place within the inshore traffic zone, or to avoid immediate danger</li><li>(e) a vessel other than a crossing vessel or one joining or leaving a lane shall not normally enter a separation zone or cross a separation line except in cases of emergency to avoid immediate danger, or to engage in fishing within a separation zone</li><li>(f) a vessel navigating in areas near the terminations of schemes shall do so with particular caution</li><li>(g) a vessel shall so far as practicable avoid anchoring in a traffic separation scheme or in areas near its terminations</li><li>(h) a vessel not using a scheme shall avoid it by as wide a margin as is practicable</li><li>(i) a vessel engaged in fishing shall not impede the passage of any vessel following a traffic lane</li><li>(j) a vessel of less than 20 m or a sailing vessel shall not impede the safe passage of a power-driven vessel following a traffic lane</li><li>(k) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre when engaged in an operation for the maintenance of safety of navigation in a traffic separation scheme is exempted from complying with this Rule to the extent necessary to carry out the operation</li><li>(l) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre when engaged in an operation for the laying, servicing or picking up of a submarine cable, within a traffic separation scheme, is exempted from complying with this Rule to the extent necessary to carry out the operation</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><b>Section II — Conduct of vessels in sight of one another (Rules 11–18)</b><ul><li>Rule 11 Application: vessels in sight of one another</li><li>Rule 12 <b>Sailing vessels</b> (risk of collision): (i) wind on different sides → the vessel with the wind on her port side keeps out of the way; (ii) wind on the same side → the windward vessel keeps out of the way of the leeward vessel; (iii) a vessel with the wind on the port side that sees a vessel to windward and cannot determine whether the other has the wind on the port or starboard side keeps out of the way; windward side = the side opposite to that on which the mainsail is carried (square-rigged: opposite the largest fore-and-aft sail)</li><li>Rule 13 <b>Overtaking</b><ul><li>(a) any vessel overtaking any other shall keep out of the way of the vessel being overtaken, notwithstanding anything in Sections I and II</li><li>(b) a vessel is overtaking when coming up with another from a direction more than <b>22.5 degrees abaft her beam</b>, i.e. in such a position that at night she would be able to see only the sternlight of that vessel but neither of her sidelights</li><li>(c) when in doubt whether she is overtaking, a vessel shall assume that this is the case and act accordingly</li><li>(d) any subsequent alteration of the bearing does not make the overtaking vessel a crossing vessel or relieve her of the duty to keep clear until finally past and clear</li></ul></li><li>Rule 14 <b>Head-on</b>: two power-driven vessels meeting on reciprocal or nearly reciprocal courses so as to involve risk of collision each alters course to <b>starboard</b> so that each passes on the port side of the other; exists when a vessel sees the other ahead or nearly ahead (at night: both masthead lights in a line or nearly in a line and/or both sidelights; by day: the corresponding aspect); in doubt, assume it exists</li><li>Rule 15 <b>Crossing</b>: two power-driven vessels crossing so as to involve risk of collision — the vessel which has the other on her own starboard side shall keep out of the way and, if the circumstances admit, avoid crossing ahead of the other vessel</li><li>Rule 16 <b>Give-way vessel</b>: take early and substantial action to keep well clear</li><li>Rule 17 <b>Stand-on vessel</b><ul><li>(a)(i) keep her course and speed · (ii) may take action to avoid collision by her manoeuvre alone, as soon as it becomes apparent that the vessel required to keep out of the way is not taking appropriate action</li><li>(b) when so close that collision cannot be avoided by the action of the give-way vessel alone, shall take such action as will best aid to avoid collision</li><li>(c) a power-driven vessel taking action in a crossing situation under (a)(ii) shall, if the circumstances admit, not alter course to port for a vessel on her own port side</li><li>(d) does not relieve the give-way vessel of her obligation to keep out of the way</li></ul></li><li>Rule 18 <b>Responsibilities between vessels</b> (except Rules 9, 10 and 13)<ul><li>(a) a power-driven vessel underway keeps out of the way of: not under command · restricted in ability to manoeuvre · engaged in fishing · a sailing vessel</li><li>(b) a sailing vessel underway keeps out of the way of: not under command · restricted in ability to manoeuvre · engaged in fishing</li><li>(c) a vessel engaged in fishing, when underway, shall so far as possible keep out of the way of: not under command · restricted in ability to manoeuvre</li><li>(d) (i) any vessel other than one not under command or restricted in her ability to manoeuvre shall, if the circumstances of the case admit, avoid impeding the safe passage of a vessel constrained by her draught, exhibiting the Rule 28 signals; (ii) a vessel constrained by her draught shall navigate with particular caution having full regard to her special condition</li><li>(e) a seaplane on the water shall in general keep well clear of all vessels and avoid impeding their navigation; where risk of collision exists she shall comply with the Rules of this Part</li><li>(f) a WIG craft: (i) when taking off, landing and in flight near the surface shall keep well clear of all other vessels and avoid impeding their navigation; (ii) when operating on the water surface shall comply with the Rules of this Part as a power-driven vessel</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><b>Section III — Conduct of vessels in restricted visibility (Rule 19)</b><ul><li>(a) applies to vessels not in sight of one another when navigating in or near an area of restricted visibility</li><li>(b) every vessel proceeds at a safe speed adapted to the prevailing circumstances and conditions of restricted visibility; a power-driven vessel shall have her engines ready for immediate manoeuvre</li><li>(c) due regard to the prevailing circumstances and conditions of restricted visibility when complying with Section I</li><li>(d) a vessel which detects by radar alone the presence of another vessel shall determine if a close-quarters situation is developing and/or risk of collision exists; if so, she shall take avoiding action in ample time; if the action consists of an alteration of course, so far as possible avoid: (i) an alteration of course to port for a vessel forward of the beam, other than for a vessel being overtaken; (ii) an alteration of course towards a vessel abeam or abaft the beam</li><li>(e) except where it has been determined that risk of collision does not exist, every vessel which hears apparently forward of her beam the fog signal of another vessel, or which cannot avoid a close-quarters situation with another vessel forward of her beam, shall reduce her speed to the minimum at which she can be kept on her course; she shall if necessary take all her way off and in any event navigate with extreme caution until danger of collision is over</li><li>There is no stand-on/give-way role between vessels not in sight of one another — each vessel acts under Rule 19</li></ul></li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"colreg-situations\"><figcaption>Rule 13 overtaking (more than 22.5 degrees abaft the beam, the overtaking vessel keeps clear), Rule 14 head-on (both alter to starboard, pass port to port), Rule 15 crossing (the vessel with the other on her starboard side gives way).</figcaption></figure><h2>Part C — Lights and shapes (Rules 20–31)</h2><ul><li>Rule 20 Application: in all weathers; lights from sunset to sunrise (no other lights unless they cannot be mistaken for the prescribed lights and do not impair them); prescribed lights, if carried, also from sunrise to sunset in restricted visibility, and may be shown in all other circumstances when considered necessary; shapes by day; lights and shapes must comply with Annex I</li><li>Rule 21 Definitions:<ul><li>masthead light: white, over the fore-and-aft centreline, <b>225°</b> arc, from right ahead to 22.5° abaft the beam on either side</li><li>sidelights: green on starboard and red on port, each <b>112.5°</b> arc, from right ahead to 22.5° abaft the beam on its side; in a vessel of less than 20 m the sidelights may be combined in one lantern on the centreline</li><li>sternlight: white, as near the stern as practicable, <b>135°</b> arc, 67.5° from right aft on each side</li><li>towing light: yellow, same characteristics as the sternlight · all-round light: 360° · flashing light: flashes at regular intervals at a frequency of <b>120 flashes or more per minute</b></li></ul></li><li>Rule 22 <b>Visibility of lights</b> (minimum ranges)<ul><li>Vessels of <b>50 m or more</b>: masthead 6 nm · sidelight 3 nm · sternlight 3 nm · towing light 3 nm · white, red, green or yellow all-round light 3 nm</li><li>Vessels of <b>12 m or more but less than 50 m</b>: masthead 5 nm (3 nm if less than 20 m) · sidelight, sternlight, towing and all-round lights 2 nm</li><li>Vessels of <b>less than 12 m</b>: masthead 2 nm · sidelight 1 nm · sternlight, towing and all-round lights 2 nm</li><li>Inconspicuous, partly submerged vessels or objects being towed: white all-round light 3 nm</li></ul></li><li>Rule 23 <b>Power-driven vessels underway</b>: masthead light forward · second masthead light abaft and higher than the forward one (vessels of less than 50 m need not, but may) · sidelights · sternlight; air-cushion vessels in non-displacement mode also an all-round flashing yellow light; WIG craft only when taking off, landing and in flight near the surface also a high-intensity all-round flashing red light; vessels of less than 12 m may show an all-round white light and sidelights instead; vessels of less than 7 m whose maximum speed does not exceed 7 knots may show an all-round white light and, if practicable, sidelights</li><li>Rule 24 <b>Towing and pushing</b><ul><li>Towing astern: two masthead lights in a vertical line (<b>three if the tow exceeds 200 m</b>, measured from the stern of the towing vessel) · sidelights · sternlight · towing light above the sternlight · a diamond shape where best seen if the tow exceeds 200 m</li><li>Pushing ahead or towing alongside (not a composite unit): two masthead lights in a vertical line, sidelights, sternlight</li><li>A pusher and pushed vessel rigidly connected as a composite unit are regarded as a power-driven vessel (Rule 23 lights)</li><li>Towing vessel also shows the Rule 23(a)(ii) light (second masthead light)</li><li>Other towed vessel or object: sidelights, sternlight and (if the tow exceeds 200 m) a diamond shape</li><li>Inconspicuous, partly submerged tow: all-round white lights at or near each end (two more at the extremities if 25 m wide or more; additional white lights not more than 100 m apart if over 100 m long); a diamond at or near the aftermost extremity (and another forward if the tow exceeds 200 m)</li><li>A vessel not normally engaged in towing, towing a vessel in distress, need not show the towing lights, but must take all possible measures to indicate the relationship (e.g. by illuminating the tow line)</li></ul></li><li>Rule 25 <b>Sailing vessels underway and vessels under oars</b>: sidelights and sternlight (under 20 m may combine in one lantern at or near the masthead) · optional at or near the top of the mast two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>red over green</b> (not with the combined lantern) · under 7 m: show the lights if practicable, otherwise have ready a white light (torch or lantern) · sailing vessel also using machinery: a cone, apex downwards, forward</li><li>Rule 26 <b>Fishing vessels</b> (underway or at anchor)<ul><li>Trawling: two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>green over white</b> (or a shape of two cones apex to apex); masthead light abaft and higher than the green light (optional under 50 m); sidelights and sternlight when making way</li><li>Fishing other than trawling: two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>red over white</b> (or two cones apex to apex); if outlying gear extends more than <b>150 m</b> horizontally, an all-round white light or a cone apex upward in the direction of the gear; sidelights and sternlight when making way</li><li>Annex II signals for fishing vessels fishing in close proximity · a vessel not fishing shows the lights for her length</li></ul></li><li>Rule 27 <b>Not under command / restricted in ability to manoeuvre</b><ul><li>Not under command: two all-round red lights in a vertical line (two balls); sidelights and sternlight when making way</li><li>Restricted in ability to manoeuvre (except mine clearance): three all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>red–white–red</b> (shapes: <b>ball–diamond–ball</b>); masthead lights, sidelights, sternlight when making way; at anchor also the Rule 30 signals</li><li>A towing vessel severely restricted shows the Rule 24(a) lights and the red–white–red lights / ball–diamond–ball shapes</li><li>Dredging or underwater operations: red–white–red lights; if an obstruction exists, two all-round red lights (or two balls) on the side of the obstruction and two all-round green lights (or two diamonds) on the side on which another vessel may pass</li></ul></li><li>Rule 28 <b>Vessels constrained by their draught</b>: may show three all-round red lights in a vertical line (or a cylinder) in addition to the Rule 23 lights</li><li>Rule 29 <b>Pilot vessels</b> on pilotage duty: two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>white over red</b>; sidelights and sternlight when underway; anchor lights/shape when at anchor</li><li>Rule 30 <b>Anchored vessels and vessels aground</b>: all-round white light or one ball in the fore part, and an all-round white light at or near the stern lower than the forward one (a vessel of less than 50 m may show only one all-round white light); working lights may be used; vessels of <b>100 m or more must</b> illuminate their decks; aground: anchor lights plus two all-round red lights in a vertical line (shapes: three balls in a vertical line); vessels under 12 m aground need not show the red lights or balls; vessels under 7 m at anchor not in or near a narrow channel, fairway, anchorage or where other vessels normally navigate need not show anchor lights</li><li>Rule 31 <b>Seaplanes and WIG craft</b> that cannot show lights and shapes with the characteristics or positions prescribed show lights and shapes as closely similar as possible</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"colreg-light-arcs\"><figcaption>Rule 21 light arcs: masthead light white 225 degrees, sidelights green starboard and red port 112.5 degrees each, sternlight white 135 degrees.</figcaption></figure><h2>Part D — Sound and light signals (Rules 32–37)</h2><ul><li>Rule 32 Definitions: <b>short blast</b> about 1 second · <b>prolonged blast</b> 4–6 seconds</li><li>Rule 33 Equipment: vessels of 12 m or more — whistle · 20 m or more — whistle and bell · 100 m or more — whistle, bell and gong (tone and sound not to be confused with the bell) · under 12 m — not obliged, but must have some other means of making an efficient sound signal</li><li>Rule 34 <b>Manoeuvring and warning signals</b><ul><li>(a) in sight, power-driven vessel underway manoeuvring: <b>1 short blast</b> = I am altering my course to starboard · <b>2 short</b> = to port · <b>3 short</b> = my engines are going astern</li><li>(b) optional light signals: one, two or three flashes with the same meanings; flash about 1 second, interval about 1 second, at least 10 seconds between signals; all-round white light visible at least 5 nm</li><li>(c) in a narrow channel or fairway: <b>2 prolonged + 1 short</b> = I intend to overtake on your starboard side · <b>2 prolonged + 2 short</b> = on your port side · agreement: <b>prolonged–short–prolonged–short</b></li><li>(d) in doubt about the other vessel's intentions or actions: <b>at least 5 short and rapid blasts</b> (may be supplemented by at least 5 short and rapid flashes)</li><li>(e) nearing a bend or area where other vessels may be obscured: <b>1 prolonged blast</b>, answered by 1 prolonged blast by any approaching vessel within hearing</li><li>(f) if whistles are more than 100 m apart, only one is used for manoeuvring and warning signals</li></ul></li><li>Rule 35 <b>Sound signals in restricted visibility</b> <ul><li>Power-driven vessel making way: <b>1 prolonged blast</b> at intervals of not more than 2 minutes</li><li>Power-driven vessel underway but stopped, making no way: <b>2 prolonged blasts</b> (about 2 s between) at intervals of not more than 2 minutes</li><li>Not under command, restricted in ability to manoeuvre, constrained by draught, sailing, fishing, towing or pushing: <b>1 prolonged + 2 short</b> at intervals of not more than 2 minutes</li><li>A vessel towed (or the last vessel of a tow, if manned): <b>1 prolonged + 3 short</b>, if possible immediately after the towing vessel's signal</li><li>At anchor: rapid ringing of the bell for about <b>5 seconds</b> at intervals of not more than <b>1 minute</b>; vessel of <b>100 m or more</b> also gong sounded rapidly aft for about 5 seconds; may add <b>1 short + 1 prolonged + 1 short</b></li><li>Aground: bell (and gong) as at anchor plus <b>3 separate and distinct strokes on the bell</b> immediately before and after the rapid ringing</li><li>Vessels of 12 m to under 20 m are not obliged to give the bell signals but must give another efficient sound signal at intervals of not more than 2 minutes · vessels under 12 m likewise</li><li>Pilot vessel on pilotage duty may also sound an identity signal of <b>4 short blasts</b></li></ul></li><li>Rule 36 Signals to attract attention: any vessel may make light or sound signals, or direct the beam of a searchlight in the direction of the danger, not mistakable for any authorized signal or navigational aid; avoid high-intensity intermittent or revolving lights (e.g. strobe lights)</li><li>Rule 37 <b>Distress signals</b>: a vessel in distress and requiring assistance uses or exhibits the signals in Annex IV</li></ul><p><b>Part E — Exemptions (Rule 38)</b>: vessels whose keel was laid before the Regulations entered into force (15 Jul 1977) — exempt for a period for lights ranges (Rule 22) and colours (Annex I Sec 7) (4 years), masthead light repositioning (Annex I Sec 3(a), Sec 2(b)), sidelights (Sec 2(g), 3(b)) and sound appliances (Annex III) (9 years); some metric repositioning of lights is permanently exempt</p><p><b>Part F — Verification of compliance (Rules 39–41)</b> (in force 1 Jan 2016, A.1085(28))</p><ul><li>Rule 39 Definitions: Audit · Audit Scheme (IMO Member State Audit Scheme) · Code for Implementation (III Code, A.1070(28)) · Audit Standard</li><li>Rule 40 Application: Parties use the III Code to discharge their obligations under the Convention</li><li>Rule 41 Verification of compliance: every Party is subject to periodic IMO audits against the audit standard; the Secretary-General administers the scheme; Parties facilitate the audit and carry out a programme of actions to address findings; audits follow an overall schedule set by the Secretary-General</li></ul><h2>Annexes</h2><ul><li><b>Annex I — Positioning and technical details of lights and shapes</b> (per the US publication of the International Rules)<ul><li>Forward masthead light: at least 6 m above the hull for vessels of 20 m or more (not more than 12 m); the after masthead at least 4.5 m higher than the forward · vessels of 12 m to under 20 m: masthead at least 2.5 m above the gunwale</li><li>Masthead lights horizontally at least half the vessel's length apart (max 100 m)</li><li>Sidelights not more than three-quarters of the height of the forward masthead light</li><li>Forward anchor light (50 m or more): at least 6 m above the hull</li><li>Minimum luminous intensity for visibility ranges (K = 0.8): 1 nm = 0.9 cd · 2 nm = 4.3 cd · 3 nm = 12 cd · 4 nm = 27 cd · 5 nm = 52 cd · 6 nm = 94 cd</li><li>Sidelights of vessels of 20 m or more need matt black inboard screens · shapes are black; a ball at least 0.6 m diameter; at least 1.5 m between shapes</li></ul></li><li><b>Annex II — Additional signals for fishing vessels fishing in close proximity</b>: lights at least 0.9 m apart, lower than the Rule 26 lights, visible all round at least 1 nm · trawlers of 20 m or more: shooting nets = two white lights in a vertical line; hauling = white over red; net fast on an obstruction = two red lights in a vertical line · purse seiners hampered by gear: two yellow lights in a vertical line flashing alternately every second</li><li><b>Annex III — Technical details of sound signal appliances</b>: whistle fundamental frequency 70–700 Hz (200 m or more: 70–200 Hz; 75–200 m: 130–350 Hz; under 75 m: 250–700 Hz) · sound pressure level at 1 m: 143 dB (200 m or more), 138 dB (75–200 m), 130 dB (20–75 m) · audibility range 2 / 1.5 / 1 / 0.5 nm by length band · bell or gong: at least 110 dB at 1 m; bell mouth at least 300 mm for vessels of 20 m or more</li><li><b>Annex IV — Distress signals</b> (as revised by A.1004(25), in force 1 Dec 2009): (a) gun or other explosive signal fired at intervals of about a minute · (b) continuous sounding of any fog-signalling apparatus · (c) rockets or shells throwing red stars fired one at a time at short intervals · (d) Morse code SOS · (e) the spoken word \"MAYDAY\" by radiotelephone · (f) International Code signal N.C. · (g) a square flag with a ball (or anything resembling a ball) above or below it · (h) flames on the vessel (e.g. burning tar barrel) · (i) a rocket parachute flare or hand flare showing a red light · (j) a smoke signal giving off orange-coloured smoke · (k) slowly and repeatedly raising and lowering arms outstretched to each side · (l) a DSC distress alert on VHF channel 70, or on MF/HF · (m) a ship-to-shore distress alert transmitted by Inmarsat or another mobile satellite service ship earth station · (n) signals transmitted by EPIRBs · (o) approved signals transmitted by radiocommunication systems, including survival craft radar transponders · using these signals for any other purpose, or other signals that may be confused with them, is prohibited · aids: orange canvas with a black square and circle, and a dye marker</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Rules 5–8 (look-out, safe speed, risk of collision, action to avoid collision) and 13–19 are the core of watchkeeping</blockquote><blockquote>Rule 2: depart from the Rules only to avoid immediate danger; Rule 17(b): the stand-on vessel must act when collision cannot be avoided by the give-way vessel alone</blockquote><blockquote>Rule 19: vessels not in sight of one another — safe speed, engines ready, radar-only detection → avoid turning to port for a vessel forward of the beam (except overtaking) and turning towards a vessel abeam or abaft the beam</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Convention articles (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/08/8-03/preventing-collisions-sea.html</li><li>UK MCA — original COLREG and amendments table: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/msn-1781-mf-amendment-3-the-merchant-shipping-regulations-1996-colreg/appendix-2-original-colreg-and-amendments</li><li>US Coast Guard — Navigation Rules (International and Inland): https://www.whoi.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/USCG-Navigation-Rules_International-Inland.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution A.1004(25) (Annex IV): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1004%2825%29.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution A.1085(28) (Part F): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1085(28).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-csc-1972","v":333336,"title":"CSC 1972","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["csc","containers","safety-approval-plate","acep","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>CSC 1972 — International Convention for Safe Containers</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>2 Dec 1972, Geneva (conference of the UN and IMO)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>6 Sep 1977 (IMO treaty table)</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>85 States, 67.71% of world tonnage (IMO table, printed 5 Sep 2023)</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>12 months after the 10th instrument is deposited (Art VIII)</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Keep a high level of human safety in container transport and handling, and facilitate international transport through uniform rules</td></tr><tr><td>Key marking</td><td>CSC Safety Approval Plate</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendments in force</td><td>MSC.355(92), 1 Jul 2014</td></tr><tr><td>Authentic languages</td><td>Chinese, English, French, Russian, Spanish</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"csc-tests\"><figcaption>Annex II structural test loads</figcaption></figure><h2>What is CSC 1972</h2><ul><li>A uniform set of strength requirements and test procedures for freight containers in international transport, whatever the mode of surface transport</li><li>Applies to new and existing containers in international transport, except containers designed specifically for air carriage (Art III)</li><li>Limited to containers of a prescribed minimum size with corner fittings</li></ul><h3>Definition of a container (Art II)</h3><ul><li>Permanent and reusable, designed for transport by one or more modes without intermediate reloading</li><li>Equipped with corner fittings</li><li>Floor area within the four bottom corners at least <b>14 m²</b>, or <b>7 m²</b> if fitted with top corner fittings</li><li>Vehicles and packaging are excluded. Containers on chassis are included.</li><li><b>New container:</b> construction began on or after entry into force. <b>Existing container:</b> any other (Art II(8)–(9)).</li><li><b>R</b> = maximum operating gross mass (weight, originally) · <b>tare</b> = empty mass · <b>P</b> = R minus tare</li></ul><h2>Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>Parties give effect to the Convention and Annexes</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Application: new containers need type or individual approval under Annex I. Existing containers had to be approved within 5 years of entry into force.</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Each Administration sets up approval procedures and may delegate to authorized bodies, notifying the IMO Secretary-General. Non-compliant approved containers are corrected or approval is withdrawn.</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Parties accept each other's approvals. No extra structural tests, except for dangerous goods, bulk liquids and air containers.</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td><b>Control:</b> officers may inspect containers, normally only to check for a valid Safety Approval Plate. Deeper checks only with clear evidence of an obvious safety risk. Defects suspected of existing at approval are reported to the approving Administration.</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Signature until 31 Dec 1973, then ratification, acceptance, approval or accession</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>Entry into force 12 months after the 10th instrument</td></tr><tr><td>IX–X</td><td>Amendment: two-thirds of the MSC and Assembly adopt, two-thirds of Parties accept, in force 12 months later · annexes by simplified MSC procedure: in force unless one-fifth of Parties or five Parties, whichever is less, object</td></tr><tr><td>XI–XII</td><td>Denunciation effective after one year · lapses if fewer than 5 Parties remain for 12 consecutive months</td></tr><tr><td>XIII</td><td>Disputes: arbitration, binding award</td></tr><tr><td>XIV</td><td>No reservations to Arts I–VI, XIII, XIV or the Annexes</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Annex I — Testing, inspection, approval and maintenance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Regulation</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Safety Approval Plate</td><td>Permanently affixed · minimum size <b>200 × 100 mm</b> · \"CSC SAFETY APPROVAL\" lettering at least 8 mm, other text at least 5 mm · the owner removes it if the container is modified so approval no longer applies, is out of service without maintenance, or approval is withdrawn</td></tr><tr><td>2 Maintenance and examination</td><td>Owner keeps the container safe (2(1)) · first examination within <b>5 years</b> of manufacture (2(2)(d)) · optional continuous examination programme, marked <b>ACEP</b>, with examinations at least every <b>30 months</b> (2(3))</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>New containers meet Annex II</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Design-type approval after examination and witnessed prototype testing</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Manufacturer's duties for design-type approval, modifications and internal production control</td></tr><tr><td>6–7</td><td>Administration may inspect production · manufacturer notifies before each new series</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Individual container approval</td></tr><tr><td>9–10</td><td>Existing containers, and new ones not approved at manufacture (original transitional dates in 1984–85)</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Owners report structural changes, and retesting may be required</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"csc-cycle\"><figcaption>Examination cycle</figcaption></figure><h2>Annex II — Structural safety requirements and tests</h2><p>Containers must pass the tests without permanent deformation or defects affecting their use.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Load</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Lifting from corner fittings</td><td><b>2R</b>. Top fittings: vertical lift if over 3,000 mm, 30° sling if 3,000 mm or less. Bottom fittings: sling angles 30°, 37°, 45° and 60° by container length. Hold 5 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>1 Lifting by fork-lift pockets or grappler arms</td><td><b>1.25R</b></td></tr><tr><td>2 Stacking</td><td><b>1.8R</b>. Offsets of 25 mm lateral and 38 mm longitudinal on the corner fittings.</td></tr><tr><td>3 Concentrated loads</td><td>Roof: 300 kg over 600 × 300 mm · floor: two 2,730 kg loads on 142 cm² contact areas, moving test device of 5,460 kg</td></tr><tr><td>4 Transverse racking</td><td>Design forces applied to the top corner fittings</td></tr><tr><td>5 Longitudinal restraint</td><td>Force <b>R</b> on each side, combined <b>2R</b> on the base</td></tr><tr><td>6 End-walls</td><td><b>0.4P</b>, unless a different strength is marked on the plate</td></tr><tr><td>7 Side-walls</td><td><b>0.6P</b>, unless a different strength is marked on the plate</td></tr><tr><td>8 One door removed (added 2012)</td><td>Must be specifically approved; stacking and racking results shown on the plate</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Amendments</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1981</td><td>Transitional plating deadline 1 Jan 1985 for existing containers · date-of-next-examination marking by 1 Jan 1987</td></tr><tr><td>1983</td><td>Re-examination interval extended to <b>30 months</b> · choice of periodic or continuous examination</td></tr><tr><td>1991</td><td>Changes to Annexes I and II, in force 1 Jan 1993</td></tr><tr><td>1993</td><td>A.737(18): <b>never entered into force</b>. Much of its content was carried into MSC.355(92).</td></tr><tr><td>2011, MSC.310(88)</td><td>In force <b>1 Jan 2012</b>: rules on Safety Approval Plates and examination programmes · new test for containers operated with one door removed · new Annex III on control and verification</td></tr><tr><td>2013, MSC.355(92)</td><td>Adopted 21 Jun 2013, in force <b>1 Jul 2014</b> (see below)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>MSC.355(92) in detail</h3><ul><li>Defines standard gravity (9.8 m/s²), load as mass, maximum operating gross mass (R), maximum permissible payload (P) and tare</li><li><b>Plate</b> (Reg 1): must show maximum operating gross mass and allowable stacking load for 1.8 g in kg and lbs, and the transverse racking test force in newtons. Markings on the container match the plate.</li><li>Walls tested to 0.4Pg and 0.6Pg · longitudinal restraint Rg on each side, 2Rg on the base</li><li><b>Annex III, section 4:</b> a table of deficiencies for structurally sensitive components (top and bottom rails, headers, sills, corner posts, corner and intermediate fittings, understructure, locking rods). Each component has a threshold for <b>serious deficiencies</b> requiring immediate out-of-service determination, and a lower threshold requiring owner notification and transport restrictions. Restrictions differ for empty and loaded containers and for sea versus other modes.</li><li><b>Transitional:</b> plate changes at or before the next scheduled examination, or another date the Administration approves, but <b>not later than 1 Jul 2015</b>. Containers completed before 1 Jul 2014 may keep their existing plate if not structurally modified.</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Amendments after MSC.355(92) (IMO's page lists it as the most recent in force)</li><li>Examination detail under harmonized guidance (CSC.1/Circ.138/Rev.1)</li><li>The exact wording of the Annex III control and verification procedure beyond section 4</li><li>Details of the examination marking (next-examination date) on the plate</li><li>Tank containers and dangerous goods container requirements</li><li>Practical points on stacking limits and weighing under SOLAS VI/2 (a separate instrument)</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — 1972 Convention for Safe Containers: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/Containers-Default.aspx</li><li>CSC 1972 text and Annexes: https://likumi.lv/ta/en/starptautiskie-ligumi/id/768-csc-1972-international-convention-for-safe-containers</li><li>Hong Kong Marine Department — MSC.355(92) amendments: https://www.mardep.gov.hk/en/msnote/pdf/msin1407anx1.pdf</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (printed 5 Sep 2023): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status of IMO Treaties.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-cape-town-agreement-2012","v":88469662,"title":"Cape Town Agreement 2012","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["cape-town","fishing-vessels","torremolinos","safety","imo","agreement","convention"],"body":"<h1>Cape Town Agreement 2012 — Agreement on the Implementation of the Provisions of the 1993 Torremolinos Protocol relating to the Torremolinos International Convention for the Safety of Fishing Vessels, 1977</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>11 Oct 2012, Cape Town</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>February 2027 (12 months after the conditions were met)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Fishing vessels of 24 m in length and over (Protocol Art 3(3))</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Fishing Vessel Safety Certificate — up to 5 years (Reg I/13(1))</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>28 States (24 Feb 2026)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Cape Town Agreement</h2><ul><li>Makes the technical rules of the 1993 Torremolinos Protocol work: the 1977 Convention and its 1993 Protocol never entered into force</li><li>Mandatory safety standards for fishing vessels of 24 m and over (IMO says more than 45,000 vessels, FAO data)</li><li>Covers design, construction, equipment, inspection, stability and seaworthiness, machinery and electrical installations, life-saving appliances, fire protection and communications equipment</li><li>IMO calls it the fourth pillar of fishing vessel safety, with STCW-F (training), the ILO Work in Fishing Convention 2007 and the FAO Port State Measures Agreement 2009</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Open for signature at IMO HQ 11 Feb 2013 – 10 Feb 2014, then accession (Art 3)</li><li>Art 4(1): in force 12 months after <b>not less than 22 States</b>, whose fishing vessels of 24 m and over operating on the high seas total <b>not less than 3,600</b>, have consented to be bound</li><li>Conditions met when Argentina deposited its instrument of accession at IMO on 24 Feb 2026: <b>28 States, 3,754 vessels</b> (24 m and over), so in force February 2027</li></ul><h2>How the Agreement is built</h2><ul><li>Art 1(1): Parties give effect to the Agreement and to the 1993 Protocol, except Protocol Art 1(1)(a), 1(2), 1(3), Art 9 and Art 10</li><li>Art 2: Protocol Arts 2–8 and 11–14 apply, read as references to this Agreement</li><li>Annex = integral part of the Agreement (Art 1(3))</li><li>Once in force, States must put it into national law; flag States make sure their vessels comply; port States may inspect foreign vessels in their ports (IMO)</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"capetown-thresholds\"><figcaption>Length and gross tonnage thresholds</figcaption></figure><h2>Where it applies</h2><ul><li>Protocol Art 3(3): the Annex applies to fishing vessels of <b>24 m in length and over</b>, unless a provision says otherwise</li><li>Art 3(4): where a chapter sets a limit above 24 m, the Administration decides which of its regulations apply to vessels of 24 m and over below that limit</li><li>Art 3(2): not applied to vessels used only for sport or recreation, fish processing, research and training, or as fish carriers</li><li>Reg I/1(1): the Annex applies to <b>new vessels</b>, unless it says otherwise</li><li>Reg I/1(2): the Administration may use gross tonnage instead of length:</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Length</th><th>Gross tonnage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>24 m</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>45 m</td><td>950</td></tr><tr><td>60 m</td><td>2,000</td></tr><tr><td>75 m</td><td>3,000</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>New vessel (Reg I/2)</h2><ul><li>A vessel for which, on or after entry into force of the Protocol: (a) the building or major conversion contract is placed; or (b) the contract was placed earlier but delivery is 3 years or more after entry into force; or (c) with no contract, the keel is laid, construction identifiable with the vessel begins, or assembly of at least 50 tonnes or 1% of the estimated structural mass (whichever is less) has started</li><li>Existing vessel = a vessel that is not a new vessel</li><li>Length (L) = 96% of the total waterline length at 85% of the least moulded depth, or the length from the foreside of the stem to the rudder stock axis on that waterline if greater</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex — the ten chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Main application rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>General provisions</td><td>New vessels (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Construction, watertight integrity and equipment</td><td>Length limits in individual regulations (45 m, 75 m)</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Stability and associated seaworthiness</td><td>No general length limit; vessels designed and built to satisfy it</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Machinery and electrical installations and periodically unattended machinery spaces</td><td>45 m and over (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Fire protection, fire detection, fire extinction and fire fighting</td><td>New vessels 45 m and over (Reg 1(1))</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Protection of the crew</td><td>See regulations</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Life-saving appliances and arrangements</td><td>New vessels 45 m and over (Reg 1(1))</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>Emergency procedures, musters and drills</td><td>New and existing vessels 24 m and over (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>IX</td><td>Radiocommunications</td><td>New and existing vessels 45 m and over (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>Shipborne navigational equipment and arrangements</td><td>New and existing vessels; each regulation sets its own limit</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter I — Surveys and certificates</h2><ul><li>Reg titles: 1 Application · 2 Definitions · 3 Exemptions · 6 Inspection and survey · 7 Surveys of life-saving appliances and other equipment · 8 Surveys of radio installations · 9 Surveys of structure, machinery and equipment · 10 Maintenance of conditions after survey · 11 Issue or endorsement of certificates · 12 Issue or endorsement by another Party · 13 Duration and validity · 14 Forms of certificates and records of equipment · 15 Availability · 16 Acceptance · 17 Privileges</li><li>Reg 6: inspections and surveys by Administration officers, nominated surveyors or recognized organizations; they must be able to require repairs and to carry out inspections requested by a port State</li><li>Reg 7: life-saving appliances — initial, renewal (not more than 5 years), periodical, annual (within 3 months before or after each anniversary date) and additional surveys</li><li>Reg 9: structure, machinery and equipment — includes <b>at least two bottom inspections in any five-year period, with not more than 36 months between any two</b></li><li>Reg 10(3): after an accident or a defect, the skipper or owner also reports at once to the port State authorities if in another Party's port</li><li>Reg 6(3): where a vessel is unfit, the port State makes sure it does not sail until it can go to sea without danger</li></ul><p><b>Certificate validity (Reg I/13)</b></p><ul><li>International Fishing Vessel Safety Certificate: period set by the Administration, <b>not more than 5 years</b></li><li>If a new certificate cannot be put on board before expiry, the old one may be endorsed for up to <b>5 months</b></li><li>Extension of up to 3 months only to reach the port where the survey is done</li><li>Certificate stops being valid if surveys are not done in time, it is not endorsed, or the vessel changes flag (Reg 13(8))</li></ul><h2>Chapter III — Stability (Reg III/2 intact stability)</h2><ul><li>GZ curve area: at least 0.055 m-rad up to 30° heel; at least 0.09 m-rad up to 40° (or the flooding angle if lower); at least 0.03 m-rad between 30° and 40°</li><li>GZ at least 200 mm at heel of 30° or more</li><li>Maximum GZ preferably above 30°, and not less than 25°</li><li>Initial GM at least 350 mm for single-deck vessels</li><li>Reg III/7: stability calculated for set operating conditions — departure with full fuel and stores, arrival with 10% stores and a minimum catch</li><li>Reg III/8: icing allowance in ice-prone areas — 30 kg/m² on exposed weather decks</li><li>Reg III/9 and III/10: inclining test on completion and stability information for the skipper</li><li>Reg III/14: subdivision and damage stability for vessels of 100 m and over carrying 100 or more persons</li></ul><h2>Chapter V — Fire protection</h2><ul><li>Part A Reg 1(1): new vessels of 45 m and over · Part B (Regs 3–27): vessels of 60 m and over · Part C (Reg 28 on): vessels of 45 m to under 60 m</li><li>Reg 1(2): accommodation and service spaces use one of three methods — IF (non-combustible divisions), IIF (automatic sprinkler and fire alarm), IIIF (automatic fire alarm and detection; spaces bounded by A or B class divisions limited to 50 m²)</li><li>Regs 17–19: at least two fire pumps and a fire main with hydrants, hoses and nozzles; vessels of 75 m and over need an independently driven emergency fire pump</li><li>Reg 22: machinery spaces — fixed extinguishing systems; new halogenated hydrocarbon systems prohibited</li><li>Regs 24–25: at least two fireman's outfits in widely separated places; fire control plan displayed permanently</li></ul><h2>Chapter VI — Protection of the crew</h2><ul><li>Reg 1: deck openings with coamings or sills under 600 mm need guards; skylights with protective bars not more than 350 mm apart; non-skid decks in working areas</li><li>Reg 2: access hatches at least 600 mm × 600 mm (or 600 mm diameter); escape hatch covers open from both sides</li><li>Reg 3: bulwarks or guard rails at least <b>1 m</b> high; gap under the lowest course not more than 230 mm; other courses not more than 380 mm apart; stanchions not more than 1.5 m apart</li><li>Reg 4: stairways and ladders — adequate size and strength, handrails, non-slip treads</li></ul><h2>Chapter VII — Life-saving appliances (new vessels 45 m and over)</h2><ul><li>Reg 5: at least two survival craft; vessels under 75 m carry craft for everyone on board on each side</li><li>Reg 8: one approved lifejacket for each person on board</li><li>Reg 9: immersion suits — one for each person assigned to the rescue boat crew, and at least three per lifeboat</li><li>Reg 10: lifebuoys — 8 on vessels of 75 m and over, 6 on vessels under 75 m; at least half with self-igniting lights, at least two with smoke signals</li><li>Reg 11: one line-throwing appliance · Reg 12: at least 12 rocket parachute flares</li><li>Reg 13: at least three two-way VHF radiotelephone sets · Reg 14: at least one radar transponder on each side</li><li>Reg 16: weekly and monthly inspections; falls turned end for end at intervals of not more than 30 months and renewed at least every 5 years; inflatable liferafts serviced every 12 months (extendable to 17)</li><li>Lifeboats (Reg 17): capacity not more than 150 persons; full boarding in not more than 3 min; fuel flashpoint above 43 °C</li><li>Liferafts (Regs 20–21): must stay afloat 30 days and survive a drop from 18 m; minimum capacity six persons</li><li>Lifejackets (Reg 24): donned within 1 min; light of at least 0.75 cd for 8 h</li><li>Immersion suits (Reg 25): donned within 2 min</li><li>Rocket parachute flares (Reg 29): reach at least 300 m and burn at least 40 s</li><li>Hand flares (Reg 30): at least 15,000 cd for at least 1 min</li></ul><h2>Chapter VIII — Emergency procedures, musters and drills (24 m and over)</h2><ul><li>Reg 2: muster list; general alarm of <b>seven or more short blasts followed by one long blast</b></li><li>Reg 3(1)(a): abandon ship and fire drills — monthly for each crew member; vessels under 45 m may follow an approved schedule of at least every three months</li><li>Reg 3(1)(f), (g): lifeboat and rescue boat launching — at least once every three months</li><li>Reg 3(3): drills recorded in a log-book</li></ul><h2>Chapter IX — Radiocommunications (45 m and over)</h2><ul><li>Functional requirements (Reg 4), general radio equipment (Reg 6), sea-area equipment (Regs 7–10), watches (Reg 11), reserve power (Reg 12), radio personnel (Reg 15)</li><li>Certified sea areas are recorded on the certificate (Reg IX/2)</li></ul><h2>Chapter X — Navigational equipment</h2><ul><li>Standard magnetic compass: vessels of 24 m and over (Reg 3(1)(a))</li><li>Gyro-compass: vessels of 45 m and over built on or after 1 Sep 1984 (Reg 3(3))</li><li>Radar: 45 m and over (Reg 3(6)) · echo-sounder (Reg 3(9)) · speed and distance device (Reg 3(11))</li><li>Bridge visibility (Reg 6): new vessels of 45 m and over — view of the sea surface ahead not blocked for more than the lesser of two vessel lengths or 500 m</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>From February 2027, fishing vessels of 24 m and over of Party States need the International Fishing Vessel Safety Certificate (up to 5 years), and Party port States may inspect foreign vessels</blockquote><blockquote>Many requirements start at 45 m — fire protection, life-saving appliances, radiocommunications, radar</blockquote><blockquote>Drills: abandon ship and fire drills monthly; bottom inspections at least twice in five years</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO consolidated text of the Agreement: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/Documents/Consolidated%20text%20of%20the%20Agreement.pdf</li><li>Treaty text (Dutch Treaty Series 2014, 49): https://zoek.officielebekendmakingen.nl/trb-2014-49.html</li><li>1993 Torremolinos Protocol (articles, NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1993-Torremolinos-Protocol-Relating-to-1977-Convention-on-the-Safety-of-Fishing-Vessels-1.pdf</li><li>IMO — Cape Town Agreement to enter into force in 2027: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/cape-town-agreement-global-treaty-fishing-vessel-safety-enter-into-force-2027.aspx</li><li>Treaty text (Japan MOFA): https://www.mofa.go.jp/files/100318355.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-fal-1965","v":266446300,"title":"FAL 1965","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["fal","facilitation","port-clearance","single-window","fal-forms","stowaways","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>FAL 1965 — Convention on Facilitation of International Maritime Traffic</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>9 Apr 1965, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>5 Mar 1967 (60 days after 10 States, Art XI)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>16 Articles · an Annex of Standards and Recommended Practices</td></tr><tr><td>Annex</td><td>7 sections and 3 appendices, replaced in full by FAL.14(46)</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic exchange</td><td>Mandatory from 9 Apr 2019 (2016 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Single window</td><td>Mandatory from 1 Jan 2024 (2022 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Not applied to</td><td>Warships and pleasure yachts (Art II(3))</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is FAL</h2><ul><li>Aim: to <b>facilitate and expedite international maritime traffic</b> and prevent unnecessary delays to ships, persons and cargo (Art I)</li><li>Parties work for the highest practicable uniformity of formalities, documents and procedures (Art III)</li><li>Public authorities may require only the declarations listed in the Annex (Standard 2.1)</li><li>Parties cooperate directly or through the IMO (Art IV)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fal-dates\"><figcaption>The Convention and its Annex amendments, with adoption and entry-into-force dates</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The 2002 amendments added the stowaway standards and practices.</li><li><b>2002:</b> the Dangerous Goods Manifest (FAL Form 7) became the basic document for dangerous goods information.</li><li><b>2005:</b> electronic transmission was encouraged, and submitting all information at a single point was recommended.</li><li><b>2009:</b> new IMO FAL forms were approved.</li><li><b>2016:</b> all IMO FAL forms were revised except the Ship's Stores Declaration (Form 3). Electronic exchange became mandatory from 9 Apr 2019, with a transition period of at least 12 months, and the \"Single Window\" wording was introduced.</li><li><b>2022 (FAL.14(46), 13 May 2022):</b> the complete Annex was replaced, and single window systems became mandatory.</li><li><b>2023 (17 Mar 2023):</b> an amendment to Recommended Practice 7.11 on combating illicit activities. The IMO page gives its expected entry into force as 1 Jan 2025.</li></ul><h2>The Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>General undertaking: facilitate and expedite international maritime traffic</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Co-operation and scope. Measures should be, as far as practicable, no less favourable than for other international transport; apply equally to ships of coastal and non-coastal Parties; <b>warships and pleasure yachts excluded</b></td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Uniformity of formalities, documents and procedures</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Co-operation directly or through the IMO</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Saving clauses: wider facilities allowed; temporary measures for public morality, order, security or health; matters not covered stay under national law</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Definitions: <b>Standards</b> are measures whose uniform application is necessary and practicable; <b>Recommended Practices</b> are measures whose application is desirable</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Amending the Annex</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>Differences from Standards (notification)</td></tr><tr><td>IX</td><td>Revision of the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>Signature, acceptance, accession</td></tr><tr><td>XI</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr><tr><td>XII</td><td>Denunciation</td></tr><tr><td>XIII</td><td>Territorial extension</td></tr><tr><td>XIV</td><td>Notifications</td></tr><tr><td>XV</td><td>Deposit and registration</td></tr><tr><td>XVI</td><td>Languages and date</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Differences (Art VIII and IMO page):</b></p><ul><li>A Government that cannot comply with a Standard, or needs different rules, must notify the Secretary-General of the differences.</li><li>For a Recommended Practice, notification is required once the Government fully complies.</li></ul><p><b>Amending the Annex (Art VII):</b></p><ul><li>By proposal to the Secretary-General: in force 6 months after a majority of the Parties accept it.</li><li>By a conference called at the request of one third of the Parties: needs a two-thirds majority of those present and voting.</li><li>Art IX allows the Convention itself to be revised by a conference.</li></ul><p><b>Entry into force and denunciation (Arts XI–XII):</b></p><ul><li>In force 60 days after at least 10 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments.</li><li>Denunciation is possible after 3 years in force for a Party, and takes effect 1 year after notice.</li></ul><h2>The Annex</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fal-annex-map\"><figcaption>Sections and appendices of the Annex, as replaced by FAL.14(46)</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Definitions and general provisions (including electronic exchange of information)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Arrival, stay and departure of the ship</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Arrival and departure of persons</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Stowaways</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Arrival, stay and departure of cargo and other articles</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Public health and quarantine, including sanitary measures for animals and plants</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Miscellaneous provisions: A Bonds and other forms of security · B Services at ports · C Emergency assistance · D National facilitation committees</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Appendix</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Maximum information public authorities may require for the declarations (Standard 2.1bis)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Declarations that may be required in paper form in exceptional circumstances (Standard 2.11), with the paper forms</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Stowaway details (Recommended Practice 4.6.2)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Electronic exchange and single window (Section 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1.3bis</td><td>Public authorities shall establish, maintain and use systems for the electronic exchange of information</td></tr><tr><td>1.3ter</td><td>A transition period of no less than 12 months, counted from when all functional and technical specifications are published</td></tr><tr><td>1.3quart</td><td>Alternative means for clearance information during the transition; existing message formats stay accepted</td></tr><tr><td>1.3quin</td><td>Introduce arrangements so all the information required under Standard 2.1 can be given to a <b>single window</b> by electronic means</td></tr><tr><td>1.3sext</td><td>Data for arrival, stay and departure is submitted once and reused as far as possible</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Single window:</b> an environment that allows standardized, harmonized information to be submitted to a single entry point, typically electronic.</li><li>The IMO says all Member States must use a Maritime Single Window since 1 Jan 2024. It also reports that authorities still required paper or mixed submissions in 64% of port calls in one survey, so adoption is uneven.</li></ul><h2>Declarations authorities may require (Standard 2.1)</h2><p>Public authorities shall <b>not require more than</b> these:</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Declaration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a</td><td>General Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>b</td><td>Cargo Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>c</td><td>Ship's Stores Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>d</td><td>Crew's Effects Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>e</td><td>Crew List</td></tr><tr><td>f</td><td>Passenger List</td></tr><tr><td>g</td><td>Dangerous Goods Manifest</td></tr><tr><td>h</td><td>Special declaration for postal items (Universal Postal Union Acts)</td></tr><tr><td>i</td><td>Maritime Declaration of Health (International Health Regulations)</td></tr><tr><td>j</td><td>Ship Sanitation Control Exemption Certificate, Ship Sanitation Control Certificate or extension (IHR)</td></tr><tr><td>k</td><td>Security-related information under SOLAS regulation XI-2/9.2.2</td></tr><tr><td>l</td><td>Advance electronic cargo information for customs risk assessment (WCO SAFE Framework of Standards)</td></tr><tr><td>m</td><td>Advance Notification Form for Waste Delivery to Port Reception Facilities</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Standard IMO FAL forms (Appendix 2):</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Document</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FAL 1</td><td>General Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 2</td><td>Cargo Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 3</td><td>Ship's Stores Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 4</td><td>Crew's Effects Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 5</td><td>Crew List</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 6</td><td>Passenger List</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 7</td><td>Dangerous Goods Manifest</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Ports may ask only for the declarations in Standard 2.1, and since 1 Jan 2024 must take them electronically through a single window. Keep the crew list, stores and cargo data accurate, because the same data is reused. For stowaways, follow Section 4 and Appendix 3.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>FAL Convention text, 1965 (NUS Centre for International Law): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1965-Convention-on-Facilitation-of-International-Maritime-Traffic-1.pdf</li><li>Resolution FAL.14(46), Annex as replaced (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/FALDocuments/FAL.14(46).pdf</li><li>IMO — FAL Convention: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Convention-on-Facilitation-of-International-Maritime-Traffic-(FAL).aspx</li><li>IMO — Maritime Single Window: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Facilitation/Pages/MaritimeSingleWindow-default.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-hns-2010","v":169596083,"title":"HNS 2010","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["hns","liability","compensation","chemicals","lng","lpg","insurance","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>HNS 2010 — International Convention on Liability and Compensation for Damage in Connection with the Carriage of Hazardous and Noxious Substances by Sea, 1996, as amended by the 2010 Protocol</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>3 May 1996 · 2010 Protocol 30 Apr 2010</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>29 Nov 2027</b></td></tr><tr><td>Tier 1</td><td>Shipowner — bulk max 100 million SDR · packaged max 115 million SDR</td></tr><tr><td>Tier 2</td><td>HNS Fund — total max 250 million SDR</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is HNS 2010</h2><ul><li>A two-tier compensation system for damage caused by hazardous and noxious substances carried by sea as cargo</li><li>Tier 1: the shipowner, with strict liability and compulsory insurance. Tier 2: the HNS Fund, paid for by receivers of HNS cargo. The 250 million SDR total includes what the shipowner pays (Art 14(5)(a))</li><li>Once in force, the 1996 Convention as amended by the 2010 Protocol is called the \"2010 HNS Convention\"</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hns-tiers\"><figcaption>Two tiers of HNS compensation (million SDR)</figcaption></figure><h2>History</h2><ul><li>The 1996 Convention never entered into force, because practical problems stopped States from ratifying. The 2010 Protocol solved them</li><li>Entry-into-force conditions met on 29 May 2026: at least 12 States, including four each with at least 2 million GT of ships, and more than 40 million tonnes of contributing cargo. Entry into force follows 18 months later</li><li>West P&amp;I (July 2026) lists 13 States that had deposited instruments: Belgium, Canada, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Netherlands, Norway, Slovakia, South Africa, Sweden, Türkiye</li></ul><h2>Where it applies (Art 3)</h2><ul><li>Damage in the territory or territorial sea of a State Party</li><li>Damage in the exclusive economic zone (or equivalent area) of a State Party — environmental damage</li><li>Damage other than environmental damage, outside any State's territory, caused by HNS on a ship registered in a State Party</li><li>Preventive measures, wherever taken</li></ul><h2>Not covered (Art 4, Art 5)</h2><ul><li>Pollution damage from oil as defined in CLC 1969 (oil pollution is under CLC/Fund)</li><li>Damage caused by radioactive material (class 7)</li><li>Warships and State ships used only on government non-commercial service (unless a State opts in)</li><li>Damage covered by the Bunkers Convention</li><li>Plastic pellets (nurdles) are excluded (West P&amp;I)</li><li>Option (Art 5(1)(a)): a State may declare the Convention not applicable to ships of <b>200 GT or less</b> carrying only packaged HNS on domestic voyages, if other conditions are met. West P&amp;I says only Canada and Sweden have used this.</li></ul><hr><h2>What counts as HNS (Art 1(5))</h2><ul><li>Defined by reference to lists in other IMO conventions and codes:<ul><li>Oils (MARPOL Annex I)</li><li>Noxious liquid substances (MARPOL Annex II)</li><li>Dangerous liquids (IBC Code)</li><li>Liquefied gases</li><li>Liquid substances with flashpoint not exceeding 60 °C</li><li>Dangerous, hazardous and harmful materials in packaged form (IMDG Code)</li><li>Solid bulk materials with chemical hazards (IMSBC Code)</li><li>Residues from the previous carriage of these (other than packaged residues)</li></ul></li><li>The HNS Finder tool on hnsconvention.org helps to check a substance</li></ul><h2>Damage covered (Art 1(6))</h2><ul><li>Loss of life or personal injury, on board or outside the ship</li><li>Loss of or damage to property outside the ship</li><li>Loss or damage by contamination of the environment (limited to reasonable reinstatement measures; loss of profit from impairment is covered)</li><li>Costs of preventive measures, and further loss or damage caused by them</li><li>The Convention covers fire and explosion risks as well as pollution</li></ul><h2>Tier 1 — Shipowner (Art 7, 9, 12)</h2><ul><li>Owner at the time of the incident is strictly liable (Art 7(1))</li><li><b>Limits (Art 9(1)) — tonnage means gross tonnage:</b></li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Ship size</th><th>Bulk HNS (SDR)</th><th>Packaged HNS (SDR)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up to 2,000 GT</td><td>10 million</td><td>11.5 million</td></tr><tr><td>2,001–50,000 GT</td><td>+ 1,500 per GT</td><td>+ 1,725 per GT</td></tr><tr><td>Above 50,000 GT</td><td>+ 360 per GT</td><td>+ 414 per GT</td></tr><tr><td><b>Maximum</b></td><td><b>100 million</b></td><td><b>115 million</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The packaged-HNS limits also apply where damage involves both bulk and packaged HNS, or the source cannot be determined</li><li>Owner loses the right to limit only for a personal act or omission done with intent, or recklessly and with knowledge that damage would probably result (Art 9(2))</li></ul><h2>Owner is not liable if the damage (Art 7(2))</h2><ul><li>Resulted from war, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, or an exceptional, inevitable and irresistible natural phenomenon</li><li>Was wholly caused by an intentional act or omission of a third party</li><li>Was wholly caused by negligence of a government authority responsible for lights or other navigational aids</li><li>Was caused by failure of the shipper or another person to give information on the hazardous nature of the HNS, if the owner neither knew nor ought reasonably to have known</li><li>Liability can be reduced or removed if the claimant's own intent or negligence contributed (Art 7(3))</li></ul><h2>Insurance and certificate (Art 12)</h2><ul><li>Owner of a ship registered in a State Party and actually carrying HNS must keep insurance or other financial security</li><li>Ship must carry a compulsory insurance <b>certificate</b> in the Annex I model</li><li>No general minimum tonnage (apart from the 200 GT option above), so small ships may need a certificate too</li><li>The flag State issues it; a State Party may also certify a ship registered in a non-Party State once insurance is checked</li><li>Claims can be made <b>directly against the insurer</b> (Art 12(8)); evidence is usually a P&amp;I \"Blue Card\"</li></ul><h2>Tier 2 — HNS Fund (Art 13–21)</h2><ul><li>Established to pay when Tier 1 is inadequate or not available (Art 13(1))</li><li>Total limit with Tier 1: <b>250 million SDR</b> (Art 14(5))</li><li>Run by an Assembly of States Parties, a claims Committee and a Secretariat headed by a Director (hnsconvention.org)</li><li><b>Accounts:</b> a general account (with sectors) plus separate accounts for <b>oil, LNG and LPG</b> (Art 16) — so one cargo type does not subsidise another</li><li><b>Who pays:</b> receivers of contributing cargo in Party States, by quantity received in the previous calendar year; the owner does not pay</li><li><b>Thresholds:</b><ul><li>General account: receivers of more than 20,000 tonnes of contributing cargo in a year (Art 18(1))</li><li>Oil account: more than 150,000 tonnes of contributing oil, or more than 20,000 tonnes of other bulk oils (Art 19(1)(a))</li><li>LPG account: more than 20,000 tonnes (Art 19(1)(b))</li><li>LNG account: receivers of any quantity — contribution is by the titleholder rules of Art 19(1bis)</li></ul></li><li>Separate accounts start only after contributing cargo reaches: oil 350 million tonnes, LNG 20 million tonnes, LPG 15 million tonnes (Art 19(3))</li><li>Each State Party must report to the Director the name and address of each person liable to contribute, and cargo data (Art 21)</li></ul><h2>Time limits (Art 37)</h2><ul><li>Claim must be brought within <b>3 years</b> from when the claimant knew (or should reasonably have known) of the damage and the owner's identity</li><li>And never later than <b>10 years</b> after the incident</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>From 29 Nov 2027 ships carrying HNS to or from Party States need an HNS certificate, as with CLC and Bunkers</blockquote><blockquote>Shipper must declare HNS properly — non-disclosure can free the owner from liability</blockquote><blockquote>Oil pollution from tankers stays under CLC/Fund; bunker oil under Bunkers 2001</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 22 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>2010 HNS Convention consolidated text: https://www.hnsconvention.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/08/2010-HNS-Convention-Consolidated-text_e.pdf</li><li>IMO — HNS Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-liability-and-compensation-for-damage-in-connection-with-the-carriage-of-hazardous-and-noxious-.aspx</li><li>HNS Convention — The Convention: https://www.hnsconvention.org/the-convention/</li><li>West P&amp;I — The 2010 HNS Convention: key provisions (July 2026): https://www.westpandi.com/News-and-Resources/News/July-2026/The-2010-HNS-Convention-Key-Provisions-and-What-Sh/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-hong-kong-convention-2009","v":235625126,"title":"Hong Kong Convention 2009","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["hkc","ship-recycling","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Hong Kong Convention 2009 — International Convention for the Safe and Environmentally Sound Recycling of Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>15 May 2009 (Hong Kong)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>26 Jun 2025</td></tr><tr><td>Main documents</td><td>Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM) · Ship Recycling Plan · Ready for Recycling Certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Yard</td><td>Authorized Ship Recycling Facility</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the HKC</h2><ul><li>IMO convention for safe, environmentally sound ship recycling: covers the ship (design, operation, preparation for recycling) and the recycling yard</li><li>Ship: Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM) all its life · surveys and certificates</li><li>Yard: Ship Recycling Facility (SRF) authorized by its State · Ship Recycling Facility Plan (SRFP) · ship-specific Ship Recycling Plan (SRP)</li><li>Convention (21 Articles) + Annex (Regulations, Ch 1–4) + 7 Appendices + IMO guidelines</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hkc-flow\"><figcaption>Steps for a ship: IHM Part I in service (initial and renewal surveys), IHM Parts II and III before recycling, Ship Recycling Plan from the authorized yard, final survey, then the Ready for Recycling Certificate (Regs 5, 9, 10, 11, 16). Drawn from the Convention text.</figcaption></figure><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Entry-into-force conditions: 15 States · 40% of world tonnage · recycling capacity ≥3% of their tonnage</li><li>Bangladesh and Liberia ratified 26 Jun 2023 → conditions met</li><li>In force 24 months after the conditions were met · IMO lists 24 Parties covering about 57% of world tonnage</li><li>Annex Reg 1: existing ship = a ship that is not a new ship. New ship = building contract on/after entry into force (26 Jun 2025), or (no contract) keel laid or similar stage on/after 6 months after (26 Dec 2025), or delivery on/after 30 months after (26 Dec 2027)</li><li>Entry-into-force conditions at June 2023: about 45.9% of world tonnage and 3.31% recycling capacity</li><li>Cybutryne: Appendix 1 follows AFS Convention Annex 1 as in force (AFS amended by MEPC.331(76)) · the 2023 IHM Guidelines MEPC.379(80) (adopted MEPC 80, Jul 2023) add cybutryne to the Appendix 1 list for the IHM, with limits of 1,000 mg/kg dry paint (hull sample) or 200 mg/kg (wet paint sample)</li><li>Older IHM guidelines: MEPC.179(59), MEPC.197(62), MEPC.269(68) → replaced by MEPC.379(80)</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>Ships entitled to fly a Party's flag or operating under its authority (Art 3)</li><li>Ship Recycling Facilities under a Party's jurisdiction</li><li>Survey and certificate requirements: ships ≥500 GT on international voyages</li><li>Not covered: warships, naval auxiliaries, State ships on non-commercial service, ships &lt;500 GT, ships operating throughout their life only in waters of the State whose flag they fly (Party should still act consistently, as far as reasonable and practicable) — Art 3</li></ul><h2>Articles (21)</h2><ul><li>Art 1 General obligations · Art 2 Definitions · Art 3 Application</li><li>Art 4 Controls related to ship recycling · Art 5 Survey and certification of ships · Art 6 Authorization of Ship Recycling Facilities</li><li>Art 7 Exchange of information · Art 8 Inspection of ships · Art 9 Detection of violations · Art 10 Violations · Art 11 Undue delay or detention of ships</li><li>Art 12 Communication of information · Art 13 Technical assistance and co-operation · Art 14 Dispute settlement</li><li>Art 15 Relationship with international law and other agreements · Art 16 Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval, accession · Art 17 Entry into force</li><li>Art 18 Amendments · Art 19 Denunciation · Art 20 Depositary · Art 21 Languages</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex Ch 1 — General</h2><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions (para 3 existing ship, para 4 new ship) · Reg 2 General applicability · Reg 3 Relationship with other standards, recommendations and guidance (take into account relevant ILO and Basel Convention standards)</li></ul><h2>Annex Ch 2 — Requirements for Ships</h2><p>*Part A — Design, construction, operation, maintenance*</p><ul><li>Reg 4 Controls of hazardous materials (Appendix 1 — prohibited or restricted):<ul><li>Asbestos: banned in all new installations</li><li>Ozone-depleting substances (Montreal Protocol list, e.g. halons, CFCs): new installations banned on all ships; exception: HCFCs were allowed until 1 Jan 2020</li><li>PCBs: new installations banned</li><li>Anti-fouling systems: organotin (TBT) not used; anything the AFS Convention prohibits (incl. cybutryne) not used</li><li>Each Party applies these controls to its ships and also in its ports, shipyards, repair yards and offshore terminals</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5 Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM):<ul><li>Part I: materials in the ship's structure and equipment (Appendix 1 and 2) — new ships from delivery</li><li>Existing ships: comply as far as practicable · Part I within 5 years of entry into force or before going for recycling, whichever is earlier (by 26 Jun 2030) · at least identify Appendix 1 materials · include a plan describing the visual/sampling checks used</li><li>Part I kept up to date during the ship's life (new equipment, repairs)</li><li>Part II operationally generated wastes · Part III stores — added and verified only before recycling</li></ul></li><li>Reg 6 Procedure for proposing amendments to Appendices 1 and 2 · Reg 7 Technical groups</li></ul><p>*Part B — Preparation for ship recycling*</p><ul><li>Reg 8 General requirements</li><li>Reg 9 Ship Recycling Plan (SRP): the owner gives the authorized yard the IHM; the yard prepares a ship-specific SRP before recycling<ul><li>Approved by the yard's competent authority, explicitly or tacitly (tacit: receipt acknowledged in 3 working days, 14-day review, no written objection = approved)</li></ul></li></ul><p>*Part C — Surveys and certification*</p><ul><li>Reg 10 Surveys:<ul><li>Initial survey (before the ship enters service or before the IHM certificate is first issued): checks IHM Part I</li><li>Renewal survey: interval set by the Administration, ≤5 years</li><li>Additional survey: at the owner's request, after a change, replacement or significant repair affecting the IHM</li><li>Final survey: before the ship leaves service and before recycling starts — checks IHM (Parts I–III), that the SRP reflects the IHM and includes safe-for-entry and safe-for-hot-work arrangements, and that the yard authorization is valid</li><li>Surveys by Administration officers, or delegated to nominated surveyors or ROs (Administration stays responsible)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 11 Issue and endorsement of certificates:<ul><li>International Certificate on Inventory of Hazardous Materials: after initial or renewal survey · validity ≤5 years</li><li>Renewal survey within 3 months before expiry → new certificate runs 5 years from the old expiry date; more than 3 months before → 5 years from the survey date</li><li>Extensions: up to 5 months (endorsement, if survey done but certificate not issued) · up to 3 months to complete a voyage · 1 month grace for short-voyage ships</li><li>International Ready for Recycling Certificate (IRRC): after final survey, taking the yard's authorization into account · validity ≤3 months</li></ul></li><li>Reg 12 Issuance or endorsement of a certificate by another Party (mutual acceptance among Parties) · Reg 13 Form of the certificates · Reg 14 Duration and validity</li></ul><h2>Annex Ch 3 — Requirements for Ship Recycling Facilities</h2><ul><li>Reg 15 Controls on ship recycling facilities</li><li>Reg 16 Authorization: Document of Authorization to conduct Ship Recycling (DASR) · ≤5 years</li><li>Reg 17 General requirements</li><li>Reg 18 Ship Recycling Facility Plan (SRFP) · Reg 19 Prevention of adverse effects to health and environment</li><li>Reg 20 Safe and environmentally sound management of hazardous materials</li><li>Reg 21 Emergency preparedness · Reg 22 Worker safety and training</li><li>Reg 23 Reporting on incidents, accidents, occupational diseases and chronic effects</li></ul><h2>Annex Ch 4 — Reporting Requirements</h2><ul><li>Reg 24 Initial notification and reporting · Reg 25 Reporting upon completion</li></ul><h2>Appendices</h2><ul><li>Appendix 1 — Controls (prohibited/restricted): asbestos, ozone-depleting substances (HCFC exception to 1 Jan 2020), PCBs, anti-fouling compounds (organotin, cybutryne)</li><li>Appendix 2 — Minimum list for the IHM: Appendix 1 items + cadmium, hexavalent chromium, lead, mercury (and their compounds), PBBs, PBDEs, polychlorinated naphthalenes (more than 3 chlorine atoms), radioactive substances, certain short-chain chlorinated paraffins (C10–C13)</li><li>Appendix 3 — form of the International Certificate on Inventory of Hazardous Materials</li></ul><h2>IMO guidelines (MEPC)</h2><ul><li>MEPC.379(80) — 2023 Guidelines for the development of the IHM</li><li>MEPC.196(62) Ship Recycling Plan · MEPC.210(63) safe recycling (yards) · MEPC.211(63) authorization of SRFs</li><li>MEPC.222(64) Survey and certification · MEPC.223(64) Inspection of ships (PSC)</li></ul><h2>EU Ship Recycling Regulation (EU 1257/2013) — on top of HKC</h2><ul><li>Large commercial seagoing EU-flag ships: recycle only at yards on the European List (from 2019)</li><li>IHM required: EU ships and non-EU ships calling at EU ports or anchorages (since 31 Dec 2020); non-EU-flag ships need a certificate of compliance</li><li>EU Annex I prohibits PFOS as well · EU IHM list (Annex II) includes HBCDD, which HKC does not require</li><li>HKC certificate does not automatically replace EU SRR compliance (EU SRR is based on HKC; check with flag and class)</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Onboard: keep IHM Part I updated with every new or changed equipment · ask for Material Declarations (MD) and Supplier Declarations of Conformity (SDoC) from suppliers</blockquote><blockquote>Before sale for recycling: final survey + IRRC (3 months) + yard with DASR</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Recycling of ships and HKC (guidelines list): https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Pages/Recycling-of-ships-and-Hong-Kong-Convention.aspx</li><li>EMSA — ship recycling (EU SRR): https://www.emsa.europa.eu/sustainable-shipping/ship-recycling.html</li><li>ABS — HKC and IHM Part I (MEPC.379(80), cybutryne): https://ww2.eagle.org/content/dam/eagle/regulatory-news/2023/ABS%20Regulatory%20News%20-%20Ship%20Recycling.pdf</li><li>Treaty text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/2009-HK-Convention-For-Safe-and-Environmentally-Sound-Ship-Recycling.pdf</li><li>IMO — HKC entry into force: https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/PressBriefings/Pages/Hong-Kong-Convention-entry-into-force.aspx</li><li>Lloyd's Register — IMO Hong Kong Ship Recycling Convention: https://www.lr.org/en/services/statutory-compliance/imo-hong-kong-ship-recycling-convention/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-inmarsat-1976","v":43288207,"title":"INMARSAT 1976","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["inmarsat","imso","satellite","gmdss","communications","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>INMARSAT 1976 — Convention on the International Maritime Satellite Organization (and Operating Agreement)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Convention</td><td>Adopted 3 Sep 1976, London · in force <b>16 Jul 1979</b></td></tr><tr><td>Operating Agreement</td><td>Same dates as the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>Depositary</td><td>IMO</td></tr><tr><td>Parties (IMO table, printed 5 Sep 2023)</td><td>89 States for the Operating Agreement, 94.85% of world tonnage</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Improve maritime communications: distress and safety of life, ship efficiency and management, maritime public correspondence, radiodetermination</td></tr><tr><td>Today</td><td><b>IMSO</b> (International Mobile Satellite Organization, intergovernmental, London) oversees public safety services. <b>Inmarsat</b> is a commercial company.</td></tr><tr><td>Link to SOLAS</td><td>1988 SOLAS amendments introduced the GMDSS</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"inmarsat-split\"><figcaption>From one organization to IMSO and Inmarsat Ltd</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Since the 1998 amendments, the Convention's safety role sits with IMSO. It oversees the performance and availability of satellite services recognized for GMDSS. It does not run the satellites.</blockquote><h2>What is INMARSAT 1976</h2><ul><li>Set up an international organization to provide space segment for maritime communications, after IMO decided in 1973 to create a satellite-based maritime communications system</li><li>Operations began in 1982, from London</li><li>The Convention is the intergovernmental treaty (<b>Parties</b> are States). The <b>Operating Agreement</b> is signed by <b>Signatories</b>, which are the Parties or their designated entities. A State becomes a Party only after it, or its designated entity, has signed the Operating Agreement (Art 32(4)).</li><li>The preamble cites UN Resolution 1721 (XVI), under which satellite communication should be available to all nations on a global and non-discriminatory basis, and the 1967 Outer Space Treaty</li></ul><h2>Convention (1976 text)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Definitions</td><td>Operating Agreement (1(a)) · Party (1(b)) · Signatory (1(c)) · space segment (1(d)) · INMARSAT space segment (1(e)) · Ship (1(f)), broad: includes hydrofoils, submersibles and floating platforms</td></tr><tr><td>2 Establishment</td><td>Establishes INMARSAT as \"the Organization\". Each Party signs the Operating Agreement or designates a competent entity to sign (2(3)).</td></tr><tr><td>3 Purposes</td><td>Improve maritime communications, distress and safety of life, ship and aircraft efficiency (3(1)) · serve all areas needing maritime and aeronautical communications (3(2)) · act <b>exclusively for peaceful purposes</b> (3(3))</td></tr><tr><td>4 Designated entities</td><td>Relations between a Party and its entity are governed by domestic law. The Party is not liable for Operating Agreement obligations (4(c)).</td></tr><tr><td>5 Financing</td><td>Signatories fund the Organization in proportion to their investment shares (5(1)) · operate on a sound economic and financial basis (5(3))</td></tr><tr><td>7 Access</td><td>Space segment open to ships and aircraft of all nations, with <b>no discrimination by nationality</b> (7(1))</td></tr><tr><td>10–12 Assembly</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>13–15 Council</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>16 Directorate</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Legal personality</td></tr><tr><td>29 Withdrawal</td><td>Written notice to the Depositary, effective 3 months after receipt. Withdrawing Signatories remain liable for previously authorized commitments.</td></tr><tr><td>30 Suspension and termination</td><td>The Assembly may terminate a Party's membership after at least one year's notice of non-compliance. The Council may suspend a Signatory's rights for unremedied non-compliance, and the Assembly may then terminate after 3 more months.</td></tr><tr><td>31 Disputes</td><td>Negotiation first. If unresolved within one year, arbitration by consent.</td></tr><tr><td>33 Entry into force</td><td>60 days after States representing <b>95% of initial investment shares</b> become Parties. Void if not in force within 36 months of opening for signature.</td></tr><tr><td>34 Amendments</td><td>A Party proposes, the Council reviews, the Assembly considers no earlier than 6 months later. In force <b>120 days</b> after two-thirds of Parties at adoption, representing at least two-thirds of total investment shares, accept. Then binding on all.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Organs</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"inmarsat-organs\"><figcaption>The three organs of the 1976 organization</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Composition and rules</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Assembly</b> (Arts 10–12)</td><td>All Parties. Regular sessions every <b>2 years</b>. One vote per Party. Substantive decisions two-thirds, procedural decisions a simple majority, of Parties present and voting. Quorum a majority of Parties (11(4)).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Council</b> (Arts 13–15)</td><td><b>22 representatives</b>: 18 from the largest investment shares and 4 elected by the Assembly for geographic representation (13(1)). Meets at least <b>3 times a year</b> (14(1)). No representative may cast more than <b>25%</b> of total voting participation for one Signatory, subject to exceptions (14(3)).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Directorate</b> (Art 16)</td><td>The Council appoints the Director General, subject to Party confirmation. Confirmation stands unless more than one-third of Parties object within 60 days. Term <b>6 years</b>.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Operating Agreement</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>II</td><td>Signatories have the rights and obligations under both the Convention and the Agreement</td></tr><tr><td>III Capital contributions</td><td>In proportion to investment share · capital requirements cover space segment costs (design, development, acquisition, construction, establishment, and leased contractual rights), operating funds and Art XI payments</td></tr><tr><td>IV Capital ceiling</td><td>Initial ceiling <b>US$ 200 million</b>, adjustable by the Council</td></tr><tr><td>V Investment shares</td><td>Based on each Signatory's share of total space segment utilization · utilization split evenly between the ship/aircraft part and the land part · a Signatory with a ship-to-land ratio above 20:1 may apply for utilization equal to twice the land part or a 0.1% share, whichever is higher · first determination 2 to 3 years after operational use begins · redetermined yearly, and when a Signatory joins or leaves · no share below <b>0.05%</b> · in one redetermination, a share may not rise more than 50% of its initial share or fall more than 50% of its current share</td></tr><tr><td>VI Financial adjustments</td><td>At each redetermination, through the Organization, using a valuation of assets net of amortization and liabilities</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Utilization charges · sanctions for payments more than 4 months overdue</td></tr><tr><td>VIII Return on capital</td><td>Revenues cover operating costs first, then operating funds, capital repayment, withdrawal settlements, and finally compensation for use of capital at a rate \"as close as possible to the cost of money in the world markets\"</td></tr><tr><td>XI</td><td>Liability for claims shared by investment share</td></tr><tr><td>XIII Withdrawal</td><td>Within 3 months of the effective date, the Council notifies the financial settlement: investment share × valuation, less amounts owed</td></tr><tr><td>XIV–XV</td><td>Earth station approval and utilization authorization by Council criteria</td></tr><tr><td>XVI Disputes</td><td>Negotiation, then arbitration if unresolved within one year</td></tr><tr><td>XVIII Amendments</td><td>Council approval, Assembly confirmation, two-thirds of Signatories holding at least two-thirds of shares, effect 120 days later</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Amendments and history</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"inmarsat-timeline\"><figcaption>Key dates</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1973</td><td>IMO decides to create a satellite-based maritime communications system</td></tr><tr><td>3 Sep 1976</td><td>Convention and Operating Agreement adopted · in force 16 Jul 1979</td></tr><tr><td>16 Oct 1985</td><td>Amendment extending services to <b>aircraft</b>, in force 13 Oct 1989</td></tr><tr><td>19 Jan 1989</td><td>Amendment extending services to <b>land vehicles</b>, in force 26 Jun 1997</td></tr><tr><td>1988</td><td>SOLAS amendments introduce the GMDSS (see At a glance)</td></tr><tr><td>9 Dec 1994</td><td>Amendment renaming the organization <b>International Mobile Satellite Organization</b> and changing Council composition (listed as not yet in force in the treaty summary checked)</td></tr><tr><td>24 Apr 1998</td><td>Amendments for <b>privatization from April 1999</b>: a commercial company and an intergovernmental body (IMSO) with public service obligations, including GMDSS. In force <b>31 Jul 2001</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>End 2003</td><td>Inmarsat Ltd fully privatised (IMO page)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>IMSO today</h2><ul><li>Intergovernmental body, headquarters London, separate from the commercial company Inmarsat (IMSO site: 112 Member States)</li><li>Oversees the <b>performance and availability</b> of the mobile satellite systems recognized by IMO for GMDSS. IMO has recognized <b>Inmarsat and Iridium</b>, with recognition given by the MSC under resolution A.1001(25).</li><li>Manages public services agreements with the providers</li><li>IMO appointed IMSO as <b>Co-ordinator of the Long-Range Identification and Tracking (LRIT)</b> system. It audits and reviews the system annually.</li><li>Observer at IMO meetings</li><li>IMO is currently reviewing the GMDSS to modernize it</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Wording of the 1998 amended Convention articles (public service obligations, fair competition) and Advisory Committee rules</li><li>The annex fixing initial investment shares</li><li>Exact current Member State count (IMSO site says 112, IMO page says more than 100)</li><li>The date IMSO was appointed LRIT Co-ordinator (sources give 2006 and 2008)</li><li>Technical details of the satellite systems, Inmarsat-C fitting requirements and sea areas (these belong to SOLAS chapter IV)</li><li>Whether the 1994 amendments are now in force</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Convention on the International Maritime Satellite Organization: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Convention-on-the-International-Maritime-Satellite-Organization.aspx</li><li>INMARSAT Convention text (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-06/maritime-satellite-organization.html</li><li>Operating Agreement text: https://www.jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-06/international-maritime-satellite.xml</li><li>Convention as amended (ITC Legacarta summary): https://legacarta.intracen.org/instrument/522258-convention-international-mobile-satellite-organization-inmarsat-as-amended</li><li>IMSO — About: https://imso.org/about/</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (printed 5 Sep 2023): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status of IMO Treaties.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-intervention-1969","v":259977239,"title":"Intervention 1969","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["intervention","high-seas","coastal-state","oil-pollution","casualty","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Intervention 1969 — International Convention Relating to Intervention on the High Seas in Cases of Oil Pollution Casualties</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>29 Nov 1969, Brussels</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>6 May 1975</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>90 (as of 10 Jul 2024, NUS CIL)</td></tr><tr><td>1973 Protocol</td><td>Extends it to substances other than oil · in force 1983</td></tr><tr><td>Core rule</td><td>Coastal State may act on the high seas against grave and imminent danger from oil pollution after a maritime casualty</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Intervention Convention</h2><ul><li>Lets a coastal State take measures on the high seas after a maritime casualty, to prevent, mitigate or eliminate grave and imminent danger to its coastline or related interests from oil pollution or the threat of it (Art I(1))</li><li>Measures must be limited to what is necessary, after consulting the flag State, owners and, where feasible, independent experts (IMO)</li><li>A State that goes beyond what is permitted pays compensation (Art VI)</li><li>Covers seagoing vessels; warships and State ships on non-commercial government service are excluded (Art I(2))</li></ul><h2>Key definitions (Art II)</h2><ul><li><b>Maritime casualty</b>: collision, stranding, other incidents of navigation, and other occurrences causing material damage or the imminent threat of it</li><li><b>Ship</b>: any seagoing vessel of any type whatsoever, and any floating craft, except installations for exploring or exploiting sea-bed resources</li><li><b>Oil</b>: crude oil, fuel oil, diesel oil and lubricating oil</li><li><b>Related interests</b> of the coastal State directly affected or threatened:<ul><li>(a) coastal, port or estuarine activities, including fisheries that are an essential means of livelihood</li><li>(b) tourist attractions of the area</li><li>(c) the health of the coastal population and the well-being of the area, including conservation of living marine resources and wildlife</li></ul></li><li><b>Organization</b>: the Inter-Governmental Maritime Consultative Organization (now IMO)</li></ul><hr><h2>Procedure before acting (Art III)</h2><ul><li>(a) Consult the other States affected, in particular the flag State(s)</li><li>(b) Promptly notify known persons whose interests may be affected (owners, others) and consider their views</li><li>(c) May consult independent experts from the list kept by the Organization</li><li>(d) In case of <b>extreme urgency</b>, may act at once without prior notice or consultation</li><li>Must use best endeavours to avoid any risk to human life</li><li>Must notify the measures taken to the States and known persons concerned, and to the IMO Secretary-General</li></ul><h2>Experts (Art IV)</h2><ul><li>IMO keeps the list of experts and sets its rules; Member States and Parties may nominate experts; the States using the experts pay for them</li></ul><h2>Proportionality (Art V)</h2><ul><li>V(1): measures must be proportionate to the damage, actual or threatened</li><li>V(2): must not go beyond what is reasonably necessary; must stop once the aim is achieved; must avoid unnecessary interference with the rights of others</li><li>V(3): proportionality is judged by (a) the extent and probability of imminent damage if no measures are taken, (b) the likelihood of the measures being effective, (c) the extent of damage the measures may cause</li></ul><h2>Compensation and disputes</h2><ul><li><b>Art VI:</b> a Party that took measures beyond what is reasonably necessary pays compensation for the damage they caused</li><li><b>Art VII:</b> the Convention does not prejudice other applicable rights, duties, privileges, immunities or remedies</li><li><b>Art VIII:</b> disputes on whether measures broke the Convention, on compensation and its amount go to <b>conciliation, then arbitration</b> under the Annex if negotiation fails; the acting Party cannot refuse because its own courts' remedies are not exhausted</li><li>Annex, conciliation: commission of three (one per party + chairman by agreement) · respondent names its conciliator within 60 days, otherwise the IMO Secretary-General · recommendation: parties have up to 90 days to accept or reject</li><li>Annex, arbitration: request within 180 days after conciliation fails · tribunal of three · award is reasoned, final, no appeal, to be complied with at once</li></ul><h2>Final clauses</h2><ul><li>Art IX: open for signature until 31 Dec 1970, then accession</li><li>Art XI: in force on the 90th day after 15 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Art XII: denunciation takes effect one year after deposit</li><li>Art XIV: revision conference convened by IMO, or if one third of Parties ask</li><li>Art XVII: authentic languages English and French</li><li>IMO Secretary-General is the depositary</li></ul><hr><h2>1973 Protocol — substances other than oil</h2><ul><li>Adopted at the 1973 London Conference on Marine Pollution · in force 1983</li><li>Only States that are Parties to the 1969 Convention can join (Art IV); it cannot enter into force before the Convention (Art VI(1)); in force 90th day after 15 States</li><li>Art I(1): same right of intervention on the high seas for grave and imminent danger from pollution by non-oil substances, after a maritime casualty or related acts, reasonably expected to cause major harmful consequences</li><li>Art I(2): applies to (a) substances on the list in the Protocol's Annex, and (b) other substances liable to harm human health, living resources, amenities or other legitimate uses of the sea</li><li>Art I(3): for (b), the intervening Party must show the substance could reasonably pose a danger similar to the listed ones</li><li>Art II: Art I(2) and Arts II–VIII of the 1969 Convention, and its Annex, apply to non-oil substances; the expert list also covers non-oil substances</li><li>Art III — the list: an IMO body keeps it · amendments need a two-thirds majority of Parties present and voting · deemed accepted after six months unless one third of Parties object · in force three months after acceptance</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>After a casualty, a coastal State may require action on the high seas — only if the danger is grave and imminent, and the measures are proportionate</blockquote><blockquote>Compensation is due if the State went beyond what was reasonably necessary</blockquote><blockquote>Keep records of orders and of consultations with the coastal State</blockquote><blockquote>Related instruments: Salvage 1989 and Nairobi WRC 2007 (wrecks)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>1969 Convention text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1969-International-Convention-on-High-Seas-Oil-Pollution-Intervention-1.pdf</li><li>1969 Convention page (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/databasecil/1969-international-convention-relating-to-intervention-on-the-high-seas-in-cases-of-oil-pollution-casualties</li><li>1973 Protocol text (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-05/intervention-high-seas.html</li><li>IMO — Intervention Convention: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-Relating-to-Intervention-on-the-High-Seas-in-Cases-of-Oil-Pollution-Casualties.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-llmc-1976","v":12209380,"title":"LLMC 1976","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["llmc","limitation","liability","sdr","p-and-i","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>LLMC 1976 — Convention on Limitation of Liability for Maritime Claims (with the 1996 Protocol and the 2012 limits)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1976 Convention</td><td>Adopted 19 Nov 1976 · in force 1 Dec 1986</td></tr><tr><td>1996 Protocol</td><td>Adopted 2 May 1996 · in force 13 May 2004</td></tr><tr><td>2012 limits</td><td>Adopted 19 Apr 2012 · in force 8 Jun 2015</td></tr><tr><td>Who can limit</td><td>Shipowners, salvors and their insurers</td></tr><tr><td>Right to limit lost only if</td><td>Intent, or reckless and knowing that loss would probably result (Art 4)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is LLMC</h2><ul><li>Lets shipowners and salvors limit their total liability for most maritime claims from one occasion to an amount based on the ship's gross tonnage</li><li>IMO describes the limitation as \"virtually unbreakable\"</li><li>Replaced the 1957 Brussels Convention (in force 1968)</li><li>Limits were raised by up to 250–300% in 1976 (IMO), again by the 1996 Protocol (which added a tacit-acceptance procedure to update them) and again by the 2012 amendments</li></ul><h2>Who may limit (Art 1)</h2><ul><li>Shipowners and salvors (Art 1(1))</li><li><b>Shipowner</b> = owner, charterer, manager and operator of a seagoing ship (Art 1(2))</li><li><b>Salvor</b> = any person rendering services in direct connection with salvage operations (Art 1(3))</li><li>People for whose acts the shipowner or salvor is responsible may also limit (Art 1(4))</li><li>Liability includes an action brought against the vessel herself (Art 1(5))</li><li>Insurers get the same benefit as the insured (Art 1(6))</li><li>Invoking limitation is not an admission of liability (Art 1(7))</li></ul><h2>Claims subject to limitation (Art 2)</h2><ul><li>(a) Loss of life or personal injury, or loss of or damage to property (including harbour works, basins, waterways and aids to navigation)</li><li>(b) Loss from delay in carriage by sea of cargo, passengers or their luggage</li><li>(c) Other loss from infringement of rights other than contractual rights</li><li>(d) Raising, removal, destruction or rendering harmless of a ship (wreck)</li><li>(e) Removal, destruction or rendering harmless of the ship's cargo</li><li>(f) Claims of a person other than the person liable, for measures taken to avert or minimize loss</li><li>Art 2(2): these claims are limited even if brought by way of recourse; but (d), (e) and (f) do not apply to the extent they relate to remuneration under a contract with the person liable</li><li>Applies whatever the basis of liability</li></ul><h2>Claims not limited (Art 3, as amended by the 1996 Protocol)</h2><ul><li>(a) Salvage claims (including special compensation under the 1989 Salvage Convention) and general average contributions</li><li>(b) Oil pollution damage under the 1969 CLC</li><li>(c) Claims subject to any international convention or national law governing or prohibiting limitation for nuclear damage</li><li>(d) Claims against the shipowner of a nuclear ship for nuclear damage</li><li>(e) Claims by servants of the shipowner or salvor, whose duties are connected with the ship or salvage, where the law governing the contract of service does not allow limitation or sets a higher limit</li></ul><h2>Conduct barring limitation (Art 4)</h2><blockquote>A person is not entitled to limit if it is proved that the loss resulted from his personal act or omission, committed with the intent to cause such loss, or recklessly and with knowledge that such loss would probably result</blockquote><hr><h2>Limits of liability (SDR)</h2><p><b>Loss of life and personal injury</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship size</th><th>1976</th><th>1996 Protocol</th><th>2012 (in force 8 Jun 2015)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up to 2,000 GT</td><td>333,000 (ships up to 500 tons) + tonnage amounts</td><td>2 million</td><td><b>3.02 million</b></td></tr><tr><td>2,001–30,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 800 per ton</td><td><b>+ 1,208 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>30,001–70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 600 per ton</td><td><b>+ 906 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>Over 70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 400 per ton</td><td><b>+ 604 per ton</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Other claims (property)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship size</th><th>1976</th><th>1996 Protocol</th><th>2012 (in force 8 Jun 2015)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up to 2,000 GT</td><td>167,000 (ships up to 500 tons) + tonnage amounts</td><td>1 million</td><td><b>1.51 million</b></td></tr><tr><td>2,001–30,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 400 per ton</td><td><b>+ 604 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>30,001–70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 300 per ton</td><td><b>+ 453 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>Over 70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 200 per ton</td><td><b>+ 302 per ton</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Passengers (Art 7(1), 1996 Protocol)</b>: 175,000 units of account × the number of passengers the ship is certified to carry (1976 text: 46,666 per passenger, maximum 25 million)</li><li>Example, 30,000 GT ship under the 2012 limits: personal claims = 3,020,000 + 28,000 × 1,208 = 36,844,000 SDR · property claims = 1,510,000 + 28,000 × 604 = 18,422,000 SDR</li><li>Unit of account (Art 8): the Special Drawing Right of the IMF, converted to national currency on the date the fund is constituted, paid or secured</li><li>Art 9: limits apply to the total of all claims from one distinct occasion</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"llmc-per-ton\"><figcaption>Added limit per ton for other claims: 1996 Protocol and 2012</figcaption></figure><h2>Updating the limits (1996 Protocol Art 8, tacit acceptance)</h2><ul><li>A proposed amendment of the limits is deemed accepted 18 months after notification, unless at least one-fourth of the Contracting States object</li><li>Limits may not be raised by more than 6% a year (compound), or above three times the original amount</li><li>No amendment may be considered within five years of the relevant date set in the Protocol</li></ul><h2>Fund, procedure and scope</h2><ul><li><b>Limitation without a fund (Art 10)</b>: limitation can be invoked without constituting a fund; a State may require one</li><li><b>Constituting the fund (Art 11)</b>: any person alleged liable may constitute a fund with the court, as a deposit or acceptable guarantee, including interest from the date of the occurrence; a fund constituted by one liable person or their insurer is deemed constituted by all</li><li><b>Distribution (Art 12)</b>: in proportion to established claims</li><li><b>Bar to other actions (Art 13)</b>: a claimant against the fund cannot act against other assets of the fund holder; an arrested ship or security is released once the fund exists in the listed places</li><li><b>Governing law (Art 14)</b>: the law of the State where the fund is constituted</li><li><b>Counterclaims (Art 5)</b>: claims from the same occurrence are set off; the Convention applies only to the balance</li><li><b>Scope (Art 15)</b>: applies when limitation is sought before a court of a State Party. States may regulate inland waterway vessels and ships of less than 300 tons by national law. It does not apply to air-cushion vehicles or to floating platforms built to explore or exploit sea-bed resources</li><li><b>Reservations (Art 18, as amended 1996)</b>: a State may exclude Art 2(1)(d) and (e) (wreck and cargo removal) and claims under the 1996 HNS Convention; no other reservation to the substantive provisions</li></ul><h2>Entry into force</h2><ul><li>1976 Convention (Art 17): first day of the month after one year, after 12 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments → 1 Dec 1986</li><li>1996 Protocol: open for signature 1 Oct 1996 – 30 Sep 1997; in force 90 days after 10 States consented → 13 May 2004</li><li>Protocol Art 12: denunciation takes effect 12 months after the instrument is deposited</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The 2012 limits are the current LLMC 1996 limits</blockquote><blockquote>Wreck removal claims are limited unless the State used its Art 18 reservation — see Nairobi WRC 2007</blockquote><blockquote>Oil pollution under CLC is outside LLMC (Art 3(b))</blockquote><blockquote>Limitation depends on the facts of conduct — report incidents fully to the P&amp;I Club</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>1976 LLMC treaty text (UK Treaty Series 1990 No 13): https://treaties.fcdo.gov.uk/data/Library2/pdf/1990-TS0013.pdf</li><li>1996 Protocol text: https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-07/limitation-liability-maritime.html</li><li>IMO — LLMC Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/convention-on-limitation-of-liability-for-maritime-claims-(llmc).aspx</li><li>IMO — new LLMC limits: https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/PressBriefings/Pages/24-LLMC-limits.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-load-lines-1966","v":200817054,"title":"Load Lines 1966","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["load-lines","freeboard","plimsoll","ll-protocol","certificate","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>LL 1966 — International Convention on Load Lines (with the 1988 Protocol)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>5 Apr 1966</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>21 Jul 1968</td></tr><tr><td>Annex I</td><td>Regulations for determining freeboard</td></tr><tr><td>Annex II</td><td>Zones, areas and seasonal periods</td></tr><tr><td>Annex III</td><td>Certificates</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is LL</h2><ul><li>Sets the <b>minimum freeboard</b> a ship must keep (Annex I) so that load lines are not submerged · zones and seasonal periods in Annex II · certificates in Annex III</li><li>Freeboard = vertical distance, measured amidships, from the upper edge of the deck line down to the upper edge of the load line (Reg 3)</li><li>Load lines and the deck line are marked amidships on each side of the ship</li><li>Conditions of assignment (Chapter II) keep the hull weathertight and watertight: doors, hatchways, openings, ventilators, air pipes, freeing ports, protection of the crew</li><li>Ships carrying timber deck cargo get a smaller (timber) freeboard (Chapter IV)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Art 28: in force 12 months after at least 15 Governments, including 7 with at least 1 million GT of shipping, signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Amendments of 1971, 1975, 1979 and 1983 needed acceptance by two-thirds of Parties and never entered into force</li><li><b>1988 Protocol</b>: adopted 11 Nov 1988 · in force <b>3 Feb 2000</b> · harmonised survey and certification with SOLAS and MARPOL, revised certain technical annex regulations, and introduced the <b>tacit amendment</b> procedure (Annex B amendments deemed accepted after 2 years from communication, or a shorter period of at least 1 year set at adoption; in force 6 months later; a Party may exempt itself for up to 1 year)</li><li>2003 amendments: MSC.143(77), adopted 5 Jun 2003 · deemed accepted 1 Jul 2004 · in force 1 Jan 2005</li><li>Ships of 24 m and over built on or after 1 Jul 2010 must meet at least Part A of the 2008 IS Code (MSC.270(85)); ships built 21 Jul 1968 – 30 Jun 2010 must meet the stability standards in force before 1 Jan 2005 (per UK MCA guidance)</li><li>Later amendments include MSC.172(79) to MSC.375(93), A.1082(28), A.1083(28) and MSC.491(104) (in force 1 Jan 2024)</li><li><b>MSC.591(111)</b> (adopted 21 May 2026): amends Annex I Ch II <b>Reg 25 Protection of the crew</b> (guard rails: see below) · deemed accepted 1 Jul 2027 · in force <b>1 Jan 2028</b> · applies to ships whose keel is laid, or which are at a similar stage of construction, on or after 1 Jan 2028</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b> (Arts 4–5)</p><ul><li>Ships of Contracting Governments (and unregistered ships flying their flag) engaged on international voyages</li><li>Annex I applies to new ships; existing ships need not increase freeboard; Annex II applies to new and existing ships</li><li>Art 5 exceptions: warships · new ships under 24 m · existing ships under 150 GT · pleasure yachts not engaged in trade · fishing vessels · ships solely on the Great Lakes/St Lawrence, Caspian Sea, and the Plate, Paraná and Uruguay Rivers (within stated limits)</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles (34)</h2><ul><li>Art 1 General obligation · Art 2 Definitions (<b>Length L = 96% of the total length on a waterline at 85% of the least moulded depth, or the stem-to-rudder-stock length on that waterline if greater</b>) · Art 3 General provisions (ships on international voyages are surveyed, marked and hold an International Load Line Certificate (1966) or an Exemption Certificate; the Administration may assign more than the minimum freeboard) · Art 4 Application · Art 5 Exceptions</li><li>Art 6 Exemptions: short voyages between neighbouring ports · ships with novel features · a single exceptional international voyage — reported to IMO</li><li>Art 7 Force majeure · Art 8 Equivalents · Art 9 Experimental approvals · Art 10 Repairs, alterations and modifications · Art 11 Zones and areas (Annex II)</li><li>Art 12 <b>Submersion</b>: load lines must not be submerged at sea, during the voyage, or on arrival; allowances for fresh water (adjusted relative to a density of 1.025) and for deeper loading when departing from rivers or inland waters</li><li>Art 13 Survey and marking (Administration officers, nominated surveyors or recognised organisations) · Art 14 <b>Surveys and inspections</b>: initial before service · periodical not exceeding 5 years · annual inspection within 3 months either side of the anniversary date (checks openings, guard rails, freeing ports and crew access; endorsed on the certificate)</li><li>Art 15 Maintenance of conditions after survey (no change without Administration sanction) · Art 16 Issue of certificates · Art 17 Issue by another Government (never to ships of non-Contracting States) · Art 18 Form of certificates (Annex III; English or French translation) </li><li>Art 19 <b>Duration</b>: up to 5 years; may be extended up to 5 months if no relevant alterations; cancelled for material alterations, fittings not maintained, missing inspection endorsements, or structure weakened so the ship is unsafe; ceases when the ship changes flag; single-voyage exemption certificate valid for that voyage</li><li>Art 20 Acceptance of certificates · Art 21 <b>Control</b>: foreign port control limited to verifying a valid certificate, that the ship is not overloaded, that the load line position matches the certificate, and that the ship has not been so materially altered as to be manifestly unfit for sea; intervention reported in writing to the flag State's consul or diplomatic representative</li><li>Art 22 Privileges only with a valid certificate · Art 23 Casualties · Art 24 Prior treaties · Art 25 Special rules by agreement · Art 26 Communication of information · Art 27 Signature, acceptance, accession · Art 28 Entry into force · Art 29 Amendments · Art 30 Denunciation (after 5 years, effective 1 year after notice) · Art 31 Suspension · Art 32 Territories · Art 33 Registration · Art 34 Languages</li></ul><p><b>Annex I — Regulations for determining load lines</b> (figures below are from the 1966 text; later amendments and the 1988 Protocol Annex B revised some regulations — check the current text before calculating)</p><ul><li><b>Chapter I General</b>: Reg 1 Strength of hull · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Definitions (freeboard deck, superstructure, block coefficient, perpendiculars, amidships, freeboard, length) · Reg 4 Deck line · Reg 5 Load line mark · Reg 6 Lines to be used with the load line mark · Reg 7 Mark of assigning authority · Reg 8 Details of marking · Reg 9 Verification of marks<ul><li><b>Deck line</b> (Reg 4): horizontal line 300 mm long × 25 mm wide, amidships on each side; its upper edge normally passes where the upper surface of the freeboard deck, extended outwards, meets the outer shell</li><li><b>Load line mark</b> (Reg 5): ring 300 mm outside diameter × 25 mm wide, crossed by a horizontal line 450 mm long × 25 mm wide whose upper edge passes through the ring's centre; the centre is amidships, below the deck line by the assigned summer freeboard</li><li><b>Lines</b> (Reg 6): horizontal lines 230 mm long × 25 mm wide, extending forward of a vertical line 25 mm wide set 540 mm forward of the ring centre: <b>S</b> Summer · <b>W</b> Winter · <b>WNA</b> Winter North Atlantic · <b>T</b> Tropical · <b>F</b> Fresh water in summer and <b>TF</b> Tropical fresh water (both marked abaft the vertical line) · timber load lines (LS, LW, LWNA, LT, LF, LTF) are 230 × 25 mm and extend abaft a vertical line 540 mm abaft the ring centre (LF and LTF forward) · lines that do not apply may be omitted</li><li><b>Mark of assigning authority</b> (Reg 7): not more than four initials, each about 115 mm high and 75 mm wide, alongside the ring</li><li><b>Painting</b> (Reg 8): white or yellow on a dark background, or black on a light one; also permanently marked on the sides · <b>Verification</b> (Reg 9): the certificate is not issued until the marks are verified correct and permanent</li></ul></li><li><b>Chapter II Conditions of assignment of freeboard</b>: Reg 10 Information to be supplied to the master (loading, ballasting, stability) · Reg 11 Superstructure end bulkheads · Reg 12 Doors (steel; weathertight; sills at least 380 mm above deck for superstructure access) · Reg 13 Position of hatchways, doorways and ventilators (Position 1 and Position 2) · Reg 14 Cargo and other hatchways · Reg 15 Hatchways closed by portable covers and tarpaulins · Reg 16 Hatchways closed by weathertight steel covers with gaskets and clamping devices · Reg 17 Machinery space openings · Reg 18 Miscellaneous openings in freeboard and superstructure decks · Reg 19 Ventilators · Reg 20 Air pipes · Reg 21 Cargo ports and similar openings · Reg 22 Scuppers, inlets and discharges · Reg 23 Side scuttles · Reg 24 Freeing ports · Reg 25 Protection of the crew · Reg 26 Special conditions of assignment for Type \"A\" ships<ul><li><b>Reg 25 guard rails</b> (1966 text): at least 1 m high; lowest opening not more than 230 mm; other courses not more than 380 mm apart. MSC.591(111) (ships with keel laid on/after 1 Jan 2028): guard rails or bulwarks around all exposed decks and sea access holes (e.g. moonpool edges) the crew can reach, minimum 1 m high (Administration may approve lower where 1 m interferes with normal operation); guard rails with <b>at least three courses</b>; opening below the lowest course not more than 230 mm; other courses not more than 380 mm apart; rounded-gunwale ships: supports on the flat of the deck; chains between two fixed stanchions/bulwarks may replace guard rails where necessary for normal operation (tightened as far as practicable, detachable)</li></ul></li><li><b>Chapter III Freeboards</b>: Reg 27 Types of ships (Type \"A\", Type \"B\"; in practice also Type B reduced, B increased, B timber) · Reg 28 Tabular freeboard (by length, interpolated) · Reg 29 Correction for Type B ships 24–100 m with enclosed superstructures: increase = 7.5 × (100 − L) × (0.35 − E/L) mm · Reg 30 Block coefficient correction (if Cb &gt; 0.68, multiply by (Cb + 0.68)/1.36) · Reg 31 Depth correction (if D &gt; L/15, add (D − L/15) × R; R = 0.48 L mm below 120 m, 250 mm at 120 m and over) · Reg 32 Deck line correction · Reg 33 Standard height of superstructure (raised quarter deck 0.90 m at ≤30 m, 1.20 m at 75 m, 1.80 m at ≥125 m; other superstructures 1.80 m, 1.80 m, 2.30 m) · Reg 34 Length of superstructure · Reg 35 Effective length · Reg 36 Trunks · Reg 37 Deduction for superstructures and trunks · Reg 38 Sheer · Reg 39 Minimum bow height (below 250 m: 56 L (1 − L/500) × (Cb + 0.68)/1.36 mm; 250 m and over: 7000 × (Cb + 0.68)/1.36 mm; Cb not less than 0.68) · Reg 40 Minimum freeboards<ul><li><b>Reg 40</b>: Summer = tabular freeboard with corrections (salt-water minimum 50 mm; 150 mm if non-compliant hatch covers in Position 1) · <b>Tropical</b> = summer − 1/48 of summer draught · <b>Winter</b> = summer + 1/48 of summer draught · <b>WNA</b> = winter + 50 mm, for ships of 100 m or less entering the North Atlantic winter area · <b>Fresh water</b> deduction = Δ/(4T) cm (Δ = salt-water displacement in tonnes, T = tonnes per cm immersion); if displacement cannot be certified, 1/48 of summer draught</li></ul></li><li><b>Chapter IV Timber freeboards (Regs 41–45)</b>: Reg 41 Application · Reg 42 Definitions (timber deck cargo) · Reg 43 Construction (forecastle at least standard height and 0.07 L; ships under 100 m need a poop or equivalent; bulwarks at least 1 m high) · Reg 44 Stowage (lashings at least 19 mm chain or equivalent; uprights and lashings not more than 3 m apart; in a winter seasonal zone in winter cargo height limited to one-third of extreme breadth; guard rails or life lines not more than 33 cm apart vertically) · Reg 45 Computation (winter timber = summer timber + 1/36 of moulded summer timber draught; tropical timber = summer timber − 1/48)</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"load-line-mark\"><figcaption>Load line mark (Annex I Regs 4 to 7): deck line 300 x 25 mm; ring 300 mm outside diameter, 25 mm wide, with a 450 mm line through its centre; load lines 230 x 25 mm; vertical line 540 mm forward of the ring centre. Not to scale.</figcaption></figure><p><b>Annex II — Zones, areas and seasonal periods</b> (Regs 46–52, 1966 text)</p><ul><li>Winter periods: North Atlantic Winter Zone I 16 Oct – 15 Apr · Zone II 1 Nov – 31 Mar · North Atlantic Winter Seasonal Area (over 100 m) 16 Dec – 15 Feb (100 m and under 1 Nov – 31 Mar) · North Pacific Winter Zone 16 Oct – 15 Apr · Southern Winter Zone 16 Apr – 15 Oct</li><li>Seasonal tropical areas (examples): North Atlantic 1 Nov – 15 Jul · Arabian Sea 1 Sep – 31 May · Bay of Bengal 1 Dec – 30 Apr</li><li>Enclosed seas, ships 100 m and under: Baltic winter 1 Nov – 31 Mar · Black Sea north of 44°N winter 1 Dec – end Feb · Sea of Japan north of 50°N winter 1 Dec – end Feb · Mediterranean (specified western part) winter 16 Dec – 15 Mar</li><li>Reg 52 Winter North Atlantic load line: part of Winter Zone II between 15°W and 50°W, and all of Winter Zone I</li><li>Art 11: a port on a zone boundary is treated as in the zone the ship arrives from or departs to</li></ul><h2>Annex III — Certificates</h2><ul><li><b>International Load Line Certificate (1966)</b>: ship name, distinctive number or letters, port of registry, length L · assigned type (A, B, B with reduced freeboard, B with increased freeboard) · freeboards from the deck line for T, S, W, WNA and the timber equivalents · fresh water allowance · survey date · reverse side: endorsements from periodical inspections and extensions · valid up to 5 years (Art 19)</li><li><b>International Load Line Exemption Certificate</b>: exemptions under Art 6(2) and any single-voyage exemption under Art 6(4)</li><li>Fresh water allowance adjusted in proportion to the difference of the water density from 1.025; allowance for river or inland-water departure for fuel and consumables used to reach the sea</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Never submerge the load line (for the zone and season) at departure, during the voyage or on arrival (Art 12)</blockquote><blockquote>Annual inspection within 3 months before or after each anniversary; renewal every ≤5 years</blockquote><blockquote>Port State control (Art 21): valid certificate, not overloaded, load line position matches the certificate, no material alteration making the ship unfit for sea</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Convention text, Arts 1–34 (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1966-International-Convention-on-Load-Lines.pdf</li><li>Convention text with Annexes (UK Treaty Series 1968): https://treaties.fcdo.gov.uk/data/Library2/pdf/1968-TS0058.pdf</li><li>IMO — Load Lines Convention: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-Load-Lines.aspx</li><li>UK MCA — MSIS41 International Load Line: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/644633e96dda69000d11e2e6/MSIS41_International_Load_Line_R05.23.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.591(111) (Reg 25 amendment): https://ltsa.lrv.lt/public/canonical/1787556830/2291/MSC.591(111).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-london-convention-and-protocol","v":158402114,"title":"London Convention and Protocol","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["london-convention","london-protocol","dumping","waste","co2","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>London Convention 1972 and London Protocol 1996 — Prevention of Marine Pollution by Dumping of Wastes and Other Matter</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>London Convention (LC)</th><th>London Protocol (LP)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>13 Nov 1972, London</td><td>7 Nov 1996</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>30 Aug 1975</td><td>24 Mar 2006</td></tr><tr><td>Approach</td><td>Lists: banned (Annex I), special permit (Annex II), general permit (all other)</td><td><b>All dumping banned except the \"reverse list\"</b> (Annex 1)</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>15 ratifications (Art XIX)</td><td>30th day after 26 States, incl. 15 LC Parties (Art 25)</td></tr><tr><td>Administered by</td><td>IMO (since 1977)</td><td>IMO</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is LC / LP</h2><ul><li>Global control of the deliberate disposal of wastes at sea</li><li>LC aim (Art I): prevent pollution of the sea by dumping that could harm human health, living resources, amenities or other legitimate uses</li><li>LP is intended to eventually replace the LC (IMO)</li><li>LP principles: <b>precautionary approach</b> (Art 3.1 — action even without conclusive evidence of a causal link) · <b>polluter pays</b> (Art 3.2) · no transfer of pollution from one part of the environment to another (IMO)</li><li>LP Art 2: Parties act according to their scientific, technical and economic capabilities to prevent, reduce and where practicable eliminate pollution from dumping or incineration</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>1993 LC amendments (in force 1994): ban on dumping low-level radioactive waste · industrial waste dumping phased out by 31 Dec 1995 · ban on incineration at sea of industrial waste</li><li><b>2006 amendment</b> (LP.1(1)): adds CO₂ sequestration in sub-seabed geological formations to Annex 1 · adopted 2 Nov 2006 · in force 10 Feb 2007 (Canada 29 Jan 2007) · excludes use of CO₂ streams for enhanced oil recovery (IMO)</li><li><b>2009 amendment</b> (LP.3(4), adopted 30 Oct 2009): Art 6 — export of CO₂ streams for sub-seabed storage allowed between countries with an agreement · not yet in force: takes effect 60 days after two-thirds of Parties accept (IMO) · <b>2019 resolution LP.5(14)</b> allows provisional application, by a declaration to the IMO Secretary-General</li><li><b>2013 amendment</b> (LP.4(8), adopted 18 Oct 2013): marine geoengineering — new Art 6bis, Annex 4 (ocean fertilization), Annex 5 (assessment framework)</li></ul><hr><h2>London Convention 1972</h2><p><b>Definition of dumping (Art III)</b></p><ul><li>Deliberate disposal at sea of wastes or other matter from vessels, aircraft or platforms, and the disposal of vessels or structures themselves</li><li>Not dumping: waste incidental to or from normal operation of vessels and platforms (unless the vessel carries waste for disposal) · placement of matter for a purpose other than mere disposal · waste from seabed mineral exploration and exploitation</li></ul><p><b>Permits (Art IV)</b></p><ul><li>Annex I matter: <b>prohibited</b></li><li>Annex II matter: <b>prior special permit</b></li><li>All other matter: <b>prior general permit</b></li><li>Permits only after careful consideration of the Annex III factors</li><li>Art V: force majeure — dumping allowed to secure the safety of human life or vessels when it is the only way to avert the threat, and must be reported to IMO</li><li>Art VI: each Party designates an authority to issue permits, keep records of the nature and quantities of matter dumped, and monitor the sea</li><li>Art VII: Parties apply the Convention to their vessels and aircraft, those loading in their territory, and must prevent and punish contraventions</li></ul><p><b>Annex I — prohibited</b></p><ul><li>Organohalogen compounds · mercury and compounds · cadmium and compounds</li><li>Persistent plastics and other persistent synthetic materials (for example netting and ropes) that interfere with fishing or navigation</li><li>Crude oil and its wastes, refined petroleum products and distillate residues taken aboard for dumping</li><li>Radioactive wastes or other radioactive matter · materials produced for biological and chemical warfare</li><li>(Added later) incineration at sea of industrial waste and sewage sludge prohibited; industrial waste covered from 1 Jan 1996</li></ul><p><b>Annex II — special permit</b></p><ul><li>Wastes with significant amounts of arsenic, beryllium, chromium, copper, lead, nickel, vanadium, zinc, organosilicon compounds, cyanides, fluorides, and pesticides not covered by Annex I</li><li>Containers, scrap metal and bulky wastes that may sink and obstruct fishing or navigation</li><li>Materials that are non-toxic but may become harmful in quantity or seriously reduce amenities</li></ul><p><b>Annex III — permit factors</b></p><ul><li>A Characteristics and composition of the matter · B Characteristics of the dumping site and method of deposit · C General considerations and conditions</li></ul><hr><h2>London Protocol 1996 — the Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>2–3</td><td>Objectives and general obligations</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Dumping of wastes or other matter</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Incineration at sea</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Export of wastes or other matter</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Internal waters</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Exceptions</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Issuance of permits and reporting</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Application and enforcement</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Compliance procedures</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Regional co-operation</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Technical co-operation and assistance</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Scientific and technical research</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Responsibility and liability</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Meetings of Contracting Parties</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Duties of the Organization</td></tr><tr><td>21–22</td><td>Amendment of the Protocol and of the Annexes</td></tr><tr><td>25–26</td><td>Entry into force · transitional period</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Key articles</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 1 definitions:</b> dumping = any deliberate disposal into the sea of wastes or other matter from vessels, aircraft, platforms or other man-made structures; includes storage in the seabed and subsoil, and abandonment or toppling of platforms for the sole purpose of deliberate disposal · incineration at sea = combustion on board a vessel, platform or other man-made structure at sea (waste from normal operations excluded) · \"sea\" = all marine waters other than internal waters</li><li><b>Art 4.1.1:</b> Parties shall prohibit dumping of any wastes or other matter <b>except those listed in Annex 1</b>; Art 4.1.2: dumping of Annex 1 matter needs a permit that meets Annex 2</li><li><b>Art 5:</b> incineration at sea prohibited</li><li><b>Art 6:</b> no export of wastes to other countries for dumping or incineration at sea (2009 amendment adds a CO₂ exception)</li><li><b>Art 7:</b> in internal waters a Party may apply the Protocol or other effective permitting and regulatory measures</li><li><b>Art 8:</b> force majeure (\"stress of weather\") — Arts 4.1 and 5 do not apply when it is the only way to avert a threat; reported forthwith; emergency permits possible after consultation</li><li><b>Art 9:</b> each Party designates authorities that issue permits, keep records and monitor the sea; annual reports to IMO</li><li><b>Art 10:</b> applies to vessels and aircraft registered or flying the flag of a Party, and to those loading waste in its territory</li><li><b>Art 11:</b> compliance procedures set by the Meeting of Contracting Parties</li><li><b>Art 26:</b> a State may notify a transitional period, not longer than five years</li></ul><h2>Annex 1 — the \"reverse list\"</h2><p>Only these may be considered for dumping, and only with a permit meeting Annex 2:</p><ul><li>1.1 Dredged material</li><li>1.2 Sewage sludge</li><li>1.3 Fish waste, or material from industrial fish processing</li><li>1.4 Vessels and platforms or other man-made structures at sea</li><li>1.5 Inert, inorganic geological material</li><li>1.6 Organic material of natural origin</li><li>1.7 Bulky items primarily of iron, steel, concrete and similarly unharmful materials</li><li>1.8 Carbon dioxide streams from CO₂ capture processes for sequestration</li></ul><p><b>Conditions in Annex 1</b></p><ul><li>Items 1.4 and 1.7: material that could create floating debris must be removed, and the item must not seriously obstruct fishing or navigation</li><li>Materials with radioactivity above de minimis levels are not eligible; a scientific study is due every 25 years from 20 Feb 1994</li><li>CO₂ streams: only into a sub-seabed geological formation, must consist overwhelmingly of CO₂, no waste added for disposal</li></ul><h2>Annex 2 — assessment of wastes</h2><ul><li>General · waste prevention audit · consideration of waste management options · chemical, physical and biological properties · action list · dump-site selection · assessment of potential effects · monitoring · permit and permit conditions</li></ul><h2>Marine geoengineering (2013 amendment LP.4(8))</h2><ul><li>Marine geoengineering = deliberate intervention in the marine environment to manipulate natural processes, with potential deleterious effects (Art 1.5bis)</li><li>Annex 4 lists <b>ocean fertilization</b> only: activity intended mainly to stimulate ocean primary productivity; aquaculture, mariculture and artificial reefs excluded</li><li>All ocean fertilization is prohibited, <b>except legitimate scientific research</b>, which may get a permit after assessment under Annex 5 (the 2010 Assessment Framework)</li><li>Annex 5 requires a full project description, a purpose other than mere disposal, and proof of financial resources; research must be peer reviewed, results published, no direct financial gain</li><li>Entry into force: not stated in the text I checked, so it is not given here</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>LP bans all dumping except Annex 1 items, and then only with a permit</blockquote><blockquote>Ship garbage, sewage and oily waste are MARPOL matters, not LC/LP dumping — LC excludes waste incidental to normal vessel operation</blockquote><blockquote>Disposing of a vessel at sea is \"dumping\" — permitted under the LP only as an Annex 1 item (1.4) with a permit</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>1996 London Protocol text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1996-Prot-to-1972-Convention-on-Prevention-of-MP-by-Dumping-1.pdf</li><li>1972 London Convention text (US EPA): https://www.epa.gov/sites/production/files/2015-10/documents/lc1972.pdf</li><li>IMO — London Convention and Protocol: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Convention-on-the-Prevention-of-Marine-Pollution-by-Dumping-of-Wastes-and-Other-Matter.aspx</li><li>IMO — Carbon Capture and Sequestration: https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/ccs-default.aspx</li><li>2009 amendment LP.3(4): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/2009-Amd-to-London-Prot.pdf</li><li>2013 amendment LP.4(8): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Environment/Documents/LC_LP/LP.4(8).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-marpol-73-78","v":50557140,"title":"MARPOL 73/78","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["marpol","imo","convention","core","pollution","annex-vi","psc"],"body":"<h1>MARPOL 73/78 — International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>The main IMO convention against pollution from ships, both operational and accidental</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>2 Nov 1973</b>. Absorbed by the <b>1978 Protocol</b> after the 1976–77 tanker accidents</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>MARPOL 73/78 on <b>2 Oct 1983</b></td></tr><tr><td>1997 Protocol</td><td>Added <b>Annex VI</b> (air pollution). In force <b>19 May 2005</b></td></tr><tr><td>Annexes</td><td><b>6</b>. Annexes I and II are compulsory. III to VI are optional for each State</td></tr><tr><td>Parties (IMO Status 2026, dated 30 Sep 2026)</td><td>Annexes I/II <b>162 States, about 98.89%</b> of world tonnage · III <b>153</b> · IV <b>150</b> · V <b>157</b> · Annex VI (1997 Protocol) <b>114</b></td></tr><tr><td>Flag State issues</td><td>IOPP, NLS, ISPP, IAPP, EIAPP, IEE</td></tr><tr><td>Port State</td><td>Inspects (PSC) and can detain. Ships of non-Party States get no better treatment</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"marpol-annexes\"><figcaption>The six Annexes and when each entered into force</figcaption></figure><h2>The six Annexes at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Subject</th><th>In force</th><th>Certificate (5 years)</th><th>Size trigger</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>Oil</td><td>2 Oct 1983</td><td>IOPP</td><td>Tankers 150 GT+, other ships 400 GT+</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Noxious liquid substances in bulk</td><td>Compulsory with Annex I</td><td>NLS (or IBC Code certificate)</td><td>Ships certified for NLS in bulk</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Harmful substances in packages</td><td>1 Jul 1992</td><td>None</td><td>All ships carrying marine pollutants</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Sewage</td><td>27 Sep 2003</td><td>ISPP</td><td>400 GT+, or certified for more than 15 persons</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Garbage</td><td>31 Dec 1988</td><td>None</td><td>All ships</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Air pollution and energy efficiency</td><td>19 May 2005</td><td>IAPP, EIAPP, IEE</td><td>400 GT+ (engines above 130 kW)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> MARPOL tells you what you may discharge, where, and how it must be recorded. Port State Control checks the records against the real situation: soundings, the oily water separator, the 15 ppm alarm, garbage logs, fuel samples.</blockquote><h2>The Convention itself</h2><ul><li><b>Art 4 Violations:</b> prohibited anywhere. The flag State must sanction them</li><li><b>Art 5 Certificates and inspection:</b> Port States may inspect. Ships of non-Party States get no more favourable treatment</li><li><b>Art 6 Detection of violations</b></li><li><b>Art 7 Undue delay:</b> a ship wrongly detained or delayed can claim compensation</li><li><b>Art 8 and Protocol I:</b> reports on incidents involving harmful substances</li><li><b>Art 16 Amendments:</b> tacit acceptance. An amendment is deemed accepted on a set date unless enough Parties object</li></ul><p><b>Who it applies to</b></p><ul><li>Ships flying the flag of a Party, including hydrofoils, air-cushion craft, submersibles, floating craft and fixed or floating platforms</li><li>Not warships, naval auxiliaries or State ships on non-commercial service</li></ul><h2>Annex I — Oil</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>IOPP Certificate</td><td>Oil tankers 150 GT+, other ships 400 GT+. Valid up to 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Sludge tank (Reg 12)</td><td>Every ship 400 GT+ must have a sludge tank of adequate capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Oil filtering equipment</td><td>Ships 400 GT+ need 15 ppm equipment. Ships 10,000 GT+ also need an alarm and <b>automatic stop</b> above 15 ppm</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery space discharge</td><td>Outside special areas, en route, oil content below <b>15 ppm</b> with the filtering equipment running</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Record Book</td><td>Part I (machinery spaces), Part II (tankers, cargo and ballast). Kept <b>3 years</b> after the last entry</td></tr><tr><td>Segregated ballast tanks</td><td>Crude tankers 20,000 dwt+ and product carriers 30,000 dwt+</td></tr><tr><td>Double hull</td><td>Tankers 5,000 dwt+ delivered on or after <b>6 Jul 1996</b></td></tr><tr><td>Pump-room bottom protection</td><td>Tankers 5,000 dwt+ built on or after <b>1 Jan 2007</b></td></tr><tr><td>SOPEP</td><td>Tankers 150 GT+, other ships 400 GT+</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Cargo area discharge from tankers (Reg 34)</b></p><p>All of these must be true together:</p><ul><li>The tanker is <b>not in a special area</b></li><li>More than <b>50 nm</b> from the nearest land</li><li>En route</li><li>Instantaneous rate not above <b>30 litres per nautical mile</b></li><li>Total quantity not above <b>1/30,000</b> of the cargo the residue came from (tankers delivered after 31 Dec 1979)</li><li>The oil discharge monitoring and control system and the slop tank arrangement are working</li></ul><p><b>Special areas (Annex I)</b></p><ul><li>Mediterranean, Baltic, Black Sea, Red Sea, Gulfs, Gulf of Aden, Antarctic, North West European waters, Oman area of the Arabian Sea, Southern South African waters</li><li><b>Antarctic:</b> all oil discharge prohibited. No heavy fuel oil carried or used</li></ul><p><b>Fuel bans and transfers †</b></p><ul><li><b>Arctic heavy fuel oil ban:</b> from 1 Jul 2024, with waivers possible until 1 Jul 2029 (MEPC.329(76))</li><li><b>Ship-to-ship transfer of oil:</b> tankers 150 GT+ need an STS Operations Plan and must notify the coastal State at least 48 hours before</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"marpol-distances\"><figcaption>How far from land each discharge is allowed</figcaption></figure><h2>Annex II — Noxious liquid substances (NLS) in bulk</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Hazard</th><th>Discharge</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>X</b></td><td>Major hazard to marine resources or human health</td><td>Prohibited</td></tr><tr><td><b>Y</b></td><td>Hazard</td><td>Limited in quality and quantity</td></tr><tr><td><b>Z</b></td><td>Minor hazard</td><td>Less strict limits</td></tr><tr><td><b>OS</b></td><td>Other substances, evaluated as outside X, Y and Z</td><td>Annex II does not apply</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Operational discharge conditions (Reg 13)</b></p><ul><li>Ship en route at <b>7 knots</b> or more (self-propelled)</li><li>Discharge <b>below the waterline</b> through the underwater outlet</li><li>At least <b>12 nm</b> from the nearest land</li><li>In water at least <b>25 m</b> deep</li><li><b>Prewash:</b> a tank that held a Category X substance must be prewashed before the ship leaves the port of unloading</li></ul><p><b>Other rules</b></p><ul><li><b>Residue limit:</b> ships constructed on or after <b>1 Jan 2007</b>: no more than <b>75 litres</b> left in each tank and its piping (Categories X, Y, Z)</li><li><b>Cargo Record Book:</b> kept <b>3 years</b> after the last entry</li><li><b>SMPEP:</b> every ship 150 GT+ certified to carry NLS in bulk</li><li><b>Certificate:</b> valid up to 5 years. Chemical tankers built to the IBC Code carry the IBC certificate of fitness</li></ul><h2>Annex III — Harmful substances in packaged form †</h2><ul><li>Applies to marine pollutants identified in the IMDG Code (IMDG Code)</li><li>Packing, marking and labelling (the mark must survive 3 months in seawater), documents, stowage, quantity limits</li><li>Shipping documents must identify the goods as <b>marine pollutant</b></li><li>Jettison is allowed only for the safety of the ship or life</li></ul><h2>Annex IV — Sewage</h2><p><b>Applies to</b> ships 400 GT+ on international voyages, and ships certified for more than 15 persons.</p><p><b>Sewage systems (Reg 9):</b> an approved sewage treatment plant, <b>or</b> an approved comminuting and disinfecting system, <b>or</b> a holding tank.</p><p><b>Discharge (Reg 11)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Sewage</th><th>Where and how</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comminuted and disinfected</td><td>More than <b>3 nm</b> from the nearest land</td></tr><tr><td>Untreated</td><td>More than <b>12 nm</b>, ship en route at <b>4 knots</b> or more, moderate rate</td></tr><tr><td>Treated in an approved plant</td><td>No visible floating solids and no discoloration of the water</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Baltic Sea special area (passenger ships)</b></p><ul><li>It is the only special area under Annex IV</li><li>Discharge is prohibited unless the ship has an approved plant that also meets nitrogen and phosphorus standards</li><li>Dates in force: <b>new ships 1 Jun 2019 · existing ships 1 Jun 2021 · existing ships on direct routes to or from a port outside the area, or east of 28°10'E, 1 Jun 2023</b> (MEPC.275(69))</li><li>Port States around the area must provide reception facilities (Reg 12bis)</li></ul><h2>Annex V — Garbage</h2><p><b>Applies to</b> all ships and fixed or floating platforms. The revised Annex V has been in force since <b>1 Jan 2013</b>. The rule is a general prohibition, with listed exceptions.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Garbage</th><th>Outside special areas</th><th>Inside special areas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Food waste, comminuted (mesh 25 mm or less)</td><td>More than <b>3 nm</b></td><td>More than <b>12 nm</b></td></tr><tr><td>Food waste, not comminuted</td><td>More than <b>12 nm</b></td><td><b>Prohibited</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cargo residues not harmful to the marine environment</td><td>More than <b>12 nm</b></td><td><b>Prohibited</b> (washwater only, under conditions)</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning agents in deck and hull wash water</td><td>Allowed if not harmful to the marine environment</td><td>Allowed in deck and external wash water</td></tr><tr><td>Animal carcasses</td><td>As far from land as possible, split so they sink</td><td><b>Prohibited</b></td></tr><tr><td>Everything else (plastics, ropes, fishing gear, bags, incinerator ash, cooking oil, paper, glass, metal)</td><td><b>Prohibited</b></td><td><b>Prohibited</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Plastics are never discharged</li><li>Mixed garbage: the stricter rule applies</li><li><b>Fixed and floating platforms (and ships within 500 m):</b> only comminuted food waste may go, and only more than 12 nm out</li><li><b>Antarctic:</b> poultry products may not be discharged unless sterilized</li><li><b>Special areas:</b> Mediterranean, Baltic, Black Sea, Red Sea, Gulfs, North Sea, Antarctic, Wider Caribbean</li></ul><p><b>Placards, plan and book †</b></p><ul><li>Placards for ships 12 m or more</li><li>Garbage Management Plan for ships 100 GT+ or certified for 15 persons or more</li><li>Garbage Record Book for ships 400 GT+ or 15 persons or more. Ships of 100 to 399 GT also from 1 May 2024 (MEPC.360(79)). Kept <b>2 years</b></li><li><b>Part I</b> (all ships): A plastics, B food waste, C domestic waste, D cooking oil, E incinerator ash, F operational waste, G animal carcasses, H fishing gear, I e-waste</li><li><b>Part II</b> (solid bulk cargo ships): J cargo residues (not harmful), K cargo residues (harmful to the marine environment)</li></ul><h2>Annex VI — Air pollution and energy efficiency</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Regulation</th><th>Subject</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>12</td><td>Ozone-depleting substances</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Nitrogen oxides (NOx)</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Sulphur oxides (SOx) and particulate matter</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Volatile organic compounds from tankers</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Shipboard incineration</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Fuel oil availability and quality</td></tr><tr><td>19 to 25</td><td>Energy efficiency, including attained EEDI (Reg 22) and EEXI (Reg 23)</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>SEEMP</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>Fuel oil data collection</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>Operational carbon intensity (CII)</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Certificates:</b> IAPP for ships 400 GT+ on voyages to other Parties' ports. Valid up to 5 years. Annual surveys within 3 months of the anniversary date. Engines above 130 kW also need an <b>EIAPP</b> certificate.</p><p><b>Ozone-depleting substances (Reg 12)</b></p><ul><li>No new installations containing ODS on ships built on or after 19 May 2005</li><li>No new HCFC installations on ships built on or after <b>1 Jan 2020</b></li><li>MEPC 84 noted work on a new rule to stop ODS being reintroduced on ships. It is not adopted</li></ul><p><b>Incineration (Reg 16)</b></p><ul><li>PVC only in an incinerator with an IMO type approval certificate</li><li>No waste fed in when the combustion chamber outlet temperature is below <b>850°C</b></li></ul><h3>Nitrogen oxides (Reg 13)</h3><p>Applies to marine diesel engines <b>above 130 kW</b> (not emergency-only engines).</p><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Ships built</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>On or after 1 Jan 2000, before 1 Jan 2011</td><td>Everywhere</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>On or after <b>1 Jan 2011</b></td><td>Everywhere</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>See the ECA table</td><td>Only inside NOx ECAs</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Sulphur (Reg 14)</h3><ul><li><b>Global limit 0.50%</b> since 1 Jan 2020</li><li><b>Inside SOx ECAs 0.10%</b> since 1 Jan 2015 (an exhaust gas cleaning system is allowed as an alternative)</li><li>Carrying non-compliant fuel has been banned since 1 Mar 2020, except on ships with a scrubber</li></ul><h3>Emission Control Areas</h3><table><thead><tr><th>ECA</th><th>Pollutants</th><th>Key dates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Baltic Sea</td><td>SOx, NOx</td><td>NOx Tier III from 1 Jan 2021</td></tr><tr><td>North Sea</td><td>SOx, NOx</td><td>NOx Tier III from 1 Jan 2021</td></tr><tr><td>North American</td><td>SOx, NOx</td><td>NOx Tier III from 1 Jan 2016</td></tr><tr><td>US Caribbean</td><td>SOx, NOx</td><td>NOx Tier III from 1 Jan 2016</td></tr><tr><td>Mediterranean</td><td>SOx, PM only</td><td>0.10% from <b>1 May 2025</b></td></tr><tr><td>Canadian Arctic</td><td>SOx, PM, NOx</td><td>In force 1 Mar 2026. 0.10% from <b>1 Mar 2027</b>. NOx Tier III for ships with keel laid on or after <b>1 Jan 2025</b>, from 1 Mar 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Norwegian Sea</td><td>SOx, PM, NOx</td><td>In force 1 Mar 2026. 0.10% from <b>1 Mar 2027</b>. NOx Tier III: contract on or after 1 Mar 2026, or keel laid on or after 1 Sep 2026, or delivery on or after 1 Mar 2030</td></tr><tr><td>North East Atlantic</td><td>SOx, PM, NOx</td><td>Adopted at MEPC 84 as <b>MEPC.407(84)</b>. In force <b>1 Sep 2027</b>. 0.10% from <b>1 Sep 2028</b>. NOx Tier III: contract on or after 1 Jan 2027, or keel laid on or after 1 Jul 2027, or delivery on or after 1 Jan 2031</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Canadian Arctic and Norwegian Sea: MEPC.392(82)</li><li>North East Atlantic area: the waters of Portugal, Spain, France, Ireland, Iceland, the Faroe Islands, Greenland and mainland UK, not counting areas already in another ECA. The Madeira, Azores and Canary Islands waters are excluded</li><li>Exemptions in the North East Atlantic: recreational ships under 24 m, ships under 750 kW where Tier III is not feasible, and temporary relief for ships in delivery or repair</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"marpol-ecas\"><figcaption>ECA and sulphur milestones, in date order</figcaption></figure><h3>Fuel oil quality (Reg 18)</h3><ul><li><b>Bunker Delivery Note:</b> kept on board <b>3 years</b> after the fuel is delivered</li><li><b>MARPOL sample:</b> sealed and signed by the supplier's representative and the ship's master or officer. Kept under the ship's control until the fuel is substantially consumed, and at least <b>12 months</b> from delivery</li><li>Suppliers keep a copy of the BDN for 3 years</li></ul><h3>Energy efficiency and carbon intensity</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EEDI</td><td>New ships. Attained index under Reg 22</td></tr><tr><td>EEXI</td><td>Existing ships 400 GT+. Verified at the first survey on or after <b>1 Jan 2023</b></td></tr><tr><td>SEEMP (Reg 26)</td><td>Every ship 400 GT+ keeps one on board. Ships 5,000 GT+ must include a data collection method</td></tr><tr><td>Data collection (Reg 27)</td><td>Ships 5,000 GT+ collect fuel data from calendar year 2019. Report to the Administration within 3 months after year end</td></tr><tr><td>CII (Reg 28)</td><td>Rated <b>A to E</b>. A ship rated <b>D three years in a row, or E once,</b> must make a corrective action plan. A or B ships may get incentives</td></tr><tr><td>Enhanced DCS</td><td>From <b>1 Jan 2026</b> for existing ships 5,000 GT+ that did not start early (MEPC.385(81)). Fuel use split by consumer, underway and not underway. 2026 data to be submitted for verification by <b>31 Mar 2027</b></td></tr><tr><td>SEEMP Part III</td><td>Ships 5,000 GT+ had to update it, with a plan for 2026 to 2028, by <b>31 Dec 2025</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>CII required reduction against the 2019 reference (MEPC.400(83))</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>2027</th><th>2028</th><th>2029</th><th>2030</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>13.625%</td><td>16.250%</td><td>18.875%</td><td>21.500%</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"marpol-cii\"><figcaption>CII rating scale and the 2027 to 2030 reduction path</figcaption></figure><h3>What changed in 2026</h3><ul><li><b>NOx Technical Code 2008:</b> engine certification after substantial modification or a new tier, from <b>1 Sep 2026</b> (MEPC.398(83)). Rules for multiple engine operating profiles, for engine groups certified from <b>1 Jan 2028</b></li><li><b>MEPC 84 (27 Apr to 1 May 2026):</b> adopted Annex VI amendments covering data reporting under Regs 27 and 28, the North East Atlantic ECA, access to the IMO fuel oil consumption database, and a review clause for the short-term GHG measure. Expected in force <b>1 Sep 2027</b></li><li><b>Also at MEPC 84:</b> EEDI guidelines cover methanol and ethanol dual-fuel engines. Voluntary guidelines for measuring methane and nitrous oxide. The 2026 strategy on marine plastic litter keeps the goal of zero plastic discharge from ships by 2030</li><li><b>Still pending, MARPOL Annex I:</b> controlled dewatering of oily bilge water by forced evaporation, for final approval at MEPC 85</li></ul><h3>IMO Net-Zero Framework (not in force)</h3><ul><li>Approved at MEPC 83 (April 2025) as a new chapter of the draft revised Annex VI. Two measures: a fuel GHG intensity standard and a GHG pricing mechanism</li><li><b>Not adopted</b> at the extraordinary session MEPC/ES.2 (14 to 17 Oct 2025), which was adjourned for 12 months</li><li>MEPC 84 made no design decisions and agreed two more intersessional meetings</li><li><b>Dates to watch:</b> intersessional meeting 23 to 27 Nov 2026 · <b>MEPC 85: 30 Nov to 3 Dec 2026</b> · the extraordinary session is due to resume on <b>4 Dec 2026</b>, subject to confirmation by MEPC 85</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Nothing in the Net-Zero Framework applies to ships yet. Check the outcome of MEPC 85 before you tell anyone it does.</blockquote><h2>Records and retention</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"marpol-records\"><figcaption>How long each MARPOL record is kept</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Annex</th><th>Kept</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oil Record Book Part I and II</td><td>I</td><td>3 years</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo Record Book</td><td>II</td><td>3 years</td></tr><tr><td>Garbage Record Book †</td><td>V</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Bunker Delivery Note</td><td>VI</td><td>3 years</td></tr><tr><td>MARPOL fuel sample</td><td>VI</td><td>At least 12 months</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>What a PSC officer looks at</b></p><ul><li>Oil Record Book entries against soundings and the oily water separator</li><li>The 15 ppm alarm and automatic stop</li><li>Garbage logs and the Garbage Management Plan</li><li>Fuel sulphur, BDNs and the changeover log for ECAs</li><li>SEEMP, CII rating and the data collection statement</li><li>A false record or a bypass pipe (\"magic pipe\") is a criminal case in many ports</li></ul><h2>Beyond the convention †</h2><p>These parts were checked on 7 Nov 2026 and were not re-read today.</p><p><b>United States</b></p><ul><li>Party to Annexes I, II, III, V and VI. <b>Not Annex IV</b></li><li>Act to Prevent Pollution from Ships (APPS, 33 USC 1901)</li><li>Sewage is under the Clean Water Act: an approved marine sanitation device (33 CFR 159) and no-discharge zones</li><li>North American ECA: 200 nm from the coast</li><li>Ballast water: the US is not a Party to BWM 2004. It has its own rules (BWM 2004)</li><li>California: distillate fuel within 24 nm, and shore power at berth</li></ul><p><b>European Union</b></p><ul><li>0.10% sulphur at berth in EU ports</li><li>EU ETS for ships 5,000 GT+: allowances for 40% of 2024 emissions, 70% for 2025, 100% from 2026</li><li>FuelEU Maritime from 1 Jan 2025: GHG intensity of energy fuel down 2% in 2025, rising to 80% by 2050</li><li>EU MRV: CO2 monitoring, reporting and verification</li></ul><p><b>China</b></p><ul><li>Coastal Domestic Emission Control Area: 0.50% sulphur since 1 Jan 2019</li><li>Inland rivers: 0.10% since 1 Jan 2020. Hainan waters: 0.10% since 1 Jan 2022</li><li>Scrubber washwater discharge banned in the coastal area, inland rivers and Bohai</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>IMDG Code marine pollutants (Annex III)</li><li>BWM 2004 ballast water</li><li>AFS 2001 anti-fouling</li><li>BWMS Code</li><li>Polar Code discharge limits in polar waters</li><li>OPRC 1990 oil pollution response</li><li>III Code audits of flag, port and coastal States</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Annex II entry-into-force date. Sources disagree (2 Oct 1983 on one, 6 Apr 1987 on others)</li><li>Adoption date of the 1978 Protocol</li><li>The 2021 stricter prewash rules for Category Y in North West European waters</li><li>The vote count at MEPC/ES.2 and any entry-into-force date for the Net-Zero Framework</li><li>Annex VI party tonnage percentage</li><li>CII reduction factors before 2027</li><li>US, EU and China details marked †, beyond the earlier check</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO Status of Treaties 2026, Vol I (30 Sep 2026): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status%202026%20-%20Vol.%20I%20SOLAS%20%26%20MARPOL%20only.pdf</li><li>IMO MEPC 84 summary: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/meetingsummaries/pages/temporary-mepc-84th-session.aspx</li><li>IMO MEPC 83 summary: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/meetingsummaries/pages/mepc-83rd-session.aspx</li><li>Resolution MEPC.392(82): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Environment/Documents/MEPC.392(82).pdf</li><li>Revised MARPOL Annex VI (draft text, IMO Circular Letter 5005): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Documents/Circular%20Letter%20No.5005%20-%20Draft%20Revised%20Marpol%20Annex%20Vi%20%28Secretariat%29.pdf</li><li>Resolution MEPC.200(62), Annex IV: https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/file/MEPC.200%2862%29.pdf</li><li>IMO sewage page, Baltic dates (MEPC.275(69)): https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/Sewage-Default.aspx</li><li>IMO simplified overview of the Annex V discharge rules: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Environment/Documents/Simplified%20overview%20of%20the%20discharge%20provisions%20of%20the%20revised%20MARPOL%20Annex%20V.pdf</li><li>MARPOL Annex II text (MEPC.118(52) as amended): https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a801932ed915d74e622c74f/MEPC_118_52__Annex_II_MARPOL_Amended_14-7-2015.pdf</li><li>Irish S.I. 788/2007 (implements Annex I; its regulation numbers differ from MARPOL's): https://www.irishstatutebook.ie/2007/en/si/0788.html</li><li>DNV, North East Atlantic ECA: https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/north-east-atlantic-joins-the-expanding-network-of-ecas/</li><li>DNV, Canadian Arctic, Norwegian Sea ECAs: https://www.dnv.com/news/2025/new-ecas-for-the-canadian-arctic-norwegian-sea-and-north-east-atlantic-ocean/</li><li>DNV, MEPC 84 and Net-Zero Framework: https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/imo-mepc-84-revisiting-the-net-zero-framework/</li><li>Turk Loydu, MEPC 84 outcomes: https://www.turkloydu.org/en-us/publications/articles/imo-marine-environment-protection-committee-mepc-84-key-regulatory-outcomes/</li><li>Lloyd's Register, CII factors and SEEMP Part III: https://www.lr.org/en/knowledge/class-news/13-25/</li><li>Clyde &amp; Co, Annex VI key developments 2026: https://www.clydeco.com/en/insights/2026/april/marpol-annex-vi-key-developments-for-2026</li><li>REMPEC, Introduction to MARPOL Annex VI: https://www.rempec.org/en/knowledge-centre/online-catalogue/1-introduction-to-marpol-annex-vi.pdf</li><li>DieselNet, IMO marine engine NOx tiers: https://dieselnet.com/standards/inter/imo.php</li><li>Canada, MARPOL page (entry-into-force dates): https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/corporate/international-affairs/partnerships-organizations/preventing-pollution-ships.html</li><li>AMSA, MARPOL amendment table: https://amsa.gov.au/node/964/printable/print</li><li>Earlier sources for the † items (7 Nov 2026): IMO amendments page, AMSA Garbage Record Book, USCG MARPOL page, CARB, American Club, Kuehne+Nagel, DieselNet China</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-nairobi-wrc-2007","v":82754773,"title":"Nairobi WRC 2007","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["nairobi","wreck-removal","wrc","imo","convention","liability"],"body":"<h1>Nairobi WRC 2007 — Nairobi International Convention on the Removal of Wrecks</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>18 May 2007 (Nairobi)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>14 Apr 2015</td></tr><tr><td>Owner liability</td><td>The registered owner pays to locate, mark and remove a hazardous wreck (Art 10)</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Ships ≥ 300 GT flying a Party's flag: compulsory insurance or other financial security, certificate carried on board (Art 12)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the WRC</h2><ul><li>IMO convention giving States a legal basis to remove, or arrange the removal of, wrecks that threaten safety at sea or the marine environment, with uniform rules beyond the territorial sea</li><li>The affected State may remove the wreck at the owner's expense if the owner does not (Art 9)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Open for signature at IMO HQ 19 Nov 2007 – 18 Nov 2008, then accession (Art 17)</li><li>Art 18: in force 12 months after 10 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>Convention area: a Party's exclusive economic zone (or, if none, an area adjacent to the territorial sea extending no more than 200 nm from the baselines) — Art 1</li><li>Territory and territorial sea: only if the Party opts in by notifying the IMO Secretary-General (Art 3(2)); then only Arts 7–9 (locating, marking, removal) apply there</li><li>Ship = seagoing vessel of any type: hydrofoils, air-cushion vehicles, submersibles, floating craft and platforms — except platforms engaged in seabed mineral exploration, exploitation or production</li><li>Not covered (Art 4): measures under the 1969 Intervention Convention and its 1973 Protocol · warships and government non-commercial ships, unless the State decides otherwise</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"wrc-convention-area\"><figcaption>Convention area: the exclusive economic zone (up to 200 nm from the baselines if no EEZ is declared). The territory and territorial sea are covered only if the State opts in (Art 3(2)), and then only Arts 7 to 9 apply. Drawn from the Convention text.</figcaption></figure><h2>Key definitions (Art 1)</h2><ul><li>Wreck: after a maritime casualty — a sunken or stranded ship · any part of a ship · any object lost from a ship that is adrift or stranded · a ship about to sink or strand if effective assistance is not already being given</li><li>Hazard: a condition or threat that impedes navigation, or may reasonably cause major harmful consequences to the marine environment, the coastline or related interests of one or more States</li><li>Related interests: e.g. fisheries, tourism, public health, marine conservation, offshore infrastructure</li><li>Affected State: the State in whose Convention area the wreck lies</li><li>Registered owner: the person registered as owner, or if none the owner at the time of the casualty</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles (21)</h2><ul><li>Art 1 Definitions · Art 2 Objectives and general principles · Art 3 Scope of application · Art 4 Exclusions</li><li>Art 5 Reporting wrecks · Art 6 Determination of hazard · Art 7 Locating wrecks · Art 8 Marking of wrecks</li><li>Art 9 Measures to facilitate the removal of wrecks · Art 10 Liability of the owner · Art 11 Exceptions to liability</li><li>Art 12 Compulsory insurance or other financial security · Art 13 Time limits</li><li>Art 14 Amendment provisions · Art 15 Settlement of disputes · Art 16 Relationship to other conventions and international agreements</li><li>Art 17 Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession · Art 18 Entry into force · Art 19 Denunciation · Art 20 Depositary · Art 21 Languages</li><li>Annex: model certificate of insurance or other financial security</li></ul><h2>Art 2 — Objectives</h2><ul><li>Measures must be proportionate to the hazard, no more than reasonably necessary, and end once the wreck is removed</li><li>The Convention gives no State sovereignty or sovereign rights over the high seas</li></ul><h2>Art 5 — Reporting wrecks</h2><ul><li>The master and the operator of a ship flying the flag of a Party report to the affected State without delay (one report from either satisfies both)</li><li>Report contents:<ul><li>Name and principal place of business of the registered owner</li><li>Precise location of the wreck</li><li>Type, size and construction of the wreck</li><li>Nature of the damage and condition of the wreck</li><li>Nature and quantity of cargo, especially hazardous and noxious substances</li><li>Amount and types of oil on board, including bunker oil and lubricating oil</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Art 6 — Determination of hazard</b> (the affected State considers)</p><ul><li>Type, size and construction of the wreck · water depth · tidal range and currents</li><li>Particularly sensitive sea areas (and areas under UNCLOS Art 211(6) special measures)</li><li>Proximity to shipping routes or traffic lanes · traffic density and frequency · type of traffic</li><li>Type and quantity of cargo and oil, and the damage likely if released</li><li>Vulnerability of port facilities · prevailing weather and hydrographic conditions · submarine topography</li><li>Height of the wreck above or below the lowest astronomical tide</li><li>Acoustic and magnetic profiles of the wreck</li><li>Proximity to offshore installations, pipelines, telecommunications cables and similar structures</li><li>Any other circumstances that might require removal</li></ul><h2>Art 7 — Locating wrecks</h2><ul><li>On learning of a wreck, the affected State urgently uses all practicable means to warn mariners and concerned States</li><li>If it has reason to believe the wreck is a hazard, it takes all practicable steps to establish the exact location</li></ul><h2>Art 8 — Marking wrecks</h2><ul><li>If the wreck is a hazard, the affected State takes all reasonable steps to mark it, using the internationally accepted buoyage system for the area</li><li>It publicizes the marking details, including through nautical publications</li></ul><h2>Art 9 — Measures to facilitate removal</h2><ul><li>The affected State informs the registry State and the registered owner at once, and consults them</li><li>The registered owner must remove a wreck found to be a hazard, and give evidence of insurance or financial security (Art 12)</li><li>The owner may contract any salvor; the State may set conditions only as needed for safety and environmental protection</li><li>The State sets a reasonable deadline, tells the owner in writing, and says it will remove the wreck at the owner's expense if the deadline is missed; it may act immediately if the hazard becomes severe</li><li>If the owner misses the deadline or cannot be contacted, or in an emergency, the State may remove the wreck by the most practical and expeditious means, consistent with safety and the environment</li></ul><h2>Art 10 — Liability of the owner (strict)</h2><ul><li>The registered owner pays the costs of locating, marking and removing the wreck, unless the owner proves the casualty:<ul><li>resulted from an act of war, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, or an exceptional, inevitable and irresistible natural phenomenon</li><li>was wholly caused by an act or omission of a third party done with intent to cause damage</li><li>was wholly caused by the negligence or wrongful act of a government or other authority responsible for the maintenance of lights or other navigational aids</li></ul></li><li>The owner keeps the right to limit liability under national or international regimes (e.g. LLMC 1976)</li><li>Costs can be claimed against the owner only under this Convention · rights of recourse against third parties are kept</li></ul><h2>Art 11 — Exceptions to liability</h2><ul><li>The owner is not liable under this Convention where that would conflict with the applicable liability conventions: the CLC (oil pollution), the 1996 HNS Convention, the Bunker Oil Convention 2001, and nuclear liability conventions or national law</li><li>If the measures count as salvage under national law or another convention, that law or convention governs the salvor's reward</li></ul><h2>Art 12 — Compulsory insurance or other financial security</h2><ul><li>Registered owner of a ship ≥300 GT flying a Party's flag must have insurance or other financial security</li><li>Amount: the limitation amount under LLMC 1976 Art 6(1)(b)</li><li>Certificate: issued by the registry State (for an unregistered ship, any Party may issue it) · carried on board · a copy filed with the authority that keeps the registry</li><li>Certificate shows: ship name and port of registry, gross tonnage, IMO number, owner, type and duration of security, insurer, period of validity</li><li>Cover cannot end before expiry without 3 months' notice to the authorities (unless a replacement certificate is issued or the old one is surrendered)</li><li>Each Party ensures, under its national law, that ships ≥300 GT entering or leaving its ports, or arriving at or leaving its offshore facilities in the territorial sea, have this cover, whatever their flag</li><li>Direct action: claims for costs can be brought directly against the insurer · the insurer may use the owner's defences (including limitation) and wilful misconduct, but not the owner's bankruptcy</li><li>State-owned ships: no insurance needed; they carry a certificate from the registry State</li></ul><h2>Art 13 — Time limits</h2><ul><li>Right to recover costs lapses unless action is brought within 3 years of the date the hazard was determined</li><li>Never later than 6 years from the date of the casualty (for a series of occurrences, from the first one)</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Onboard: after a casualty resulting in a wreck, the master and operator report to the affected State without delay (owner, precise position, damage, cargo, oil on board)</blockquote><blockquote>Keep the wreck removal insurance certificate valid and on board (≥300 GT)</blockquote><blockquote>The owner pays for locating, marking and removal; the State may remove the wreck itself if the deadline is missed or in an emergency, at the owner's expense</blockquote><blockquote>The owner may still limit liability (e.g. LLMC 1976)</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Treaty text (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-04/imo_removal_wrecks.html</li><li>IMO — Nairobi International Convention on the Removal of Wrecks: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Nairobi-International-Convention-on-the-Removal-of-Wrecks.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-nuclear-1971","v":237484133,"title":"Nuclear 1971","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["nuclear","liability","nuclear-material","paris-convention","vienna-convention","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Nuclear 1971 — Convention relating to Civil Liability in the Field of Maritime Carriage of Nuclear Material</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>17 Dec 1971, Brussels</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>15 Jul 1975</b></td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>18 States, 20.58% of world tonnage (IMO treaty table, printed 5 Sep 2023)</td></tr><tr><td>Convened by</td><td>IMO, with the IAEA and the OECD's European Nuclear Energy Agency</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Settle overlaps between maritime liability conventions and nuclear liability conventions, so the <b>nuclear installation operator</b> alone is liable for damage from a nuclear incident during maritime carriage</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>90th day after 5 States sign without reservation or deposit instruments (Art 6)</td></tr><tr><td>Depositary</td><td>IMO Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>Languages</td><td>English and French equally authentic; Russian and Spanish official translations</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"nuclear-flow\"><figcaption>Who is liable for nuclear damage at sea</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> If the nuclear installation operator is liable for the damage under the Paris Convention, the Vienna Convention or equivalent national law, no one else, such as the shipowner or carrier, is liable under maritime or other law.</blockquote><h2>What is Nuclear 1971</h2><ul><li>Nuclear damage during a sea voyage could fall under maritime conventions covering shipowners and also under conventions making the operators of the nuclear installations involved liable. The two systems could conflict (IMO).</li><li>The preamble recalls the <b>Paris Convention</b> (29 Jul 1960, with its 1964 Additional Protocol) and the <b>Vienna Convention</b> (21 May 1963)</li><li>The Convention contains no definitions of \"nuclear incident\" or \"nuclear material\" of its own in the operative articles checked. Those concepts come from the nuclear liability conventions it refers to.</li></ul><h2>Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preamble</td><td>Recalls Paris 1960 and Vienna 1963. Aims to ensure that the operator \"will be exclusively liable\" for damage from a nuclear incident in maritime carriage.</td></tr><tr><td><b>1 Exoneration</b></td><td>A person who might otherwise be liable under maritime or national law is <b>exonerated</b> if: (a) the operator of the nuclear installation is liable under the Paris or Vienna Convention, <b>or</b> (b) the operator is liable under national law that is \"in all respects as favourable\" to injured persons as either convention</td></tr><tr><td><b>2 Scope of exoneration</b></td><td>(1) Extends to damage to the nuclear installation, to property on its site, and to the means of transport carrying the material, where the operator's liability for them is excluded under those conventions · (2) liability of an individual who <b>caused damage intentionally</b> is preserved</td></tr><tr><td><b>3 Nuclear ships</b></td><td>Does not affect the liability of the operator of a <b>nuclear ship</b> for incidents involving nuclear fuel, products or waste on that ship</td></tr><tr><td><b>4 Conflicts</b></td><td>Supersedes earlier maritime conventions only to the extent of conflict. Obligations to non-Contracting States under those conventions are unaffected.</td></tr><tr><td>5 Signature and accession</td><td>Open for signature in Brussels, then in London at the IMCO headquarters until 31 Dec 1972, then accession. Open to UN members, members of specialized agencies or the IAEA, and parties to the ICJ Statute.</td></tr><tr><td>6 Entry into force</td><td>90th day after 5 States have signed without reservation or deposited instruments · for later States, 90 days after signature or deposit</td></tr><tr><td>7 Denunciation</td><td>At any time after entry into force for that State, effective one year after receipt (or longer if stated) · the Convention still applies to incidents before denunciation takes effect</td></tr><tr><td>8 Territories</td><td>Extension to territories by notice, withdrawable with one year's notice</td></tr><tr><td>9 Revision</td><td>IMO may convene a revision conference, and must on request of at least one-third of Contracting Parties</td></tr><tr><td>10 Reservations</td><td>Allowed if they correspond to those validly made under the Paris or Vienna Convention</td></tr><tr><td>11 Depositary</td><td>Notifications, certified copies, registration with the UN under Art 102 of the UN Charter</td></tr><tr><td>12 Languages</td><td>English and French; Russian and Spanish translations prepared by the Secretariat</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How to read it in practice</h2><p>1. Identify the nuclear installation operator liable under Paris, Vienna or equivalent national law for the carriage.</p><p>2. If that operator is liable for the nuclear damage, the shipowner or carrier cannot be held liable for it (Art 1).</p><p>3. The exoneration also covers damage to the nuclear installation and its site property, and to the means of transport (Art 2(1)).</p><p>4. It does <b>not</b> protect a person who caused the damage <b>intentionally</b> (Art 2(2)), and it does <b>not</b> change the position of a <b>nuclear ship</b> operator for its own fuel, products or waste (Art 3).</p><h2>Related conventions in this collection</h2><ul><li>CLC and Fund 1992: liability of oil tanker owners</li><li>HNS 2010: liability for hazardous and noxious substances carried by sea</li><li>LLMC 1976: limitation of liability for maritime claims</li><li>Athens 2002: carriage of passengers and luggage</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The definitions of \"nuclear incident\" and \"nuclear material\" and the limits of operator liability under Paris 1960 and Vienna 1963</li><li>The list of the 18 Parties, and any ratification after the IMO table (printed 5 Sep 2023)</li><li>Interaction with the 1997 Vienna Convention amendments and other nuclear liability regimes</li><li>Whether any State has used the Art 10 reservations</li><li>The exact wording of Arts 5–12 beyond the paraphrases above</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>NUCLEAR 1971 text (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-07/nuclear-convention.html</li><li>IMO — NUCLEAR Convention: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Convention-relating-to-Civil-Liability-in-the-Field-of-Maritime-Carriage-of-Nuclear-Material-(NUCLEAR).aspx</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (printed 5 Sep 2023): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status of IMO Treaties.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-oprc-1990","v":117579828,"title":"OPRC 1990","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["oprc","oprc-hns","oil-pollution","sopep","smpep","contingency","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>OPRC 1990 — International Convention on Oil Pollution Preparedness, Response and Co-operation (and the OPRC-HNS Protocol 2000)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>OPRC 1990</th><th>OPRC-HNS Protocol 2000</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>30 Nov 1990, London</td><td>15 Mar 2000, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>13 May 1995</td><td>14 Jun 2007</td></tr><tr><td>Covers</td><td>Oil pollution incidents</td><td>Hazardous and noxious substances (anything other than oil likely to harm health, living resources, amenities or other legitimate uses of the sea)</td></tr><tr><td>In force rule</td><td>12 months after 15 States (Art 16)</td><td>12 months after 15 States (Art 15)</td></tr><tr><td>Ship plan</td><td>SOPEP (MARPOL Annex I Reg 37)</td><td>Plan under MARPOL Annex II Reg 17; combined plan = SMPEP</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is OPRC</h2><ul><li>A global framework for international co-operation in preparing for and responding to major oil pollution incidents or threats</li><li>Started with a July 1989 conference of leading industrial nations in Paris, which asked IMO to develop further measures; the IMO Assembly endorsed this in Nov 1989 (IMO)</li><li>Parties: take all appropriate measures to prepare for and respond to oil pollution incidents (Art 1(1))</li><li>Annex is an integral part of the Convention (Art 1(2))</li><li>Warships, naval auxiliaries and other State ships on non-commercial service are excluded; Parties still ensure they act consistently where reasonable (Art 1(3))</li><li>IMO has an important coordinating role</li></ul><h2>Definitions (Art 2)</h2><ul><li><b>Oil</b>: petroleum in any form, including crude oil, fuel oil, sludge, oil refuse and refined products</li><li><b>Oil pollution incident</b>: an occurrence or series of occurrences with the same origin that results or may result in a discharge of oil, threatens the marine environment or the coastline or related interests of one or more States, and needs emergency or other immediate response</li><li><b>Ship</b>: any vessel operating in the marine environment, including hydrofoils, air-cushion vehicles, submersibles and floating craft</li><li><b>Offshore unit</b>: fixed or floating installation engaged in oil or gas exploration, exploitation or production, or loading or unloading of oil</li><li><b>Sea ports and oil handling facilities</b>: those that present a risk of an oil pollution incident, including ports, oil terminals and pipelines</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles</h2><p><b>Art 3 — Oil pollution emergency plans</b></p><ul><li>3(1)(a): ships flying a Party's flag carry a shipboard oil pollution emergency plan, as required by IMO</li><li>3(1)(b): such a ship may be inspected in a port or offshore terminal under a Party's jurisdiction</li><li>3(2): offshore unit operators under a Party's jurisdiction have plans coordinated with the national system (Art 6) and approved by the national authority</li><li>3(3): ports and oil handling facilities have plans or similar arrangements coordinated with the national system</li></ul><p><b>Art 4 — Reporting</b></p><ul><li>Masters or persons in charge of ships and offshore units report without delay any event involving a discharge or probable discharge of oil — ships to the nearest coastal State; offshore units to the coastal State they are under</li><li>Same duty for any observed event at sea involving a discharge or the presence of oil</li><li>Persons in charge of ports and oil handling facilities report to the competent national authority</li><li>Maritime inspection vessels and aircraft are instructed to report; civil aircraft pilots are requested to report to the nearest coastal State</li><li>Reports follow IMO guidelines</li></ul><p><b>Art 5 — Action on receiving a report</b></p><ul><li>The Party assesses whether an incident has occurred, and its nature, extent and consequences, then promptly informs affected or likely affected States</li><li>Where severity justifies, informs IMO directly or via regional bodies</li></ul><p><b>Art 6 — National and regional systems</b></p><ul><li>Each Party establishes a national response system, with: designated competent authority · national operational contact point · an authority entitled to request or grant assistance · a <b>national contingency plan</b></li><li>Within its capabilities: pre-positioned oil spill combating equipment · programme of exercises and training · detailed response plans and communications · mechanism to coordinate response</li><li>Keeps IMO informed of contact points, equipment, expertise and the national contingency plan</li></ul><p><b>Other articles</b></p><ul><li>Art 7 International co-operation: advice, technical support and equipment on request (subject to capabilities); costs follow the Annex; Parties facilitate entry, use and departure of response ships, aircraft, personnel and equipment</li><li>Art 8 Research and development · Art 9 Technical co-operation · Art 10 Bilateral and multilateral co-operation · Art 11 Relation to other conventions</li><li>Art 12 Institutional arrangements: IMO provides information services, education and training, technical services and technical assistance</li><li>Art 13 Evaluation · Art 14 Amendments (two-thirds of Parties accept; in force six months later for those that accepted)</li><li>Art 15 Signature: 30 Nov 1990 – 29 Nov 1991, then accession</li><li>Art 16 Entry into force: 12 months after 15 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Art 17 Denunciation after five years; takes effect 12 months after notice · Art 19: done at London 30 Nov 1990</li></ul><p><b>Annex — reimbursement of costs of assistance</b></p><ul><li>Without a prior agreement: if a Party acts at another Party's express request, the requester reimburses the assisting Party (it may cancel but pays costs already incurred); if a Party acts on its own initiative, it bears its own costs</li><li>Reimbursable costs are calculated under the assisting Party's law and practice, unless agreed otherwise</li><li>The requesting Party may ask for waivers, reductions or postponements, with regard to developing countries</li><li>Does not prejudice recovery of costs from third parties under other laws (CLC 1969, FUND 1971)</li></ul><hr><h2>Shipboard plans (SOPEP / SMPEP)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Rule</th><th>Ships</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SOPEP</td><td>MARPOL Annex I Reg 37</td><td>Oil tankers of 150 GT and above; all other ships of 400 GT and above</td></tr><tr><td>Marine pollution plan for noxious liquid substances</td><td>MARPOL Annex II Reg 17</td><td>Ships of 150 GT and above certified to carry noxious liquid substances in bulk</td></tr><tr><td>SMPEP</td><td>Combined SOPEP + NLS plan</td><td>Practical, because the two largely overlap</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Plans must be approved</li><li>The plan carries, as an appendix, the <b>List of National Operational Contact Points</b> — the agencies or officials that receive and process incident reports</li><li>IMO Guidelines for the Development of Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plans (2010 edition): SOPEP guidelines — MEPC.54(32) as amended by MEPC.86(44) · SMPEP guidelines — MEPC.85(44) as amended by MEPC.137(53)</li></ul><h2>OPRC-HNS Protocol 2000</h2><ul><li>Open for signature 15 Mar 2000 – 14 Mar 2001, then accession; only OPRC Parties may join (Art 13)</li><li>In force 12 months after 15 States; for later States, three months after deposit (Art 15)</li><li>Art 1: same duties, for HNS pollution incidents; warships and non-commercial State ships excluded</li><li>Art 2: HNS = any substance other than oil which, if introduced into the marine environment, is likely to create hazards to human health, harm living resources, damage amenities or interfere with other legitimate uses of the sea</li><li>Art 3: ships carry an on-board pollution incident emergency plan; masters follow reporting procedures; ports and HNS handling facilities have plans coordinated with the national system; a Party that learns of an incident notifies States likely to be affected. Floating production and storage units (FPSO, FSU) are not covered by this article</li><li>Art 4: national and regional systems (authorities, contact points, national contingency plan, equipment, exercises, training)</li><li>Art 5: international co-operation · Art 6 research · Art 7 technical co-operation · Art 8 bilateral/multilateral agreements</li><li>Denouncing the OPRC Convention also denounces the Protocol (Art 16(4))</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>SOPEP and, where certified for noxious liquids, a marine pollution plan (SMPEP) must be approved and on board — check the ship-size thresholds above</blockquote><blockquote>Report any discharge or sighting of oil at sea to the nearest coastal State without delay</blockquote><blockquote>Keep the list of national operational contact points up to date</blockquote><blockquote>A port State may inspect the shipboard plan (Art 3(1)(b))</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>OPRC Convention text (Univ. of Oslo): https://www.jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-05/convention-oil-pollution.xml</li><li>OPRC-HNS Protocol text (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-04/co-operation-pollution-incidents.html</li><li>IMO — OPRC: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-oil-pollution-preparedness,-response-and-co-operation-(oprc).aspx</li><li>IMO — Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plans: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/Shipboard-Marine-Pollution-Emergency-Plans.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-oprc-hns-2000","v":185941575,"title":"OPRC-HNS 2000","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["oprc-hns","hns","pollution","emergency-plan","response","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>OPRC-HNS 2000 — Protocol on Preparedness, Response and Co-operation to Pollution Incidents by Hazardous and Noxious Substances</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>15 Mar 2000</b>, London (Diplomatic Conference at IMO)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>14 Jun 2007</b></td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>43 (as of 2 Sep 2024, NUS Centre for International Law database)</td></tr><tr><td>Parent treaty</td><td>OPRC 1990. Only OPRC Parties can join.</td></tr><tr><td>Covers</td><td>Pollution incidents by hazardous and noxious substances (HNS), meaning substances <b>other than oil</b></td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>12 months after at least <b>15 States</b> sign without reservation or deposit instruments (Art 15)</td></tr><tr><td>Denunciation</td><td>After 5 years from entry into force for that Party, effective 12 months after notice (Art 16)</td></tr><tr><td>Depositary</td><td>IMO Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>Languages</td><td>Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian, Spanish, all equally authentic</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"oprchns-system\"><figcaption>Three layers of preparedness under the Protocol</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The Protocol does for HNS incidents what OPRC 1990 does for oil: ships carry an emergency plan, States have a national response system, and Parties help each other. It deals with <b>preparedness and response</b>, not liability. Liability and compensation are in HNS 2010.</blockquote><h2>Definitions (Art 2)</h2><ul><li><b>Pollution incident:</b> an occurrence or series of occurrences with a common origin, including fire or explosion, that releases or may release HNS, threatens the marine environment or a State's coastline or related interests, and needs emergency or other immediate response</li><li><b>HNS:</b> any substance <b>other than oil</b> which, if introduced into the marine environment, is likely to create hazards to human health, harm living resources and marine life, damage amenities or interfere with other legitimate uses of the sea</li><li><b>Sea ports and HNS handling facilities:</b> those where HNS are loaded onto or unloaded from ships</li><li>The HNS definition here differs from the one used in the HNS Convention on liability (IMO)</li></ul><h2>Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>1 General</b></td><td>Parties take suitable measures, alone or jointly, to prepare for and respond to HNS incidents. The Annex is part of the Protocol. <b>Warships and state ships</b> on non-commercial service are excluded, but Parties must act consistently with the Protocol where reasonable and practicable.</td></tr><tr><td>2 Definitions</td><td>See above</td></tr><tr><td><b>3 Emergency plans and reporting</b></td><td>Each Party requires <b>ships flying its flag</b> to carry a pollution incident emergency plan and follow reporting procedures. Offshore units (FPSOs, FSUs) are covered by national rules or company systems and are excluded. Port and HNS facility operators have emergency plans coordinated with the national system and approved by the competent authority. When an incident becomes known, States likely to be affected are notified.</td></tr><tr><td><b>4 National and regional systems</b></td><td>National response system with a competent authority, a national operational contact point, and an authority able to request or decide on assistance · a national contingency plan setting out the roles of public and private bodies · within capabilities: pre-positioned equipment, exercises and training, communications, coordination mechanism · information to IMO on contacts, equipment, expertise and the plan</td></tr><tr><td><b>5 International co-operation</b></td><td>On request, and where the severity justifies it, Parties provide advice, technical support and equipment, subject to their capabilities. Costs follow the Annex. The requesting Party may ask IMO to help find provisional financing. Parties <b>facilitate the entry, use and departure</b> of ships, aircraft, personnel, cargo and equipment involved in the response.</td></tr><tr><td>6 Research and development</td><td>Cooperation on surveillance, containment, recovery, dispersal, clean-up and restoration. Links between research bodies, international symposia, compatible standards.</td></tr><tr><td>7 Technical co-operation</td><td>Training of personnel, access to technology and facilities, other preparedness measures, joint research, technology transfer subject to national law</td></tr><tr><td>8 Bilateral and multilateral agreements</td><td>Parties endeavour to conclude them and send copies to IMO</td></tr><tr><td>9 Relation to other agreements</td><td>No change to rights and obligations under other conventions or agreements</td></tr><tr><td>10 Institutional arrangements</td><td>IMO, subject to its consent and resources, provides information services, education and training, technical services and technical assistance, with attention to developing countries</td></tr><tr><td>11 Evaluation</td><td>Parties evaluate the Protocol's effectiveness within IMO</td></tr><tr><td>12 Amendments</td><td>Via MEPC (proposals circulated at least 6 months before, two-thirds vote of Parties present and voting; articles and Annex take effect 6 months after two-thirds acceptance; appendices by tacit acceptance after at least 10 months unless one-third object) or via a Conference of Parties (needs request of one Party backed by at least one-third)</td></tr><tr><td>13 Signature and accession</td><td>Open at IMO from 15 Mar 2000 to 14 Mar 2001, then accession. Only OPRC Parties may join.</td></tr><tr><td>14 Multiple legal systems</td><td>A State with several territorial units may declare which the Protocol covers</td></tr><tr><td>15 Entry into force</td><td>12 months after 15 States · later deposits: later of entry into force or 3 months after deposit · after entry into force: 3 months after deposit</td></tr><tr><td>16 Denunciation</td><td>After 5 years, 12 months' notice. <b>Denouncing OPRC 1990 automatically denounces the Protocol.</b></td></tr><tr><td>17 Depositary</td><td>Notifications, registration with the UN under Art 102</td></tr><tr><td>18 Languages</td><td>Six authentic languages</td></tr><tr><td><b>Annex</b></td><td>Reimbursement of costs</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Annex: who pays for assistance</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"oprchns-costs\"><figcaption>Cost reimbursement under the Annex</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Default: each Party bears its own response costs, unless a bilateral or multilateral financial agreement exists</li><li>Assistance given at another Party's <b>express request</b>: the requesting Party reimburses the assisting Party. The request may be cancelled, but costs already incurred or committed are still paid.</li><li>Assistance on the assisting Party's <b>own initiative</b>: it bears the cost</li><li>Reimbursable costs are calculated fairly under the assisting Party's law and practice, unless agreed otherwise</li><li>The requesting Party may ask for a waiver, reduction or deferral, and developing countries' needs are to be considered</li><li>Nothing stops a Party recovering costs from third parties under other law</li></ul><h2>What it means on board</h2><ul><li>Under Art 3 your flag State requires a pollution incident <b>emergency plan</b> and <b>reporting procedures</b> that fit the IMO conventions in force for that State</li><li>The Protocol text above does not itself list the plan's contents. See the Not included section.</li></ul><h2>Related conventions in this collection</h2><ul><li>OPRC 1990: parent convention for oil pollution</li><li>HNS 2010: liability and compensation for HNS</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The required contents of the shipboard HNS emergency plan, which come from IMO guidelines and other conventions rather than the Protocol articles</li><li>Names of the 43 Parties and any change after 2 Sep 2024</li><li>IMO guidance documents and manuals for HNS response</li><li>Entry-into-force dates for individual States</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Protocol text (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-04/co-operation-pollution-incidents.html</li><li>Protocol text (Norwegian government, Prop. 133 S 2010–2011): https://www.regjeringen.no/no/dokumenter/prop-133-s-20102011/id648879/?ch=8</li><li>IMO — OPRC-HNS Protocol: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Protocol-on-Preparedness,-Response-and-Co-operation-to-pollution-Incidents-by-Hazardous-and-Noxious-Substances-(OPRC-HNS-Pr.aspx</li><li>NUS CIL — OPRC-HNS 2000 (parties, Art 15): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/databasecil/2000-protocol-on-preparedness-response-and-co-operation-to-pollution-incidents-by-hazardous-and-noxious-substances</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-sar-1979","v":13223082,"title":"SAR 1979","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["sar","search-and-rescue","rcc","iamsar","place-of-safety","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>SAR 1979 — International Convention on Maritime Search and Rescue</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>27 Apr 1979, Hamburg</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>22 Jun 1985</td></tr><tr><td>Revised Annex</td><td>Adopted May 1998 · in force Jan 2000</td></tr><tr><td>2004 amendments</td><td>MSC.155(78), adopted 20 May 2004 · in force 1 Jul 2006</td></tr><tr><td>Global plan</td><td>13 SAR areas</td></tr><tr><td>Manual</td><td>IAMSAR Manual (IMO and ICAO), 3 volumes</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is SAR</h2><ul><li>An international SAR plan, so that a rescue anywhere at sea is coordinated by a SAR organization, helped by neighbouring organizations when needed (IMO)</li><li>Countries delimit their own SAR regions inside the 13 SAR areas set by IMO's Maritime Safety Committee</li><li>Art I: Parties adopt all measures needed to give full effect to the Convention and its Annex</li><li>The Convention has 8 Articles: I General obligations · II Other treaties and interpretation · III Amendments · IV Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession · V Entry into force · VI Denunciation · VII Deposit and registration · VIII Languages</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li><b>1998 revised Annex</b> (adopted May 1998, in force Jan 2000): clearer government responsibilities, regional approach, better co-ordination between maritime and aeronautical SAR; RCCs to run 24 hours a day with English-speaking staff; encourages immediate entry of other States' rescue units into territorial waters for SAR; operations continue \"until all reasonable hope of rescuing survivors has passed\"</li><li><b>2004 amendments</b> (MSC.155(78)): adopted 20 May 2004 · deemed accepted 1 Jan 2006 unless more than one third of Parties objected · in force <b>1 Jul 2006</b></li><li>Global plan: provisional plans for all 13 areas completed when the Indian Ocean plans were finalized at a conference in Fremantle, Western Australia, in Sep 1998</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex — Chapter 1: Terms (1.3)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Search (1.3.1)</td><td>An operation, normally co-ordinated by an RCC or sub-centre, to locate persons in distress</td></tr><tr><td>Rescue (1.3.2)</td><td>An operation to retrieve persons in distress and <b>deliver them to a place of safety</b></td></tr><tr><td>SAR region (1.3.4)</td><td>An area of defined dimensions associated with an RCC</td></tr><tr><td>RCC (1.3.5)</td><td>A unit responsible for promoting efficient organization of SAR services</td></tr><tr><td>RSC (1.3.6)</td><td>A unit subordinate to an RCC</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty phase (1.3.11)</td><td>Uncertainty exists as to the safety of a person, vessel or other craft</td></tr><tr><td>Alert phase (1.3.12)</td><td>Apprehension exists as to the safety of a person, vessel or other craft</td></tr><tr><td>Distress phase (1.3.13)</td><td>Reasonable certainty that a person, vessel or other craft is threatened by grave and imminent danger</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter 2 — Organization and co-ordination</h2><ul><li>2.1.1: Parties ensure assistance is rendered to any person in distress at sea</li><li>2.1.2: Parties establish basic service elements — legal framework, responsible authority, resources, communications, operations, improvement processes</li><li>2.1.3–2.1.7: SAR regions, preferably contiguous and not overlapping, are set <b>by agreement among the Parties concerned</b>; delimitation does not prejudice State boundaries</li><li>2.1.10: assistance is provided to any person in distress at sea, regardless of nationality, status or the circumstances</li><li>2.3: RCCs operate on a <b>24-hour basis</b> with trained personnel</li><li>2.4: closest practicable co-ordination with aeronautical services; joint centres where practicable</li><li>2.5: Parties designate facilities able to take part, as SAR units</li></ul><h2>Chapter 3 — Co-operation between States</h2><ul><li>3.1.1: Parties co-ordinate their SAR organizations, and with neighbouring States where necessary</li><li>3.1.2: unless agreed otherwise, a Party should authorize immediate entry of other Parties' rescue units into its territory or territorial sea</li><li>3.1.3–3.1.4: the requesting Party sends a request with full details to the other Party's RCC; the receiving authority acknowledges promptly and states conditions</li><li>3.1.5: agreements with neighbouring States on entry conditions</li><li>3.1.6: RCCs may request assistance and grant entry permissions</li></ul><h2>Chapter 4 — Operating procedures</h2><ul><li>4.1 Preparatory measures: up-to-date facility data, vessel position data, operational plans, readiness</li><li>4.2 Information concerning emergencies: 24-hour capability to receive distress alerts</li><li>4.3 Initial action: a unit receiving distress information acts at once if it can help, and notifies the RCC or RSC without delay</li><li>4.4 Emergency phases: 4.4.1 uncertainty · 4.4.2 alert · 4.4.3 distress</li><li>4.5 Procedures during the phases (including unknown position)</li><li>4.6 Co-ordination when two or more Parties are involved</li><li>4.7 On-scene co-ordination: \"the most capable person\" is designated the on-scene co-ordinator</li><li>4.8 Termination and suspension: operations continue, when practicable, until all reasonable hope of rescuing survivors has passed</li></ul><h2>Chapter 5 — Ship reporting systems</h2><ul><li>5.1 General · 5.2 Operational requirements · 5.3 Types of reports: <b>sailing plan, position report, final report</b> · 5.4 Use of the systems</li></ul><hr><h2>2004 amendments (in force 1 Jul 2006) — place of safety</h2><ul><li><b>2.1.1</b>: adds a definition of \"person in distress at sea\" — includes people in need of assistance who reached a remote coast in an area that rescue facilities can reach only as the Annex provides</li><li><b>3.1.6.4</b> (new): RCCs co-operate to identify the most appropriate place(s) for disembarking people found in distress</li><li><b>3.1.9</b> (new): Parties co-ordinate and co-operate so that masters who provide assistance by embarking persons in distress at sea are released from their obligations with <b>minimum further deviation</b> from the voyage, provided this does not further endanger life at sea; the <b>Party responsible for the SAR region</b> in which the assistance is rendered has <b>primary responsibility</b> for this</li><li><b>4.8.5</b> (new): the RCC or RSC starts the process of identifying the most appropriate place(s) for disembarking persons found in distress at sea, and informs the ship and other parties concerned</li></ul><h2>IAMSAR Manual</h2><ul><li>Developed jointly by IMO and ICAO; three volumes: <b>Organization and Management · Mission Co-ordination · Mobile Facilities</b></li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The State responsible for the SAR region has primary responsibility to arrange the place of safety; the master is released with minimum further deviation</blockquote><blockquote>A rescue means delivery to a place of safety (1.3.2)</blockquote><blockquote>Phases: uncertainty → alert → distress</blockquote><blockquote>Operations continue until all reasonable hope of rescuing survivors has passed</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>SAR Convention text, as amended: https://sosmediterranee.org/app/uploads/2023/10/sar-convention-1979.pdf</li><li>IMO — SAR Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-maritime-search-and-rescue-(sar).aspx</li><li>Resolution MSC.155(78), text and dates (Norwegian Parliament proposition): https://www.regjeringen.no/no/dokumenter/stprp-nr-27-2005-2006-/id211703/?ch=10</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-solas-1974","v":148339963,"title":"SOLAS 1974","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["solas","imo","convention","core","safety","certificates","psc"],"body":"<h1>SOLAS 1974 — International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>1 Nov 1974</b></td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>25 May 1980</b></td></tr><tr><td>1978 Protocol</td><td>Tanker safety. In force <b>1 May 1981</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>1988 Protocol</td><td>Harmonized System of Survey and Certification (HSSC). In force <b>3 Feb 2000</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>Amendments</td><td><b>Tacit acceptance</b>: an amendment takes effect on a set date unless enough Parties object first</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>168 States, 98.91% of world tonnage (IMO treaty table printed <b>5 Sep 2023</b>)</td></tr><tr><td>1978 Protocol parties</td><td>123 States, 97.59% · <b>1988 Protocol:</b> 128 States, 97.91% (same table)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Articles plus an Annex of <b>17 chapter headings</b> (I to XV, with II-1, II-2, XI-1 and XI-2)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"solas-chapters\"><figcaption>The SOLAS chapters</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The Chapter is the rule. The Code is the detailed standard behind it (LSA, FSS, IBC, IGC and others). PSC and MARINA focus on certificates (I), drills and LSA (III), GMDSS (IV), navigation equipment and voyage planning (V), ISM (IX) and ISPS (XI-2).</blockquote><h2>What SOLAS is</h2><ul><li>The main IMO convention for the safety of merchant ships: minimum standards for <b>construction, equipment and operation</b></li><li>The <b>flag State certifies</b>. <b>Port State Control</b> inspects.</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>1912 Titanic led to SOLAS <b>1914</b> (never in force, because of WWI)</li><li>Later versions: 1929, 1948, <b>1960</b> (the first under IMO, then IMCO)</li><li><b>1974:</b> the current Convention, with tacit acceptance of amendments (protocols: see At a glance)</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>International voyages</li><li><b>Passenger ships:</b> more than 12 passengers</li><li><b>Cargo ships:</b> 500 GT and above. Chapter IV and parts of Chapter V apply from 300 GT.</li><li><b>Not covered (I/3):</b> warships and troopships, cargo ships under 500 GT, non-mechanically propelled ships, primitive wooden ships, pleasure yachts not in trade, fishing vessels</li></ul><h2>Surveys and certificates (Chapter I and the HSSC)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"solas-surveys\"><figcaption>Survey windows and certificate validity</figcaption></figure><h3>Survey windows (HSSC Survey Guidelines 2025)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Survey</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Initial</td><td>Before the ship enters service, or when a new instrument first applies</td></tr><tr><td><b>Annual</b></td><td>Within <b>3 months before or after</b> each anniversary date</td></tr><tr><td><b>Intermediate</b></td><td>Within 3 months before or after the <b>2nd or 3rd</b> anniversary. It replaces one annual survey.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Periodical</b> (equipment)</td><td>Within 3 months before or after the 2nd or 3rd anniversary. Radio: each anniversary.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Renewal</b></td><td>May be completed up to <b>3 months before expiry</b> with no loss of validity. The construction renewal may start at the 4th annual survey and finish by the 5th anniversary.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Outside of bottom</b></td><td>At least <b>2 inspections in any 5 years</b>, never more than <b>36 months</b> apart. One is with the renewal. Passenger ships: 2 dry-dockings in 5 years, 36 months apart. A non-ro-ro passenger ship may be accepted with one, at 60 months.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Certificate validity</h3><ul><li><b>Cargo ship certificates:</b> up to <b>5 years</b></li><li><b>Passenger Ship Safety Certificate:</b> up to <b>12 months</b></li><li><b>Extension</b> to reach the survey port: <b>not more than 3 months</b>, and <b>1 month</b> for ships on short voyages (short voyage = neither the distance to the final destination nor the return exceeds 1,000 miles)</li><li>A certificate ceases to be valid if a required survey is not carried out in time. Validity is restored by carrying out the missed survey.</li><li>Certificates: Passenger Ship Safety · Cargo Ship Safety Construction, Equipment and Radio (or one Cargo Ship Safety Certificate) · Polar Ship Certificate · Industrial Personnel Safety Certificate (with the related convention certificates, such as Load Line, IOPP and IBWM)</li></ul><h3>Chapter I regulations</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Part A</b></td><td>1 Application · 2 Definitions (passenger ship, international voyage, new and existing ship) · 3 Exceptions · 4 Exemptions (Exemption Certificate) · 5 Equivalents (reported to IMO)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Part B</b></td><td>6 Inspection and survey · 7 Passenger ships · 8 Cargo ship LSA and equipment · 9 Cargo ship radio · 10 Cargo ship structure and machinery · 11 Maintenance after survey · 12 Issue of certificates · 13 Issue by another Government · 14 Duration and validity · 15 Forms · 16 Availability on board · 17 Acceptance · 18 Qualification · 19 <b>Control (PSC)</b> · 20 Privileges</td></tr><tr><td><b>Part C</b></td><td>21 Casualties: flag State investigates and reports to IMO</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Reg 19 (PSC):</b> check valid certificates. Clear grounds lead to a detailed inspection and possible detention.</li><li><b>Non-Party ships</b> get no more favourable treatment (1988 Protocol Art I(3)). PSC inspects them to the same SOLAS standard.</li></ul><h2>Chapter II-1 — Construction: structure, subdivision, stability, machinery, electrical</h2><p>*Applies to passenger ships and cargo ships of 500 GT and above, mostly new ships.*</p><h3>Part A-1 — Structure</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3-1</td><td>Structure per class society rules or equivalent</td></tr><tr><td>3-2</td><td><b>PSPC</b> protective coatings: ballast tanks 500 GT+ and double-side skin bulk carriers 150 m+ (MSC.215(82))</td></tr><tr><td>3-3</td><td>Safe access to tanker bows</td></tr><tr><td>3-4</td><td>Emergency towing: tankers <b>20,000 dwt+</b> at both ends · towing procedure for all ships (MSC.256(84))</td></tr><tr><td>3-5</td><td><b>Asbestos:</b> new installation banned (1 Jan 2011)</td></tr><tr><td>3-6</td><td>Access to cargo area spaces: oil tankers 500 GT+, bulk carriers 20,000 GT+ (MSC.133(76)) · Ship Structure Access Manual</td></tr><tr><td>3-7</td><td>Construction drawings kept on board and ashore</td></tr><tr><td>3-8</td><td>Towing and mooring equipment: design, inspection, maintenance (updated 1 Jan 2024)</td></tr><tr><td>3-9</td><td>Means of embarkation and disembarkation</td></tr><tr><td>3-10</td><td><b>Goal-Based Standards:</b> bulk carriers and oil tankers 150 m+ · Ship Construction File (MSC.287(87))</td></tr><tr><td>3-11</td><td>Corrosion protection of cargo oil tanks: crude oil tankers 5,000 dwt+</td></tr><tr><td>3-12</td><td><b>Noise:</b> 1,600 GT+, contracted from 1 Jul 2014 (see Noise Code 2014, MSC.337(91))</td></tr><tr><td><b>3-13</b></td><td><b>Lifting appliances and anchor handling winches</b> (MSC.532(107), <b>1 Jan 2026</b>)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Part B — Subdivision and stability</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>General: probabilistic damage stability (SOLAS 2009)</td></tr><tr><td>5, 5-1</td><td>Intact stability: inclining test on completion, lightship check every 5 years (passenger) · stability information for the master</td></tr><tr><td>6, 7, 7-1 to 7-3</td><td>Required index <b>R</b>, attained index <b>A (A ≥ R)</b>, factors p and s, permeability</td></tr><tr><td>8, 8-1</td><td>Passenger ship special stability · flooding casualty: safe return to port</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Double bottoms</td></tr><tr><td>10–12</td><td>Watertight bulkheads: construction, testing · collision bulkhead · peak and machinery bulkheads</td></tr><tr><td>13, 13-1, 15, 15-1, 16, 16-1, 17, 17-1</td><td>Openings in bulkheads and shell plating · watertight doors, sidescuttles, decks, trunks · internal watertight integrity · ro-ro passenger hull integrity</td></tr><tr><td>18, 19, 20</td><td>Subdivision load lines · damage control information on the bridge · loading of passenger ships (stability check before departure)</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Watertight doors (passenger): operated daily, drills weekly, inspected at least weekly</td></tr><tr><td>22, 22-1</td><td>Water ingress: doors closed at sea unless necessary, logbook entries · <b>flooding detection</b> on passenger ships carrying 36 persons or more</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Ro-ro passenger ships: bow doors, inner doors, indicators, TV monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>24, 25, 25-1</td><td>Cargo ships: water ingress control · water level detectors (single hold, then multiple holds, MSC.482(103), 1 Jan 2024)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Parts C to G — Machinery, electrical, UMS, alternative design, low-flashpoint fuels</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>26–28</td><td>General · overspeed and safety devices · means of going astern</td></tr><tr><td><b>29</b></td><td><b>Steering gear.</b> Main: rudder 35° one side to 30° the other in <b>28 s or less</b> at maximum draught and speed. Auxiliary: 15° to 15° in <b>60 s or less</b> at half speed.</td></tr><tr><td>30–35-1</td><td>Electric steering alarms · machinery controls · boilers · steam pipes · air systems · ventilation · bilge pumping</td></tr><tr><td>37, 38, 39</td><td>Bridge to engine room communication (<b>two independent means</b>) · engineers' alarm · emergency installations (passenger ships)</td></tr><tr><td>40–41</td><td>Electrical: general, main source and lighting</td></tr><tr><td><b>42</b></td><td>Emergency source, passenger ships: <b>36 h</b></td></tr><tr><td>43</td><td>Emergency source, cargo ships: <b>18 h</b>. Emergency lighting, VHF, fire pumps, steering.</td></tr><tr><td>44</td><td>Emergency generator starting: stored energy for <b>3 consecutive starts</b> plus a second source for 3 more within 30 min</td></tr><tr><td>45</td><td>Protection against shock, fire and electrical hazards</td></tr><tr><td>46–54</td><td><b>Periodically unattended machinery spaces:</b> fire detection, bilge and flooding alarms, bridge propulsion control, alarms, safety systems</td></tr><tr><td>55</td><td>Alternative design and arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>56–57</td><td>Low-flashpoint fuels (LNG and others), IGF Code (MSC.391(95))</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter II-2 — Fire protection, detection and extinction</h2><p>*Applies to all SOLAS ships, with stricter rules for passenger ships and tankers.*</p><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1–3</td><td>Application · fire safety objectives · definitions (A, B, C class divisions)</td></tr><tr><td><b>4</b></td><td><b>Probability of ignition:</b> oil fuel flashpoint not below <b>60 °C</b> · tanker inert gas (8,000 dwt+) · cargo tank venting. <b>From 1 Jan 2026:</b> a supplier-certified declaration before bunkering, and the BDN shows the flashpoint (ISO 2719:2016) or states it is at or above 70 °C (MSC.520(106))</td></tr><tr><td>5, 6</td><td>Fire growth potential: ventilation and fuel shut-offs · smoke and toxicity</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><b>Detection and alarm:</b> fixed detection in machinery and accommodation, manual call points</td></tr><tr><td>8, 9</td><td>Control of smoke spread · containment of fire (A and B class divisions, fire doors, main vertical zones)</td></tr><tr><td><b>10</b></td><td><b>Fire fighting:</b> fire pumps, fire main, hydrants, extinguishers, fixed systems, fire-fighter's outfits (cargo ships: at least 2), EEBDs, two-way radios. <b>PFOS</b> extinguishing media banned: new ships from 1 Jan 2026, existing ships by the first survey on or after that date (MSC.532(107))</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Structural integrity</td></tr><tr><td>12, 13</td><td>Notification (general emergency alarm, PA) · <b>means of escape</b>: two routes, EEBDs, low-location lighting (passenger)</td></tr><tr><td>14–16</td><td>Operational readiness and maintenance plan · instructions, training and drills, <b>fire control plan</b> · operations booklet</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Alternative design</td></tr><tr><td>18, 19</td><td>Helicopter facilities · dangerous goods: <b>Document of Compliance</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>20, 20-1</b></td><td><b>Vehicle, special category and ro-ro spaces.</b> From 1 Jan 2026 (MSC.550(108)): fixed detection and alarm, fire patrol in special category spaces, video monitoring. Existing passenger ships by first survey on or after <b>1 Jan 2028</b>. Video recordings kept at least <b>7 days</b> (new ro-ro passenger ships) or <b>24 h</b> (existing). 20-1: vehicle carriers carrying hydrogen or CNG vehicles.</td></tr><tr><td>21–23</td><td>Casualty threshold, safe return to port · systems stay operational after fire · <b>safety centre</b> (passenger). It monitors the fire detection and alarm system.</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Codes: FSS Code (MSC.98(73)) · FTP Code 2010 (MSC.307(88))</li></ul><h2>Chapter III — Life-saving appliances and arrangements</h2><p>*Applies to passenger ships and cargo ships of 500 GT and above.*</p><h3>Part A and Part B, Section I (all ships)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1–5</td><td>Application, exemptions, definitions · evaluation, testing and approval · production tests</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Communications: on-board communications, general alarm. <b>Since 1 Jan 2024 the two-way VHF and SART provisions sit in IV/7.</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>7</b></td><td><b>Personal LSA:</b> lifebuoys (lights, smoke signals, line) · a lifejacket for every person, with children's lifejackets · immersion suits</td></tr><tr><td>8–11</td><td><b>Muster list</b> and emergency instructions · operating instructions · manning of survival craft · muster and embarkation arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>12–17-1</td><td>Launching stations · stowage of survival craft, rescue boats, MES · launching and recovery · <b>recovery of persons from the water</b></td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Line-throwing appliances</td></tr><tr><td><b>19</b></td><td><b>Emergency training and drills</b> (table below)</td></tr><tr><td><b>20</b></td><td><b>Operational readiness, maintenance and inspections</b> (table below)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Section II (passenger) and Section III (cargo)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Ship</th><th>Survival craft</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Passenger</b> (21)</td><td>Lifeboats for at least <b>50% on each side</b>, plus liferafts (at least <b>25%</b>). Liferafts may replace lifeboats but never below <b>37.5% each side</b>. A rescue boat.</td></tr><tr><td>Passenger (22)</td><td>Lifejackets <b>+5%</b>, children's and infant lifejackets</td></tr><tr><td>Passenger (23–30)</td><td>Embarkation, stowage, muster stations · ro-ro: MES, fast rescue boats · passenger information · helicopter areas · decision support · <b>drills weekly (abandon ship and fire)</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Cargo</b> (31)</td><td>Lifeboats <b>100% on each side</b> (or one free-fall lifeboat for <b>100%</b>) plus liferafts for <b>100%</b> (each side if not easily moved)</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo (32)</td><td><b>Immersion suit for every person</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cargo (33)</td><td>Embarkation and launching. Free-fall launch test (MSC.485(103), 1 Jan 2024).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sections IV–V</b> (34–37)</td><td>All LSA meet the LSA Code (MSC.48(66)) · training manual · maintenance instructions · muster list</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Rocket parachute flares: not less than <b>12</b>, stowed on or near the bridge.</p><h3>Drills and training (III/19)</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"solas-drills\"><figcaption>Drill and maintenance intervals</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Drill</th><th>Interval</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Abandon ship and fire drill</b> (each crew member)</td><td><b>Monthly</b> (passenger ships also weekly)</td></tr><tr><td>New crew rule</td><td>Drills within <b>24 h of leaving port</b> if more than <b>25%</b> of the crew have not taken part in both drills on that ship in the previous month</td></tr><tr><td>Lifeboat launch and manoeuvre</td><td>At least every <b>3 months</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Free-fall lifeboat</b> launch</td><td>Every <b>6 months</b> (up to 12 months if simulated launches are done at most 6 months apart)</td></tr><tr><td>Rescue boat launch</td><td><b>Monthly</b> as far as practicable, and at least every <b>3 months</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Enclosed space entry and rescue</b></td><td>At least every <b>2 months</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Davit-launched liferaft</b> training</td><td>At most every <b>4 months</b></td></tr><tr><td>On-board training</td><td>All LSA and fire appliances covered within any <b>2 months</b>. New crew trained within <b>2 weeks</b> of joining.</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency steering drill (V/26)</td><td>At least every <b>3 months</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Maintenance (III/20)</h3><ul><li><b>Weekly:</b> visual check of survival craft, rescue boats and launching appliances · engines run · general alarm tested</li><li><b>Monthly:</b> checklist inspection, recorded in the log</li><li>Liferafts, inflatable lifejackets and MES serviced every <b>12 months</b> (max 17)</li><li>Hydrostatic release units serviced every 12 months</li><li>Falls turned end for end at most every <b>30 months</b> and renewed at most every <b>5 years</b></li><li>Lifeboat release gear: annual thorough examination, 5-yearly overhaul (MSC.402(96), 1 Jan 2020)</li><li><b>Ahead:</b> new lifeboat ventilation rules for lifeboats installed from 1 Jan 2029 (MSC.535(107))</li></ul><h2>Chapter IV — Radiocommunications (GMDSS)</h2><p>*Applies to passenger ships and cargo ships of 300 GT and above. <b>Modernized from 1 Jan 2024</b> (MSC.496(105)).*</p><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"solas-gmdss\"><figcaption>GMDSS sea areas after 1 Jan 2024</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Regs 8, 9, 10 and 11 now define sea areas A1, A2, A3 and A4 individually.</b> Before 2024 each covered a combined set (A1, A1+A2, A1+A2+A3, A1+A2+A3+A4).</li><li><b>A3:</b> an area excluding A1 and A2 within the coverage of a <b>recognized mobile satellite service</b> (no longer tied to Inmarsat only). <b>A4:</b> the area outside A1, A2 and A3.</li><li><b>NAVTEX, EGC and HF NBDP receivers are no longer mandatory.</b> Under MSC.1/Circ.1645 ships should carry NAVTEX where international NAVTEX is provided, otherwise an EGC receiver (SafetyNET or Iridium SafetyCast) covering the operating area. The mandatory NBDP function was removed. See GMDSS.</li><li><b>1 Jan 2028:</b> under MSC.1/Circ.1676 flag States may allow continued installation of VHF, MF/HF and Inmarsat-C equipment approved under the previous standards</li><li>The two-way VHF and SART provisions moved from III/6 to <b>IV/7</b></li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1–3</td><td>Application · terms (sea areas) · exemptions</td></tr><tr><td><b>4</b></td><td><b>Functional requirements:</b> distress alerting ship to shore by <b>2 independent means</b> · receiving shore-to-ship alerts · ship-to-ship alerting · SAR coordination and on-scene communications · locating signals · maritime safety information · general radiocommunications · bridge-to-bridge communications</td></tr><tr><td>5, 5-1</td><td>Radio services ashore · GMDSS identities (MMSI)</td></tr><tr><td>6, 7</td><td>Radio installations: protected, at the conning position, with instructions · equipment for all ships: VHF DSC (ch 70) and voice (ch 16, 13), SART or AIS-SART, EPIRB (406 MHz)</td></tr><tr><td>8–11</td><td>Equipment by sea area A1, A2, A3, A4</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Watches: continuous DSC watch</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Sources of energy: reserve source for all GMDSS equipment</td></tr><tr><td>14, 15</td><td>Performance standards (new standards for equipment installed on or after 1 Jan 2024) · maintenance: A1 and A2 one method, A3 and A4 two methods</td></tr><tr><td>16–18</td><td>Radio personnel (GOC, ROC) · radio records · automatic position updating</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter V — Safety of navigation</h2><p>*Applies to all ships on all voyages, with some regulations by size. Warships excepted.*</p><h3>Government services (Regs 1–13)</h3><p>Application · definitions · exemptions · 4 navigational warnings · 5 meteorological services · 6 ice patrol · 7 SAR services · 8 life-saving signals · 9 hydrographic services · <b>10 ships' routeing</b> (TSS, ATBA, deep-water routes) · <b>11 ship reporting systems</b> · <b>12 VTS</b> · 13 aids to navigation</p><h3>Ship requirements (Regs 14–35)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>14</td><td><b>Manning:</b> Minimum Safe Manning Document · English is the working language for bridge-to-bridge, bridge-to-shore and pilot-to-bridge communication, unless those involved share another language</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Bridge design and procedures (BRM principles)</td></tr><tr><td>16, 17</td><td>Maintenance of equipment · electromagnetic compatibility</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Approval and performance standards · <b>annual VDR performance test</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>19</b></td><td><b>Carriage requirements</b> (table below)</td></tr><tr><td>19-1</td><td><b>LRIT:</b> passenger ships, cargo ships 300 GT+, MODUs · 4 position reports a day (MSC.202(81))</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td><b>VDR:</b> passenger ships built on or after 1 Jul 2002 and cargo ships 3,000 GT+ built on or after 1 Jul 2002</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>International Code of Signals and IAMSAR Manual Vol III on board</td></tr><tr><td><b>22</b></td><td><b>Bridge visibility</b> (ships 45 m+ built on or after 1 Jul 1998)</td></tr><tr><td><b>23</b></td><td><b>Pilot transfer arrangements</b> (replaced by MSC.572(110), see below)</td></tr><tr><td>24, 25</td><td>Heading and track control: manual steering available at once, tested after long autopilot use and before special-caution areas · steering gear: more than one power unit running in special-caution areas</td></tr><tr><td><b>26</b></td><td><b>Steering gear tests and drills:</b> tested within <b>12 h before departure</b> · emergency steering drill at least every <b>3 months</b> · logged. Regular short voyages: waiver of the pre-departure test, but test weekly.</td></tr><tr><td>27, 28, 29</td><td>Nautical charts and publications up to date · records of navigational activities, daily reporting to company · life-saving signals table</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>Operational limitations (passenger ships)</td></tr><tr><td><b>31, 32</b></td><td><b>Danger messages:</b> dangerous ice, derelicts, tropical storms, wind force 10 or more, ice accretion. <b>From 1 Jan 2026 (MSC.550(108)): lost and drifting containers.</b> The master alerts nearby ships and the coastal State, the flag State reports to IMO, and the company takes over the duty if the ship is abandoned. Reg 32: ship identity, position, number of containers, goods and dangerous goods status. A final message follows once the loss is verified.</td></tr><tr><td><b>33</b></td><td><b>Distress:</b> the master proceeds with all speed to assist, and logs the reason if unable</td></tr><tr><td><b>34, 34-1</b></td><td><b>Safe navigation:</b> voyage plan before sailing (appraisal, planning, execution, monitoring; A.893(21)) · <b>master's discretion</b>: the company cannot constrain safety decisions</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>Misuse of distress signals prohibited</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Regulation 19 carriage requirements</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"solas-bridge\"><figcaption>Navigation equipment by gross tonnage</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Equipment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>All ships</b></td><td>Magnetic compass · pelorus or bearing device (360°) · means to correct to true · charts and publications (ECDIS accepted) with back-up · GNSS receiver · radar reflector if practicable under 150 GT · sound reception system if the bridge is totally enclosed</td></tr><tr><td><b>150 GT+</b> and all passenger ships</td><td>Spare magnetic compass · daylight signalling lamp · <b>BNWAS</b> (see below)</td></tr><tr><td><b>300 GT+</b> and all passenger ships</td><td>Echo sounder · <b>9 GHz radar</b> · electronic plotting aid · speed and distance through water · transmitting heading device</td></tr><tr><td><b>AIS</b></td><td>300 GT+ on international voyages · cargo ships 500 GT+ not on international voyages · all passenger ships</td></tr><tr><td><b>500 GT+</b></td><td>Gyro compass and repeaters · rudder, propeller, thrust, pitch and mode indicators · <b>automatic tracking aid</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>3,000 GT+</b></td><td><b>3 GHz radar</b> (or a second 9 GHz) independent of the first · <b>second automatic tracking aid</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>10,000 GT+</b></td><td><b>ARPA</b> tracking at least <b>20 targets</b> · heading or track control system</td></tr><tr><td><b>50,000 GT+</b></td><td>Rate of turn indicator · speed and distance over the ground, forward and athwartships</td></tr><tr><td><b>From 1 Jan 2026</b></td><td><b>Electronic inclinometer</b> on containerships and bulk carriers of 3,000 GT+ built on or after 1 Jan 2026 (MSC.532(107))</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>ECDIS (ENC chart carriage), international voyages</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship</th><th>New ships (built on or after)</th><th>Existing ships (first survey on or after)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passenger 500 GT+</td><td>1 Jul 2012</td><td>1 Jul 2014</td></tr><tr><td>Tankers 3,000 GT+</td><td>1 Jul 2012</td><td>1 Jul 2015</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo 10,000 GT+</td><td>1 Jul 2013</td><td>50,000 GT+: 1 Jul 2016 · 20,000 to under 50,000: 1 Jul 2017 · 10,000 to under 20,000: 1 Jul 2018</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo 3,000 to under 10,000 GT</td><td>1 Jul 2014</td><td>no requirement</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>S-100 ECDIS:</b> performance standard MSC.530(106)/Rev.1 applies voluntarily to new installations from 1 Jan 2026 and becomes <b>mandatory for new installations from 1 Jan 2029</b></li></ul><p><b>BNWAS</b> (applies whether or not the ship is on an international voyage)</p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship</th><th>New (built on or after)</th><th>Existing (first survey after)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passenger ships, any size</td><td>1 Jul 2011</td><td>1 Jul 2012</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo ships 150 GT+</td><td>1 Jul 2011</td><td>3,000 GT+: 1 Jul 2012 · 500 to under 3,000 GT: 1 Jul 2013 · 150 to under 500 GT: 1 Jul 2014</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Bridge visibility (Reg 22)</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"solas-visibility\"><figcaption>Bridge visibility limits</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The sea surface forward of the bow, out to <b>10° on each side</b>, must not be obscured by more than <b>two ship lengths or 500 m</b>, whichever is less</li><li>Blind sectors: <b>10° each</b>, <b>20° total</b>. Within that forward view each blind sector is limited to <b>5°</b>.</li></ul><h3>Pilot transfer (Reg 23)</h3><ul><li><b>MSC.572(110)</b> (adopted 26 Jun 2025) replaces V/23 and amends II-2/11. Performance standards in <b>MSC.576(110)</b>.</li><li><b>In force 1 Jan 2028.</b> Arrangements installed from then comply at installation. Existing SOLAS ships comply by the <b>first survey on or after 1 Jan 2029</b>. Ships outside Chapter I by <b>1 Jan 2030</b>.</li><li><b>Mechanical pilot hoists are prohibited.</b> Lighting over the side and at the boarding position. Pilots may refuse a non-compliant arrangement.</li><li>Height of climb (ABS summary): under 1.5 m no ladder, a risk-based alternative · <b>1.5 to 9 m</b> pilot ladder · <b>over 9 m</b> combination arrangement</li><li>Side ropes at least <b>24 kN</b>, securing points and shackles at least <b>48 kN</b>. Inspection every <b>3 months</b>. At least one spare ladder and one spare set of manropes. Replace within <b>36 months</b> of manufacture or <b>30 months</b> in service.</li></ul><h2>Chapter VI — Carriage of cargoes and oil fuels</h2><p>*Applies to cargo ships carrying solid cargoes (not liquids or gases in bulk).*</p><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1, 1-1, 1-2</td><td>Application · definitions · solid bulk cargoes other than grain: IMSBC Code (MSC.268(85))</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Cargo information: shipper gives details before loading</td></tr><tr><td>3, 4</td><td>Oxygen analysis and gas detection instruments (and training) · pesticides (fumigation)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Stowage and securing: <b>Cargo Securing Manual</b> (CSS Code, A.714(17))</td></tr><tr><td>5-1, 5-2</td><td>Safety data sheets: MARPOL Annex I cargoes and fuel oil · no blending of bulk liquid cargoes or production processes at sea</td></tr><tr><td>6, 7</td><td>Acceptability for shipment: moisture below TML (Group A) · loading and unloading: loading plan agreed with the terminal (BLU Code, A.862(20))</td></tr><tr><td>8, 9</td><td><b>Grain:</b> Grain Code (MSC.23(59)). Document of Authorization. Heel from grain shift <b>12° or less</b>, initial GM <b>0.30 m or more</b>.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter VII — Carriage of dangerous goods</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A, Regs 1–6</td><td><b>Packaged form:</b> IMDG Code (MSC.122(75)). Documents: DG declaration, container packing certificate, manifest or stowage plan. Cargo Securing Manual. Reporting of DG incidents. Amendment 42-24 (MSC.556(108)) mandatory <b>1 Jan 2026</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>A-1, Regs 7–7-4</td><td>Solid dangerous goods in bulk</td></tr><tr><td>B, Regs 8–10</td><td><b>Chemical tankers:</b> IBC Code (MSC.4(48)), ship types 1, 2, 3, Certificate of Fitness</td></tr><tr><td>C, Regs 11–13</td><td><b>Gas carriers:</b> IGC Code (MSC.5(48), revised MSC.370(93)), ship types 1G–3G</td></tr><tr><td>D, Regs 14–16</td><td><b>INF cargo:</b> INF Code (MSC.88(71)), INF Class 1, 2, 3 ships</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters VIII to XV</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Subject</th><th>Main points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>VIII</b></td><td>Nuclear ships</td><td>Regs 1–12: reactor approval, radiation safety, safety assessment, operating manual, surveys, Nuclear Ship Safety Certificate, casualty reporting. Code A.491(XII).</td></tr><tr><td><b>IX</b></td><td>Management for the safe operation of ships</td><td>ISM Code (A.741(18)). <b>1 Jul 1998:</b> passenger ships, tankers, gas carriers, bulk carriers and cargo HSC 500 GT+. <b>1 Jul 2002:</b> other cargo ships and MODUs 500 GT+. DOC and SMC valid 5 years. Interim DOC 12 months, interim SMC 6 months.</td></tr><tr><td><b>X</b></td><td>High-speed craft</td><td>HSC built on or after 1 Jan 1996. HSC Code 1994 (MSC.36(63)) and 2000 (MSC.97(73)). Safety Certificate and Permit to Operate.</td></tr><tr><td><b>XI-1</b></td><td>Special measures to enhance maritime safety</td><td>1 Recognized organizations (RO Code) · 2 enhanced surveys (ESP Code) · 2-1 harmonized survey periods · <b>3 IMO number</b> (passenger 100 GT+, cargo 300 GT+) · 4 PSC on operational requirements · <b>5 Continuous Synopsis Record</b> · 6 casualty investigation (Casualty Investigation Code) · 7 atmosphere testing instrument (1 Jul 2016)</td></tr><tr><td><b>XI-2</b></td><td>Maritime security</td><td>ISPS Code. Regs 1–13: security levels, company and ship requirements, <b>Ship Security Alert System</b>, master's discretion, control and compliance (records of the last 10 port calls), port facilities, alternative agreements</td></tr><tr><td><b>XII</b></td><td>Bulk carriers</td><td>Reg 4 damage stability for 150 m+ with cargo density 1,000 kg/m³ or more · 5 structural strength · 8 solid triangle mark · 10 cargo density declaration · 11 loading instrument · <b>12 water ingress alarms</b> · 13 pumping · 14 restrictions on sailing with any hold empty</td></tr><tr><td><b>XIII</b></td><td>Verification of compliance</td><td>III Code (A.1070(28)). IMSAS audits since 1 Jan 2016.</td></tr><tr><td><b>XIV</b></td><td>Polar waters</td><td>Polar Code Part I-A (MSC.385(94)), 1 Jan 2017 · Polar Ship Certificate, categories A, B, C, PWOM</td></tr><tr><td><b>XV</b></td><td>Industrial personnel</td><td>Cargo ships and HSC cargo craft 500 GT+ carrying more than 12 industrial personnel · new ships from 1 Jul 2024 · adopted by MSC.521(106) · IP Code (MSC.527(106))</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Polar chapter extension from 1 Jan 2026 (MSC.532(107))</h3><ul><li>Chapter XIV now also covers <b>fishing vessels 24 m LOA and above</b>, <b>pleasure yachts 300 GT and above</b> not in trade, and <b>cargo ships 300 to under 500 GT</b></li><li>These ships must meet chapters <b>9-1 and 11-1</b> of Part I-A of the Polar Code (voyage planning and navigation). Those built before 1 Jan 2026 have until <b>1 Jan 2027</b>.</li><li>Polar Code amendments: MSC.538(107)</li></ul><h2>Amendments in force on 1 Jan 2026</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"solas-2026\"><figcaption>SOLAS changes of 1 Jan 2026</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>Changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>MSC.532(107)</b></td><td>8 Jun 2023</td><td>II-1/3-13 lifting appliances and anchor handling winches · II-2/10 PFOS ban · V/19 electronic inclinometer · Chapter XIV polar extension · \"Containership\" added to certificates</td></tr><tr><td><b>MSC.520(106)</b></td><td>10 Nov 2022</td><td>II-2/4 flashpoint: supplier declaration, BDN flashpoint, governments report confirmed cases</td></tr><tr><td><b>MSC.550(108)</b></td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>V/31 and V/32 lost containers · II-2/20 ro-ro and vehicle space fire safety · II-2/7, 9 and 23 changes · II-2/4 oil fuel must not jeopardize safety</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Later changes: <b>1 Jan 2028</b> pilot transfer (MSC.572(110)) · ro-ro fire items for existing passenger ships · <b>1 Jan 2029</b> S-100 ECDIS for new installations and lifeboat ventilation (MSC.535(107))</li></ul><h2>Special areas and routeing (V/10–V/12)</h2><ul><li><b>V/10:</b> Traffic Separation Schemes, Areas To Be Avoided, deep-water routes. Follow those IMO made mandatory.</li><li><b>V/11:</b> mandatory ship reporting systems, for example <b>STRAITREP</b> (Malacca and Singapore Straits, MSC.73(69), 1 Dec 1998), REEFREP (Torres Strait), OUESSREP, WETREP and others</li><li><b>V/12:</b> follow VTS where a coastal State sets it up</li></ul><h2>Beyond the convention</h2><h3>United States (USCG)</h3><ul><li>The US is a SOLAS Party. USCG does PSC and enforces SOLAS, MARPOL and STCW on foreign ships.</li><li><b>33 CFR 164.25:</b> the listed equipment must be tested <b>within 12 hours</b> before entering or getting underway: primary and secondary steering gear, internal communications and alarms, standby or emergency generator, emergency batteries, main propulsion ahead and astern</li><li><b>Emergency steering drill:</b> logged within <b>48 hours</b> before entry, or conducted and logged regularly at least every <b>3 months</b> (164.25(d))</li><li>Notice of Arrival, equipment (164.37), AIS (164.46) and failure reporting (164.53): see Not included</li></ul><h3>Other regimes</h3><ul><li><b>PSC regimes:</b> Paris MoU (Europe and North Atlantic) · <b>Tokyo MoU</b> (Asia-Pacific, including the Philippines) · USCG · Indian Ocean, Black Sea, Mediterranean, Caribbean, Viña del Mar, Abuja and Riyadh MoUs</li><li><b>CIC 2026 (Paris and Tokyo MoU):</b> cargo securing, <b>1 Sep to 30 Nov 2026</b></li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Regulation-by-regulation wording of Chapters II-1 to XV. The tables above follow the earlier checked version of this note and the sources below. Check the consolidated SOLAS text for the exact words.</li><li>The A1 and A2 sea area definitions in the 2024 text, and the equipment lists for each area</li><li>Current numbers of Contracting States after the IMO table of 5 Sep 2023</li><li>The resolution numbers for some older amendments (for example MSC.256(84), MSC.133(76), MSC.287(87), MSC.482(103), MSC.485(103), MSC.402(96), MSC.535(107), MSC.553(108), MSC.556(108)) and details of the MSC.550(108) detection changes beyond the points listed</li><li>USCG Notice of Arrival timing, 33 CFR 164 equipment, AIS and reporting rules, the COC exam rules, and casualty reporting. They were in the older note but I could not re-check them today.</li><li>The EU and Paris MoU directive numbers</li><li>The STRAITREP size thresholds and the other reporting systems' details</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — SOLAS 1974: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-for-the-safety-of-life-at-sea-(solas),-1974.aspx</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (printed 5 Sep 2023): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status%20of%20IMO%20Treaties.pdf</li><li>IMO — HSSC Survey Guidelines 2025, A.34 Res.1207: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/IIIS/Documents/A 34-Res.1207 - SURVEY GUIDELINES UNDER THE HARMONIZED SYSTEMOF SURVEY AND CERTIFICATION (HSSC), 2025 (Secretariat).pdf</li><li>IMO — rules in force from 1 January 2026: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>MSC.532(107): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.532(107).pdf</li><li>MSC.550(108): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution MSC.550(108).pdf</li><li>MSC.520(106): https://ltsa.lrv.lt/uploads/ltsa/documents/files/TJO/MSC_520(106).pdf</li><li>MSC.572(110): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.572(110).pdf</li><li>ABS — SOLAS V/23 pilot transfer: https://ww2.eagle.org/en/rules-and-resources/regulatory-updates/SOLAS-Pilot-Transfer-Arrangements.html</li><li>ABS — Amendment to SOLAS Chapter IV (MSC.496(105)): https://ww2.eagle.org/content/dam/eagle/regulatory-news/2024/Amendment-to-SOLAS-Chapter-IV.pdf</li><li>UK MCA — SOLAS V guidance (MGN 610): https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5f2140d3e90e071a603d33b3/MGN_610_2020.pdf</li><li>UK MCA — SOLAS V text: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f0081ed915d74e33f3c6e/solas_v_on_safety_of_navigation.pdf</li><li>West P&amp;I — SOLAS ECDIS and BNWAS: https://www.westpandi.com/publications/news/solas-mandatory-requirements-for-ecdis-and-bnwas/</li><li>Nautical Institute — SOLAS onboard drill requirements: https://www.nautinst.org/static/095339d6-8534-4630-a725c69f502dd9b5/HE01195-SOLAS-onboard-drill-requirements.pdf</li><li>Ireland — Merchant Shipping (Life-Saving Appliances) Rules 2018: https://www.irishstatutebook.ie/2018/en/si/0438.html</li><li>33 CFR 164.25 (Cornell LII): https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/164.25</li><li>IRClass — Paris and Tokyo MoU CIC on cargo securing 2026: https://www.irclass.org/technical-circulars/concentrated-inspection-campaign-on-cargo-securing-of-cargo-units-and-cargo-transport-units-beginning-from-1st-september-2026-and-ending-on-30th-november-2026-by-tokyo-and-paris-mou/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-stcw-1978","v":36957535,"title":"STCW 1978","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["stcw","imo","convention","core","seafarers","certification","psc"],"body":"<h1>STCW 1978 — Training, Certification and Watchkeeping for Seafarers</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>Minimum international standards for training, certification and watchkeeping of seafarers on seagoing ships. The first convention to set them</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>7 Jul 1978</b></td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>28 Apr 1984</b></td></tr><tr><td>1995 amendments</td><td>Split the annex into Regulations and the <b>STCW Code</b> (Part A mandatory, Part B guidance). In force <b>1 Feb 1997</b></td></tr><tr><td>2010 Manila amendments</td><td>Adopted <b>25 Jun 2010</b>. In force <b>1 Jan 2012</b></td></tr><tr><td>Latest in force</td><td><b>1 Jan 2026</b>: violence and harassment training, MSC.560(108)</td></tr><tr><td>Under review</td><td>Comprehensive review, revised STCW expected to go to MSC in <b>2029 or 2030</b></td></tr><tr><td>Fishing vessels</td><td>Separate convention: STCW-F 1995</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"stcw-structure\"><figcaption>How STCW is built</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> STCW says who is qualified to do what on board, and how rested they must be. The numbers for sea service are in the Convention annex. The competence tables, rest hours and alcohol limits are in the Code.</blockquote><h2>History</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"stcw-timeline\"><figcaption>STCW milestones</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>1978:</b> the first global standards. Before it, each government set its own</li><li><b>1995:</b> a full revision, to fix vague wording that had led to different readings. Parties report to IMO under Reg I/7, and the Maritime Safety Committee confirms a list of Parties that have given full effect to the Convention</li><li><b>2010 Manila:</b> covered fraud prevention, work and rest hours, drug and alcohol rules, medical fitness, and certificates for able seafarers. It added training for ECDIS, leadership and teamwork, electro-technical officers, tankers, security and piracy, distance learning, polar waters and dynamic positioning</li><li><b>Manila transition:</b> seafarers who began approved service or training before <b>1 Jul 2013</b> could be certified under the old rules until <b>1 Jan 2017</b></li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Subject</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>General provisions</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Master and deck department</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Engine department</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Radiocommunication and radio operators</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Special training for certain ship types</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Emergency, safety, security, medical care, survival</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Alternative certification</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>Watchkeeping</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter I — General provisions</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Regulation</th><th>What it requires</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I/2 Certificates and endorsements</td><td>Certificates of competency issued by the Administration only. Endorsements last <b>no more than 5 years</b>. Originals kept on board. An English translation is needed if the certificate is not in English</td></tr><tr><td>I/7 Communication of information</td><td>Parties report to IMO. The MSC confirms the Parties that have given full effect to the Convention. Other Parties may accept their certificates in principle</td></tr><tr><td>I/8 Quality standards</td><td>A quality standards system for training and certification, evaluated independently at intervals of no more than <b>5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>I/9 Medical standards</td><td>Certificate valid up to <b>2 years</b>, up to <b>1 year</b> if under 18. Colour vision tested every <b>6 years</b>. If it expires at sea, it stays valid to the next port with a doctor, up to <b>3 months</b></td></tr><tr><td>I/10 Recognition of certificates</td><td>Needs an evaluation showing full compliance. A non-Party's certificates are not recognized. A seafarer can serve up to <b>3 months</b> while an endorsement application is pending</td></tr><tr><td>I/11 Revalidation</td><td>Every <b>5 years</b>. See below</td></tr><tr><td>I/14 Company responsibilities</td><td>Seafarers certified and familiarized with the ship before duties</td></tr><tr><td>I/15 Transitional provisions</td><td>Ran to 1 Jan 2017</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Revalidating a certificate of competency (A-I/11)</b></p><ul><li>At intervals of no more than <b>5 years</b></li><li>Sea service of <b>12 months in the preceding 5 years</b>, or <b>3 months in the 6 months before revalidating</b></li><li>Approved refresher courses and updating are also accepted under the Code</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"stcw-validity\"><figcaption>How long each certificate or check lasts</figcaption></figure><h2>Chapter II — Deck</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Regulation</th><th>Rank</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>II/1</td><td>Officer in charge of a navigational watch (500 GT+)</td><td>Age <b>18</b>. <b>12 months</b> of approved seagoing service in an approved training programme, or <b>36 months</b> otherwise. At least <b>6 months</b> of bridge watchkeeping under supervision</td></tr><tr><td>II/2</td><td>Chief mate, 3,000 GT+</td><td><b>12 months</b> as officer in charge of a navigational watch</td></tr><tr><td>II/2</td><td>Master, 3,000 GT+</td><td><b>36 months</b> as OOW, or <b>24 months</b> if at least 12 were as chief mate</td></tr><tr><td>II/2</td><td>Master, 500 to 3,000 GT</td><td><b>36 months</b>, or <b>24 months</b> if at least 12 were as chief mate. No separate service period is given for the chief mate</td></tr><tr><td>II/4</td><td>Rating in a navigational watch</td><td>Age <b>16</b>. <b>6 months</b> of training and experience, or special training plus at least <b>2 months</b> of approved seagoing service</td></tr><tr><td>II/5</td><td>Able seafarer deck</td><td>Age <b>18</b>. <b>18 months</b> of deck department service, or <b>12 months</b> plus approved training</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"stcw-deck\"><figcaption>Deck career path (3,000 GT+)</figcaption></figure><p><b>What the OOW tables cover (Code A-II/1)</b></p><ul><li>Passage planning, position fixing, keeping a safe navigational watch</li><li>ECDIS: \"operational procedures for using ECDIS are established, applied and monitored\"</li><li>Use of leadership and managerial skill</li><li>Emergencies, cargo, ship operations</li></ul><h2>Chapter III — Engine</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Regulation</th><th>Rank</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>III/1</td><td>Officer in charge of an engineering watch (750 kW+)</td><td>Age <b>18</b>. <b>12 months</b> of approved seagoing service in an approved programme, or <b>36 months</b> of which at least <b>30</b> in the engine department. At least <b>6 months</b> of watchkeeping under supervision</td></tr><tr><td>III/2</td><td>Second engineer, 3,000 kW+</td><td><b>12 months</b> as a qualified engineer officer</td></tr><tr><td>III/2</td><td>Chief engineer, 3,000 kW+</td><td><b>36 months</b>, or <b>24 months</b> if at least 12 were as second engineer</td></tr><tr><td>III/3</td><td>Second engineer, 750 to 3,000 kW</td><td><b>12 months</b> as assistant or engineer officer</td></tr><tr><td>III/3</td><td>Chief engineer, 750 to 3,000 kW</td><td><b>24 months</b>, of which at least <b>12</b> as second engineer</td></tr><tr><td>III/4</td><td>Rating in an engine watch</td><td>Age <b>16</b>. <b>6 months</b> of training and experience, or special training plus <b>2 months</b> of approved seagoing service</td></tr><tr><td>III/5</td><td>Able seafarer engine</td><td>Age <b>18</b>. <b>12 months</b> of engine department service, or <b>6 months</b> plus approved training</td></tr><tr><td>III/6</td><td>Electro-technical officer</td><td>Age <b>18</b>. <b>12 months</b> of combined workshop training and seagoing service (at least 6 months at sea in an approved programme), or <b>36 months</b> of which at least 30 in the engine department</td></tr><tr><td>III/7</td><td>Electro-technical rating</td><td>Age <b>18</b>. One of: <b>12 months</b> of approved seagoing service with training, <b>6 months</b> at sea after approved training, or <b>3 months</b> at sea with the qualifying technical competence</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"stcw-engine\"><figcaption>Engine career path (3,000 kW+)</figcaption></figure><h2>Chapter IV — Radio</h2><ul><li>Radiocommunication and radio operators for ships in the GMDSS</li></ul><h2>Chapter V — Special ship types</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Regulation</th><th>Training</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>V/1-1 Oil and chemical tankers</td><td><b>Basic:</b> at least <b>3 months</b> of approved seagoing service on oil or chemical tankers, or approved basic training. <b>Advanced:</b> at least <b>3 months</b> at sea on that tanker type, or at least <b>1 month</b> of supernumerary onboard training including <b>3 loadings and 3 unloadings</b></td></tr><tr><td>V/1-2 Liquefied gas tankers</td><td>Basic and advanced training</td></tr><tr><td>V/2 Passenger ships</td><td>Crowd management, passenger safety, crisis management and human behaviour. Refresher at intervals of no more than <b>5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>V/3 and V/4 †</td><td>Ships under the IGF Code, and polar waters</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter VI — Emergency, safety, security, medical care</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Regulation</th><th>Training</th><th>Must the standard be maintained?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>VI/1 Basic training</td><td>Personal survival techniques, fire prevention and firefighting, elementary first aid, personal safety and social responsibilities</td><td><b>Every 5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>VI/2</td><td>Survival craft, rescue boats and fast rescue boats</td><td><b>Every 5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>VI/3</td><td>Advanced firefighting</td><td><b>Every 5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>VI/4</td><td>Medical first aid, medical care</td><td>Not revalidated</td></tr><tr><td>VI/5</td><td>Ship security officer. <b>12 months</b> of approved seagoing service, or appropriate service and knowledge of ship operations</td><td>Not revalidated</td></tr><tr><td>VI/6</td><td>Security awareness, and security training for designated duties</td><td>Not revalidated</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>1 January 2026: violence and harassment</h3><ul><li><b>Resolution MSC.560(108)</b>, adopted 23 May 2024, in force <b>1 Jan 2026</b></li><li>It amends STCW Code <b>Table A-VI/1-4</b> (personal safety and social responsibilities) with a new mandatory competence: <b>contribute to the prevention of and response to violence and harassment</b></li><li>Covers harassment, bullying and sexual assault: how to recognize, report and respond, the effect on safety, health and wellbeing, and support, including trauma-informed principles</li><li><b>Example of national implementation (UK, MGN 718):</b> all UK-certified officers and ratings, and holders of older PSSR certificates, must complete a short module by <b>31 Mar 2027</b>. Other flag States set their own deadlines, so check with the flag</li></ul><h2>Chapter VII — Alternative certification</h2><ul><li>A function-based route as an alternative to Chapters II and III</li></ul><h2>Chapter VIII — Watchkeeping and fitness for duty</h2><p><b>Rest hours (Code A-VIII/1)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Minimum rest</td><td><b>10 hours</b> in any 24 hours, and <b>77 hours</b> in any 7 days</td></tr><tr><td>Division</td><td>Normally no more than <b>2 periods</b>, one of at least <b>6 hours</b>. No more than <b>14 hours</b> between rest periods</td></tr><tr><td>Records</td><td>Watch schedules posted. Rest records kept</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"stcw-rest\"><figcaption>Rest limits</figcaption></figure><p><b>Exceptions (need a collective or workforce agreement and flag State approval)</b></p><ul><li>Weekly rest may drop to <b>70 hours</b> in any 7 days</li><li>The weekly exception may not run for more than <b>2 consecutive weeks</b></li><li>The gap between two exception periods must be at least <b>twice</b> the length of the exception</li><li>Daily rest may be split into up to <b>3 periods</b>: one of at least <b>6 hours</b>, the other two at least <b>1 hour</b> each</li><li>Daily rest exceptions may not extend beyond <b>two 24-hour periods</b> in any 7 days</li><li>Rest can be overridden only for emergencies or essential work that cannot be delayed for safety, security or environmental reasons, or that could not reasonably have been foreseen. Planned pilotage or routine cargo work in port is not covered</li></ul><p><b>Alcohol</b></p><ul><li>Limit: <b>0.05% blood alcohol</b> or <b>0.25 mg/l in the breath</b></li><li>The Manila amendments recommend that companies ban alcohol for <b>4 hours</b> before a watch</li></ul><h2>Comprehensive review of STCW</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Apr 2022</td><td>MSC 105 starts the review</td></tr><tr><td>Feb 2024</td><td>Guiding principles agreed at HTW 10</td></tr><tr><td>May 2024</td><td>MSC 108 endorses a two-phase approach</td></tr><tr><td>Feb 2025</td><td>Phase 1 done: more than <b>400 gaps</b> found</td></tr><tr><td>Jun 2025</td><td>MSC 110 endorses roadmaps and moves the target to <b>2029 or 2030</b></td></tr><tr><td>Feb and Mar 2026</td><td>HTW 12 and a second intersessional group continue work on Chapters II and III</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Topics include advanced training technology, cybersecurity, ballast water management, violence and harassment, mental health and gender and cultural diversity</li><li>Phase 1 focused on Chapters II and III. Chapters IV, VI and the rest of Chapter I come later</li><li>Adoption date is not yet set</li></ul><h2>What PSC inspectors look at</h2><ul><li>Valid certificates of competency, endorsements and certificates of proficiency</li><li>Medical certificates</li><li>Rest-hour records against the logbook, and watch schedules</li><li>Familiarization records and the working language on board</li><li>Alcohol limits</li></ul><h2>Beyond the convention †</h2><p>Checked on 7 Nov 2026, not re-read today.</p><ul><li><b>Philippines:</b> MARINA is the single STCW Administration and its STCW Office issues certificates. MARINA STCW Advisory 2025-40 covers the updated personal safety and social responsibilities training</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>STCW-F 1995 fishing vessel personnel</li><li>SOLAS 1974 manning and bridge rules</li><li>MARPOL 73/78 for the pollution competences in the officer tables</li><li>Polar Code polar training</li><li>IGF Code training for gas and low flashpoint fuels</li><li>MLC 2006 (ILO) has the same rest limits (10 hours, 77 hours)</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Number of Parties to STCW and their tonnage</li><li>Exceptions to the Convention (Art III), and the Art VIII dispensation rules</li><li>GMDSS operator certificate limits (GOC, ROC)</li><li>Exact dates and wording for the IGF and polar training regulations (V/3, V/4)</li><li>Competence table details for Chapter V and VI beyond what is listed above</li><li>The full list of Manila 2010 new grades beyond those named above</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO, STCW Convention page: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-standards-of-training,-certification-and-watchkeeping-for-seafarers-(stcw).aspx</li><li>Manila amendments, Resolution 1 (Convention annex, STCW/CONF.2/33): https://www.riigiteataja.ee/aktilisa/2230/8201/3001/Manila%20muudatused%20resolutsioon%20nr%201%20ing.pdf</li><li>Manila amendments, Resolution 2 (STCW Code): https://www.riigiteataja.ee/aktilisa/2230/8201/3001/Manila%20muudatused%20resolutsioon%20nr%202%20ing.pdf</li><li>UK MCA, MGN 565 (rest hours and alcohol under Manila): https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f3802e5274a2e87db4853/MGN_565_STCW_Manila_Amendments_Hours_of_Work__Alcohol_limits.pdf</li><li>UK MCA, MGN 718 (MSC.560(108)): https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-718-mf-2026-imo-amendment-to-pssr/mgn-718-mf-2026-imo-amendment-to-pssr</li><li>IMO, rules in force from 1 Jan 2026: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>IMO, comprehensive review of STCW FAQ: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/comprehensive-review-of-the-stcw-convention-and-code-faqs.aspx</li><li>MARINA STCW Office advisories (7 Nov check): https://stcw.marina.gov.ph/marina-stcw-advisories/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-stcw-f-1995","v":25886329,"title":"STCW-F 1995","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["stcw-f","fishing","training","certification","watchkeeping","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>STCW-F 1995 — International Convention on Standards of Training, Certification and Watchkeeping for Fishing Vessel Personnel</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>7 Jul 1995, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>29 Sep 2012</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>15 Articles · 1 Annex (Ch I–IV) · STCW-F Code (from 1 Jan 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Revision</td><td>Annex amended by MSC.561(108) · STCW-F Code MSC.562(108) — both adopted 23 May 2024, in force <b>1 Jan 2026</b></td></tr><tr><td>Main threshold</td><td>Skipper and watch officers: fishing vessels of 24 m and over</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is STCW-F</h2><ul><li>The international standard for training, certification and watchkeeping of crews of seagoing fishing vessels</li><li>Art 6: fishing vessel personnel shall be certificated in line with the Annex</li><li>Art 3: applies to personnel serving on board seagoing fishing vessels entitled to fly the flag of a Party</li><li>\"Seagoing\" excludes vessels that navigate only in inland waters (Art 2)</li><li>IMO calls it one of the pillars of fishing vessel safety, with the Cape Town Agreement (vessel safety), the ILO Work in Fishing Convention 2007 and the FAO Port State Measures Agreement 2009</li><li>A Party may exempt certain provisions for vessels under 45 m that operate only from its ports and within its limited waters (IMO)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Art 12: in force 12 months after not less than 15 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Annex amendments follow a comprehensive review of the Convention, to meet the needs of the fishing industry (IMO)</li><li><b>MSC.561(108)</b> (revised Annex): deemed accepted 1 Jul 2025 unless more than one third of Parties objected</li><li><b>MSC.562(108)</b>: the new <b>STCW-F Code</b>, Part A (mandatory) and Part B (guidance)</li></ul><h2>The 15 Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General obligations</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Other treaties and interpretation</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Certification</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>National provisions</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Control</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Promotion of technical co-operation</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Amendments</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Denunciation</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Depositary</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Languages</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Art 8 Control: port State control of certification; the vessel may not sail if a danger is not corrected</li><li>Art 10 Amendments: annex amendments are deemed accepted after two years unless a different period is set</li><li>Art 13 Denunciation: possible after five years from entry into force for that Party; takes effect 12 months after notice</li><li>Art 14: the IMO Secretary-General is the depositary</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex (as revised by MSC.561(108), in force 1 Jan 2026)</h2><p><b>Chapter I — General provisions</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I/1</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>I/2</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>I/3</td><td>Certificates and endorsements</td></tr><tr><td>I/4</td><td>Control procedures</td></tr><tr><td>I/5</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>I/6</td><td>Administration of certification arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>I/7</td><td>Recognition of certificates</td></tr><tr><td>I/8</td><td>Transitional provisions</td></tr><tr><td>I/9</td><td>Dispensation</td></tr><tr><td>I/10</td><td>Equivalents</td></tr><tr><td>I/11</td><td>Use of simulators</td></tr><tr><td>I/12</td><td>Medical standards</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Reg I/2: 300 GT is equivalent to 24 m, and 950 GT to 45 m</li><li>Transitional: certificates of competency (I/8.2) — up to five years after entry into force; certificates of service (I/8.3) — may be issued within two years after entry into force</li></ul><p><b>Chapter II — Certification of skippers, officers in charge of a navigational watch (OICNW), engineer officers and radio operators</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>II/1</td><td>Skippers, 24 m and over, unlimited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/2</td><td>OICNW, 24 m and over, unlimited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/3</td><td>Skippers, 24 m and over, limited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/4</td><td>OICNW, 24 m and over, limited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/5-1-1</td><td>Chief and second engineer officers, propulsion power 3,000 kW or more</td></tr><tr><td>II/5-1-2</td><td>Chief and second engineer officers, 750 kW to 3,000 kW</td></tr><tr><td>II/5-2</td><td>Officer in charge of an engineering watch or designated duty engineer, 750 kW or more</td></tr><tr><td>II/6</td><td>GMDSS radio operators</td></tr><tr><td>II/7</td><td>Revalidation for skippers and officers</td></tr><tr><td>II/8</td><td>Revalidation for GMDSS radio operators</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Chapter III — Basic training and onboard safety familiarization</b></p><ul><li>III/1: mandatory minimum requirements for all fishing vessel personnel</li></ul><p><b>Chapter IV — Watchkeeping</b></p><ul><li>IV/1 Fitness for duty · IV/2 Basic watchkeeping principles</li></ul><h2>Age, sea service and medical (MSC.561(108))</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Candidates for certification (I/12.4)</td><td>At least 16, or 15 if authorized by the competent authority</td></tr><tr><td>OICNW (II/2, II/4), OICEW (II/5-2), GMDSS radio operator (II/6)</td><td>At least 18</td></tr><tr><td>Skipper, unlimited waters (II/1)</td><td>12 months as OICNW or skipper on vessels of 12 m or more</td></tr><tr><td>OICNW, unlimited waters (II/2)</td><td>12 months in an approved training programme, or two years in the deck department</td></tr><tr><td>Second engineer, 3,000 kW or more (II/5-1-1)</td><td>12 months as qualified engineer officer</td></tr><tr><td>Chief engineer, 3,000 kW or more (II/5-1-1)</td><td>36 months, reducible to 24 months if at least 12 months were served as second engineer</td></tr><tr><td>Second engineer, 750–3,000 kW (II/5-1-2)</td><td>12 months as assistant or engineer officer</td></tr><tr><td>Chief engineer, 750–3,000 kW (II/5-1-2)</td><td>24 months, including at least 12 months qualified as second engineer</td></tr><tr><td>OICEW (II/5-2)</td><td>12 months of workshop training and sea service (at least six months on fishing or training vessels), or 12 months in the engine room</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Medical certificate (I/12.3): holders serving at sea must hold a valid medical certificate</li><li>Validity (I/12.5): maximum two years, or one year for crew under 18</li><li>Expiry during a voyage (I/12.6): valid until the next port with a recognized practitioner, for not more than three months</li><li>Urgent cases (I/12.7): work allowed for up to three months with a recently expired certificate</li><li>Revalidation (II/7.1, II/8.1): at intervals not exceeding five years, including medical fitness</li></ul><h2>Basic training (III/1)</h2><ul><li>Before any shipboard duty: approved basic training and onboard safety familiarization, to the standard of competence (III/1.1)</li><li>A certificate of proficiency is issued where basic training is not part of a certificate's qualification (III/1.2)</li><li>Code Table A-III/1 gives four areas:<ul><li>A-III/1-1 Personal survival techniques — including lifejacket and immersion suit, jumping from height, righting an inverted liferaft, streaming a drogue or sea anchor</li><li>A-III/1-2 Fire prevention and fire fighting — including fire triangle, detection, breathing apparatus, rescue in smoke-filled spaces</li><li>A-III/1-3 Elementary first aid — casualty assessment, resuscitation, bleeding, shock, burns, safe transport</li><li>A-III/1-4 Personal safety and social responsibilities — emergency procedures and muster duties, marine pollution precautions (including fishing gear and plastic waste), safe working practices, communication and working relationships, fatigue</li></ul></li></ul><h2>Watchkeeping (IV/1, IV/2)</h2><ul><li>Watch arrangements must prevent fatigue; the first watch and relieving watches must be rested and fit for duty (IV/1)</li><li>OICNW must be on the bridge, or a directly associated place, at all times (IV/2.2.1)</li><li>Radio operators keep a continuous radio watch on duty (IV/2.2.2)</li><li>Engineering watch officers must be immediately available and, when required, present in the machinery space (IV/2.2.3)</li><li>Very small fishing vessels in limited waters may be excluded from fully observing the basic principles (IV/2.3)</li><li>Code section A-IV/2 covers: navigational watch (to and from fishing grounds, pilot on board, while fishing, anchor watch) · engineering watch · radio watchkeeping</li></ul><h2>STCW-F Code (MSC.562(108))</h2><ul><li>Part A: mandatory standards. Part B: recommended guidance, numbered like the Convention's regulations</li><li>Competence standards grouped in seven functions (suffix -F): navigation · catch handling and stowage · controlling the operation of the vessel and care for persons on board · marine engineering · electrical, electronic and control engineering · maintenance and repair · radiocommunications</li><li>Three levels of responsibility: management, operational, support</li><li>Tables have four columns: competence · knowledge, understanding and proficiency · methods for demonstrating competence · criteria for evaluating competence</li><li>Table A-II/1 skippers (24 m and over, unlimited waters), A-II/2 OICNW (unlimited waters), A-II/3 skippers (limited waters) — competences include voyage planning, position fixing, watchkeeping, meteorology, manoeuvring, ECDIS, catch handling, stability, fire fighting, life-saving, medical care, maritime law</li><li>Table A-I/12: minimum in-service eyesight standards</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Fishing skippers and officers need STCW-F certificates, not the STCW merchant certificates</blockquote><blockquote>Certificates are revalidated at intervals not exceeding five years; the medical certificate lasts at most two years (one year under 18)</blockquote><blockquote>Port States can check the certificates of foreign fishing vessel crews (Art 8)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>STCW-F Convention text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1995-STCW.pdf</li><li>IMO — STCW-F Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/HumanElement/Pages/STCW-F-Convention.aspx</li><li>IMO — Raft of shipping rules in force from 1 January 2026: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>Revised Annex (MSC.561(108)): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/Revised%20annex%20of%20STCW-F%2095.pdf</li><li>STCW-F Code (MSC.562(108)): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/STCW-F%20Code.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-stp-1971","v":27994733,"title":"STP 1971","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["stp","special-trade","pilgrims","passenger-ships","space","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>STP 1971 — Special Trade Passenger Ships Agreement (and 1973 Space Protocol)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>STP Agreement 1971</th><th>Space Requirements Protocol 1973</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>6 Oct 1971</td><td>13 Jul 1973, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>2 Jan 1974</td><td>2 Jun 1977</td></tr><tr><td>Parties (IMO table, 5 Sep 2023)</td><td>18</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>6 months after 3 governments sign without reservation or deposit acceptance or accession (at least 2 with special trade ships or nationals carried)</td><td>Same rule, for governments party to the 1971 Agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Languages</td><td>English and French equally authentic</td><td>English and French equally authentic</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"stp-figures\"><figcaption>Key figures of the 1973 Space Rules</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The 1971 Agreement sets the general safety rules. The 1973 Protocol adds the space rules (headroom, deck area, bunks, ventilation, a space certificate) for passengers carried in special trades.</blockquote><h2>What is STP 1971</h2><ul><li>Sets minimum safety rules for <b>special trade passenger ships</b>, such as pilgrim ships, carrying large numbers of unberthed passengers, especially in the Indian Ocean area</li><li>Made after the 1931 <b>Simla Rules</b> became outdated. Among its parties the Agreement replaces and abrogates the Simla Rules (Art IV(b)).</li><li>Its annexed Rules modify chapters II and III of the <b>1960 SOLAS Convention</b> (IMO)</li><li>The Agreement and its Rules together are the general rules for the special trades referred to in SOLAS 1960 (Art IV(a))</li></ul><h2>STP 1971 — Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preamble</td><td>Parties to SOLAS 1960 recognize its chapters II and III could be modified for passenger ships carrying large numbers of unberthed passengers in special trades</td></tr><tr><td>I General obligations</td><td>Give effect to the Agreement and its Rules, an integral part of it</td></tr><tr><td>II Application</td><td>Passenger ships in the special trades registered in party countries. Such ships comply with the Convention's requirements for passenger ships, as modified by the Rules. Parties will consider applying later Convention revisions.</td></tr><tr><td>III Communication of information</td><td>Deposit with IMO: laws and regulations, specimens of certificates, list of non-governmental agencies authorized to act</td></tr><tr><td>IV Prior conventions</td><td>Replaces the Simla Rules, 1931 among parties</td></tr><tr><td>V Signature, acceptance, accession</td><td>Open for signature three months, then for accession. Open to SOLAS 1960 parties.</td></tr><tr><td>VI Entry into force</td><td>Six months after three governments, at least two with special trade ships or nationals carried in them · later acceptances: the later of entry into force or three months after deposit</td></tr><tr><td>VII Amendments</td><td>Unanimous acceptance, or a conference: adopted by two-thirds present and voting, in force 12 months after two-thirds accept. For acceptance by all, silence for 12 months counts as acceptance.</td></tr><tr><td>VIII Denunciation</td><td>After 5 years from entry into force for that government · effective 1 year after receipt</td></tr><tr><td>IX–XI</td><td>Territories · deposit and registration · languages</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>1973 Protocol — Space requirements</h2><ul><li>Technical rules on the space for carrying passengers, developed with bodies including the WHO (IMO)</li><li>Applies to passenger ships in special trades registered in states party to SOLAS 1960, the 1971 Agreement and the Protocol (Art II)</li><li>The Rules apply to new and existing ships, with relaxations for existing ships in Part III (Rule 3)</li><li>An Administration may exempt a ship for a single voyage in exceptional circumstances, except from the Part IV health rules (Rule 4)</li></ul><h3>Part II — Space rules</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>9 Unsuitable spaces</td><td>No passengers where clear headroom is <b>less than 1.90 m (6 ft 3 in)</b> · not forward of the collision bulkhead · not on lower between decks within 10% of the ship's length from the forward perpendicular · not on unsheathed weather decks</td></tr><tr><td>10 Number permitted</td><td>Limited by the Agreement, the bunk count (voyages of 72 hours or more) and airing space</td></tr><tr><td>11 Spaces without bunks</td><td>Area per passenger from the scale in Appendix I · airing space on between decks not less than <b>0.37 m² (4 sq ft)</b> per passenger · for voyages of 24 hours or more, passengers in a space no more than the gross volume divided by <b>3.06 m³ (108 cu ft)</b></td></tr><tr><td>12 Deductions</td><td>5% overall for baggage, plus deductions near stairways, lavatories, water taps, lifeboat areas and hatchways</td></tr><tr><td>13 Spaces with bunks</td><td>Required for voyages of <b>72 hours or more</b> · bunks at least <b>1.90 m × 0.70 m</b> · passageways at least 0.70 m wide · bunks kept 0.90 m from hatches, 0.60 m from the ship's side frames and 0.75 m from stairways · the 3.06 m³ limit also applies</td></tr><tr><td>15 Stairways</td><td>At least 0.05 m of aggregate width for every five passengers, minimum width 0.75 m</td></tr><tr><td>16 Lighting</td><td>Required in all passenger spaces, day and night</td></tr><tr><td>17 Ventilation</td><td>Trunked mechanical ventilation giving <b>at least 10 fresh air changes per hour</b>, or an approved air-conditioning system</td></tr><tr><td>18 Awnings</td><td>Weather protection on exposed passenger decks</td></tr><tr><td>19 Cooking</td><td>Passengers may not cook food on board</td></tr><tr><td>20 Hatchways</td><td>Guarded to at least 0.90 m when open</td></tr><tr><td>21 Obstruction</td><td>Passenger spaces, including airing spaces, kept free of cargo</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Part III — Existing ships (Rule 22)</h3><ul><li>Relaxed per-person areas of 0.9 m² and 1.12 m² for certain fair-weather voyages of 24 to 48 hours</li><li>Bunks of 1.80 m length allowed in some cases</li></ul><h3>Part IV — Health (Rule 23)</h3><ul><li>Ships comply with the International Health Regulations (1969)</li></ul><h3>Space certificate (Rules 5–8)</h3><ul><li><b>Special Trade Passenger Ship Space Certificate</b> issued after inspection</li><li>Valid for <b>not more than twelve months</b>, in the model form in Appendix II</li><li>Certificate or certified copy posted in a prominent place on board</li><li>Certificates issued by one party are accepted by other parties</li><li>Privileges under the Rules need a valid certificate</li></ul><h2>Key terms</h2><ul><li><b>Season of fair weather / season of foul weather:</b> defined in Rule 2 (used for the space scales)</li><li><b>Upper and lower between deck, gross volume of a space:</b> defined in Rule 2</li><li>The text speaks of spaces \"fitted with bunks\" and \"not fitted with bunks\"</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The per-passenger deck area table (Appendix I) and the Rules annexed to the 1971 Agreement</li><li>Definitions of \"special trade\" and \"special trade passenger ship\" (not in the text checked)</li><li>Current standing: IMO's treaty table (printed 5 Sep 2023) lists the dates and party counts only, with no note on amendment or replacement</li><li>The 1960 SOLAS chapters II and III modified by the Rules, as later superseded by SOLAS 1974</li><li>Practical figures on pilgrim trades and modern use of the Agreement</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Special Trade Passenger Ships: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Special-Trade-Passenger-Ships-.aspx</li><li>STP Agreement 1971 text: https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/08/8-03/passenger-ships.html</li><li>Protocol on Space Requirements 1973 text: https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/imo_space_prot.html</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (printed 5 Sep 2023): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status of IMO Treaties.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-sua-1988","v":214786516,"title":"SUA 1988","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["sua","security","unlawful-acts","terrorism","boarding","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>SUA 1988 — Convention for the Suppression of Unlawful Acts against the Safety of Maritime Navigation (with the 1988 Fixed Platforms Protocol and the 2005 Protocols)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SUA 1988 and Fixed Platforms Protocol</td><td>Adopted 10 Mar 1988, Rome · in force 1 Mar 1992</td></tr><tr><td>2005 Protocols</td><td>Adopted 14 Oct 2005 · in force 28 Jul 2010</td></tr><tr><td>Core duty</td><td>Make the offences crimes, take jurisdiction, <b>prosecute or extradite</b></td></tr><tr><td>2005 additions</td><td>Art 3bis terrorism-related offences · Art 8bis boarding procedures</td></tr><tr><td>In force rule (2005 Protocol, Art 18)</td><td>90 days after 12 States</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is SUA</h2><ul><li>Makes serious unlawful acts against ships international offences, and requires States to prosecute or extradite offenders (Arts 10–11)</li><li>The Fixed Platforms Protocol applies the same system to fixed platforms on the continental shelf; the 2005 amendments add Art 2bis and Art 2ter to it, for similar intimidation-based offences</li><li>Why: hijackings, kidnappings and ships deliberately grounded or blown up increased in the 1980s (IMO)</li><li>IMO's 14th Assembly (Nov 1985) backed a US proposal for preventive measures (resolution A.584(14); MSC/Circ.443 in 1986); in Nov 1986 Austria, Egypt and Italy proposed a convention, which led to the 1988 treaty</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>2005: the diplomatic conference ran 10–14 Oct 2005 and adopted the 2005 Protocols</li><li>2005 Protocol: open for signature 14 Feb 2006 – 13 Feb 2007, then accession (Art 17)</li></ul><hr><h2>Application</h2><p><b>Scope (Art 4)</b></p><ul><li>Applies when a ship is navigating, or scheduled to navigate, beyond the outer limit of the territorial sea of a single State (or beyond its lateral limits with adjacent States)</li><li>Also applies when the offender is found in another Party's territory (Art 4(2))</li></ul><p><b>Not covered (Art 2)</b></p><ul><li>Warships · State-owned or State-operated ships used as naval auxiliaries or for customs or police purposes · ships withdrawn from navigation or laid up</li><li>Art 2bis: saves other rights under international law (UN Charter, human rights, refugee and humanitarian law); does not cover armed forces in armed conflict; rights under the NPT, BWC and CWC are unaffected</li></ul><p><b>Ship (Art 1(1)(a))</b>: a vessel of any type whatsoever not permanently attached to the sea-bed</p><h2>Offences</h2><p><b>Art 3(1) — unlawfully and intentionally:</b></p><ul><li>(a) Seizing or exercising control over a ship by force, threat or intimidation</li><li>(b) Violence against a person on board, if likely to endanger the safe navigation of the ship</li><li>(c) Destroying a ship, or damaging it or its cargo, so as to endanger safe navigation</li><li>(d) Placing on a ship a device or substance likely to destroy or damage it</li><li>(e) Destroying or seriously damaging maritime navigational facilities or interfering with their operation, so as to endanger safe navigation</li><li>(f) Communicating information known to be false, endangering safe navigation</li><li><b>Art 3(2)</b>: threatening to commit (b), (c) or (e) to compel a person to do or refrain from an act, if the threat is likely to endanger safe navigation</li></ul><p><b>Added by the 2005 Protocol</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 3bis(1)(a)</b>: to intimidate a population or compel a government or organization — using a ship against or on it, or discharging from it, explosive, radioactive or BCN weapons, oil, LNG or other hazardous or noxious substances (where death, serious injury or damage results or is likely); using a ship in a manner that causes death or serious injury; threats</li><li><b>Art 3bis(1)(b)</b>: transporting on a ship explosives, radioactive material, BCN weapons, nuclear material or related equipment, knowing the intended use</li><li><b>Art 3ter</b>: transporting a person, knowing they committed a listed offence, to help them evade prosecution</li><li><b>Art 3quater</b>: injuring or killing in connection with an offence · attempts · complicity · organizing or directing others · contributing to a group's offences</li><li><b>Art 5bis</b>: legal entities can be held liable (criminal, civil or administrative) when a manager or controller commits an offence; sanctions effective, proportionate and dissuasive</li></ul><hr><h2>Jurisdiction, custody and extradition</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>Penalties reflecting the grave nature of the offences</td></tr><tr><td>6(1)</td><td><b>Mandatory</b> jurisdiction: offence against or on a ship flying the State's flag · in its territory (including territorial sea) · by its national</td></tr><tr><td>6(2)</td><td><b>Optional</b> jurisdiction: stateless person with habitual residence · victim is a national · aimed at compelling the State to act</td></tr><tr><td>6(4)</td><td>The State where the alleged offender is present, and does not extradite, establishes jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><b>Custody</b>: the State where the offender is present takes custody or other measures, makes a preliminary inquiry, lets the person contact their State's representative, and informs interested States</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><b>Delivery by the master</b> (see below)</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><b>Prosecute without exception, or extradite</b>; fair treatment guaranteed</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Offences are deemed extraditable; the Convention can be the legal basis</td></tr><tr><td>11bis</td><td>Offences are not political offences for extradition or mutual assistance</td></tr><tr><td>11ter</td><td>No obligation to extradite or assist if the request aims to persecute on grounds such as race, religion or nationality</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Greatest measure of mutual assistance</td></tr><tr><td>13–14</td><td>Co-operation on prevention and exchange of information; avoid undue delay of ships</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Reporting to the IMO Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Disputes: negotiation, then arbitration; ICJ if arbitration cannot be organized within six months; States may opt out</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Delivery by the master (Art 8)</b></p><ul><li>The master of a flag State ship may deliver a person to the authorities of another Party if there are reasonable grounds to believe the person committed an offence</li><li>The master should notify the receiving State in advance (8(2)) and provide the evidence in the master's possession (8(4))</li><li>The receiving State generally must accept delivery (8(3))</li></ul><h2>Boarding procedures — Art 8bis (2005 Protocol)</h2><ul><li>8bis(5): boarding a ship of another Party seaward of any territorial sea requires (a) confirmation of nationality, (b) a request for authorization, and (c) a flag State response — authorize, board itself, join in boarding, or decline</li><li>Boarding without the flag State's express authorization is prohibited</li><li>States may notify an optional rule: authorization deemed given if the flag State does not respond within <b>four hours</b></li><li>8bis(9): use of force must be minimal and necessary</li><li>8bis(10) safeguards: safety of life at sea · human dignity and human rights · environmental soundness · notice to the master · States are liable for unfounded or unlawful measures and must give effective recourse · only identifiable government ships and aircraft may act · officials show government identification</li><li>8bis(15): Parties designate competent authorities and notify the Secretary-General within one month</li></ul><h2>Final clauses (2005 Protocol)</h2><ul><li>Art 18: in force 90 days after 12 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Art 19: denunciation takes effect one year after deposit</li><li>Art 20: revision conference by IMO, or at the request of one third of States Parties or ten, whichever is higher</li><li>Art 24: authentic in Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian and Spanish</li><li>Annex: lists nine treaties (aviation, hostage-taking, nuclear material, terrorist bombings, terrorism financing and others) used for Art 3ter</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>SUA is criminal law after an attack — prevention is in SOLAS XI-2 and the ISPS Code</blockquote><blockquote>The master may deliver a suspect to a Party's authorities, giving evidence and advance notice</blockquote><blockquote>Boarding at sea needs the flag State's authorization (or the optional 4-hour rule)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>SUA Convention, consolidated with the 2005 Protocol (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/safety-maritime-consolidated.html</li><li>IMO — SUA Treaties: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/SUA-Treaties.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-salvage-1989","v":69645737,"title":"Salvage 1989","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["salvage","lof","special-compensation","environment","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Salvage 1989 — International Convention on Salvage</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>28 Apr 1989, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>14 Jul 1996</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>1910 Brussels salvage convention (\"no cure, no pay\")</td></tr><tr><td>New feature</td><td>Enhanced award and special compensation for protecting the environment</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>1 year after 15 States (Art 29)</td></tr><tr><td>Time bar</td><td>2 years from the end of the salvage operation (Art 23)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is Salvage 1989</h2><ul><li>Rules for salvage operations: duties and rights of salvors, owners and masters, and how rewards are set</li><li>The 1910 rule ignored pollution: a salvor who prevented a major spill but could not save the ship or cargo got nothing, so there was little reason to try long-odds operations (IMO)</li><li>The 1989 Convention gives an enhanced award that counts the salvor's skill and efforts to prevent or minimize environmental damage</li><li>The Convention applies unless a contract provides otherwise (Art 6(1))</li></ul><h2>Applies to (Arts 2–5)</h2><ul><li>Art 2: when judicial or arbitral proceedings are brought in a State Party</li><li>Art 3: <b>not</b> fixed or floating platforms or mobile offshore drilling units engaged in sea-bed mineral work, when on location</li><li>Art 4: <b>not</b> warships and non-commercial State vessels with sovereign immunity, unless the State decides otherwise (and notifies the Secretary-General)</li><li>Art 5: does not affect national law or conventions on salvage controlled by public authorities</li></ul><hr><h2>Chapter I — General (Arts 1–7)</h2><p><b>Art 1 definitions</b></p><ul><li>(a) Salvage operation: any act or activity undertaken to assist a vessel or any other property in danger</li><li>(b) Vessel · (c) Property · (e) Payment · (f) Organization (IMO) · (g) Secretary-General</li><li>(d) <b>Damage to the environment</b>: substantial physical damage to human health or to marine life or resources in coastal or inland waters or areas adjacent to them, caused by pollution, contamination, fire, explosion or similar major incidents</li></ul><p><b>Contracts</b></p><ul><li>Art 6: the master has authority to conclude salvage contracts for the owner of the vessel</li><li>Art 7: a contract can be annulled or modified if made under undue influence or the influence of danger and its terms are inequitable, or if the payment is grossly too large or too small</li></ul><h2>Chapter II — Performance of salvage operations (Arts 8–11)</h2><ul><li><b>Art 8 — Salvor:</b> act with due care · take due care to prevent or minimize damage to the environment · seek help from other salvors when circumstances require · accept the intervention of other salvors when reasonably asked</li><li><b>Art 8 — Owner and master:</b> co-operate fully with the salvor · take due care to prevent or minimize damage to the environment · accept redelivery of the vessel or property when it is reasonably asked and the salvor has secured a place of safety</li><li><b>Art 9:</b> coastal State keeps its right to take measures to protect its coastline from pollution, including giving directions about salvage operations</li><li><b>Art 10 — Duty to render assistance:</b> every master must, as far as he can do so without serious danger to his vessel and persons on board, render assistance to any person in danger of being lost at sea; States Parties adopt measures to enforce this; the owner is not liable for the master's breach</li><li><b>Art 11:</b> States should consider the need for co-operation with salvors when deciding on admission of vessels in distress to ports</li></ul><h2>Chapter III — Rights of salvors (Arts 12–19)</h2><ul><li><b>Art 12:</b> a useful result is needed for a reward; no useful result, no payment unless otherwise provided</li><li><b>Art 13(1) — Criteria for the reward</b> (no order of priority):<ul><li>(a) salved value of the vessel and other property</li><li>(b) skill and efforts of the salvors in preventing or minimizing damage to the environment</li><li>(c) measure of success obtained</li><li>(d) nature and degree of the danger</li><li>(e) skill and efforts of the salvors in salving the vessel, other property and life</li><li>(f) time used and expenses and losses incurred</li><li>(g) risk of liability and other risks run by the salvors or their equipment</li><li>(h) promptness of the services</li><li>(i) availability and use of vessels or other equipment intended for salvage</li><li>(j) state of readiness and efficiency of the salvor's equipment, and its value</li></ul></li><li>Art 13(2): payment is shared by the vessel and other property interests in proportion to their salved values</li><li>Art 13(3): the reward, excluding interest and recoverable legal costs, cannot exceed the salved value of the vessel and other property</li></ul><p><b>Art 14 — Special compensation</b></p><ul><li>14(1): if the salvor worked on a vessel which, by itself or its cargo, threatened damage to the environment, and did not earn a reward under Art 13 at least equal to the special compensation, the salvor is entitled to special compensation from the owner, equal to the salvor's expenses</li><li>14(3): <b>salvor's expenses</b> = out-of-pocket expenses reasonably incurred, plus a fair rate for equipment and personnel actually and reasonably used</li><li>14(2): if the salvor prevented or minimized environmental damage, the compensation may be increased up to <b>30%</b> of expenses; a tribunal may increase it further if fair and just, but the total increase cannot be more than <b>100%</b> of expenses</li><li>14(4): special compensation is paid only to the extent it exceeds the Art 13 reward</li><li>14(5): if the salvor was negligent and failed to prevent or minimize environmental damage, special compensation may be denied or reduced</li><li>14(6): the owner keeps his right of recourse</li></ul><p><b>Other</b></p><ul><li>Art 15: apportionment among salvors by the Art 13 criteria; division between owner, master and crew of the salving vessel by the law of its flag</li><li>Art 16: no payment from persons whose lives are saved (national law may differ); life salvors share fairly in the reward for salving the vessel or property or preventing environmental damage</li><li>Art 17: no payment for services under a contract, unless they go beyond what the contract reasonably requires</li><li>Art 18: payment may be reduced or removed for fault, neglect, fraud or dishonest conduct</li><li>Art 19: no payment for services rendered against the express and reasonable prohibition of the owner or master</li></ul><h2>Chapter IV — Claims and actions (Arts 20–27)</h2><ul><li>Art 20: the salvor's maritime lien is not affected; it cannot be enforced once adequate security has been provided</li><li>Art 21: on request, the liable party provides security; salved property is not removed from the port or place where it is first brought, without the salvor's consent, until security is provided</li><li>Art 22: interim payment may be ordered by a tribunal</li><li>Art 23: actions must be started within <b>two years</b> from the day the salvage operations ended</li><li>Art 24: interest — by the law of the State where the tribunal sits</li><li>Art 25: no seizure of State-owned non-commercial cargo with sovereign immunity without the State's consent</li><li>Art 26: no seizure of donated humanitarian cargo if the donor State agreed to pay for the salvage</li><li>Art 27: States should encourage publication of arbitral awards, with the parties' consent</li></ul><h2>Chapter V — Final clauses (Arts 28–34)</h2><ul><li>Art 28: open for signature 1 Jul 1989 – 30 Jun 1990, then accession</li><li>Art 29: in force one year after 15 States consented</li><li>Art 30 reservations: a State may exclude (a) inland-waters salvage with only inland navigation vessels, (b) inland-waters salvage with no vessel involved, (c) cases where all interested parties are its nationals, (d) maritime cultural property of historic interest on the sea-bed</li><li>Art 31: denunciation after one year from entry into force for that State; effective one year after receipt</li><li>Art 32: revision conference — by IMO, or by the Secretary-General at the request of eight States or one quarter of the Parties, whichever is higher</li><li>Art 34: six authentic languages</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The master's duty to save life at sea (Art 10) is a separate duty from a property salvage contract</blockquote><blockquote>The master may conclude a salvage contract for the vessel's owner (Art 6) — inform the company and P&amp;I at once</blockquote><blockquote>Environmental protection is paid through special compensation (Art 14) even if the ship is lost</blockquote><blockquote>Services against an express and reasonable prohibition are not paid (Art 19)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Salvage 1989 treaty text (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/convention-salvage.html</li><li>IMO — International Convention on Salvage: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-Salvage.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-tonnage-1969","v":52621338,"title":"Tonnage 1969","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["tonnage","gross-tonnage","net-tonnage","itc","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Tonnage 1969 — International Convention on Tonnage Measurement of Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>23 Jun 1969</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>18 Jul 1982</td></tr><tr><td>Existing ships</td><td>Could keep old tonnage until 18 Jul 1994 (12 years)</td></tr><tr><td>Gross tonnage</td><td>GT = K1 × V</td></tr><tr><td>Net tonnage</td><td>Never less than 0.30 GT</td></tr><tr><td>Not applied to</td><td>Warships · ships under 24 m in length (Art 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Tonnage Certificate (1969)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is Tonnage 1969</h2><ul><li>IMO calls it \"the first successful attempt to introduce a universal tonnage measurement system\"</li><li>Replaced varying national methods, all descended from Moorsom's 1854 approach</li><li>Separate <b>gross tonnage (GT)</b> and <b>net tonnage (NT)</b>, each calculated independently; the terms changed from grt and nrt to GT and NT</li><li>GT depends on the moulded volume of all enclosed spaces; NT comes from a formula based on the moulded volume of cargo spaces</li><li>GT governs manning, safety and registration fees; both GT and NT are used for port dues (IMO)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Open for signature six months from 23 Jun 1969, then accession (Art 16)</li><li>Art 17: in force <b>24 months after at least 25 Governments, whose merchant fleets are at least 65% of world gross tonnage</b>, signed without reservation or deposited instruments → in force <b>18 Jul 1982</b></li><li>Existing ships could keep their old tonnage for 12 years to protect operators from higher charges tied to tonnage</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles (the Convention has 23; Arts 1–18 below)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General obligation — the Annexes are an integral part</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Exceptions</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Force majeure</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Determination of tonnages</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Issue of certificate</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Certificate issued by another Government</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Form of certificate</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Cancellation</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Inspection</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Privileges</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Prior treaties</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Signature and accession</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Coming into force</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Amendments</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Key articles</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 2</b>: nine defined terms — Regulations, Administration, international voyage, gross tonnage, net tonnage, new ship (keel laid on or after entry into force), existing ship, length, Organization. <b>Length</b> = 96% of the total length on a waterline at 85% of the least moulded depth, or the length from the foreside of the stem to the axis of the rudder stock on that waterline if greater (Art 2(8))</li><li><b>Art 3</b>: applies to ships on international voyages that are registered in Contracting States, or unregistered and flying their flag; covers new ships, existing ships with substantial alterations, existing ships on owner's request, and all existing ships 12 years after entry into force</li><li><b>Art 4</b>: excludes warships and ships under 24 m in length; also ships navigating only the Great Lakes and St Lawrence, the Caspian Sea, or the Plate, Paraná and Uruguay Rivers</li><li><b>Art 5</b>: deviation due to weather or other force majeure does not make a ship subject to the Convention</li><li><b>Art 6</b>: the Administration determines tonnages; may delegate to recognised persons or organizations but keeps full responsibility</li><li><b>Art 7</b>: an International Tonnage Certificate (1969) is issued to every ship whose tonnages were determined under the Convention</li><li><b>Art 8</b>: another Contracting Government may determine tonnages and issue the certificate at the flag State's request; none to ships of non-Contracting States</li><li><b>Art 9</b>: in the issuing country's language, with English or French translation; follows the Annex II model</li><li><b>Art 10</b>: cancelled when alterations increase gross or net tonnage; generally lapses on transfer to another flag (for a transfer between Contracting States, up to three months or until replaced)</li><li><b>Art 11</b>: other Contracting Governments accept the certificate as valid as their own</li><li><b>Art 12</b>: port inspection is limited to checking the certificate's validity and that the main characteristics match; must not delay the ship; flag State informed of any tonnage increase</li><li><b>Art 13</b>: Convention privileges only for ships with a valid certificate</li><li><b>Art 18</b>: amendments in force 12 months after acceptance by two-thirds of Contracting Governments</li></ul><hr><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"tonnage-k1\"><figcaption>K1 against volume, as listed in the Annex</figcaption></figure><h2>Annex I — Regulations</h2><p><b>Reg 2 — Definitions</b></p><ul><li><b>Upper deck</b>: the uppermost complete deck exposed to weather and sea, with permanent weathertight closings, and watertight closings for side openings below it</li><li><b>Moulded depth</b>: vertical distance from the top of the keel to the underside of the upper deck at side</li><li><b>Breadth</b>: maximum breadth amidships (moulded frame line for metal shells)</li><li><b>Enclosed spaces</b>: spaces bounded by the hull, fixed or portable partitions, decks or coverings other than permanent or movable awnings; breaks or openings do not by themselves exclude a space</li><li><b>Excluded spaces</b> (a)–(e): for example spaces within an erection opposite an end opening · spaces under an open overhead deck covering · spaces in a side-to-side erection opposite side openings · spaces immediately below an uncovered weather-exposed deck opening, limited to the opening's area · recesses in a bulkhead with weather-exposed deck-to-deck opening</li><li><b>Passenger</b>: any person other than the master, crew or others employed on the ship's business, and other than a child under one year old</li><li><b>Cargo spaces</b> (for NT): enclosed spaces for cargo to be discharged, already counted in GT, marked \"CC\" at least 100 mm high</li></ul><p><b>Reg 3 — Gross tonnage</b></p><blockquote>GT = K1 × V</blockquote><blockquote>V = total volume of all enclosed spaces, in m³ · K1 = 0.2 + 0.02 log₁₀ V (or the Appendix 2 table, with interpolation)</blockquote><table><thead><tr><th>V (m³)</th><th>K1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100</td><td>0.2400</td></tr><tr><td>1,000</td><td>0.2600</td></tr><tr><td>10,000</td><td>0.2800</td></tr><tr><td>100,000</td><td>0.3000</td></tr><tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>0.3200</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Reg 4 — Net tonnage</b></p><blockquote>NT = K2 × Vc × (4d/3D)² + K3 × (N1 + N2/10)</blockquote><ul><li>Vc = total volume of cargo spaces (m³) · K2 = 0.2 + 0.02 log₁₀ Vc</li><li>K3 = 1.25 × (GT + 10,000) / 10,000</li><li>D = moulded depth amidships (m) · d = moulded draught amidships (m)</li><li>N1 = passengers in cabins with not more than 8 berths · N2 = other passengers · N1 + N2 = passengers permitted by the passenger certificate; if less than 13, N1 and N2 are zero</li><li>Limits: (4d/3D)² not more than 1 · the first term K2 × Vc × (4d/3D)² not less than 0.25 GT · <b>NT not less than 0.30 GT</b></li><li><b>Moulded draught d</b>: (a) ships under the Load Line Convention — the Summer Load Line draught (excluding timber) · (b) passenger ships — deepest subdivision load line draught · (c) ships with a national load line — summer load line draught · (d) ships with a restricted draught and no load line — maximum permitted draught · (e) other ships — 75% of the moulded depth amidships</li></ul><p><b>Reg 5 — Change of net tonnage</b></p><ul><li>Increase: apply the new net tonnage without delay</li><li>Decrease: a new certificate with lower net tonnage cannot be issued until 12 months after the current certificate was issued, except on transfer to another flag, after major alterations approved by the Administration, or for passenger ships in special trades (such as the pilgrim trade)</li></ul><p><b>Reg 6 and 7</b></p><ul><li>Volumes measured to the inner side of metal shell or structural boundary plating, regardless of insulation; volume of appendages included; volumes of spaces open to the sea may be excluded</li><li>Measurements to the nearest centimetre; volumes by generally accepted methods acceptable to the Administration</li></ul><h2>Annex II — International Tonnage Certificate (1969)</h2><ul><li>Ship name, distinctive number or letters, port of registry</li><li>Date keel laid (or similar stage), or date of major alteration</li><li>Main dimensions: length, breadth, moulded depth to upper deck</li><li><b>Gross tonnage and net tonnage</b></li><li>Tables of spaces included in GT and NT; passengers (N1, N2); excluded spaces and moulded draught; dates and places of original and last measurement</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The International Tonnage Certificate (1969) must be on board and match the ship</blockquote><blockquote>GT is a number from volume, not weight; DWT is weight</blockquote><blockquote>Alterations that increase tonnage cancel the certificate (Art 10)</blockquote><blockquote>A lower net tonnage cannot be certified until 12 months after the current certificate (Reg 5)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Convention text, Arts 1–23 (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/imo_tonnage.html</li><li>Annexes I–II (UK Treaty Series 1982 No 50): https://treaties.fcdo.gov.uk/data/Library2/pdf/1982-TS0050.pdf</li><li>Regulation 4 formula check (Dutch treaty text): https://inview.nl/openCitation/idd622cd2688aed2d5f2ae37f1ef323f8f/international-convention-on-tonnage-measurement-of-ships-1969-bijlage-i</li><li>IMO — Tonnage Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/page/international-convention-on-tonnage-measurement-of-ships.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-torremolinos-1977","v":41331629,"title":"Torremolinos 1977","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["torremolinos","sfv","fishing-vessels","safety","protocol-1993","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Torremolinos 1977 — International Convention for the Safety of Fishing Vessels (and the 1993 Protocol)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Convention</td><td>Adopted 2 Apr 1977, Torremolinos, Spain · <b>never entered into force</b></td></tr><tr><td>1993 Protocol</td><td>Adopted Apr 1993 (done at Torremolinos, 2 Apr 1993) · <b>not in force</b></td></tr><tr><td>Replaced by</td><td>Cape Town Agreement 2012 (see Cape Town Agreement 2012)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>New, decked, seagoing fishing vessels of <b>24 m in length and over</b>, including those that process their catch</td></tr><tr><td>Why it matters</td><td>The first international convention on the safety of fishing vessels. Its Annex is the technical basis of today's rules.</td></tr><tr><td>Depositary</td><td>IMO Secretary-General</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"tor-path\"><figcaption>From 1977 to the Cape Town Agreement</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Neither the 1977 Convention nor the 1993 Protocol is in force. The rules live on through the Cape Town Agreement 2012, which brings the Protocol's technical rules into force. Conference Resolution 2 says the Agreement \"replaces and supersedes\" the 1993 Protocol, and States should become Parties to the Agreement instead.</blockquote><h2>What is Torremolinos 1977</h2><ul><li>Set safety requirements for the construction and equipment of those vessels</li><li>Recognised that fishing vessels differ from other ships: they sail empty and load their catch at sea</li><li>Existing vessels were covered only for radio requirements (IAPH summary of the 1977 text)</li><li>Subject areas: stability · construction, watertight integrity and equipment · machinery, electrical installations and unattended machinery spaces · fire protection · protection of the crew · life-saving appliances · emergency procedures, musters and drills · radiocommunications · shipborne navigational equipment</li><li>Port State officers verify that a valid certificate is on board (Art 4)</li></ul><h2>Why it never entered into force</h2><ul><li>Entry into force required at least 15 States with fleets of at least 50% of the world's 24 m and over fishing fleet by number, plus 12 months (IAPH summary of Art 10)</li><li>In the 1980s it became clear that it was unlikely to enter into force, largely for technical reasons (IMO)</li><li>IMO therefore prepared the <b>1993 Protocol</b> to update, amend and absorb the 1977 Convention</li></ul><h2>1993 Protocol — Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 General obligations</td><td>Parties give effect to the Protocol and to the regulations in the Annex of the 1977 Convention, as modified. The two are read as <b>one instrument</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>2 Definitions</td><td>\"Fishing vessel\": any vessel used commercially for catching fish, whales, seals, walrus or other living resources of the sea</td></tr><tr><td>3 Application</td><td>Seagoing vessels flying a Party's flag (3(1)) · <b>excluded</b>: vessels used only for sport or recreation, fish processing, research and training, or as fish carriers (3(2)) · the Annex applies to vessels of <b>24 m and over</b> unless stated otherwise (3(3)) · where a chapter sets a longer limit, the Administration decides which regulations apply to vessels from 24 m up to that limit (3(4)) · Parties seek uniform regional standards (3(5))</td></tr><tr><td>4 Certification and port State control</td><td>Port officers may verify certificate validity. A valid certificate is accepted unless there are clear grounds to doubt it. A vessel with an invalid certificate may be held in port. A vessel unduly detained is entitled to compensation (4(6)).</td></tr><tr><td>5 Force majeure</td><td>Deviation due to weather or force majeure does not bring a vessel under the Protocol. Persons aboard for force majeure or rescue are not counted.</td></tr><tr><td>6 Information</td><td>Parties send laws, lists of authorized bodies and certificate specimens to IMO</td></tr><tr><td>7 Casualties</td><td>Parties may investigate casualties and share findings with IMO, without identifying vessels or assigning fault</td></tr><tr><td>8 Other treaties</td><td>No prejudice to legal views on the law of the sea or on coastal and flag State jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>9 Signature and accession</td><td>Open 1 Jul 1993 to 30 Jun 1994, then accession. Parties report their fleet counts of 24 m and over vessels.</td></tr><tr><td>10 Entry into force</td><td>12 months after <b>not less than 15 States</b> whose 24 m and over fishing vessels total <b>not less than 14,000</b> are Parties. Later ratifications take effect 3 months after deposit.</td></tr><tr><td>11 Amendments</td><td>IMO route: articles need two-thirds of Parties. Annex amendments are deemed accepted after 2 years from adoption, or another period of at least 1 year, unless more than one-third of Parties, or Parties with 65% of the fleet, object. Conference route also possible. Structural amendments apply only to vessels whose keel is laid or whose construction begins on or after entry into force (11(5)).</td></tr><tr><td>12 Denunciation</td><td>After 5 years from a Party's entry into force, effective 12 months after notice</td></tr><tr><td>13–14</td><td>Depositary: the IMO Secretary-General · languages: Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian and Spanish, all equally authentic</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>The Annex — ten chapters</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"tor-chapters\"><figcaption>Annex chapters and their length thresholds</figcaption></figure><p>The Annex of the 1977 Convention is the technical core. As carried into the Cape Town Agreement, it has ten chapters:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Main application rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>General provisions</td><td>New vessels (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Construction, watertight integrity and equipment</td><td>Length limits in individual regulations (45 m, 75 m)</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Stability and associated seaworthiness</td><td>No general length limit</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Machinery and electrical installations and periodically unattended machinery spaces</td><td>45 m and over</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Fire protection, fire detection, fire extinction and fire fighting</td><td>New vessels 45 m and over</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Protection of the crew</td><td>See individual regulations</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Life-saving appliances and arrangements</td><td>New vessels 45 m and over</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>Emergency procedures, musters and drills</td><td>New and existing vessels 24 m and over</td></tr><tr><td>IX</td><td>Radiocommunications</td><td>New and existing vessels 45 m and over</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>Shipborne navigational equipment and arrangements</td><td>New and existing vessels; each regulation sets its own limit</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Chapter I — General provisions (Regulations 1–5)</h3><ul><li><b>Reg 1 Application:</b> the Annex applies to new vessels unless stated otherwise. The Administration may use gross tonnage instead of length (L) for all chapters, with <b>300 GT equal to 24 m</b>. Existing vessels may be brought into compliance progressively under a plan: up to <b>10 years</b> for chapter IX and up to <b>5 years</b> for chapters VII, VIII and X. The Administration may also exempt a vessel from annual surveys.</li><li><b>Reg 2 Definitions:</b> new vessel, existing vessel, length (L), breadth, moulded depth, superstructure, weathertight, watertight, collision bulkhead, gross tonnage, anniversary date. Collision bulkhead position: for vessels of 45 m and over, not less than 0.05L and not more than 0.08L from the forward perpendicular. For vessels under 45 m, within 0.05L plus 1.35 m, except as the Administration allows.</li><li><b>Reg 3 Exemptions:</b> for vessels with novel features, and for certain vessels operating in specified zones, subject to safety requirements</li><li><b>Reg 4 Equivalents:</b> alternatives at least as effective as those required</li><li><b>Reg 5 Repairs, alterations and modifications:</b> the vessel must keep meeting at least its previously applicable requirements. Major changes meet new-vessel requirements as far as the Administration finds reasonable and practicable.</li></ul><h2>Where the rules are enforced today</h2><ul><li>See Cape Town Agreement 2012: adopted 11 Oct 2012, enters into force in February 2027, the International Fishing Vessel Safety Certificate (up to 5 years), surveys and bottom inspections</li><li>See STCW-F 1995: training and certification of fishing vessel personnel</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Full text of the 1977 Convention articles, and the exact entry-into-force wording of its Art 10 (the figure above comes from an IAPH summary)</li><li>Regulation-level detail of chapters II–X (stability criteria such as GZ areas and GM values, fire protection, life-saving numbers). These are not in the Protocol text checked.</li><li>Chapter titles as printed in the 1977 text (the titles above follow the Cape Town Agreement's Annex)</li><li>Number of States that ratified the 1977 Convention or the 1993 Protocol, and the later 1993 status table</li><li>Technical amendments to the Annex over time</li><li>Any 1977 Final Act resolutions</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — The Torremolinos International Convention for the Safety of Fishing Vessels: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/The-Torremolinos-International-Convention-for-the-Safety-of-Fishing-Vessels.aspx</li><li>1993 Torremolinos Protocol text (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/08/8-03/safety-fishing-vessels.html</li><li>IAPH legal database — Torremolinos Convention 1977: https://www.iaphworldports.org/n-iaph/wp-content/uploads/legal-db/A-7-THE-TORREMOLINOS-INTERNATIONAL-CONVENTION-FOR-THE-SAFETY-OF-FISHING-VESSELS.pdf</li><li>PAME — Cape Town Agreement chapter 1 summary: https://www.pame.is/images/03_Projects/CTA/CTA_Chapters/Summaries/Chapter_1_-_Part_A_Summary.pdf</li><li>Cape Town Agreement 2012 note in this collection (Annex chapters, verified earlier)</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-alerts-and-indicators-code-2009","v":198323147,"title":"Alerts and Indicators Code 2009","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["alarms","alerts","indicators","general-alarm","bridge","machinery","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>Alerts and Indicators Code 2009 — Code on Alerts and Indicators</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>2 Dec 2009</b>, IMO Assembly Resolution <b>A.1021(26)</b> (26th session). Document dated 18 Jan 2010.</td></tr><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>Resolution <b>A.830(19)</b>, the 1995 Code on Alarms and Indicators</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Governments are recommended to implement it and to use it as an <b>international safety standard for designing alarms and indicators</b> on ships, equipment and machinery</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Design guidance and uniform <b>type, location and priority</b> of the alerts and indicators required by SOLAS, MARPOL and IMO codes</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Sections 1–11 plus an appendix with sample indicator columns</td></tr><tr><td>Related</td><td>MSC.302(87), Bridge Alert Management performance standards (17 May 2010)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"alert-priorities\"><figcaption>The four alert priorities</figcaption></figure><h2>Alert priorities (3.1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Emergency alarm</b> (3.1.1)</td><td>Immediate danger to human life or to the ship and its machinery exists, requiring immediate action</td></tr><tr><td><b>Alarm</b> (3.1.2)</td><td>High-priority alert. Needs immediate attention and action to maintain safe navigation and operation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Warning</b> (3.1.3)</td><td>No immediate attention or action needed. Precautionary, for changed conditions that are not yet hazardous but may become so.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Caution</b> (3.1.4)</td><td>Lowest priority. A condition that does not warrant an alarm or warning but needs attention beyond normal consideration.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Other definitions</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Indicator (3.4)</td><td>Visual indication of the condition of a system or equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Signal (3.5)</td><td>Audible indication of the condition</td></tr><tr><td>Required (3.6)</td><td>Alerts and indicators required by the instruments listed in 1.1. All others are non-required.</td></tr><tr><td>Call (3.7)</td><td>A request for contact, assistance or action</td></tr><tr><td>Silence (3.8)</td><td>Manually stopping an audible signal</td></tr><tr><td>Acknowledge (3.9)</td><td>Manual response to receiving an alert or call</td></tr><tr><td>Aggregation (3.10)</td><td>Combining individual alerts into one</td></tr><tr><td>Grouping (3.11)</td><td>Arranging alerts or indicators on panels</td></tr><tr><td>Prioritization (3.12)</td><td>Ordering alerts by severity, function or sequence</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which alerts are which</h2><h3>Emergency alarms (3.2)</h3><ul><li>General emergency alarm</li><li>Fire alarm</li><li>Water ingress detection <b>main</b> alarm</li><li>Alerts warning of immediate personnel hazard: <b>fire-extinguishing pre-discharge alarm</b> and <b>power-operated sliding watertight door closing alarm</b></li><li>Additional emergency alarms may be defined for special ships such as high-speed craft</li></ul><h3>Alarms (3.3)</h3><p>Machinery · steering gear · control system fault · bilge · water ingress <b>pre</b>-alarm · engineers' · personnel · BNWAS second and third stage remote audible · fire detection · fixed local application fire-extinguishing activation · faults in alert management or detection systems or loss of their power · cargo · gas detection · power-operated watertight door faults · navigation-related alarms under INS</p><ul><li>No specific warnings or cautions are listed in the annex. Examples in Table 10.1.1: a <b>BNWAS malfunction or power failure is a warning</b>, and a condition needing action to prevent degradation to an unsafe condition is a caution.</li></ul><h2>General requirements (Section 4)</h2><ul><li><b>4.1:</b> clear, distinctive, unambiguous, consistent presentation</li><li><b>4.2:</b> required alerts need <b>both audible and visual</b> indication. Emergency alarms are primarily signal-based. In high-noise machinery spaces signals are supplemented by indicators.</li><li><b>4.4:</b> a new alert must be clearly distinguishable from acknowledged ones. Unacknowledged alarms and warnings <b>flash and sound</b>, acknowledged ones show a <b>constant light</b>. Audible signals stop when silenced or acknowledged.</li><li><b>4.5:</b> alerts stay until acknowledged. Visual indication stays until the fault is corrected. A second fault after acknowledgement repeats the signals.</li><li><b>4.6:</b> reset only after the abnormal condition is rectified</li><li><b>4.8:</b> required alert systems continuously powered with automatic changeover to standby power</li><li><b>4.10:</b> failure of the alert power supply triggers an audible and visual alarm or warning</li><li><b>4.11:</b> fail-to-safety where practicable. A detection circuit fault gives an audible and visual alarm.</li><li><b>4.12:</b> alerts must be testable, and crews trained and drilled on all alerts</li><li><b>4.13:</b> alert systems functionally independent of control systems, or with equivalent redundancy</li><li><b>4.15:</b> cables for fire and emergency alarms fire-resistant through high fire-risk areas and routed to limit total loss from fire, collision or flooding</li><li><b>4.16:</b> general emergency, fire and pre-discharge signals work despite failure of any one circuit or component, where practicable</li><li><b>4.17:</b> time delays or similar means prevent false alerts from normal transients</li><li><b>4.18:</b> non-required alerts on the bridge minimized</li></ul><h2>Sound and light</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"alert-signal\"><figcaption>Audible and visual characteristics</figcaption></figure><h3>Audible (Section 5)</h3><ul><li><b>5.1:</b> required alerts clearly audible and distinguishable throughout the spaces where required</li><li><b>5.2:</b> the fire-extinguishing pre-discharge alarm and fire alarms easily distinguishable from all other signals</li><li><b>5.6:</b> adjusting the sound pressure level of required signals is <b>not permitted</b></li><li><b>5.10:</b> general emergency alarm performance follows LSA Code Chapter VII, 7.2. <b>No signal may exceed 120 dB(A)</b> in a space.</li><li><b>5.11:</b> except bells, audible signals between <b>200 Hz and 2,500 Hz</b></li><li><b>5.13:</b> bridge navigational alerts <b>75–85 dB(A) at one metre</b>, or at least 10 dB(A) above ambient noise with an upper limit of 85 dB(A)</li></ul><h3>Visual (Section 6)</h3><ul><li><b>6.1:</b> supplemental indicators in high-noise machinery spaces and accommodation clearly visible from all parts of the space, of high luminous intensity, multiple in large spaces</li><li><b>6.2:</b> flashing indicators lit for at least <b>50% of the cycle</b>, at <b>0.5–1.5 Hz</b></li><li><b>6.3:</b> bridge indicators must not interfere with night vision</li><li><b>6.5:</b> colours follow ISO 2412, as the Administration deems appropriate</li><li><b>6.6:</b> on MODUs the general emergency alarm indicator may be amber if it flashes at <b>4 Hz or faster</b></li></ul><h2>Colours (Tables 7.1.1–7.1.3)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"alert-colours\"><figcaption>Indicator colours</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Alert</th><th>Colour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General emergency alarm</td><td><b>Green/white</b> for summoning passengers and crew. <b>Red</b> for summoning crew to fire stations on passenger ships.</td></tr><tr><td>Fire alarm</td><td><b>Red</b></td></tr><tr><td>Fire-extinguishing pre-discharge alarm</td><td><b>Red</b> for CO₂</td></tr><tr><td>Sliding watertight door closing alarm</td><td>Red and green indicators. The red bridge indicator flashes while the door closes.</td></tr><tr><td>Water ingress main alarm</td><td><b>Red</b></td></tr><tr><td>Machinery, steering gear, control system fault, bilge, engineers', personnel, water ingress pre-alarm, alert system fault, cargo, gas detection (except chlorine), watertight door fault alarms</td><td><b>Amber</b></td></tr><tr><td>Fire detection, fixed local application fire-extinguishing activation, <b>chlorine</b> gas alarms</td><td><b>Red</b></td></tr><tr><td>Calls (telephone, engine-room telegraph)</td><td><b>White</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Audible waveforms (Table 7.2): code 1.a/1.b general emergency alarm per the muster list, code 2 continuous until silenced or acknowledged, codes 3.a–3.d optional with pulse frequency 0.5–2.0 Hz</li></ul><h2>Particular alarms (Section 8)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"alert-timers\"><figcaption>Alarm time limits</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Alarm</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Personnel alarm</b> (8.1)</td><td>Pre-warning <b>3 minutes</b> before the alarm. Alarm sounds on the bridge or in officers' quarters if not reset within a period <b>not exceeding 30 minutes</b>. Can be automatic or manual. The engineer acknowledges in the machinery space before disconnection.</td></tr><tr><td><b>BNWAS</b> (8.2)</td><td>Conforms to MSC.128(75)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Engineers' alarm</b> (8.3)</td><td>On periodically unattended machinery spaces, activates if the machinery alarm is <b>not acknowledged within 5 minutes at most</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>General emergency alarm</b> (8.4)</td><td>Operable from the bridge and at least one other strategic point. Passenger ships also from the safety centre. Audible throughout accommodation and normal crew workspaces, and on passenger ships recognizable in passenger places and on open decks.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Grouping and locations</h2><ul><li><b>Section 9:</b> grouping and aggregation must not hide information needed for safe operation. Individual visual alerts remain at the appropriate position when alerts are aggregated (9.4).</li><li><b>Section 10, Tables 10.1.1–10.1.9:</b> locations for alerts: navigation bridge, machinery space or control room, central fire control station, at the equipment, engineers' accommodation, miscellaneous, cargo control station, not specified by IMO instruments, and central ballast control station of column-stabilized MODUs. Priority abbreviations are EM, A, W, C and I.</li><li><b>Section 11:</b> references the IBC, IGC, SFV 1993, IGS, HSC 2000, IMDG, MODU 2009, FSS and LSA Codes, MSC.128(75) and A.481(XII)</li></ul><h2>Bridge Alert Management, MSC.302(87)</h2><ul><li>Adopted <b>17 May 2010</b> at MSC 87. Applies to all alerts presented on or transferred to the bridge, and takes precedence over conflicting alert requirements in existing performance standards.</li><li>Recommended for a central alert management system (CAM) and its interface if installed on or after <b>1 Jul 2014</b></li><li>Four modules: <b>A</b> alert priorities and states, <b>B</b> CAM interface (including an alert history kept for at least <b>24 hours</b>), <b>C</b> interfacing and power supply, <b>D</b> documentation and familiarization</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The entry-into-force date and the exact types of ship the Code is required on, because the text sets no date. It applies through the instruments in 1.1.</li><li>The text of the contents of Tables 9.1–9.3 and 10.1.1–10.1.9</li><li>Any amendment to the Code and any equipment-specific standard that refers to it</li><li>The three exceptions to the power supply rule in 4.8</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>A.1021(26) text: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.a.1021.2009.pdf</li><li>IMO — A.1021(26): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1021(26).pdf</li><li>MSC.302(87): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.302(87).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-blu-code","v":229898650,"title":"BLU Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["blu","bulk-carrier","loading","unloading","terminal","solas-vi","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>BLU Code — Code of Practice for the Safe Loading and Unloading of Bulk Carriers</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>27 Nov 1997, Assembly resolution A.862(20)</td></tr><tr><td>Effective</td><td>1 Jul 1998</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Introduction · 6 Sections · 5 Appendices</td></tr><tr><td>Aim</td><td>Prevent loss of, or damage to, ships carrying solid bulk cargoes through improper loading and unloading</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo</td><td>Solid bulk cargoes, <b>excluding grain</b></td></tr><tr><td>Linked to</td><td>SOLAS Chapter VI, regulation 7</td></tr><tr><td>Used with</td><td>IMSBC Code (cargo), BLU Manual (for terminals)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the BLU Code</h2><ul><li>Practical recommendations for <b>ship and terminal</b> to load and unload solid bulk cargoes safely</li><li>Main tools: the agreed <b>loading or unloading plan</b> and the <b>ship/shore safety checklist</b></li><li>The Code revoked MSC/Circ.690 and DSC/Circ.3</li><li>Its recommendations are subject to terminal, port or national requirements</li><li>SOLAS, MARPOL and Load Line issues are not specifically covered</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>27 Nov 1997</td><td>A.862(20) adopts the Code · effective 1 Jul 1998</td></tr><tr><td>1 Dec 2006</td><td>MSC.238(82): amends the Introduction, Section 5 and Appendix 4 · effective 1 Jan 2007</td></tr><tr><td>14 May 2010</td><td>MSC.304(87): amends Sections 1, 3 and 5 and Appendices 4 and 5 · effective 1 Jan 2011</td></tr><tr><td>May 2005</td><td>MSC 80 approves the <b>BLU Manual</b> for terminal representatives; amendments approved at MSC 87 (MSC.1/Circ.1356)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The Code is linked to SOLAS regulation VI/7 (loading, unloading and stowage of bulk cargoes).</li><li>SOLAS VI/7.3: a copy of the plan is lodged with the appropriate authority of the port State.</li><li><b>EU:</b> Directive 2001/96/EC, Article 8, requires the plan to follow the form in BLU Code Appendix 2 and the checklist to follow the Appendix 4 guidelines. The directive defines a bulk carrier as a ship of 500 GT or more of the bulk-carrier type described in it (UK MCA summary).</li></ul><h2>Procedure at a glance</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"blu-flow\"><figcaption>The four stages: before arrival, before cargo handling, loading or unloading, completion</figcaption></figure><h2>Sections</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Definitions (1.1–1.11)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Suitability of ships and terminals</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Procedures between ship and shore before arrival</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Procedures between ship and terminal before cargo handling</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Cargo loading and handling of ballast</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Unloading cargo and handling of ballast</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Section 1 — Definitions:</b> air draught, combination carriers (OBO or O/O), conveyor system, hot work, list indication lights, master, pour, terminal representative, and three types of trimming (loading cargo, unloading cargo, ship trimming).</p><p><b>Section 2 — Suitability</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ships (2.1–2.2)</th><th>Terminals (2.3)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Valid statutory certificates, with validity covering loading, voyage and unloading plus a reserve for delays</td><td>Accept only ships that can berth safely, considering water depth, maximum ship size, mooring, fendering, safe access and obstructions</td></tr><tr><td>Weathertight; approved stability and loading booklet</td><td>Equipment maintained; weighing equipment calibrated at regular intervals</td></tr><tr><td>Adequate hatches, hatch numbers clearly shown</td><td>Personnel trained and rested to avoid fatigue</td></tr><tr><td>Approved loading instrument where stress calculations are needed</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Machinery and hatches working; list indication lights tested before operations</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Section 3 — Before arrival</b></p><ul><li>Information is exchanged early enough for preparations.</li><li>Loading rate and cargo distribution are agreed using one of three options: the restrictions in the ship's manual or booklet, an approved loading instrument, or a conservative procedure.</li><li><b>Ship to terminal:</b> ETA (updated), ship particulars, loading plan, draughts, de-ballasting time, hatch distances, accommodation ladder location, air draught, cargo gear, mooring details. Part-loaded ships report cargo on board and the distribution to be unloaded. Combination carriers report previous cargoes and gas-free status. The ship confirms holds are clean.</li><li><b>Terminal to ship:</b> berth details, loader or unloader characteristics and rates, water depth, water density, air draught, gangway arrangements, approach speed, tug availability, cargo sequence, cargo hazards, mooring requirements. The terminal confirms the ship has received the cargo declaration.</li></ul><p><b>Section 4 — Before cargo handling</b></p><ul><li><b>4.1:</b> the master is responsible at all times for safe loading and unloading.</li><li><b>4.2 loading:</b> the plan is signed by both parties; the master specifies hold order and weights; ballast and loading rates are coordinated.</li><li><b>4.2 unloading:</b> the terminal proposes the rate; the master sets hold order and weights.</li><li><b>4.3:</b> the plan follows Appendix 2. It changes only by a revised plan signed by both parties. Plans are kept for <b>6 months</b>, and a copy is lodged with the port State authority.</li></ul><h2>Who does what</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"blu-roles\"><figcaption>Duties of the master and of the terminal representative</figcaption></figure><p><b>Section 5 — Loading and ballast</b></p><ul><li>Confirm there is no excessive hull stress.</li><li>The terminal alerts the ship to high impact loads from heavy cargo until the tank top is covered.</li><li>Trimming follows the BC Code.</li><li>Both parties keep accurate records of total and per-pour quantities.</li><li>Completion of loading is agreed in writing.</li></ul><p><b>Section 6 — Unloading and ballast</b></p><ul><li>Confirm the method avoids excessive stress and damage; keep communication open.</li><li>On completion both parties agree in writing that the ship was unloaded to plan, holds were cleaned to the master's requirements, and any damage was recorded.</li><li>Flammable vapour risks apply when ballasting holds.</li><li>The ship warns the terminal at once of damage or hazards.</li><li>The terminal matches port and starboard unloading, reports when each hold is complete, avoids and reports damage, and forecasts adverse weather.</li><li>Damage affecting structural or watertight integrity is reported to the Administration and the port State authority.</li></ul><h2>Right to stop</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Master</td><td>May stop loading or unloading if the agreed plan is not followed or anything else endangers the ship (resolution para 3.4)</td></tr><tr><td>Port authorities</td><td>May stop operations when the ship's safety is endangered (para 3.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Checklist item 18</td><td>Both parties accept that cargo operations must stop if ballasting falls out of step with cargo operations</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Appendices</h2><table><thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Recommended contents of port and terminal information books</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Loading or unloading plan: sample form and worked examples; the heavy-lined box holds the minimum information; bending moments and shear forces stay within permitted limits</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ship/shore safety checklist: 21 questions, signed by the master and the terminal representative</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Guidelines for completing the checklist</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Form for cargo information from the shipper: cargo description, stowage factor, angle of repose, trimming procedures, hazards, signed declaration</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 21 checklist questions cover water depth, mooring, emergency departure, access, communications, crewing, bunkering, repairs, damage reporting, cargo information, atmosphere safety, loader limits, the plan, trimming, ballast-cargo alignment, residue removal, final trim and time to sail.</p><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> No loading or unloading starts until the master and the terminal representative have agreed and signed the plan and completed the checklist. Keep ballast in step with cargo, and watch hull stresses at every pour. The master can stop the operation when the ship is endangered.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO Assembly resolution A.862(20), BLU Code text: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.862(20).pdf</li><li>IMO — BLU Code and BLU Manual: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/BLU-Code-and-BLU-Manual.aspx</li><li>Amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vi/blu-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Directive 2001/96/EC, Article 8: https://www.legislation.gov.uk/eudr/2001/96/article/8</li><li>UK MCA — MGN 699 Annex B: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-699-m-guidance-on-the-merchant-shipping-carriage-of-cargoes-regulations-2024/annex-b-loading-unloading-and-stowage-of-solid-bulk-cargoes-and-the-blu-code</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-bwms-code","v":253025724,"title":"BWMS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["bwms","ballast-water","type-approval","d-2","g8","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>BWMS Code — Code for Approval of Ballast Water Management Systems</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>13 Apr 2018</b>, MEPC 72, resolution <b>MEPC.300(72)</b> (Annex 5 of MEPC 72/17/Add.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Takes effect</td><td><b>13 Oct 2019</b>, on entry into force of the associated amendments</td></tr><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>The <b>2016 Guidelines (G8)</b>, MEPC.279(70), revoked when the Code takes effect. References to G8 in other IMO instruments now mean the BWMS Code.</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Mandatory through the BWM 2004 Convention, regulation D-3</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Type approval of ballast water management systems so they meet the <b>D-2 standard</b></td></tr><tr><td>Result</td><td><b>Type Approval Certificate</b> with the system's design limitations</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>8 sections, Annex Parts 1–7, and the Type Approval Certificate appendix</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bwms-approval\"><figcaption>From test to certificate</figcaption></figure><h2>The D-2 standard behind the tests (Section 2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bwms-d2\"><figcaption>D-2 discharge limits</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Organisms or microbe</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Viable organisms <b>50 µm or more</b></td><td>Fewer than <b>10 per m³</b></td></tr><tr><td>Viable organisms <b>10 to 50 µm</b></td><td>Fewer than <b>10 per mL</b></td></tr><tr><td>Toxicogenic *Vibrio cholerae* (O1, O139)</td><td>Fewer than <b>1 cfu per 100 mL</b> (or per 1 g zooplankton wet weight)</td></tr><tr><td>*Escherichia coli*</td><td>Fewer than <b>250 cfu per 100 mL</b></td></tr><tr><td>Intestinal enterococci</td><td>Fewer than <b>100 cfu per 100 mL</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Transitional provisions</h2><ul><li>Systems approved by <b>28 Oct 2018</b> under the old G8 (MEPC.174(58)) may be <b>installed before 28 Oct 2020</b></li><li>\"Installed\" means the <b>contractual delivery date</b> to the ship, or the actual delivery date if there is no contract date</li><li>Systems approved under the <b>2016 G8 are deemed to comply</b> with the Code (1.13). Makers of such systems submit only the additional tests and documents (6.12).</li><li>A type approval certificate expiring does <b>not</b> affect the operational validity of systems already installed. They need not be renewed or replaced for that reason (preamble).</li></ul><h2>Sections</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Introduction</td><td>Aim is regulation D-3 and the D-2 standard. Applies to all ships under D-2. Approval screens out systems that would fail D-2 but does not guarantee performance on every ship (1.5).</td></tr><tr><td>2 Background</td><td>The D-2 limits</td></tr><tr><td>3 Definitions</td><td>BWMS, Active Substance, failed, valid and invalid test cycle, <b>System Design Limitations</b>, <b>Treatment Rated Capacity (TRC)</b></td></tr><tr><td>4 Technical specifications</td><td>Effective on short and long voyages whatever the temperature. Safe design, alarms, maintenance access, seals and bypass alarms, calibration. Automatic recording, 24-month data storage, gas detection per IEC 60079-29-1.</td></tr><tr><td>5 Type approval process</td><td>Pre-test evaluation, land-based and shipboard tests, SDL on the certificate, installation survey</td></tr><tr><td>6 Approval and certification</td><td>Type Approval Certificate. Active Substance systems need prior IMO approval under D-3.2. Reports go to IMO.</td></tr><tr><td>7 Installation requirements</td><td>Sampling facilities. Any <b>bypass must raise an alarm and be logged</b>, except internal transfers such as anti-heeling.</td></tr><tr><td>8 Installation survey and commissioning</td><td>Documents kept on board and checks before the International Ballast Water Management Certificate is issued</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Annex parts</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Pre-test documentation. Major components cannot change during testing. A change means new evaluation and repeated tests (1.10).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Test and performance specifications</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Environmental testing of electrical and electronic parts at an accredited laboratory. Reference standards IACS UR E10 and ISO/IEC 17025.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sample analysis. A cycle succeeds only if <b>all D-2 limits are met</b>, with no averaging or discounting of failed cycles (4.7).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Self-monitoring. Data tamper-resistant, time-stamped and kept at least <b>24 months</b> (5.7–5.8).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>SDL validation, overseen by the Administration, independent of the maker, not a stress test</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Format of the Type Approval Certificate and the type approval report</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Test requirements (Part 2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bwms-tests\"><figcaption>Key figures in the test programme</figcaption></figure><h3>Land-based tests</h3><ul><li><b>At least 5 consecutive successful cycles in each salinity</b> (2.11)</li><li>At least <b>2 cycles per salinity</b> at the minimum holding time (2.13)</li><li>Control and treated tanks each hold at least <b>200 m³</b> (2.21)</li><li>No downscaling for models of 200 m³/h or less (2.26)</li><li>Test facility independent of the manufacturer (2.4)</li><li>Samples analysed live within <b>6 hours</b> (2.44)</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Test water</th><th>Salinity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Marine</td><td><b>28–36 PSU</b></td></tr><tr><td>Brackish</td><td><b>10–20 PSU</b></td></tr><tr><td>Fresh</td><td><b>below 1 PSU</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>An <b>unlimited</b> certificate needs tests in all three salinity ranges, separated by at least 10 PSU (2.31)</li><li><b>Dissolved and particulate organic carbon:</b> above 1 mg/L (marine) or 5 mg/L (brackish and fresh). <b>Total suspended solids:</b> above 1 mg/L (marine) or 50 mg/L (brackish and fresh).</li><li><b>Challenge organisms:</b><ul><li>50 µm and above: preferably 10⁶, not less than 10⁵ per m³, from at least <b>5 species in 3 phyla</b></li><li>10–50 µm: preferably 10⁴, not less than 10³ per mL</li><li>heterotrophic bacteria: at least 10⁴ per mL</li><li>coliform, *Enterococcus* and *V. cholerae* are measured, not added</li></ul></li><li><b>Temperature:</b> effective operation from <b>0–40 °C</b> (2–40 °C for fresh water)</li><li><b>Regrowth:</b> assessed in at least 2 cycles per salinity, with holding time of at least <b>5 days</b> or enumeration at least 5 days after treatment (2.49–2.52)</li><li>A control cycle discharging at or below <b>10 times the D-2.1 limit</b> makes the cycle invalid (2.45)</li></ul><h3>Shipboard tests</h3><ul><li>Test cycles span at least <b>6 months</b> (2.8.7)</li><li><b>3 consecutive compliant cycles</b> required (2.8.8)</li><li>Uptake water must exceed <b>10 times</b> the D-2.1 limits for the test to be valid (2.8.5)</li><li>Sample volumes: at least <b>1 m³ influent and 3 m³ discharge</b> for 50 µm and above, and at least <b>10 L</b> for 10–50 µm (2.8.6)</li><li>The shipboard test unit should be at the upper limit of TRC unless the Administration approves otherwise (4.14–4.16)</li></ul><h2>Type Approval Certificate (Part 7.1)</h2><ul><li>Identifies the BWMS type and model, with dated assembly drawings</li><li>References the full performance test protocol</li><li>States installation conditions and limitations, and the SDL under the heading \"This equipment has been designed for operation in the following conditions\"</li><li>Includes restrictions such as UV transmittance, pressure or TRO limits</li><li>Appendix with results for each land-based and shipboard cycle</li><li>Appendix form lists the <b>Treatment Rated Capacity in m³/h</b>, limiting operating conditions (temperature and salinity), signature, stamp, issue and validity dates, and a requirement to <b>keep a copy on board</b></li><li>The certificate cannot incorporate documents by reference (7.5). Proprietary information may be redacted (7.3).</li></ul><h2>Approval of changes</h2><ul><li><b>Major components:</b> new test proposal and repeated land-based and shipboard tests (Part 1, 1.10)</li><li><b>Non-major components:</b> equivalent replacements allowed with reporting (1.11)</li><li><b>Safety upgrades:</b> allowed and reported. If they affect D-2 performance they count as major changes (1.12).</li><li><b>Software:</b> changes after pre-test evaluation follow a traceable change-handling procedure (4.22)</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Any amendment to the Code after 2018, such as later MEPC resolutions</li><li>The \"experience-building phase\" and its dates, which did not appear in the Code text</li><li>Whether a given system was installed under the Code or the older G8 on a particular ship. This depends on installation date and flag State practice.</li><li>Active Substance approval under G9, which is a separate IMO procedure</li><li>The \"ten days holding time\" often quoted. It is <b>not in the Code text</b>. The Code uses <b>5 days</b> for regrowth assessment.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>MEPC.300(72) BWMS Code text: https://www.samgongustofa.is/media/english/MEPC.300-72-.pdf</li><li>IMO — Ballast water management FAQ: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/BWMFAQ.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-css-code","v":168056270,"title":"CSS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["css","cargo-securing","lashing","csm","solas-vi","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>CSS Code — Code of Safe Practice for Cargo Stowage and Securing</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>6 Nov 1991, Assembly resolution A.714(17) (effective 1 Jul 1996)</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>Resolution A.288(VIII)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>7 chapters · 14 annexes (Annexes 13 and 14 added later)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Cargoes other than solid and liquid bulk cargoes and timber stowed on deck</td></tr><tr><td>Main tool on board</td><td>Cargo Securing Manual (SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5)</td></tr><tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>The master remains responsible for the voyage, the ship, the crew and the cargo</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the CSS Code</h2><ul><li>An international standard for safe stowage and securing of cargo</li><li>Covers: ship suitability and equipment, general stowage advice, cargoes known to be hard to stow, actions in heavy weather, and what to do after cargo shifts</li><li>The Assembly urged Governments to implement it at the earliest opportunity and asked the MSC to keep it under review</li><li>SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5: cargo units must be loaded, stowed and secured throughout the voyage under a <b>Cargo Securing Manual approved by the Administration</b>, to a standard at least equivalent to the IMO guidelines</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Circular</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1994 (MSC 64)</td><td>MSC/Circ.664</td><td>Paragraphs 1.9.1, 2.9.1, 3.2 and new <b>Annex 13</b></td></tr><tr><td>1995 (MSC 65)</td><td>MSC/Circ.691</td><td>Paragraph 1.6.2 and Annex 5 changes</td></tr><tr><td>1996 (MSC 66)</td><td>MSC/Circ.740</td><td>Annex 12: new 2.3, 2.4, 2.5</td></tr><tr><td>1997 (MSC 68)</td><td>MSC/Circ.812</td><td>Paragraphs 4.3.1, 6.1 and Annex 13 (Table 1)</td></tr><tr><td>2002 (MSC 75)</td><td>MSC/Circ.1026</td><td>Annex 13 sections 1, 4, 5, 6, 7 and new appendices</td></tr><tr><td>2010 (MSC 87)</td><td>MSC.1/Circ.1352</td><td>New <b>Annex 14</b></td></tr><tr><td>2014 (MSC 94)</td><td>MSC.1/Circ.1352/Rev.1 (15 Dec 2014)</td><td>Amended Annex 14; revoked Circ.1352</td></tr><tr><td>2020 (MSC 102)</td><td>MSC.1/Circ.1623 (7 Dec 2020)</td><td>Annex 13 replaced in full with its four appendices</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Annex 14 applies in full to ships with keels laid on or after 1 Jan 2015.</li><li>Cargo Securing Manual guidelines: MSC.1/Circ.1353/Rev.1 (15 Dec 2014), then Rev.2 (7 Dec 2020), which amended its Appendix 2 and revoked Rev.1.</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td><td>Application, definitions (cargo unit, portable tank, ro-ro ship, unit load), forces, cargo behaviour, risk criteria, the Cargo Securing Manual, equipment, special cargo, cargo information</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Principles of safe stowage and securing</td><td>Cargo suitability, distribution, securing arrangements, residual strength, friction, supervision, entry into enclosed spaces, pre-loading checks, cargo stowage and securing declarations (including dangerous goods)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Standardized systems</td><td>Ships for standardized cargo (containers, railway wagons) must be designed and equipped to secure it and give the master adequate information</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Semi-standardized systems</td><td>Securing points and arrangements for road vehicles and roll-trailers on ro-ro ships; acceptance of road vehicles</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Non-standardized systems</td><td>General advice and twelve cargo types with annexes; equivalent methods allowed if they give at least the same safety</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Heavy weather actions</td><td>Course or speed changes, heaving to, avoiding bad weather, timely ballasting or deballasting, voyage planning</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Actions after cargo shift</td><td>Course or speed changes, monitoring the ship's integrity, restowing or resecuring, route diversion; ballasting only if stability is adequate</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Forces:</b> longitudinal and transverse forces are predominant. Transverse forces increase with stow height, distance from the ship's centre of motion, and metacentric height.</p><h2>Annexes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Subject</th><th>Figures stated in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Containers on deck of ships not specially designed for them</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Portable tanks (tank-containers)</td><td>Over 450 litres · lashing angle against sliding not more than 25° · against tipping 45° to 60° or more</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Portable receptacles</td><td>1,000 litres or less (cylinders 150 litres or less)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Wheel-based (rolling) cargoes</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Heavy cargo items (locomotives, transformers)</td><td>Optimum lashing angle about 25° against sliding, 45° to 60° against tipping</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Coiled sheet steel</td><td>Coils typically over 10 tonnes gross</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Heavy metal products</td><td>Shoring on every frame, at intervals of at least 1 m</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Anchor chains</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Metal scrap in bulk</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Flexible intermediate bulk containers (FIBCs)</td><td>Up to 3 m³</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Under-deck stowage of logs</td><td>Sliding into the sides begins at about 2 m above the inner bottom</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Unit loads</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Methods to assess the efficiency of securing arrangements (semi- and non-standardized cargo)</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Safe working conditions for securing containers on deck</td><td>See below</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Annex 13 — assessing securing arrangements</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"css-msl\"><figcaption>MSL as a percentage of breaking strength, Annex 13 Table 1</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Timber: MSL is 0.3 kN/cm² normal to the grain.</li><li>A device marked with a permissible working load may use that value as its MSL (para 4.3).</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"css-rule-of-thumb\"><figcaption>The rule-of-thumb check for transverse securing</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Rule of thumb (section 5):</b> the total MSL of the devices on each side, port and starboard, should equal the weight of the item in kN. It assumes 1 g (9.81 m/s²) transverse acceleration and ignores lashing-angle effects, uneven load sharing and friction.</li><li>Transverse lashing angles to the deck should not exceed 60°. Lashings above 60° can help against tipping but are not counted in the rule-of-thumb total.</li><li>The full method uses basic acceleration data (Table 2) with length and speed correction factors (Table 3) and B/GM correction factors (Table 4).</li></ul><h2>Annex 14 — safe working conditions for securing containers</h2><p>Applies to dedicated containerships and parts of other ships designed and fitted to carry containers on deck. It protects the people who lash and unlash.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>General: reduce work on container tops and heavy manual equipment; approved <b>Cargo Safe Access Plan (CSAP)</b>; training in lashing, defective gear, working at heights, reefer cable checks</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Responsibilities of Administrations, owners and operators, designers, shipbuilders and terminals</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Design: risk assessment, safe access, fencing (top rail about 1 m, gaps 300 mm or less), lashing system standards, <b>lighting at least 10 lux in access ways and 50 lux at work spaces</b>, at least 70 mm between turnbuckles</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Operations and maintenance: lashing in the ISM system, keep transit areas clear, work in pairs with heavy gear, PPE, safety cages for container tops, <b>no lashing where safe access cannot be provided</b>, record book</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Reefers: outlets interlocked, circuit-breaker protected, de-energized when unplugged; defective plugs locked and tagged out</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For existing containerships (keels laid before 1 Jan 2015), sections 4.4, 7.1, 7.3 and 8 apply, and sections 6 and 7.2 as far as practical.</p><h2>Cargo Securing Manual (MSC.1/Circ.1353/Rev.1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 General</td><td>Definitions (securing devices, <b>MSL</b>, standardized, semi-standardized, non-standardized cargo); written in the ship's working language, with an English, French or Spanish translation if needed; consistency with the trim and stability booklet, Load Line Certificate and IMDG Code; reserve devices; maintenance</td></tr><tr><td>2 Securing devices and arrangements</td><td>Fixed devices and portable devices with MSL; inspection and maintenance: the master's responsibility, routine and periodic examinations, a record book kept with the manual</td></tr><tr><td>3 Non-standardized and semi-standardized cargo</td><td>Forces, accelerations, calculation of devices and safety factors (refers to Annex 13); ro-ro ships</td></tr><tr><td>4 Containers and other standardized cargo</td><td>Stowage and securing plan: stack masses, sequences, heights, sight lines; alternative patterns; forces</td></tr><tr><td>5 Cargo Safe Access Plan</td><td>Required for containerships; refers to Annex 14</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The full guidelines apply to containerships with keels laid on or after 1 Jan 2015; chapters 1 to 4 apply to existing containerships.</li><li>A manual drafted to the older MSC/Circ.385 is acceptable if it meets these guidelines.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Secure cargo as the approved Cargo Securing Manual says: right devices, correct MSL, records of inspection and maintenance. Check securing arrangements and act early in heavy weather (Chapter 6). On a containership, the Cargo Safe Access Plan and Annex 14 protect the lashing crew.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO Assembly resolution A.714(17) (Code text): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.714(17).pdf</li><li>IMO — CSS Code: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/CSS-Code.aspx</li><li>CSS Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vi/css-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1623, Annex 13: https://www.liscr.com/sites/default/files/MSC.1-Circ.1623.pdf</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1352/Rev.1, Annex 14: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.circ.1352.2014.pdf</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1353/Rev.1, Cargo Securing Manual guidelines: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.circ.1353.2014.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ctu-code","v":45557179,"title":"CTU Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ctu","cargo-securing","container","packing","vgm","imo","ilo","unece","code"],"body":"<h1>CTU Code — IMO/ILO/UNECE Code of Practice for Packing of Cargo Transport Units</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Published as</td><td>IMO circular <b>MSC.1/Circ.1497</b>, dated <b>16 Dec 2014</b></td></tr><tr><td>Approved by</td><td>UNECE Inland Transport Committee (76th session, 25–27 Feb 2014) · IMO MSC 93 (14–23 May 2014) · ILO Governing Body (322nd session, 30 Oct–13 Nov 2014)</td></tr><tr><td>Developed by</td><td>IMO, ILO and UNECE jointly. A Group of Experts revised the earlier guidelines in 2011–2013.</td></tr><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>The 1997 IMO/ILO/UNECE Guidelines for Packing of CTUs. The circular <b>revokes MSC/Circ.787</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td><b>Non-mandatory</b> global code of practice for sea and land transport</td></tr><tr><td>Aim</td><td>Advice on safe packing and securing of cargo in CTUs, for packers, trainers and everyone in the supply chain up to the unpacker</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>13 chapters, 10 annexes, 5 appendices</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ctu-structure\"><figcaption>Layout of the CTU Code</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The CTU Code is guidance, not law (see 1.1.3 below), but flag States, ports and companies use it as the standard of good practice for packing containers and other cargo transport units.</blockquote><h2>Purpose and scope (Chapter 1)</h2><ul><li><b>1.1.1:</b> advice on safe packing of CTUs for people who pack and secure cargo and for those who train them, with the theory and practical measures</li><li><b>1.1.2:</b> guidance for all parties in the supply chain, up to and including those who unpack the CTU</li><li><b>1.1.3:</b> not intended to conflict with, replace or supersede existing national or international regulations</li><li>Poor packing, blocking, bracing or lashing can injure people and damage cargo and equipment, even though a CTU reduces some physical hazards</li></ul><h2>Chapters and annexes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Introduction</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Key requirements</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Chains of responsibility and information</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>General transport conditions</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>CTU properties</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>CTU suitability</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Arrival, checking and positioning of CTUs</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Packing cargo into CTUs</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Additional advice on the packing of dangerous goods</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>On completion of packing</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Advice on receipt and unpacking of CTUs</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Training in packing of CTUs</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Information flow</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Safe handling of CTUs</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Prevention of condensation damages</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Approval plates</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Receiving CTUs</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Minimizing the risk of recontamination</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Packing and securing cargo into CTUs (Appendices 1–5: packaging marks, friction factors, practical methods for friction factor µ, specific packing and securing calculations, practical inclination test)</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Access to tank and bulk tops, working at height</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Fumigation</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Topics for consideration in a training programme</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Who does what (Chapter 4)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ctu-chain\"><figcaption>Chain of responsibility</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>Duty (from Ch 4 and the CTU Code quick guide)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Consignor</td><td>Protective packaging where appropriate</td></tr><tr><td><b>Packer</b></td><td>Checks the CTU and cargo for damage and pests, places and secures the cargo, determines the gross mass of the packed CTU (11.3.2)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Shipper</b></td><td>Classifies and declares the cargo, seals the CTU, makes sure documents are accurate and reach the carrier in time (11.1.2, 11.3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier (4.2.8)</td><td>Secures the CTU on the means of transport, transports it per agreements and regulations, provides trained personnel</td></tr><tr><td>Consignee (4.2.9)</td><td>Checks the atmosphere before entry, ventilates, returns the CTU empty and clean unless agreed otherwise</td></tr><tr><td>All parties (4.2.12)</td><td>Pass on CTU number, seal number, verified gross mass and an accurate cargo description</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Forces on cargo (Chapter 5)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ctu-forces\"><figcaption>Acceleration coefficients c (a = c · g)</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Force is <b>F = m · a</b>, and acceleration <b>a = c · g</b>, with g = 9.81 m/s² (5.2)</li><li><b>Mass and friction alone do not secure cargo</b>, because vibration can make it move. Lashing is needed where blocking is not possible (5.4).</li><li>Cargo is secured for the worst combination of horizontal and vertical acceleration, each horizontal direction separately (5.3)</li><li><b>Sea areas by significant wave height (20-year return)</b> (5.5): <b>A</b> Hs ≤ 8 m · <b>B</b> 8 m &lt; Hs ≤ 12 m · <b>C</b> Hs &gt; 12 m</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Longitudinal, forward / rearward (cx)</th><th>Transverse (cy)</th><th>Vertical down, minimum (cz)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Road</td><td>0.8 / 0.5</td><td>0.5</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>Rail</td><td>0.5 (1.0 for shocks ≤ 150 ms)</td><td>0.5</td><td>1.0 (0.7 for shocks ≤ 150 ms)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sea A</b></td><td>0.3</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.5 for longitudinal, 1.0 for transverse</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sea B</b></td><td>0.3</td><td>0.7</td><td>0.3 for longitudinal, 1.0 for transverse</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sea C</b></td><td>0.4</td><td>0.8</td><td>0.2 for longitudinal, 1.0 for transverse</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Packing (Chapter 9)</h2><ul><li><b>9.1.1 Planning:</b> plan in advance, segregate incompatible cargo, observe special handling, do not exceed the maximum payload, and respect limits on concentrated loads and centre-of-gravity eccentricity</li><li><b>9.2.1 Securing materials:</b> strong enough, in good condition, suitable for CTU and goods, and meeting phytosanitary standards</li><li><b>9.3.1 Principles:</b> distribute the load properly and use stowage suited to the cargo</li><li><b>9.4.1 Securing:</b> tight stowage must not overstress the CTU boundaries. Weak-boundary CTUs need securing forces from the arrangement itself. Large, heavy or shaped packages are secured individually.</li><li><b>9.5.1 Bulk:</b> observe liquid filling ratios, check tank fittings for compatibility, avoid overstressing the CTU with solid bulk</li></ul><h2>On completion of packing (Chapter 11)</h2><ul><li><b>11.1.2 Seal:</b> for international transport the shipper seals the CTU immediately after packing with a seal bearing a unique number. Some countries may require <b>ISO 17712</b> seals.</li><li><b>11.1.3 Tracking devices:</b> securely fitted. Powered devices must be of a certified safe type. SOLAS requires no ignition sources in enclosed cargo spaces holding highly flammable goods at sea.</li><li><b>11.3.2 Gross mass:</b> the packer determines the gross mass of the packed CTU by any prescribed method</li><li><b>11.3.3:</b> the packer tells the shipper the <b>CTU number, verified gross mass and seal number</b>, so they appear on all transport documents</li><li><b>11.3.4:</b> if cargo projects beyond the CTU, state the exact maximum overheight, overwidth or overlength</li><li><b>11.3.5:</b> containers with an allowable stacking mass <b>under 192,000 kg</b> must be flagged to the carrier if shipped by sea</li><li><b>11.3.6:</b> for sea transport a container/vehicle packing certificate may be required, certifying compliance with dangerous goods rules</li></ul><h2>Training (Chapter 13)</h2><ul><li><b>13.1.1:</b> effective use of the Code depends on initial and retraining programmes for everyone involved in packing</li><li><b>13.1.2:</b> in-house, external or e-learning, with external providers able to meet the Code's requirements</li><li><b>13.1.4:</b> training is completed before staff take immediate responsibility</li><li><b>13.3.1:</b> training matches each person's responsibilities. Untrained staff work only under direct supervision of a trained person.</li><li><b>13.3.2:</b> covers consequences of poor packing, legal requirements, transport forces and basic packing principles. Topics are in Annex 10.</li><li><b>13.4:</b> training records kept in line with local practice</li></ul><h2>Companion documents</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.1/Circ.1497</td><td>The CTU Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.1/Circ.1498</td><td><b>Informative Material</b> with practical guidance and background. Finalized by the CCC Sub-Committee. Referenced by the Code but not part of it, and not endorsed by UNECE and ILO.</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.1/Circ.1531</td><td><b>Due diligence checklist</b> for choosing providers of CTU-related services such as packing. Dated 6 Jun 2016, approved by MSC 96. It does not amend the Code.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Chapter 3 key requirements, Chapter 6 and 7 detail, Chapter 8, Chapter 10 and Chapter 12 detail, and the content of Annexes 1–10 apart from titles</li><li>Any amendment to the Code after 2014, and whether MSC.1/Circ.1497 has been revised</li><li>Legal status of the Code in individual flag States</li><li>Figures in the industry quick guides that are not in the Code itself, such as the 15 cm void-space gap and the 25% heavy-item threshold, because I did not find them in the Code text</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>CTU Code text, Annex of MSC.1/Circ.1497 (Hong Kong Marine Department copy): https://www.mardep.gov.hk/en/msnote/pdf/msin1669anx1.pdf</li><li>IMO — CTU Code: https://imo.org/en/ourwork/safety/pages/ctu-code.aspx</li><li>ILO — CTU Code: https://www.ilo.org/resource/imoilounece-code-practice-packing-cargo-transport-units</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1531: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/MSC.1-CIRC.1531 (E).pdf</li><li>CTU Code quick guide (CIG, 2020), chapter mapping: https://assets.ippc.int/static/media/files/publication/en/2022/01/CIGCTU_Code__a_quick_guide_Sep_2020.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-casualty-investigation-code","v":125676457,"title":"Casualty Investigation Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["casualty-investigation","ci-code","accident","solas-xi-1","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>Casualty Investigation Code</h1><p><b>Code of the International Standards and Recommended Practices for a Safety Investigation into a Marine Casualty or Marine Incident</b></p><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>16 May 2008, resolution MSC.255(84) (MSC 84)</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory</td><td>Parts I and II mandatory under SOLAS chapter XI-1 from 1 Jan 2010 (resolution MSC.257(84)) · Part III recommended</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>3 Parts · 26 chapters</td></tr><tr><td>Core duty</td><td>A marine safety investigation into every <b>very serious marine casualty</b></td></tr><tr><td>Reports</td><td>Final report to IMO and made public</td></tr><tr><td>Guidance</td><td>Resolution A.1075(28) (investigator guidelines)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Casualty Investigation Code</h2><ul><li>A common approach for States to investigate marine casualties and incidents, to <b>prevent future casualties and incidents</b></li><li>It expands SOLAS I/21, which only required an investigation when the Administration judged it could inform regulatory changes</li><li>The Code requires an investigation of every very serious marine casualty, and recommends investigating other casualties and incidents likely to prevent future accidents</li><li>Related provisions cited by IMO: MARPOL articles 8 and 12, Load Lines Convention article 23, UNCLOS article 94(7)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li><b>A.1075(28)</b> provides guidelines to help investigators apply the Code, and revoked resolutions A.849(20) and A.884(21)</li><li>IMO's GISIS Maritime Casualties and Incidents module stores casualty data and uploaded investigation reports (some restricted to IMO Members)</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ci-structure\"><figcaption>The three parts and 26 chapters of the Code</figcaption></figure><h2>Key definitions (Chapter 2)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Marine casualty (2.9)</td><td>An event or sequence of events directly connected with a ship's operations that causes: death or serious injury; loss of a person from a ship; loss or abandonment of a ship; material damage to a ship; stranding, disabling or collision; damage to external marine infrastructure that could seriously endanger safety; or severe environmental damage (or its potential). Deliberate harmful acts are excluded</td></tr><tr><td>Marine incident (2.10)</td><td>A non-casualty event that endangered, or would endanger if not corrected, a ship, its occupants, other persons or the environment</td></tr><tr><td>Very serious marine casualty (2.22)</td><td>A casualty involving <b>total loss of the ship, a death, or severe environmental damage</b></td></tr><tr><td>Serious injury (2.18)</td><td>Incapacitation where a person cannot function normally for more than <b>72 hours</b>, starting within <b>7 days</b> of the injury</td></tr><tr><td>Marine safety investigation (2.11)</td><td>An investigation or inquiry, however a State names it, conducted to prevent future casualties and incidents: evidence collection and analysis, identification of causal factors, safety recommendations</td></tr><tr><td>Substantially interested State (2.20)</td><td>The flag State or coastal State involved; a State whose environment was severely or significantly damaged; whose serious interests were harmed or threatened; whose nationals died or were seriously injured; that holds important information; or that has another significant interest</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Code does not define \"serious marine casualty\"; it uses only \"very serious\".</p><h2>Part II — Mandatory standards</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>Marine safety investigation Authority</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Notification</td><td>On the high seas or in an EEZ: the flag State notifies other substantially interested States as soon as reasonably practicable. In a coastal State's territory or territorial sea: flag and coastal States notify each other, then other interested States. Not delayed for lack of complete information</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Requirement to investigate very serious marine casualties</td><td>An investigation into every very serious marine casualty; the flag State is responsible, subject to a Chapter 7 agreement</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Flag State's agreement with another substantially interested State</td><td>States consult to agree who investigates; full participation in another State's investigation fulfils the flag State's obligations</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Powers of an investigation</td><td>National law must let investigators board ships, interview the master, crew and others, and obtain evidence</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Parallel investigations</td><td>Other substantially interested States may run separate investigations; timing coordinated</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Co-operation</td><td>Interested States cooperate, with participation as far as practicable</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Not subject to external direction</td><td>Investigators impartial and free from external direction</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Obtaining evidence from seafarers</td><td>Taken at the earliest practical opportunity; seafarers return to their ship or are repatriated early; informed of their rights, including legal advice and protection against self-incrimination</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Draft reports</td><td>Sent to a substantially interested State on request, if it guarantees non-circulation; comments within <b>30 days</b> or another agreed period; accuracy and completeness verified</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Final reports</td><td>Submitted to IMO for every very serious marine casualty, and for other casualties or incidents where the information could prevent or lessen future occurrences; made available to the public and the shipping industry</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>From casualty to final report</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ci-path\"><figcaption>Notification, investigation, evidence, draft report and final report</figcaption></figure><h2>Part III — Recommended practices</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>15</td><td>Administrative responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Principles of investigation</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Investigation of marine casualties (other than very serious) and marine incidents</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Factors for seeking agreement under Chapter 7 of Part II</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Acts of unlawful interference</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Notification to parties involved and commencement of an investigation</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Co-ordinating an investigation</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Collection of evidence</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Confidentiality of information</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Protection for witnesses and involved parties</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Draft and final report</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>Re-opening an investigation</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Ch 17:</b> the flag State should investigate other casualties and incidents where this is likely to prevent future occurrences.</li><li><b>Ch 20:</b> the investigation starts as soon as reasonably practicable without unnecessarily delaying the ship.</li><li><b>Ch 22:</b> ships are not detained unnecessarily; copies of documents are made where practicable.</li><li><b>Ch 25:</b> reports completed as quickly as practicable; 30 days for comments on a draft; interim safety measures may be recommended at any stage; a State that disagrees with a final report may submit its own report to IMO.</li><li><b>Ch 26:</b> an investigation should be reconsidered and may be re-opened when new evidence may materially change the conclusions.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Every very serious marine casualty (total loss, a death, or severe pollution) must be investigated, and the final report goes to IMO and the public. The purpose is prevention, not blame. Seafarers must be told their rights, including legal advice and protection against self-incrimination. Other legal proceedings can run in parallel.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>MSC.255(84) Casualty Investigation Code text (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/MSAS/Documents/Res.%20MSC.255(84)%20Casualty%20Iinvestigation%20Code.pdf</li><li>IMO — Casualty investigation: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/MSAS/Pages/Casualties.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-esp-code","v":252622198,"title":"ESP Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["esp","survey","bulk-carrier","oil-tanker","solas-xi-1","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>ESP Code — 2011 International Code on the Enhanced Programme of Inspections during Surveys of Bulk Carriers and Oil Tankers</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>30 Nov 2011, Assembly resolution A.1049(27)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jan 2014 (takes effect with the SOLAS chapter XI-1 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Made mandatory by</td><td>SOLAS regulation XI-1/2</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Annex A (bulk carriers) · Annex B (oil tankers), each in Parts A and B</td></tr><tr><td>Amendments</td><td>See the History table: consolidated text MSC.461(101); latest MSC.553(108) and MSC.584(111)</td></tr><tr><td>Bulk carrier survey cycle</td><td>Renewal every 5 years · annual · intermediate</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the ESP Code</h2><ul><li>The enhanced programme of inspections during surveys of <b>bulk carriers and oil tankers</b>, mainly the hull structure</li><li>Close-up surveys, thickness measurement, coating condition and corrosion assessment</li><li>Surveys are planned and documented, and the documents stay on board</li><li>The Maritime Safety Committee keeps the Code under review and updates it</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A.1049(27)</td><td>30 Nov 2011</td><td>1 Jan 2014</td><td>The 2011 Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.371(93)</td><td>22 May 2014</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td><td>Amended the Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.381(94)</td><td>21 Nov 2014</td><td>1 Jul 2016</td><td>Sections 5.3, 5.5, 5.6, 5.7, 6.1, 6.3, 6.4 (and 2.6 for oil tankers)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.405(96)</td><td>19 May 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2018</td><td>Survey preparation sections</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.412(97)</td><td>25 Nov 2016</td><td>1 Jul 2018</td><td>Sections 1.5, 2.4.4 and Annex 1 and others</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.461(101)</td><td>14 Jun 2019</td><td>1 Jan 2021</td><td>Completely revised (consolidated)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.483(103)</td><td>13 May 2021</td><td>1 Jan 2023</td><td>Annex B, Part A: Annex 2</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.553(108)</td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>Approval and certification of firms doing thickness measurement</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.584(111)</td><td>MSC 111, 13–22 May 2026</td><td>1 Jan 2028 (expected; deemed accepted 1 Jul 2027 unless objections)</td><td>Remote inspection techniques (RIT) in ESP surveys</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Entry into force of amendments follows the SOLAS tacit acceptance procedure.</p><h2>Structure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"esp-structure\"><figcaption>Annexes A and B, their Parts, and the eight sections in each part</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Part</th><th>Applies to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A: Bulk carriers</td><td>A</td><td>Self-propelled ships of 500 GT and above, <b>other than</b> double-side skin bulk carriers</td></tr><tr><td>A: Bulk carriers</td><td>B</td><td>Double-side skin bulk carriers</td></tr><tr><td>B: Oil tankers</td><td>A</td><td>Double-hull oil tankers</td></tr><tr><td>B: Oil tankers</td><td>B</td><td>Oil tankers other than double-hull</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Hybrid ships apply Part B to double-side skin holds and their wing spaces.</li><li>Each part has eight sections: <b>1</b> General · <b>2</b> Renewal survey · <b>3</b> Annual survey · <b>4</b> Intermediate survey · <b>5</b> Preparations for survey · <b>6</b> Documentation on board · <b>7</b> Procedures for thickness measurements · <b>8</b> Reporting and evaluation of survey.</li><li>Bulk carrier Part A has Annexes 1–15 and Part B has Annexes 1–12. Oil tanker Parts A and B each have Annexes 1–12, including tank testing and hull girder longitudinal strength criteria (Annex 12).</li></ul><h2>Key definitions (Annex A, Part A)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Substantial corrosion</td><td>Wastage in excess of <b>75% of allowable margins</b>, but within acceptable limits. For ships under the IACS Common Structural Rules, gauged thickness between tnet + 0.5 mm and tnet</td></tr><tr><td>Close-up survey</td><td>Structural details within close visual range, normally within reach of hand</td></tr><tr><td>Overall survey</td><td>Reports the hull's overall condition and determines the extent of close-up surveys</td></tr><tr><td>Suspect areas</td><td>Locations showing substantial corrosion, or considered prone to rapid wastage</td></tr><tr><td>Coating condition</td><td>Rated GOOD, FAIR or POOR by breakdown and rust percentages (POOR, for example: general breakdown over 20% of an area, or hard scale at 10% or more)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate survey</td><td>Carried out at the second or third annual survey, or between them</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Survey types (bulk carriers)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"esp-cycle\"><figcaption>Annual, intermediate and renewal surveys over the five-year cycle</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Survey</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Renewal</td><td>5-year cycle. May begin at the fourth annual survey and be completed by the fifth anniversary. At least two bottom inspections in the five years, with at most 36 months between them. For ships 15 years and older, the bottom inspection is done in dry-dock</td></tr><tr><td>Annual</td><td>Hull, weather decks, hatch covers, coamings and piping. Hatch cover sets in the forward 25% of the ship's length are surveyed open, closed and in operation each year</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate, 5–10 years</td><td>Overall survey of representative ballast tanks, plus close-up examination of at least 25% of frames in the forward hold and one other selected hold</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate, 10–15 years</td><td>Same extent as the previous renewal survey. Internal fuel tank examination and pressure testing not required unless the surveyor deems them necessary</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate, over 15 years</td><td>As 10–15 years, plus a dry-dock survey as part of the intermediate survey</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Planning, thickness measurement and documents</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey programme (5.1)</td><td>Agreed in writing with the owner before the survey, based on Annex 4A; the owner completes the planning questionnaire (Annex 4B) first</td></tr><tr><td>Planning meeting (5.6)</td><td>Held before any part of a renewal or intermediate survey starts</td></tr><tr><td>Surveyors (1.4)</td><td>Bulk carriers of 20,000 dwt and above: two surveyors for the third and later renewal and intermediate surveys. 100,000 dwt and above: two surveyors for the 10–15-year intermediate survey</td></tr><tr><td>Thickness measurement</td><td>Normally ultrasonic. Done by a <b>certified company</b>, certification renewed at intervals not exceeding 3 years. Reports give locations, measured and original thicknesses, equipment and personnel and are signed by the operator; the surveyor countersigns the cover page</td></tr><tr><td>Firm approval (MSC.553(108))</td><td>After reviewing a firm's documents, the Administration <b>must audit</b> the firm to confirm it is duly organized and managed and capable of hull thickness measurement. Applies to all annexes</td></tr><tr><td>Documents on board</td><td>The <b>survey report file</b> and supporting documents, kept on board for the ship's lifetime</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>The Condition Evaluation Report must include an English translation and be endorsed by the Administration or recognized organization</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Remote inspection techniques (MSC.584(111))</h2><ul><li>Adopted unanimously at <b>MSC 111</b> (13 to 22 May 2026) to permit the use of <b>remote inspection techniques (RIT)</b> in ESP Code surveys. The guidelines MSC.1/Circ.1700 were approved alongside.</li><li>Expected in force <b>1 Jan 2028</b>: deemed accepted 1 Jul 2027 unless objections are communicated to the Secretary-General before then.</li><li>Applies to periodical surveys that commence on or after 1 Jan 2028 when RIT is used, whatever the ship's construction date.</li><li>RIT can serve as an alternative means of access for close-up surveys and related thickness measurements.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>System</td><td>Assessed and certified against Appendix 4 of MSC.1/Circ.1700, with a Statement of Capability (SoC)</td></tr><tr><td>Firm</td><td>Approved, using a certified system with a valid SoC, within its limits</td></tr><tr><td>Survey programme</td><td>Includes an RIT inspection plan and a copy of the SoC</td></tr><tr><td>Limits</td><td>Depend on the ship's condition history, defect records and the company's performance; flag Administrations may add restrictions from the third special survey onward</td></tr><tr><td>Thickness measurement</td><td>Still taken alongside close-up surveys; alternative arrangements if RIT limitations prevent its use</td></tr><tr><td>Checks</td><td>Random confirmatory surveys validate RIT results</td></tr><tr><td>Report</td><td>The RIT firm submits a detailed report with labelled videos and photos</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The ship keeps the survey report file and supporting documents on board for life. Prepare safe access and the owner's inspection records before surveys. Thickness measurement must be done by a certified firm. Substantial corrosion means wastage over 75% of the allowable margin.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution A.1049(27), 2011 ESP Code (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1049(27).pdf</li><li>ESP Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/xi-1/esp-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Report of MSC 111, MSC.584(111) (IMO via MPA Singapore): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/api/media/e783b0c8-e1f3-44bf-8462-d2357ff91654/MSC-111-22---Report-Of-The-Maritime-Safety-Committee-On-Its-111Th-Session-(Secretariat).pdf</li><li>Cyprus Shipping News, RIT conditions: https://cyprusshippingnews.com/2026/10/15/new-imo-esp-code-framework-for-remote-inspection-techniques/</li><li>Resolution MSC.553(108) (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.553(108).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-fss-code","v":161572745,"title":"FSS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["fss","fire-safety","solas-ii-2","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>FSS Code — International Code for Fire Safety Systems</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>5 Dec 2000, resolution MSC.98(73)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jul 2002, with the revised SOLAS chapter II-2</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Mandatory under revised SOLAS chapter II-2</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>17 chapters (1–15 original; 16 added 2012, 17 added 2020)</td></tr><tr><td>Role</td><td>SOLAS II-2 says which systems a ship needs; the FSS Code says how they are built and perform</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the FSS Code</h2><ul><li>Gives the technical standards for the fire safety systems required by SOLAS chapter II-2. Some original technical provisions were moved from the Convention into the Code</li><li>Chapter 1 also covers general provisions: applicability, definitions, equivalents and alternative designs, and a ban on toxic extinguishing media</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.98(73)</td><td>5 Dec 2000</td><td>1 Jul 2002</td><td>The Code (chapters 1–15)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.206(81)</td><td>18 May 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.217(82)</td><td>8 Dec 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2008 / 1 Jul 2010</td><td>Chapters 4, 6, 7 (cabin balconies), 9 (individual remote identification of detectors on passenger ships)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.292(87)</td><td>21 May 2010</td><td>1 Jan 2012</td><td>Chapter 10 and new <b>Chapter 16</b></td></tr><tr><td>MSC.311(88)</td><td>3 Dec 2010</td><td>1 Jul 2012</td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.327(90)</td><td>25 May 2012</td><td>1 Jan 2014</td><td>Chapters 6 and 8</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.339(91)</td><td>30 Nov 2012</td><td>1 Jul 2014</td><td>Chapters 3, 5, 7 (new 2.4), 8, 9, 12, 13, 14</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.367(93)</td><td>22 May 2014</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td><td>Chapter 15</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.403(96)</td><td>19 May 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Chapter 8 (2.4.1) and new <b>Chapter 17</b></td></tr><tr><td>MSC.457(101)</td><td>14 Jun 2019</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Chapter 15 (2.2.3.2 and 2.2.4)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.484(103)</td><td>13 May 2021</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Chapter 9 (new 2.1.8)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.555(108)</td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>Chapters 7 and 9</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Chapter 1 applies to ships whose keels are laid on or after 1 Jul 2002, as in the 2000 text; later amendments set their own application dates.</li></ul><h2>The chapters</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fss-chapters\"><figcaption>The 17 chapters, with the two later additions marked</figcaption></figure><h2>Chapter 2 — International shore connection</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fss-shore-connection\"><figcaption>Flange dimensions from Table 2.1</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Kept on board with a gasket, four bolts, four nuts and eight washers.</li></ul><h2>Chapter 3 — Personnel protection</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire-fighter's outfit</td><td>Protective clothing with a water-resistant outer surface; rubber or non-conducting boots; rigid impact-resistant helmet; electric safety lamp with at least <b>3 h</b> burning time (explosion-proof on tankers and hazardous areas); insulated axe</td></tr><tr><td>Breathing apparatus</td><td>Compressed-air SCBA with at least <b>1,200 litres</b> of air in the cylinders, or another SCBA that runs at least <b>30 min</b>; cylinders interchangeable</td></tr><tr><td>Lifeline</td><td>Fireproof, at least 30 m long, proof-tested at 3.5 kN for 5 min, with a snap-hook</td></tr><tr><td>EEBD</td><td>At least <b>10 min</b> service duration; flame-resistant hood or full face piece with a clear viewing window; for <b>escape only</b>, not for fire-fighting or entering oxygen-deficient spaces</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter 4 — Fire extinguishers</h2><ul><li>Powder or CO₂ extinguisher: at least 5 kg. Foam extinguisher: at least 9 litres.</li><li>Portable extinguishers must not exceed <b>23 kg</b> and must be at least as effective as a 9-litre fluid extinguisher.</li><li>Portable foam applicator: inductor-type nozzle on the fire main, a tank of at least 20 litres of foam liquid plus one spare tank, output at least 1.5 m³/min.</li></ul><h2>Chapter 5 — Fixed gas systems (CO₂)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cargo spaces</td><td>Free gas volume at least <b>30%</b> of the largest protected cargo space</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery spaces</td><td>The larger of <b>40%</b> of the largest machinery space (casing excluded above the 40% horizontal-area level) or <b>35%</b> including the casing. Cargo ships under 2,000 GT with two or more non-separate machinery spaces: 35% and 30%</td></tr><tr><td>Calculation</td><td>Free CO₂ volume at 0.56 m³/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Discharge</td><td>Machinery spaces: <b>85%</b> of the gas within <b>2 min</b></td></tr><tr><td>Controls</td><td>Two separate controls in a release box: one opens the space valve, the other releases the gas. Key in a break-glass enclosure</td></tr><tr><td>Alarm</td><td>Audible pre-discharge alarm in ro-ro and occupied spaces, at least <b>20 s</b> before release</td></tr><tr><td>Automatic release</td><td>Not permitted unless the Administration allows it</td></tr><tr><td>Pipes</td><td>Through accommodation: welded, substantial thickness, pressure-tested at not less than 5 N/mm²</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters 6 to 8 — Foam, water-spray, sprinklers</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Key figures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6 Fixed foam</td><td><b>High-expansion:</b> fill the largest space at least 1 m depth per minute; foam volume five times the largest space; expansion ratio not above 1,000:1. <b>Low-expansion:</b> 150 mm depth over the largest oil-spread area within 5 min; ratio not above 12:1</td></tr><tr><td>7 Water-spray and water-mist</td><td>Average at least 5 l/m²/min in machinery spaces; pump starts automatically on pressure drop; pump and controls outside the protected spaces</td></tr><tr><td>8 Sprinklers, detection and alarm</td><td>Wet-pipe systems (saunas dry-pipe, heads up to 140 °C); spare heads 6 (fewer than 300 installed), 12 (300–1,000), 24 (over 1,000); operating range 68–79 °C in accommodation; sections of at most 200 sprinklers, and on passenger ships at most two decks; two power sources</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters 9 and 10 — Detection</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Heat detectors</td><td>Operate before <b>78 °C</b>, not below <b>54 °C</b>, at a rise under 1 °C/min; drying rooms up to 130 °C, saunas up to 140 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Smoke detectors</td><td>In stairways, corridors and escape routes: operate before 12.5%/m obscuration, not below 2%/m</td></tr><tr><td>Spacing</td><td>Heat: 37 m² each, 9 m apart, 4.5 m from bulkheads. Smoke: 74 m², 11 m, 5.5 m</td></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td>Two sources, one of them emergency</td></tr><tr><td>Alarm</td><td>An unacknowledged alarm sounds throughout accommodation, service, control and category A machinery spaces after <b>2 min</b></td></tr><tr><td>Panel</td><td>On the bridge or in a manned central control station</td></tr><tr><td>Sample extraction (Ch 10)</td><td>Sensing unit operates before 6.65%/m obscuration; duplicate fans and alternative power; sampling pipes at least 12 mm internal diameter</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters 11 to 13</h2><ul><li><b>Ch 11 Low-location lighting:</b> approved under IMO guidelines (A.752(18)) or ISO 15370:2001.</li><li><b>Ch 12 Emergency fire pumps:</b> capacity at least <b>40%</b> of the required fire pump capacity, and not less than 25 m³/h (passenger ships under 1,000 GT and cargo ships of 2,000 GT and above) or 15 m³/h (cargo ships under 2,000 GT). Diesel drive must start by hand at 0 °C, at least six times within 30 min, including twice in the first 10 min. Service tank holds 3 h at full load, with reserve fuel outside the machinery space for 15 h more.</li><li><b>Ch 13 Means of escape:</b> passenger ships' stairways at least 900 mm wide, plus 10 mm per person above 90; at most 3.5 m rise without a landing; inclination at most 45°; handrails on both sides. Cargo ships: stairways and corridors at least 700 mm wide, handrail on one side (both sides at 1,800 mm and over).</li></ul><h2>Chapters 14 to 17 — Tankers and helicopters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Key figures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>14 Fixed deck foam</td><td>Solution rate: the greatest of 0.6 l/min/m² of cargo tank deck area, 6 l/min/m² of the largest tank's sectional area, or 3 l/min/m² of the monitor-protected area (at least 1,250 l/min). Foam supply <b>20 min</b> with inert gas, <b>30 min</b> without. Expansion ratio generally not above 12:1. Applicators at least 400 l/min each, throw at least 15 m, at least four</td></tr><tr><td>15 Inert gas</td><td>Cargo tank atmosphere at <b>8% oxygen or less</b> by volume and positive pressure. Supply at 125% of maximum discharge rate, with no more than <b>5%</b> oxygen in the supply main. At least two blowers and two non-return devices, one a water seal. Alarms for oxygen above 8%, gas pressure below 100 mm water gauge, and high pressure; automatic shutdown of blowers and the gas regulating valve</td></tr><tr><td>16 Fixed hydrocarbon gas detection</td><td>Central gas measurement unit with sampling pipes in ballast tanks and void spaces of double-hull and double-bottom spaces next to cargo tanks, including the forepeak. At least two sampling points per space (upper at least 1 m from tank top, lower at least 0.5 m from tank bottom). Each line analysed in turn at intervals not over 30 min. Alarm at a pre-set value not over <b>30% of the LFL</b>; separate alarm for clogged lines</td></tr><tr><td>17 Helicopter facility foam</td><td>Foam systems protecting helidecks and landing areas: discharge rates, foam concentrate quantities, nozzle types, equipment height limits</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>The 2026 amendments (MSC.555(108), with SOLAS MSC.550(108))</h2><p>Both resolutions take effect 1 Jan 2026. MSC.555(108) updates FSS chapters 7 and 9.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>New requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ch 7, new 2.5</td><td>Fixed water-based systems on weather decks carrying vehicles on ro-ro passenger ships built on or after 1 Jan 2026: monitors at least 2.0 l/min per m² of protected area, none below 1,250 l/min; monitors outside the protected area; far end within 75% of the monitor's still-air throw; areas monitors cannot reach need nozzles giving 5.0 l/min per m²; four jets where dangerous goods are carried on the weather deck</td></tr><tr><td>Ch 9, 2.3.1.3 to 2.3.1.5</td><td>Heat detector tests to EN 54:2001 and IEC 60092-504; <b>new</b> linear heat detectors tested to EN 54-22:2015 and IEC 60092-504</td></tr><tr><td>Ch 9, 2.4.2.2</td><td>Revised spacing: heat 37 m², 9 m, 4.5 m · smoke 74 m², 11 m, 5.5 m · combined smoke and heat 74 m², 9 m, 4.5 m · linear heat cable not more than 9.0 m apart and 4.5 m from bulkheads</td></tr><tr><td>Ch 9, 2.5.1.2 to 2.5.1.4</td><td>Ro-ro passenger ships built from 1 Jan 2026: alarms shown consistently and recognisable on the bridge; alarm addressability, history, latest alarm and suppression; smoke detection in special category and ro-ro spaces may be disconnected during loading and unloading (time-limited, resets automatically, shown on the central unit); heat detection and manual call points cannot be disconnected</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>SOLAS II-2 side (UK MGN 713):</b> passenger ships built from 1 Jan 2026 need individually addressable smoke and heat detection in vehicle, special category and ro-ro spaces, and continuous video monitoring with footage replayable for at least 7 days. Older passenger ships need water monitors by their first survey from 1 Jan 2028 and video monitoring by 1 Jan 2028 with 24 hours of replay.</p><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Keep the international shore connection on board and ready. EEBDs are for escape only. CO₂ is released only from two separate controls, after the pre-discharge alarm has run at least 20 seconds. Inert gas: at most 8% oxygen in the tanks and at most 5% in the supply main.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.98(73), FSS Code text (via US Government Printing): https://corpora.tika.apache.org/base/docs/govdocs1/668/668672.pdf</li><li>IMO — History of fire protection requirements: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/History-of-fire-protection-requirements.aspx</li><li>FSS Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/ii-2/fss-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>MSC.217(82): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.217(82).pdf</li><li>MSC.292(87), Chapter 16: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.292(87).pdf</li><li>MSC.403(96), Chapter 17: https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/file/MSC.403%2896%29.pdf</li><li>MSC.555(108): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution%20MSC.555(108).pdf</li><li>UK MCA MGN 713: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-713-m-fire-protection-regulations-adoptions-of-amendments-in-imo-resolutions-in-force-on-1-jan-2026/mgn-713-m-fire-protection-regulations-adoptions-of-amendments-in-imo-resolutions-in-force-on-1-jan-2026</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ftp-code","v":15625674,"title":"FTP Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ftp","fire-test","solas-ii-2","materials","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>FTP Code — International Code for Application of Fire Test Procedures (2010 FTP Code)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>3 Dec 2010, resolution MSC.307(88)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jul 2012 (with the SOLAS II-2 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Mandatory under SOLAS chapter II-2, for products required to be tested, evaluated and approved on or after 1 Jul 2012</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>The 1998 FTP Code, MSC.61(67)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>9 chapters · Annex 1 (11 Parts) · Annexes 2–4</td></tr><tr><td>Amendment</td><td>MSC.437(99), adopted 24 May 2018, in force 1 Jan 2020 (Annex 3, Table 1)</td></tr><tr><td>Used by</td><td>Administrations, competent authorities and testing laboratories</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the FTP Code</h2><ul><li>The <b>test procedures</b> used to approve fire-resistant and fire-restricting materials and products for ships</li><li>Used by the Administration to approve products and by laboratories to test them</li><li>Applies where SOLAS requires products to be tested \"in accordance with the Fire Test Procedures Code\"</li><li>On board you meet the result: approved products with their certificates</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>The 1998 Code was made mandatory by MSC.57(67) for ships constructed on or after 1 Jul 1998.</li><li>Type approvals based on tests under the previous Code were accepted if the tests were made no later than one year after entry into force; renewals without retesting were allowed for reports up to 15 years old.</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Scope</td><td>Use by Administrations, authorities and testing laboratories</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Application</td><td>Where the Convention requires products to be tested to the FTP Code</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Definitions</td><td>Standard time-temperature curve; \"sustained flaming\" = flames lasting 5 s or longer</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Testing</td><td>Test procedures (Annex 1); laboratories recognized by the Administration (quality control based on ISO/IEC 17025); test reports</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Approval</td><td>Type approval: certificates valid up to <b>5 years</b>, test reports under 5 years old when submitted. Case-by-case approval: valid only for the specific ship</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Products installed without testing and/or approval</td><td>Listed in Annex 2</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Equivalents and modern technology</td><td>Alternative tests need written analyses</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Period of grace</td><td>See History</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>List of references</td><td>ISO and IEC standards, including ISO 834-1, ISO 1182, ISO 5659-2, ISO 5660-1/-2, ISO 9705, ISO 19702</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ftp-fire-curve\"><figcaption>The standard time-temperature curve used in the fire resistance tests</figcaption></figure><h2>Annex 1 — Fire test procedures</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Non-combustibility (ISO 1182)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Smoke and toxicity</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>\"A\", \"B\" and \"F\" class divisions</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Fire door control systems</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Surface flammability (low flame-spread surfaces, primary deck coverings)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Blank</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Vertically supported textiles and films</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Upholstered furniture</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Bedding components</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Fire-restricting materials for high-speed craft</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Fire-resisting divisions of high-speed craft</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Part 1 — Non-combustibility</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Furnace temperature</td><td>750 ± 5 °C, test up to 30 min</td></tr><tr><td>Average furnace and surface temperature rise</td><td>≤ 30 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Average sustained flaming</td><td>≤ 10 s</td></tr><tr><td>Average mass loss</td><td>≤ 50%</td></tr><tr><td>Specimens</td><td>5 (homogeneous) or 10 (non-homogeneous), each 43–45 mm diameter and 50 ± 3 mm high</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Part 2 — Smoke and toxicity</b></p><ul><li>Appendix 1: smoke test to ISO 5659-2 at 25 kW/m² (with and without pilot flame) and 50 kW/m² (no pilot).</li><li>Appendix 2: toxic gases measured by FTIR (ISO 19702).</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Smoke density Dm (max)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bulkhead, lining and ceiling surfaces</td><td>200</td></tr><tr><td>Primary deck coverings</td><td>400</td></tr><tr><td>Floor coverings</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Plastic pipes</td><td>400</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Toxic gas</th><th>Limit (ppm)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CO</td><td>1,450</td></tr><tr><td>HCl, HBr, HF</td><td>600 each</td></tr><tr><td>HCN</td><td>140</td></tr><tr><td>SO₂</td><td>120 (200 for floor coverings)</td></tr><tr><td>NOx</td><td>350</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Part 3 — \"A\", \"B\" and \"F\" class divisions</b></p><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ftp-class-divisions\"><figcaption>Insulation criteria for the unexposed side</figcaption></figure><ul><li>\"A\" class: average rise ≤ 140 °C, any single point ≤ 180 °C. Durations: A-60, A-30, A-15, A-0.</li><li>\"B\" and \"F\" class: average rise ≤ 140 °C, any single point ≤ 225 °C. Durations: B-15, B-0, F-15, F-0.</li><li>Integrity: no flaming on the unexposed face, no ignition of the cotton-wool pad, and gap gauges must not pass.</li><li>Aluminium cores may rise no more than 200 °C.</li><li>Appendices cover fire resistance (1), windows, dampers and penetrations (2), thermal radiation through windows (3), and continuous \"B\" ceilings and linings (4).</li></ul><p><b>Part 4 — Fire door control systems</b></p><ul><li>The system is tested as a prototype, independent of its power supply (pneumatic, hydraulic or electrical), in the Part 3 furnace.</li><li>The door starts open, and the system must show it can close the door at the start of the test.</li><li>Furnace temperature raised and held at <b>200 ± 50 °C</b> within 5 min, held for the first <b>60 min</b>, then following the standard curve up to 945 °C.</li><li>The opening and closing function is activated every 5 min for the first 60 min.</li><li>Power to the control system is isolated at average furnace temperatures up to 300 °C. The door must stay closed at least up to 945 °C.</li><li>Criteria: the system must not fail in the first 60 min; from 60 min to the end of the test the door must remain closed.</li></ul><p><b>Part 5 — Surface flammability</b></p><ul><li>Averages must meet Table 1 for materials to be considered low flame-spread.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Linings (bulkhead, wall, ceiling)</th><th>Floor coverings</th><th>Primary deck coverings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qt (MJ)</td><td>≤ 0.7</td><td>≤ 2.0</td><td>≤ 2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Qp (kW)</td><td>≤ 4.0</td><td>≤ 10.0</td><td>≤ 10.0</td></tr><tr><td>Burning droplets</td><td>Not produced</td><td>No more than 10 burning drops</td><td>Not produced</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Burning droplets make a lining or primary deck covering a reject, whatever the other criteria.</li></ul><p><b>Part 10:</b> full-scale room test per ISO 9705 and heat release, smoke and mass loss per ISO 5660-1/-2.</p><h2>Annex 2 — Products that may be installed without testing or approval</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Products</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-combustible materials</td><td>Only glass, concrete, ceramics, natural stone, masonry units, common metals or metal alloys: treated as non-combustible, no testing or approval</td></tr><tr><td>Low smoke and toxicity</td><td>Non-combustible materials meet Part 2. Surface materials and primary deck coverings with total heat release ≤ 0.2 MJ and peak heat release rate ≤ 1 kW meet Part 2</td></tr><tr><td>Steel divisions</td><td><b>\"A-0\" bulkhead:</b> 4 mm plating with 60 × 60 × 5 mm stiffeners at 600 mm spacing, or a structural equivalent. <b>\"A-0\" deck:</b> 4 mm plating with 95 × 65 × 7 mm stiffeners at 600 mm spacing, or a structural equivalent</td></tr><tr><td>Low flame-spread surfaces</td><td>Non-combustible materials meet Part 5 (but consider the method of fixing, for example glue). Primary deck coverings rated not readily ignitable meet the floor covering requirements</td></tr><tr><td>High-speed craft</td><td>Fire-restricting materials meet Parts 2 and 5 without further testing</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Annexes 3 and 4</h2><ul><li><b>Annex 3:</b> fire protection materials and the required approval test methods. Table 1 is for passenger ships carrying more than 36 passengers and high-speed craft; Table 2 is for cargo ships (method IC).</li><li><b>Annex 4:</b> interpretation of SOLAS II-2 regulations 5.3 and 6.2 (MSC/Circ.1120), with tables for passenger ship accommodation bulkheads and cargo ship accommodation spaces (methods IC, IIC and IIIC).</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Use only products with a valid FTP approval when you replace a bulkhead panel, door, floor covering or curtain. Annex 2 lists the few products that need no test. Fire door control systems are tested to keep the door closed up to 945 °C.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.307(88), 2010 FTP Code text: https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/regulation/21812-MSC_307%2888%29.pdf</li><li>FTP Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/ii-2/ftp-code-amendment-status.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-grain-code","v":109046623,"title":"Grain Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["grain","bulk","stability","solas-vi","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>Grain Code — International Code for the Safe Carriage of Grain in Bulk</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>23 May 1991, resolution MSC.23(59)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jan 1994</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Mandatory under SOLAS chapter VI, part C</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Part A (specific requirements) · Part B (calculation of assumed heeling moments and general assumptions)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>All ships regardless of size, including under 500 GT, carrying grain in bulk</td></tr><tr><td>Stability criteria</td><td>Heel ≤ 12° · residual area ≥ 0.075 m·rad · GM ≥ 0.30 m</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendment</td><td>MSC.552(108), in force 1 Jan 2026</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Grain Code</h2><ul><li>An international standard for the safe carriage of grain in bulk: stability, stowage, fittings and securing</li><li><b>Grain</b> means wheat, maize, oats, rye, barley, rice, pulses, seeds and processed forms with similar behaviour (A 2.1)</li><li>Each ship loaded under the Code needs a <b>Document of Authorization</b> showing it can meet the Code</li><li>The document accompanies or is included in the <b>grain loading manual</b>, which gives the master the stability information</li><li>A copy of the document, with the stability data and plans, is kept on board for inspection by the loading port's authorities</li></ul><h2>Part A — Specific requirements</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Title and key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A 1</td><td>Application: all ships regardless of size, including under 500 GT</td></tr><tr><td>A 2</td><td>Definitions (see below)</td></tr><tr><td>A 3</td><td>Document of authorization: issued for each ship loaded under the Code; accompanies the grain loading manual; copies on board; a ship without one must demonstrate compliance before loading</td></tr><tr><td>A 4</td><td>Equivalents: recorded in the document or manual</td></tr><tr><td>A 5</td><td>Exemptions: the Administration may exempt ships or classes for sheltered voyages</td></tr><tr><td>A 6</td><td>Stability and loading information: booklet with ship particulars, lightship data, KG, free surface corrections, capacities, flooding angle curve (where less than 40°), hydrostatic curves and cross curves of stability at 12° and 40°; approved data on volumes and heeling moments per compartment and maximum permissible heeling moments</td></tr><tr><td>A 7</td><td>Stability requirements (see below)</td></tr><tr><td>A 8</td><td>Existing ships (keel laid before 25 May 1980)</td></tr><tr><td>A 9</td><td>Optional requirements for partial cargoes (ships without documents)</td></tr><tr><td>A 10</td><td>Stowage</td></tr><tr><td>A 11</td><td>Strength of grain fittings</td></tr><tr><td>A 12</td><td>Divisions loaded on both sides</td></tr><tr><td>A 13</td><td>Divisions loaded on one side only</td></tr><tr><td>A 14</td><td>Saucers</td></tr><tr><td>A 15</td><td>Bundling</td></tr><tr><td>A 16</td><td>Overstowing arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>A 17</td><td>Strapping or lashing</td></tr><tr><td>A 18</td><td>Securing with wire mesh</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Definitions (A 2)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Filled compartment, trimmed (2.2)</td><td>Grain at its highest possible level after loading and trimming under A 10.2</td></tr><tr><td>Filled compartment, untrimmed (2.3)</td><td>Filled to the maximum in way of the hatch but not trimmed outside the hatch periphery</td></tr><tr><td>Partly filled compartment (2.4)</td><td>Grain not loaded as in 2.2 or 2.3</td></tr><tr><td>Angle of flooding (2.5)</td><td>Heel angle at which openings that cannot be closed weathertight immerse</td></tr><tr><td>Stowage factor (2.6)</td><td>Volume per unit weight, with no allowance for lost space</td></tr><tr><td>Specially suitable compartment (2.7)</td><td>At least two grain-tight longitudinal divisions, coincident with hatch side girders or positioned to limit transverse shift; sloping divisions at least 30° to the horizontal</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Stability requirements (A 7)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"grain-criteria\"><figcaption>The three stability criteria, with a schematic righting and heeling arm diagram</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7.1.1 Angle of heel from grain shift</td><td>Not more than <b>12°</b>, or for ships constructed on or after 1 Jan 1994 the deck-edge immersion angle, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>7.1.2 Residual area</td><td>Between the heeling arm and righting arm curves, up to the smaller of the angle of maximum difference, 40° or the flooding angle, at least <b>0.075 m·rad</b> in all loading conditions</td></tr><tr><td>7.1.3 Initial GM</td><td>Corrected for free surfaces in tanks, at least <b>0.30 m</b></td></tr><tr><td>7.2</td><td>The master may be required to demonstrate stability at all stages of the voyage before loading</td></tr><tr><td>7.3</td><td>The ship must be upright after loading and before sailing</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Existing ships (A 8):</b> ships loaded under previously approved documents (SOLAS 1960 regulation 12, resolutions A.184(VI) or A.264(VIII)) are considered equivalent to A 7. Ships without a document may use A 9 with no deadweight limit.</p><p><b>Partial cargoes without documents (A 9):</b></p><ul><li>Grain not more than <b>one third of deadweight</b></li><li>Centreline divisions in filled, trimmed compartments, extending below deck at least 1/8 of the breadth or 2.4 m, whichever is greater (saucers may replace them in and beneath a hatch, except for linseed and similar seeds)</li><li>Hatches to trimmed compartments closed and secured</li><li>Free grain surfaces in partly filled spaces levelled and secured under A 16, 17 or 18</li><li>GM at least 0.3 m, or the greater value from the formula in the Code</li><li>The master demonstrates compliance to the Administration or port State</li></ul><h2>Stowage (A 10)</h2><p>Free surfaces are trimmed and shifting minimized (10.1).</p><ul><li>Trimmed compartments (10.2):<ul><li>filled under decks and hatch covers as far as possible</li></ul></li><li>Untrimmed compartments (10.3):<ul><li>filled in way of the hatch</li><li>grain may rest at its natural angle outside the hatch periphery</li><li>only for compartments with feeder ducts or perforated decks, or specially suitable compartments</li></ul></li><li>Hatch covers:<ul><li>non-grain-tight 'tween-deck hatch covers are sealed by taping, tarpaulins or similar</li></ul></li><li>Partly filled compartments:<ul><li>surface levelled</li><li>secured against overstowing (A 16), or strapped or lashed (A 17 or A 18), unless the heeling effect is accounted for</li></ul></li></ul><h2>Securing the grain surface (A 11 to A 18)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Key figures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11 Strength of fittings</td><td>Working stresses: 19.6 kN/cm² for steel, 1.57 kN/cm² for wood. Upright housing at least 75 mm at each end</td></tr><tr><td>12 Shifting boards</td><td>At least <b>50 mm</b> thick, grain-tight. Maximum unsupported span 2.5 m (50 mm), 3.0 m (60 mm), 3.5 m (70 mm), 4.0 m (80 mm). Ends housed at least 75 mm. Steel wire stays: working load not more than one third of breaking load, designed for 4.9 kN/m². Shores not steeper than 45°</td></tr><tr><td>13 One-sided loading</td><td>Loads from Tables A 13-1 to A 13-6; stays and shores not over one third of breaking load</td></tr><tr><td>14 Saucers</td><td>Only in filled, trimmed compartments, not for linseed-type seeds. Depth from saucer bottom to deck line at least 1.2 m (moulded breadth up to 9.1 m) to at least 1.8 m (18.3 m or more)</td></tr><tr><td>15 Bundling</td><td>Alternative to saucer filling; lining of 2,687 N per 5 cm strip tensile strength, or 1,344 N per 5 cm with athwartship lashings at 2.4 m or less</td></tr><tr><td>16 Overstowing</td><td>Separation cloth or platform on levelled grain; bearers at most 1.2 m apart; boards 25 mm thick with gaps of at most 100 mm; topped with tightly stowed bagged grain to at least 1/16 of the free surface breadth or 1.2 m, whichever is greater</td></tr><tr><td>17 Strapping or lashing</td><td>Grain slightly crowned; separation cloths overlapping at least 1.8 m; lashings of 19 mm steel wire, 50 × 1.3 mm steel strapping (breaking load at least 49 kN) or equivalent chain; 32 mm turnbuckles; attached about 450 mm below the final surface, at most 2.4 m apart; inspect and tighten during the voyage</td></tr><tr><td>18 Wire mesh</td><td>Burlap (at least 1,344 N per 5 cm), then two layers of 3 mm wire mesh with 150 mm squares; hold-down lashings at most 2.4 m apart, first and last within 300 mm of bulkheads</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Part B — Assumed heeling moments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"grain-shift-angles\"><figcaption>Assumed grain surface angles after shifting for the three cases</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trimmed (B 2)</td><td>Grain surface after shifting assumed at <b>15°</b></td></tr><tr><td>Untrimmed (B 3)</td><td><b>25°</b> generally, or 15° where the void area in a section is no greater than the trimmed-equivalent void. End-untrimmed: 15° abreast the hatch, 25° in the ends</td></tr><tr><td>Trunks (B 4 in the 1991 text)</td><td>25° shift where wing spaces cannot be trimmed</td></tr><tr><td>Partly filled (B 5 in the 1991 text)</td><td><b>25°</b> if not secured under A 16, 17 or 18; total heeling moment × <b>1.12</b> (B 1.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Void depth (B 1.1.1)</td><td>Vd = Vd1 + 0.75(d − 600) mm, Vd1 from Table B 1-1 (430–570 mm), never less than 100 mm</td></tr><tr><td>Credited underdeck voids (B 1.3)</td><td>Total heeling moment multiplied by 1.06</td></tr><tr><td>Divisions in partly filled compartments (B 5.2)</td><td>Extend 1/8 of the maximum breadth above and below the grain surface</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>2026 amendments (MSC.552(108))</h2><ul><li>Adopted 23 May 2024 at MSC 108; deemed accepted 1 Jul 2025 unless objections; <b>in force 1 Jan 2026</b>.</li><li><b>New definition A 2.8:</b> a \"specially suitable compartment, partly filled in way of the hatch opening, with ends untrimmed\": filled to at least the bottom edge of the hatch end beams and not trimmed outside the hatch periphery under the new A 10.4.</li><li><b>New A 10.4:</b> grain outside the hatch periphery may rest at its natural angle of repose; end trimming may be waived for specially suitable compartments. Only the surface in way of the hatch must be level (A 10.7).</li><li><b>New B 1.1.5:</b> grain surfaces assumed to slope at <b>30°</b> from the lower edge of the hatch end beam.</li><li><b>B 1.5 replaced:</b> total heeling moment = 1.12 × the calculated transverse heeling moment, for partly filled compartments and the new category.</li><li><b>New B section 4:</b> these compartments follow the B 2 trimmed rules, but the surface in way of the hatch and in the ends is assumed at <b>25°</b> after shifting. The old section 4 is renumbered section 5 (section numbers above are those of the 1991 text).</li><li>Practical point (Gard): the grain loading manual and loading computer must carry the new heeling moment data and be approved before the new condition is used.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> No grain loading without a Document of Authorization and the approved grain loading manual. Check the three criteria: heel at most 12°, residual area at least 0.075 m·rad, GM at least 0.30 m. A partly filled hold must have its surface levelled and secured, or the larger 25° shift moment applies.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.23(59), Grain Code text (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.23(59).pdf</li><li>IMO — Grain Code: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/Grain-Code.aspx</li><li>Resolution MSC.552(108) (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/SOLAS%201974%20(202)%20-%20Resolution%20MSC.552(108).pdf</li><li>Gard — The Grain Code is being revised: https://gard.no/insights/the-grain-code-is-being-revised-are-you-ready/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-hsc-code","v":142152032,"title":"HSC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["hsc","high-speed-craft","fast-ferry","solas-x","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>HSC Code — International Code of Safety for High-Speed Craft</h1><p><b>1994 HSC Code and 2000 HSC Code</b></p><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1994 HSC Code</td><td>MSC.36(63), adopted 20 May 1994 · mandatory from 1 Jan 1996 · craft built on or after 1 Jan 1996 and before 1 Jul 2002</td></tr><tr><td>2000 HSC Code</td><td>MSC.97(73), adopted 5 Dec 2000 · in force 1 Jul 2002 · craft built on or after 1 Jul 2002</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory by</td><td>SOLAS chapter X (MSC.99(73) for the 2000 Code)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Preamble · 19 chapters · 11 annexes</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>High-Speed Craft Safety Certificate, up to 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Also needed</td><td>Permit to Operate High-Speed Craft</td></tr><tr><td>Voyage limit</td><td>Passenger craft up to 4 h, cargo craft up to 8 h at operational speed from a place of refuge</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the HSC Code</h2><ul><li>Complete safety standards for <b>high-speed craft</b> on international voyages: design, equipment, operation and crew</li><li>Requires the Code to be applied in its entirety, a quality-management system, operation only by persons qualified for the craft type, operating limits, proximity to a place of refuge, and no enclosed sleeping berths for passengers (1.2)</li><li>The 1994 Code introduced \"assisted craft\" (basis for Category A) and \"unassisted craft\" (basis for cargo craft and Category B)</li></ul><h2>What counts as a high-speed craft (1.4.30)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hsc-speed-line\"><figcaption>The speed threshold, from 3.7 × displacement^0.1667</figcaption></figure><ul><li>A craft whose maximum speed in m/s is equal to or greater than <b>3.7 ∇^0.1667</b>, where ∇ is the displacement volume at the design waterline in m³</li><li>Craft supported entirely clear of the water by ground-effect aerodynamic forces are excluded</li><li><b>Maximum speed (1.4.37):</b> at maximum continuous propulsion power certified, at maximum operational weight, in smooth water</li><li><b>Operational speed (1.4.44):</b> 90% of maximum speed</li></ul><h2>Application (1.3)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Construction date (1.3.1)</td><td>Keels laid, or similar stage of construction, on or after 1 Jul 2002 (2000 Code)</td></tr><tr><td>\"Similar stage\" (1.3.2)</td><td>Construction identifiable with a specific craft has begun and assembly has started of at least <b>50 tonnes or 3%</b> of the estimated mass of all structural material, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Conversions (1.3.3)</td><td>A cargo craft converted to passenger use is treated as a passenger craft constructed when the conversion begins</td></tr><tr><td>Passenger craft (1.3.4)</td><td>Not more than <b>4 hours</b> at operational speed from a place of refuge</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo craft (1.3.4)</td><td>500 GT and above; not more than <b>8 hours</b> at operational speed from a place of refuge, fully laden</td></tr><tr><td>Excluded (1.3.5–1.3.6)</td><td>Craft of war and troopcraft, unpropelled craft, primitive wooden craft, non-trading pleasure craft, fishing craft, and craft operating solely on the Great Lakes and the lower St. Lawrence</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Craft types</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hsc-craft-types\"><figcaption>Category A, Category B and cargo craft</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Passenger craft (1.4.47):</b> carries more than <b>12 passengers</b>. A passenger excludes the master, crew, persons employed on the craft's business, and children under one year.</li><li><b>Place of refuge (1.4.48):</b> a naturally or artificially sheltered area a craft can use under conditions likely to endanger it.</li><li><b>Worst intended conditions (1.4.61):</b> the specified environmental limits within which the craft's operation is certified.</li></ul><h2>Chapters and annexes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General comments and requirements</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Buoyancy, stability and subdivision</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Structures</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Accommodation and escape measures</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Directional control systems</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Anchoring, towing and berthing</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Fire safety</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Life-saving appliances and arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Machinery</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Auxiliary systems</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Remote control, alarm and safety systems</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Electrical installations</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Shipborne navigational systems and equipment and voyage data recorders</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Radiocommunications</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Operating compartment layout</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Stabilization systems</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Handling, controllability and performance</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Operational requirements</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Inspection and maintenance requirements</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Form of High-Speed Craft Safety Certificate and Record of Equipment</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Form of Permit to Operate High-Speed Craft</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Use of probability concept</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Procedures for failure mode and effects analysis</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ice accretion applicable to all types of craft</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Stability of hydrofoil craft</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Stability of multihull craft</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Stability of monohull craft</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Definitions, requirements and compliance criteria related to operational and safety performance</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Criteria for testing and evaluation of revenue and crew seats</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Open reversible liferafts</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Certificates and permits (Chapter 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>High-Speed Craft Safety Certificate (1.8)</td><td>Issued after an initial or renewal survey to a craft that complies; the Administration assumes full responsibility. Valid <b>up to 5 years</b> (1.8.4); renewal surveys at intervals not over 5 years (1.5.1.2); periodical surveys within 3 months before or after each anniversary (1.5.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term endorsement (1.8.9)</td><td>An existing certificate may be endorsed for up to 5 months after expiry</td></tr><tr><td>Extension (1.8.10)</td><td>To reach a survey location, limited to one month</td></tr><tr><td>Permit to Operate (1.9)</td><td>Needed for commercial operation in addition to the Safety Certificate (transit voyages without passengers or cargo exempt). Sets the operating conditions from the route operational manual (Ch 18); the Administration consults each port State before issuing; port States may inspect, and deficiencies make the Permit invalid until corrected</td></tr><tr><td>Type rating</td><td>Type rating training: 18.6 (passenger craft) and 18.8 (cargo craft)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Selected requirements</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Figures stated in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 Stability</td><td>Displacement mode, intact: inclination not over <b>10°</b> in still water (2.11). Non-displacement: heel from turning not over 8°, total heel from beam wind and turning not over 12° (2.12.2). Damaged passenger craft: normally not over 10°, up to 15° allowed right after damage if reduced to 10° within 15 min (2.13.1). Category B, raking damage: not over 20°, positive righting lever range at least 15°, positive area at least 0.015 m·rad (2.13.2). Damaged cargo craft: normally not over 15°, up to 20° right after damage reducing to 15° within 15 min (2.15). Passenger craft lightweight survey at intervals not over 5 years; re-incline if lightweight displacement deviates more than 2% or LCG more than 1% of L (2.14.1)</td></tr><tr><td>4 Accommodation</td><td>A seat for each passenger and crew member (4.5.1). At least two unobstructed evacuation paths per person (4.7.12) and two exits from each enclosed public space (4.7.4). Corridors, doorways and stairs at least <b>900 mm</b> (passenger craft) and <b>700 mm</b> (cargo craft) (4.7.13). Guard rails at least 1 m high (4.11.1). Noise: public spaces and crew accommodation generally not above 75 dB(A), operating compartment not above 65 dB(A) (4.10)</td></tr><tr><td>7 Fire safety</td><td>Manual call points: no part of the space more than 20 m from one (7.8.3.2). Ro-ro spaces: no point more than about 15 m walking distance from an extinguisher (7.8.4.3). Hinged fire doors close in no more than 40 s and no less than 10 s (7.9.3.3.1). Fire-fighter's breathing apparatus works at least 30 min (7.10.3.2.2)</td></tr><tr><td>8 Life-saving</td><td>Survival craft for at least <b>100%</b> of persons certified, minimum two craft (8.10.1.1), plus additional craft for at least <b>10%</b> (8.10.1.2). Davit-launched craft not fitted: marine evacuation system or equivalent (8.7.4). MES serviced at intervals not over 12 months (8.9.7) and deployed at least once every six years (8.9.8)</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Evacuation time (4.8.1):</b> the craft is arranged so that evacuation time = <b>(SFP − 7) / 3 minutes</b>, where SFP is the structural fire protection time from 7.4.2 for areas of major fire hazard, and 7 minutes is deducted for initial detection and extinguishing. Worked example: the SFP times are based on protection for 60 minutes, so (60 − 7) / 3 is about 17.7 minutes.</p><h2>Amendments to the 2000 Code</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.175(79)</td><td>10 Dec 2004</td><td>1 Jul 2006</td><td>2.2.1; Annex 1</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.222(82)</td><td>8 Dec 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2008</td><td>Largest amendment: Chapters 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 13, 14, 18 and Annexes 1, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; new Annex 12</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.260(84)</td><td>16 May 2008</td><td>1 Jan 2010</td><td>8.2.1.2; 14.7.1.3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.271(85)</td><td>4 Dec 2008</td><td>1 Jan 2011</td><td>7.17.1 and tables 7.17-1 and 7.17-3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.326(90)</td><td>24 May 2012</td><td>1 Jan 2014</td><td>14.15.10</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.352(92)</td><td>21 Jun 2013</td><td>1 Jan 2015</td><td>18.5.4, 18.5.8, 18.5.12</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.424(98)</td><td>15 Jun 2017</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>8.10</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.439(99)</td><td>24 May 2018</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Chapter 14 and Annex 1</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Operational requirements (Chapter 18)</h2><ul><li><b>Part A (general):</b> craft operational control · craft documentation · training and qualifications · manning of survival craft and supervision · emergency instructions and drills</li><li><b>Part B (passenger craft):</b> type rating training (18.6) · emergency instructions and drills (18.7)</li><li><b>Part C (cargo craft):</b> type rating training (18.8)</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The craft must hold both a valid HSC Safety Certificate and a Permit to Operate, and stay within the Permit's limits. Crew need training for the craft type (type rating), not only general certificates. Evacuation time is set by (SFP − 7) / 3 minutes.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.97(73), 2000 HSC Code text (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.97(73).pdf</li><li>HSC Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/x/hsc-code-2000-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>H. Hoppe (IMO), overview of the DSC and HSC Codes, FAST 2005: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/International.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ibc-code","v":218231681,"title":"IBC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ibc","chemical-tanker","nls","marpol-ii","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>IBC Code — International Code for the Construction and Equipment of Ships Carrying Dangerous Chemicals in Bulk</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>17 Jun 1983, resolution MSC.4(48)</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory under</td><td>SOLAS chapter VII (MSC.6(48)) and MARPOL Annex II (MEPC.19(22), Dec 1985)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Chemical tankers of any size, keels laid on or after <b>1 Jul 1986</b></td></tr><tr><td>Older ships</td><td>Chemical tankers built before 1 Jul 1986 follow the predecessor <b>BCH Code</b> (MARPOL Annex II regulation 11)</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Certificate of Fitness for the Carriage of Dangerous Chemicals in Bulk, up to 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>21 chapters and an appendix (certificate model)</td></tr><tr><td>Current text</td><td>2020 edition, with amendments in force 1 Jan 2021 and 1 Jul 2024</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the IBC Code</h2><ul><li>An international standard for the <b>safe bulk carriage of dangerous and noxious liquid chemicals</b> listed in Chapter 17</li><li>Covers ship design, construction, equipment and operation, to minimize risk to the ship, crew and environment</li><li>SOLAS covers products marked S or S/P in column d of Chapter 17; MARPOL covers NLS tankers carrying products marked X, Y or Z in column c</li></ul><h2>History</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ibc-dates\"><figcaption>From adoption in 1983 to the 2024 amendment</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>2004 amendments (MSC.176(79)):</b> adopted 10 Dec 2004, deemed accepted 1 Jul 2006, in force <b>1 Jan 2007</b>. They go with the revised MARPOL Annex II (in force 1 Jan 2007), which brought a four-category system for noxious liquid substances (X, Y, Z, OS).</li><li><b>MEPC.318(74) and MSC.460(101):</b> in force 1 Jan 2021; amend chapters 1, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19 and 21, with a complete revision of the product information in chapters 17 and 18.</li><li><b>MEPC.302(72) and MSC.440(99):</b> new model form of the Certificate of Fitness, in force 1 Jan 2020.</li><li><b>MEPC.345(78) and MSC.526(106):</b> adopted 10 Jun 2022 and 10 Nov 2022; in force <b>1 Jul 2024</b>; replace paragraph 2.9.2.1 on the final waterline in damage survival.</li></ul><h2>Application and definitions (Chapter 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Size</td><td>All ships regardless of size, including under 500 GT</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo</td><td>Bulk dangerous chemicals and noxious liquid substances, excluding petroleum and similar flammable products, where the cargo has greater fire hazards than petroleum products or other significant hazards</td></tr><tr><td>Vapour pressure</td><td>Covered liquids do not exceed 0.28 MPa absolute at 37.8 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Construction date</td><td>Keels laid, or similar stage, on or after 1 Jul 1986; the stage is when identifiable construction begins and assembly reaches <b>at least 50 tonnes or 1%</b> of the estimated structural mass, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Conversions</td><td>A ship of any age converted to a chemical tanker on or after 1 Jul 1986 counts as constructed when the conversion begins</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Chemical tanker</td><td>A cargo ship built or adapted to carry in bulk any liquid product listed in Chapter 17</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo area</td><td>Cargo tanks, slop tanks, cargo pump-rooms, cofferdams, adjacent ballast or void spaces, and the deck areas above them</td></tr><tr><td>Cofferdam</td><td>An isolating space between two adjacent steel bulkheads or decks</td></tr><tr><td>Noxious liquid substance (NLS)</td><td>A substance listed in the Pollution Category column of Chapter 17 or 18, or in the current MEPC circular, or provisionally assessed as category X, Y or Z</td></tr><tr><td>Products</td><td>The collective term for NLS and dangerous chemicals</td></tr><tr><td>Length L</td><td>96% of the waterline length at 85% of the least moulded depth</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Certificate and surveys (Chapter 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Issued after an initial or renewal survey; valid for a period set by the Administration, <b>not more than 5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>Renewal</td><td>Completed within 3 months before expiry: valid up to 5 years from the existing expiry date. Completed after expiry: same. More than 3 months before expiry: up to 5 years from the survey completion</td></tr><tr><td>Extension</td><td>An existing certificate may be endorsed for up to 5 further months</td></tr><tr><td>Invalid if</td><td>Surveys or endorsements are missed, or the ship changes flag</td></tr><tr><td>Initial</td><td>Before service, a full examination for compliance</td></tr><tr><td>Renewal survey</td><td>At intervals set by the Administration, not more than 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate</td><td>Within 3 months before or after the 2nd or 3rd anniversary; replaces one annual survey</td></tr><tr><td>Annual</td><td>Within 3 months before or after each anniversary</td></tr><tr><td>Additional</td><td>After an investigation or important repairs or renewals</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Ship types and cargo tank location (Chapter 2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ibc-ship-types\"><figcaption>Where cargo tanks may be placed for each ship type</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Hazard</th><th>Tank location (2.6.1)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>Most severe hazards; maximum preventive measures; survives the most severe damage</td><td>Side and bottom setbacks equal to the damage extents, and at least <b>760 mm</b> from the shell</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>Appreciably severe hazards; significant preventive measures</td><td>Bottom setback equal to the vertical damage extent, and at least 760 mm from the shell</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>Sufficiently severe hazards; moderate containment</td><td>No requirement</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Diluted slops tanks from tank washing are exempt from the 760 mm rule.</li></ul><p><b>Damage extents (2.5.1)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Damage</th><th>Extent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Side, transverse</td><td>B/5 or 11.5 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Side, longitudinal</td><td>1/3 L^(2/3) or 14.5 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, vertical</td><td>B/15 or 6 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, forward of 0.3L</td><td>Longitudinal 1/3 L^(2/3) or 14.5 m; transverse B/6 or 10 m</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, elsewhere</td><td>Longitudinal 1/3 L^(2/3) or 5 m; transverse B/6 or 5 m</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Other Chapter 2 figures</b></p><ul><li>Suction wells (non-Type 1 ships) may protrude into bottom damage zones by no more than 25% of the double-bottom depth or 350 mm, whichever is less.</li><li>Permeability: stores 0.60, accommodation 0.95, machinery 0.85, voids 0.95.</li><li>Damage standard: Type 1 ships survive damage anywhere along their length; Type 2 and 3 requirements depend on length (thresholds at 150 m, 125 m and 225 m).</li><li>Final equilibrium (2.9): heel not more than 25° (30° if no deck is immersed); righting lever range at least 20° beyond equilibrium, residual righting lever at least 0.1 m, area at least 0.0175 m·rad.</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ship survival capability and location of cargo tanks</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ship arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Cargo containment</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Cargo transfer</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Materials of construction, protective linings and coatings</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Cargo temperature control</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Cargo tank venting and gas-freeing arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Environmental control</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Electrical installations</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Fire protection and fire extinction</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Mechanical ventilation in the cargo area</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Instrumentation</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Personnel protection</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Special requirements</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Operational requirements</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Summary of minimum requirements (the product table)</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Products reviewed and found not to warrant inclusion in the Code (pollution only)</td></tr><tr><td>19 to 21</td><td>Further chapters (19 and 21 amended in 2021)</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Figures stated in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11</td><td>Cargo-area deck foam: solution rate at least <b>2 l/min per m²</b> (11.3.5.1); at least <b>four</b> foam applicators (11.3.10)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Pump-rooms normally entered: at least <b>20 air changes per hour</b> (12.2). Cargo spaces generally at least 30 changes per hour (12.1.3); 45 for certain products (15.17)</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Cargo tank gauging is open, restricted or closed (13.1.1); overflow control in 15.19</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Protective equipment includes aprons, long-sleeved gloves, footwear, chemical-resistant coveralls, goggles or face shields (14.1.1); ships carrying certain cargoes need at least <b>three</b> complete sets of safety equipment (14.2.1)</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Applies where column o of Chapter 17 refers to it; molten sulphur temperature limit 155 °C (15.10.6)</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Maximum cargo per tank: <b>1,250 m³</b> on Type 1 ships (16.1.1), <b>3,000 m³</b> on Type 2 ships (16.1.2); tanks with liquids at ambient temperature must not become liquid-full during the voyage (16.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>The product table: pollution category (column c), safety hazards (column d), ship type, and further requirements by column</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Before loading, check the product against Chapter 17 and the ship's Certificate of Fitness: ship type, tank requirements and special requirements. Type 1 and Type 2 tanks must stay at least 760 mm from the shell. Pump-rooms normally entered need at least 20 air changes per hour.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.4(48) (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.4(48).pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.176(79), 2004 IBC Code text (MLIT Japan copy): https://www.mlit.go.jp/kaiji/naikouchemical/dai1kai/sanko3e.pdf</li><li>IMO — IBC Code: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/IBCCode.aspx</li><li>IMO publication flyer, IBC Code 2020 edition: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/publications/Documents/Flyers/Flyers/IE100E.pdf</li><li>IMO supplement July 2024, MEPC.345(78) and MSC.526(106): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/publications/Documents/Supplements/English/QE100E_supplement_July2024_PQ.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-igc-code","v":14654869,"title":"IGC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["igc","gas-carrier","lng","lpg","ammonia","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>IGC Code — International Code for the Construction and Equipment of Ships Carrying Liquefied Gases in Bulk</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>17 Jun 1983, resolution MSC.5(48); mandatory under SOLAS chapter VII (MSC.6(48))</td></tr><tr><td>Original Code</td><td>Ships built on or after <b>1 Jul 1986</b></td></tr><tr><td>Revised Code</td><td>MSC.370(93), adopted 22 May 2014; in force <b>1 Jan 2016</b>; applies to keels laid on or after <b>1 Jul 2016</b></td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Ships of any size, including under 500 GT, carrying liquefied gases with vapour pressure above <b>0.28 MPa absolute at 37.8 °C</b>, and other products listed in chapter 19</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Certificate of Fitness for the Carriage of Liquefied Gases in Bulk, up to 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>19 chapters and 5 appendices</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which version applies</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ship constructed</th><th>Requirements</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Jul 1986 to before 1 Jul 2016</td><td>The earlier IGC Code, as amended by the resolutions listed in the revised Code</td></tr><tr><td>On or after 1 Jul 2016</td><td>The revised IGC Code (MSC.370(93))</td></tr><tr><td>Converted to a gas carrier on or after 1 Jul 2016</td><td>Counts as constructed when the conversion begins</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>\"Keel laid or similar stage\" means identifiable construction begins and assembly reaches at least <b>50 tonnes or 1%</b> of the estimated structural mass, whichever is less.</li></ul><h2>History and amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"igc-dates\"><figcaption>Key dates from 1983 to 2026</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Subject</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.5(48)</td><td>17 Jun 1983</td><td>1 Jul 1986</td><td>Original IGC Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.17(58)</td><td>24 May 1990</td><td>3 Feb 2000</td><td>Paragraphs 1.3, 1.5; certificate model</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.30(61)</td><td>11 Dec 1992</td><td>1 Jul 1994</td><td>Many paragraphs in chapters 1–19; Tables 6.1, 6.2</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.32(63)</td><td>23 May 1994</td><td>1 Jul 1998</td><td>1.1.2, 1.1.3, chapter 15, parts of 8.2</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.59(67)</td><td>5 Dec 1996</td><td>1 Jul 1998</td><td>Numerous paragraphs</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.103(73)</td><td>5 Dec 2000</td><td>1 Jul 2002</td><td>Several paragraphs, including 3.7, 5.6, 5.7, 18.9</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.177(79)</td><td>10 Dec 2004</td><td>1 Jul 2006</td><td>3.6.4, 10.1.4, 10.2 deleted, certificate</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.220(82)</td><td>8 Dec 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2008</td><td>Chapters 1, 3, 11, 12, 19; adds carbon dioxide and dimethyl ether to chapter 19</td></tr><tr><td><b>MSC.370(93)</b></td><td>22 May 2014</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td><td>Complete revision of chapters 1–19 and appendices 1–5</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.411(97)</td><td>25 Nov 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Paragraph 3.2.5 (windows and sidescuttles)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.441(99)</td><td>24 May 2018</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Appendix 2: new paragraph 6, loading and stability information booklet</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.476(102)</td><td>11 Nov 2020</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Paragraph 6.5.3.5.1 (welding procedure tests: cross-weld tensile strength)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.492(104)</td><td>8 Oct 2021</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Paragraph 2.7.1.1 (waterline below openings allowing progressive flooding)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.523(106)</td><td>10 Nov 2022</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>Table 6.3 replaced (cargo tank materials, -55 °C to -165 °C, including high-manganese austenitic steel)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.566(109)</td><td>6 Dec 2024</td><td>1 Jul 2026</td><td>Paragraph 16.9.2 replaced (see below)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The revised Code was the first major overhaul since 1983, after a five-year review.</li><li>Corrections to MSC.370(93) were issued in 2015 and 2016 (for example paragraphs 5.9.3.1, 5.11.6.3 and 13.6.4).</li></ul><p><b>MSC.566(109): paragraph 16.9.2</b></p><ul><li>Cargoes that must be carried in <b>type 1G</b> ships (column \"c\" of chapter 19) are not allowed as fuel.</li><li>Toxic products in column \"f\" that must be carried in <b>type 2G/2PG</b> ships may be used as fuel only if the Administration accepts it, the same level of safety as natural gas (methane) is ensured, and the Administration has given special consideration, taking IMO guidelines into account.</li></ul><p><b>MSC 111 (13–22 May 2026)</b></p><ul><li>Approved draft amendments to the IGC Code (annex 20 of the report), for adoption at MSC 112, with entry into force on <b>1 Jul 2028</b>. They follow the \"one ship, one code\" policy for gas carriers.</li><li>Approved MSC.1/Circ.1702, Interim Guidelines for use of ammonia cargo as fuel.</li><li>These amendments are <b>not yet adopted</b>; treat them as pending.</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ship survival capability and location of cargo tanks</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ship arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Cargo containment</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Process pressure vessels and liquid, vapour and pressure piping systems</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Materials of construction and quality control</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Cargo pressure/temperature control</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Vent systems for cargo containment</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Cargo containment system atmosphere control</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Electrical installations</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Fire protection and extinction</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Artificial ventilation in the cargo area</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Instrumentation and automation systems</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Personnel protection</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Filling limits for cargo tanks</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Use of cargo as fuel</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Special requirements</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Operating requirements</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Summary of minimum requirements</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Appendices: 1 product data reporting form; 2 model Certificate of Fitness; 3 example of an addendum to the certificate; 4 non-metallic materials; 5 standard for limit state methodologies for novel cargo containment systems.</p><h2>Certificate and surveys (Chapter 1)</h2><ul><li>Certificate valid for a period set by the Administration, not more than <b>5 years</b>.</li><li>Renewal surveys at intervals not over 5 years; annual surveys within 3 months before or after each anniversary; intermediate survey within 3 months before or after the 2nd or 3rd anniversary.</li><li>If renewal is completed within 3 months before expiry, the new certificate runs up to 5 years from the old expiry date.</li><li>The certificate stops being valid if surveys are not completed on time, endorsements are missing, or the ship changes flag.</li></ul><h2>Ship types and tank location (Chapter 2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"igc-ship-types\"><figcaption>Ship types and where cargo tanks may sit</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>For products needing</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1G</td><td>Maximum preventive measures</td><td>Survives the most severe damage standard; tanks at maximum distance inboard</td></tr><tr><td>2G</td><td>Significant preventive measures</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2PG</td><td>Significant preventive measures</td><td>Ship 150 m or less; type C tanks; MARVS at least 0.7 MPa gauge; design temperature -55 °C or above. Over 150 m it is treated as 2G</td></tr><tr><td>3G</td><td>Moderate preventive measures</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The required ship type for each product is in column \"c\" of the chapter 19 table.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Damage assumption</th><th>Extent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Side, longitudinal</td><td>1/3 L^(2/3) or 14.5 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Side, transverse</td><td>B/5 or 11.5 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Side, vertical</td><td>Upwards from the shell, without limit</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, transverse</td><td>B/6 or 10 m (forward 0.3L); B/6 or 5 m (elsewhere)</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, vertical</td><td>B/15 or 2 m, whichever is less</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Tank location (2.4.1)</b></p><ul><li>Type 1G: at least the transverse side-damage extent from the shell, and the vertical bottom-damage extent from the bottom; never less than d.</li><li>Types 2G/2PG: at least the vertical bottom-damage extent from the bottom; never less than d.</li><li>Type 3G: at least the vertical bottom-damage extent from the bottom; at least 0.8 m from the shell.</li><li>d depends on tank volume Vc: 0.8 m for up to 1,000 m³; 2 m for 30,000 m³ or more.</li><li>Cargo tanks must not be forward of the collision bulkhead.</li></ul><p><b>Survival (2.7)</b></p><ul><li>Waterline must stay below the lower edge of openings that could cause progressive flooding or downflooding.</li><li>Maximum heel from unsymmetrical flooding: 30°.</li><li>At final equilibrium: righting lever range at least 20° beyond equilibrium, residual righting lever at least 0.1 m, area at least 0.0175 m·rad.</li></ul><h2>Ship arrangements (Chapter 3)</h2><ul><li>Hold spaces are segregated from machinery, accommodation, service spaces and stores.</li><li>No accommodation, service spaces or control stations in the cargo area.</li><li>Entrances, air inlets and openings must not face the cargo area. Where on the end bulkhead facing it, they must be at least 4% of L and at least 3 m from the superstructure end (need not exceed 5 m).</li><li>Windows facing the cargo area in that zone are fixed and A-60 class.</li><li>Airlock between hazardous weather deck and non-hazardous spaces: two self-closing, substantially gastight steel doors, <b>1.5 to 2.5 m apart</b>, held at overpressure.</li></ul><h2>Cargo containment (Chapter 4)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"igc-tank-types\"><figcaption>Containment types and design vapour pressure Po</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Secondary barrier: not required if the cargo is at or above <b>-10 °C</b> at atmospheric pressure (4.4.5). Hull structure may act as the secondary barrier where cargo temperature is not below -55 °C (4.6.1). A secondary barrier must contain leakage for <b>15 days</b>, unless other criteria apply for particular voyages (4.6.2.1).</li><li>Membranes normally do not exceed 10 mm thickness.</li></ul><h2>Piping, materials and other chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Figures stated in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>Water curtain for cargo temperatures below -110 °C (5.7.3). Butt welds double welded or equivalent below -10 °C (5.8.2.1). 100% radiographic or ultrasonic inspection below -10 °C, inside diameter above 75 mm, or wall above 10 mm (5.9.3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Table 6.3 materials down to -165 °C: for example 9% nickel steel or austenitic steel (impact test -196 °C); aluminium alloy 5083 (no impact test). Table 6.3 was replaced by MSC.523(106) from 1 Jan 2026</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Fire pumps: two jets from 19 mm nozzles at least 0.5 MPa (11.2.1). Water spray at least 10 l/m²/min on horizontal surfaces and 4 l/m²/min on vertical surfaces (11.3.2.1). Dry chemical: at least two independent units, or one for ships under 1,000 m³ (11.4.3); monitor at least 10 kg/s, hand hose at least 3.5 kg/s (11.4.4). Breathing apparatus at least 1,200 l of free air (11.6.3)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Continuous ventilation of hazardous spaces at least <b>30 air changes per hour</b> (12.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Each tank has a high-level alarm independent of other level indicators (13.3.1) and a separate sensor that automatically closes a valve to prevent the tank becoming liquid full (13.3.2). Gas alarm at <b>30% LFL</b> (13.6.15)</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>At least <b>three</b> complete safety-equipment sets, in addition to firefighter's outfits (14.3.1). Escape breathing apparatus at least 15 minutes (14.4.2.2)</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Default filling limit <b>98%</b> at the reference temperature (15.3); never above 99.5% (15.4.2)</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Methane (LNG) is the only cargo vapour or boil-off allowed in category A machinery spaces unless 16.9 applies (16.1). Gas fuel alarm at 30% LFL; master valve shutdown at no more than 60% LFL (16.4.8)</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Cargo data sheets on board (18.3); compatibility care (18.4); training in protective equipment (18.7); emergency shutdown and alarm systems checked and tested before cargo operations (18.10.5); liquid-line ESD valves fully close within <b>30 s</b> (18.10.2.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Product table: a = product, c ship type, d type C tank required, e vapour space control, f vapour detection, g gauging, i special requirements</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The cargo must appear on the ship's Certificate of Fitness. Check its row in chapter 19: ship type, tank type and special requirements. Test emergency shutdown before cargo operations, keep the filling limit at 98% unless a higher limit is approved, and remember that type 1G cargoes can never be used as fuel.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Chapter 8 vent system figures, chapter 17 product-specific rules, and the type C secondary barrier row of Table 4.5 (the table could not be read reliably).</li><li>Details of the pre-1986 gas carrier codes and the content of MSC.1/Circ.1681 and Circ.1703.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.5(48): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.5(48).pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.370(93), revised IGC Code text (law.resource.org copy): https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.msc.370.2014.pdf</li><li>IMO supplement QA104E (MSC.411(97), MSC.441(99)): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/publications/Documents/Supplements/English/QA104E_122019.pdf</li><li>Deutsche Flagge, IGC Code amendment status: https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vii/igc-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.476(102): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vii/msc-476-102.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.492(104): https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/file/MSC.492%28104%29.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.523(106): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.523%28106%29.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.566(109): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution MSC.566(109).pdf</li><li>Report of MSC 111 (MPA Singapore copy): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/api/media/e783b0c8-e1f3-44bf-8462-d2357ff91654/MSC-111-22---Report-Of-The-Maritime-Safety-Committee-On-Its-111Th-Session-(Secretariat).pdf</li><li>IMO MSC 93 summary: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/MeetingSummaries/Pages/MSC-93.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-igf-code","v":122457803,"title":"IGF Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["igf","lng-fuel","alternative-fuels","solas-ii-1","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>IGF Code — International Code of Safety for Ships Using Gases or other Low-flashpoint Fuels</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>11 Jun 2015, resolution MSC.391(95), at MSC 95</td></tr><tr><td>Takes effect</td><td><b>1 Jan 2017</b>, with SOLAS amendments (chapters II-1 and II-2) adopted by MSC.392(95)</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory under</td><td>SOLAS chapter II-1, <b>Part G</b> (added by MSC.392(95))</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Ships to which SOLAS II-1 Part G applies, unless expressly provided otherwise</td></tr><tr><td>Not for</td><td>Gas carriers covered by the IGC Code (the Code covers low-flashpoint fuel ships other than those)</td></tr><tr><td>Approach</td><td>Goal-based: goals and functional requirements for each section</td></tr><tr><td>Detailed rules</td><td>Part A-1 covers <b>natural gas (LNG)</b>; other fuels need other means (alternative design, interim guidelines)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>19 chapters in Parts A, A-1, B-1, C-1 and D</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What it covers</h2><ul><li>Mandatory provisions for the arrangement, installation, control and monitoring of machinery, equipment and systems that use gas or other low-flashpoint fuels</li><li>Aims to reduce risks to the ship, the crew and the environment</li><li><b>Low-flashpoint fuel</b> (2.2): a gaseous or liquid fuel with a flashpoint lower than otherwise permitted under SOLAS II-2/4, paragraph 2.1.1</li></ul><h2>History and amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"igf-dates\"><figcaption>From adoption in 2015 to pending 2028 changes</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Subject</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.391(95)</td><td>11 Jun 2015</td><td>1 Jan 2017</td><td>Adopts the Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.422(98)</td><td>15 Jun 2017</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Part A-1, paragraph 11.3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.458(101)</td><td>14 Jun 2019</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Paragraphs 2.2, 5.3, 6.8, 9.5, 10.3, 11.3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.475(102)</td><td>11 Nov 2020</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Paragraphs 6.7, 11.8 and 16.3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.551(108)</td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.567(109)</td><td>6 Dec 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2028 (deemed accepted 1 Jul 2027)</td><td>See below</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>MSC.524(106) (adopted at MSC 106) concerns high-manganese austenitic steel for cryogenic service.</li></ul><p><b>MSC.551(108): main changes (in force 1 Jan 2026)</b></p><ul><li>New definition 2.2.43 of a ship \"constructed on or after 1 January 2026\" (building contract, keel laying or delivery date)</li><li>Airlock rules (5.12.1): gastight bulkheads, two doors <b>1.5 to 2.5 m</b> apart, 300 mm sill (unless the Load Line Convention applies), self-closing doors without holdback</li><li>Residual pressure relief valves (6.7.3.1): capacity to cover one valve failing; pressure rise limited to <b>20% above MARVS</b></li><li>Bunkering (8.4): <b>dry-disconnect</b> connections; manual, hydraulic or bolted connections need a risk assessment and special consideration; emergency release coupler or equivalent</li><li>Fuel supply (9.3, 9.4): redundancy and segregation in single-fuel systems; automatic venting of gas piping after shutdown; manual shutdown valve for each gas consumer</li><li>Fire safety (11.3.1, 11.6.2): fuel preparation rooms treated as <b>category A machinery spaces</b>; a <b>5 kg</b> dry powder extinguisher near the bunkering station and in the fuel preparation room (existing ships by first survey on or after 1 Jan 2026)</li><li>Hazardous zones 0 and 1 expanded (12.5); permitted liquid level gauge types for fuel tanks (15.4.1.3)</li><li>Production weld tests about every 50 m of butt weld (16.3.5.1)</li><li>Bunkering (18.4.1.1): the receiving ship's master and the bunkering source's person in charge <b>agree in writing</b> on transfer procedures and limits</li></ul><p><b>MSC.567(109): main changes (in force 1 Jan 2028)</b></p><ul><li>New definition 2.2.44 of a ship \"constructed on or after 1 January 2028\"</li><li>Fuel tank suction wells may protrude below the inner bottom within limits (5.3.3.5.1)</li><li>Pressure relief valve discharge to fuel tanks, or to the vent mast with liquid detection (7.3.1.4)</li><li>A-60 shielding for boundaries facing open-deck fuel tanks (11.3.2); fuel storage hold space may count as a cofferdam with a minimum <b>900 mm</b> distance (11.3.3.1)</li><li>Zone 1 and zone 2 around vent mast outlets (12.5.2.4, 12.5.3.3); ventilation air inlet rules (13.3.5, 13.3.8)</li></ul><p><b>Pending: MSC 111 (13–22 May 2026)</b></p><ul><li>Draft amendments (annex 19 of the report) clarify the Code covers \"gases or other low-flashpoint fuels\", revise the definitions of \"gas\" and \"low-flashpoint fuel\", and update risk assessment (4.2.1) and training (19.2) paragraphs. They follow the \"one ship, one code\" policy and respond to doubt over whether ammonia fell in scope.</li><li>Expected adoption at MSC 112, with deemed acceptance [1 Jan 2028] and entry into force [1 Jul 2028] — dates were still in brackets.</li><li>MSC 111 also approved hydrogen guidelines MSC.1/Circ.1701.</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"igf-structure\"><figcaption>Parts and chapters of the Code</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Chapters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A: General</td><td>2 General (application, definitions, alternative design); 3 Goal and functional requirements; 4 General requirements</td></tr><tr><td>A-1: Natural gas fuel</td><td>5 Ship design and arrangement; 6 Fuel containment system; 7 Material and general pipe design; 8 Bunkering; 9 Fuel supply to consumers; 10 Power generation including propulsion and other gas consumers; 11 Fire safety; 12 Explosion prevention; 13 Ventilation; 14 Electrical installations; 15 Control, monitoring and safety systems</td></tr><tr><td>B-1</td><td>16 Manufacture, workmanship and testing</td></tr><tr><td>C-1</td><td>17 Drills and emergency exercises; 18 Operation</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>19 Training</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Key requirements for natural gas fuel</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>Fuel tanks at least <b>B/5 or 11.5 m</b> (whichever is less) inboard from the ship's side at the summer load line (5.3.3.1). Fuel pipes at least 800 mm from the ship's side (5.7.1)</td></tr><tr><td>5.4</td><td><b>Gas-safe machinery space:</b> arranged so it is gas safe in normal and abnormal conditions; a single failure cannot release fuel gas into it. <b>ESD-protected machinery space:</b> non-hazardous in normal conditions, but may become hazardous in abnormal conditions, when non-safe equipment is shut down automatically</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Secondary barrier contains leakage for <b>15 days</b> unless other criteria apply for particular voyages (6.4.4.1). Filling limit <b>98%</b> at reference temperature (6.8.1); a higher limit, never above 95%, may be allowed in special cases (6.8.2). Tank types: independent A, B, C and membrane</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Bunkering station on open deck (8.3.1.1). Dry-disconnect connection plus a dry break-away or self-sealing quick release coupling (8.4.1). Manual stop valve and remote shutdown valve in series in each bunkering line (8.5.3). Hose bursting pressure at least 5 times the maximum bunkering pressure (8.3.2.2)</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Main gas supply line has a manual stop valve and an automatic <b>master gas fuel valve</b>, outside the machinery space and operable from safe places including the bridge (9.4.2, 9.4.3). Ducts or double-wall pipes: underpressure ventilation of 30 air changes per hour (9.5.1), reducible to 10 with automatic nitrogen filling on gas detection (9.6.1.2)</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Water spray: <b>10 l/min/m²</b> on largest horizontal surfaces and 4 l/min/m² on vertical surfaces (11.5.3); main supply stop valves no more than 40 m apart (11.5.4). Dry powder at the bunkering station at least <b>3.5 kg/s for 45 s</b> (11.6.1). Smoke detectors alone are not enough for rapid fire detection (11.7.2)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><b>Zone 0:</b> inside fuel tanks, relief and vent pipework, and pipes containing fuel. <b>Zone 1:</b> includes tank connection spaces, fuel storage hold spaces, interbarrier spaces; on open deck 3 m from tank outlets and bunker manifold valves, 1.5 m from fuel preparation room entrances. <b>Zone 2:</b> 1.5 m around zone 1 open or semi-enclosed spaces (12.5)</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><b>30 air changes per hour</b> for tank connection space extraction (13.4.1), fuel preparation rooms (13.6.1), ducts and double pipes (13.8.1). ESD-protected machinery spaces: 30, or 15 in normal operation rising automatically to 30 on gas detection (13.5.2)</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Redundant gas detection in each ESD-protected machinery space (15.8.2)</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>A suitably detailed <b>fuel handling manual</b> on board (18.2.3); fuel system P&amp;ID mounted at the bunker control station and bunker station (18.4.2.2); pre-bunkering verification recorded in the bunker safety checklist (18.4.3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Seafarers complete training matching their duties, taking into account the STCW Convention and Code (19.2)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"igf-gas-detection\"><figcaption>Gas detection alarm and shutdown set points</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Check the ship's construction date against the 2026 and 2028 definitions, because the detailed rules changed. Bunkering needs a dry-disconnect connection, a written agreement on transfer limits and a completed bunker safety checklist. Gas detection alarms at 20% LEL and the safety system acts at 40% LEL on two detectors.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Numeric flashpoint limit in the definition, and the alternative-design route under SOLAS II-1/55.</li><li>Entry-into-force date for MSC.524(106).</li><li>Interim guidelines for methyl/ethyl alcohol, fuel cells, low-flashpoint diesel and ammonia: circular numbers and dates (only hydrogen MSC.1/Circ.1701 is confirmed as approved at MSC 111).</li><li>Details of STCW Basic and Advanced training under chapter 19.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.391(95), IGF Code text: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.391(95).pdf</li><li>Deutsche Flagge, IGF Code amendment status: https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/ii-1/igf-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.551(108): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution MSC.551(108).pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.567(109): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution MSC.567(109).pdf</li><li>IMO press briefing on the IGF Code: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/PressBriefings/Pages/01-IGF.aspx</li><li>IMO press briefing, rules in force 1 Jan 2026: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>BSMOU Notice to PSCOs No. 15 on the IGF Code: https://bsmou.org/2022/05/notice-to-pscos-no-15-guidance-for-psco-on-the-igf-code/</li><li>Report of MSC 111, add. 2 (MPA Singapore copy): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/api/media/3679142b-70fa-407b-a8fb-6eebd86be579/MSC-111-22-Add.2---Report-Of-The-Maritime-Safety-Committee-On-Its-111Th-Session-(Secretariat).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-iii-code","v":249000894,"title":"III Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["iii-code","flag-state","port-state","imsas","audit","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>III Code — IMO Instruments Implementation Code</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>4 Dec 2013, Assembly resolution A.1070(28), at the 28th Assembly</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>A.1054(27), Code for the Implementation of Mandatory IMO Instruments, 2011</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory</td><td>From <b>1 Jan 2016</b>, through amendments to nine instruments</td></tr><tr><td>Who it is for</td><td><b>Governments</b> (Contracting Governments acting as flag, coastal and port States), not ships or companies directly</td></tr><tr><td>Role in audits</td><td>The <b>audit standard</b> of the IMO Member State Audit Scheme (IMSAS)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>4 parts, 63 numbered paragraphs, no chapters; no definitions section</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What it is</h2><ul><li>A code for how States implement and enforce IMO instruments.</li><li><b>Objective (para 1):</b> enhance global maritime safety and protection of the marine environment, and help States implement IMO instruments.</li><li><b>Scope (para 6):</b> safety of life at sea, pollution prevention, seafarer training and certification, load lines, tonnage measurement and collision regulations.</li><li>A State is bound only for the instruments to which it is a Contracting Government or Party (para 2).</li><li>Coastal and port State requirements apply to the extent that ships subject to IMO mandatory instruments can reach the State's ports.</li></ul><h2>History</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iii-dates\"><figcaption>From the voluntary scheme to the 2025 framework</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Voluntary scheme (VIMSAS):</b> A.946(23) (Nov 2003), framework A.974(24) (Nov 2005). Voluntary audits ran from Sep 2006 to Dec 2015, covering 67 Member States, 2 Associate Members and 5 dependent territories.</li><li><b>First code:</b> A.973(24), Nov 2005; then the 2011 Code, A.1054(27); then the III Code, A.1070(28), Nov 2013.</li><li><b>A.1018(26) (Nov 2009):</b> endorsed a phased introduction of the audit scheme as a mandatory process.</li><li><b>A.1068(28):</b> transitional arrangements for voluntary audits from Dec 2013 to Dec 2015, using the III Code as the standard.</li></ul><p><b>Made mandatory (in force 1 Jan 2016)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Amending resolution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SOLAS 1974 (new chapter XIII, Verification of compliance)</td><td>MSC.366(93), adopted 22 May 2014</td></tr><tr><td>STCW Convention and STCW Code</td><td>MSC.373(93), MSC.374(93)</td></tr><tr><td>Load Lines Protocol 1988</td><td>MSC.375(93)</td></tr><tr><td>MARPOL Annexes I–V</td><td>MEPC.246(66), adopted 4 Apr 2014 (and MEPC.247(66))</td></tr><tr><td>COLREG 1972, Load Lines 1966, Tonnage 1969</td><td>A.1083(28), A.1084(28), A.1085(28)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Each instrument says Contracting Governments <b>shall use the III Code</b> in meeting their obligations and are subject to periodic IMO audits.</li><li>Every \"should\" in the III Code is read as \"shall\", except in paragraphs 29 to 32.</li><li>In SOLAS chapter XIII the Secretary-General administers the Audit Scheme; each Government must facilitate the audit and carry out a programme of actions to address the findings (regulation 3).</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iii-structure\"><figcaption>Parts and paragraph ranges</figcaption></figure><p><b>Part 1: Common areas (paras 1–14)</b></p><ul><li>Strategy (3): develop an overall strategy for flag, port and coastal State obligations, monitor and review it.</li><li>Laws (4–5): promulgate laws giving the instruments full effect; pollution control must not shift damage or hazards from one area to another.</li><li>Initial actions (8): when an instrument enters into force, the State must have legal authority, enforcement processes and enough maritime personnel.</li><li>Records (10): legible, retrievable records under a documented control procedure.</li><li>Improvement (11–14): continual improvement, training, drills, incentives, and corrective and preventive action for non-conformities.</li></ul><p><b>Part 2: Flag States (paras 15–44)</b></p><ul><li>Implementation (15–17): national legislation and guidance; a safety and environmental protection programme; ships sufficiently and efficiently manned.</li><li>Delegation (18–21): recognized organizations act under a formal written agreement; the flag State must oversee them and must not require them to apply more than convention requirements to ships of other flags (para 19).</li><li>Enforcement (22–27): periodic inspections, penalties and proceedings; a control and monitoring programme; oversee corrective measures after a port State detention; issue certificates only after requirements are met.</li><li>Surveyors (28–37): marine qualifications and experience, knowledge of ships and instruments, a documented qualification system, and an identification document.</li><li>Investigations (38–41): impartial and objective; casualties investigated under the Casualty Investigation Code.</li><li>Evaluation (42–44): performance indicators such as detention rates, casualty statistics and loss ratios.</li></ul><p><b>Part 3: Coastal States (paras 45–51)</b></p><ul><li>Services that may be covered (48): radiocommunication, search and rescue, hydrography, ship routeing, vessel traffic services and aids to navigation.</li><li>Enforcement (49–50): a control and monitoring programme, including statistical data, timely response to pollution incidents and cooperation in casualty investigations.</li></ul><p><b>Part 4: Port States (paras 52–63)</b></p><ul><li>Obligations may include reception facilities, port State control and a register of fuel oil suppliers (56).</li><li>Conditions apply to both Parties and non-Parties (59).</li><li>Port State control follows the Organization's procedures (60–61; the Code cites resolution A.1052(27), Procedures for Port State Control, 2011).</li><li>Port State control officers must be free from commercial pressures and must not be employed by recognized organizations or classification societies (62).</li></ul><h2>The audit scheme</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Rule (A.1211(34), adopted 3 Dec 2025)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>Framework A.1067(28), revoked from <b>30 Jun 2026</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cycle</td><td>Not more than <b>9 years</b>; Member States grouped by previous audit start; each group audited within 2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Notice</td><td>Projected audit date notified at least 18 months ahead</td></tr><tr><td>Follow-up</td><td>Generally 3 to 4 years after the audit</td></tr><tr><td>Type</td><td>Average score of 70% or more across three indicators (verification index, gap analysis, State audit questionnaire) qualifies for a limited on-site audit; others have a full on-site audit</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>The III Code</td></tr><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Interim report and audit records: audited State, audit team and Secretary-General only. Executive summary, corrective action plan, final report and follow-up reports go to all Member States; public release needs the State's authorization. A limited graphical summary of implementation levels is public</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The III Code is the standard governments are audited against. For a flag State, delegation to recognized organizations needs a written agreement and oversight, surveyors must be qualified, and casualty investigations follow the Casualty Investigation Code. For a port State, control follows the IMO procedures and applies to ships of Parties and non-Parties alike.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The old note's \"annex of mandatory obligations by instrument\": the Code has no such annex; its annex is the Code itself.</li><li>Procedures for Port State Control \"A.1185(33)\": the Code cites A.1052(27), which is what I confirmed.</li><li>Statements about MARINA, Paris and Tokyo MoU lists and PSC targeting.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution A.1070(28), III Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/fr/OurWork/MSAS/Documents/MSAS/Basic documents/A.1070(28) III Code.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.366(93), SOLAS chapter XIII: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/fr/OurWork/MSAS/Documents/MSAS/Amendments/MSC 366 93- amendments to SOLAS.pdf</li><li>Resolution MEPC.246(66), MARPOL: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/MSAS/Documents/MSAS/Amendments/MEPC.246(66)-Amendments to the annex of MARPOL Protocol of 1978.pdf</li><li>Resolution A.1211(34), audit framework and procedures: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1211%2834%29.pdf</li><li>IMO — Member State Audit Scheme: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/MSAS</li><li>IMO — Historic background: https://imo.org/fr/OurWork/MSAS/Pages/Historic-background.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-imdg-code","v":41230972,"title":"IMDG Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["imdg","dangerous-goods","packaged","solas-vii","marpol-iii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>IMDG Code — International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Safe carriage of dangerous goods in <b>packaged form</b> by sea; harmonize requirements; prevent pollution</td></tr><tr><td>First adopted</td><td>1965, as a recommendatory instrument</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory Code</td><td>Adopted 24 May 2002, MSC.122(75); SOLAS VII amended by MSC.123(75); voluntary from 1 Jan 2003; <b>mandatory from 1 Jan 2004</b></td></tr><tr><td>Legal basis</td><td>SOLAS chapter VII (carriage of dangerous goods) and MARPOL Annex III (harmful substances in packaged form)</td></tr><tr><td>Amendments</td><td>Every <b>two years</b>, adopted by MSC</td></tr><tr><td>Current mandatory edition</td><td>2024 edition, Amendment 42-24, mandatory <b>1 Jan 2026</b></td></tr><tr><td>Next</td><td>Amendment 43-26 (adopted MSC 111, May 2026), MSC.587(111), in force <b>1 Jan 2028</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What it covers</h2><ul><li>Packing, container traffic and stowage, including keeping incompatible substances apart</li><li>Detailed requirements for each individual substance, material or article</li><li>Some parts remain <b>recommendatory</b>: training paragraphs 1.3.1.4 to 1.3.1.7, security provisions in chapter 1.4 (except 1.4.1.1), and several sections including 7.8 and appendix B</li></ul><h2>History and amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"imdg-cycle\"><figcaption>The two-year amendment cycle</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The IMDG Code was recommended by a 1960 SOLAS conference; an IMO working group began drafting in 1961 and the 4th Assembly adopted it in 1965.</li><li>The mandatory Code supersedes the earlier version adopted by resolution A.716(17). Future amendments follow SOLAS article VIII.</li><li>Amendments come from Member Government proposals and from updates to the UN Recommendations on the Transport of Dangerous Goods. Amendments that do not change the Code's principles can be adopted by MSC alone.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>Voluntary from</th><th>Mandatory from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>41-22</td><td>MSC.501(105)</td><td>28 Apr 2022</td><td>1 Jan 2023</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td></tr><tr><td>42-24</td><td>MSC.556(108)</td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2025</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td></tr><tr><td>43-26</td><td>MSC.587(111)</td><td>MSC 111, 13–22 May 2026</td><td>Not confirmed</td><td>1 Jan 2028</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Structure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"imdg-structure\"><figcaption>Parts of the Code and the two volumes</figcaption></figure><p><b>Part 1: General provisions, definitions and training</b></p><ul><li>1.1 General provisions · 1.2 Definitions, units of measurement and abbreviations · 1.3 Training · 1.4 Security provisions</li></ul><p><b>Part 2: Classification</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Class</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2.1</td><td>Class 1: Explosives</td></tr><tr><td>2.2</td><td>Class 2: Gases</td></tr><tr><td>2.3</td><td>Class 3: Flammable liquids</td></tr><tr><td>2.4</td><td>Class 4: Flammable solids; substances liable to spontaneous combustion; substances which emit flammable gases in contact with water (divisions 4.1, 4.2, 4.3)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5</td><td>Class 5: Oxidizing substances (5.1) and organic peroxides (5.2)</td></tr><tr><td>2.6</td><td>Class 6: Toxic substances (6.1) and infectious substances (6.2)</td></tr><tr><td>2.7</td><td>Class 7: Radioactive material</td></tr><tr><td>2.8</td><td>Class 8: Corrosive substances</td></tr><tr><td>2.9</td><td>Class 9: Miscellaneous dangerous substances and articles</td></tr><tr><td>2.10</td><td>Marine pollutants</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Part 3: Dangerous Goods List (Volume 2)</b></p><ul><li>3.2 Dangerous Goods List, with special provisions, limited quantities and excepted quantities</li><li>Segregation column 16b of the list takes precedence over the segregation table (7.2.3.1)</li></ul><p><b>Part 4: Packing and tank provisions</b></p><ul><li>4.1 Packagings, IBCs and large packagings · 4.2 Portable tanks and MEGCs · 4.3 Bulk containers</li></ul><p><b>Part 5: Consignment procedures</b></p><ul><li>5.1 General · 5.2 Marking and labelling of packages including IBCs · 5.3 Placarding and marking of cargo transport units · 5.4 Documentation</li></ul><p><b>Part 6: Construction and testing</b></p><ul><li>6.1 Packagings · 6.2 Pressure receptacles · 6.3 Class 6.2 packagings · 6.4 Class 7 packages and material · 6.5 IBCs · 6.6 Large packagings · 6.7 Portable tanks and MEGCs · 6.8 Road tank vehicles · 6.9 Bulk containers</li></ul><p><b>Part 7: Transport operations</b></p><ul><li>7.1 Stowage · 7.2 Segregation · 7.3 Packing of cargo transport units · 7.4 Containerships · 7.5 Ro-ro ships · 7.6 General cargo ships · 7.7 Barge-carrying ships · 7.8 Special provisions for incidents and fire precautions · 7.9 Exemptions, approvals and certificates</li></ul><h2>Segregation (7.2)</h2><ul><li><b>Segregation</b> is separating two or more mutually incompatible substances or articles (7.2.2.1). It is done by distance, by one or more steel bulkheads or decks, or both. Compatible cargo may fill the space between.</li><li>Four terms: \"Away from\", \"Separated from\", \"Separated by a complete compartment or hold from\", and \"Separated longitudinally by an intervening complete compartment or hold from\" (7.2.2.2).</li><li>Goods subject to these terms may not be packed in the same outer packaging or carried in the same cargo transport unit, with exceptions (7.2.3.2).</li><li>A reference to a class in the list (for example \"away from class …\") covers every substance of that class and every substance needing a subsidiary risk label for that class.</li><li>Chemically similar goods (acids, alkalis, peroxides) form <b>segregation groups</b> (7.2.5).</li><li>Dangerous goods of certain hazard classes must be kept separate from foodstuffs (7.3.4, 7.6.3, 7.7.3).</li></ul><h2>Documents</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Who</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dangerous Goods Declaration (5.4.1.6)</td><td>Consignor or shipper, signed and dated</td><td>Accompanies the consignment or is part of the transport document; confirms contents are fully and accurately described, classified, packaged, marked, labelled and in proper condition</td></tr><tr><td>Container/Vehicle Packing Certificate (5.4.2)</td><td>The person who packs the unit (consignor, or consolidator for groupage)</td><td>Required for any container or vehicle, not for tanks. Certifies the unit was clean, dry and fit; segregation met; packages inspected and sound; drums upright; goods secured; unit marked and placarded; a transport document received for each consignment</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Masters and operators must not accept dangerous goods without the required document and signed certification.</li><li>Personnel involved in sea transport of dangerous goods need training suited to their responsibilities.</li></ul><h2>Amendment 42-24 (mandatory 1 Jan 2026)</h2><ul><li><b>Lithium batteries:</b> articles that may contain lithium cells or batteries are classified as dangerous goods N.O.S.; damaged or defective batteries in an article must be removed and packed under special packaging instructions; manufacturers must make test summaries available.</li><li><b>New Class 9 entries:</b> UN 3556 (vehicle, lithium ion battery powered), UN 3557 (vehicle, lithium metal battery powered), UN 3558 (vehicles powered by sodium-ion batteries) and UN 3551/3552 (sodium-ion batteries and equipment). Eleven new substances and articles in total. A new packing instruction applies to battery-powered vehicles.</li><li><b>UN 3171</b> no longer covers lithium batteries; it applies only to vehicles and equipment powered by wet batteries, metallic sodium batteries or sodium alloy batteries.</li><li><b>Charcoal and carbon (UN 1361, 1362):</b> SP 925 and SP 223 withdrawn; SP 978 and SP 979 introduced. SP 978 sets conditions on weathering, packaging (including a 40 °C limit on the day of packing) and stowage. SP 979 exempts steam- or chemically-activated charcoal that meets its criteria.</li><li><b>Stowage plan</b> must show primary and subsidiary hazard classes and the location of all dangerous goods and marine pollutants, and can replace a manifest or special list.</li><li>Data loggers, sensors and tracking devices in a cargo transport unit must be securely installed and certified safe for the goods carried.</li><li>A new stowage code for flammable gases or liquids with flashpoint below 23 °C requires separation from potential ignition sources.</li><li>Some existing labelling and warning mark provisions may continue to apply until <b>31 Dec 2026</b>.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Check every dangerous goods unit against its declaration and packing certificate, keep the stowage plan showing primary and subsidiary hazards, and apply segregation from the Dangerous Goods List (column 16b prevails over the table). Record which amendment is in use: 42-24 from 1 Jan 2026, then 43-26 from 1 Jan 2028.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Packing group definitions, placard minimum size, stowage categories A–E, and EmS schedule codes.</li><li>Class 1 and class 2 divisions, class 3 flashpoint limit.</li><li>Voluntary start date of Amendment 43-26 and its list of changes.</li><li>Old note's items on Amendment 42-24: P912, UN 3423 reclassification, and stowage category D for UN 3129, 3130, 3148 and 3536.</li><li>The Supplement contents (EmS Guide, MFAG) and detailed training content.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — IMDG Code: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/DangerousGoods-default.aspx</li><li>IMO — IMDG Code publication page: https://www.imo.org/en/publications/pages/imdg%20code.aspx</li><li>Resolution MSC.122(75) (Deutsche Flagge copy): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vi/msc-122-75-adoption-of-the-international-maritime-dangerous-goods-imdg-code.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.501(105): https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/file/MSC.501%28105%29.pdf</li><li>MSC.556(108) (Hong Kong Marine Department notice): https://www.mardep.gov.hk/filemanager/en/share/msnote/pdf/msin2468.pdf</li><li>West P&amp;I — Amendment 42-24: https://www.westpandi.com/News-and-Resources/News/September-2024/SOLAS-Amendments-to-the-IMDG-Code-(Amendment-42-24/</li><li>MPA Singapore circular SC 8 of 2026 (MSC.587(111)): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/api/media/32148767-1ad5-4219-92cf-7835a30d00ce/sc_no_8_of_2026.pdf</li><li>IMDG Code 2006 edition table of contents (law.resource.org): https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/004/imo.imdg.1.2006.pdf</li><li>BAM — IMDG Code 7.2: https://www.dgg.bam.de/dgginfo/detailinfo/imdg/adcjbgwzyldi?lang=en</li><li>UK MCA — packing certificates guidance: https://www.gov.uk/mca-guidance-for-container-packing-certificated-on-dangerous-goods-notes</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-imsbc-code","v":101734528,"title":"IMSBC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["imsbc","solid-bulk","liquefaction","solas-vi","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>IMSBC Code — International Maritime Solid Bulk Cargoes Code</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>4 Dec 2008, resolution MSC.268(85), at MSC 85</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory</td><td>Voluntary from 1 Jan 2009; <b>mandatory from 1 Jan 2011</b> under SOLAS chapters VI and VII</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>Code of Safe Practice for Solid Bulk Cargoes, 2004 (MSC.193(79))</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>SOLAS ships carrying solid bulk cargoes as defined in SOLAS VI/1-1 (1.4.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Current mandatory amendment</td><td>07-23 (MSC.539(107)), mandatory since 1 Jan 2025</td></tr><tr><td>Next</td><td>08-25 (MSC.575(110)), voluntary 1 Jan 2026, mandatory <b>1 Jan 2027</b></td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>13 sections and 5 appendices</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How the Code works</h2><ul><li>A cargo <b>listed in Appendix 1</b> follows its individual schedule plus sections 1 to 10 and 11.1.1 (1.2.2).</li><li>An <b>unlisted cargo</b> (1.3.1): before loading, the shipper gives the loading-port competent authority the cargo's characteristics under section 4, and that authority assesses acceptability. If the cargo may present Group A or B hazards, the port-of-unloading and flag State authorities are consulted and the three set preliminary conditions (1.3.1.1). The loading-port authority must apply to IMO within one year to add the cargo to Appendix 1.</li><li>Each cargo has a <b>Bulk Cargo Shipping Name (BCSN)</b>, shown in capitals in the schedules.</li></ul><h2>Cargo groups</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"imsbc-groups\"><figcaption>Group A, B and C (section 1.7)</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Group A:</b> cargoes with a moisture hazard that may cause <b>liquefaction or dynamic separation</b> if shipped above their transportable moisture limit (TML). Some Group A cargoes may also heat spontaneously (7.1.3).</li><li><b>Group B:</b> cargoes with chemical hazards that could create a dangerous situation on a ship. Three kinds (section 9): dangerous goods under the IMDG Code (classes 4.1 to 9); materials hazardous only in bulk (MHB); and dangerous goods that also have MHB hazards.</li><li><b>Group C:</b> cargoes that are neither Group A nor Group B.</li><li>A cargo can have more than one group: coal is Group A and B unless classified Group B only by a test determined by the appropriate authority.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning (1.7)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>TML</td><td>For a Group A cargo, the maximum moisture content considered safe for carriage on ships not meeting 7.3.2</td></tr><tr><td>Flow moisture point (FMP)</td><td>Moisture content (wet mass basis) at which a flow state develops in the prescribed test (Appendix 2, paragraph 1)</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamic separation</td><td>Forming a liquid slurry of water and fine solids above the solid material, creating a free surface effect that can significantly affect stability</td></tr><tr><td>Angle of repose</td><td>Maximum slope angle of non-cohesive granular material, between a horizontal plane and the cone slope</td></tr><tr><td>MHB</td><td>Materials that may have chemical hazards when carried in bulk, other than dangerous goods under the IMDG Code</td></tr></tbody></table><p>MHB notation codes: CB (combustible), SH (self-heating), WF (flammable gas when wet), WT (toxic gas when wet), TX (toxic), CR (corrosive), OH (other hazards).</p><h2>History and amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"imsbc-amendments\"><figcaption>Recent amendments and their dates</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>Voluntary from</th><th>Mandatory from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original Code</td><td>MSC.268(85)</td><td>4 Dec 2008</td><td>1 Jan 2009</td><td>1 Jan 2011</td></tr><tr><td>(2011 amendments)</td><td>MSC.318(89)</td><td>20 May 2011</td><td>1 Jan 2012</td><td>1 Jan 2013</td></tr><tr><td>(2013 amendments)</td><td>MSC.354(92)</td><td>21 Jun 2013</td><td>not stated</td><td>1 Jan 2015</td></tr><tr><td>(2015 amendments)</td><td>MSC.393(95)</td><td>11 Jun 2015</td><td>not stated</td><td>1 Jan 2017</td></tr><tr><td>(2017 amendments)</td><td>MSC.426(98)</td><td>15 Jun 2017</td><td>not stated</td><td>1 Jan 2019</td></tr><tr><td>05-19</td><td>MSC.462(101)</td><td>13 Jun 2019</td><td>not stated</td><td>1 Jan 2021</td></tr><tr><td>06-21</td><td>MSC.500(105)</td><td>28 Apr 2022</td><td>1 Jan 2023</td><td>1 Dec 2023</td></tr><tr><td>07-23</td><td>MSC.539(107)</td><td>Jun 2023</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2025</td></tr><tr><td>08-25</td><td>MSC.575(110)</td><td>Jun 2025</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>1 Jan 2027</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>06-21 (MSC.500(105))</b></p><ul><li>Group A redefined to cover <b>dynamic separation</b> as well as liquefaction; \"dynamic separation\" newly defined.</li><li>New schedules for ammonium nitrate based fertilizer, ammonium nitrate based fertilizer MHB, clam shell and leach residue containing lead; new triple granular superphosphate schedule. Old schedules for non-hazardous ammonium nitrate based fertilizer and triple granular superphosphate deleted.</li></ul><p><b>07-23 (MSC.539(107))</b> — a consolidated version of the whole Code</p><ul><li>Shippers must declare <b>bulk density</b> (new 4.2.2.7), in line with SOLAS XII/10 (ships 150 m and above).</li><li>Twelve new schedules, including barytes, brown fused alumina, dunite, potassium and sodium nitrate, and electric arc furnace dust (pelletised). Direct reduced iron (D) by-product fines added. Stabilized fish meal (anti-oxidant treated) no longer under UN 2216 but stays Group B (MHB).</li><li>List of non-cohesive cargoes amended (angle of repose must be determined before loading).</li></ul><p><b>08-25 (MSC.575(110))</b></p><ul><li><b>12 new schedules:</b> Aluminium Sulphate Granular; Apatite Concentrate; Asphalt Granulates; Crushed Granodiorite (Coarse); Ferric Sulphate Granular; Fish Meal (Stabilized); Fish Scrap (Stabilized); Iron Ore Briquettes; Pea Protein Concentrate Pellets; Phosphate Rock Fines (uncalcined); Tuff (Coarse); Zinc Slag (Coarse).</li><li><b>11 amended schedules:</b> Aluminium Ferrosilicon Powder (UN 1395); Aluminium Silicon Powder, Uncoated (UN 1398); Aluminium Smelting or Remelting By-products (UN 3170); Castor Beans, Meal, Pomace or Flake (UN 2969); Direct Reduced Iron (A) and (B); Ferrosilicon (two schedules); Ferrous Metal Borings, Shavings, Turnings or Cuttings (UN 2793); Fish Meal (Fish Scrap), Stabilized, Anti-oxidant treated; Iron Ore Pellets.</li><li>Section 3: updated fumigation guidance (revised pesticide recommendations, MSC.1/Circ.1264/Rev.1).</li><li>Section 9.3.3: segregation table amended (updated terminology, clearer separation for radioactive materials, outdated \"MHB\" references removed).</li><li>Section 13: references updated (for example MSC.1/Circ.1395/Rev.7).</li></ul><h2>Sections</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General provisions</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>General loading, carriage and unloading precautions</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Safety of personnel and ship</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Assessment of acceptability of consignments for safe shipment</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Trimming procedures</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Methods of determining the angle of repose</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Cargoes which may liquefy or undergo dynamic separation</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Test procedures for Group A cargoes</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Materials possessing chemical hazards</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Carriage of solid wastes in bulk</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Security provisions</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Stowage factor conversion tables</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>References to related information and recommendations</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Appendices: 1 Individual schedules of solid bulk cargoes; 2 Laboratory test procedures, associated apparatus and standards; 3 Properties of solid bulk cargoes; 4 Index; 5 Bulk Cargo Shipping Names in three languages.</p><p><b>Section 2: loading</b></p><ul><li>Cargo is distributed to keep adequate stability and avoid overstressing the structure (2.1.1); special attention to high-density cargo (2.1.2); cargo spaces are inspected and prepared before loading (2.2.1).</li><li>The loading or unloading plan is agreed by the master and terminal representative under SOLAS VI Regulation 7, paragraph 3.</li><li>Trimming (5.1.1): cargo is trimmed reasonably level, as necessary, to reduce shifting and air entry.</li></ul><p><b>Section 4: cargo information (4.2.2)</b></p><ul><li>The shipper gives the master, before loading: BCSN, group, IMO class, UN number, total quantity, stowage factor, bulk density, trimming needs, likelihood of shifting (angle of repose), moisture certificate and TML for Group A cargoes, likelihood of a wet base, toxic or flammable gas, flammability, toxicity, corrosiveness, oxygen depletion, self-heating, flammable gas with water, radioactive properties, MARPOL Annex V classification, and other national requirements.</li><li>Cargo information must say whether the cargo is harmful to the marine environment (4.2.2.2, added by an earlier amendment).</li></ul><h2>Group A cargoes: certificates and acceptance</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"imsbc-timing\"><figcaption>TML and moisture test timing before loading</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The shipper gives the master a signed <b>TML certificate</b> and a signed <b>moisture content certificate or declaration</b>, issued by an entity recognized by the port's competent authority (4.3.2).</li><li>The TML test must be done <b>within six months</b> of loading and repeated if the cargo's composition or characteristics are reasonably assumed to have changed (4.5.1).</li><li>Moisture sampling and testing must be as close as practicable to the start of loading, and the interval must <b>never exceed seven days</b> (4.5.2).</li><li>The shipper sets sampling, testing and moisture-control procedures to keep moisture below the TML while the cargo is on board (4.3.3). The shipper gives the ship's nominated representative access to stockpiles for inspection, sampling and testing (4.4.3).</li><li>If Group A cargo is loaded into several spaces, the certificate states the moisture content of each finely grained material in each space (4.3.5).</li><li><b>Acceptance (7.3.1.1):</b> loaded only if the actual moisture content is <b>less than the TML</b>. Cargo above the TML may be carried only in a specially constructed or fitted ship under 7.3.2, or a specially constructed dry powdery cargo ship under 7.3.3.</li><li>At sea, introducing water may bring the cargo to a flow state (7.3.1.5).</li><li><b>Can test</b> (crew check): free moisture on the sample at the end means laboratory testing is needed. It is not quantitative and must not be the only check: a pass does not guarantee moisture is below the TML.</li></ul><h2>Trimming and angle of repose (Section 5.4)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Angle of repose</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>30° or less</td><td>Cargo flows freely like grain</td></tr><tr><td>Over 30° to 35° inclusive</td><td>Surface unevenness Δh not more than <b>B/10</b> (B = beam in metres), maximum <b>1.5 m</b>; or approved trimming equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Over 35°</td><td>Δh not more than B/10, maximum <b>2 m</b>; or approved trimming equipment</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Other points</h2><ul><li><b>Section 9:</b> segregation of Group B cargoes from packaged dangerous goods (9.3.3.1, 9.3.3.2, IMDG Code consulted) and from each other (9.3.4). Cargo spaces should be cleaned after unloading because residues may be highly corrosive (9.3.2.4.4).</li><li><b>Section 10:</b> transboundary movements of wastes under the Basel Convention may start only after notification and written consent of the destination country (10.4).</li><li><b>Section 11:</b> high-consequence cargoes include ammonium nitrate UN 1942 and ammonium nitrate based fertilizers UN 2067 (11.3.1).</li><li><b>Section 12:</b> 1 m³/t = 35.87 ft³/ton; 1 ft³/ton = 0.02788 m³/t.</li><li><b>Examples from schedules:</b> ammonium nitrate and fertilizer UN 1942/UN 2067 not accepted if cargo temperature is above 40 °C; ammonium nitrate based fertilizer MHB not stowed next to heated fuel oil tanks unless controlled to not exceed 50 °C; aluminium ferrosilicon powder UN 1395 needs at least two extra sets of self-contained breathing apparatus; aluminium silicon powder UN 1398 needs mechanical ventilation of at least six air changes per hour.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Never load Group A cargo without a valid TML certificate (not older than six months) and a moisture certificate (sampled within seven days) showing moisture below the TML. A passing can test is not proof. Check the cargo's schedule and group, and have the latest amendment on board: 07-23 now, 08-25 mandatory from 1 Jan 2027.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The old note's \"≤3 days if heavy rain\" rule for moisture sampling.</li><li>The BC Code date of 1965, and a future amendment 09-27.</li><li>Full content of section 3 (enclosed spaces, fumigation, gas detection) and section 8 test methods.</li><li>The Supplement contents (BLU Code, EmS, MFAG).</li><li>Individual cargo schedule details beyond the examples above.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMSBC Code, Amendment 07-23 text (MLIT Japan): https://www.mlit.go.jp/maritime/content/001852905.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.500(105): https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/file/MSC.500%28105%29.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.426(98): https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/regulation/MSC.426%2898%29.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.393(95): https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/regulation/23448-Resolution_MSC_393_95.pdf</li><li>Deutsche Flagge, IMSBC Code amendment status: https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vi/imsbc-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.268(85) (Intermanager): https://www.intermanager.org/2009/02/msc8526add-2-international-maritime-solid-bulk-cargoes-imsbc-code/</li><li>West P&amp;I — Amendment 06-21: https://westpandi.com/news-and-resources/news/october-2022/solas-amendments-to-the-imsbc-code-(amendment-06-2</li><li>West P&amp;I — Amendment 07-23: https://www.westpandi.com/News-and-Resources/News/December-2023/SOLAS-IMSBC-Code-2023-Edition-(Amendment-07-23)/</li><li>UK P&amp;I — IMSBC update 2024–2025: https://www.ukpandi.com/news-and-resources/news/article/articles/2024/imsbc-update-2024-2025/</li><li>West P&amp;I — Amendment 08-25: https://www.westpandi.com/News-and-Resources/News/October-2025/SOLAS-Amendments-to-the-IMSBC-Code-(Amendment-08-2/</li><li>Swedish Club — Cargo Advice: Liquefaction: https://www.swedishclub.com/uploads/2025/12/Cargo-Advice-Liquefaction.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-inf-code","v":48052303,"title":"INF Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["inf-code","nuclear","radioactive","class-7","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>INF Code — International Code for the Safe Carriage of Packaged Irradiated Nuclear Fuel, Plutonium and High-Level Radioactive Wastes on Board Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>27 May 1999, resolution MSC.88(71), at MSC 71</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory</td><td>SOLAS chapter VII <b>Part D</b>, added by MSC.87(71) (adopted 27 May 1999); in force <b>1 Jan 2001</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cargo</td><td><b>INF cargo</b>: packaged irradiated nuclear fuel, plutonium and high-level radioactive wastes (IMDG Class 7)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>All ships, regardless of build date or size, including cargo ships under 500 GT, carrying INF cargo (SOLAS VII/15)</td></tr><tr><td>Not for</td><td>Warships, naval auxiliaries and government non-commercial vessels (their administrations act consistently as far as reasonable)</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Certificate of Fitness for the Carriage of INF Cargo</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>11 chapters and an appendix (certificate form)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What it is</h2><ul><li>Additional <b>ship</b> requirements for carrying INF cargo. The package and transport rules of the IMDG Code also apply to INF cargo (1.2.2).</li><li>SOLAS VII Part D has three regulations: 14 (definitions, including the INF Code), 15 (application) and 16 (requirements: comply with the Code, be surveyed and certified).</li><li>INF certificates are subject to control under SOLAS I/19 and XI/4 and are treated as certificates issued under I/12 or I/13.</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.88(71) recalls Assembly resolutions A.748(18), A.790(19), A.853(20) and A.854(20), and recognizes the need for mandatory application of the standards.</li><li>Amendments to the Code follow SOLAS article VIII.</li><li>Amendments listed by the IMO: <b>MSC.118(74)</b> (2001, in force 1 Jan 2003), <b>MSC.135(76)</b> (2002, 1 Jul 2004), <b>MSC.178(79)</b> (2004, 1 Jul 2006) and <b>MSC.241(83)</b> (2007, 1 Jul 2009).</li><li><b>MSC.118(74)</b>, adopted 6 Jun 2001: paragraph 1.1.1.3 now refers to \"transport schedule 10, 11, 12, 13 or 14\", adding schedule 14 to align with amendment 30 to the IMDG Code.</li></ul><h2>Ship classes (1.1.2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"inf-classes\"><figcaption>Class is set by total activity carried</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Cargo and limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>INF 1</b></td><td>Any INF cargo, aggregate activity less than <b>4,000 TBq</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>INF 2</b></td><td>Irradiated nuclear fuel or high-level wastes less than <b>2 × 10⁶ TBq</b>; plutonium less than <b>2 × 10⁵ TBq</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>INF 3</b></td><td>Irradiated nuclear fuel or high-level wastes, and plutonium, with <b>no restriction</b> on aggregate activity</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>INF cargo that would need an INF 3 ship may <b>not</b> be carried on passenger ships (1.2.3).</li></ul><h2>Certificate and surveys (Chapter 1)</h2><ul><li>An <b>initial survey</b> is required before carriage (1.3.1).</li><li>The Administration, or a recognized organization, issues the certificate (1.3.2). Certified ships get periodic surveys under SOLAS chapter I (1.3.3).</li><li>The certificate lapses if required surveys are missed or show non-compliance, or when the related SOLAS certificate expires (1.3.4).</li></ul><h2>Requirements by class</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"inf-requirements\"><figcaption>Key figures in the Code</figcaption></figure><p><b>Chapter 2: damage stability</b></p><ul><li>INF 1: to the Administration's satisfaction (2.1).</li><li>INF 2: SOLAS II-1 Part B if built to passenger-ship standard; Part B-1 if built to cargo-ship standard, regardless of length (2.2).</li><li>INF 3: IBC Code chapter 2 survival capability and cargo location for a <b>type 1 ship</b>; or SOLAS II-1 Part B-1, regardless of length, with the subdivision index <b>R(INF) = R + 0.2(1 − R)</b> (2.3).</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 3: fire safety</b></p><ul><li>INF 1: to the Administration's satisfaction (3.1).</li><li>INF 2 and 3, any size (3.2): a water fire-extinguishing system (SOLAS II-2/4); fixed fire-extinguishing in category A machinery spaces (II-2/7); fixed cargo space cooling (II-2/54.2.1.3); fixed fire detection and alarm for machinery, accommodation and service spaces (II-2/13).</li><li>INF 3: accommodation, service spaces, control stations and category A machinery spaces are forward or aft of the cargo spaces (3.3).</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 4: temperature (all classes, 4.1)</b></p><ul><li>Enclosed cargo spaces have ventilation or refrigeration keeping the average ambient temperature at or below <b>55 °C</b> at all times.</li><li>Cargo space ventilation or refrigeration is independent of systems serving other spaces.</li><li>Essential items (fans, compressors, heat exchangers, cooling water supply) are duplicated for each cargo space, with spare parts.</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 5: structure</b></p><ul><li>Deck areas and support arrangements are strong enough for the loads they carry.</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 6: securing (6.1)</b></p><ul><li>Permanent securing devices prevent package movement, for accelerations of <b>1.5 g</b> longitudinally, <b>1.5 g</b> transversely, <b>1.0 g</b> vertically up and <b>2.0 g</b> vertically down.</li><li>On open or vehicle decks, packages may instead be secured under Administration-approved principles based on IMO guidelines (6.2).</li><li>Collision chocks must not block the cooling airflow required by 4.1 (6.3).</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 7: electrical power</b></p><ul><li>INF 1: to the Administration's satisfaction.</li><li>INF 2 and 3: an alternative power source, unaffected by damage to the main supply, supplying for <b>at least 36 hours</b> the flooding and cooling equipment and all emergency services required by SOLAS (7.2).</li><li>INF 3: the alternative source is outside the damage extent considered under chapter 2 (7.3).</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 8: radiological protection</b></p><ul><li>Additional radiological protection arrangements or equipment where needed, depending on cargo and ship design, to the Administration's satisfaction.</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 9: management and training</b></p><ul><li>To the Administration's satisfaction, taking into account IMO developments.</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 10: shipboard emergency plan</b></p><ul><li>Every ship carrying INF cargo carries a plan, approved by the Administration, based on IMO guidelines (A.854(20)), in a language the master and officers understand (10.1, 10.2).</li><li>It includes the reporting procedure under chapter 11; a list of authorities or persons to contact; detailed immediate actions to prevent, reduce or control a release; and procedures and contact points for coordinating with national and local authorities.</li><li>It may be combined with other required plans into a single <b>Shipboard Marine Emergency Plan</b> (10.3).</li></ul><p><b>Chapter 11: notification</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS regulation VII/7-1 reporting applies to actual or likely loss of INF cargo overboard and to any actual or probable release, whatever the cause (11.1).</li><li>Reports are also required for damage or breakdown affecting safety or navigation: collision, grounding, fire, explosion, structural failure, flooding, cargo shifting, or failure of steering, propulsion, electrical generation or essential navigational aids (11.2).</li><li>\"Incident\" covers loss of container integrity and probable cargo release; \"release\" covers escape of INF cargo from containment or loss of a package (1.1.1).</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Know the three classes: INF 1 under 4,000 TBq, INF 2, INF 3 with no limit. Cargo spaces stay at or below 55 °C on average, securing is designed for 1.5 g / 1.5 g / 1.0 g up / 2.0 g down, INF 2 and 3 need 36 hours of alternative power, and any release or probable release is reported at once under SOLAS VII/7-1.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The old claim that the Code was first adopted as recommendations in 1993 (A.748(18) is recalled in the preamble; its year and nature were not confirmed).</li><li>What MSC.135(76), MSC.178(79) and MSC.241(83) changed: only their numbers and dates are confirmed.</li><li>The old description of chapter 8 as \"shielding, radiation monitoring, doses as low as reasonably achievable\" and chapter 9 as \"crew trained on INF risks\": the Code text says only that these satisfy the Administration.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.88(71), INF Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.88(71).pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.87(71), SOLAS chapter VII Part D: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.87(71).pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.118(74): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.118(74).pdf</li><li>IMO — List of the Conventions and their amendments: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/List%20of%20the%20Conventions%20and%20their%20amendments%20.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ip-code","v":192986445,"title":"IP Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ip-code","industrial-personnel","offshore-wind","transfer","solas-xv","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>IP Code — International Code of Safety for Ships Carrying Industrial Personnel</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>10 Nov 2022, resolution MSC.527(106), at MSC 106</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory</td><td>SOLAS <b>chapter XV</b> (MSC.521(106)); both take effect <b>1 Jul 2024</b></td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Cargo ships and high-speed cargo craft of <b>500 GT and above</b> carrying <b>more than 12 industrial personnel</b>, on international voyages</td></tr><tr><td>Voluntary</td><td>Governments are invited to apply it, as far as practicable, to ships under 500 GT and to ships not on international voyages</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Industrial Personnel Safety Certificate, with Record of Equipment (Form IP)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Parts I to V and an appendix</td></tr><tr><td>Pending</td><td>MSC.588(111): dimensioning weight of a person raised from 75 kg to 90 kg (from 1 Jan 2028)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What it is</h2><ul><li>Safety standards for ships carrying <b>industrial personnel (IP)</b> and for those people, including risks during <b>personnel transfer</b> at sea.</li><li><b>Overall goal (Part I, 1):</b> safe carriage of industrial personnel and their safety during personnel transfer, addressing risks not adequately covered by SOLAS.</li><li><b>Industrial personnel (2.4):</b> all persons transported or accommodated on board for offshore industrial activities on other ships or offshore facilities.</li><li><b>Offshore industrial activities (2.6):</b> construction, maintenance, decommissioning, operation or servicing of offshore facilities, such as renewable or hydrocarbon energy, aquaculture and ocean mining.</li><li><b>Personnel transfer (2.7):</b> the full sequence of transferring personnel and their equipment at sea between the ship and another ship or offshore facility.</li><li>IP are <b>not passengers</b> (SOLAS XV).</li></ul><h2>SOLAS chapter XV (MSC.521(106))</h2><ul><li>Adopted 10 Nov 2022; deemed accepted 1 Jan 2024; in force <b>1 Jul 2024</b>. It adds chapter XV, \"Safety measures for ships carrying industrial personnel\", after chapter XIV.</li><li>When the number of IP is a parameter, it is the combined total of IP, special personnel and passengers, with passengers capped at 12.</li><li><b>Regulation 3 (application):</b> cargo ships and high-speed cargo craft of 500 GT and above, constructed on or after 1 Jul 2024, carrying more than 12 IP.</li><li><b>Existing ships</b> already authorized to carry more than 12 IP must meet the specified IP Code regulations by their first intermediate or renewal survey after 1 Jul 2024; high-speed cargo craft by their third periodical or first renewal survey.</li><li>Ships not authorized before 1 Jul 2024 must comply and be certified before carrying more than 12 IP, regardless of construction date.</li><li><b>Regulation 5:</b> the ship is certified as a cargo ship or high-speed cargo craft, meets the IP Code, and is surveyed and certified under it. The certificate is subject to port State control and treated as issued under SOLAS I/12 or I/13.</li><li>For high-speed craft, certification under the chapter is deemed to satisfy HSC Code chapters 2 to 12 and 18.</li><li>Ships under 500 GT carrying more than 12 persons (combined) may have the goals and functional requirements applied as far as practicable, with relaxations noted on the certificate.</li></ul><h2>Certificate and survey (Part I, 3)</h2><ul><li>Every covered ship carries a valid Industrial Personnel Safety Certificate, issued after an initial or renewal survey (3.1, 3.2).</li><li>Issued by the Administration or a recognized organization; the Administration takes full responsibility (3.3).</li><li>Follows the model in the appendix; translated into English, French or Spanish if not in one of them (3.4).</li><li>Validity, survey dates and endorsements are harmonized with SOLAS I/14 or X/3.2 (3.5). It is issued in addition to certificates required under SOLAS XV/5.1.1 (3.6).</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I: General</td><td>1 Goal; 2 Definitions; 3 Certificate and survey</td></tr><tr><td>II: Goals and functional requirements</td><td>1 Industrial personnel; 2 Safe transfer of personnel; 3 Subdivision and stability; 4 Machinery installations; 5 Electrical installations; 6 Periodically unattended machinery spaces; 7 Fire safety; 8 Life-saving appliances and arrangements; 9 Dangerous goods</td></tr><tr><td>III: Regulations</td><td>Regulation 1 Industrial personnel; Regulation 2 Safe transfer</td></tr><tr><td>IV: Additional regulations for ships certified under SOLAS chapter I</td><td>Regulations 1 to 8 (general, subdivision and stability, machinery, electrical, periodically unattended machinery spaces, fire safety, life-saving appliances, dangerous goods)</td></tr><tr><td>V: Additional regulations for craft certified under SOLAS chapter X</td><td>Regulations 1 to 8 (same subjects)</td></tr><tr><td>Appendix</td><td>Model Industrial Personnel Safety Certificate; Record of Equipment (Form IP)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Part III: before and during transfer</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ip-flow\"><figcaption>From joining the ship to a safe transfer</figcaption></figure><p><b>Regulation 1: industrial personnel</b></p><ul><li><b>Age and fitness (1.1):</b> at least <b>16 years</b>; the master holds proof of physical and medical fitness to a standard the Administration accepts.</li><li><b>Language (1.2):</b> understand the working language well enough to communicate and follow crew instructions.</li><li><b>Pre-boarding training (1.3):</b> personal survival (including entering water from a height, surviving in water, boarding a survival craft in a lifejacket); fire safety; personal safety (master's authority, crew directions, signs, symbols and alarms). Training meeting accepted offshore industry standards (a footnote mentions GWO or OPITO) may be accepted.</li><li><b>Documents (1.4):</b> nobody is carried unless the master holds documentation of the required training.</li><li><b>Ship familiarization (1.5):</b> before departure or right after boarding: layout, life-saving equipment, muster and embarkation stations, escape routes, first aid stations, signs and alarms, and action on an alarm or emergency.</li><li><b>Transfer familiarization (1.6):</b> before transfer, a briefing on the ship's transfer procedures, arrangements and added safety measures or equipment.</li></ul><p><b>Regulation 2: safe transfer</b></p><ul><li>Equipment kept in good condition, maintained and regularly inspected; a responsible officer supervises rigging and use, with a means of communication to the bridge (2.1.1 to 2.1.3).</li><li>Each appliance <b>permanently marked</b>, with a record of use and maintenance on board (2.1.4); checked for function before operations (2.1.5).</li><li>A safe, unobstructed route from the transfer arrangements to where personnel are carried or accommodated (2.1.6); lighting from the emergency source over the transfer arrangements, the water below and the route (2.1.7); a designated, clear transfer deck area (2.1.8).</li><li>A <b>job safety analysis</b> during planning and before execution, accounting for environmental conditions and equipment limits (2.1.9). The regulation sets no numerical weather limits.</li><li>Transfer arrangements designed, built, tested and installed to standards accepted by the Administration or a recognized classification society (2.2).</li><li>A <b>failure analysis</b> (qualitative or FMEA) covers single failures, fire in any space and flooding of any watertight compartment, to keep transfer available and people safe (2.3).</li><li>Position keeping: evaluate manoeuvrability and the likely need to hold position (2.4).</li><li><b>Headcount:</b> procedures keep the number and identity of persons on board accurate at all times (2.5).</li></ul><h2>Part IV: number of persons</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ip-thresholds\"><figcaption>What changes as the number on board grows</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Persons certified</th><th>Requirement (Part IV)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>More than 240</td><td>Subdivision as passenger ship (2.1.1); fire safety as SOLAS II-2 for passenger ships carrying more than 36 passengers (6.1)</td></tr><tr><td>More than 60, up to 240</td><td>Fire safety as passenger ships carrying not more than 36 passengers, except II-2/21 and 22 (6.2); subdivision factor interpolated</td></tr><tr><td>60 or fewer</td><td>Subdivision reduction: R = 0.8R</td></tr><tr><td>More than 60</td><td>SOLAS chapter III as for passenger ships on international voyages that are not short; III/2 and III/19.2.3 do not apply; \"passengers\" read as industrial personnel (7.1 to 7.3). Toxic products, low-flashpoint products and acids are not allowed (8.4.1.1)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Subdivision formula (2.1.2): <b>R = 1 − 5,000 / (Ls + 2.5N + 15,225)</b>, where N = N1 + 2N2 (N1 persons with lifeboats provided; N2 additional persons).</li><li>Infant and child lifejacket numbers are calculated from passengers only (7.4).</li><li><b>Part V (high-speed craft):</b> at most <b>60 persons</b> (Reg 1.1); stability calculations use <b>90 kg</b> per person instead of 75 kg (Reg 2.2).</li></ul><h2>MSC 111 amendment</h2><ul><li><b>MSC.588(111)</b>, adopted at MSC 111 (13–22 May 2026), amends the IP Code. ABS's summary lists it as increasing industrial personnel weight from <b>75 kg to 90 kg</b>, with entry into force on <b>1 Jan 2028</b>.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> More than 12 industrial personnel means the ship needs an Industrial Personnel Safety Certificate. Nobody boards without training documents; everyone gets ship familiarization and a transfer briefing. Each transfer needs a job safety analysis, a checked and marked appliance, and an accurate headcount.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Numerical weather limits and specific rescue provisions for transfers: the Code sets none in Regulation 2.</li><li>The detail of MSC.588(111) beyond the weight change (the annex text could not be read).</li><li>Details of Part IV regulations 3 to 5 (machinery, electrical, unattended spaces) beyond the thresholds shown.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.527(106), IP Code text (AMSA copy): https://www.amsa.gov.au/sites/default/files/2024-03/msc.527106.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.521(106), SOLAS chapter XV (Norwegian Maritime Authority copy): https://www.sdir.no/contentassets/9d09fcfcd83c431a891f1690e8164c4c/msc.521106.pdf</li><li>IMO — MSC 106 press briefing: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/msc106.aspx</li><li>Report of MSC 111, add. 1 (MPA Singapore copy): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/api/media/cfd61030-4bf0-4995-b8d9-3f87d3600cf8/MSC-111-22-Add.1---Report-Of-The-Maritime-Safety-Committee-On-Its-111Th-Session-(Secretariat).pdf</li><li>ABS — Brief: MSC 111: https://ww2.eagle.org/en/rules-and-resources/regulatory-updates/regulatory-news/msc111.html</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-is-code-2008","v":196503628,"title":"IS Code 2008","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["is-code","intact-stability","gz","gm","weather-criterion","solas-ii-1","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>2008 IS Code — International Code on Intact Stability, 2008</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>4 Dec 2008, resolution MSC.267(85), at MSC 85</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory</td><td>Introduction and <b>Part A</b>, through SOLAS (MSC.269(85)) and the 1988 Load Lines Protocol (MSC.270(85))</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>1 Jul 2010</b></td></tr><tr><td>Part B</td><td>Recommendatory; governments are asked to use it as a basis for standards unless national requirements give at least equivalent safety</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Ships and other marine vehicles of <b>24 m in length and over</b> (unless stated otherwise)</td></tr><tr><td>Ship types</td><td>Cargo ships, ships with timber deck cargo, passenger ships, fishing vessels, special purpose ships, offshore supply vessels, MODUs, pontoons, containerships and ships carrying containers on deck</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What it is</h2><ul><li>One document consolidating mandatory (Introduction and Part A) and recommended (Part B) intact stability provisions, based mainly on existing IMO instruments.</li><li>The preamble notes that the criteria reflect the best concepts at the time, should be re-evaluated periodically, and that dynamic stability in a seaway is not fully understood.</li><li>Since 2018/2020 amendments it also covers ships <b>engaged in anchor handling, towing and escort, and lifting operations</b>.</li></ul><h2>Amendments</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Amends</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.267(85)</td><td>4 Dec 2008</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td><td>Adopts the Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.319(89)</td><td>20 May 2011</td><td>20 May 2011</td><td>Part B, chapter 2, section 2.6</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.398(95)</td><td>5 Jun 2015</td><td>5 Jun 2015</td><td>Part B, chapter 6</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.413(97), MSC.414(97)</td><td>25 Nov 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Introduction and Part A</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.415(97)</td><td>25 Nov 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Part B: 1.2.2; 2.4.3.4, 2.7, 2.8, 2.9; 3.4, 3.5, 3.6, 3.8, 3.9; 4.1; Annex 3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.443(99), MSC.444(99)</td><td>24 May 2018</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Part A, chapter 2 (read together with the 2016 amendments)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>2016/2018 amendments (Introduction paragraph 1.2):</b> new subparagraphs 1.2.7 to 1.2.9 for ships engaged in anchor handling, towing and escort, and lifting operations. New definitions 2.27 to 2.31: anchor handling; harbour towing; coastal or ocean-going towing; lifting operation (fishing vessels are not included); escort operation.</li><li><b>Second-generation intact stability criteria:</b> SDC 7 (Feb 2020) approved interim guidelines for five dynamic stability failure modes in waves — pure loss of stability, parametric roll, surf-riding/broaching, dead ship condition and excessive accelerations — covering vulnerability criteria, direct stability failure assessment and operational measures. MSC 102 (4–11 Nov 2020) approved them for use in practice.</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Introduction</td><td>1 Purpose; 2 Definitions</td></tr><tr><td><b>Part A</b> (mandatory)</td><td>Ch 1 General (1.1 application; 1.2 dynamic stability phenomena in waves); Ch 2 General criteria (2.1 general; 2.2 righting lever curve; 2.3 severe wind and rolling criterion); Ch 3 Special criteria (3.1 passenger ships; 3.2 oil tankers 5,000 dwt and above; 3.3 timber deck cargoes; 3.4 grain in bulk; 3.5 high-speed craft)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Part B</b> (recommended)</td><td>Ch 1 General; Ch 2 Design criteria (fishing vessels, pontoons, containerships over 100 m, offshore supply vessels, special purpose ships, MODUs); Ch 3 Stability information; Ch 4 Stability instruments; Ch 5 Operational provisions against capsizing; Ch 6 Icing; Ch 7 Watertight and weathertight integrity; Ch 8 Lightship parameters</td></tr><tr><td>Annexes</td><td>1 Inclining test; 2 Recommendations for skippers of fishing vessels in ice-forming conditions</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>General criteria (Part A, chapter 2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"is-gz-criteria\"><figcaption>Righting lever curve criteria (2.2), schematic and not to scale</figcaption></figure><p><b>2.1 General</b></p><ul><li>Criteria apply to all loading conditions in Part B 3.3 and 3.4 (2.1.1).</li><li><b>Free surface effects</b> are accounted for in all loading conditions (2.1.2).</li><li>A safe stability margin at all stages of the voyage, allowing for water absorption and icing (2.1.5).</li><li>Each ship has an Administration-approved <b>stability booklet</b> (2.1.6).</li></ul><p><b>2.2 Righting lever (GZ) curve</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Area under GZ, 0 to 30° (2.2.1)</td><td>at least <b>0.055 m·rad</b></td></tr><tr><td>Area under GZ, 0 to 40° or downflooding angle if less (2.2.1)</td><td>at least <b>0.09 m·rad</b></td></tr><tr><td>Area between 30° and 40° or downflooding angle (2.2.1)</td><td>at least <b>0.03 m·rad</b></td></tr><tr><td>GZ at 30° or more (2.2.2)</td><td>at least <b>0.2 m</b></td></tr><tr><td>Angle of maximum GZ (2.2.3)</td><td><b>25° or more</b> (alternative criteria with Administration approval)</td></tr><tr><td>Initial metacentric height GM₀ (2.2.4)</td><td>at least <b>0.15 m</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Severe wind and rolling (weather) criterion (2.3)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"is-weather\"><figcaption>Steady wind and gust levers against the GZ curve (2.3), schematic</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The ship is tested for combined beam wind and rolling. <b>Area b</b> (margin from the roll-to-windward angle φ1 to the second intercept) must be at least <b>area a</b> (from the steady-wind equilibrium heel φ0 to the roll angle) (2.3.1).</li><li>Steady wind heel angle φ0 must not exceed <b>16°</b> or <b>80% of the deck-edge immersion angle</b>, whichever is less (2.3.1).</li><li>Wind pressure <b>P = 504 Pa</b> (may be reduced for restricted service with Administration approval). Steady wind lever lw1 = P·A·Z / (1000·g·Δ); gust lever lw2 = <b>1.5 × lw1</b> (2.3.2). A is the projected lateral area above the waterline, Z the vertical distance from the centre of A to the centre of the underwater lateral area (or about half the mean draught), Δ displacement.</li><li>The roll angle tables (2.3.4) are based on ships with B/d below 3.5, (KG/d − 1) between −0.3 and 0.5, and roll period T below 20 s; other ships may need model tests (2.3.5, MSC.1/Circ.1200).</li></ul><h2>Special criteria (Part A, chapter 3)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ship</th><th>Criterion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passenger ships (3.1)</td><td>Meet 2.2 and 2.3, plus: heel from crowding of passengers to one side not more than <b>10°</b> (minimum 75 kg per passenger; passenger centre of gravity 1 m above deck standing, 0.3 m above seat when seated); heel from turning not more than <b>10°</b></td></tr><tr><td>Oil tankers, 5,000 dwt and above (3.2)</td><td>Regulation 27 of MARPOL Annex I</td></tr><tr><td>Timber deck cargo (3.3)</td><td>2.2 and 2.3, or the alternative in 3.3.2: GZ area to 40° (or flooding angle) at least <b>0.08 m·rad</b>; maximum GZ at least <b>0.25 m</b>; GM at least <b>0.1 m</b> at all times, allowing for water absorption and icing; the 16° steady-heel limit applies but the 80% deck-edge immersion limit may be ignored</td></tr><tr><td>Grain in bulk (3.4)</td><td>International Grain Code (MSC.23(59))</td></tr><tr><td>High-speed craft (3.5)</td><td>1994 HSC Code (MSC.36(63)) or 2000 HSC Code (MSC.97(73)), depending on construction date</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Part B: selected guidance</h2><ul><li><b>Stability instruments (4.1):</b> an approved instrument supplements, and does not replace, the approved stability booklet (4.1.1.2). Three software types: Type 1 intact stability only; Type 2 adds a damage check against a limit curve or approved loading conditions; Type 3 applies pre-programmed damage cases to each loading condition (4.1.3). At least four loading conditions from the booklet are test conditions (4.1.6.2.2); one approved condition is tested at each annual survey and all at each renewal survey (4.1.9).</li><li><b>Operational provisions (chapter 5):</b> meeting the criteria does not guarantee immunity from capsizing (5.1.1). Watertight and weathertight closures stay closed at sea unless needed for operations (5.2.2). In following, quartering and head seas change speed or course (5.3.3). Excessive GM can cause harmful accelerations (5.1.6).</li><li><b>Icing (chapter 6):</b> allowance <b>30 kg/m²</b> on exposed weather decks and gangways and <b>7.5 kg/m²</b> on the projected lateral area of each side above the waterplane (6.3.1).</li><li><b>Weathertight integrity (chapter 7):</b> hatch coamings on exposed working decks at least 300 mm high at 12 m length and 600 mm at 24 m (7.1.3.8); non-wood hatch covers designed for 10 kN/m² or the cargo load (7.1.3.2).</li><li><b>Lightship (chapter 8):</b> every passenger ship and every cargo ship of 24 m or more is inclined on completion (8.1.1). Lightweight surveys at intervals not exceeding five years on passenger ships (8.1.5). Inclining test: ship nearly upright (list up to half a degree), test weights giving 1° to 4° of heel to each side (8.2.2.7, 8.2.2.8); standard test uses eight weight movements (8.4.1.2).</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Every loading condition must pass the 2.2 GZ criteria and the 2.3 weather criterion, with free surface correction applied, and the stability booklet must be approved. Check with the approved loading instrument, and apply the special criteria for passenger ships, timber deck cargo and grain.</blockquote><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The circular number of the second-generation interim guidelines (the sources did not give it); levels 1 and 2 of the criteria.</li><li>Paragraphs 2.2.5 to 2.2.7 and Part B chapter 2 design criteria (fishing vessels, pontoons, containerships over 100 m, offshore supply vessels, MODUs).</li><li>Content of the 2016 operational criteria for anchor handling, towing and lifting ships beyond the definitions.</li><li>The old note's references to the A.749(18) predecessor and older resolutions, and the 2015–2016 \"anchor handling\" criteria wording.</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>2008 IS Code text, Resolution MSC.267(85) (law.resource.org copy): https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.msc.267.2008.pdf</li><li>Deutsche Flagge, IS Code amendment status: https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/ii-1/is-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Resolutions MSC.413(97) and MSC.443(99): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.413(97) and MSC.443(99).pdf</li><li>Resolutions MSC.414(97) and MSC.444(99): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.414(97) and MSC.444(99).pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.414(97) (MARINA copy): https://marina.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/ANNEX-E-RES-MSC.414-97.pdf</li><li>IMO — SDC 7 meeting summary: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/MeetingSummaries/Pages/SDC-7.aspx</li><li>IMO — MSC 102 meeting summary: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/MeetingSummaries/Pages/MSC-102nd-session.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ism-code","v":150103078,"title":"ISM Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ism","sms","safety-management","solas-ix","imo","code","core"],"body":"<h1>ISM Code — International Safety Management Code</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>International Management Code for the Safe Operation of Ships and for Pollution Prevention</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>4 Nov 1993, Assembly resolution A.741(18)</td></tr><tr><td>Made mandatory by</td><td>SOLAS chapter IX (in force 1 Jul 1998)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Part A: Implementation (elements 1–12) · Part B: Certification and verification (13–16)</td></tr><tr><td>Company certificate</td><td>Document of Compliance (DOC)</td></tr><tr><td>Ship certificate</td><td>Safety Management Certificate (SMC)</td></tr><tr><td>Validity</td><td>DOC and SMC: up to 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Key person</td><td>Designated Person(s) ashore (element 4)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ism-elements\"><figcaption>The 16 elements of the ISM Code</figcaption></figure><h2>What the Code does</h2><ul><li>Gives an international standard for the safe management and operation of ships and for pollution prevention</li><li>The <b>Company</b> builds a Safety Management System (SMS). The flag State (or its recognized organization) audits it.</li><li>Objectives (1.2): safety at sea, prevention of injury or loss of life, avoidance of damage to the environment and property</li><li>IMO says the cornerstone of good safety management is commitment from the top</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The ISM Code certifies the *management system*, not the ship's hardware. The DOC belongs to the Company; the SMC belongs to the ship.</blockquote><h2>Application dates (SOLAS IX)</h2><p>Applies to ships on international voyages, regardless of build date.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Ship types</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Jul 1998</td><td>Passenger ships (including passenger high-speed craft) · oil tankers, chemical tankers, gas carriers, bulk carriers and cargo high-speed craft of 500 GT and above</td></tr><tr><td>1 Jul 2002</td><td>Other cargo ships and mobile offshore drilling units (MODUs) of 500 GT and above</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ism-dates\"><figcaption>ISM Code dates: adoption, entry into force and amendments</figcaption></figure><h2>Amendments</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.104(73)</td><td>5 Dec 2000</td><td>1 Jul 2002</td><td>Definitions in 1.1, chapter 7, Part B chapter 13, new chapters 14–16, certificate forms</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.179(79)</td><td>10 Dec 2004</td><td>1 Jul 2006</td><td>Amends the Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.195(80)</td><td>20 May 2005</td><td>1 Jan 2009</td><td>Amends the Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.273(85)</td><td>4 Dec 2008</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td><td>Part A chapters 1, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12; Part B chapters 13, 14</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.353(92)</td><td>21 Jun 2013</td><td>1 Jan 2015</td><td>Part A chapters 6 and 12</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Related IMO instruments</h2><ul><li><b>A.1118(30)</b> (adopted 6 Dec 2017): Revised Guidelines on implementation of the ISM Code <b>by Administrations</b>. It revokes A.1071(28).</li><li><b>MSC.428(98)</b> (adopted 16 Jun 2017): Maritime cyber risk management in safety management systems. Administrations are encouraged to ensure cyber risks are addressed in the SMS <b>no later than the first annual verification of the Company's DOC after 1 Jan 2021</b>.</li></ul><h2>Part A — Implementation</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 General</td><td>Definitions (1.1: objective evidence, observation, non-conformity, major non-conformity, anniversary date) · objectives (1.2) · application (1.3) · functional requirements of an SMS (1.4)</td></tr><tr><td>2 Policy</td><td>Safety and environmental-protection policy</td></tr><tr><td>3 Company responsibilities</td><td>If the owner is not the Company, the owner reports the operator's details to the Administration (3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>4 Designated Person(s)</td><td>Link between the Company and those on board</td></tr><tr><td>5 Master's responsibility and authority</td><td>Includes the <b>overriding authority</b> to take decisions on safety and pollution prevention (5.2)</td></tr><tr><td>6 Resources and personnel</td><td>Manning (6.2, with reference to A.1047(27)) · working language</td></tr><tr><td>7 Shipboard operations</td><td>Plans and instructions for key operations</td></tr><tr><td>8 Emergency preparedness</td><td>Identify potential emergencies, drills, response (8.1–8.3)</td></tr><tr><td>9 Reports and analysis</td><td>Non-conformities, accidents and hazardous occurrences</td></tr><tr><td>10 Maintenance</td><td>Inspections, records; critical equipment (10.3)</td></tr><tr><td>11 Documentation</td><td>Controlled documents; Safety Management Manual</td></tr><tr><td>12 Verification, review, evaluation</td><td>Internal audits at intervals of <b>no more than 12 months</b> (12.1); auditors independent of the area audited (12.5)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Internal audits (12.1) are normally at intervals of up to 12 months. The Code allows this to be exceeded by up to 3 months only in exceptional circumstances.</blockquote><h2>Part B — Certification and verification</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ism-certificates\"><figcaption>Certificate cycle: DOC and SMC</figcaption></figure><h3>Document of Compliance (DOC) — the Company</h3><ul><li>Valid up to 5 years</li><li>Valid only for the ship types listed on it</li><li>Annual verification within 3 months before or after the anniversary date</li><li>Withdrawn if annual verification is not requested, or if there is a major non-conformity</li></ul><h3>Safety Management Certificate (SMC) — the ship</h3><ul><li>Valid up to 5 years</li><li>At least one intermediate verification. If only one, it is between the 2nd and 3rd anniversary.</li><li>Endorsement possible for up to 5 months (13.13)</li><li>Extension of up to 3 months allowed to complete a voyage (13.14)</li></ul><h3>Interim certificates (chapter 14)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Certificate</th><th>Maximum validity</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interim DOC</td><td>12 months</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Interim SMC</td><td>6 months</td><td>Extendable by up to a further 6 months</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Conditions in 14.4 include an internal audit planned within 3 months</li></ul><h2>Verification (A.1118(30))</h2><ul><li>Types: interim, initial, annual (DOC), intermediate (SMC), renewal, additional</li><li>Initial verification needs an SMS operating at least 3 months ashore and on at least one ship of each type</li><li>Corrective actions for non-conformities should not normally exceed 3 months</li><li>Major non-conformities are handled per MSC/Circ.1059-MEPC/Circ.401</li><li>Auditors: at least 5 years' experience and at least 4 training audits</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Accident origins of the Code (Herald of Free Enterprise, Scandinavian Star)</li><li>Reported 2026 IMO III 12 revision of ISM implementation guidelines</li><li>Statement that a 2010 amendment emphasized risk assessment and near-miss reporting</li><li>Examples of \"critical equipment\" and statements on port State control detention patterns</li><li>Exemption of government ships</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — ISM Code page: https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/humanelement/pages/ISMCode.aspx</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.428(98): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Facilitation/Documents/RESOLUTION MSC.428 -98 -.pdf</li><li>Deutsche Flagge — ISM Code amendment status</li><li>EMSA — consolidated ISM Code text (2018)</li><li>IMO — Resolution A.1118(30), Revised Guidelines for Administrations</li><li>Steamship Mutual — ISM application dates: https://www.steamshipmutual.com/publications/articles/ism_deadline0602</li><li>shipinspection.eu — SOLAS chapter IX application: https://shipinspection.eu/which-ships-does-solas-chapter-ix-apply-to/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-isps-code","v":27931084,"title":"ISPS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["isps","security","solas-xi-2","ssp","imo","code","core"],"body":"<h1>ISPS Code — International Ship and Port Facility Security Code</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>International Code for the Security of Ships and of Port Facilities</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>December 2002 (Conference on Maritime Security)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jul 2004</td></tr><tr><td>Legal basis</td><td>SOLAS chapter XI-2 (regulation XI-2/2)</td></tr><tr><td>Part A</td><td><b>Mandatory</b></td></tr><tr><td>Part B</td><td><b>Recommendatory</b> guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Key people</td><td>CSO (Company Security Officer) · SSO (Ship Security Officer) · PFSO (Port Facility Security Officer)</td></tr><tr><td>Ship certificate</td><td>International Ship Security Certificate (ISSC), up to 5 years</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"isps-flow\"><figcaption>From assessment to certificate</figcaption></figure><h2>What the Code does</h2><ul><li>Creates an international framework of co-operation between Contracting Governments, ships and port facilities to detect security threats and take preventive measures</li><li>Only the SOLAS Contracting Governments are bound. IMO does not impose penalties or keep a blacklist; enforcement is by governments.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Part A is mandatory. Drills every 3 months and the 25% crew change rule are in <b>Part B</b> (guidance), so check what your flag State requires.</blockquote><h2>Application (Part A, 3)</h2><ul><li>Passenger ships, <b>cargo ships including high-speed craft of 500 GT and above</b>, and mobile offshore drilling units, on international voyages</li><li>Port facilities serving those ships</li><li><b>Not</b> covered: warships, naval auxiliaries, government ships on non-commercial service</li><li>Domestic voyages are left to each government</li></ul><h2>Security levels</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"isps-levels\"><figcaption>The three security levels</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning (Part A, 2.1)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Minimum appropriate protective security measures maintained at all times</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Additional protective measures for a period, because of a heightened risk of a security incident</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Further specific protective measures for a limited period, when an incident is probable or imminent</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Governments set the security levels (4.1), considering how credible, corroborated, specific or imminent the threat is and the possible consequences</li><li>Levels can move directly from 1 to 3. Level 3 should last only as long as the threat.</li><li>The ship acts upon the levels set by the Contracting Government (7.1)</li></ul><h3>Level 1 activities on all ships (Part A, 7.2)</h3><ul><li>Ensuring performance of all ship security duties</li><li>Controlling access to the ship</li><li>Controlling embarkation of persons and their effects</li><li>Monitoring restricted areas so only authorized persons enter</li><li>Monitoring deck areas and areas surrounding the ship</li><li>Supervising handling of cargo and ship's stores</li><li>Ensuring security communication is readily available</li></ul><p>At levels 2 and 3, the additional or further measures <b>specified in the ship security plan</b> are implemented for each of these activities (7.3, 7.4).</p><h2>Part A — Mandatory</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4 Governments</td><td>Set security levels · test approved plans to the extent they consider appropriate (4.4)</td></tr><tr><td>5 Declaration of Security (DoS)</td><td>Agreement between ship and port facility, completed when the government or a ship considers it necessary (5.1, 5.4) · signed and dated, states duration, security level(s) and contacts · in English, French, Spanish or a language common to the parties (5.5)</td></tr><tr><td>6 Company</td><td><b>Master's overriding authority</b> and responsibility on ship safety and security, and the right to request Company help (6.1) · Company supports CSO, master and SSO (6.2)</td></tr><tr><td>7 Ship security</td><td>Measures at each level</td></tr><tr><td>8 Ship Security Assessment (SSA)</td><td>CSO ensures it is done (8.1) · covers physical security, structural integrity, personnel protection systems, procedures, radio and telecom systems, other vulnerable areas (8.3) · report protected from unauthorized access (8.12)</td></tr><tr><td>9 Ship Security Plan (SSP)</td><td>Approved by the Administration (9.1) · provides for all three levels · protected from unauthorized access or disclosure (9.7)</td></tr><tr><td>10 Records</td><td>Kept on board at least the minimum period set by the Administration (10.1) · any format, protected from unauthorized access (10.2)</td></tr><tr><td>11 CSO</td><td>Company designates a CSO (11.1)</td></tr><tr><td>12 SSO</td><td>Designated on each ship (12.1) · regular security inspections, maintains the SSP, reports security incidents (12.2)</td></tr><tr><td>13 Training, drills, exercises</td><td>Drills at appropriate intervals (13.4)</td></tr><tr><td>14–18</td><td>Port facility security, PFSA, PFSP, PFSO</td></tr><tr><td>19 Verification and certification</td><td>See below</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What the SSP contains (Part A, 9.2 and 9.4)</h3><ul><li>Ship's security organizational structure and its relationships with the Company, port facilities, other ships and authorities</li><li>Communication systems for effective continuous communication</li><li>Basic measures for level 1, and additional measures for levels 2 and 3</li><li>Regular review or audit of the SSP, and reporting to Contracting Government contact points</li><li>Location of the SSAS activation points (9.4.17)</li><li>Procedures for using the SSAS: testing, activation, deactivation, resetting, limiting false alerts (9.4.18)</li></ul><h3>Records kept on board (Part A, 10.1)</h3><ul><li>Training, drills and exercises</li><li>Security threats and security incidents</li><li>Breaches of security</li><li>Changes in security level</li><li>Communications directly related to ship security</li><li>Internal audits and reviews of security activities</li><li>Periodic reviews of the SSA and the SSP</li><li>Implementation of plan amendments</li><li>Maintenance, calibration and testing of security equipment, including the SSAS</li></ul><h3>Confidentiality of the SSP (9.8)</h3><ul><li>Officers authorized by a Contracting Government generally <b>cannot inspect</b> the SSP</li><li>Limited access to specific sections only where there are clear grounds for non-compliance, and only with the consent of the government concerned or the master</li><li>Provisions on the SSAS are confidential unless the governments agree otherwise</li></ul><h2>Certification (Part A, 19)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"isps-cycle\"><figcaption>ISSC verification cycle</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ISSC validity</td><td>Period set by the Administration, <b>not more than 5 years</b> (19.3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Renewal verification</td><td>At intervals set by the Administration, not exceeding 5 years (19.1.1.2)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate verification</td><td>At least one. If only one, between the 2nd and 3rd anniversary (19.1.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>Interim ISSC</td><td>Valid 6 months or until the full certificate is issued, whichever is first. <b>Cannot be extended</b> (19.4.4)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Conditions for an Interim ISSC (19.4.2)</h3><ul><li>SSA completed</li><li>Copy of the SSP on board, submitted for approval and being implemented</li><li>SSAS fitted, if required</li><li>CSO has arranged drills, exercises and internal audits so the ship passes its verification within 6 months</li><li>Master, SSO and personnel with security duties know their duties and the plan, in a language they understand</li></ul><h2>Part B — Guidance (recommendatory)</h2><ul><li>Drills at least once every 3 months (B/13.6)</li><li>If more than 25 percent of personnel have changed, with people who have not drilled on that ship in the last 3 months, a drill within 1 week of the change (B/13.6)</li><li>Exercises at least once each calendar year, no more than 18 months apart (B/13.7)</li></ul><h2>Linked SOLAS XI-2 rules</h2><ul><li><b>XI-2/6 Ship Security Alert System (SSAS):</b> ship-to-shore alert to the authority designated by the flag. It raises <b>no alarm on board</b>. Activated from the bridge and at least one other location.</li><li><b>XI-2/8 Master's discretion:</b> if, in the master's professional judgement, safety and security requirements conflict, the master gives effect to those needed to maintain the safety of the ship. The master may take interim security measures and must promptly inform the Administration and, where relevant, the port State.</li><li><b>XI-2/9 Control and compliance:</b> port States may delay, detain, restrict operations, expel or refuse entry to non-compliant ships. Authorities must avoid undue delay.</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Rule that the ship's security level must be at least the port's</li><li>Records language and DoS retention periods</li><li>Part B guidance on pre-arrival information and the Continuous Synopsis Record</li><li>Cyber risk guidance links, USCG 33 CFR Part 104</li><li>SOLAS XI-1 (ship number, CSR) and V/19 (AIS) details</li><li>Wording of XI-2/5 and XI-2/10</li><li>The statement \"adopted 12 Dec 2002\" (source gives December 2002)</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>ISPS Code text (Part A and Part B): https://likumi.lv/ta/en/en/starptautiskie-ligumi/id/1506</li><li>IMO — Frequently asked questions on maritime security: https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/security/pages/faq.aspx</li><li>SOLAS XI-2 reg 8 text: https://shipinspection.eu/what-does-solas-say-about-resolving-conflicts-between-ship-safety-and-security-requirements/</li><li>EU Reg 725/2004 Annex II (ISPS Code): https://legislation.gov.uk/eur/2004/725/annex/II/2009-01-31</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-international-code-of-signals","v":49375399,"title":"International Code of Signals","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["signals","flags","morse","distress","medical","mayday","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>International Code of Signals — visual, sound and radio communications</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>27 Sep 1965</b>, IMCO Assembly Resolution <b>A.80(IV)</b> (4th session, on approval by the Maritime Safety Committee)</td></tr><tr><td>Effective</td><td>Resolution: <b>1 Jan 1968</b>, provided the Radio Conference had finalized the parts needing ITU agreement. The US edition says the <b>1969 edition took effect on 1 Apr 1969</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>Aim</td><td>Safe communication in situations related to the safety of navigation and of persons, <b>especially when language difficulties arise</b></td></tr><tr><td>Methods</td><td>Flag signalling, flashing light, sound, radiotelephony and radiotelegraphy, hand flags or arms, Morse</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>4 chapters, an appendix and indexes</td></tr><tr><td>Edition checked</td><td>US edition, Pub. 102, 2005 edition, corrected through Notice to Mariners 24/06</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ics-structure\"><figcaption>The four chapters</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The Code uses <b>complete-meaning signals</b>. A single letter or a short group of letters carries a whole message, so ships with no common language can still understand each other.</blockquote><h2>Recommendations in A.80(IV)</h2><ul><li>Governments should make carriage of the Code <b>mandatory</b> on ships required to carry radiotelephone or radiotelegraph installations under SOLAS 1960</li><li>Other ships with an operational need should be required to carry it</li><li>Knowledge of the <b>spelling tables</b> should be required of masters and navigating officers</li><li>The Maritime Safety Committee may incorporate amendments from future Administrative Radio Conferences</li><li>Governments are invited to send comments so the Code is kept up to date</li></ul><h2>History (preface)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ics-history\"><figcaption>A short history</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>1855–1857:</b> a British Board of Trade committee drafted the first International Code, with about <b>70,000 signals using eighteen flags</b></li><li><b>1887–1897:</b> revision, completed 1897. It did not hold up during World War I.</li><li><b>1927–1932:</b> the International Radiotelegraph Conference in Washington decided on a new revision, finished in 1930 and adopted at Madrid in <b>1932</b></li><li><b>1959–1965:</b> IMCO took over the Code's functions. The revision was completed in 1964 and adopted in <b>1965</b>.</li><li>The revised Code uses complete-meaning signals and dropped the old vocabulary method and the Geographical Section</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>1</b></td><td>Signalling instructions</td><td>Definitions, methods, general instructions, flag signalling, flashing light, sound, radiotelephony, hand flags or arms, Morse, phonetic tables, procedure signals</td></tr><tr><td><b>2</b></td><td>General Signal Code</td><td>10 sections (below)</td></tr><tr><td><b>3</b></td><td>Medical Signal Code</td><td>Requests for medical assistance, medical advice, tables of body regions, diseases and medicaments</td></tr><tr><td><b>4</b></td><td>Distress and lifesaving signals and radiotelephone procedures</td><td>14 distress signals, lifesaving signals, radiotelephone procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Appendix</td><td>US/Russia supplementary signals for naval vessels</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Chapter 2 sections</h3><p>1. Distress-Emergency</p><p>2. Casualties-Damages</p><p>3. Aids to Navigation-Hydrography</p><p>4. Maneuvers</p><p>5. Miscellaneous (cargo and ballast, crew, fishery, pilots, port and harbour)</p><p>6. Meteorology-Weather</p><p>7. Routing of Ships</p><p>8. Communications</p><p>9. International Health Regulations (pratique)</p><p>10. Tables of Complements</p><h2>Single-letter signals</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ics-letters\"><figcaption>Key single-letter signals</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>I have a diver down. Keep well clear at slow speed.</td></tr><tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>I am taking in, or discharging, or carrying dangerous goods.</td></tr><tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>Yes (affirmative).</td></tr><tr><td><b>D</b></td><td>Keep clear of me. I am maneuvering with difficulty.</td></tr><tr><td><b>E</b></td><td>I am altering my course to starboard.</td></tr><tr><td><b>F</b></td><td>I am disabled. Communicate with me.</td></tr><tr><td><b>G</b></td><td>I require a pilot. (Fishing vessels: hauling nets.)</td></tr><tr><td><b>H</b></td><td>I have a pilot on board.</td></tr><tr><td><b>I</b></td><td>I am altering my course to port.</td></tr><tr><td><b>J</b></td><td>I am on fire and have dangerous cargo on board. Keep well clear of me, or I am leaking dangerous cargo.</td></tr><tr><td><b>K</b></td><td>I wish to communicate with you.</td></tr><tr><td><b>L</b></td><td>You should stop your vessel instantly.</td></tr><tr><td><b>M</b></td><td>My vessel is stopped and making no way through the water.</td></tr><tr><td><b>N</b></td><td>No (negative).</td></tr><tr><td><b>O</b></td><td>Man overboard.</td></tr><tr><td><b>P</b></td><td>In harbour: all persons should report on board as the vessel is about to proceed to sea. (At sea, fishing vessels use it for a fouled net.)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Q</b></td><td>My vessel is \"healthy\" and I request free pratique.</td></tr><tr><td><b>S</b></td><td>I am operating astern propulsion.</td></tr><tr><td><b>T</b></td><td>Keep clear of me. I am engaged in pair trawling.</td></tr><tr><td><b>U</b></td><td>You are running into danger.</td></tr><tr><td><b>V</b></td><td>I require assistance.</td></tr><tr><td><b>W</b></td><td>I require medical assistance.</td></tr><tr><td><b>X</b></td><td>Stop carrying out your intentions and watch for my signals.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Y</b></td><td>I am dragging my anchor.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Z</b></td><td>I require a tug. (Fishing vessels: shooting nets.)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>R</b> is not given in the extract I checked. See Not included.</li><li><b>C, N and RQ</b> cannot be used with single-letter signals as marker signals.</li><li>Some single-letter signals are also sound signals under the collision regulations (marked with an asterisk in the Code)</li></ul><h2>Procedure signals (Chapter 1, Section 10)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>AR</b></td><td>Ending signal or end of transmission</td></tr><tr><td><b>AS</b></td><td>Waiting signal or period</td></tr><tr><td><b>CS</b></td><td>What is the name or identity signal of your vessel (or station)?</td></tr><tr><td><b>RPT</b></td><td>Repeat signal</td></tr><tr><td><b>OK</b></td><td>Acknowledging a correct repetition</td></tr><tr><td><b>C / N / RQ</b></td><td>Affirmative, negative, question markers</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How signals are built</h2><ul><li><b>Numerals:</b> numeral pennants in flag signalling, Morse numerals or spelled out in light or sound, and the Figure Spelling Table in voice</li><li><b>Decimal point:</b> shown with the answering pennant, <b>AAA</b>, or the word DECIMAL</li><li><b>Complements</b> qualify a basic signal. Example: <b>DY</b> reports a sinking, <b>DY 4</b> asks for the depth where the vessel sank.</li><li><b>Formats:</b> bearings and courses 3 figures · dates <b>D</b> + numerals · latitude <b>L</b> · longitude <b>G</b> · distance <b>R</b> · speed <b>S</b> (knots) or <b>V</b> (km/h) · time <b>T</b> (local) or <b>Z</b> (GMT)</li></ul><h2>Distress signals (Chapter 2, Section 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>NC</b></td><td>I am in distress and require immediate assistance</td></tr><tr><td><b>CC</b></td><td>I am (or vessel indicated is) in distress in lat... long... and require immediate assistance</td></tr><tr><td><b>DY</b></td><td>Vessel (name or identity) has sunk in lat... long...</td></tr><tr><td><b>GW</b></td><td>Man overboard. Please take action to pick him up.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter 4: distress and lifesaving signals</h2><ul><li><b>Section 1: Distress signals.</b> <b>14 distress signals</b>, including a gun or explosive signal fired about once a minute, orange smoke, flares, slowly raising and lowering outstretched arms, EPIRB signals, and the spoken word <b>MAYDAY</b> (signal 5). SOS in Morse is signal 4. The <b>radiotelegraph alarm is a series of twelve dashes</b>.</li><li><b>Section 2: Lifesaving signals.</b> Six parts: I landing signals for small boats, II shore lifesaving apparatus signals, III replies from lifesaving stations (\"You are seen, assistance will be given as soon as possible\"), IV air-to-surface signals from aircraft and ship responses, V surface-to-air signals, VI signals to survivors. Also medical transport emblems, a flashing blue light of <b>60 to 100 flashes per minute</b>, and the group <b>YYY</b> for radar and underwater identification.</li><li><b>Section 3: Radiotelephone procedures.</b> Prefixes <b>MAYDAY</b> (distress), <b>PAN</b> (urgency), <b>SECURITE</b> (safety). Table 1 phonetic alphabet and figure spelling, Table 2 position coding, Table 3 nature-of-distress and answer codes.</li></ul><h2>Medical Signal Code (Chapter 3)</h2><ul><li><b>Section 1:</b> explanation and the standard way to describe a case: patient description, previous health, localization of symptoms, general symptoms, particular symptoms. Instructions to doctors for diagnosis and dosing.</li><li><b>Section 2:</b> request for medical assistance (codes start with <b>MAA to MAP</b> for general information)</li><li><b>Section 3:</b> medical advice</li><li><b>Section 4:</b> tables M-1 body regions, M-2 common diseases, M-3 medicaments</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The meaning of letter <b>R</b> and the full text of the flag, light, sound and hand-flag instructions in Chapter 1. The source extract I read did not show them.</li><li>The flag designs and Morse codes for the letters</li><li>Whether a given ship must carry the Code today, because carriage rules sit in other instruments and I did not check them</li><li>Any 2005 or later IMO amendments, and the differences between the IMO English text and the US edition</li><li>Contents of Chapter 2 sections 2–9 beyond their headings, and the full lists in the Medical Code tables</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>International Code of Signals, Pub. 102, 2005 ed. (US Coast Guard National Maritime Center copy): https://www.dco.uscg.mil/Portals/9/NMC/pdfs/examinations/01_international_code_of_signals_pub_102_2005_ed.pdf</li><li>IMO Resolution A.80(IV): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.80(IV).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-lsa-code","v":186974560,"title":"LSA Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["lsa","life-saving","lifeboat","solas-iii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>LSA Code — International Life-Saving Appliance Code</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>International Life-Saving Appliance (LSA) Code</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>4 Jun 1996, MSC.48(66)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jul 1998</td></tr><tr><td>Role</td><td>Technical standards for life-saving appliances required by SOLAS chapter III</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>7 chapters (I General · II Personal LSA · III Visual signals · IV Survival craft · V Rescue boats · VI Launching and embarkation · VII Other LSA)</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendments in force</td><td>1 Jan 2026: MSC.535(107), MSC.554(108)</td></tr><tr><td>Test standard</td><td>Revised Recommendation on testing of LSA, MSC.81(70)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-structure\"><figcaption>The seven chapters of the LSA Code</figcaption></figure><h2>Amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-dates\"><figcaption>LSA Code amendments by entry into force</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Amends</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.48(66)</td><td>4 Jun 1996</td><td>1 Jul 1998</td><td>The Code itself</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.207(81)</td><td>18 May 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td><td>Chapter I 1.2.2 · chapter II (2.2 lifejackets completely revised)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.218(82)</td><td>8 Dec 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2008</td><td>Chapter I · chapter IV 4.1–4.7 · chapters V, VI, VII</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.272(85)</td><td>4 Dec 2008</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td><td>Chapter IV (4.4.2.2, 4.4.9.1, 4.7.2) · chapter V 5.1</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.293(87)</td><td>21 May 2010</td><td>1 Jan 2012</td><td>Chapter IV liferafts (4.2.2.1, 4.2.3.3, 4.3.3.3)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.320(89)</td><td>20 May 2011</td><td>1 Jan 2013</td><td>Chapter IV 4.4.7.6 (release gear; refers to MSC.1/Circ.1393)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.368(93)</td><td>22 May 2014</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td><td>Chapter II 2.2</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.425(98)</td><td>15 Jun 2017</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Chapter VI 6.1</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.459(101)</td><td>14 Jun 2019</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Chapter IV 4.4 · chapter VI 6.1</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.485(103)</td><td>13 May 2021</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Chapter IV 4.4</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.535(107)</td><td>8 Jun 2023</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>Chapter IV: new 4.6.6, 4.6.7 (ventilation)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.554(108)</td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>Chapter II 2.2.1.6.2 · chapter IV 4.4.7.6.8 and 4.4.7.6.17 · chapter VI 6.1.2.8 and 6.1.2.10</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>The 2026 changes in detail</h3><p><b>MSC.554(108)</b> — applies to appliances <b>installed on or after 1 Jan 2026</b></p><ul><li><b>Lifejackets (2.2.1.6.2):</b> must turn an unconscious, face-down person to a face-up position, nose and mouth clear of the water, in an average time not exceeding that of the reference test device (RTD) plus 1 s. No allowance for failing to do so in testing.</li><li><b>Release hooks (4.4.7.6.8):</b> a hook cannot support any load until fully reset. Handles, safety pins and indicators must not show a reset position until then. Danger signs at each hook station.</li><li><b>Lowering speed (6.1.2.8, 6.1.2.10):</b> minimum S = 0.4 + 0.02H, or 1.0 m/s, whichever is less (H = height in metres from davit head to waterline in the lightest sea-going condition). <b>Maximum 1.3 m/s</b>, although the Administration may accept a different maximum.</li></ul><p><b>MSC.535(107)</b> — totally enclosed lifeboats <b>installed on or after 1 Jan 2029</b></p><ul><li>Ventilation of at least <b>5 m³/h per person</b> the lifeboat may carry, for at least <b>24 hours</b></li><li>Operable from inside, no stagnant or unventilated pockets</li><li>Powered ventilation must not draw from the radio batteries. Engine-dependent systems need fuel for the engine endurance requirement.</li></ul><p><b>MSC.559(108)</b> (not an LSA Code amendment) — amends MSC.402(96), the maintenance and thorough examination requirements for lifeboats, rescue boats, launching appliances and release gear. In force 1 Jan 2026. Item \".10 ventilation system, where fitted\" is added to the annual thorough examination and operational test (6.2.3).</p><p><b>MSC.594(111)</b> (adopted 21 May 2026) amends the testing recommendation MSC.81(70) for free-fall lifeboat release systems tested under load without launching the boat (linked to LSA Code amendments MSC.589(111)). Test arrangements installed on or after 1 Jan 2031.</p><h2>Chapter I — General</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement (1.2)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stowage temperature</td><td>No damage from −30 °C to +65 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Personal appliances</td><td>Operational −15 °C to +40 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Sea water</td><td>Operational −1 °C to +30 °C</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter II — Personal life-saving appliances</h2><h3>2.1 Lifebuoys</h3><ul><li>Outer diameter at most 800 mm, inner diameter at least 400 mm</li><li>Mass at least 2.5 kg</li><li>Supports at least 14.5 kg of iron in fresh water for 24 h</li><li>Grabline at least 9.5 mm diameter, length at least 4 times the buoy's outside diameter</li><li>Self-igniting light: at least 2 cd, flashing 50 to 70 times a minute, at least 2 h of energy</li><li>Smoke signal: at least 15 min in calm water</li><li>Buoyant lifeline: at least 8 mm diameter, breaking strength at least 5 kN</li></ul><h3>2.2 Lifejackets</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Weight</th><th>Height</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Infant</td><td>under 15 kg</td><td>under 100 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Child</td><td>15 to under 43 kg</td><td>100 to under 155 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Adult</td><td>43 kg or more</td><td>155 cm or more</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>At least 75% of persons unfamiliar with the jacket must don it correctly within 1 min unaided</li><li>Jump from at least 4.5 m with the jacket held, and from at least 1 m with arms overhead</li><li>Buoyancy loss no more than 5% after 24 h in fresh water</li><li>Light: at least 0.75 cd for at least 8 h, with a manual switch. Whistle on a lanyard.</li></ul><h3>2.3 Immersion suits</h3><ul><li>Don unaided within <b>2 min</b></li><li>Survive a jump from at least 4.5 m · climb a vertical ladder at least 5 m long</li><li>Mouth at least 120 mm clear of the water</li><li>Turn from face-down to face-up in <b>no more than 5 s</b></li><li>Uninsulated: core temperature falls no more than 2 °C after 1 h in calm water at 5 °C</li><li>Insulated: no more than 2 °C after 6 h in calm circulating water at 0 °C to 2 °C</li></ul><h3>2.5 Thermal protective aids</h3><ul><li>Thermal conductance no more than 7,800 W/(m²·K)</li><li>Operating air temperature −30 °C to +20 °C</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The 5 s righting time belongs to immersion suits. Lifejackets are tested against a reference device (RTD + 1 s).</blockquote><h2>Chapter III — Visual signals</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3.1 Rocket parachute flare</td><td>Reaches at least 300 m · bright red, at least 30,000 cd · burns at least 40 s · descends at no more than 5 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>3.2 Hand flare</td><td>Bright red, at least 15,000 cd · burns at least 1 min · keeps burning after 10 s under 100 mm of water</td></tr><tr><td>3.3 Buoyant smoke signal</td><td>Smoke at least 3 min in calm water · keeps smoking at least 10 s under 100 mm of water</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter IV — Survival craft</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-craft\"><figcaption>Key survival craft figures</figcaption></figure><h3>4.1 Liferafts (general)</h3><ul><li>Withstand a drop from <b>18 m</b> (4.1.1.2) · survive a jump from 4.5 m onto it (4.1.1.3)</li><li>Capacity at least <b>6 persons</b> (4.1.2.1)</li><li>Maximum mass 185 kg, with exceptions (4.1.2.2)</li><li>Painter: at least 10 m plus the stowage-to-waterline distance, or 15 m, whichever is greater (4.1.3.2)</li><li>Float-free: weak link breaks at 2.2 ± 0.4 kN (4.1.6.2) · hydrostatic release unit releases at no more than 4 m depth (4.1.6.3.2)</li><li>Equipment: water 1.5 L per person, food at least 10,000 kJ per person · quoit with at least 30 m buoyant line</li></ul><h3>4.2 Inflatable liferafts</h3><ul><li>Main buoyancy chamber has at least <b>two separate compartments</b> (4.2.2.1)</li><li>Design load 82.5 kg per person</li><li>Inflation within 1 min at 18–20 °C, within 3 min at −30 °C (4.2.2.3)</li><li>Carrying capacity is the lowest of: main tube volume (m³) ÷ 0.096 · floor area (m²) ÷ 0.372 · persons of 82.5 kg who can sit comfortably (4.2.3)</li><li>Boarding ramp supports a 100 kg person · boarding ladder's lowest step at least 0.4 m below the light waterline</li></ul><h3>4.4 Lifeboats (general)</h3><ul><li>Boarding by full complement: <b>not more than 3 min</b> for cargo ship lifeboats (4.4.3.2), <b>not more than 10 min</b> for passenger ship lifeboats (4.4.3.1)</li><li>Engine starts at <b>−15 °C within 2 min</b> of beginning the start procedure (4.4.6.2)</li><li>Speed at least <b>6 knots</b> in calm water, fully loaded (4.4.6.8), with fuel for at least <b>24 h</b> at that speed</li><li>Painter device holds the lifeboat stable when towed by a ship making headway up to <b>5 knots</b> in calm water (4.4.7.7)</li><li>Release gear safety factors: load-bearing parts 6 · hydrostatic interlock 6 · operating cables 2.5</li><li>Equipment: water 3 L per person · food at least 10,000 kJ per person · 4 rocket parachute flares, 6 hand flares, 2 buoyant smoke signals · searchlight 2,500 cd for at least 3 h</li><li>Exterior light at least 4.3 cd for at least 12 h · interior light at least 0.5 cd for at least 12 h</li></ul><h3>Lifeboat types</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Key requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4.5 Partially enclosed</td><td>Rigid covers over at least 20% of the length from the stem and 20% from the aftermost part</td></tr><tr><td>4.6 Totally enclosed</td><td>Pressure inside no more than 20 hPa above or below outside while the engine runs, enclosure closed · stable when flooded, water at most 500 mm above the seat pan · impact protection against ship's side at not less than 3.5 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>4.7 Free-fall</td><td>Withstands a free-fall launch from at least 1.3 times the certified height · release system works from no load up to at least 200% of normal load, factor of safety 6</td></tr><tr><td>4.8 Self-contained air support</td><td>Engine runs at least 10 min with all openings closed · internal pressure not below outside and no more than 20 hPa above</td></tr><tr><td>4.9 Fire-protected</td><td>Protects occupants during a continuous oil fire for at least <b>8 min</b> while waterborne</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter V — Rescue boats</h2><ul><li>Rescue boats tow the largest liferaft at not less than <b>2 knots</b> (5.1.1.7)</li><li>Speed at least 6 knots for at least 4 h, fully loaded (5.1.1.6)</li><li><b>Fast rescue boats</b> (5.1.4): hull length at least 6 m and no more than 8.5 m · at least <b>20 knots</b> in calm water with a 3-person crew, at least <b>8 knots</b> fully loaded · run at least 4 h</li><li>Inflated boats: buoyancy at least 0.17 m³ per person at 82.5 kg per person</li></ul><h2>Chapter VI — Launching and embarkation appliances</h2><ul><li>Lifeboat launching appliances safe with 10° trim and 20° list either way (6.1.1.1)</li><li>Lowering speed: see MSC.554(108) above</li><li>Powered winch hoists a rescue boat at not less than 0.3 m/s (6.1.1.9)</li><li>Fast rescue boat: lowering with full complement at no more than 1 m/s, hoisting with 6 persons and full equipment at not less than 0.8 m/s (6.1.7.5)</li></ul><h2>Chapter VII — Other life-saving appliances</h2><ul><li><b>7.1 Line-throwing appliance:</b> at least <b>4 projectiles</b>, each able to carry the line at least <b>230 m</b> in calm weather · at least 4 lines, each with breaking strength at least 2 kN</li><li><b>7.2 General alarm:</b> alarm tone <b>80 dB(A)</b> and at least <b>10 dB(A)</b> above ambient noise</li><li><b>Public address:</b> 75 dB(A) in interior spaces, 80 dB(A) in exterior spaces</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Old statements that the Code was made mandatory by \"SOLAS III/3.10\"</li><li>Old note: \"existing release hooks evaluated or replaced by first dry-dock after 1 Jul 2014\"</li><li>Old note: \"MSC.402(96) in force 1 Jan 2020\" and the weekly/monthly/annual/5-year service intervals</li><li>Old claim: \"LSA amendments adopted at MSC 111 for simulated free-fall launching, in force 1 Jan 2028\" (the verified item is MSC.594(111) on the testing recommendation)</li><li>Rescue boat length 3.8–8.5 m and \"5 seated plus 1 stretcher\"</li><li>Anti-exposure suits for rescue boat crews</li><li>Liferaft servicing intervals (12 months, up to 17 months)</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>LSA Code text (2017 edition): https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.lsa.2017.pdf</li><li>Deutsche Flagge — LSA Code amendment status: https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/iii/lsa-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.554(108): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution%20MSC.554(108).pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.535(107): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/MSC.535(107).pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.559(108): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution%20MSC.559(108).pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.594(111): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.594(111).pdf</li><li>UK MCA — MGN 714 (LSA Code amendments): https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-714-mf-adoption-of-amendments-to-the-life-saving-appliances-lsa-code/mgn-714-mf-adoption-of-amendments-to-the-life-saving-appliances-lsa-code</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-mass-code","v":134543442,"title":"MASS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["mass","autonomous","remote-operation","roc","cyber","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>MASS Code — International Code of Safety for Maritime Autonomous Surface Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Resolution</td><td>MSC.595(111), adopted 21 May 2026 at MSC 111</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td><b>Non-mandatory</b> (1.2) but designed to ease a later move to mandatory</td></tr><tr><td>Effective</td><td>Governments invited to give effect to it from <b>1 Jul 2026</b></td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td><b>Cargo ships</b> under SOLAS chapter I, and their Remote Operations Centres (ROCs)</td></tr><tr><td>Approach</td><td>Supplementary, holistic, goal-based, technology neutral</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Part I Introduction · Part II Principles · Part III Goals, functional requirements, expected performances · Appendix and 2 annexes</td></tr><tr><td>Certificates</td><td>MASS Safety Certificate · MASS ROC Certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory Code</td><td>Expected entry into force 1 Jan 2032</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mass-structure\"><figcaption>Structure of the MASS Code</figcaption></figure><h2>What the Code does</h2><ul><li>Gives an international framework for autonomous or remote operation of key ship functions and systems, for safe, secure and environmentally sound operation (1.1)</li><li>Objectives (1.3): reach the safety and security level expected of a conventional ship · let ships coexist safely · avoid unintended regulatory barriers · require <b>human oversight and control</b> of MASS operations and functions</li><li>It is <b>supplementary</b>. It applies where the Administration judges compliance with other instruments impracticable or insufficient.</li><li>Enhanced automation alone does not make a ship a MASS (Preamble)</li><li>Governments are invited to apply it, as far as practicable, to MASS under 500 GT</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The master keeps overall responsibility, wherever the master is. One master is responsible for a MASS at any given time.</blockquote><h2>Application (Chapter 2)</h2><ul><li>Cargo ships under SOLAS chapter I, including their ROCs</li><li><b>Excluded:</b> cargo high-speed craft under SOLAS chapter X · warships, naval auxiliaries, government ships on non-commercial service</li></ul><h2>Timeline</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mass-timeline\"><figcaption>From adoption to a mandatory code</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>21 May 2026</td><td>MSC.595(111) adopted (MSC 111, 13–22 May 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>1 Jul 2026</td><td>Code takes effect (non-mandatory).</td></tr><tr><td>10 Sep 2026</td><td>Resolution re-issued with the adoption date added to its title</td></tr><tr><td>Dec 2026</td><td>MSC 112 to re-establish a working group on an Experience-Building Phase framework</td></tr><tr><td>2028</td><td>Development of the mandatory Code and consideration of a new SOLAS chapter</td></tr><tr><td>By 1 Jul 2030</td><td>Expected adoption of the first mandatory Code</td></tr><tr><td>1 Jan 2032</td><td>Expected entry into force (may be revisited)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Key definitions (Chapter 4)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MASS (4.19)</td><td>A ship which, to a varying degree, can operate independently of human interaction</td></tr><tr><td>MASS function (4.20)</td><td>A task or group of tasks, duties and responsibilities needed for the MASS's operation</td></tr><tr><td>Autonomous (4.2)</td><td>Designed and verified to determine control actions without human intervention under specified conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Remote operation (4.30)</td><td>Operating functions and systems from outside the MASS</td></tr><tr><td>ROC (4.31)</td><td>A location remote from the MASS from which some or all of its functions can be operated</td></tr><tr><td>Remote operator (4.32)</td><td>A qualified person employed or engaged to operate some or all MASS functions from a ROC</td></tr><tr><td>Operational Design Domain, ODD (4.26)</td><td>The design range within which a system can operate as intended</td></tr><tr><td>Operational Envelope, OE (4.27)</td><td>The MASS's operational capabilities and limitations</td></tr><tr><td>Fallback state (4.12)</td><td>A predefined state entered when the MASS cannot stay within its OE</td></tr><tr><td>Override (4.28)</td><td>Taking over control of functions or systems under autonomous or remote operation</td></tr><tr><td>Third parties (4.40)</td><td>Parties engaging with the MASS or ROC without being involved in operating it, such as VTS, ports and pilots</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Part II — Principles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5 Surveys and certificates</td><td>MASS Safety Certificate with a MASS Record (5.1) · MASS ROC Certificate with a MASS ROC Record, one per MASS the ROC operates (5.3) · provisional certificates for testing (5.5)</td></tr><tr><td>6 Approval process</td><td>Five steps (6.1.6), see below</td></tr><tr><td>7 Risk assessment</td><td>Covers risks to persons on board, the environment and the MASS, at the level expected of a conventional ship (7.1) · done by people with relevant expertise (7.5) · mitigation in independent layers, including predefined fallback states (7.9)</td></tr><tr><td>8 Operational context</td><td>Concept of Operations (ConOps, 8.2) · OE and ODD (8.3) · fallback states (8.4) · contingency plans (8.5) · modes of operation (8.6) · human oversight (8.7)</td></tr><tr><td>9, 10</td><td>System design · software principles</td></tr><tr><td>11 Management of safe operations</td><td>Supplements SOLAS and the ISM Code (11.2.1) · the SMS covers all foreseeable modes including ROC roles, HMI, cyber risks, fallback actions · addresses personnel well-being, including stress, and keeps the master's overriding authority (11.2.3)</td></tr><tr><td>12 Security</td><td>Security at least equal to a conventional ship (12.3.2) · ISPS Code applies to the MASS and any ROC (5.8) · compromised ROC: isolate communications, go to a fallback state (12.4.3)</td></tr><tr><td>13, 15</td><td>Alert management · maintenance</td></tr><tr><td>14 Manning, training, watchkeeping</td><td>See below</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Certificates</h3><ul><li>Issued by the Administration or a recognized organization after initial or renewal survey</li><li>Survey timing and endorsements are harmonized with SOLAS certificates under HSSC 2025</li><li>A certificate ceases to be valid if required surveys are not completed on time, it is not endorsed, or on transfer to another flag (5.6.1)</li><li>The text does not state a fixed certificate duration</li><li>Alternative ROC management: a ROC safety management system (ROCSMS), a ROM Document of Compliance and a ROC Management Certificate (5.7.5, 11.3)</li></ul><h3>Approval process (Chapter 6, Annex 1)</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mass-approval\"><figcaption>The five approval steps</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The Administration assesses the MASS with the <b>Submitter</b>, the entity seeking approval (6.1.1)</li><li>Follows the approach of MSC.1/Circ.1455 on alternatives and equivalents (6.1.2)</li><li>The ConOps is the base document at each step (6.1.3)</li><li>Changes to assumptions, systems or the ConOps require repeating the affected part of the risk assessment (6.4.2)</li><li>Simulation does not replace full-scale testing (Annex 1)</li></ul><h3>Master, manning and watchkeeping (Chapters 8 and 14)</h3><ul><li>A human master is responsible for a MASS regardless of mode of operation and must have means to intervene (8.7.3)</li><li>The master may be on board or at a ROC. <b>If crew or other persons are on board, the master must be physically on board</b> (14.2.1.11–12).</li><li>Only one master is responsible at any given time (14.2.1.14)</li><li>Remote operators must be qualified to at least the STCW standard (14.2.2)</li><li>Manning accounts for fatigue and provides sufficient rest under STCW and IMO safe manning principles. No fixed hours are set; the Code refers to MSC.1/Circ.1598.</li><li>Remote operators need additional remote-operations training. Crew and remote operators drill together on emergencies and system failures (14.4).</li></ul><h2>Part III — Goals, functional requirements, expected performances</h2><ul><li>A MASS meets a functional requirement by meeting all its expected performances (EPs), or by confirming relevant arrangements against applicable IMO instruments with the rest meeting the EPs</li><li>Not every Part III chapter applies to every MASS. Chapters may be waived by agreement with the Administration, depending on the ConOps.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>16</td><td>Safety of navigation</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Connectivity</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Remote operations</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Structure, subdivision, stability, watertight integrity</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Fire protection, detection and extinction</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Search and rescue</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Carriage of cargoes</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Anchoring, towing and mooring</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Machinery and electrical installations</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Connectivity (17.2)</h3><ul><li>Keep full connectivity capacity even if a single system fails (17.2.5)</li><li>Redundancy based on the risk assessment (17.2.6)</li><li>Data categorized and prioritized, so higher-priority data takes precedence when capacity drops (17.2.7)</li><li>Connectivity requirements for each voyage phase and mode (17.2.8)</li><li>Monitoring, with measures for disconnection or degradation, including entering a <b>fallback state</b> (17.2.9)</li><li>Data integrity and authenticated sources (17.2.10)</li></ul><h3>Remote operations (18.2)</h3><ul><li>ROC needs a suitable location, secure facilities, reliable connectivity, access to certificates, recovery arrangements, verified systems, enough qualified personnel and tamper-proof data storage (18.2.2)</li><li>Operators can keep a watch, exchange information, take the decisions needed for safe operation (including supporting abandonment), and detect deviations from the OE (18.2.4)</li><li><b>Handover</b> (18.2.5): safe and secure · information synchronized · control never exercised at several locations at once · current control location shown at the ROC and on board · control not lost during transfer</li></ul><h3>Search and rescue (21.2)</h3><ul><li>The master meets SOLAS duties to assist persons in distress (21.2.1)</li><li>The MASS and its ROC need a ship-specific plan and procedures for giving assistance (21.2.2)</li><li>Rescue equipment must be usable safely without crew present (21.2.3)</li><li>Retrieved persons are accommodated safely until landed, with information and a means to communicate with a ROC (21.2.4)</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Old \"degrees of autonomy 1 to 4\" list. This is from the IMO scoping exercise and is <b>not part of the Code</b>.</li><li>\"Regulatory scoping exercise 2017–2021 (MSC 98–103)\" details</li><li>Old statement \"not passenger ships\". The Code applies to <b>cargo ships</b> and excludes cargo high-speed craft, warships, naval auxiliaries and government non-commercial ships.</li><li>Chapter 16 content on lookout and COLREG compliance (the excerpt checked had only a footnote)</li><li>Chapters 19, 20, 22, 23, 24 content beyond titles</li><li>Appendix certificate forms</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.595(111), MASS Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.595(111).pdf</li><li>IMO press briefing — MASS Code adopted: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/imo-adopts-mass-code.aspx</li><li>DNV — IMO MSC 111: new MASS Code adopted: https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/imo-mcs-111-new-mass-code-adopted/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-modu-code-2009","v":38005670,"title":"MODU Code 2009","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["modu","offshore","drilling","rig","safety","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>MODU Code 2009 — Code for the Construction and Equipment of Mobile Offshore Drilling Units</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>2 Dec 2009</b>, IMO Assembly Resolution <b>A.1023(26)</b> (26th session, on MSC 86 recommendation)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>MODUs whose keels are laid, or which are at a similar stage of construction, on or after <b>1 Jan 2012</b></td></tr><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>1989 MODU Code (A.649(16)), which had replaced the 1979 Code (A.414(XI))</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>A recommendation to governments. Governments are asked to give effect to it and to consider it <b>equivalent, for MODUs, to the technical requirements of SOLAS and Load Lines</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Mobile Offshore Drilling Unit Safety Certificate (2009), valid for up to <b>5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>14 chapters in the original text, plus the model certificate. The 2024 edition also lists a Chapter 15, \"Special measures to enhance safety\".</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"modu-structure\"><figcaption>The 14 chapters of the 2009 MODU Code</figcaption></figure><h2>Definition and aim</h2><ul><li><b>MODU:</b> a vessel capable of drilling for resources beneath the seabed, such as hydrocarbons, sulphur or salt (1.3.40)</li><li>The Code aims at safety equivalent to SOLAS and the 1988 Load Lines Protocol for conventional ships</li><li>Existing units that comply may be certified, and are not barred only for non-conformity (preamble)</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Construction, strength and materials</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Subdivision, stability and freeboard</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Machinery installations for all types of units</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Electrical installations for all types of units</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Machinery and electrical installations in hazardous areas</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Machinery and electrical installations for self-propelled units</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Periodically unattended machinery spaces</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Fire safety</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Life-saving appliances and equipment</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Radiocommunication and navigation</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Lifting devices, personnel and pilot transfer</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Helicopter facilities</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Operations</td></tr><tr><td>Appendix</td><td>Model form of the MODU Safety Certificate (2009)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Surveys and certificate (Chapter 1)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"modu-surveys\"><figcaption>Survey cycle for a MODU</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Survey</th><th>Timing (1.6.1)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Initial</td><td>Before the unit enters service or first certification</td></tr><tr><td>Renewal</td><td>At intervals not exceeding <b>5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate</td><td>Within 3 months of the 2nd or 3rd anniversary date</td></tr><tr><td>Annual</td><td>Within 3 months of each anniversary date</td></tr><tr><td>Dry-dock</td><td>At least <b>2 in any 5-year period</b>, with no interval over <b>36 months</b></td></tr><tr><td>Radio</td><td>Under section 11.9</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Certificate is issued after an initial or renewal survey (1.6.7). The Administration keeps full responsibility.</li><li>A Contracting Government may issue it at the Administration's request (1.6.9)</li><li>Validity set by the Administration, <b>not more than 5 years</b> (1.6.11.1)</li><li>The certificate ceases to be valid if required surveys are not done, it is not endorsed, or the unit changes flag (1.6.11.8)</li><li>Without a valid certificate the Code's privileges cannot be claimed (1.6.12)</li></ul><h3>Equivalents and exemptions</h3><ul><li><b>Equivalents (1.5):</b> an alternative design, material, appliance or arrangement may be accepted if at least as effective. The Administration reports it to IMO.</li><li><b>Exemptions (1.4):</b> for units with novel features that the Code would impede, if safety is adequate. Listed on the certificate (1.6.8) and reported to IMO with reasons.</li></ul><h2>Key figures in the original 2009 text</h2><p>These are from the 2009 text. Check the amendments below for units built from 2020.</p><h3>Machinery and electrical (Chapters 4–7)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Static inclination, machinery (4.1.4)</td><td>Column-stabilized <b>15°</b>; self-elevating <b>10°</b>; surface <b>15°</b> either way plus up to <b>5°</b> trim</td></tr><tr><td>Electrical design inclination (5.4.15)</td><td>Column-stabilized <b>25°</b>; self-elevating <b>15°</b>; surface <b>22.5°</b> longitudinal and <b>10°</b> transverse</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamic angles, self-propelled (7.1.3)</td><td>Column-stabilized <b>22.5°</b>; self-elevating <b>15°</b>; surface <b>22.5°</b> roll with <b>7.5°</b> pitch</td></tr><tr><td>Main power (5.3.1)</td><td>At least <b>2</b> generating sets</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency lighting (5.4.6)</td><td><b>18 h</b>; marking lights <b>4 days</b></td></tr><tr><td>Emergency generator (5.4.8.3)</td><td>Starts and loads within <b>45 s</b>, or a transitional battery source is needed</td></tr><tr><td>Transitional battery (5.4.10)</td><td>At least <b>half an hour</b></td></tr><tr><td>Fuel oil service tanks (4.8.6)</td><td><b>2</b> per fuel type, each at least <b>8 h</b> at rated load</td></tr><tr><td>Ballast, column-stabilized (4.10.2)</td><td>Maximum operating draught to severe storm draught within <b>3 h</b></td></tr><tr><td>Ballast pumps (4.10.3)</td><td>At least <b>2</b> independent pumps</td></tr><tr><td>Steering, main (7.5.3)</td><td>Rudder <b>35°</b> each side, 35° to 30° opposite side in not more than <b>28 s</b></td></tr><tr><td>Steering, auxiliary (7.5.7)</td><td><b>15°</b> each side in not more than <b>60 s</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Hazardous areas (Chapter 6)</h3><ul><li><b>Zones 0, 1 and 2</b> by likelihood of ignitable gas or vapour (6.1)</li><li>Hazardous mud processing spaces ventilated at <b>12 air changes per hour</b> (6.4.1)</li><li>Temperature classes from <b>T1 (450 °C)</b> to <b>T6 (85 °C)</b> (6.6.7 table)</li><li>Hazardous drilling well and mud areas need at least <b>Group IIA, class T3</b> equipment (6.6.7)</li></ul><h3>Fire safety (Chapter 9)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire pumps (9.7.1)</td><td>At least <b>2</b> independently driven power pumps drawing directly from the sea</td></tr><tr><td>Pump location (9.7.3)</td><td>A fire in one space must not disable both pumps</td></tr><tr><td>Pump output (9.7.5)</td><td><b>2 jets</b> while keeping at least <b>0.35 N/mm²</b> at any hydrant</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery of <b>750 kW</b> or more (9.8.2)</td><td>Fixed extinguishing system and portable foam extinguishers</td></tr><tr><td>Fire-fighter's outfits (9.13.1)</td><td>At least <b>2</b>, with oxygen and flammable vapour instruments</td></tr><tr><td>H₂S alarm (9.12.1)</td><td>If unanswered within <b>2 min</b>, it triggers the toxic gas alarm and helideck status light</td></tr><tr><td>Helideck (9.16.4)</td><td>At least 2 dry powder extinguishers totalling <b>45 kg</b> or more, and foam at <b>6 l/m²</b> for at least <b>5 min</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Life-saving appliances (Chapter 10)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lifeboats (10.3.1)</td><td>Each side holds the <b>total persons on board</b></td></tr><tr><td>Liferafts (10.3.2)</td><td>Hold the total persons on board</td></tr><tr><td>Self-elevating and column-stabilized units (10.3.4–10.3.5)</td><td>Lifeboats in at least <b>2 widely separated locations</b>, plus liferafts for all</td></tr><tr><td>Survival craft height (10.6.2)</td><td>At least <b>2 m</b> above the waterline in the limiting damaged condition</td></tr><tr><td>Launch preparation (10.6.1.3)</td><td><b>2 crew</b> in under <b>5 min</b></td></tr><tr><td>Lifeboat launch (10.7.8)</td><td>Full complement within <b>10 min</b> of the abandon signal</td></tr><tr><td>Rescue boat (10.8–10.9)</td><td>At least one, ready to launch within <b>5 min</b></td></tr><tr><td>Lifejackets (10.11.1)</td><td>One per person plus extras for duty stations</td></tr><tr><td>Immersion suits (10.12.1)</td><td>One per person plus extras for duty and remote craft positions</td></tr><tr><td>Lifebuoys (10.13)</td><td>From <b>8</b> (under 100 m) to <b>14</b> (200 m and over). At least half with self-igniting lights, at least 2 with smoke signals.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"modu-timeline\"><figcaption>From 2009 to 2024</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Adopted</th><th>Effective</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A.1023(26), the Code</td><td>2 Dec 2009</td><td>1 Jan 2012</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.359(92)</td><td>21 Jun 2013</td><td>1 Jan 2015</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.384(94)</td><td>21 Nov 2014</td><td>1 Jul 2016</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.387(94)</td><td>18 Nov 2014</td><td>18 Nov 2014</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.407(96)</td><td>19 May 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2020, for units built on or after that date</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.435(98)</td><td>9 Jun 2017</td><td>1 Jan 2020, for units built on or after that date</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.506(105)</td><td>28 Apr 2022</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What MSC.435(98) changed (units on or after 1 Jan 2020)</h3><ul><li><b>Ch 1:</b> new definition of <b>\"H\" class divisions</b>, tested against a hydrocarbon fire curve</li><li><b>Ch 6:</b> dynamic positioning shutdown logic revised so well integrity and station keeping are kept (6.5.2). Hazardous-area equipment repaired by qualified personnel and kept in a <b>register</b> (6.6.3).</li><li><b>Ch 9:</b> A-60 becomes <b>H-60</b> (9.2.4). Radiant heat at escape routes from a drill floor fire limited to <b>2.5 kW/m²</b> (9.4.5). New section 9.8 on fixed water spray or monitor protection for the drill floor.</li><li><b>Ch 10:</b> lifeboat design assumes <b>95 kg</b> average person and <b>265 mm</b> seat radius (10.3.7–10.3.8). At least one rescue boat on each unit, and <b>a lifeboat cannot be accepted as the rescue boat</b> (10.8).</li><li><b>Ch 14:</b> where a master is assigned, the master is in charge at all times (14.10.3). <b>Man overboard drills at least quarterly</b>, with quarterly lowering of a training liferaft for davit-launched liferafts (14.13). Records of the hazardous-area equipment register (14.16). New 14.17 limits portable or spark-producing equipment in zones 0, 1 and 2 unless certified or the area is free of flammable vapours.</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Intact stability area ratios, wind heeling criteria and damage survival rules in Chapter 3</li><li>Chapter 2 structure rules, Chapter 8 details, and the contents of Chapters 11–14 in the original text</li><li>The content of MSC.359(92), MSC.384(94), MSC.387(94), MSC.407(96) and MSC.506(105)</li><li>When and how Chapter 15 was added, and its content</li><li>Whether a given flag State or the coastal State treats the Code as mandatory</li><li>Any amendments adopted after the 2024 edition</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>A.1023(26), MODU Code 2009 text: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.a.1023.2009.pdf</li><li>MODU Code 2009, 2024 edition (US Coast Guard OCS NCOE), amendment table and contents: https://www.dco.uscg.mil/Portals/9/OCSNCOE/References/IMO/MODU-Codes/2009/2009-MODU-Code-2024Ed-Rev2022.09.07.pdf</li><li>MSC.435(98): https://www.dco.uscg.mil/Portals/9/OCSNCOE/References/IMO/MODU-Codes/2009/Amendments/MSC.435(98).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-nox-technical-code","v":136672310,"title":"NOx Technical Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["nox","nox-technical-code","eiapp","marpol-annex-vi","engines","tier-iii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>NOx Technical Code 2008 — Technical Code on Control of Emission of Nitrogen Oxides from Marine Diesel Engines</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Resolution</td><td>MEPC.177(58), adopted 10 Oct 2008</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td></tr><tr><td>Original Code</td><td>Adopted 26 Sep 1997 (Conference resolution 2). Revised by MEPC 53–58.</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Testing, survey and certification so engines meet the NOx limits of MARPOL Annex VI regulation 13 (1.1.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Diesel engines of <b>more than 130 kW</b>, installed or designed for installation on ships under Annex VI (1.2.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>EIAPP Certificate (engine)</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendment in force</td><td>MEPC.398(83), 1 Sep 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Chapters 1–7 and Appendices 1–8</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"nox-survey\"><figcaption>From engine test to certification</figcaption></figure><h2>NOx limits (Annex VI, regulation 13)</h2><p>Limits in g/kWh by rated engine speed n (rpm).</p><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"nox-tiers\"><figcaption>NOx limits at 720 rpm</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Ship constructed</th><th>n below 130</th><th>130 ≤ n below 2,000</th><th>n 2,000 or more</th><th>At 720 rpm</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>On or after 1 Jan 2000</td><td>17.0</td><td>45 · n^(−0.2)</td><td>9.8</td><td>12.1</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>On or after 1 Jan 2011</td><td>14.4</td><td>44 · n^(−0.23)</td><td>7.7</td><td>9.7</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>On or after 1 Jan 2016 (in NOx ECAs)</td><td>3.4</td><td>9 · n^(−0.2)</td><td>2.0</td><td>2.4</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>NOx controls apply to installed marine diesel engines over 130 kW, other than those used solely for emergency purposes, regardless of ship tonnage</li></ul><h3>Where Tier III applies (NOx Emission Control Areas)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>ECA</th><th>Tier III applies to ships constructed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>North American ECA and US Caribbean Sea ECA</td><td>On or after 1 Jan 2016</td></tr><tr><td>Baltic Sea ECA and North Sea ECA</td><td>On or after 1 Jan 2021</td></tr><tr><td>Canadian Arctic ECA (amendments effective 1 Mar 2026)</td><td>Keel laid on or after 1 Jan 2025 (per DNV)</td></tr><tr><td>Norwegian Sea ECA (amendments effective 1 Mar 2026)</td><td>Contract on or after 1 Mar 2026, or, with no contract, keel laid on or after 1 Sep 2026, or delivered on or after 1 Mar 2030 (per DNV)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Outside these areas, Tier II applies</li><li>The North-East Atlantic ECA was still pending at the time of checking (adjourned MEPC session 16–27 Nov 2026)</li></ul><h2>Amendments</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MEPC.177(58)</td><td>10 Oct 2008</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td><td>The 2008 Code</td></tr><tr><td>MEPC.251(66)</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Amends the Code (cited by IMO as the current amendment to the 2008 Code)</td></tr><tr><td>MEPC.398(83)</td><td>11 Apr 2025</td><td>1 Sep 2026</td><td>Chapter 7 split into 7.1 and 7.2 · new route for engines that have a substantial modification or are certified to a Tier they did not hold at installation · new Figure 4 in Appendix 2</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>MEPC.398(83) in detail</h3><ul><li><b>7.1</b> (existing renumbered 7.1.1–7.1.6): existing engines certified under regulation 13.7</li><li><b>7.2</b> (new 7.2.1–7.2.12): engines that undergo <b>substantial modification</b>, or are certified to a Tier they did not hold at installation<ul><li>Applies to individual engines or engine group parent engines, not engine families</li><li>Nameplate replaced if rated power or speed changes</li><li>The 100% load point may be set no lower than 85% of rated power. If it cannot be reached, testing is deferred.</li><li>The engine emission test plan is agreed with the Administration before testing</li></ul></li><li>Parties were invited to consider early application</li></ul><h2>Chapter 1 — General</h2><ul><li>1.1 Purpose · 1.2 Application · 1.3 Definitions (1.3.1–1.3.19)</li><li>Key definitions: substantial modification (1.3.2), components (1.3.3), <b>Technical File</b> (1.3.15), <b>Record Book of Engine Parameters</b> (1.3.16), <b>Approved Method</b> (1.3.17), Approved Method File (1.3.19)</li><li>Administrations may delegate functions to authorized organizations but keep full responsibility (1.2.2)</li></ul><h2>Chapter 2 — Surveys and certification</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Survey</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pre-certification survey, leading to the <b>EIAPP Certificate</b></td><td>2.1.1.1</td></tr><tr><td>Initial certification survey on board</td><td>2.1.1.2</td></tr><tr><td>Renewal, annual and intermediate surveys</td><td>2.1.1.3</td></tr><tr><td>Initial survey after a major conversion</td><td>2.1.1.4</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>2.2 Pre-certification: test-bed (2.2.1), engine family and engine group concepts (2.2.2), engines not pre-certified on a test bed (2.2.4)</li><li>2.3 Certification: IAPP Certificate and Technical File (2.3.4) · adjustments outside approved limits (2.3.9)</li><li>2.4 Technical File and on-board verification (2.4.1 minimum Technical File contents)</li></ul><h3>On-board verification methods (2.4.3)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Engine parameter check</td><td>6.2 (checklist: Appendix 7)</td></tr><tr><td>Simplified measurement</td><td>6.3</td></tr><tr><td>Direct measurement and monitoring</td><td>6.4</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter 3 — Test cycles and weighting factors</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"nox-cycles\"><figcaption>Test cycle weighting factors</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Cycle</th><th>Used for</th><th>Speed</th><th>Power points</th><th>Weights</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>E2</td><td>Constant-speed main propulsion, including diesel-electric and controllable-pitch propeller</td><td>100%</td><td>100, 75, 50, 25%</td><td>0.2, 0.5, 0.15, 0.15</td></tr><tr><td>E3</td><td>Propeller-law main and auxiliary engines</td><td>100, 91, 80, 63%</td><td>100, 75, 50, 25%</td><td>0.2, 0.5, 0.15, 0.15</td></tr><tr><td>D2</td><td>Constant-speed auxiliary engines</td><td>100%</td><td>100, 75, 50, 25, 10%</td><td>0.05, 0.25, 0.3, 0.3, 0.1</td></tr><tr><td>C1</td><td>Variable-speed, variable-load auxiliary engines not covered above</td><td>Rated, intermediate, idle</td><td>Torque 100, 75, 50, 10% (rated) · 100, 75, 50% (intermediate) · 0% (idle)</td><td>0.15, 0.15, 0.15, 0.1 · 0.1, 0.1, 0.1 · 0.15</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters 4–7</h2><ul><li><b>4 Serially manufactured engines:</b> engine family (4.3) and engine group (4.4) concepts, with a parent engine</li><li><b>5 Test-bed measurements</b></li><li><b>6 On-board demonstration of compliance</b><ul><li>6.3 Simplified measurement is limited to on-board confirmation tests and to renewal, annual and intermediate surveys (6.3.1.1). The allowance is 10% of the limit value, with a combined allowance with RM-grade fuel of no more than 15% (6.3.11).</li><li>6.4 Direct measurement and monitoring: NOx is the minimum species measured, CO₂ added if the carbon balance method is used (6.4.2) · load points within ±5% of rated power, or +0/−10% at 100% load (6.4.6.7) · data averaged over a stable 10-minute interval per load point (6.4.9.2) · data current within 30 days and kept on board at least 3 months (6.4.16.1)</li></ul></li><li><b>7 Certification of existing engines (regulation 13.7)</b> and, since 1 Sep 2026, substantial modification (7.2): application to the Administration (7.1) · the Approved Method File travels with the engine and includes an on-board verification description · a post-installation survey leads to amending the IAPP Certificate</li></ul><h2>Appendices</h2><p>1. Form of EIAPP Certificate</p><p>2. Flow charts for survey and certification</p><p>3. Specifications for analysers</p><p>4. Calibration of analytical and measurement instruments</p><p>5. Parent engine test report and test data</p><p>6. Calculation of exhaust gas mass flow (carbon-balance method)</p><p>7. Check list for an engine parameter check method</p><p>8. Implementation of the Direct Measurement and Monitoring method</p><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Old claim: \"Tier I for ships built 1 Jan 2000 to 31 Dec 2010\" as a closed period (IMO lists Tier I from 1 Jan 2000, then Tier II from 1 Jan 2011)</li><li>Old claim of the 1997 Code \"in force 19 May 2005\" with Annex VI</li><li>Old claim of an MEPC 84 (2026) amendment on \"multiple engine operational profiles\"</li><li>The exemption of engines used only for lifeboats and emergency equipment (IMO says engines used solely for emergency purposes)</li><li>Chapter 5 test-bed details</li><li>Chapter 7 criteria for pre-2000 engines (over 5,000 kW and 90 L per cylinder)</li><li>North-East Atlantic ECA Tier III dates (pending, and sources differ)</li><li>\"Tier change-over log\" and PSC checks</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>NOx Technical Code 2008 text: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.nox.2008.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MEPC.177(58): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MEPCDocuments/MEPC.177(58).pdf</li><li>IMO — Nitrogen oxides (NOx), regulation 13: https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/nitrogen-oxides-(nox)-%E2%80%93-regulation-13.aspx</li><li>IMO — Resolution MEPC.398(83): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/MARPOL PROT 1997 - (27) - RESOLUTION MEPC.398(83).pdf</li><li>UK MCA — MGN 717: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-717-mf-air-quality-amendments-to-mandatory-international-instruments/mgn-717-mf-air-quality-amendments-to-mandatory-international-instruments</li><li>DNV — New ECAs for the Canadian Arctic, Norwegian Sea and North-East Atlantic: https://www.dnv.com/news/2025/new-ecas-for-the-canadian-arctic-norwegian-sea-and-north-east-atlantic-ocean/</li><li>Clyde &amp; Co — MARPOL Annex VI key developments for 2026: https://www.clydeco.com/en/insights/2026/april/marpol-annex-vi-key-developments-for-2026</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-noise-code-2014","v":123226618,"title":"Noise Code 2014","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["noise","hearing-protection","accommodation","solas","ii-1-3-12","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>Noise Code 2014 — Code on Noise Levels on Board Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>30 Nov 2012</b>, MSC resolution <b>MSC.337(91)</b> (MSC 91)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>1 Jul 2014</b>, with SOLAS regulation <b>II-1/3-12</b></td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td><b>Mandatory under SOLAS</b>, but some parts are recommendatory or informative (1.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td><b>New ships of 1,600 GT and above</b> (1.3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Existing ships 1,600 GT+</td><td>Hazardous-noise, mitigation and PPE provisions may be applied as far as reasonable and practicable (1.3.2)</td></tr><tr><td>Ships under 1,600 GT</td><td>The Code may be applied to new ships, on the same basis (1.3.3)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>7 chapters and 4 appendices</td></tr><tr><td>Interpretations</td><td>MSC.1/Circ.1509, 5 Jun 2015</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"noise-limits\"><figcaption>Maximum noise levels by space (dB(A))</figcaption></figure><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Main content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td><td>Scope, purpose, application, definitions (including Lex,24h and potentially hazardous noise)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Measuring equipment</td><td>Class 1 integrating sound level meters (IEC 61672-1), octave filter sets (IEC 61260), calibrators (IEC 60942), wind screens</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Measurement</td><td>Sea-trial and port conditions, environment, procedures, exposure per ISO 9612:2009, calibration, uncertainty, measuring points</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Maximum levels</td><td>Limits in dB(A), survey report</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Exposure limits</td><td>Daily exposure, hearing protection zones, hearing conservation</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Acoustic insulation</td><td>Minimum Rw between accommodation spaces</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Hearing protection and warnings</td><td>Protectors, selection, entrance warning notices</td></tr><tr><td>Appendices</td><td>1 Format for noise survey report · 2 Noise in safety management systems · 3 Suggested methods of attenuating noise · 4 Simplified procedure for determining noise exposure</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Provisions that are recommendatory or informative: 1.3.2, 1.3.3, 3.4.2, 3.4.3, Chapter 5, sections 6.3 and 7.3, and Appendices 2 to 4.</p><h2>Maximum noise levels (4.2)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Space</th><th>1,600 to &lt; 10,000 GT</th><th>10,000 GT and above</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Machinery spaces</b></td><td>110</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery control rooms</td><td>75</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>Workshops (outside machinery spaces)</td><td>85</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td>Non-specified work spaces</td><td>85</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td><b>Navigating bridge and chartrooms</b></td><td>65</td><td>65</td></tr><tr><td>Look-out posts, including bridge wings and windows</td><td>70</td><td>70</td></tr><tr><td>Radio rooms (no audio signals)</td><td>60</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Radar rooms</td><td>65</td><td>65</td></tr><tr><td><b>Cabins and hospitals</b></td><td>60</td><td><b>55</b></td></tr><tr><td>Messrooms</td><td>65</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Recreation rooms</td><td>65</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Open recreation areas</td><td>75</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>Offices</td><td>65</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Galleys (food processing equipment off)</td><td>75</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>Serveries and pantries</td><td>75</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>Normally unoccupied spaces</td><td>90</td><td>90</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Values are dB(A) and are <b>maximum levels, not targets</b> (4.1.1)</li><li>Each space is assessed with the equivalent continuous level <b>LAeq</b> before the ship enters service (4.1.2)</li></ul><h2>Hearing protection and exposure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"noise-signs\"><figcaption>Entrance warning notices (7.4)</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Above 85 dB(A):</b> personnel should wear hearing protectors (4.1.3, 5.1.2)</li><li><b>Peak limit (5.1.4):</b> no crew member exposed unprotected to peaks above <b>135 dB(C)</b></li><li><b>Protector standard (7.2.2):</b> should reduce the level to <b>85 dB(A) or less</b>, selected by the HML method (ISO 4869-2). Noise-cancelling headsets qualify only if they perform like passive protectors when unpowered.</li><li><b>Warning notices (7.4)</b> at entrances to spaces above 85 dB(A):</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>80 to 85 dB(A)</td><td>High noise level, use hearing protectors</td></tr><tr><td>85 to 110 dB(A)</td><td>Dangerous noise, protectors mandatory</td></tr><tr><td>110 to 115 dB(A)</td><td>Short stay only, protectors mandatory</td></tr><tr><td>Above 115 dB(A)</td><td>No stay longer than <b>10 minutes</b>, protectors mandatory</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Exposure zones (5.3)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>No exposure above <b>120 dB(A)</b> or Lex(24) above <b>105 dB(A)</b>, even with protectors</td></tr><tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>Occasional exposure only, with protectors giving <b>25 to 35 dB(A)</b> attenuation</td></tr><tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>Occasional exposure only, protectors giving at least <b>25 dB(A)</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>D</b></td><td>Daily exposure allowed with protectors giving at least <b>25 dB(A)</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>E</b></td><td>Unprotected. Exposures under 8 hours should not exceed <b>85 dB(A)</b>. Longer stays: Lex(24) at or below <b>80 dB(A)</b>, with at least a third of each 24 hours below <b>75 dB(A)</b>.</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Alternative (5.4):</b> unprotected 24-hour equivalent at or below 80 dB(A). Protected daily exposure in high-noise spaces not more than <b>4 hours continuously or 8 hours total</b>.</li><li><b>Hearing conservation programme (5.5):</b> optional for spaces above 85 dB(A LAeq), with audiometric testing, training and records</li><li><b>SMS (5.1.3):</b> include a hearing protection policy, logged training and exposure limits</li></ul><h2>Measurement</h2><ul><li><b>Timing (3.1.1):</b> on completion of the ship or as soon as practicable after, in sea-trial and port conditions</li><li><b>Sea trial (3.3.2):</b> normal service speed and not less than <b>80% of MCR</b></li><li><b>LCeq and LCpeak</b> are also measured where LAeq exceeds 85 dB(A) (3.1.3)</li><li><b>Equipment (2.2.2):</b> calibrator and sound level meter <b>verified at least every two years</b> by a national standard laboratory or an ISO 17025 accredited laboratory. Meter calibrated with the calibrator <b>before and after</b> measurements (3.8).</li><li><b>Environment (3.5):</b> wind force <b>4</b> and wave height <b>1 m</b> should not be exceeded, or actual conditions are reported. Water depth less than 5 times the draught or nearby reflecting surfaces may affect readings and must be noted. Extraneous noise must not affect readings.</li><li><b>Exposure (3.7, 5.1.1):</b> determined per ISO 9612:2009, or by the simplified method in Appendix 4</li><li><b>Survey report (4.3):</b> one for each ship, with readings at each point marked on plans, <b>kept on board and accessible to the crew</b> (4.3.3), in the format of Appendix 1</li></ul><h2>Acoustic insulation (6.2.1)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"noise-rw\"><figcaption>Minimum Rw between spaces</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Partition</th><th>Minimum Rw</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cabin to cabin</td><td><b>35</b></td></tr><tr><td>Messrooms, recreation rooms, public spaces and entertainment areas to cabins and hospitals</td><td><b>45</b></td></tr><tr><td>Corridor to cabin</td><td><b>30</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cabin to cabin with communicating door</td><td><b>30</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The requirements apply to components within bulkheads, such as doors. A composite bulkhead is acceptable if each component meets the Rw, or if the combined Rw calculated from component areas and sound reduction indices is not below the requirement (MSC.1/Circ.1509 on 6.2.1).</li><li>Decks meet the requirements together with their coverings, tested as installed, but ceiling panels are excluded (MSC.1/Circ.1509).</li></ul><h2>Exclusions and flexibility</h2><ul><li><b>Not covered (1.3.4):</b> dynamically supported craft, high-speed craft, fishing vessels, pipe-laying barges, crane barges, MODUs, pleasure yachts not engaged in trade, warships and troopships, unpowered ships, pile drivers, dredgers</li><li><b>Dispensation (1.3.6):</b> by the Administration where compliance is documented as impossible despite reasonable measures. Cabins are excluded unless exceptional circumstances apply.</li><li><b>Short voyages (1.3.7):</b> 4.2.3 and 4.2.4 may apply only in port condition if rest periods are adequate</li><li><b>Passenger spaces (1.3.8):</b> not intended to apply, except work spaces within them</li><li><b>Major conversions (1.3.9):</b> changed areas meet the Code for new ships as far as the Administration finds reasonable</li><li><b>Out of scope (1.3.10):</b> wind, wave and ice noise, alarms and public address systems</li></ul><h2>Interpretations (MSC.1/Circ.1509, 5 Jun 2015)</h2><ul><li>Passenger spaces also used by crew, such as recreation rooms, count as \"other passenger spaces\" and are outside the Code. Adjacent crew cabin and hospital bulkheads and decks must still meet Chapter 6.</li><li>Navigating bridge wings include enclosed navigating bridge spaces</li><li>A bridge with radio equipment is still a navigating bridge (<b>65 dB(A)</b>). Radio rooms are separate rooms dedicated to radio messages.</li><li>A day-room and bedroom serving one occupant count as <b>one cabin</b></li><li>Bathrooms and toilets count as sources of airborne sound, not as cabins</li><li>During measurement keep air conditioning vents and cabin door grilles open unless they normally operate closed</li><li>The uncertainty provision (3.9) acknowledges uncertainty, it is <b>not an allowance</b></li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The exact definition of \"new ship\" under SOLAS II-1/3-12 and its contract or keel dates</li><li>The definitions of Lex,24h and potentially hazardous noise in Chapter 1</li><li>The contents of Appendices 1–4 apart from their titles</li><li>Any amendment to the Code after 2014 and any flag State additions</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>MSC.337(91) text: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.msc.337.2012.pdf</li><li>IMO Noise Code publication flyer: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/publications/Documents/Flyers/Flyers/I817E.PDF</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1509 (Traficom copy): https://www.traficom.fi/files/media/regulation/22609-MSC_1-Circ_1509_-_tulkinta-_The_Code_On_Noise_Levels_On_Board_Ships.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-polar-code","v":174870798,"title":"Polar Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["polar","arctic","antarctic","solas-xiv","marpol","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>Polar Code — International Code for Ships Operating in Polar Waters</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Safety part (Part I)</td><td>Adopted 21 Nov 2014, MSC.385(94)</td></tr><tr><td>Environmental part (Part II)</td><td>Adopted 15 May 2015, MEPC.264(68)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jan 2017</td></tr><tr><td>Legal basis</td><td>Mandatory under SOLAS (chapter XIV) and MARPOL Annexes I, II, IV and V</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Introduction · Part I-A safety (12 chapters) · Part I-B guidance · Part II-A pollution (5 chapters) · Part II-B guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Polar Ship Certificate (1.3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Manual</td><td>Polar Water Operational Manual (PWOM) carried on board (2.3.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendment in force</td><td>MSC.538(107), 1 Jan 2026</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"polar-structure\"><figcaption>Structure of the Polar Code</figcaption></figure><h2>Ship categories (Introduction, 2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"polar-categories\"><figcaption>Ship categories A, B and C</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Design</th><th>Structure (3.3.2)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>Polar waters in at least medium first-year ice, which may include old ice inclusions</td><td>Polar Class 1–5</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Not category A; polar waters in at least thin first-year ice, which may include old ice inclusions</td><td>Polar Class 6–7</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Open water, or ice conditions less severe than A and B</td><td>Approved against the ice types and concentrations in the area. Need not be ice-strengthened if the Administration judges the structure adequate.</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Polar Class is defined in 1.2.10, based on the IACS Unified Requirements</li><li>Category C cargo ships may receive the Polar Ship Certificate on documented verification, if no additional equipment or structural modification is needed (1.3.3)</li></ul><h2>Hazards the Code addresses (Introduction, 3.1)</h2><p>1. Ice · 2. Topside icing · 3. Low temperature · 4. Extended darkness or daylight · 5. High latitude · 6. Remoteness and incomplete hydrographic data · 7. Crew inexperience in polar operations · 8. Lack of suitable emergency response equipment · 9. Rapidly changing, severe weather · 10. Environmental sensitivity to harmful substances</p><h2>Key definitions (Part I-A, 1.2)</h2><ul><li><b>Polar Service Temperature (PST)</b> (1.2.11): set at least <b>10 °C below the lowest Mean Daily Low Temperature (MDLT)</b> (1.2.9) for the intended area and season</li><li><b>Polar waters:</b> the Arctic waters and Antarctic area defined by SOLAS regulations XIV/1.2 and XIV/1.3</li></ul><h2>Part I-A — Safety measures (mandatory)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Title</th><th>Highlights</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td><td>Polar Ship Certificate issued after initial or renewal survey (1.3.1, 1.3.2) · maximum expected time of rescue <b>never less than 5 days</b> (1.2.7)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>PWOM</td><td>Carried on board (2.3.1) · methodology for ice capabilities and limitations (2.3.2) · risk-based procedures for voyage planning, forecasts, equipment operation, low temperature, icing and sea ice (2.3.3)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ship structure</td><td>Materials suitable for the PST · ice-strengthened ships resist global and local ice loads (3.2)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Subdivision and stability</td><td>Icing allowances (4.3.1.1) · ice damage for category A and B ships (4.3.2)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Watertight and weathertight integrity</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Machinery installations</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Fire safety</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Life-saving appliances</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Safety of navigation</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Communication</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Voyage planning</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Manning and training</td><td>See below</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Chapter 4 — Icing and ice damage</h3><ul><li><b>Icing allowances (4.3.1.1):</b> 30 kg/m² on exposed weather decks and gangways · 7.5 kg/m² on the projected lateral area of each side above the waterplane · for discontinuous surfaces such as rails, booms and rigging, increase the continuous projected area by 5% and static moments by 10%</li><li><b>Ice damage (4.3.2)</b>, category A and B ships built on or after 1 Jan 2017: must survive flooding from ice penetration, with the SOLAS survival factor si equal to 1<ul><li>Longitudinal extent: 4.5% of the upper ice waterline length if centred forward of maximum breadth, otherwise 1.5%</li><li>Transverse extent: 760 mm</li><li>Vertical extent: the lesser of 20% of the upper ice waterline draught or the longitudinal extent</li></ul></li></ul><h3>Chapter 7 — Fire safety (7.3)</h3><ul><li>Isolating and pressure/vacuum valves in exposed locations protected from ice and kept accessible; two-way portable radios work at the PST</li><li>Fire pumps in compartments kept above freezing · exposed fire main sections can be isolated and drained · firefighter outfits stored in warm locations</li><li>Extinguishers in freezing locations work at the PST</li></ul><h3>Chapter 8 — Life-saving appliances</h3><ul><li>Where immersion suits are required, they must be the <b>insulated</b> type (8.2.3.1 / 8.3.3.1)</li><li>Passenger ships: a properly sized immersion suit or thermal protective aid for each person</li><li>Lifeboats partially or totally enclosed. Where abandonment onto ice or land is possible, personal and group survival equipment for <b>110%</b> of persons on board (8.3.3.3)</li><li>Adequate emergency rations for the maximum expected time of rescue (8.3.3.4)</li><li>Ships built on or after 1 Jan 2017: exposed escape routes must not hinder persons in polar clothing (8.3.1.2)</li></ul><h3>Chapter 9 — Safety of navigation (9.3)</h3><ul><li>Ice-strengthened ships built on or after 1 Jan 2017: <b>two independent echo-sounding devices</b>, or one with two separate independent transducers (9.3.2.1.1)</li><li><b>Two independent non-magnetic heading means</b>, both connected to main and emergency power (9.3.2.2.1)</li><li>Ships going above <b>80° latitude</b>: at least one GNSS compass or equivalent (9.3.2.2.2)</li><li>Two remotely rotatable searchlights, or another means to see ice in darkness, unless operating only in 24-hour daylight (9.3.3.1)</li><li>Icebreaker-escorted operations: a manually initiated flashing red light visible from astern to show when stopped (9.3.3.2)</li></ul><h3>Chapter 10 — Communication</h3><ul><li>Two-way ship-to-ship and ship-to-shore communication along intended routes, taking high-latitude limits into account (10.2.1.1 / 10.3.1.1)</li><li>Search and rescue communications include voice and data with rescue centres and voice with aircraft on 121.5 and 123.1 MHz (10.3.1.3)</li><li>Two-way voice and data with a Telemedical Assistance Service (10.3.1.4)</li><li>Rescue boats and lifeboats have devices for distress alerting, location and on-scene communication in low air temperatures (10.3.2.1)</li></ul><h3>Chapter 11 — Voyage planning</h3><p>The master considers (11.3): PWOM procedures · current ice and iceberg information · historical ice and temperature statistics · places of refuge · marine mammal areas · protected areas along the route · operation far from search and rescue capabilities (11.3.9)</p><h3>Chapter 12 — Manning and training</h3><ul><li>Masters, chief mates and watch officers qualified under STCW chapter V. Requirements vary by ice condition and ship type (12.3.1).</li><li>Open-water operations: basic polar training for tankers and passenger ships. Advanced training for masters and chief mates in \"other waters\".</li><li>The Administration may allow other qualified persons to meet the training requirements, with enough polar-trained personnel to cover all watches (12.3.2)</li><li>Every crew member is familiar with the PWOM procedures and equipment relevant to their duties (12.3.4)</li><li>STCW polar training became mandatory on 1 Jul 2018</li></ul><h2>Part II-A — Pollution prevention (mandatory)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"polar-discharge\"><figcaption>Discharge distances in Arctic waters</figcaption></figure><h3>Chapter 1 — Oil (MARPOL Annex I)</h3><ul><li><b>Arctic waters:</b> all discharge into the sea of oil or oily mixtures from any ship is prohibited (1.1.1). Clean or segregated ballast is exempt (1.1.2).</li><li>Category A and B ships built on or after 1 Jan 2017, total fuel capacity under 600 m³: fuel tanks at least <b>0.76 m</b> from the outer hull. Tanks of 30 m³ or less are exempt (1.2.1).</li><li>Category A and B non-tankers built on or after 1 Jan 2017: oil cargo tanks at least 0.76 m from the outer hull (1.2.2)</li><li>Sludge tanks and oily bilge water holding tanks at least 0.76 m from the outer hull, with the same 30 m³ exemption (1.2.4)</li><li>Tankers under 5,000 dwt: cargo tanks protected along the full length by double bottom and wing spaces (1.2.3)</li></ul><h3>Chapter 2 — Noxious liquid substances (MARPOL Annex II)</h3><ul><li>Discharge of noxious liquid substances (NLS), or mixtures containing them, is prohibited in <b>Arctic waters</b> (2.1.1)</li><li>Category A and B ships built on or after 1 Jan 2017 carrying NLS assigned ship type 3 in the IBC Code need Administration approval (2.1.3)</li></ul><h3>Chapter 4 — Sewage (MARPOL Annex IV)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Sewage</th><th>Discharge allowed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comminuted and disinfected</td><td>More than <b>3 nm</b> from any ice shelf or fast ice, and as far as possible from areas with ice concentration above 1/10 (4.2.1.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Untreated</td><td>More than <b>12 nm</b> from any ice shelf or fast ice, and as far as possible from areas above 1/10 (4.2.1.2)</td></tr><tr><td>Approved treatment plant</td><td>As far as possible from the nearest land, ice shelf, fast ice, or areas above 1/10 (4.2.1.3)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Category A and B ships, and all passenger ships, built on or after 1 Jan 2017: discharge prohibited unless through an approved treatment plant (4.2.2)</li></ul><h3>Chapter 5 — Garbage (MARPOL Annex V)</h3><ul><li><b>Arctic:</b> food waste only as far as possible from areas above 1/10 ice concentration, and never less than <b>12 nm</b> from the nearest land, ice shelf or fast ice (5.2.1.1)</li><li>Food waste comminuted or ground to pass screens with openings of at most <b>25 mm</b>, not mixed with other garbage (5.2.1.2)</li><li>Food waste must not be discharged onto ice (5.2.1.3) · animal carcass discharge prohibited (5.2.1.4)</li><li><b>Antarctic:</b> discharges under Annex V regulation 6.1 as far as possible from areas above 1/10 ice concentration and never less than 12 nm from the nearest fast ice (5.2.2.1)</li></ul><h2>Amendments and related rules</h2><h3>MSC.538(107) — Chapters 9-1 and 11-1</h3><ul><li>Adopted 8 Jun 2023, in force <b>1 Jan 2026</b>. Part I-B amendments take effect the same date.</li><li>Adds chapter <b>9-1</b> (safety of navigation) and <b>11-1</b> (voyage planning) for fishing vessels of 24 m and above, pleasure yachts of 300 GT and above not engaged in trade, and cargo ships of 300 GT but below 500 GT</li><li>Existing chapters 9 and 11 are limited to ships certified under SOLAS chapter I</li><li>Ships built on or after 1 Jan 2026: ice-strengthened ships need two independent echo sounders (or one with two independent transducers), and category A and B ships need enclosed or protected bridge wings</li><li>Chapter 9-1 includes: ice information display · navigation equipment that works in expected conditions · two means of illuminating ice in darkness · ice protection for sensors and antennas on ice-strengthened ships · two independent non-magnetic heading sources for ships of 500 GT and above · GNSS compass above 80° latitude</li></ul><h3>Other</h3><ul><li><b>STCW polar training:</b> minimum training adopted November 2016, mandatory from 1 Jul 2018</li><li><b>Arctic heavy fuel oil:</b> MARPOL Annex I regulation 43A (in force 1 Nov 2022) prohibits using or carrying HFO as fuel in Arctic waters from <b>1 Jul 2024</b></li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Old claim that existing ships comply by the first intermediate or renewal survey after 1 Jan 2018</li><li>POLARIS risk-indexing as part of the Code (not found in the text checked)</li><li>Radar for ice detection as a requirement</li><li>HFO waivers until 1 Jul 2029 and the Antarctic HFO ban since 2011</li><li>The compliance date of 1 Jan 2027 for existing non-SOLAS ships under MSC.538(107) (the resolution text sets no other build-date compliance dates)</li><li>Chapters 5 and 6 content, and the 2002 and 2009 guidelines history</li><li>Exact Arctic and Antarctic boundaries (defined in SOLAS XIV/1.2 and 1.3)</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.385(94), Polar Code Part I: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC 385 94.pdf</li><li>IMO — MEPC 68/21/Add.1 (Resolution MEPC.264(68), Part II): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/es/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Documents/Pages from MEPC 68-21-Add.1.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.538(107): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/MSC.538(107).pdf</li><li>IMO — Shipping in polar waters: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/polar-default.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ro-code","v":220160919,"title":"RO Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ro-code","recognized-organization","class","flag-state","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>RO Code — Code for Recognized Organizations</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>21 Jun 2013, MSC.349(92) · 17 May 2013, MEPC.237(65) (identical text)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jan 2015, with the related SOLAS, 1988 Load Lines Protocol and MARPOL amendments</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory parts</td><td>Parts 1 and 2, under SOLAS, the Load Lines Protocol and MARPOL Annexes I and II</td></tr><tr><td>Non-mandatory</td><td>Part 3 (oversight): recommended as a basis for national standards</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Preamble · Part 1 General (6) · Part 2 Requirements (8) · Part 3 Oversight (7) · Appendices 1–3</td></tr><tr><td>Who</td><td>Recognized Organizations (ROs) acting for flag States, and the flag States that authorize them</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ro-structure\"><figcaption>Structure of the RO Code</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The flag State may delegate, but must not authorize functions beyond the RO's capabilities. Oversight stays with the flag State.</blockquote><h2>What the Code does</h2><ul><li>The international standard and consolidated instrument of <b>minimum criteria for recognition and authorization</b> of ROs, plus guidelines for flag State oversight (Part 1, section 1)</li><li>Covers ROs seeking or holding recognition, and flag States that intend to recognize ROs (2.1)</li><li>Mandatory requirements for ROs and flag States (2.2); guidance for oversight (2.2.4)</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ro-flow\"><figcaption>Delegation and oversight</figcaption></figure><h2>Part 1 — General</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Purpose</td><td>Minimum criteria and consolidated standard</td></tr><tr><td>2 Scope</td><td>ROs and flag States</td></tr><tr><td>3 Contents</td><td>The three-part layout</td></tr><tr><td>4 Delegation of authority</td><td>A flag State may delegate authority to a recognized RO (4.1) · must not authorize functions beyond the RO's capabilities (4.2) · should cooperate with other flag States (4.3)</td></tr><tr><td>5 Communication of information</td><td>The flag State deposits its list of ROs and their delegated responsibilities with the IMO Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>6 References</td><td>IMO instruments, ISO 9000/9001, ISO/IEC 17020, ISO 19011, IACS QMSR</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Part 2 — Recognition and authorization requirements (mandatory)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Terms</td><td>RO, authorization, statutory certification and services, assessment, interested parties, location, site, Vertical Contract Audit (VCA)</td></tr><tr><td>2 General requirements</td><td>2.2 Rules published and maintained, with an English version · 2.3 <b>Independence</b>: no conflicts with judgement, no substantial dependence on a single commercial client · 2.4 Impartiality · 2.5 Integrity, under a Code of Ethics · 2.6 Competence · 2.7 Responsibility · 2.8 Transparency, including flag State reporting</td></tr><tr><td>3 Management and organization</td><td>Quality management system (QMS) · quality, safety and pollution prevention policy · quality manual · document and record control · designated management representative · communication, including cooperation between ROs on ship transfers · <b>management review at intervals of no more than 13 months</b> (3.10)</td></tr><tr><td>4 Resources</td><td>Personnel (4.2) · infrastructure · safe work environment</td></tr><tr><td>5 Statutory certification and services processes</td><td>Design and development of services · subcontracting (5.9) · calibration of monitoring equipment (5.10) · complaints (5.11) · appeals (5.12)</td></tr><tr><td>6 Performance measurement</td><td>Internal audits, <b>each location at least once every 3 years</b> (6.2) · annual Vertical Contract Audits (6.3) · non-conformity control · continual improvement</td></tr><tr><td>7 QMS certification</td><td>QMS based on the ISO 9000 series, periodically certified by an accredited body (ISO/IEC 17021:2006). IMO monitors this process (7.3).</td></tr><tr><td>8 Authorization of ROs</td><td>See below</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ro-cycles\"><figcaption>Audit and review intervals</figcaption></figure><h3>Personnel (4.2)</h3><ul><li>Statutory surveys and services are performed only by full-time surveyors and auditors <b>employed exclusively by the RO</b> (4.2.4). Radio survey work may be subcontracted under conditions.</li><li>Qualified personnel with adequate geographic coverage and local representation as needed (4.2.3)</li><li>Minimum education (4.2.5): a university degree in a relevant field (at least 2 years), or maritime training with sea experience as a certified officer, plus good English</li><li>A training-tracking system (4.2.7)</li></ul><h3>Vertical Contract Audits (6.3)</h3><p>Annual, for plan approval, new construction, in-service surveys, and type approval or equipment survey.</p><h3>Authorization (section 8)</h3><ul><li>Applies only to ships entitled to fly the flag State's flag (8.1)</li><li>8.2 Legal basis: written agreement, laws, interpretations and deviations</li><li>8.3 Specification: ship types, conventions, drawings, surveys, certificates, corrective actions, reporting</li><li>8.4 Resources · 8.5 Instruments · 8.6 Instructions · 8.7 Records</li></ul><h2>Part 3 — Oversight of recognized organizations (non-mandatory)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1–2</td><td>Purpose and scope: guidance for flag State inspection, auditing and monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>4 Terms</td><td><b>Oversight</b> is any flag State activity that assures RO compliance. <b>Monitoring</b> is a component of oversight: witnessing services or reviewing documentation.</td></tr><tr><td>5 Establishing an oversight programme</td><td>5.1 Supplementary surveys and qualified staff · 5.2 Supervision of delegated duties (QMS documentation, instructions, access to records, statistics) · 5.3 Verification and monitoring, including reporting obligations (such as notifying the flag State of major deficiencies) and certificate transfer cooperation</td></tr><tr><td>6 Principles of auditing</td><td>Ethical conduct, fair presentation, due professional care, independence, evidence-based approach</td></tr><tr><td>7 Managing an oversight programme</td><td>Verify RO compliance, taking into account factors such as remote monitoring and port State control results (7.1) · objectives and extent · responsibilities, resources, procedures · monitoring and review with performance indicators (7.4)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Appendices</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Appendix</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Training and qualification of survey, plan approval and audit staff, trainees, tutors and support staff</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Specification of survey and certification tasks: scope (A2.1), technical and management modules (A2.2), certificate-specific requirements (A2.3.1–A2.3.11), including SOLAS, Load Lines, MARPOL, ISM and tonnage certificates</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Elements to be included in an agreement between the flag State and the RO</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Old note's structure of \"Part 3 oversight\" mandatory and \"Part 4 model agreement\". The Code has <b>three</b> parts plus three appendices, and the agreement elements are Appendix 3.</li><li>The SOLAS regulation number (the text says \"SOLAS\", the Load Lines Protocol and MARPOL Annexes I and II)</li><li>Earlier resolutions A.739(18) and A.789(19) as predecessors</li><li>List of IACS member societies</li><li>Port State control \"RO-related detention\" ratings and MoU performance tables</li><li>IACS PR 1A/1B transfer-of-class procedures</li><li>Statutory work list beyond the Appendix 2 certificates named above (ISPS, MLC, AFS and BWM delegation)</li><li>Later amendments to the Code (not checked)</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.349(92), RO Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.349(92).pdf</li><li>Deutsche Flagge — RO Code publication (MSC.349(92) and MEPC.237(65)): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/xi-1/2014-s-942-msc-349-92-sonderdruck-code-fuer-anerkannte-organisationen-ro-code.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-sps-code-2008","v":167285373,"title":"SPS Code 2008","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["sps","special-purpose-ships","special-personnel","research-vessel","offshore-vessel","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>SPS Code 2008 — Code of Safety for Special Purpose Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>13 May 2008</b>, MSC resolution <b>MSC.266(84)</b> (MSC 84)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Special purpose ships of <b>500 GT or more</b> certified on or after <b>13 May 2008</b></td></tr><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>The code in Assembly resolution <b>A.534(13)</b> for ships certified from that date</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Contracting Governments to SOLAS are invited to give effect to it. It is a recommendation, not a convention.</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td><b>Special Purpose Ship Safety Certificate</b>, with Record of Equipment (Form SPS)</td></tr><tr><td>Idea</td><td>Safety equal to SOLAS 1974 for ships carrying <b>more than 12 special personnel</b> without treating them as passengers</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Preamble, 11 chapters, Annex (certificate and record)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"sps-bands\"><figcaption>What applies depends on persons on board</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> A special purpose ship carries <b>more than 12 special personnel</b> besides its crew. Special personnel are expected to be fit, know the ship's layout and have basic safety training, so the ship need not be treated as a passenger ship. What is required then depends on the <b>total number of persons on board</b>: more than 240, 60 to 240, or 60 or fewer.</blockquote><h2>Definitions (Chapter 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Special purpose ship</b> (1.3.12)</td><td>A mechanically self-propelled ship that, because of its function, carries <b>more than 12 special personnel</b> (500 GT or more per the preamble)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Special personnel</b> (1.3.11)</td><td>People who are not passengers, crew or infants, carried for the ship's special purpose or special work. The count may include <b>up to 12 passengers</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Research and survey staff, trainees, fish-processing staff on factory ships, salvage, diving, cable, pipe-laying, seismic and crane personnel</td></tr><tr><td>Passenger (1.3.8)</td><td>Anyone other than the master, crew or others engaged on ship's business, and children under one year</td></tr><tr><td>Crew (1.3.3)</td><td>People carried to navigate and maintain the ship or to serve others on board</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>A ship carrying more than 12 <b>SOLAS passengers</b> is a passenger ship, not a special purpose ship (footnote to 1.3.12)</li><li>The Code does not set a cap on special personnel in its definition. <b>240 is a threshold of total persons</b>, not a limit on special personnel.</li></ul><h2>Application (1.2) and flexibility</h2><ul><li><b>1.2.1:</b> ships of 500 GT or more certified on or after 13 May 2008. Administrations may apply it \"as far as reasonable and practicable\" to smaller and older ships.</li><li><b>1.2.2:</b> does not apply to ships that meet the <b>MODU Code</b></li><li><b>1.2.3:</b> not intended for ships carrying <b>industrial personnel who do not work on board</b></li><li><b>1.4 Exemptions:</b> a ship not normally special purpose may be exempted for one exceptional voyage if the safety measures suit that voyage</li><li><b>1.5 Equivalents:</b> accepted if at least as effective, and reported to IMO</li><li>The preamble also extends voluntary use of the Code to <b>training ships</b>, whether or not SOLAS applies</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Subject</th><th>Main rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td><td>Purpose, application, definitions, exemptions, equivalents, surveys, certification</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Stability and subdivision</td><td>Intact: refers to Part B of the Intact Stability Code (2.1). Damage: SOLAS II-1 as a passenger ship with special personnel as passengers, with an R-value by persons certified</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Machinery</td><td>SOLAS II-1 Part C. Steering gear per II-1/29: ships up to 240 persons may use 29.6.1.2, over 240 use 29.6.1.1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Electrical</td><td>SOLAS II-1 Part D. Emergency power per II-1/43 for <b>60 or fewer</b> persons, II-1/42 for <b>more than 60</b></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Unattended machinery spaces</td><td>SOLAS II-1 Part E. Over <b>240</b> persons: Administration decides case by case</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Fire protection</td><td>Over <b>240</b>: SOLAS II-2 for passenger ships carrying more than 36 passengers · <b>60 to 240</b>: passenger ships up to 36 passengers · <b>60 or fewer</b>: cargo ships</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Dangerous goods</td><td>Ships' stores are generally outside the IMDG Code but IMDG guidance should be followed. Safety assessment for goods in use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Life-saving appliances</td><td>SOLAS Chapter III. Over 60 persons: passenger ship standard. 60 or fewer: cargo ship standard. \"Passenger\" reads as \"special personnel\".</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Radiocommunications</td><td>SOLAS Chapter IV as for cargo ships</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Safety of navigation</td><td>SOLAS Chapter V</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Security</td><td>SOLAS Chapter XI-2</td></tr><tr><td>Annex</td><td>Certificate</td><td>Special Purpose Ship Safety Certificate, endorsements, Record of Equipment (Form SPS)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Damage stability by persons on board</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"sps-rvalue\"><figcaption>R-value by certified persons</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The ship is treated as a <b>passenger ship</b> with special personnel counted as passengers</li><li><b>240 persons or more:</b> required index <b>R</b></li><li><b>60 persons or fewer:</b> <b>0.8 R</b></li><li><b>Between 60 and 240:</b> linear interpolation</li><li>Since MSC.408(96), where full N = N1 + 2N2 is impractical for the service and the Administration finds a suitably reduced hazard, a lower N may be used, but <b>not below N = N1 + N2</b></li></ul><h2>Surveys and certificate (1.6–1.7)</h2><ul><li>Surveys follow those for <b>SOLAS cargo ships other than tankers</b>, and cover the Code's provisions</li><li>Certificate issued by the Administration or an authorized body. The Administration keeps <b>full responsibility</b>.</li><li>Validity and duration follow the SOLAS <b>cargo ship</b> provisions</li><li>Certificate for ships <b>under 500 GT</b> must state the relaxations accepted</li><li>Every special purpose ship holds the Special Purpose Ship Safety Certificate. Ships on international voyages also carry the relevant SOLAS certificates.</li></ul><h2>Amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"sps-timeline\"><figcaption>Amendments to the 2008 SPS Code</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.299(87)</td><td>14 May 2010</td><td>14 May 2010</td><td>Ch 5: ships with no more than 240 persons follow SOLAS II-1 regulations 46 to 53. Ch 8, para 8.3: \"sail training\" inserted before \"ship\". Cross-references in Form SPS corrected.</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.408(96)</td><td>13 May 2016</td><td>13 May 2016</td><td>Ch 2: damage stability paragraph replaced (R-value by persons) and a new paragraph for reduced N</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.503(105)</td><td>28 Apr 2022</td><td><b>1 Jan 2024</b></td><td>New form of Special Purpose Ship Safety Certificate and Record of Equipment (Form SPS), with SOLAS amendments MSC.496(105)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The edition and exact section of the Intact Stability Code cited in 2.1, and the details of SOLAS regulations referred to in Chapters 3–6</li><li>Whether any amendment was adopted after MSC.503(105)</li><li>The exact paragraph numbers in Chapter 2 after the 2016 renumbering</li><li>Flag State rules, such as UK Marine Guidance Notes</li><li>Contents of the older A.534(13) Code</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>MSC.266(84), 2008 SPS Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.266(84).pdf</li><li>MSC.299(87): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.299(87).pdf</li><li>MSC.408(96): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.408(96).pdf</li><li>MSC.503(105): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.503(105).pdf</li><li>UK MCA — MSN 1919 (M) SPS Code 2008: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/msn-1919-m-code-of-safety-for-special-purpose-ships-2008/msn-1919-m-code-of-safety-for-special-purpose-ships-2008</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-tdc-code","v":212623897,"title":"TDC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["tdc","timber","deck-cargo","logs","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>TDC Code — Code of Safe Practice for Ships Carrying Timber Deck Cargoes, 2011</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Resolution</td><td>A.1048(27), adopted 30 Nov 2011 (Assembly, 27th session)</td></tr><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>The 1991 Code, A.715(17) (revoked by the resolution)</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Voluntary (recommendatory), not mandatory</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>All ships of <b>24 m or more in length</b> carrying timber deck cargo (1.2.1)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Chapter 1 · Part A Operational requirements (chapters 2–4) · Part B Design of cargo securing arrangements (chapters 5–8) · Annexes A–D</td></tr><tr><td>Related</td><td>Cargo Securing Manual (CSM) · CSS Code Annex 13 · 2008 IS Code (stability)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"tdc-structure\"><figcaption>Structure of the 2011 TDC Code</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Timber is secured according to the ship's Cargo Securing Manual, based on chapter 5 or chapter 6 of Part B (1.2.2). National rules may restrict either chapter and require third-party inspection (1.2.3).</blockquote><h2>What the Code does</h2><ul><li>Gives the IMO standard for safe stowage and securing of timber deck cargo</li><li>Covers round and sawn wood, loose or packaged</li><li>Intended for shipowners, operators and ship's officers</li><li>Includes guidance for drafting cargo securing manual procedures and checklists (Annex A)</li><li>Loop lashing was added as an optional practice alongside top-over lashing</li></ul><h2>Definitions (1.3)</h2><ul><li><b>Timber</b> (1.3.1.15): a collective term for wooden material covered by the Code, including round and sawn wood. Wood pulp and similar cargo are excluded.</li><li><b>Timber deck cargo</b> (1.3.1.18): timber carried on an uncovered part of a freeboard or superstructure deck</li></ul><h2>Part A — Operational requirements</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>General recommendations on stowage and securing</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Visibility</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Physical properties of timber cargoes</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Stability (2.4)</h3><ul><li>The ship complies with its <b>stability booklet at all times</b> (2.4.1)</li><li>Damage stability and the <b>2008 IS Code</b>, particularly its timber deck cargo requirements, must still be met (2.4.2)</li><li>GM should preferably <b>not exceed 3% of the ship's breadth</b>, because excessive GM causes large accelerations (2.4.2)</li><li>Timber buoyancy may be considered at up to <b>25% permeability</b> for sawn wood. Round wood is typically 40% to 60%, depending on log diameter (2.4.4).</li><li><b>Water absorption</b> (4.5, Annex C): sea spray can increase cargo weight, and the increase is considered under the 2008 IS Code</li><li><b>Icing</b> (4.6): refers to 6.2 of the 2008 IS Code. Any water-absorption increase is counted before the icing increase.</li><li>The Code does not repeat the stability criteria (GZ area, residual stability). Those come from the 2008 IS Code.</li></ul><h3>Stowage (2.9)</h3><ul><li>Avoid voids in the stow. Where they occur, fill with loose timber or block with H-frames (2.9.5).</li><li>Timber overhanging hatch coamings by more than <b>one-third of the package length</b> is supported at the outer end (2.9.6)</li></ul><h3>Access and fall protection (2.8)</h3><ul><li>Where there is no convenient passage for crew, guard lines or rails no more than <b>330 mm apart vertically</b>, extending to at least <b>1 m above the cargo</b> (2.8.6)</li><li>Where cargo is uneven, a walking surface at least <b>600 mm wide</b> (2.8.6)</li><li>Alternative: a taut lifeline about <b>2 m above the cargo</b>, near the centreline, used with a fall protection system (2.8.9)</li></ul><h3>Voyage (2.10, 2.12, Annex A)</h3><ul><li>Examine and tighten lashings at the start of the voyage, then at regular intervals and as needed, because vibration and working of the ship make the cargo settle (2.10.7)</li><li>Enter all examinations and adjustments in the ship's logbook (2.10.8)</li><li>Reduce ship motion before checking or re-tightening lashings (2.8.12)</li><li>Severe weather: reduce speed or alter course early. Avoid roll amplitudes above <b>30°</b> (2.12.4).</li></ul><h3>Jettison (Annex A, A.4.22–A.4.23)</h3><ul><li>A <b>final resort</b>, after other options are exhausted</li><li>Jettison is unlikely to clear the cargo entirely, risks propeller damage if still turning, and is dangerous to those involved</li><li>Report the position and navigational hazard immediately to coastal authorities</li><li>If timber is lost overboard, the master warns nearby ships and the nearest competent authority, giving the kind of danger, position and time in UTC</li></ul><h2>Part B — Design of cargo securing arrangements</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>Design principles</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Alternative design principles</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Uprights</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Denotations used</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Lashing requirements</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"tdc-lashing\"><figcaption>Lashing spacing</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breaking strength (5.1.2)</td><td>At least <b>133 kN</b> · elongation not more than 5% at 80% of breaking strength · no permanent deformation after a proof load of at least 40% of breaking strength</td></tr><tr><td>Tightening device (5.1.3)</td><td>At least 27 kN in the horizontal part and 16 kN in the vertical part</td></tr><tr><td>Spacing, sawn wood (5.3.2)</td><td>Maximum <b>3 m</b> for heights up to 2.5 m · maximum <b>1.5 m</b> above 2.5 m · halved at the foremost and aftmost sections</td></tr><tr><td>Spacing, round wood (5.4.1)</td><td>No more than <b>1.5 m</b> apart</td></tr><tr><td>Alternative design (chapter 6)</td><td>Pretension at least 16 kN (vertical) and 27 kN (horizontal) (6.5.20) · Maximum Securing Load derived from the Minimum Breaking Load using CSS Code Annex 13 (6.4) · elongation 2% for chain and wire, 7% for web lashings, unless certified otherwise (6.5.16) · heel from cargo movement due to lashing elongation not more than <b>5°</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Uprights (5.2, chapter 7)</h3><ul><li>Used where the nature, height or character of the cargo requires them (5.2.1, 2.10.12)</li><li>Spaced no more than <b>3 m</b> apart between centrelines, so most of the stow is supported by at least two uprights (5.2.2.2)</li><li>Fastened to the deck or hatch and restrained from falling inward during loading and discharge (2.10.13)</li><li>Bending stress not more than 50% of the material's ultimate stress (7.6)</li></ul><h2>Annexes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>Guidance in developing procedures and checklists</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Samples of stowage and securing arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Instruction on calculating mass change due to water absorption</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>References</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Old claim of \"GM ≥0.10 m at all times\". The Code points to the stability booklet and the 2008 IS Code, and says GM should preferably not exceed 3% of breadth.</li><li>Old claim \"water absorption +10% weight of deck timber\". Annex C gives the calculation method.</li><li>History \"1972 first Code, 1991 Code\" details (a secondary page gives 1972, 1978 and 1991) and \"timber load lines LT, LS, LW\" marks</li><li>Old claim \"height on deck within SOLAS V/22 visibility limits\" (chapter 3 content not checked)</li><li>Old claim that securing is \"calculations like CSS Annex 13\" for all cases (Annex 13 applies in chapter 6)</li><li>Old structure \"Part A chapters 1–7\" and \"Part B Ch 3 physical properties\"</li><li>The formula for Calculated Strength (garbled in the source) and the Annex B stowage samples</li><li>IMO's summary page describes the Code as five parts; the Code text itself has Part A, Part B and Annexes A–D, which is what this note follows</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Resolution A.1048(27), 2011 TDC Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/Documents/A - Assembly/1048(27).pdf</li><li>IMO — 2011 TDC Code page: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/Timber-Code.aspx</li><li>Wikipedia — Code of Safe Practice for Ships Carrying Timber Deck Cargoes (history, used only for context): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_of_Safe_Practice_for_Ships_Carrying_Timber_Deck_Cargoes</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-mlc-2006-ilo","v":74634908,"title":"MLC 2006 (ILO)","category":"Maritime › ILO › Conventions","tags":["mlc","ilo","convention","core","seafarers","psc","labour"],"body":"<h1>MLC 2006 — Maritime Labour Convention (ILO)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>The ILO's seafarers' rights convention, often called the \"fourth pillar\" with SOLAS, MARPOL and STCW</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>23 Feb 2006</b>, 94th (Maritime) Session of the International Labour Conference</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>20 Aug 2013</b></td></tr><tr><td>Ratifications</td><td><b>113 ILO Members, about 96.6%</b> of world gross tonnage (ILO page)</td></tr><tr><td>Replaces</td><td>36 older maritime Conventions and 1 Protocol (ILO)</td></tr><tr><td>Amended</td><td>Code amendments in <b>2014, 2016, 2018, 2022 and 2025</b></td></tr><tr><td>Latest in force</td><td>2022 amendments, <b>23 Dec 2024</b></td></tr><tr><td>Next</td><td>2025 amendments, expected <b>Dec 2027</b></td></tr><tr><td>Ship paperwork</td><td>Maritime Labour Certificate and DMLC Parts I and II (ships 500 GT+ on international voyages)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mlc-structure\"><figcaption>How the Convention is built</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The MLC is the seafarer's contract, pay, rest, leave, food, cabin, medical care and complaints. Port State Control checks it, and the flag State certifies it.</blockquote><h2>How it is built</h2><ul><li><b>Articles and Regulations:</b> the basic rights and principles</li><li><b>Code Part A:</b> mandatory <b>Standards</b></li><li><b>Code Part B:</b> non-mandatory <b>Guidelines</b></li><li><b>Five Titles:</b> each Title holds Regulations, and each Regulation has a Standard (A) and a Guideline (B)</li><li><b>Amendments:</b> made by the Special Tripartite Committee (governments, shipowners, seafarers). Code amendments are accepted tacitly</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Title</th><th>Subject</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Minimum requirements to work on a ship</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Conditions of employment</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Accommodation, recreational facilities, food and catering</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Health protection, medical care, welfare and social security</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Compliance and enforcement</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Title 1 — Minimum requirements</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Minimum age (Reg 1.1)</td><td><b>16</b>. Night work is prohibited under 18. \"Night\" is at least <b>9 hours</b>, starting no later than midnight and ending no earlier than 5 am</td></tr><tr><td>Medical certificate (Std A1.2)</td><td>Valid up to <b>2 years</b>. Up to <b>1 year</b> for seafarers under 18. Colour vision: up to <b>6 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>Certificate expires at sea</td><td>Stays valid until the next port where a medical practitioner is available, for no more than <b>3 months</b></td></tr><tr><td>Training and qualifications (Reg 1.3)</td><td>Trained and certified (see the STCW convention)</td></tr><tr><td>Recruitment and placement (Reg 1.4)</td><td>Licensed or regulated services. Seafarers must be told their rights under the agency financial protection system (2022)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Title 2 — Conditions of employment</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Seafarers' Employment Agreement (Std A2.1)</td><td>Each party gets a <b>signed original</b>. Notice for early termination is <b>not shorter than 7 days</b></td></tr><tr><td>Wages (Std A2.2)</td><td>Paid at intervals of <b>no more than one month</b>, with a <b>monthly account</b></td></tr><tr><td>Hours of work and rest (Std A2.3)</td><td>See the box below</td></tr><tr><td>Annual leave (Std A2.4)</td><td>At least <b>2.5 calendar days per month</b> of employment</td></tr><tr><td>Repatriation (Std A2.5.1)</td><td>Maximum time on board before the right to repatriation: <b>less than 12 months</b></td></tr><tr><td>Compensation for loss of ship (Reg 2.6)</td><td>Indemnity for unemployment</td></tr><tr><td>Manning levels (Reg 2.7)</td><td>Enough seafarers for safe, efficient and secure operation</td></tr><tr><td>Career and skill development (Reg 2.8)</td><td>Opportunities for seafarers</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mlc-hours\"><figcaption>Hours of work and rest: the two limits</figcaption></figure><p><b>Hours of work and rest (Std A2.3).</b> The flag State sets one of two limits. They are not mixed.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Per 24 hours</th><th>Per 7 days</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maximum hours of <b>work</b></td><td><b>14 hours</b></td><td><b>72 hours</b></td></tr><tr><td>Minimum hours of <b>rest</b></td><td><b>10 hours</b></td><td><b>77 hours</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Rest can be split into <b>no more than two periods</b>, one of them at least <b>6 hours</b></li><li>The interval between two rest periods must not exceed <b>14 hours</b></li></ul><p><b>Abandonment and financial security (Std A2.5.2)</b></p><ul><li>Failure to pay contractual wages for at least <b>2 months</b> can count as abandonment</li><li>The financial security must cover outstanding wages and other entitlements for up to <b>4 months</b></li><li>The security cannot end before its validity ends unless the provider gives the flag State at least <b>30 days'</b> notice</li><li>The financial security certificates must be carried on board (2014 amendments)</li></ul><h2>Title 3 — Accommodation, food and catering</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Headroom (Std A3.1)</td><td>At least <b>203 cm</b> where full and free movement is needed</td></tr><tr><td>Hospital (Std A3.1)</td><td>Separate hospital accommodation on ships with <b>15 or more seafarers</b> on voyages longer than <b>3 days</b></td></tr><tr><td>Social connectivity (2022)</td><td>Internet access on board where reasonably practicable, at reasonable cost</td></tr><tr><td>Food and water (2022)</td><td>Drinking water free of charge and of good quality. Balanced meals. Food and water supplies inspected</td></tr><tr><td>Ship's cook (Std A3.2)</td><td><b>Under 18</b> may not work as a ship's cook. On ships with a prescribed manning below 10, a fully qualified cook may not be needed</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Title 4 — Health, medical care, welfare and social security</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Doctor on board (Std A4.1)</td><td>Ships with <b>100 or more persons</b> on international voyages longer than <b>3 days</b></td></tr><tr><td>Prompt disembarkation (2022)</td><td>A seafarer needing urgent care is disembarked promptly and gets access to shore medical care for serious conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Remains (2022)</td><td>Member States must facilitate repatriation of a deceased seafarer's body or ashes</td></tr><tr><td>Shipowner's liability (Std A4.2.1)</td><td>National law may limit liability for medical costs and wages to not less than <b>16 weeks</b></td></tr><tr><td>Safety committee (Std A4.3)</td><td>Ships with <b>5 or more seafarers</b></td></tr><tr><td>Protective equipment (2022)</td><td>PPE that fits properly, including for women seafarers</td></tr><tr><td>Deaths (2022)</td><td>Every seafarer death is recorded, reported yearly to the ILO and published</td></tr><tr><td>Social security (Std A4.5)</td><td>Protection in at least <b>3 of the 9</b> listed branches</td></tr><tr><td>Shore welfare (Reg 4.4)</td><td>Access to shore-based welfare facilities</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Title 5 — Compliance and enforcement</h2><p><b>Flag State certificate (Reg 5.1.3)</b></p><ul><li>Applies to ships of <b>500 GT or over</b> on international voyages</li><li><b>Maritime Labour Certificate:</b> valid up to <b>5 years</b></li><li><b>Intermediate inspection:</b> between the <b>2nd and 3rd anniversary</b></li><li><b>Interim certificate:</b> up to <b>6 months</b></li><li><b>DMLC Part I:</b> the flag State's national requirements. <b>Part II:</b> the measures the shipowner uses to stay compliant between inspections</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mlc-certificate\"><figcaption>Certificate cycle</figcaption></figure><p><b>The 16 areas (listed in the DMLC and for detailed port State inspection)</b></p><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mlc-areas\"><figcaption>The 16 areas checked</figcaption></figure><p><b>Complaints and inspection</b></p><ul><li><b>On-board complaint procedure (Reg 5.1.5):</b> every ship has one</li><li><b>Marine casualties (Reg 5.1.6):</b> official inquiry for serious casualties</li><li><b>Port State inspection (Reg 5.2):</b> checks the MLC certificate and DMLC. Detailed inspection when there are clear grounds or a complaint</li></ul><h2>Amendments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"mlc-amendments\"><figcaption>MLC amendments and when each took effect</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What changed</th><th>In force</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2014</td><td>Financial security for abandonment, and for death and long-term disability. Certificates on board</td><td><b>18 Jan 2017</b></td></tr><tr><td>2016</td><td>Guidance on eliminating harassment and bullying</td><td><b>8 Jan 2019</b></td></tr><tr><td>2018</td><td>Shipowners keep paying seafarers held captive after piracy or armed robbery</td><td><b>26 Dec 2020</b></td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>Eight amendments: see below</td><td><b>23 Dec 2024</b></td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>Adopted by the Special Tripartite Committee on 7 to 11 Apr 2025: see below</td><td>Expected <b>Dec 2027</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>2022 amendments, in force 23 Dec 2024</b></p><ul><li><b>Recruitment:</b> seafarers must know their rights under the agency financial protection system</li><li><b>Repatriation:</b> States facilitate prompt repatriation of abandoned seafarers. Replacement seafarers get MLC rights</li><li><b>Accommodation and welfare:</b> social connectivity on board. Port States do the same for ships in port</li><li><b>Food:</b> free good-quality drinking water, balanced meals, inspected supplies</li><li><b>Medical care:</b> prompt disembarkation, shore care, repatriation of remains</li><li><b>Health and safety:</b> fitting PPE, all deaths reported to the ILO</li><li><b>Financial security:</b> the certificate shows the registered owner's name if it differs from the shipowner</li></ul><p><b>2025 amendments, expected Dec 2027</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Repatriation (A2.5.1, B2.5.1)</td><td>Without discrimination, whatever the ship's flag. Shipowner's costs cover passage to the chosen destination, accommodation on the way, <b>up to 30 kg of luggage</b> and medical care until the seafarer can travel</td></tr><tr><td>Shore leave (A2.4.2, B2.4.5)</td><td>Without discrimination whatever the flag. A seafarer cannot be required to hold a visa or special permit for shore leave</td></tr><tr><td>Key workers (B2.5.2)</td><td>Seafarers should be recognized as key workers and given safe movement when travelling for work. This is a Guideline</td></tr><tr><td>Detentions and casualties (B4.4.6, A5.1.6)</td><td>Detained seafarers dealt with promptly, using the ILO/IMO fair treatment guidelines. Casualty inquiries consider the IMO Casualty Investigation Code</td></tr><tr><td>Medical (B4.1.1)</td><td>Medical training reflects the International Medical Guide for Seafarers and Fishers</td></tr><tr><td>Harassment and bullying</td><td>Prevention covered in recruitment, safety and health programmes, investigations and the complaint procedure (B1.4.1, B3.1.10, A4.3, B4.3.1, B4.3.6, B4.3.11, A5.1.5)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Four resolutions were adopted with them. They are guidance, not binding</li><li><b>No new DMLC inspection items.</b> DMLC Part II procedures will need updating</li><li><b>Hours of work:</b> no amendment. The Committee asked for a Joint ILO/IMO Tripartite Working Group meeting on hours of work and rest</li></ul><h2>What inspectors look at</h2><ul><li>Employment agreement against the crew list</li><li>Wage records and monthly accounts</li><li>Hours of work and rest records</li><li>MLC certificate, DMLC Parts I and II, financial security certificates</li><li>Food and water inspection records, cabin condition</li><li>The complaint procedure, and that seafarers have a copy</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>SOLAS 1974 and MARPOL 73/78, the other pillars</li><li>ISM Code safety management</li><li>Casualty Investigation Code</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The 2016 certificate extension of up to 5 months</li><li>Exact wording of Part B limits, for example the 2-month unemployment indemnity</li><li>Cabin floor areas and the date from which the new accommodation rules apply</li><li>Hazardous work under 18</li><li>Which ships are excluded (fishing vessels, traditional ships, warships, inland waters). Checked 7 Nov only</li><li>The Philippines' ratification date and national agency details. Checked 7 Nov only</li><li>Ratification count date. The ILO page gives 113 Members and 96.6% without a date</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>ILO, MLC 2006 page (ratifications, adoption, in force): https://www.ilo.org/international-labour-standards/maritime-labour-convention-2006</li><li>MLC 2006 as amended, full text (Malta Transport copy): https://www.transport.gov.mt/mlc-2006-as-amended.pdf-f3668</li><li>ILO, 2022 amendments in force 23 Dec 2024: https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/new-important-set-amendments-mlc-2006-will-enter-force-23-december-2024</li><li>ILO, 2025 amendments agreed at STC5: https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/seafarers%E2%80%99-conditions-set-improve-after-shipping-industry-adopts-amendments</li><li>ILO, text of amendments adopted 11 Apr 2025: https://www.ilo.org/resource/other/text-amendments-adopted-11-april-2025</li><li>DNV, 2025 amendments: https://www.dnv.com/news/2025/new-amendments-to-the-mlc-2006/</li><li>West P&amp;I, MLC amendments 2014 to 2025: https://www.westpandi.com/Special/mlc/</li><li>IMO, 2014 amendments in force: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/06-mlc-amendments-eif.aspx</li><li>ILO list of the 36 Conventions and 1 Protocol replaced: https://www.ilo.org/resource/list-36-maritime-conventions-and-1-protocol-revised-maritime-labour</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-bills-of-lading","v":169566729,"title":"Bills of Lading","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["bill-of-lading","document-of-title","clausing","letter-of-indemnity","cargo","delivery","electronic-bill","topic","imo"],"body":"<h1>Bills of Lading — The cargo document</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>The transport document the carrier issues for goods carried by sea</td></tr><tr><td>Three functions</td><td><b>Receipt</b>, <b>evidence of the contract</b>, <b>document of title</b></td></tr><tr><td>Not</td><td>The contract of carriage itself. The bill is only evidence of it</td></tr><tr><td>Main rules</td><td>Hague and Hague-Visby Rules, Art III. UK Carriage of Goods by Sea Act 1992. UK Electronic Trade Documents Act 2023</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bl-flow\"><figcaption>From issue to delivery</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Whoever holds an original bill can demand the cargo. A carrier that delivers to anyone else carries the risk of the full cargo value.</blockquote><h2>The three functions</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Receipt</b></td><td>Records that the carrier received the goods: the date the full quantity was received, their apparent condition, and the figures (marks, number, quantity or weight)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Evidence of the contract</b></td><td>Shows the terms of carriage. The contract exists from loading, before the bill is issued</td></tr><tr><td><b>Document of title</b></td><td>The rightful holder can take delivery on surrender of an original. Right to delivery is <b>not</b> the same as ownership</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Types</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Straight</b></td><td>Names the consignee and <b>cannot be transferred</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Order</b></td><td>Delivery \"to order\". Passes by endorsement</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bearer</b></td><td>Delivery to whoever holds it. Passes by delivery alone</td></tr><tr><td><b>Shipped (on board)</b></td><td>States the goods are loaded</td></tr><tr><td><b>Received for shipment</b></td><td>States the goods are received, not that they are loaded</td></tr><tr><td><b>Clean</b></td><td>No remark on the goods' apparent order and condition</td></tr><tr><td><b>Claused</b></td><td>Carries a remark on a defect</td></tr><tr><td><b>Charterparty bill</b></td><td>Issued under a charter. The Hague Rules apply to it from the point it regulates the carrier and the holder (Art I(b))</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sea waybill</b> (not a bill of lading)</td><td>Names who gets delivery and <b>only identification is needed</b>. Not a document of title. Hague-Visby does not apply to it</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What the bill must show (Hague Rules Art III)</h2><ul><li><b>Rule 3:</b> on the shipper's demand, after receiving the goods, the carrier, master or agent issues a bill showing:<ul><li>the <b>leading marks</b>, as the shipper gave them in writing before loading</li><li>the <b>number of packages or pieces, or the quantity or weight</b>, as the shipper gave them in writing</li><li>the <b>apparent order and condition</b> of the goods</li></ul></li><li>The carrier need <b>not</b> state marks, number, quantity or weight it has reasonable grounds to suspect are inaccurate, or had no reasonable means of checking</li><li><b>Rule 7:</b> after loading, if the shipper demands it, the bill must be a <b>shipped</b> bill</li><li>The P&amp;I guide adds: place of issue, and the load and discharge ports</li></ul><h2>Evidence and the shipper's guarantee</h2><ul><li><b>Rule 4:</b> the bill is <b>prima facie evidence</b> of receipt of the goods as described. Against a third party acting in good faith, proof to the contrary is <b>not admissible</b></li><li><b>Rule 5:</b> the shipper is deemed to guarantee the accuracy of the marks, number, quantity and weight it gave, and must indemnify the carrier for loss from errors</li><li><b>UK COGSA 1992 s.4:</b> a bill signed by the master or an authorised person, stating goods were shipped or received, is <b>conclusive evidence against the carrier</b> in favour of a lawful holder</li></ul><h2>Clausing</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bl-clause\"><figcaption>The master's test</figcaption></figure><ul><li>\"Apparent\" means <b>observable to the master on a reasonable examination</b>. It can include smell</li><li><b>Pre-shipment damage seen at loading:</b> clause the bill. Example in the Swedish Club guide: *250 steel coils, wet and rust stained on shipment*</li><li><b>Damage after the carrier took the goods:</b> no clause needed. A plain factual remark can be used, such as *received clean on board but approximately 30 MT subsequently damaged in a fire*</li><li>A hidden fault that no reasonable look would show does not have to be investigated</li><li>If the defects are too many for the bill, refer to a survey report. The report only counts if it is <b>attached to all bills, pages numbered, and the bill number stamped on it</b></li><li><b>Refuse to sign</b> a bill that wrongly states the loading completion date, the port of loading, or clearly misdescribes the cargo's colour, form, quantity or condition</li><li>A bill not properly claused can leave the carrier liable for damage it did not cause, and can put P&amp;I cover at risk</li></ul><h2>Holders: rights and liabilities (UK COGSA 1992)</h2><ul><li><b>s.1:</b> applies to bills of lading, sea waybills and ship's delivery orders. A bill of lading here excludes any document that cannot be transferred by endorsement, or by delivery if it is a bearer bill</li><li><b>s.2:</b> the <b>lawful holder</b> gets the rights of suit under the contract of carriage as if an original party</li><li><b>s.3:</b> that person takes on the liabilities of the contract if he takes or demands delivery, or makes a claim on the carrier for the goods</li><li><b>s.5:</b> a holder is a lawful holder if he became holder in good faith</li></ul><h2>Originals and delivery</h2><ul><li>Bills are traditionally issued in <b>sets of three originals</b></li><li>Delivery against an original that stayed on board is a practice with risk: it has not passed through the bank chain and gives little proof of title. It raises the risk of misdelivery, which would be for the full value of the cargo</li><li>If agreed, the clubs recommend marking every original that <b>one original is kept on board and delivery may be made against it on the shipper's or charterer's instruction</b></li><li>Check the identity of the person who receives the cargo, and keep proof of delivery</li></ul><h2>Letters of indemnity (LOI)</h2><p>Used when the carrier is asked to deliver without the original bill, or at a port other than the one named.</p><table><thead><tr><th>International Group form</th><th>Cargo delivered</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>Without the original bill</td></tr><tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>At a different port, against at least one original</td></tr><tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>At a different port <b>and</b> without the original</td></tr><tr><td><b>AA, BB, CC</b></td><td>Same, with a bank also signing</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The requestor indemnifies the carrier and must put up security to prevent or lift an arrest of the ship, or any ship under the same ownership, management or control</li><li><b>P&amp;I cover:</b> unless the Club's committee decides otherwise, there is <b>no cover</b> for delivery without an original bill or at another port. Check the signer's standing and authority</li><li><b>2023 review</b> (Gard circular 8/2023, 13 Sep 2023): updated wordings, an introductory warning on cover, and a representation on the status of the receiver. The security duty now also reaches vessels or property owned or controlled by the LOI recipient. The LOI ends only when the bills have reached the party that took delivery. Disputes go to the English courts</li></ul><h2>Electronic bills</h2><ul><li>The UK <b>Electronic Trade Documents Act 2023</b> came into force on <b>21 September 2023</b>. It gives certain electronic trade documents, including electronic bills of lading, the same legal recognition as paper in English law</li><li>It meets the requirements of the UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Transferable Records (MLETR)</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>Hague-Visby, Hamburg and Rotterdam Rules carrier liability, limits and time bar</li><li>York-Antwerp, Arrest and Collision Rules general average, normally written into the bill</li><li>Salvage 1989 salvage of ship and cargo</li><li>Draft Survey how the cargo weight on a bill can be checked</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li><b>Switch, house, master, through, combined and stale</b> bills</li><li>The <b>number of originals</b> and any rule on it in law (only the traditional set of three is sourced)</li><li>The <b>LOI amount</b> (an old Gard circular suggested at least 200% of cargo value where a bank joins. The 2023 circular gives no amount) and the LOI time limits</li><li>Current list of <b>approved electronic bill systems</b>, and the P&amp;I Clubs' position on them</li><li>The Hague-Visby wording of Art III rules 3 to 5, if it differs from the Hague Rules text used here</li><li>Hamburg and Rotterdam Rules on transport documents</li><li><b>Bills in the Philippines</b>, US law (Pomerene Act, COGSA 1936) and other national laws</li><li>Charterparty clauses on who signs the bill, and \"as presented\" or \"as agent for the master\" wording</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Hague Rules 1924, Art I and III, original text (Univ. of Oslo): https://www.jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/hague-rules.xml</li><li>UK, Carriage of Goods by Sea Act 1992: https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/50</li><li>West of England P&amp;I Club, Guide to bills of lading: https://www.westpandi.com/getattachment/907f94bf-b7dd-469e-913f-4baae23fca1d/p_guide_bills_of_lading_1_2pp_v2_lr.pdf</li><li>Swedish Club, Clausing bills of lading: https://www.swedishclub.com/uploads/2023/12/Clausing-bills-of-lading-In-Focus.pdf</li><li>Gard, One is the loneliest number (originals on board): https://gard.no/insights/one-is-loneliest-number-enduring-practice-on-board-bill-lading</li><li>Gard, Circular 8/2023, IG proforma LOI review: https://gard.no/en/circulars/8-2023-ig-proforma-letters-of-indemnity-review</li><li>Gard, Standard forms of letters of indemnity: https://www.gard.no/en/circulars/bills-of-lading-delivery-of-cargo-standard-forms-of-letters-of-indemnity</li><li>UK P&amp;I Club, Electronic Trade Documents Act 2023: https://www.ukpandi.com/news-and-resources/news/article/articles/2023/uk-electronic-trade-documents-act-2023-comes-into-force</li><li>Wikipedia, Bill of lading (secondary; types): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_of_lading</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-draft-survey","v":6143758,"title":"Draft Survey","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["draft-survey","draught-survey","cargo-weight","displacement","trim-correction","density","hydrostatics","topic","imo"],"body":"<h1>Draft Survey — Cargo weight from the ship's draft</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Principle</td><td>Archimedes: a floating ship displaces water equal to her own weight. <b>Cargo = change in displacement</b>, after the corrections</td></tr><tr><td>Method</td><td>Survey before and after loading or discharging, then compare the net displacements</td></tr><tr><td>Readings</td><td><b>Six draft marks</b>: forward, midships, aft, on port and starboard</td></tr><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>About <b>±0.5%</b> of the cargo figure when done properly</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>UNECE *Code of uniform standards and procedures for the performance of draught surveys of coal cargoes* (ECE/ENERGY/19, Geneva, 3 Feb 1992)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"draft-steps\"><figcaption>The order of work</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Every correction is small, but the errors add up. A wrong density or a missed ballast tank can cost far more than a misread draft.</blockquote><h2>Before the survey</h2><ul><li>Draft marks painted, visible and unobstructed</li><li>List not more than <b>0.5°</b>, and trim small</li><li>Safe access to read the marks, a small boat preferred</li><li>Ballast tanks pressed up or empty, with slack tanks kept to a minimum</li><li>Sounding pipes marked and clear</li><li>Approved hydrostatic tables and tank tables on hand</li></ul><h2>Step 1. Read the drafts</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"draft-marks\"><figcaption>Six readings, and the distances to the perpendiculars</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Read forward, midships and aft, on <b>both sides</b>, and average each pair</li><li><b>Fm</b> = (FP + FS) / 2, <b>Mm</b> = (MP + MS) / 2, <b>Am</b> = (AP + AS) / 2</li><li><b>Apparent trim (AT)</b> = Am − Fm. Positive means trim by the stern</li></ul><h2>Step 2. Correct to the perpendiculars</h2><p>The marks are not on the perpendiculars. Draft is moved along the trim line to the <b>FP</b>, <b>AP</b> and midships.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Length between marks</td><td><b>LBM</b> = LBP − (Fd + Ad)</td></tr><tr><td>Forward correction</td><td><b>Fc</b> = Fd × AT / LBM</td></tr><tr><td>Aft correction</td><td><b>Ac</b> = Ad × AT / LBM</td></tr><tr><td>Corrected drafts</td><td><b>Fcd</b> = Fm ∓ Fc, <b>Acd</b> = Am ± Ac</td></tr><tr><td>True trim</td><td><b>TT</b> = Acd − Fcd</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Fd and Ad are the distances from the forward and aft marks to the perpendiculars</li><li>Signs, for marks <b>inside</b> the perpendiculars: trim by the <b>stern</b>, subtract Fc and add Ac. Trim by the <b>head</b>, add Fc and subtract Ac</li><li>The midships correction works the same way, and is zero when the mid mark is at midships</li></ul><h2>Step 3. Mean draft</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"draft-hogsag\"><figcaption>Why the middle reading is weighted</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Mean</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fore-and-aft mean</td><td><b>M2</b> = (Fcd + Acd) / 2</td></tr><tr><td>Mean of means</td><td><b>M3</b> = (M2 + Mcd) / 2</td></tr><tr><td><b>Quarter mean</b></td><td><b>M4</b> = (M3 + Mcd) / 2 = <b>(Fcd + Acd + 6 × Mcd) / 8</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The quarter mean compensates for hull bend (hogging or sagging)</li><li>A plain mean (Fcd + Acd) / 2 is only for a ship with no trim and no bend</li></ul><h2>Step 4. Displacement from the tables</h2><ul><li>Enter the hydrostatic table at the <b>quarter mean M4</b></li><li>Interpolate if M4 is between tabulated drafts: displacement = value at the lower draft + <b>TPC × (M4 − lower draft in cm)</b></li><li>Take <b>TPC</b>, <b>LCF</b> and <b>MCTC</b> at M4. LCF is positive aft of midships</li></ul><h2>Step 5. Trim corrections</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Correction</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>First</b> (tonnes)</td><td><b>FTc</b> = TT × TPC × LCF × 100 / LBP</td></tr><tr><td><b>Second</b> (Nemoto)</td><td><b>STc</b> = TT² × dMCTC × 50 / LBP</td></tr><tr><td>dMCTC</td><td>MCTC at (M4 + 0.5 m) − MCTC at (M4 − 0.5 m)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>First correction sign:</b> trim by the stern with LCF aft, or trim by the head with LCF forward, is <b>added</b>. The other two cases are <b>subtracted</b></li><li><b>Second correction:</b> always <b>added</b>, whichever way the ship is trimmed</li><li>Needed when trim exceeds <b>LBP / 100</b>. Small at small trims</li><li><b>Displacement corrected for trim</b> = Disp + FTc + STc</li></ul><h2>Step 6. Density correction</h2><ul><li>Tables assume seawater at <b>1.025 t/m³</b></li><li>Take a <b>dock water sample</b> at the time and measure it with a hydrometer</li><li><b>Corrected displacement</b> = trim-corrected displacement × (measured density / 1.025)</li></ul><h2>Step 7. Deductibles and constant</h2><ul><li><b>Deductibles:</b> ballast water (including unpumpable), fresh water, fuel oil, diesel oil, lube oil, sludge and bilge, and other known weights such as dunnage and lashing</li><li><b>Tank weight</b> = volume from the sounding table (corrected for trim) × density</li><li>Densities: fresh water <b>1.000</b>, seawater ballast <b>1.025</b>. Fuel density is given by the Chief Engineer, measured at the time of sounding if possible</li><li><b>Constant</b> = light displacement − lightship − weights − ballast − consumables, found by a survey of the ship in light condition</li><li>Net displacement = corrected displacement − deductibles − constant</li></ul><h2>Step 8. Cargo weight</h2><ul><li><b>Cargo</b> = net displacement (final) − net displacement (initial)</li><li>For one survey with cargo on board: <b>Cargo</b> = corrected displacement − lightship − deductibles − constant</li></ul><h2>Worked example (illustrative figures, not a real ship)</h2><p>LBP 180.0 m, Fd 3.0 m, Ad 6.0 m, so LBM = 171.0 m. Marks are inside the perpendiculars. Mid mark at midships.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Working</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fm, Mm, Am</td><td>(8.10 + 8.14) / 2, (8.52 + 8.56) / 2, (9.30 + 9.34) / 2</td><td>8.120, 8.540, 9.320 m</td></tr><tr><td>Apparent trim</td><td>9.320 − 8.120</td><td>1.200 m by the stern</td></tr><tr><td>Fc, Ac</td><td>3.0 × 1.200 / 171 and 6.0 × 1.200 / 171</td><td>0.021 m, 0.042 m</td></tr><tr><td>Fcd, Acd</td><td>8.120 − 0.021 and 9.320 + 0.042</td><td>8.099 m, 9.362 m</td></tr><tr><td>True trim</td><td>9.362 − 8.099</td><td>1.263 m</td></tr><tr><td>Quarter mean</td><td>(8.099 + 9.362 + 6 × 8.540) / 8</td><td><b>8.588 m</b></td></tr><tr><td>Table values at 8.588 m</td><td>Displacement 24,680.0 t, TPC 31.2, LCF 2.40 m aft, dMCTC 8.0</td><td>given</td></tr><tr><td>First correction</td><td>1.263 × 31.2 × 2.40 × 100 / 180</td><td><b>+52.5 t</b></td></tr><tr><td>Second correction</td><td>1.263² × 8.0 × 50 / 180</td><td><b>+3.5 t</b></td></tr><tr><td>Trim-corrected displacement</td><td>24,680.0 + 52.5 + 3.5</td><td>24,736.1 t</td></tr><tr><td>Density correction</td><td>24,736.1 × (1.019 / 1.025)</td><td>24,591.3 t (−144.8 t)</td></tr><tr><td>Deductibles</td><td>Ballast 3,412.6 + fresh water 186.4 + fuel oil 742.8 + diesel oil 58.2 + lube oil 71.0</td><td>4,471.0 t</td></tr><tr><td>Constant, lightship</td><td>210.0 t and 6,800.0 t</td><td>given</td></tr><tr><td><b>Cargo</b></td><td>24,591.3 − 6,800.0 − 4,471.0 − 210.0</td><td><b>13,110.3 t</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Density alone moved the answer by 144.8 t here, more than any other correction.</p><h2>Common sources of error</h2><ul><li>Density not measured, or assumed to be 1.025, or a sample from the surface layer only</li><li>Swell, current, or ship movement at an exposed berth; marks hidden by fenders, or a high jetty</li><li>List above 0.5°, or heavy trim</li><li>A tank missed, or sounding pipes not clear</li><li>Wrong table entry or wrong interpolation direction</li><li>Fuel burned or water used between the two surveys, and not recorded</li><li>An out-of-date constant</li><li>Wrong sign on a trim correction. Always check the sign convention of the ship's own table</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>Tonnage 1969 gross and net tonnage (a measure of volume, not weight)</li><li>Load Lines 1966 the draft limits for the ship</li><li>SOLAS 1974 carriage of grain and bulk cargoes</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The <b>original UNECE code (ECE/ENERGY/19)</b>. Only its title, date and symbol are checked. The formulas above agree across published guides, not the code itself</li><li>Section numbers and the exact accuracy wording of the code</li><li>The <b>Nemoto second correction</b>: one guide writes the divisor as LBP², two write LBP. The LBP form is used here</li><li>Marks <b>outside</b> the perpendiculars, imperial (TPI, MTI) versions, and the deadweight-survey method</li><li>How to find the constant, step by step, and the usual size of a constant</li><li>Tonnes-per-centimetre rules of thumb and the 0.1% accuracy claim</li><li>Charterparty or port rules on who may survey and when</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Draught Survey Book (Canada), ch. 3, procedure and formulas: https://mariners.narod.ru/dsprocedure.html</li><li>Mascot Maritime, draft survey procedure: https://mascotmaritime.com/draft-survey/</li><li>Draft Surveys .com, calculation guide: https://www.draftsurveys.com/en/guides/draft-survey-calculation-guide.html</li><li>UN Digital Library record of ECE/ENERGY/19: https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/168125</li><li>Wikipedia, Draft survey (six marks, principle): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Draft_survey</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-fire-fighting-equipment-guide","v":137326863,"title":"Fire-Fighting Equipment Guide","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["ffe","fire-fighting","fire-main","extinguishers","fireman-outfit","eebd","scba","fixed-systems","maintenance","solas-ii-2","topic","imo"],"body":"<h1>Fire-Fighting Equipment Guide — What is on board, how many, and how it is kept</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Equipment</th><th>SOLAS II-2</th><th>Detail in this note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water</td><td>Fire pumps, fire main, hydrants, hoses, nozzles, international shore connection</td><td>10.2</td><td>Fire pumps section, emergency fire pump</td></tr><tr><td>Portable</td><td>Extinguishers, spare charges, foam applicator</td><td>10.3</td><td>Portable extinguishers section</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed</td><td>CO₂, high-expansion foam, water spray, local application, sprinklers</td><td>10.4 to 10.9</td><td>Fixed systems section</td></tr><tr><td>Detection</td><td>Fire detection and alarm</td><td>7, 10.6</td><td>Detection section</td></tr><tr><td>Personal</td><td>Fire-fighter's outfit, breathing apparatus, EEBD</td><td>10.10, 13.3.4</td><td>Personal protection section</td></tr><tr><td>Plans and training</td><td>Fire control plan, training manual</td><td>15</td><td>Plans section</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ffe-main\"><figcaption>The fire main</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Two jets of water from two different hydrants must reach every part of the ship. Everything else on this list is there to back that up, or to protect the people using it.</blockquote><h2>Fire pumps, hydrants, hoses (SOLAS II-2/10.2)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Fire pumps, number</b></td><td>Passenger ≥4,000 GT: <b>three</b>, independently driven. Passenger under 4,000 GT: <b>two</b>. Cargo ≥1,000 GT: <b>two</b>. Cargo under 1,000 GT: <b>two</b> power-driven, one independently driven</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pumps used</b></td><td>Sanitary, ballast, bilge or general service pumps, if not normally used for oil</td></tr><tr><td><b>Emergency fire pump</b></td><td>Needed if one fire could disable all the pumps. Its power and sea connection are <b>outside the main pump spaces</b>. Its space is not next to a Category A or main pump space. Access by an airlock or remotely operated watertight door, with a second route</td></tr><tr><td><b>Total capacity</b></td><td>Passenger: <b>two-thirds</b> of the bilge pump quantity. Cargo: <b>four-thirds</b> of the bilge quantity of a same-size passenger ship, <b>capped at 180 m³/h</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Each pump</b></td><td><b>≥80%</b> of the total divided by the minimum pump count, and <b>≥25 m³/h</b>. Each must deliver the two required jets</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pressure at hydrants</b> (two pumps running)</td><td>Passenger ≥4,000 GT <b>0.40 N/mm²</b>. Passenger under 4,000 GT <b>0.30</b>. Cargo ≥6,000 GT <b>0.27</b>. Cargo under 6,000 GT <b>0.25</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Fire main size</b></td><td>For two pumps running together. A cargo ship's main need only carry <b>140 m³/h</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Hydrants</b></td><td><b>Two jets</b> from different hydrants, one from a single length of hose, reach any normally accessible part, any empty cargo space and any ro-ro or vehicle space</td></tr><tr><td><b>Isolating valves</b></td><td>Outside the machinery space and accessible. A valve on each hydrant lets a hose be removed with the pumps running. <b>Relief valves</b> where pumps can exceed design pressure</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ready water</b></td><td>Cargo ships with a machinery space unattended or only one person on watch: water delivered immediately by <b>remote start</b> of a main pump from the bridge and fire control station, or by <b>permanent pressurization</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>International shore connection</b></td><td>Ships <b>≥500 GT</b>, usable on either side. Dimensions in the FSS Code</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Hoses and nozzles</b></p><ul><li><b>Length:</b> at least <b>10 m</b>. At most <b>15 m</b> in machinery spaces, <b>20 m</b> elsewhere and on open decks, <b>25 m</b> on open decks where the breadth is over 30 m</li><li><b>Couplings</b> fully interchangeable, unless each hydrant has its own hose and nozzle</li><li><b>Number, cargo ship ≥1,000 GT:</b> one for each <b>30 m</b> of ship length, plus one spare, <b>at least five</b>. Engine and boiler room hoses are not counted. Ships carrying dangerous goods add <b>three</b></li><li><b>Number, cargo ship under 1,000 GT:</b> the same rule, at least <b>three</b></li><li><b>Passenger ships:</b> at least one per hydrant, used only for fire-fighting or testing</li><li><b>Nozzle sizes:</b> <b>12, 16 and 19 mm</b>. 12 mm is enough in accommodation and service spaces. In machinery spaces and outside, the size that gives the most water from two jets, <b>not above 19 mm</b></li><li><b>Nozzle type:</b> dual-purpose (spray and jet), with a shut-off</li></ul><h2>Portable extinguishers (SOLAS II-2/10.3, FSS Code chapter 4)</h2><ul><li><b>Approved types</b> only, to IMO guidelines (FSS 1.2)</li><li><b>Size:</b> powder or CO₂ at least <b>5 kg</b>. Foam at least <b>9 L</b>. No portable unit above <b>23 kg</b>, and each at least as effective as a 9 L fluid extinguisher</li><li><b>Number:</b> suitable types in accommodation, service and control spaces. Ships <b>≥1,000 GT carry at least five</b>. One near the entrance of each space</li><li><b>No CO₂ extinguishers in accommodation.</b> Electrical and electronic spaces use non-conductive media that do not damage the equipment</li><li><b>Condition:</b> visible, quickly reachable, protected from weather and vibration, with a <b>used indicator</b></li><li><b>Spare charges:</b> <b>100%</b> of the first <b>10</b> rechargeable extinguishers and <b>50%</b> of the rest, <b>maximum 60</b>. Recharging instructions on board. A non-rechargeable unit gets extra extinguishers instead</li><li><b>Recharging</b> only with the charge approved for that extinguisher</li><li><b>Portable foam applicator:</b> inductor nozzle on the fire main by a hose, a tank of at least <b>20 L</b> of foam liquid plus a spare tank, foam at least <b>1.5 m³/min</b></li></ul><h2>By space: what else is required</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Space</th><th>Required</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Machinery, oil-fired boiler or oil fuel unit</b> (Cat A)</td><td>Fixed system. A <b>portable foam applicator</b>. At least <b>two</b> portable foam extinguishers per firing space. A foam extinguisher of at least <b>135 L</b> per boiler room, with hose reels. <b>0.1 m³</b> of sand per firing space, with a shovel</td></tr><tr><td><b>Machinery, internal combustion</b> (Cat A)</td><td>Fixed system. A portable foam applicator. Foam extinguishers of at least <b>45 L</b>, no point more than <b>10 m</b> walking distance from one, at least two in the space</td></tr><tr><td><b>Steam turbines or enclosed steam engines</b> (≥375 kW, periodically unattended)</td><td>Fixed system. Foam extinguishers of at least <b>45 L</b> with the same 10 m rule</td></tr><tr><td><b>Local application</b></td><td>Passenger ≥500 GT and cargo ≥2,000 GT: a fixed water-based local system in Cat A spaces over <b>500 m³</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Cargo spaces</b></td><td>Cargo ships ≥2,000 GT (not ro-ro or vehicle spaces): fixed <b>CO₂ or inert gas</b>, or an equivalent. Dangerous goods ships: the same. Ore, coal and grain ships may be exempted, with steel hatch covers</td></tr><tr><td><b>Paint lockers</b></td><td><b>CO₂</b> for <b>40%</b> of the gross volume, or dry powder at <b>0.5 kg/m³</b>, or water spray at <b>5 L/m²·min</b>. Operable from outside</td></tr><tr><td><b>Galley deep-fat cooking</b></td><td>Automatic or manual extinguishing system, primary and backup thermostats with a failure alarm, automatic power cut-off, galley alarm</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tankers</b></td><td>Deck foam system (FSS Code chapter 14). Cargo pump-room: fixed CO₂, high-expansion foam or water spray, from outside</td></tr><tr><td><b>Passenger ships over 36 passengers</b></td><td>Sprinklers, detection and alarm in control stations, accommodation and service spaces. Two water fog applicators per Cat A space</td></tr><ul><li><b>Fixed systems allowed:</b> gas, high-expansion foam, pressure water-spray. <b>Halon 1211, 1301, 2402 and perfluorocarbons are banned.</b> Steam is not allowed except as a small extra in restricted areas</li><li><b>Storage</b> is behind the forward collision bulkhead, for no other purpose, with its own entrance. Below deck it must be within one deck of the open deck, with at least <b>6 air changes an hour</b> by mechanical exhaust</li></ul></tbody></table><h2>Fixed systems: the technical figures (FSS Code chapters 5 to 8)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Specification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>CO₂, cargo spaces</b></td><td>Free gas at least <b>30%</b> of the gross volume of the largest cargo space protected</td></tr><tr><td><b>CO₂, machinery spaces</b></td><td>The larger of <b>40%</b> of the largest space's gross volume (casing partly excluded) or <b>35%</b> of its gross volume including the casing. Cargo ships under 2,000 GT with two or more non-separate machinery spaces counted as one: <b>35%</b> and <b>30%</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>CO₂ calculation</b></td><td>Free CO₂ taken as <b>0.56 m³/kg</b>. Starting-air receivers' free air volume is added to the space volume</td></tr><tr><td><b>CO₂ discharge</b></td><td><b>85%</b> of the gas into a machinery space within <b>2 min</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>CO₂ alarm and controls</b></td><td>Audible pre-discharge alarm for at least <b>20 s</b>. <b>Two separate controls</b>, one for the pipe valve and one for the cylinders, in a clearly marked release box. Any key in a break-glass enclosure</td></tr><tr><td><b>CO₂ piping and storage</b></td><td>Pipes pressure-tested at not less than <b>5 N/mm²</b> after installation, not run through refrigerated spaces. Nozzles give uniform distribution. Containers outside the protected space unless the Administration permits otherwise. Spare parts on board</td></tr><tr><td><b>Steam</b> (where allowed)</td><td>At least <b>1 kg of steam per hour per 0.75 m³</b> of the largest protected space</td></tr><tr><td><b>High-expansion foam</b></td><td>Fills the largest protected space at <b>1 m depth per minute</b>. Foam volume <b>five times</b> the space. Expansion ratio not above <b>1,000:1</b>. Generators at least <b>450 mm</b> from the space, delivery ducts of steel at least <b>5 mm</b>, stainless dampers at least <b>3 mm</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Low-expansion foam</b></td><td><b>150 mm</b> depth over the largest oil-spread area within <b>5 min</b>. Expansion ratio not above <b>12:1</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Deck foam, tankers</b> (chapter 14)</td><td>Solution rate the greatest of <b>0.6 L/min/m²</b> of cargo tank deck area, <b>6 L/min/m²</b> of the largest tank's section, or <b>3 L/min/m²</b> of the monitor-protected area (at least <b>1,250 L/min</b>). Supply <b>20 min</b> with inert gas, <b>30 min</b> without. Applicators at least <b>400 L/min</b>, throw at least <b>15 m</b>, at least four</td></tr><tr><td><b>Water-spray and water-mist</b></td><td>Average at least <b>5 L/m²/min</b>. Pump supplies all sections of a compartment together at the required pressure and <b>starts automatically</b> on a pressure drop. Nozzles over bilges, tank tops and other oil-spread or high-hazard areas. Pump and controls outside the protected space</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sprinklers: layout</b></td><td>Wet-pipe (saunas dry-pipe, heads up to <b>140 °C</b>). Sections of at most <b>200</b> sprinklers, passenger ships at most <b>two decks</b> and one main vertical zone. Each section: one <b>stop valve</b>, a <b>pressure gauge</b>, a <b>test valve</b>. Spacing for an average of at least <b>5 L/m²/min</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Sprinklers: heads</b></td><td>Operating range <b>68 to 79 °C</b> in accommodation. Spare heads: <b>6</b> (under 300 installed), <b>12</b> (300 to 1,000), <b>24</b> (over 1,000)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sprinklers: water</b></td><td><b>Pressure tank</b> holds at least <b>twice</b> the standing charge (one minute of pump discharge), air pressure kept up, sight gauge, seawater kept out. An <b>independent power pump</b> starts automatically on pressure drop and covers at least <b>280 m²</b> at the required rate, with a test valve. Fire main connection through a <b>lockable non-return valve</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Sprinklers: power and alarm</b></td><td><b>Two power sources</b>: passenger ships with an automatic changeover switch near the pump, cargo ships with the main source fed by at least two generators. Pump and tank remote from Category A spaces. Visual and audible alarm per section at the bridge or a manned control station, with a fault indication. Means to test the automatic start</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Detection and alarm (FSS Code chapter 9)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Specification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Smoke detectors</b> (stairways, corridors, escape routes)</td><td>Operate before <b>12.5%/m</b> obscuration and not below <b>2%/m</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Heat detectors</b></td><td>Operate before <b>78 °C</b> and not below <b>54 °C</b>, at a rise under <b>1 °C/min</b>. Drying rooms and similar up to <b>130 °C</b>, saunas up to <b>140 °C</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Spacing: heat</b></td><td>Up to <b>37 m²</b> per detector, <b>9 m</b> between centres, <b>4.5 m</b> from bulkheads</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spacing: smoke</b></td><td>Up to <b>74 m²</b> per detector, <b>11 m</b> between centres, <b>5.5 m</b> from bulkheads</td></tr><tr><td><b>Overhead position</b></td><td>At least <b>0.5 m</b> from bulkheads, except in corridors, lockers and stairways</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sections</b></td><td>At most <b>50</b> enclosed spaces in one section</td></tr><tr><td><b>Alarm</b></td><td>An audible alarm sounds if a fire signal is <b>not acknowledged within 2 min</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Emergency fire pump (FSS Code chapter 12)</h2><ul><li><b>Capacity</b> at least <b>40%</b> of the total fire pump capacity, and not less than <b>25 m³/h</b> (passenger ships under 1,000 GT, cargo ships <b>2,000 GT and above</b>) or <b>15 m³/h</b> (cargo ships under 2,000 GT)</li><li><b>Diesel drive:</b> starts by hand at <b>0 °C</b>, with heating if that is impracticable. Other starting means: at least <b>six starts in 30 min</b>, two in the first 10 min</li><li><b>Fuel:</b> service tank for <b>3 h</b> at full load, with reserve outside the machinery space for <b>15 h</b> more</li></ul><h2>Personal protection</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ffe-outfit\"><figcaption>The fire-fighter's outfit</figcaption></figure><p><b>Fire-fighter's outfit (FSS Code chapter 3)</b></p><ul><li><b>Protective clothing</b> against radiant heat, burns and steam scalds, with a water-resistant outer surface</li><li><b>Boots</b> of rubber or other non-conducting material. <b>Rigid helmet</b> with impact protection</li><li><b>Electric safety lamp</b> with at least <b>3 h</b> burning time, explosion-proof on tankers and in hazardous areas</li><li><b>Axe</b> with a high-voltage insulated handle</li><li><b>Breathing apparatus:</b> compressed air with at least <b>1,200 L</b> in the cylinders, or another type lasting at least <b>30 min</b>. Cylinders interchangeable</li><li><b>Lifeline:</b> fireproof, at least <b>30 m</b>, withstands <b>3.5 kN for 5 min</b>, snap-hook to the harness or a belt</li></ul><p><b>How many (II-2/10.10)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship</th><th>Outfits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>All ships</b></td><td>At least <b>two</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Tankers</b></td><td><b>Two more</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Passenger ships</b></td><td>For every <b>80 m</b> (or part) of the length of passenger and service spaces on the deck with the most, <b>two outfits</b> and <b>two sets of personal equipment</b>. Over 36 passengers: <b>two more outfits</b> in each main vertical zone</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spare air charges</b></td><td><b>Two</b> per breathing apparatus. One per apparatus on a passenger ship of up to 36 passengers, or a cargo ship that can recharge cylinders. Passenger ship over 36: at least two</td></tr><ul><li>Stored where <b>easily accessible</b>, <b>permanently marked</b>, and <b>widely separated</b> if more than one</li><li>Passenger ships over 36 passengers: one <b>water fog applicator</b> per pair of breathing apparatus, stored beside it</li></ul></tbody></table><p><b>Emergency escape breathing devices (II-2/13.3.4)</b></p><ul><li><b>Accommodation:</b> at least <b>two</b>, on every ship</li><li><b>Passenger ships:</b> at least <b>two in each main vertical zone</b>, and <b>two more</b> if over 36 passengers</li><li><b>Machinery spaces:</b> kept in easily visible places, reachable quickly at any time. Number and place consider the layout and the people who work there. They are shown on the <b>fire control plan</b></li><li><b>Specification:</b> service time at least <b>10 min</b>. Flame-resistant hood or full face piece with a clear window. For <b>escape only</b>: not for fire-fighting, not for entering oxygen-deficient spaces, and not worn by fire-fighters</li><li><b>Marking:</b> maintenance needs, trademark, serial number, shelf life with the date of manufacture, approving authority. Training units are marked</li><li>Spare devices are carried. Crew training in EEBD is part of on-board training</li></ul><h2>Plans, manual and training (II-2/15)</h2><ul><li><b>Fire control plans:</b> general arrangement plans <b>permanently exhibited</b> for officers, showing for each deck the control stations, A-class and B-class sections, detection, alarm, sprinklers and extinguishing appliances, access routes, and the ventilation fan controls and damper positions. Alternatively a booklet, a copy for each officer and one always on board. Kept up to date</li><li>A <b>duplicate set</b> is stored in a <b>prominently marked weathertight enclosure outside the deckhouse</b> for shore-side fire-fighters</li><li><b>Training manual:</b> one in each crew mess room and recreation room, or in each crew cabin. In the ship's working language, illustrated where possible. Covers fire safety, fire-fighting procedures, the ship's alarms, appliances, fire doors, dampers and escape</li><li><b>Drills</b> are recorded (III/19). Interval in SOLAS 1974</li></ul><h2>Maintenance and testing</h2><p>*Intervals from Malta Technical Notice SLS.6 Rev.4 (Feb 2025), which follows MSC.1/Circ.1432. Other flag States may differ.*</p><table><thead><tr><th>Equipment</th><th>Weekly</th><th>Monthly</th><th>Annual</th><th>Longer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Fire main, hydrants, hoses, nozzles</b></td><td></td><td>Check. Run pumps, confirm pressure</td><td>Inspect, flow-test pumps, test hydrant valves, check relief valves</td><td><b>Hoses:</b> a sample pressure-tested so all are tested within <b>5 years</b>, at maximum fire main pressure. Shore connection checked <b>quarterly</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Fire-fighter's outfit</b></td><td></td><td>Lockers hold the full inventory, serviceable</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>SCBA</b></td><td>General check and cylinder gauges</td><td></td><td><b>Air quality</b> tested at an accredited laboratory. Masks and demand valves checked</td><td><b>5-yearly:</b> cylinders tested at an approved shore station</td></tr><tr><td><b>EEBD</b></td><td>General check and gauges</td><td></td><td>Per maker</td><td>Hydrostatic test at maker's interval, at a shore facility</td></tr><tr><td><b>Portable extinguishers</b></td><td></td><td></td><td>Serviced per the ship's schedule. Permanently pressurised units go ashore</td><td><b>5-yearly:</b> discharge-test at least one of each type made in the same year. <b>10-yearly:</b> hydraulic test of extinguishers and cartridges</td></tr><tr><td><b>Detection and alarm</b></td><td>Panel lamp test</td><td>A sample of detectors and call points, so all are tested within <b>5 years</b></td><td>Full test, including units that release extinguishing systems</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>Fire doors, dampers</b></td><td>Panel indicators</td><td></td><td>Remote release of doors and dampers</td><td><b>Quarterly:</b> local operation of fire doors and dampers</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sprinklers, water spray</b></td><td>Panel, pumps, valves</td><td>Valve positions, tank levels, pump starts</td><td>Test with section valves, flow-test pumps</td><td>5-yearly valve inspection. 10-yearly cylinder tests</td></tr><tr><td><b>Foam systems</b></td><td></td><td>Valve positions</td><td>Test, sample concentrate</td><td><b>Quarterly:</b> foam tank quantity. <b>5-yearly:</b> inspect control valves</td></tr><tr><td><b>Other fixed gas</b></td><td>Panel, valve positions</td><td>Gauges, leaks</td><td>Inspect</td><td><b>Biennial:</b> weigh cylinders, refill below <b>95%</b>. <b>10-yearly:</b> test 10% of cylinders</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Fixed CO₂ (MSC.1/Circ.1318/Rev.1)</b></p><ul><li><b>Monthly (at least every 30 days):</b> visual check for damage, stop valves closed, release controls accessible, cylinders secured, alarms in place</li><li><b>Annual:</b> protected-space boundaries, containers, piping and nozzles, manifold hoses, doors and signs</li><li><b>Biennial</b> (2 years ± 3 months for passenger ships, at each survey for cargo ships): weigh cylinders, <b>refill any below 90%</b> of nominal charge, blow-test piping, test the activating heads and alarms</li><li><b>10-yearly:</b> internally inspect and hydrostatically test at least <b>10%</b> of cylinders. If any fail, test <b>50%</b>, then all. <b>5-yearly:</b> inspect control valves internally</li></ul><h2>Certificates and records</h2><p><b>For the ship</b></p><ul><li><b>Approved types:</b> extinguishers and equipment are approved under IMO guidelines (FSS Code 1.2)</li><li><b>Records</b> of inspection, maintenance and tests. Extinguisher <b>test dates</b> are stamped on CO₂ cylinders and tagged on other types</li><li><b>SCBA</b> air-quality test from an accredited laboratory (ISO/IEC 17025), each year</li><li><b>Foam concentrate</b> sample tests (MSC.1/Circ.1312 for low expansion, MSC/Circ.670 for high expansion)</li><li><b>EEBD</b> shelf life and serial number marked on each unit</li><li><b>Fire control plan</b> and <b>training manual</b> on board, drills recorded</li></ul><p><b>For people</b></p><ul><li><b>STCW A-VI/1-2, basic fire prevention and fire-fighting:</b> fire organization, location of appliances and escape routes, the fire triangle, ignition sources, fire and smoke detection, classes of fire and the right agent, and use of fire-fighting equipment and breathing apparatus</li><li><b>STCW A-VI/3, advanced fire-fighting:</b> control fire-fighting operations, organize and train fire parties, <b>inspect and service</b> detection and extinguishing systems, and investigate fire incidents</li><li>Renewal intervals are in STCW 1978</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>FSS Code technical standard and amendment history</li><li>SOLAS 1974 chapter II-2, drills and certificates</li><li>FTP Code fire test procedures</li><li>IGC Code, IBC Code gas and chemical tankers</li><li>Life-Saving Appliances Guide the matching guide for life-saving equipment</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The <b>fire part of the Record of Equipment</b> (Form E) and the exact certificate wording</li><li><b>Extinguisher classes</b> (A, B, C, D) and which agent suits which class</li><li><b>Fire hose</b> material and construction standards, and the <b>fire blanket</b></li><li>Which <b>detection and sprinkler</b> coverage each ship type needs (SOLAS II-2/7 and 10), and <b>EEBD</b> numbers for cargo pump-rooms and tankers</li><li>The text of <b>MSC.1/Circ.1432</b> itself. The intervals come from a flag State notice that follows it</li><li>SOLAS II-2 regulations <b>14</b> (operational readiness, maintenance plan) and <b>16</b> (operations booklet)</li><li>The monthly <b>drill</b> rule, and how many crew join each fire drill</li><li>STCW A-VI/1-2 and A-VI/3 <b>eligibility</b> and assessment criteria</li><li>Which ships need an <b>emergency fire pump</b> (the FSS chapter's application wording was not clear in the source)</li><li>FSS chapter <b>13 to 17</b> details (arrangement of means of escape, inert gas, helicopter foam), the <b>inert gas system</b>, <b>water-mist</b> design tests, and <b>fixed gas detection</b></li><li>Requirements for <b>passenger ships built on or after 1 Jan 2026</b>, ro-ro spaces and <b>PFOS</b> (see the SOLAS and FSS notes)</li><li>Flag State variations, including the Philippines</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>UK MCA, SOLAS II-2 Regulation 10 Fire-fighting: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f91bde5274a2e8ab4cf90/Regulation_10_-_Fire-Fighting.pdf</li><li>UK MCA, Regulation 13 Means of escape (EEBD): https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a80f3c6ed915d74e33fd543/Regulation_13_-_Means_of_Escape.pdf</li><li>UK MCA, Regulation 15 Instructions, on-board training and drills: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a747c16ed915d0e8e3988ff/Regulation_15_-_Instructions__On-Board_Training_and_Drills.pdf</li><li>FSS Code text (chapters 2 to 9, 12, 14, as printed by the US Government Printing Office; figures cross-checked against the FSS Code note): https://corpora.tika.apache.org/base/docs/govdocs1/668/668672.pdf</li><li>Transport Malta, Technical Notice SLS.6 Rev.4 (Feb 2025), fire protection maintenance: https://transport.gov.mt/maritime/merchant-shipping-notices/technical-notices/technical-notice-sls-6-rev-4-fire-protection-systems-and-appliances-and-compressed-gas-cylinders-periodic-maintenance-inspection-and-testing-2477</li><li>IMO MSC.1/Circ.1318/Rev.1, fixed CO₂ maintenance (HK Marine Department copy): https://www.mardep.gov.hk/en/msnote/pdf/msin2136anx1.pdf</li><li>STCW Table A-VI/1-2: https://weh.maritime.edu/stcw/2018/tables/612.html</li><li>STCW Table A-VI/3: https://weh.maritime.edu/stcw/2018/tables/63.html</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-gmdss","v":139232417,"title":"GMDSS","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["gmdss","imo","system","radio","distress","solas","safety","topic"],"body":"<h1>GMDSS — Global Maritime Distress and Safety System</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>The worldwide system of radio equipment and procedures for distress alerting, search and rescue communications and maritime safety information</td></tr><tr><td>Legal basis</td><td><b>SOLAS Chapter IV</b>, with the ITU Radio Regulations for frequencies and procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Fully in force</td><td><b>1 Feb 1999</b></td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Passenger ships and cargo ships of <b>300 GT and over</b> on international voyages</td></tr><tr><td>Modernized</td><td><b>MSC.496(105)</b> and <b>MSC.497(105)</b>, in force <b>1 Jan 2024</b></td></tr><tr><td>Partners</td><td>IMO works with the ITU, WMO, IHO, IMSO and the Cospas-Sarsat partners</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> GMDSS means a ship in distress is heard by rescue centres and nearby ships automatically, not just by someone listening. What you carry depends on the <b>sea area</b> you trade in.</blockquote><h2>The nine functions (Reg IV/4)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"gmdss-functions\"><figcaption>The nine functions every ship must be able to perform</figcaption></figure><p>1. Ship-to-shore distress alerting by <b>at least two separate and independent means</b></p><p>2. Shore-to-ship distress alert relay</p><p>3. Ship-to-ship distress alerting</p><p>4. Search and rescue coordination communications</p><p>5. On-scene communications</p><p>6. Transmitting and receiving locating signals</p><p>7. Receiving maritime safety information (MSI)</p><p>8. Urgency and safety communications</p><p>9. Bridge-to-bridge communications</p><p>General radiocommunications are required in addition (Reg IV/4.1.2).</p><h2>Sea areas (Reg IV/2)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Definition (as amended 2024)</th><th>Rough range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>A1</b></td><td>Within coverage of a VHF coast station with continuous DSC alerting</td><td>About 20 to 30 nm</td></tr><tr><td><b>A2</b></td><td>Outside A1, within coverage of an MF coast station with continuous DSC alerting</td><td>About 100 nm</td></tr><tr><td><b>A3</b></td><td>Outside A1 and A2, within coverage of a recognized mobile satellite service with continuous alerting</td><td>Depends on the satellite service fitted</td></tr><tr><td><b>A4</b></td><td>Outside A1, A2 and A3</td><td>Whatever is left</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"gmdss-areas\"><figcaption>What each sea area needs</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Since 2024 A3 is no longer tied to Inmarsat. Coverage depends on the recognized mobile satellite service (RMSS) ship earth station (SES) you fit</li><li>Where two SES units are fitted, the one with the smaller coverage sets the ship's A3 area. The duplicate must cover at least the primary's range</li><li>Using another RMSS system (for example Iridium instead of Inmarsat) is subject to flag State acceptance</li></ul><h2>Equipment</h2><h3>For every ship</h3><ul><li><b>VHF</b> with DSC on channel 70 and radiotelephony on channels <b>6, 13 and 16</b></li><li>A <b>406 MHz EPIRB</b></li><li>A <b>radar SART or AIS-SART</b></li><li>Two-way VHF radiotelephone for survival craft and SARTs. Since 2024 these requirements are in <b>Reg IV/7</b>, moved from SOLAS III/6</li><li>Cargo ships <b>500 GT and over</b> and passenger ships carry <b>2 SARTs</b>. Cargo ships <b>300 to under 500 GT</b> carry <b>1</b></li></ul><h3>By sea area (Regs IV/8 to IV/11)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Required (primary and duplicated)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A1</td><td>VHF (IV/7.1.1)</td></tr><tr><td>A2</td><td>VHF and <b>MF</b> (IV/9.1.1)</td></tr><tr><td>A3</td><td>VHF and either <b>MF/HF</b> (IV/11.1.1) or an <b>RMSS SES</b> (IV/10.1.1)</td></tr><tr><td>A4</td><td>VHF and <b>MF/HF</b> (IV/11.1.1)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>MF/HF is no longer primary equipment for A3. It stays primary for A4</li><li>A single MF/HF installation may count as both the MF and the MF/HF installation</li><li>Under COMSAR.1/Circ.32/Rev.3, A3 can be met with four units: 2 VHF, 1 MF/HF and 1 RMSS</li></ul><h3>What changed on 1 Jan 2024</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Regulation numbers</td><td>Reg 8 is A1 only, Reg 9 is A2, Reg 10 is A3, Reg 11 is A4. Before 2024 each one also covered the lower areas</td></tr><tr><td>VHF-EPIRB</td><td>No longer accepted in place of a satellite EPIRB. A1-only ships need a satellite EPIRB</td></tr><tr><td>A3 alternative</td><td>The old option of VHF, MF/HF and an EPIRB is gone. MF/HF-only A3 ships must fit at least one SES</td></tr><tr><td>NBDP</td><td>Narrow-band direct printing is no longer a GMDSS requirement. HF NBDP can still be kept to receive MSI</td></tr><tr><td>MSI receivers</td><td><b>NAVTEX</b> where an international NAVTEX service is provided in the area. Otherwise an <b>EGC</b> receiver (SafetyNET or Iridium SafetyCast) covering the area, under MSC.1/Circ.1645</td></tr><tr><td>Performance standards</td><td>New VHF and MF/HF DSC standards in MSC.511(105) and MSC.512(105)</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Transitional rules</b></p><ul><li>Equipment installed on or after 1 Jan 2024 must meet the new standards. Under IACS UI SC298, for ships contracted on or after 1 Jan 2024 any installation must meet them</li><li><b>1 Jan 2028:</b> flag States may allow VHF, MF and MF/HF equipment and Inmarsat-C SES approved under the old standards to keep being installed (MSC.1/Circ.1676). The deadline for VHF equipment to follow the revised ITU Appendix 18 channelling is also 1 Jan 2028 (MSC.1/Circ.1460/Rev.4)</li><li>Existing ships get revised certificates at the first radio survey after 1 Jan 2024, whether or not equipment changes. Certificates must also be updated when GMDSS equipment is added or removed</li></ul><h2>How a distress alert travels</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"gmdss-alert\"><figcaption>Three ways a distress alert goes out</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>DSC</b> (digital selective calling): the alert identifies the ship in distress and its position. If nobody acknowledges it by DSC, it repeats automatically every few minutes</li><li><b>Satellite SES:</b> sent straight to the rescue coordination centre</li><li><b>EPIRB:</b> the 406 MHz signal goes through Cospas-Sarsat to the rescue coordination centre</li><li>Satellite alerts go directly to the RCC, so nearby ships do not receive them directly</li></ul><p><b>DSC distress frequencies</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Band</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>VHF</td><td>Channel 70, <b>156.525 MHz</b></td></tr><tr><td>MF</td><td><b>2187.5 kHz</b></td></tr><tr><td>HF</td><td>4207.5, 6312, 8414.5, 12577 and 16804.5 kHz</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Voice and priority (ITU Radio Regulations)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Signal</th><th>Priority</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Distress</td><td><b>MAYDAY</b></td><td>Absolute priority over everything</td></tr><tr><td>Urgency</td><td><b>PAN PAN</b></td><td>Below distress, above everything else</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td><b>SECURITE</b></td><td>Below distress and urgency</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>VHF channel 16 is <b>156.8 MHz</b>. A ship without DSC should, where practical, start its distress call on channel 16</li><li>The rescue authority may impose radio silence with <b>SEELONCE MAYDAY</b>. Stations that are not part of the distress traffic must stay off the frequencies until normal working is announced</li></ul><h2>Cospas-Sarsat and the EPIRB</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"gmdss-cospas\"><figcaption>From a 406 MHz beacon to a rescue centre</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>406 MHz</b> is reserved internationally for distress. The EPIRB band is 406 to 406.1 MHz</li><li>Three beacon types: <b>EPIRB</b> (maritime), <b>ELT</b> (aviation) and <b>PLB</b> (personal)</li><li>Each beacon sends a unique <b>HEX ID</b>. If the beacon is registered, responders also see the ship, owner and emergency contacts. <b>Keep your registration current</b></li><li>Satellites are in low, medium and geostationary orbit, provided by the United States, the Russian Federation, India and EUMETSAT</li><li>Ground chain: satellite, <b>LUT</b> (decodes and calculates the position), national <b>MCC</b>, then the <b>RCC</b>, which verifies and sends help</li><li>The system has saved <b>over 50,000</b> people worldwide since 1982 (NOAA, 2024)</li><li>EPIRB test every year. EPIRB maintenance at intervals of no more than <b>5 years</b> (Reg IV/15)</li></ul><h2>Maritime safety information (MSI)</h2><h3>NAVTEX</h3><ul><li>International broadcasts on <b>518 kHz</b>. Also <b>490 kHz</b> (often in a local language) and <b>4209.5 kHz</b></li><li>Designed range about <b>400 nm</b></li><li>IMO designates NAVTEX as the primary means of sending coastal urgent MSI to ships</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"gmdss-navtex\"><figcaption>Reading a NAVTEX message header</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>B2</th><th>Subject</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>Navigational warnings</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Meteorological warnings</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Ice reports</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Search and rescue, piracy warnings, tsunamis and other natural phenomena</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>Meteorological forecasts</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>Pilot and VTS messages</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>AIS service messages</td></tr><tr><td>L</td><td>Other navigational warnings</td></tr><tr><td>Z</td><td>No message on hand</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Each message starts with a four-character header: <b>B1</b> station, <b>B2</b> subject, <b>B3 B4</b> message number</li><li>The receiver uses B1 and B2 to accept or reject messages, and B3 B4 to avoid repeats</li><li>Signal: narrow-band direct printing with forward error correction (Mode B), 100 baud FSK</li><li><b>SafetyNET</b> (Inmarsat) broadcasts MSI between about 76°N and 76°S. <b>Iridium SafetyCast</b> is the equivalent on the Iridium service</li></ul><h2>Watches, power and maintenance</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"gmdss-power\"><figcaption>Reserve power, maintenance and watches</figcaption></figure><p><b>Radio watch (Reg IV/12)</b></p><ul><li>Continuous DSC watch on <b>VHF channel 70</b></li><li><b>2187.5 kHz</b> if MF DSC is fitted</li><li><b>8414.5 kHz</b> and at least one other HF DSC frequency if HF DSC is fitted</li><li>A listening watch on <b>channel 16</b> where practicable</li><li>Distress alerts by satellite if an SES is fitted. MSI by NAVTEX or EGC as fitted</li></ul><p><b>Reserve source of energy (Reg IV/13)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 hour</b> if the ship has a compliant emergency source of power, <b>6 hours</b> if not</li><li>Batteries recharge within <b>10 hours</b>. Capacity is checked at intervals of no more than <b>12 months</b> when the ship is not at sea</li></ul><p><b>Keeping equipment available (Reg IV/15)</b></p><ul><li>A1 and A2 ships use <b>at least one</b> method. A3 and A4 ships use <b>at least two</b></li><li>The three methods: <b>duplication</b> of equipment, <b>shore-based maintenance</b>, <b>at-sea electronic maintenance</b> by qualified personnel such as an ETO</li></ul><p><b>People, position and records</b></p><ul><li><b>Reg IV/16:</b> qualified radio personnel. One person is designated to lead distress radiocommunications. Passenger ships also have at least one person doing distress communications only</li><li><b>Reg IV/17:</b> radio incidents important to safety of life are recorded</li><li><b>Reg IV/18:</b> if the navigation receiver fails, update the position by hand at least every <b>4 hours</b> while under way</li><li><b>UK practice (MGN 641):</b> radio logs kept 1 year on board, 7 years in total</li></ul><h2>What inspectors look at</h2><ul><li>The radio certificate, and that equipment matches the area the ship trades in</li><li>EPIRB registration and the last annual test</li><li>Maintenance method for the area (one or two methods)</li><li>Reserve battery capacity</li><li>Operator certificates and the person designated for distress communications</li><li>The radio log</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>SOLAS 1974 Chapter IV</li><li>SAR 1979 search and rescue regions</li><li>INMARSAT 1976 and International Code of Signals</li><li>LSA Code survival craft radio equipment</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Step-by-step distress procedure and cancelling a false alert</li><li>Daily, weekly and monthly equipment test intervals</li><li>Radio operator certificate categories (GOC, ROC) in the STCW Convention</li><li>BeiDou and other satellite services still being recognized</li><li>NAVDAT and VDES, which are still being introduced</li><li>Cospas-Sarsat founding date and the end of 121.5 MHz processing</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO, Radiocommunications page: https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/safety/pages/radiocommunications-default.aspx</li><li>Resolution MSC.496(105), amended SOLAS Chapter IV: https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/file/MSC.496%28105%29.pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.509(105), GMDSS sea areas and radio services criteria: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.509(105).pdf</li><li>Bureau Veritas, modernized GMDSS in force 1 Jan 2024: https://marine-offshore.bureauveritas.com/newsroom/modernization-gmdss-entry-force-1-january-2024-amended-solas-chapter-iv</li><li>ABS, amendment to SOLAS Chapter IV: https://ww2.eagle.org/content/dam/eagle/regulatory-news/2024/Amendment-to-SOLAS-Chapter-IV.pdf</li><li>Key points of the modernized GMDSS (TransNav): https://www.transnav.eu/files/Key_Points_of_the_Modernized_GMDSS_System,1284.pdf</li><li>UK MCA, MGN 641 (radiocommunications under SOLAS IV): https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-641-m-guidance-on-radiocommunications-under-chapter-iv-of-solas/mgn-641-m-guidance-on-radiocommunications-under-chapter-iv-of-solas</li><li>AMSA, GMDSS Handbook 2018: https://www.amsa.gov.au/sites/default/files/gmdss-handbook-2018.pdf</li><li>ITU Radio Regulations, distress and safety chapter: https://life.itu.int/radioclub/rr/chapt-7.pdf</li><li>NAVTEX in the United States (USCG NAVCEN): https://navcen.uscg.gov/node/228</li><li>Cospas-Sarsat system overview (NOAA): https://www.sarsat.noaa.gov/sys-diag.html</li><li>Cospas-Sarsat partnership (NOAA): https://www.sarsat.noaa.gov/cospas-sarsat-partnership/</li><li>Cospas-Sarsat overview 2024 (NOAA): https://www.sarsat.noaa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024-SAR-WKSHOP_SARSAT-Overview.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-hague-visby-hamburg-and-rotterdam-rules","v":3417498,"title":"Hague-Visby, Hamburg and Rotterdam Rules","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["hague-visby","hamburg","rotterdam","bill-of-lading","cargo-claims","carrier-liability","convention","imo","topic"],"body":"<h1>Hague-Visby, Hamburg and Rotterdam Rules — Carrier liability for cargo</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><b>Hague-Visby Rules</b></th><th><b>Hamburg Rules (1978)</b></th><th><b>Rotterdam Rules (2008)</b></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>Hague Rules as amended by the Brussels Protocol 1968</td><td>UN Convention on the Carriage of Goods by Sea</td><td>UN Convention on Contracts for the International Carriage of Goods Wholly or Partly by Sea</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>In force, widely applied</td><td>In force (earliest national date shown: 1 Nov 1992). <b>36 parties</b> (UNCITRAL table)</td><td><b>Not in force</b>. Needs 20 ratifications, then one year. Five ratifications as of Dec 2019 (secondary source)</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier responsible</td><td><b>Tackle to tackle</b> (loading to discharge)</td><td><b>Port to port</b>: from taking charge at the port of loading until discharge</td><td>Receipt to <b>delivery</b>, door to door if there is a sea leg</td></tr><tr><td>Seaworthiness</td><td>Due diligence <b>before and at the start</b> of the voyage</td><td>Not verified</td><td>Due diligence (not strict liability)</td></tr><tr><td>Nautical fault defence</td><td><b>Yes</b></td><td>Not verified</td><td><b>Removed</b></td></tr><tr><td>Fire</td><td>Carrier not liable unless its own actual fault or privity</td><td>Carrier liable if the claimant proves fault or neglect of the carrier</td><td>Not verified</td></tr><tr><td>Limit per package</td><td><b>666.67 SDR</b></td><td><b>835 SDR</b></td><td><b>875 units of account</b></td></tr><tr><td>Limit per kilogram</td><td><b>2 SDR</b></td><td><b>2.5 SDR</b></td><td><b>3 units of account</b></td></tr><tr><td>Time bar</td><td><b>1 year</b></td><td><b>2 years</b> (Art 20)</td><td><b>2 years</b></td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"cargo-regimes\"><figcaption>Where the carrier is responsible</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Which regime applies depends on the bill of lading and the countries involved, not on the ship's flag alone. Check the clause paramount on the bill.</blockquote><h2>Hague-Visby Rules</h2><p><b>Application (Art X)</b></p><ul><li>Apply to a <b>bill of lading</b> (or similar document of title) when the Art X conditions are met. In practice the contract says so, usually by a <b>clause paramount</b>. The exact Art X test is not verified here</li><li>Waybills and other non-negotiable documents are outside the Rules</li><li>A bill issued under a charterparty is generally governed by the charter, unless a clause paramount brings the Rules in</li></ul><p><b>Carrier duties (Art III)</b></p><ul><li><b>Rule 1, seaworthiness:</b> exercise due diligence, <b>before and at the start</b> of the voyage, to make the ship seaworthy, properly man and supply her, and make the holds fit for the cargo. It is not a continuing duty</li><li><b>Rule 2, cargo care:</b> properly and carefully load, handle, stow, carry, keep, care for and discharge the goods</li><li><b>Rule 6, time bar:</b> the claim is lost unless proceedings start within <b>one year</b> of delivery, or of the date delivery should have occurred</li></ul><p><b>Carrier defences (Art IV rule 2)</b></p><ul><li>17 listed exceptions. The two that matter most:<ul><li><b>Nautical fault:</b> act, neglect or default of the master, mariner, pilot or crew in the navigation or management of the ship</li><li><b>Fire</b>, unless caused by the carrier's actual fault or privity</li></ul></li><li>A listed exception cannot be used if the peril could have been avoided with reasonable care, or if unseaworthiness was the operative cause</li><li>Once damage during the voyage is shown, the carrier must bring the cause within an exception or show it was not in breach of its care duty</li></ul><p><b>Limitation (Art IV rule 5)</b></p><ul><li>Not liable beyond <b>666.67 SDR per package or unit</b>, or <b>2 SDR per kilogram</b> of gross weight lost or damaged, whichever is <b>higher</b>. The unit is the IMF special drawing right</li><li>Higher limits apply if the <b>nature and value</b> were declared before shipment and put in the bill of lading</li><li><b>Containers and pallets:</b> the packages listed in the bill as packed in the container count. Otherwise the container is the package</li><li>Value is taken at the place and time of discharge (commodity exchange price, then market price, then normal value)</li><li>Parties may agree a <b>higher</b> maximum, never a lower one</li><li><b>Right to limit lost</b> if the damage came from the carrier's act done with intent to cause damage, or recklessly with knowledge that damage would probably result</li><li>The carrier is not responsible if the shipper <b>knowingly misstated</b> the goods' nature or value</li></ul><h2>Hamburg Rules (1978)</h2><ul><li><b>Scope (Art 2):</b> contracts of carriage by sea between two different States, when the loading or discharge port, the bill of lading or an agreement links the carriage to a contracting State</li><li><b>Period of responsibility (Art 4):</b> from when the carrier is in charge of the goods at the port of loading until discharge</li><li><b>Basis (Art 5):</b> liability is <b>presumed</b>. The carrier escapes only by proving that it, its servants or agents took all measures that could reasonably be required</li><li><b>Fire (Art 5(4)):</b> the claimant must prove the fire came from the carrier's fault or neglect. After a fire, a survey into its cause <b>must</b> be held if either side wants one</li><li><b>Limits (Art 6):</b> <b>835 SDR per package</b> or <b>2.5 SDR per kilogram</b>, whichever is higher. Right to limit lost for intentional or reckless conduct (Art 8)</li><li><b>Deck cargo (Art 9):</b> the carrier may carry on deck only if allowed by agreement (or by trade usage or law)</li><li><b>Bill of lading (Art 15):</b> must include the listed particulars</li><li><b>Time bar (Art 20):</b> <b>2 years</b></li><li><b>Unit (Art 26):</b> the IMF special drawing right</li><li><b>Entry into force (Art 30):</b> first day of the month, one year after the 20th instrument</li></ul><h2>Rotterdam Rules (2008)</h2><ul><li>Adopted by the UN on <b>11 Dec 2008</b>, signing ceremony in Rotterdam on <b>23 Sep 2009</b></li><li><b>Door to door:</b> covers the whole contract if it includes an international sea leg. Contracts with no sea leg are outside</li><li>Removes the <b>nautical fault</b> defence. Seaworthiness is a due diligence duty</li><li>Limits <b>875</b> units of account per shipping unit or <b>3</b> per kilogram. <b>Time bar</b> 2 years from delivery</li><li>Allows more electronic transport records, and <b>volume contracts</b> that can depart from some liability rules</li><li>A State that ratifies must denounce the Hague-Visby and Hamburg Rules</li><li>Not in force. The shipping industry and ABA supported US ratification, but this is not binding on anyone</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>LLMC 1976 the shipowner's limit for all claims (a different limit from the per-package cargo limit)</li><li>Salvage 1989 salvage of ship and cargo</li><li>CSC 1972 containers</li><li>IMDG Code dangerous goods</li><li>Bills of Lading the document the Rules apply to</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The original <b>Hague Rules of 1924</b> and the 1979 SDR Protocol</li><li>The exact wording of the Hague-Visby Art X application test</li><li>Hague-Visby notice of loss, Art IV bis, and Art IV rule 6 (dangerous goods)</li><li>Hamburg Rules on seaworthiness, actual carriers, live animals, jurisdiction and arbitration</li><li>Rotterdam Rules on fire and the number and names of States</li><li>A current list of Hague-Visby parties, and Rotterdam ratifications after Dec 2019</li><li>Which regime applies in the Philippines or any other country</li><li>Marine cargo insurance, general average (York-Antwerp Rules) and charterparty terms</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>UK, Carriage of Goods by Sea Act 1971, Schedule (Hague-Visby Rules, Art IV rule 5): https://legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1971/19/schedule/part/4/paragraph/5/data.html</li><li>Standard P&amp;I Club, Hague-Visby Rules handout (June 2018, secondary): https://www.standard-club.com/fileadmin/uploads/standardclub/Documents/Import/publications/goto-handouts/2767683-contracts-of-carriage-and-bills-of-lading-the-hague-visby-rules.pdf</li><li>UNCITRAL, Hamburg Rules text: https://uncitral.un.org/sites/uncitral.un.org/files/media-documents/uncitral/en/xi_d_3_e.pdf</li><li>UNCITRAL, Hamburg Rules status: https://uncitral.un.org/en/texts/transportgoods/conventions/hamburg_rules/status</li><li>Wikipedia, Rotterdam Rules (secondary, status to Dec 2019): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotterdam_Rules</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-iamsar-manual","v":202332884,"title":"IAMSAR Manual","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["iamsar","imo","system","search-and-rescue","sar","icao","solas","topic"],"body":"<h1>IAMSAR Manual — International Aeronautical and Maritime Search and Rescue Manual</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>One shared guide for aviation and maritime search and rescue, published jointly by <b>IMO and ICAO</b></td></tr><tr><td>Volumes</td><td><b>3</b>: I Organization and Management, II Mission Co-ordination, III Mobile Facilities</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td><b>November 1999</b>. It replaced the <b>MERSAR</b> (first published 1971) and <b>IMOSAR</b> (first published 1978) manuals</td></tr><tr><td>Linked to</td><td>The 1979 SAR Convention (SAR 1979) and SOLAS Chapter V (SOLAS 1974)</td></tr><tr><td>Updated</td><td>Amendments are prepared by the ICAO/IMO Joint Working Group, endorsed by the NCSR Sub-Committee, and adopted in principle by the Maritime Safety Committee, normally every three years</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendments</td><td><b>MSC.1/Circ.1686</b>, applicable <b>1 Jan 2026</b></td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iamsar-volumes\"><figcaption>The three volumes and who they are for</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> For a ship, <b>Volume III</b> is the one that matters. It tells you what to do on receiving a distress alert, how to coordinate on scene, and how to search and rescue. You must have the current edition in hard copy on board.</blockquote><h2>The three volumes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Volume</th><th>Title</th><th>Main readers</th><th>What it covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>Organization and Management</td><td>Governments and SAR agencies</td><td>International, regional and national SAR systems, and cooperation between States</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Mission Co-ordination</td><td>Rescue coordination centres (RCCs, MRCCs, JRCCs)</td><td>Planning and carrying out rescue operations and exercises. Also carried by rescue units, and by aircraft and ships that can or must take part in rescue</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Mobile Facilities</td><td>Ships, aircraft and rescue units</td><td>On-scene communication, organization and search methods, and guidance on evacuating a craft in an emergency</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Each volume can stand alone or be used with the others</li><li>Volume III must be carried by every craft that may be asked to help, including all merchant ships, because any of them may have to search or act as on-scene commander</li></ul><h2>Carrying it on board</h2><ul><li><b>SOLAS V/21:</b> all ships carry an up-to-date copy of Volume III</li><li><b>MSC-MEPC.2/Circ.2:</b> Volume III must be available in hard copy, because it is used in emergencies</li><li><b>Hard copy on board by 1 Jan 2026</b>, to match the changes below</li><li>For flag State practice, such as the Isle of Man, the rule applies to ships that must carry life-saving appliances</li></ul><h2>What changed on 1 Jan 2026</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iamsar-2026\"><figcaption>MSC.1/Circ.1686, dated 27 Jan 2025</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Approval:</b> MSC 109 (2 to 6 Dec 2024), after ICAO approval and agreement at NCSR 11 (4 to 13 June 2024)</li><li><b>Volume III:</b> updated to match the <b>2024 GMDSS changes</b> to SOLAS Chapter IV (see GMDSS). Volume II also revises carriage text for radar SARTs or AIS-SARTs on cargo ships of 300 to 500 GT, and for survival craft VHF</li><li><b>Volume I:</b> new <b>Appendix P</b> (sample agreement between a SAR service and an accident investigation authority) and <b>Appendix Q</b> (sample process for quick approval of assisting SAR units entering another State's territory). Updated exercise template (Appendix O), and text on vessel and flight tracking</li><li><b>Volume II:</b> new <b>Appendix W</b> on night searches by aircraft using EO/IR equipment and night vision devices. New <b>section 6.15</b> on SAR near offshore wind and renewable energy installations. Updated flight tracking, GADSS and autonomous distress tracking, Cospas-Sarsat and MEOSAR content, Inmarsat unbarring procedures, search techniques and sweep width</li><li><b>What to do on board:</b> update muster lists, emergency checklists and procedures for the revised distress communications</li></ul><h2>Emergency phases</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iamsar-phases\"><figcaption>The three emergency phases</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Meaning</th><th>ICAO term</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Uncertainty</b></td><td>Doubt exists about the safety of a vessel or aircraft and the people on board</td><td>INCERFA</td></tr><tr><td><b>Alert</b></td><td>Apprehension about their safety</td><td>ALERFA</td></tr><tr><td><b>Distress</b></td><td>Reasonable certainty of grave and imminent danger, and immediate assistance is needed</td><td>DETRESFA</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>The five stages of a SAR response</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iamsar-stages\"><figcaption>From first report to debrief</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Awareness</b></td><td>Someone or some agency learns an emergency may exist</td></tr><tr><td><b>Initial action</b></td><td>Preliminary action to alert SAR facilities and get more information</td></tr><tr><td><b>Planning</b></td><td>Operational plans for search, rescue and final delivery</td></tr><tr><td><b>Operations</b></td><td>Sending rescue units to the scene, searching, rescuing survivors</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mission conclusion</b></td><td>Units return and are debriefed. Records are completed</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Stages can overlap, and a mission may not have every stage.</p><h2>Who does what</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iamsar-roles\"><figcaption>Chain of command in a SAR case</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Alerting posts:</b> relay a distress alert to the responsible rescue centre. Examples: coast radio stations, LUTs, land earth stations, air traffic units</li><li><b>SAR coordinator:</b> appoints a <b>SAR mission coordinator (SMC)</b> immediately when an alert is received</li><li><b>SMC:</b> prepares the search plan and sends units for the first search</li><li><b>On-scene coordinator (OSC):</b> appointed by the SMC when <b>two or more</b> SAR units work together. <b>Until then, the first facility to arrive takes the OSC's duties</b></li><li><b>OSC duties:</b> coordinate all units on scene, carry out and adjust the plan while keeping the SMC informed, keep units <b>safely separated</b>, send regular <b>SITREPs</b> (weather, results, plan changes, survivors found and who holds them), keep a detailed record, and tell the SMC when units can be released</li><li><b>Communication:</b> if links to the rescue centre are poor, the OSC has more authority to start actions on scene</li><li><b>Avoid changing the OSC often</b></li></ul><h2>If your ship receives a distress alert</h2><ul><li><b>Acknowledge</b> receipt</li><li>Get details of the craft in distress if possible</li><li><b>Proceed to help</b></li><li><b>Relay</b> the distress and the information to the rescue coordination centre</li><li>Keep watch on the distress frequencies and stay in contact with the craft while keeping the SAR system informed</li><li>Ships should monitor <b>VHF channel 16</b> (156.8 MHz). Merchant ships can also be informed on 2182 kHz. For ditched aircraft, contact is often on 121.5 or 243.0 MHz, or 4125 and 5680 kHz</li><li>A merchant ship near the position of distress is a resource the SMC can call on, called a vessel of opportunity</li></ul><p><b>When survivors are found</b></p><ul><li>Rescue them <b>without delay</b> if able. After rescue, do not leave survivors alone, especially if injured or showing signs of hypothermia</li><li>Hoisting a hypothermic person <b>vertically</b> can cause severe shock or even cardiac arrest</li><li>Carry hot liquids and insulating blankets for hypothermic survivors</li><li>In an aircraft ditching, survivors in the water or on the aircraft are usually taken first and those safe in rafts last</li></ul><h2>Searching</h2><h3>Planning a search</h3><p>1. Evaluate the situation, including earlier searching</p><p>2. Estimate where the incident happened, and the error</p><p>3. Estimate how survivors have moved since</p><p>4. Fix the <b>datum</b> and its uncertainty</p><p>5. Allocate search effort to give the best chance of finding survivors</p><p>6. Define sub-areas and patterns, then issue a search action plan</p><p>The steps repeat until survivors are found or searching is no longer reasonable.</p><ul><li><b>Datum:</b> the most probable location of the search object, corrected for movement over time</li><li><b>Track spacing (S):</b> the distance between two adjacent parallel search legs</li><li><b>Sweep width (W):</b> how well a sensor detects an object in the given conditions</li><li><b>Coverage factor:</b> C = W / S (corrected sweep width over track spacing). Pushing C beyond about 1.25 gives only a small gain in the chance of detection</li></ul><h3>Search patterns</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"iamsar-patterns\"><figcaption>The four common patterns</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Use it when</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Expanding square</b></td><td>The datum is known within close limits</td><td>Starts at the datum, grows outward in squares. A second search shifts the pattern <b>45°</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Sector</b></td><td>The datum is known well and the object is hard to see</td><td>Searches a circle around the datum. Ships usually use a radius of <b>2 to 5 nm</b>, with each turn <b>120°</b>. Aircraft 5 to 20 nm</td></tr><tr><td><b>Creeping line</b></td><td>A large area, and the object is more likely at one end</td><td>Legs run parallel to the <b>short</b> sides</td></tr><tr><td><b>Parallel track</b></td><td>A large area where the position is very uncertain</td><td>Legs run parallel to the <b>long</b> sides. The commence search point is half a track spacing inside the corner</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Ending a search</h3><ul><li>A search ends when the craft is no longer in distress, when survivors are rescued, or when the authority decides further searching has no significant chance of success</li><li>It is not ended while there is any reasonable hope of rescuing survivors</li></ul><h2>What is coming next (IMO NCSR 12, May 2025)</h2><ul><li>Model Course 3.14 (SAR mission coordinator, Volume II) validated. Review of Model Course 3.15 (on-scene coordinator, Volume III) under way, validation planned for NCSR 14</li><li>SOLAS amendments on MSI and SAR information being sent through all recognized mobile satellite services (IV/5, V/4, V/5) are headed for adoption at MSC 111 in 2026, entry into force <b>1 Jan 2028</b></li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>SAR 1979 search and rescue regions and rescue centres</li><li>GMDSS distress alerting</li><li>SOLAS 1974 Chapter V obligations</li><li>International Code of Signals signals used on scene</li><li>LSA Code survival craft</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Man overboard manoeuvres (Williamson, Anderson, Scharnow)</li><li>Track spacing tables and sweep width tables</li><li>Survival time in cold water</li><li>Exact edition years of each volume (sources differ: Volume I 2025, Volumes II and III 2022 on one source, Volume III published Oct 2025 on another)</li><li>Helicopter hoisting and aircraft ditching procedure in detail</li><li>Mass rescue operations</li><li>Section numbers in the 2022 edition. Some wording above comes from IAMSAR-based national manuals that follow the structure</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO, IAMSAR Manual page: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/IAMSARManual.aspx</li><li>IMO, MSC.1/Circ.1686 as circulated by Hong Kong Marine Department: https://www.mardep.gov.hk/filemanager/en/share/msnote/pdf/msin2532anx1.pdf</li><li>IIMS, updated IAMSAR Manual on board from January 2026: https://www.iims.org.uk/updated-iamsar-manual-to-be-carried-onboard-from-january-2026/</li><li>Isle of Man Ship Registry, IAMSAR Volume III amendments: https://www.iomshipregistry.com/media/q3gin3uc/amendments-to-the-international-aeronautical-and-maritime-search-and-rescue-iamsar-manual-volume-iii.pdf</li><li>Indian Register of Shipping, Technical Circular 014/2025: https://www.irclass.org/technical-circulars/amendments-to-the-international-aeronautical-and-maritime-search-and-rescue-iamsar-manual-2/</li><li>Marine Insight, key amendments to the IAMSAR Manual: https://www.marineinsight.com/know-more/key-amendments-to-the-iamsar-manual/</li><li>Wikipedia, IAMSAR Manual (volumes and contents): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Aeronautical_and_Maritime_Search_and_Rescue_Manual</li><li>ICAO, SAR workshop on the on-scene coordinator: https://www.icao.int/NACC/Documents/Meetings/2023/SARTF4/SARWorkshop-Handout4.pdf</li><li>ICAO, SAR conference paper (phases, search planning): https://www.icao.int/sites/default/files/Meetings/AMC/MA/2007/SARafrica/sarconf_wp11_en.pdf</li><li>Indian Coast Guard SAR manual, based on IAMSAR: https://indiancoastguard.gov.in/WriteReadData/bookpdf/202112240654491871725SAR.pdf</li><li>USCG Auxiliary, SAR fundamentals: https://wow.uscgaux.info/Uploads_wowII/013-07/SARfund.pdf</li><li>IMO, NCSR 12 meeting summary: https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/MeetingSummaries/Pages/NCSR-12th-session.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ihr-2005","v":94372788,"title":"IHR 2005","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["ihr","who","health","sanitation","quarantine","mdh","ship-sanitation","topic","imo"],"body":"<h1>IHR 2005 — International Health Regulations, as they apply to ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Issued by</td><td><b>WHO</b> (not IMO). Legally binding on 196 States Parties, including all 194 WHO Member States</td></tr><tr><td>Amended</td><td><b>2024</b> amendments adopted at the 77th World Health Assembly. In force <b>19 Sep 2025</b> (11 States rejected them, so the earlier text still applies to them)</td></tr><tr><td>Ship documents</td><td><b>Maritime Declaration of Health</b> (Art 37, Annex 8). <b>Ship Sanitation Control Exemption Certificate</b> or <b>Control Certificate</b> (Art 39, Annex 3)</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate validity</td><td><b>6 months</b> maximum. One extra month if the inspection or control cannot be done at the port (Art 39)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Any ship on an <b>international voyage</b> (a \"conveyance\", Art 1)</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ihr-arrival\"><figcaption>What happens when a ship arrives</figcaption></figure><h2>Main rules for ships</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>20</td><td>States designate ports that develop the core health capacities, and send WHO a list of ports <b>authorized to issue</b> the certificates</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Operators comply with WHO health measures adopted by the State, and keep the ship <b>free of sources of infection or contamination</b></td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>A ship only passing through a State's waters, without calling at a port, gets no health measure. It may take on fuel, water, food and supplies under supervision</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>If clinical signs and evidence of a public health risk are found, the ship is treated as <b>affected</b>. It may be disinfected, decontaminated, disinsected or deratted, and isolated. Findings and measures go on the Ship Sanitation Control Certificate</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>A ship <b>cannot be refused free pratique</b> for public health reasons, but may be inspected. It can be redirected if the port lacks capacity. The master reports ill persons or health risks on board <b>before arrival</b></td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>The master completes the <b>Maritime Declaration of Health</b> and gives it to the port's competent authority. The ship's surgeon countersigns it, if one is carried</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>Certificates are issued as in Annex 3, valid up to 6 months</td></tr><tr><td>41</td><td>One published tariff per State for charges. Not more than actual cost, and the same for all flags</td></tr><tr><td>43</td><td>A State may add stricter measures for a specific risk, but not more restrictive than needed, and based on science or WHO guidance. Delays of more than 24 hours must be explained to WHO within 48 hours. Review within 3 months</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Maritime Declaration of Health (Annex 8)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ihr-mdh\"><figcaption>What the declaration asks</figcaption></figure><p><b>Ship details:</b> port and date, ship name and IMO number, flag, ports of arrival and departure, master, gross tonnage, number of crew and passengers.</p><p><b>Voyage:</b> whether a valid sanitation certificate is carried (place and date), whether re-inspection is needed, whether the ship visited an area WHO has identified as affected, and <b>ports of call with departure dates</b> for the voyage or the last 30 days, whichever is shorter.</p><p><b>Nine health questions (yes or no, details in an attached schedule):</b></p><ul><li>Any death on board, other than by accident</li><li>Any suspected infectious disease</li><li>More ill passengers than normal or expected</li><li>Anyone ill now</li><li>A doctor consulted, and what advice was given</li><li>Any condition on board that could spread infection</li><li>Any sanitary measure applied (quarantine, isolation, disinfection), with type, place and date</li><li>Any stowaways</li><li>Any sick animal or pet</li></ul><p>The attached schedule records for each case: name, rating, age, sex, nationality, joining port and date, illness, onset, whether the port medical officer was told, outcome and treatment. The master signs that it is correct.</p><h2>Ship Sanitation Certificates (Art 39, Annex 3)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ihr-certs\"><figcaption>Exemption certificate or control certificate</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Exemption Certificate:</b> issued at an authorized port if the inspection finds no infection or contamination</li><li><b>Control Certificate:</b> issued after control measures are completed, when the inspection finds a risk</li><li><b>They replaced</b> the older Deratting Certificate and Deratting Exemption Certificate (IHR 1969)</li><li><b>Inspection covers:</b> galley, pantry, stores, holds and cargo, crew, officer, passenger and deck quarters, potable water, sewage, ballast tanks, solid and medical waste, standing water, engine room and medical facilities</li><li><b>Evidence looked for:</b> disease vectors at any life stage, animal reservoirs, rodents, microbiological or chemical hazards, and signs of poor sanitation. Sampling and documents (medical log, ship's log) are reviewed</li><li><b>Attachment checks:</b> food (source, storage, preparation), water (source, storage, distribution), waste, pools and spas, medical equipment and medicines</li><li><b>Re-inspection</b> date is written on the certificate</li><li>Only the competent authority of an <b>authorized port</b> may issue them. Private certificates are not valid. In the <b>United States</b> a cargo ship cannot get one at all and must obtain it at a port in another country on the WHO list</li><li><b>Pandemic-era extension (2020):</b> a WHO joint statement asked States to grant repeated one-month extensions under Art 39(1) when no authorized port on the route could inspect. It could not run past the end of the declared emergency</li></ul><h2>What the port must provide (Annex 1)</h2><ul><li>Access to an appropriate medical service and diagnostic facilities</li><li>Equipment and personnel to transport ill travellers to a medical facility</li><li>Trained staff to inspect ships</li><li>A safe environment: safe water, food establishments, washrooms and waste disposal</li><li>A vector control programme</li><li>For emergencies: isolation and treatment of affected travellers, separate interview space, quarantine, treatment of baggage and cargo, entry and exit controls</li></ul><h2>2024 amendments in brief</h2><ul><li>New alert level, a <b>pandemic emergency</b>, above a public health emergency of international concern</li><li>States set up a <b>National IHR Authority</b> to coordinate implementation</li><li>Provisions on fair access to medical products and on financing</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>MLC 2006 (ILO) seafarer health protection and medical care</li><li>FAL 1965 arrival formalities and documents</li><li>SOLAS 1974 ship certificates</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Exact Annex 8 wording of the symptom list used when no surgeon is on board</li><li>Which authorities and ports issue certificates in the Philippines or each State</li><li>The WHO Handbook for Inspection of Ships and Guide to Ship Sanitation (3rd edition) contents</li><li>Whether the IMO FAL Convention forms include the Maritime Declaration of Health</li><li>National quarantine flag signals and free pratique procedures</li><li>Specific disease reporting lists, such as the Irish list of cholera, plague, yellow fever and others</li><li>Medical guidance for the ship's medical chest (see the planned Medical note)</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>WHO, International Health Regulations (2005), text (Arts 1, 20, 24, 25, 27, 28, 31, 32, 37, 39, 41, 43 and Annex 1): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/03/3-01/int_health_reg.html</li><li>WHO, IHR (2005) third edition PDF (Annexes 3, 4 and 8): https://extranet.who.int/sph/sites/default/files/document-library/document/9789241580496-eng.pdf</li><li>WHO, amended IHR enter into force, 19 Sep 2025: https://www.who.int/news/item/19-09-2025-amended-international-health-regulations-enter-into-force</li><li>WHO, joint statement on ship sanitation certificates (22 Apr 2020): https://www.who.int/news/item/22-04-2020-a-joint-statement-on-medical-certificates-of-seafarers-ship-sanitation-certificates-and-medical-care-of-seafarers-in-the-context-of-the-covid-19-pandemic</li><li>US CDC, Ship Sanitation Certificate information: https://www.cdc.gov/quarantine/maritime/shipsanitation.html</li><li>Ireland, S.I. No. 4/2008 Infectious Diseases (Shipping) Regulations 2008 (national example): https://www.irishstatutebook.ie/eli/2008/si/4/made/en/print</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-life-saving-appliances-guide","v":47725644,"title":"Life-Saving Appliances Guide","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["lsa","life-saving-appliances","lifeboat","liferaft","lifebuoy","lifejacket","immersion-suit","rescue-boat","solas-iii","equipment","topic","imo"],"body":"<h1>Life-Saving Appliances Guide — What is on board and what is in it</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Equipment</th><th>SOLAS III</th><th>Test and design figures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Personal</td><td>Lifebuoys, lifejackets, immersion suits, thermal protective aids</td><td>7, 32</td><td>LSA Code chapter II</td></tr><tr><td>Signals</td><td>Rocket parachute flares, hand flares, smoke signals</td><td>6</td><td>LSA Code chapter III</td></tr><tr><td>Survival craft</td><td>Liferafts, lifeboats, free-fall lifeboats</td><td>21, 31</td><td>LSA Code chapter IV</td></tr><tr><td>Rescue</td><td>Rescue boats, fast rescue boats</td><td>21, 31</td><td>LSA Code chapter V</td></tr><tr><td>Launching</td><td>Davits, ramps, embarkation ladder</td><td>11, 12–17, 33</td><td>LSA Code chapter VI</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>Line-throwing appliance, general alarm</td><td>6, 18</td><td>LSA Code chapter VII</td></tr><tr><td>Papers</td><td>Training manual, maintenance instructions, muster list</td><td>35, 36, 37</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-plan\"><figcaption>Where the equipment sits on a cargo ship</figcaption></figure><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> This note lists what each item is, how many, and what is inside the craft. Test figures are in LSA Code. Craft quantities, drills and maintenance intervals are in SOLAS 1974.</blockquote><h2>Every appliance in one table</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Appliance</th><th>Key specifications</th><th>Donning, boarding, use</th><th>Approval and service</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Lifebuoy</b></td><td>Outer diameter <b>≤800 mm</b>, inner <b>≥400 mm</b>. Mass <b>≥2.5 kg</b>. Supports <b>14.5 kg</b> of iron for <b>24 h</b>. Grabline <b>≥9.5 mm</b>, length <b>≥4×</b> the outer diameter, four loops. Lifeline <b>≥8 mm</b>, <b>≥5 kN</b>. Dropped from stowage height or <b>30 m</b> without damage</td><td>Thrown. Light and smoke signal release from the bridge. A buoy that operates the quick-release must weigh <b>≥4 kg</b></td><td>Approval marking. Light and smoke signal are age-limited (dated)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lifebuoy light</b></td><td>White, <b>≥2 cd</b> steady, or <b>50 to 70</b> flashes a minute. Energy for <b>≥2 h</b>. Cannot be put out by water</td><td>Self-igniting</td><td>Battery with no expiry date: replace yearly</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lifebuoy smoke signal</b></td><td>Smoke for <b>≥15 min</b> in calm water. No flame or explosion. Still smokes after <b>10 s</b> under water</td><td>Self-activating</td><td>Dated</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lifejacket</b></td><td>Three sizes (infant, child, adult). Buoyancy loss <b>≤5%</b> in 24 h. Jump from <b>4.5 m</b> and from <b>1 m</b> with arms overhead. Light <b>≥0.75 cd</b> for <b>8 h</b>. Whistle on a lanyard. Buddy line. Lifting loop for the rescuer</td><td><b>Donning:</b> <b>75%</b> of people who have never seen it don it correctly within <b>1 min</b>, unaided. After a demonstration <b>all</b> do. Wearable only one way, or inside out. Quick closure, <b>no knots</b></td><td>Light and battery dated. Retro-reflective <b>400 cm²</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Inflatable lifejacket</b></td><td><b>Two separate compartments</b>. Inflates <b>automatically</b> on immersion, by <b>one manual action</b>, and <b>orally</b></td><td>Same donning test. Still works with one compartment lost</td><td><b>Serviced</b> at an approved station (SOLAS note for the interval)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Immersion suit</b></td><td>Mouth <b>≥120 mm</b> clear. Face-down to face-up in <b>≤5 s</b>. Uninsulated: core temperature falls <b>≤2 °C</b> in <b>1 h</b> at 5 °C. Insulated: <b>≤2 °C</b> in <b>6 h</b> at 0 to 2 °C. Jump <b>4.5 m</b>, climb a <b>5 m</b> ladder</td><td><b>Don unaided within 2 min</b></td><td>Retro-reflective <b>400 cm²</b>, with <b>100 cm²</b> on the back if it does not turn the wearer face up</td></tr><tr><td><b>Thermal protective aid</b></td><td>Thermal conductance <b>≤7,800 W/(m²·K)</b>. Operating air temperature <b>−30 to +20 °C</b></td><td>In the craft, for people without a suit</td><td>Count in each craft: <b>10% of capacity or 2</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Inflatable liferaft</b></td><td>Survives a drop from <b>18 m</b>. Capacity <b>≥6 persons</b>. Mass <b>≤185 kg</b>. At least <b>two</b> buoyancy compartments. Design load <b>82.5 kg</b> a person. Inflates in <b>1 min</b> (18–20 °C) and <b>3 min</b> (−30 °C)</td><td>Ramp supports <b>100 kg</b>. Boarding ladder's lowest step <b>≥0.4 m</b> below the waterline. Painter <b>10 m plus stowage height, or 15 m</b>. Weak link breaks at <b>2.2 ± 0.4 kN</b>. Hydrostatic release at <b>≤4 m</b></td><td><b>Serviced</b> at an approved station. Container and raft marked</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lifeboat</b></td><td>Not over <b>150 persons</b>. Engine starts at <b>−15 °C</b> within <b>2 min</b>. <b>6 knots</b> loaded, fuel for <b>24 h</b>. Holds steady under tow at <b>5 knots</b>. Release gear safety factor <b>6</b></td><td><b>Boarding by the full complement ≤3 min</b> (cargo ship), <b>≤10 min</b> (passenger ship)</td><td>Release gear examined yearly. Ventilation rule for new boats from 1 Jan 2029</td></tr><tr><td><b>Partially enclosed</b></td><td>Rigid covers over <b>≥20%</b> of the length from each end</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>Totally enclosed</b></td><td>Inside pressure within <b>20 hPa</b> of outside. Stable when flooded. Withstands ship's-side impact at <b>3.5 m/s</b></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>Free-fall</b></td><td>Launch from <b>1.3 times</b> the certified height. Release system works from no load to <b>200%</b> of normal load</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>Self-contained air support</b></td><td>Engine runs <b>≥10 min</b> with every opening closed. Pressure never below outside, no more than <b>20 hPa</b> above</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>Fire-protected</b></td><td>Protects occupants in a continuous oil fire for <b>≥8 min</b> afloat</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>Rescue boat</b></td><td>Tows the largest liferaft at <b>≥2 knots</b>. <b>6 knots for 4 h</b> loaded. Inflated boat buoyancy <b>≥0.17 m³</b> a person</td><td>Launched with the ship making <b>5 knots</b>, recovered in <b>≤5 min</b></td><td>Equipment list above</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fast rescue boat</b></td><td>Length <b>6 m to 8.5 m</b>. <b>20 knots</b> with a crew of 3, <b>8 knots</b> loaded, for <b>4 h</b></td><td>Lowered at <b>≤1 m/s</b>, hoisted at <b>≥0.8 m/s</b></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>Launching appliance</b></td><td>Safe at <b>10° trim</b> and <b>20° list</b>. Lowering speed <b>0.4 + 0.02H</b> or <b>1.0 m/s</b>, whichever is less, and <b>≤1.3 m/s</b> (new from 1 Jan 2026)</td><td>Rescue boat winch hoists at <b>≥0.3 m/s</b></td><td>Falls and release gear: SOLAS note</td></tr><tr><td><b>Rocket parachute flare</b></td><td>Reaches <b>≥300 m</b>. <b>≥30,000 cd</b>. Burns <b>≥40 s</b>. Descends <b>≤5 m/s</b></td><td>Fired</td><td>Dated</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hand flare</b></td><td><b>≥15,000 cd</b>. Burns <b>≥1 min</b>. Burns on after <b>10 s</b> under 100 mm of water</td><td>Held</td><td>Dated</td></tr><tr><td><b>Buoyant smoke signal</b></td><td>Smoke <b>≥3 min</b> in calm water. Smokes on after <b>10 s</b> under 100 mm of water</td><td>Thrown</td><td>Dated</td></tr><tr><td><b>Line-throwing appliance</b></td><td><b>4 projectiles</b>, each carrying the line <b>≥230 m</b>. <b>4 lines</b>, each <b>≥2 kN</b></td><td>Fired</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>General alarm</b></td><td><b>80 dB(A)</b> and <b>10 dB(A)</b> above ambient noise. Public address <b>75 dB(A)</b> inside, <b>80 dB(A)</b> outside</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Personal appliances</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-personal\"><figcaption>The four personal appliances</figcaption></figure><h3>Lifebuoys (III/7, III/32)</h3><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-lifebuoy\"><figcaption>A lifebuoy and its fittings</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Spread so they are <b>readily available on both sides</b> of the ship, with at least one <b>near the stern</b></li><li>At least <b>one on each side</b> has a buoyant lifeline, at least <b>twice its stowage height above the lightest waterline, or 30 m</b>, whichever is greater</li><li>At least <b>half</b> have a self-igniting light. At least <b>two</b> of those also have a smoke signal that can be <b>released from the bridge</b>, split equally between the sides</li><li>Each is marked in <b>block capitals</b> with the ship's name and port of registry</li><li>On tankers the self-igniting lights are <b>electric battery type</b></li></ul><p><b>Minimum number on a cargo ship (III/32.1.1)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship length</th><th>Lifebuoys</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under 100 m</td><td><b>8</b></td></tr><tr><td>100 m to under 150 m</td><td><b>10</b></td></tr><tr><td>150 m to under 200 m</td><td><b>12</b></td></tr><tr><td>200 m and over</td><td><b>14</b></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Lifejackets (III/7, III/32)</h3><ul><li>One for <b>every person on board</b>, plus <b>children's lifejackets</b> for at least <b>10% of passengers</b></li><li><b>Extra</b> lifejackets for watchkeepers and for remote survival craft stations</li><li>Stowed where <b>readily accessible</b> and clearly marked. Watchkeepers' lifejackets go on the <b>bridge, in the engine control room</b> and at other manned stations</li><li>Every lifejacket carries a <b>light</b> (LSA Code 2.2.3)</li></ul><h3>Immersion suits and thermal protective aids</h3><ul><li>Each <b>rescue boat crew member</b> and each <b>marine evacuation system party member</b> has an immersion suit or anti-exposure suit of the right size</li><li><b>Cargo ship:</b> <b>three immersion suits per lifeboat</b>, and thermal protective aids for anyone without a suit</li><li>The three-per-lifeboat rule is dropped for ships with <b>enclosed lifeboats on both sides</b> with enough capacity, or <b>free-fall lifeboats with liferafts on each side</b>, or ships working only in <b>warm climates</b></li><li><b>Cargo ships using the alternative arrangement</b> (III/31.1.3, ships under 85 m other than tankers, chemical tankers and gas carriers): immersion suits <b>for everyone</b>, unless the ship has davit-launched liferafts, an equivalent approved appliance usable on both sides, or works only in warm climates</li></ul><h2>Survival craft</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-lifeboats\"><figcaption>The lifeboat types</figcaption></figure><h3>Lifeboat design (LSA Code 4.4)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capacity</td><td><b>Not more than 150 persons</b>. The lesser of the number at <b>75 kg</b> each (passenger ship) or <b>82.5 kg</b> each (cargo ship) in lifejackets, and the seats that fit the seating diagram</td></tr><tr><td>Seats</td><td>Each supports <b>100 kg</b>. At least <b>350 mm</b> between upper and lower seats</td></tr><tr><td>Extra buoyancy</td><td>Inherently buoyant material of <b>280 N per person</b></td></tr><tr><td>Hull</td><td>Rigid. Positive stability upright and when holed below the waterline. Fire-retardant or non-combustible</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td><b>Compression-ignition</b>. Fuel flashpoint <b>above 43 °C</b> (closed cup)</td></tr><tr><td>Drain</td><td>Valve that opens out of the water and closes afloat. Not required on free-fall boats</td></tr><tr><td>Plate</td><td>Maker, model and serial number, month and year, persons approved</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Liferaft markings</h3><ul><li><b>Inflatable:</b> maker or trademark, serial number, month and year, approving authority, servicing station, and <b>persons permitted at each entrance</b> in characters at least <b>100 mm</b> high</li><li><b>Ship's name and port of registry</b> can be changed without opening the container</li><li><b>Container:</b> also shows SOLAS, emergency pack type, service date, painter length, <b>mass if over 185 kg</b>, and maximum stowage height</li></ul><h2>What is inside the craft</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-liferaft\"><figcaption>Inflatable liferaft</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Liferaft (LSA 4.1.5)</th><th>Lifeboat (LSA 4.4.8)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Water</b></td><td><b>1.5 L</b> per person</td><td><b>3 L</b> per person</td></tr><tr><td><b>Food</b></td><td><b>10,000 kJ</b> per person</td><td><b>10,000 kJ</b> per person</td></tr><tr><td>Drinking vessel</td><td>1, graduated, rustproof</td><td>1, graduated, rustproof. Plus a rustproof <b>dipper</b> with lanyard</td></tr><tr><td>Anti-seasickness</td><td>48 h supply and a bag per person</td><td>48 h supply and a bag per person</td></tr><tr><td><b>Rocket parachute flares</b></td><td>4</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hand flares</b></td><td>6</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td><b>Buoyant smoke signals</b></td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Waterproof torch</td><td>1, with spare batteries and bulb</td><td>1, with spare batteries and bulb</td></tr><tr><td>Radar reflector</td><td>1, unless a survival craft radar transponder is stowed</td><td>Same</td></tr><tr><td>Signalling mirror</td><td>1</td><td>1, with instructions</td></tr><tr><td>Life-saving signals card</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Whistle</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>First-aid outfit</td><td>1, waterproof case</td><td>1, waterproof case</td></tr><tr><td><b>Thermal protective aids</b></td><td><b>10% of capacity or 2</b>, whichever is greater</td><td>Same</td></tr><tr><td>Rescue quoit, 30 m buoyant line</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Knife</td><td>1 non-folding, buoyant handle, on the canopy by the painter. <b>2nd if 13 or more persons</b></td><td>1 jack-knife on a lanyard</td></tr><tr><td>Bailer</td><td>1 (<b>2</b> if 13 or more persons)</td><td>1, plus <b>2 buckets</b></td></tr><tr><td>Sea-anchor</td><td>2 (one spare, one fixed)</td><td>1, with tripping line if fitted</td></tr><tr><td>Tin-openers</td><td>3, with 1 pair of scissors</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Fishing tackle</td><td>1 set</td><td>1 set</td></tr><tr><td>Sponges</td><td>2</td><td>not listed</td></tr><tr><td>Paddles or oars</td><td>2 buoyant paddles</td><td>Enough buoyant oars for headway in calm seas (not free-fall), with thole pins on lanyards</td></tr><tr><td>Survival instructions</td><td>1 survival, 1 immediate action</td><td>1 survival manual</td></tr><tr><td>Boat-hooks, hatchets</td><td>not listed</td><td><b>2</b> boat-hooks, <b>2</b> hatchets (one at each end)</td></tr><tr><td>Painters</td><td>one on the raft, 10 m plus the stowage height, or 15 m, whichever is greater</td><td><b>2</b>, each twice the stowage height to the lightest waterline, or 15 m, whichever is greater</td></tr><tr><td>Compass</td><td>not listed</td><td>1, luminous or illuminated</td></tr><tr><td>Searchlight</td><td>not listed</td><td>1, <b>6° sector, 2,500 cd, 3 h</b></td></tr><tr><td>Engine and fire</td><td>not listed</td><td>Engine tools for minor adjustments. Portable fire extinguishing equipment for oil fires</td></tr><tr><td>Manual pump</td><td>not listed</td><td>1, only if <b>not self-bailing</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The Administration may dispense with <b>food</b> and <b>fishing tackle</b> in lifeboats on certain voyages</li><li>Water: up to 1 L (lifeboat) can be replaced by a <b>desalting apparatus</b>, or up to 2 L by a <b>manual reverse osmosis desalinator</b></li></ul><h3>Rescue boat equipment (LSA 5.1.2)</h3><ul><li><b>All boats:</b> oars or paddles with thole pins, 1 buoyant bailer, binnacle with compass, sea-anchor with hawser of at least <b>10 m</b>, 1 painter, <b>1 buoyant line of at least 50 m</b>, torch with spares, whistle, first-aid outfit, <b>2 rescue quoits</b> with 30 m line, searchlight, radar reflector, thermal protective aids for <b>10% of capacity or 2</b>, portable fire extinguishing equipment for oil fires</li><li><b>Rigid boat, in addition:</b> boat-hook, bucket, knife or hatchet</li><li><b>Inflated boat, in addition:</b> buoyant safety knife, 2 sponges, manual bellows or pump, repair kit, safety boat-hook</li></ul><h2>Launching and embarkation</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-launch\"><figcaption>The four ways craft leave the ship</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Launch time:</b> all survival craft launchable within <b>10 minutes</b> on a cargo ship, <b>30 minutes</b> on a passenger ship</li><li><b>Embarkation ladder</b> at each embarkation station, or at every two adjacent stations for craft launched down the ship's side. It runs <b>in a single length from deck to waterline</b> in the lightest seagoing condition</li><li><b>Rescue boat:</b> launched with the ship making up to <b>5 knots</b> in calm water, recovered in <b>not more than 5 minutes</b> in moderate seas with the full complement</li><li><b>Line-throwing appliance:</b> one, to LSA Code 7.1</li><li><b>Cargo ships of 20,000 GT and over:</b> lifeboats launched with the ship making up to <b>5 knots</b> in calm water</li></ul><h2>Approval, marking and certificates</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-approval\"><figcaption>From design to ship</figcaption></figure><p><b>Approval (SOLAS III/4 and III/5)</b></p><ul><li>Every required LSA is <b>approved by the Administration</b>, after tests to the LSA Code following IMO's testing recommendation <b>MSC.81(70)</b>, or tests the Administration accepts as substantially equivalent</li><li><b>Novel</b> appliances must give safety at least equal to the chapter and the Code</li><li>The approval states when it <b>continues or is withdrawn</b></li><li><b>Production tests</b> make sure each item is made to the <b>same standard as the approved prototype</b></li></ul><p><b>Marking and expiry</b></p><ul><li>Each item is marked with the <b>approving Administration</b> and any <b>operating limits</b> (LSA Code 1.2.2.9)</li><li>Age-limited items show their <b>age or replacement date</b>, with a <b>permanent expiry date</b> preferred. A battery with no expiry date is <b>replaced yearly</b>. Pyrotechnics carry the maker's <b>permanent expiry date</b></li></ul><p><b>Equipment certificate: Record of Equipment (Form E)</b> lists persons provided for, davit-launched and free-fall lifeboats (by type), motor lifeboats and searchlights, rescue boats, liferafts (with or without launching appliances), lifebuoys, lifejackets, immersion suits, anti-exposure suits, radar SARTs, AIS-SARTs and two-way VHF. It is certified by an authorised official with place, date, signature and seal</p><p><b>Personal certificate: STCW A-VI/2-1</b>, proficiency in survival craft and rescue boats (other than fast rescue boats). Five competences: take charge of the craft during and after launch, operate the engine, manage survivors and the craft, use locating devices and pyrotechnics, apply first aid. The renewal interval is in STCW 1978</p><p><b>Servicing:</b> inflatable liferafts, inflatable lifejackets and marine evacuation systems go to an <b>approved servicing station</b> with proper facilities and trained personnel (III/20.8.1). <b>Posters</b> on or near craft and launching controls show the controls and procedure, readable under <b>emergency lighting</b>, with IMO symbols (III/9)</p><h2>General requirements for every appliance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement (LSA Code 1.2.2)</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stowage</td><td>No damage from <b>−30 °C to +65 °C</b></td></tr><tr><td>Personal LSA</td><td>Operational <b>−15 °C to +40 °C</b></td></tr><tr><td>In seawater</td><td>Works from <b>−1 °C to +30 °C</b></td></tr><tr><td>Durability</td><td>Resists rot, corrosion, seawater, oil, fungus and sunlight</td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td><b>Vivid reddish-orange</b> or equally visible, where it helps detection</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"lsa-retro\"><figcaption>Retro-reflective material, IMO A.658(16)</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Retro-reflective material</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Lifebuoy</b></td><td>Strips about <b>5 cm</b> wide at <b>four</b> evenly spaced points</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lifejacket</b></td><td>Patches of at least <b>400 cm²</b> in total, high up, visible from all directions. A reversible jacket must comply both ways</td></tr><tr><td><b>Immersion suit</b></td><td>At least <b>400 cm²</b>, and <b>100 cm²</b> on the back if it does not turn the wearer face up</td></tr><tr><td><b>Liferaft</b></td><td>Pieces of at least <b>150 cm²</b>, about <b>80 cm</b> apart, round the canopy. Inflatable rafts also have a <b>cross</b> under the floor and on top of the canopy, each <b>half the raft's diameter</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Lifeboat, rescue boat</b></td><td>Pieces of at least <b>150 cm²</b>, about <b>80 cm</b> apart, on the gunwale and the outside near it, and on top of the cover. Boats that do not self-right also have it on the bottom</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Documents</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Where and what</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Training manual</b> (III/35)</td><td>One in each crew mess and recreation room, or in each crew cabin. Easy wording, illustrated. Covers lifejackets and suits, mustering, boarding and launching, release gear, survival equipment, radio, engines, recovery, cold exposure and retrieval</td></tr><tr><td><b>Maintenance instructions</b> (III/36)</td><td>Illustrated. Inspection checklist, maintenance and repair instructions, a periodic maintenance schedule, a lubrication diagram, replaceable parts and spares sources, and an inspection and maintenance <b>log</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Muster list</b> (III/37)</td><td>Alarm and public address details, how the abandon-ship order is given, crew duties, officers responsible for keeping the appliances ready, and substitutes. Made <b>before the ship sails</b> and revised if the crew changes</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>LSA Code the technical standard, with amendment history and chapter text</li><li>SOLAS 1974 craft quantities, drills and maintenance intervals</li><li>GMDSS EPIRB, SART and survival craft radios</li><li>STCW 1978 survival craft proficiency and renewal</li><li>IAMSAR Manual search and rescue</li><li>Torremolinos 1977 fishing vessels</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li><b>EU wheelmark</b> and other national approval schemes, the <b>test report</b> procedure of MSC.81(70), and Type I and Type II reflection values</li><li><b>Record of Equipment</b> for passenger ships, and the validity and survey rules of the certificate</li><li>STCW A-VI/2 <b>eligibility</b>, assessment criteria and <b>fast rescue boat</b> proficiency</li><li><b>Donning</b> rules for thermal protective aids, and how a <b>lifejacket</b> is checked on board</li><li><b>Marine evacuation systems</b> (LSA Code 6.2) and their capacity and deployment time</li><li><b>Passenger ship</b> lifebuoy numbers and lifejacket extras (III/22), and the amount of passenger-ship equipment</li><li><b>Hydrostatic release</b> servicing, <b>pyrotechnic expiry</b> and liferaft <b>service intervals</b> beyond the SOLAS note</li><li>Whether the 1996 text means <b>stern</b> or <b>stem</b> for the one lifebuoy at the ship's end (the scanned text is unclear). The stern is used here</li><li>Later amendments to regulations 7, 31, 32 and 33 after their 1996 adoption text. The numbers here come from that text and the LSA Code (2017 edition)</li><li>The VHF, SART and EPIRB carriage rules (moved to SOLAS IV/7 on 1 Jan 2024. See the GMDSS note)</li><li>Exact lifeboat <b>fuel quantities</b> for other speeds, and the full <b>engine</b> list</li><li>Equipment of <b>free-fall</b> boats beyond the lifeboat list, and of <b>totally enclosed</b> boats' air supply</li><li>Flag State variations, including the Philippines</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO, Resolution A.658(16), retro-reflective materials: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.658(16).pdf</li><li>IMO, Resolution MSC.81(70), testing (West P&amp;I copy): https://www.westpandi.com/globalassets/news/110727-msc.81-70-revised-recommendation-on-testing-of-life-saving-appliances.pdf</li><li>Record of Equipment, Form E (copy): https://dieselship.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/RECORD-OF-EQUIPMENT-FOR-CARGO-SHIP-SAFETY-FORM-E.pdf</li><li>STCW Table A-VI/2-1: https://weh.maritime.edu/stcw/2018/tables/621.html</li><li>Chapter II to VII figures also in the LSA Code note (2017 edition and IMO amendment resolutions)</li><li>LSA Code (2017 edition), sections 4.1.5, 4.2.7, 4.4.1–4.4.8, 5.1.2: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.lsa.2017.pdf</li><li>IMO, Resolution MSC.47(66), SOLAS chapter III (regulations 7, 11, 17, 21, 31, 32, 33, 35, 36, 37 as adopted 1996): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.47(66).pdf</li><li>IMO, Resolution MSC.48(66), the LSA Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.48(66).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-medical-care-at-sea","v":6526214,"title":"Medical Care at Sea","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["medical","health","mlc","stcw","mfag","imgs","medical-chest","first-aid","topic","imo"],"body":"<h1>Medical Care at Sea — Fitness, Care on Board, Medical Chest and MFAG</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fitness to work</td><td><b>ILO/IMO Guidelines on the medical examinations of seafarers</b>, supporting MLC 2006 (ILO) and STCW 1978</td></tr><tr><td>Care on board</td><td><b>MLC Regulation 4.1 and Standard A4.1</b>, with first aid and medical care training under <b>STCW Table A-VI/4</b></td></tr><tr><td>Medicine chest</td><td>Required by MLC Std A4.1 para 4(a). Contents set by each flag State. Reference: WHO <b>International Medical Guide for Ships (IMGS)</b>, 3rd edition (2007)</td></tr><tr><td>Dangerous goods poisoning</td><td><b>MFAG</b>, the chemicals supplement to the IMGS</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Medical examination and certificate</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"med-validity\"><figcaption>How long a seafarer's medical certificate lasts</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The Guidelines give maritime administrations an internationally recognized set of criteria for deciding whether a seafarer can safely do routine and emergency duties at sea</li><li>They replaced the <b>1997 ILO/WHO Guidelines</b>. A joint ILO–IMO working group developed them, and the ILO Governing Body and the IMO Maritime Safety Committee endorsed them</li><li>Appendices A to E set the fitness criteria for <b>vision, hearing, physical capability, medication</b> and <b>common conditions</b></li><li>Validity (MLC Std A1.2 para 7, STCW reg I/9): <b>2 years</b> maximum. <b>1 year</b> under age 18. <b>6 years</b> for colour vision</li><li>A certificate that expires during a voyage stays valid until the next port with a recognized practitioner, for no more than <b>3 months</b></li><li>Examiners issue a shorter certificate only when they can justify it in that case</li></ul><h2>Medical care on board (MLC Reg 4.1, Std A4.1)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"med-care\"><figcaption>Who gives medical care on board</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Ship's doctor:</b> ships with <b>100 or more persons</b> on board, on international voyages usually longer than <b>3 days</b>, must carry a qualified medical doctor. Persons are not only seafarers</li><li><b>Other ships:</b> each flag State's law decides which ships need a doctor, considering voyage length, conditions and number of seafarers</li><li><b>Ships without a doctor</b> need at least one seafarer who is in charge of medical care and gives medicine as part of normal duties, or at least one who can give medical first aid. Both must be trained to STCW requirements</li><li><b>Medicine chest, equipment and medical guide</b> are carried on every ship (Std A4.1 para 4(a)). The flag State sets them, considering ship type, number of people and the nature, destination and duration of voyages. The chest is <b>regularly inspected</b> by the competent authority</li><li><b>Radio or satellite medical advice</b> must be available to ships at sea through a pre-arranged system</li><li><b>Free care:</b> UK guidance on the Convention says care is at no cost to the seafarer, with a right to go ashore for treatment not available on board where reasonably practicable. Costs can be recovered only in limited cases such as wilful misconduct</li><li><b>Records:</b> completed medical forms with patient details are confidential</li></ul><h2>STCW first aid and medical care (Table A-VI/4-1)</h2><p>The competence is \"apply immediate first aid in the event of accident or illness on board\". It covers:</p><ul><li>The first-aid kit and body structure and function</li><li>Toxicological hazards on board, including the <b>MFAG</b> or its national equivalent</li><li>Examination of a casualty or patient, spinal injuries, burns, scalds, heat and cold effects, fractures, dislocations and muscular injuries</li><li>Medical care of rescued persons</li><li>Radio medical advice, pharmacology and sterilization</li><li>Cardiac arrest, drowning and asphyxia</li></ul><h2>MFAG — Medical First Aid Guide for dangerous goods</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"med-mfag\"><figcaption>The three steps of the MFAG</figcaption></figure><ul><li>It is the <b>chemicals supplement to the IMGS</b>. The Maritime Safety Committee adopted it in May 1998, for use with Amendment 30-00 of the IMDG Code</li><li>Its aim: <b>initial management of chemical poisoning</b> and diagnosis within the limits of facilities at sea</li><li>Covers substances in the IMDG Code and the materials of Appendix B of the BC Code. It covers <b>accidental</b> exposure during carriage, not deliberate ingestion. Non-chemical illness goes to the IMGS. National guides apply where practice differs</li><li><b>Step 1</b> emergency action and diagnosis (\"Start here\"). <b>Step 2</b> twenty tables with short instructions for specific situations. <b>Step 3</b> appendices: detailed information, a list of medicines, and a list of chemicals by UN number and alphabetically</li><li>First priority is airway, breathing and circulation, then decontamination</li><li>Used together with the IMDG Code, BC Code, EmS (Emergency Procedures for Ships Carrying Dangerous Goods), IBC Code and IGC Code</li></ul><h2>Medical stores: national examples</h2><p>There is <b>no internationally agreed list</b> of medicines. Each flag State sets scales. Examples:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Categories</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Australia</b> (AMSA guidance, 2002)</td><td><b>A:</b> unlimited voyages, scaled for about 20 crew. <b>B:</b> short voyages, generally within 150 miles of a port with adequate medical equipment (200 if within helicopter range). <b>C:</b> smooth waters or close to shore</td></tr><tr><td><b>United Kingdom</b> (MSN 1905, 2021)</td><td><b>A:</b> seagoing, unlimited. <b>B:</b> under 150 nm (175 nm within helicopter range). <b>C:</b> harbour and close-to-shore craft. <b>D:</b> seagoing ships with no doctor carrying more than 12 passengers</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>UK: oxygen giving set with a reservoir of at least 400 litres, regulator, flow meter 1–15 litres/min and masks. A checklist is reviewed at least once a year. Ships carrying dangerous substances carry the antidotes named in the MFAG. Doctor required on UK ships with 100+ persons on voyages of 72 hours or more</li><li>Australia: store medicines in date, below 25 °C where possible, never frozen. Some need a locked cupboard, some refrigeration at 2 to 8 °C</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>MLC 2006 (ILO) seafarer health and welfare</li><li>STCW 1978 medical fitness and first aid training</li><li>IHR 2005 illness on board and port health</li><li>IMDG Code dangerous goods carried by sea</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The full text of MLC Standard A4.1 and Guideline B4.1.1</li><li>The IMO medical chest guidelines and the content of any national scale list beyond the examples above</li><li>STCW Table A-VI/4-2 (medical care) and which ranks need each course</li><li>The current MFAG edition and how it is updated</li><li>The ILO standard medical report form</li><li>Radio medical advice systems (for example, the Centres for Radio Medical Advice)</li><li>Refresher training intervals</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>ILO, Guidelines on the medical examinations of seafarers: https://www.ilo.org/resource/guidelines-medical-examinations-seafarers</li><li>Luxembourg maritime administration, MLC medical examination guidelines text: https://maritime.public.lu/dam-assets/texte/mlc/mlcmedical-en.pdf</li><li>ILO MLC FAQ, C4.1.b doctor on board: https://faqmlc.ilo.org/en/knowledgebase-category/c41/c41b/</li><li>ILO MLC FAQ, C4.1.c medical chest: https://faqmlc.ilo.org/en/?p=20538</li><li>UK MCA, MGN 482 (M) MLC 2006 Medical care: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5f6328578fa8f51065f7a5ea/MGN_482_Medical_Care_-_Gov.pdf</li><li>UK MCA, MSN 1905 Ships' medical stores: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/972284/MSN_1905__M+F_.pdf</li><li>AMSA, Guidance notes on medical facilities aboard ships (2002): https://www.legislation.gov.au/F2006B00669/asmade/2001-12-14/supportingmaterial1/original/pdf</li><li>STCW Table A-VI/4-1 (Maritime Academy copy): https://weh.maritime.edu/stcw/2016/tables/641.html</li><li>IMDG Code Supplement incl. MFAG (2014 text): https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.imdg.supp.2014.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-meteorology","v":225184957,"title":"Meteorology","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["meteorology","weather","imo","topic","wmo","solas","ice-patrol","beaufort"],"body":"<h1>Meteorology at Sea — Weather Services, Warnings and Danger Messages</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal basis</td><td><b>SOLAS Chapter V</b>: Reg 5 (weather services), Reg 6 (ice patrol), Regs 31–32 (danger messages), Reg 34 (voyage planning)</td></tr><tr><td>Who provides the forecasts</td><td>National meteorological services, in <b>21 METAREAs</b>, under the <b>WWMIWS</b>, run by <b>WMO and IMO</b></td></tr><tr><td>How you receive them</td><td><b>NAVTEX</b> and <b>SafetyNET (EGC)</b>, part of the GMDSS (GMDSS)</td></tr><tr><td>Main reference</td><td>WMO Publication No. 9, <b>Volume D – Information for Shipping</b></td></tr><tr><td>Wind warning levels</td><td><b>Gale</b> = Beaufort 8 to 9. <b>Storm</b> = Beaufort 10 or over</td></tr><tr><td>Master's duty</td><td>Send a <b>danger message</b> for dangerous ice, a tropical storm, or force 10 or more with no storm warning received</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"meteo-solas\"><figcaption>Who does what under SOLAS V</figcaption></figure><h2>SOLAS Chapter V: weather duties</h2><h3>Regulation 5: Meteorological services and warnings</h3><p><b>Governments</b></p><ul><li>Encourage ships to collect weather data, and encourage use of accurate instruments. Instrument checks are available on request, <b>free of charge</b> to the ship</li><li><b>Warn ships</b> of gales, storms and tropical cyclones, in text and, where practicable, in graphic form</li><li>Issue weather information (data, analyses, warnings and forecasts for weather, waves and ice) <b>at least twice daily</b></li><li>Equip selected ships with tested instruments and have them observe <b>at least four times daily</b>, where circumstances permit</li><li>Transmit weather observations free of charge to ships, and receive and relay weather messages</li></ul><p><b>Masters</b></p><ul><li>Are encouraged to report winds of <b>50 knots or more (Beaufort force 10)</b> to nearby ships and shore stations</li></ul><h3>Regulation 34: Safe navigation and avoidance of dangerous situations</h3><ul><li>Before sailing, the master must <b>plan the voyage</b> with appropriate charts and publications, following IMO voyage planning guidelines</li><li>The route must allow for relevant ships' routeing systems, <b>sufficient sea room</b>, known hazards and <b>adverse weather</b>, and marine environmental protection</li><li>The <b>owner, charterer or company must not stop or restrict</b> the master from any decision he thinks necessary for safe navigation or environmental protection</li></ul><h2>Beaufort scale</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"meteo-beaufort\"><figcaption>Wind force, speed and typical sea state</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Force</th><th>Name</th><th>Wind (knots)</th><th>Typical wave height</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Calm</td><td>under 1</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Light air</td><td>1–3</td><td>0.1 m</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Light breeze</td><td>4–6</td><td>0.2 m</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Gentle breeze</td><td>7–10</td><td>0.6 m</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Moderate breeze</td><td>11–16</td><td>1.0 m</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Fresh breeze</td><td>17–21</td><td>2.0 m</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Strong breeze</td><td>22–27</td><td>3.0 m</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Near gale</td><td>28–33</td><td>4.0 m</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gale</td><td>34–40</td><td>5.5 m</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Strong gale</td><td>41–47</td><td>7.0 m</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Storm</td><td>48–55</td><td>9.0 m</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Violent storm</td><td>56–63</td><td>11.5 m</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Hurricane</td><td>64 and over</td><td>14 m and over</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Wave heights are for well-developed wind waves in the open sea</li><li>Waves lag behind the wind, so the sea keeps building after the wind rises</li></ul><h2>Warnings and bulletins (IMO/IHO/WMO MSI Manual)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"meteo-bulletin\"><figcaption>Structure of a weather and sea bulletin</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Warnings are given for</b> gales (force 8 or 9) and storms (force 10 or over). Near gale (force 7) warnings are optional</li><li><b>Tropical cyclone warnings</b> are required: hurricanes (North Atlantic, eastern North Pacific), typhoons (Western Pacific), cyclones (Indian Ocean), and similar systems elsewhere</li><li><b>Bulletins</b> are issued at least <b>twice daily</b>, at a set time, in this order: <b>Part I</b> warnings, <b>Part II</b> synopsis, <b>Part III</b> forecasts</li><li>If no gale, storm or cyclone warning is in force, Part I says so</li><li>A warning is issued <b>immediately</b> when the need is clear, and broadcast at once. An unscheduled warning is <b>repeated 6 minutes later</b></li><li>A warning stays in force until amended or cancelled</li><li>Every warning gives: type of warning, UTC date and time, location, extent of the area, wind speed or force and direction. Cyclones and extra-tropical storms also give the time last located</li><li>Visibility is reported when it is <b>less than 5 nm</b>:</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Very poor</td><td>under 0.5 nm</td></tr><tr><td>Poor</td><td>0.5 to 2 nm</td></tr><tr><td>Moderate</td><td>2 to 5 nm</td></tr><tr><td>Good</td><td>over 5 nm</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>METAREAs and the WWMIWS</h2><ul><li>The oceans are divided into <b>21 METAREAs</b>. A <b>METAREA Coordinator</b> coordinates the forecasts for each area</li><li>A <b>Preparation Service</b> writes the forecasts and warnings. An <b>Issuing Service</b> broadcasts them for its METAREA</li><li>The <b>WWMIWS</b> (Worldwide Met-Ocean Information and Warning Service) is coordinated by <b>WMO and IMO</b>. Its warnings go out by <b>EGC</b> and <b>NAVTEX</b></li><li>METAREA limits do <b>not</b> affect national boundaries</li><li>Navigational warnings are a different service (NAVAREAs, from the IHO). See GMDSS</li><li><b>WMO Volume D</b> (Information for Shipping) lists the stations, broadcast schedules, satellite and NAVTEX details, visual storm warning signals, and contacts for METAREA coordinators, Port Meteorological Offices and ship weather routeing services. It is a reference to keep on the bridge</li></ul><h2>Danger messages</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"meteo-danger\"><figcaption>When a master must send a danger message</figcaption></figure><h3>Regulation 31: when you must send one</h3><p>A master who meets any of these must broadcast it <b>by all means</b> to nearby ships and to the competent authorities:</p><ul><li>Dangerous <b>ice</b></li><li>A dangerous <b>derelict</b>, or any other direct danger to navigation</li><li>A <b>tropical storm</b></li><li><b>Sub-freezing air with gale-force winds</b>, causing severe ice accretion</li><li><b>Force 10 or over with no storm warning received</b></li></ul><p>Other rules</p><ul><li>No fixed form. Plain language (preferably English) or the International Code of Signals can be used</li><li><b>Free of charge</b> to the ship. Radio messages are preceded by the <b>safety signal</b></li><li>Governments pass such information on promptly to those concerned and to other governments</li></ul><h3>Regulation 32: what the message must contain</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Danger</th><th>Information</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ice, derelicts, other dangers</td><td>Kind of danger, <b>position</b> when last seen, <b>time and date (UTC)</b> last seen</td></tr><tr><td>Tropical cyclone</td><td>That one has been met, time, date (UTC) and ship position. As far as practicable: corrected barometric pressure, <b>3-hour pressure tendency</b>, true wind direction, Beaufort force, sea state, swell (height, true direction, period), and ship's true course and speed</td></tr><tr><td>Force 10 or over, no storm warning</td><td>As for a cyclone, without sea and swell details</td></tr><tr><td>Sub-freezing air with gale and ice accretion</td><td>Time and date (UTC), air temperature, sea temperature if practicable, wind force and direction</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Follow-up reports</b> are desirable, not mandatory: hourly where practicable, and at least every <b>3 hours</b> while under the storm's influence</li></ul><h2>Ice patrol (Regulation 6)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"meteo-ice\"><figcaption>North Atlantic Ice Patrol under SOLAS V/6</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Ships</b> crossing the iceberg region guarded by the Ice Patrol in the ice season must <b>use its services</b></li><li><b>Ice season in the SOLAS text: 15 February to 1 July</b></li><li><b>Governments</b> keep up the ice patrol and ice study, and guard the south-eastern, southern and south-western limits of the <b>Grand Banks</b> iceberg region</li><li>The <b>United States</b> manages the service. Governments whose ships pass the region pay a share, based on their average gross tonnage over the previous three seasons</li><li>The US and/or Canada may stop the service with <b>18 months' written notice</b></li><li><b>International Ice Patrol (IIP)</b> is run by the <b>US Coast Guard</b>. It was formed after the <b>Titanic</b> sank on 15 April 1912 and has worked since 1913, apart from wartime</li><li>IIP bulletins go out by SafetyNET, NAVTEX and radiofax. Ships can send iceberg reports to IIP</li><li>IIP's own working season is longer than the SOLAS dates: in 2022 it ran from 11 January to 7 September</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>SOLAS 1974 full chapter structure</li><li>GMDSS NAVTEX, SafetyNET and MSI</li><li>IAMSAR Manual distress response</li><li>International Code of Signals signals for danger messages</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>Weather routeing methods and ship routeing company services</li><li>Heavy weather and tropical cyclone avoidance rules of thumb (for example the 1-2-3 rule, dangerous semicircle)</li><li>Voluntary Observing Ship (VOS) scheme details and observation hours</li><li>WMO sea state (Douglas) scale and swell definitions</li><li>Barometric pressure and cloud reading</li><li>Radiofax schedules and frequencies</li><li>The ice limit coordinates for each month</li><li>Visual storm warning signals</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>SOLAS Chapter V, text as published by the UK government: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f0081ed915d74e33f3c6e/solas_v_on_safety_of_navigation.pdf</li><li>IMO MSC.1/Circ.1310/Rev.1, Joint IMO/IHO/WMO Manual on Maritime Safety Information: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/Documents%20relevant%20to%20GMDSS/MSC.1-Circ.1310-Rev.1.pdf</li><li>WMO, Marine Services: https://wmo.int/all-activities/deliver-services/marine-services</li><li>WWMIWS website (Météo-France for WMO): https://wwmiws.wmo.int/</li><li>WMO No. 9 Volume D, Information for Shipping (NHC copy): https://www.Nhc.Noaa.Gov/pdf/Weather_Reporting_Volume_D_Information_for_Shipping.pdf</li><li>Met Office, Beaufort wind force scale: https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/guides/coast-and-sea/beaufort-scale</li><li>US Coast Guard, International Ice Patrol Annual Report 2022: https://www.navcen.uscg.gov/sites/default/files/pdf/Annual_Report_2022_Season.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-opa-90","v":218851146,"title":"OPA 90","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["opa-90","oil-pollution-act","usa","responsible-party","limits-of-liability","cofr","double-hull","oil-spill-liability-trust-fund","topic","imo"],"body":"<h1>OPA 90 — The US Oil Pollution Act of 1990</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>US federal law (33 U.S.C. 2701 and following) that makes the <b>responsible party</b> pay for oil spill removal and damages</td></tr><tr><td>Covers</td><td>Oil discharged, or a substantial threat of discharge, into <b>navigable waters</b>, the shoreline or the <b>EEZ</b></td></tr><tr><td>In force for</td><td>Incidents on or after <b>18 Aug 1990</b></td></tr><tr><td>Key rules</td><td>Liability (2702), defences (2703), limits (2704), claims (2713), financial responsibility (2716), double hull (46 U.S.C. 3703a)</td></tr><tr><td>Regulations</td><td><b>33 CFR 138</b> (limits and COFR), <b>33 CFR 153</b> (spill reporting), <b>33 CFR 155</b> subpart D (response plans)</td></tr><tr><td>Enforced by</td><td>US Coast Guard, with EPA</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> OPA 90 is <b>strict liability</b>. The ship owner, operator or demise charterer pays first, and the limits of liability break if a safety regulation was violated or the spill was not reported.</blockquote><h2>Words that matter (33 U.S.C. 2701)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Oil</b></td><td>Oil of any kind or form, including petroleum, fuel oil, sludge and oil refuse. Substances listed as hazardous under CERCLA are not \"oil\"</td></tr><tr><td><b>Discharge</b></td><td>Any emission, intentional or not, including spilling, leaking, pumping or dumping. Natural seepage is excluded</td></tr><tr><td><b>Incident</b></td><td>An occurrence, or series with the same origin, involving vessels or facilities, that causes a discharge or a substantial threat of one</td></tr><tr><td><b>Navigable waters</b></td><td>US waters including the <b>territorial sea, 3 miles</b> seaward from ordinary low water</td></tr><tr><td><b>Barrel</b></td><td>42 US gallons at 60°F</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tank vessel</b></td><td>A vessel built or adapted to carry, or that carries, oil or hazardous material in bulk as cargo or cargo residue, that is US, in navigable waters, or transferring in a place under US jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td><b>Responsible party, vessel</b></td><td>\"Any person owning, operating, or demise chartering the vessel\". For a single-hull tank vessel, also the owner of the oil carried, after 31 Dec 2010</td></tr><tr><td><b>Removal costs</b></td><td>Costs after a discharge, or to prevent or reduce a substantial threat</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fund</b></td><td>The <b>Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund</b> (26 U.S.C. 9509)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Liability (2702)</h2><p>The responsible party pays <b>removal costs</b> and <b>damages</b>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Removal costs</th><th>By</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Costs of the United States, a State or an Indian tribe</td><td>Under the Clean Water Act, the Intervention on the High Seas Act or State law</td></tr><tr><td>Costs of <b>any person</b></td><td>For acts consistent with the <b>National Contingency Plan</b></td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Damage</th><th>Claimant</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Natural resources</b>, including assessment costs</td><td>US, State, tribal or foreign trustee</td></tr><tr><td><b>Real or personal property</b></td><td>Owner or lessee</td></tr><tr><td><b>Subsistence use</b></td><td>Any person who uses the resource, whoever owns it</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lost taxes, royalties, rents, fees, profit shares</b></td><td>US, State or local government</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lost profits or earning capacity</b></td><td>Any claimant</td></tr><tr><td><b>Public services</b> (fire, safety, health)</td><td>State or local government</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Natural resource damages (2706)</b> are measured by the cost of restoration, rehabilitation, replacement or acquisition, plus the loss in value until restored, plus reasonable assessment costs. No double recovery. Trustees' assessments made under the regulations carry a <b>rebuttable presumption</b> of validity</li><li><b>Interest (2705)</b> runs from the <b>30th day</b> after the claim is presented. It is paid <b>outside</b> the liability limits</li><li><b>Foreign claimants (2707)</b> must show they were not otherwise compensated, and a treaty or State Department certification must allow it</li></ul><h2>Defences (2703)</h2><p>No liability if the responsible party proves, by a preponderance of the evidence, that the discharge was caused <b>solely</b> by:</p><ul><li>an <b>act of God</b></li><li>an <b>act of war</b></li><li>an <b>act or omission of a third party</b>, excluding its employees, agents and parties in a contractual relationship with it</li><li>or a combination of these</li></ul><p>For the third-party defence the party must also show <b>due care</b> and <b>precautions against foreseeable acts</b> of that third party.</p><p><b>All defences are lost</b> if the party fails or refuses to:</p><ul><li>report the incident as required by law, when it knows or has reason to know of it</li><li>give all reasonable cooperation and assistance to the responsible official</li><li>comply, without sufficient cause, with an order under 33 U.S.C. 1321(c) or (e) or the Intervention on the High Seas Act</li></ul><h2>Limits of liability (2704, 33 CFR 138.230)</h2><p>The limit covers liability <b>and removal costs</b> for each incident. It is raised for inflation by rule. Current vessel figures, as printed in the 2025 CFR:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Vessel</th><th>Limit, whichever is greater</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tank vessel, <b>single hull</b>, over 3,000 GT</td><td><b>$4,000 per GT</b> or <b>$29,591,300</b></td></tr><tr><td>Tank vessel, <b>not single hull</b>, over 3,000 GT</td><td><b>$2,500 per GT</b> or <b>$21,521,000</b></td></tr><tr><td>Tank vessel, single hull, 3,000 GT or less</td><td><b>$4,000 per GT</b> or <b>$8,070,400</b></td></tr><tr><td>Tank vessel, not single hull, 3,000 GT or less</td><td><b>$2,500 per GT</b> or <b>$5,380,300</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Any other vessel</b> (including edible-oil tankers and oil spill response vessels)</td><td><b>$1,300 per GT</b> or <b>$1,076,000</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Statute's base figures</b> (before inflation): tankers $3,000 per GT (single hull) and $1,900 (others); other vessels $950 per GT or $800,000</li><li>Adjustment: the President adjusts the limits for the Consumer Price Index at least <b>every 3 years</b>. The 138.230 amendment history lists 2015, 2019 and 2022</li><li><b>Offshore facility:</b> all removal costs plus <b>$75,000,000</b>. <b>Onshore facility and deepwater port:</b> <b>$350,000,000</b></li></ul><p><b>The limit does not apply (2704(c))</b> if the incident was proximately caused by:</p><ul><li><b>gross negligence or wilful misconduct</b>, or</li><li><b>violation of an applicable federal safety, construction or operating regulation</b></li></ul><p>by the responsible party, its agent or employee, or a person under contract with it. It also does not apply if the party fails to report, fails to cooperate, or ignores an order without sufficient cause.</p><h2>Reporting a spill (33 CFR 153.203)</h2><ul><li>The <b>person in charge</b> of the vessel or facility <b>immediately</b> notifies the <b>National Response Center (NRC)</b> as soon as there is knowledge of a discharge in violation of the Clean Water Act</li><li><b>NRC:</b> toll free <b>800-424-8802</b>, direct 202-267-2675</li><li>If the NRC cannot be reached, report to the Coast Guard or EPA <b>On-Scene Coordinator</b>, who relays it. If neither can be reached, report to the <b>nearest Coast Guard unit</b> and notify the NRC as soon as possible</li><li>A report under this section also satisfies <b>33 CFR 151.15</b> and <b>46 CFR 4.05-1</b></li></ul><h2>Claims (2713, 2714)</h2><p>1. The claimant first <b>presents the claim to the responsible party</b> (or its guarantor)</p><p>2. If every party denies liability, or nobody settles within <b>90 days</b>, the claimant may <b>sue</b> or present the claim to the <b>Fund</b></p><p>3. Claims may go <b>straight to the Fund</b> where the President has advertised, where a Governor claims State removal costs, where a responsible party claims under 2708, or where a US claimant is hurt by a foreign offshore unit</p><p>4. The Fund cannot approve a claim while the claimant is suing for the same costs</p><p><b>Designation of the source (2714):</b> the President designates the source of the spill and notifies the responsible party and guarantor. If the party does not deny it within <b>5 days</b>, it must advertise the designation and the claim procedure, <b>starting within 15 days</b> and running at least <b>30 days</b>. The advertisement states that interim short-term claims are allowed and that payment does not bar later recovery.</p><p><b>Time limits to sue (2717)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Within</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Damages</b></td><td><b>3 years</b> from when the loss and its link to the discharge were reasonably discoverable. For natural resource damages, 3 years from completion of the damage assessment</td></tr><tr><td><b>Removal costs</b></td><td><b>3 years</b> from completion of the removal action</td></tr><tr><td><b>Contribution</b></td><td><b>3 years</b> from the judgment or approved settlement</td></tr><tr><td><b>Subrogation</b></td><td><b>3 years</b> from the date of payment</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Court:</b> US district courts have exclusive original jurisdiction, wherever the discharge or damage happened, or where the defendant lives, has its principal office or an agent. State courts can also hear claims</li><li><b>Other parties (2708 to 2710):</b> a responsible party can claim its own costs only if it has a defence or a limit, and then only the amount above its limit. <b>Contribution</b> is allowed against others. <b>Indemnity</b> and insurance contracts are allowed, but cannot move the liability off the responsible party</li></ul><h2>The Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund (2712)</h2><p>The Fund pays for:</p><p>1. removal costs of the federal government, and of States, local governments or tribes under agreement</p><p>2. trustee costs for damage assessment and restoration plans</p><p>3. removal costs and damages from a foreign offshore unit</p><p>4. claims <b>not compensated</b> by the responsible party, including Coast Guard costs of processing them</p><p>5. federal administration, capped each fiscal year at <b>$25,000,000</b> (Coast Guard operations), <b>$15,000,000</b> (NOAA response and damage assessment) and <b>$27,250,000</b> (research and development under subchapter IV)</p><p>Anyone who pays compensation, including the Fund, takes over the claimant's rights (<b>subrogation</b>, 2715). The Fund recovers from the responsible party, with interest and costs.</p><h2>Financial responsibility (2716, 33 CFR 138)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Who must have it</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vessel <b>over 300 GT</b> using US waters, ports or places, or deepwater ports</td><td>Yes. A non-self-propelled vessel that carries no oil as cargo or fuel is excluded</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tank vessel over 100 GT</b></td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Vessel of <b>any size</b> transshipping or lightering oil in the EEZ for a US place</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Public vessels</td><td>Not covered</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Amount:</b> at least the maximum liability under 2704(a). For several vessels, cover only the one with the highest limit</li><li><b>Methods:</b> insurance, surety bond, guarantee, letter of credit, self-insurance, or other evidence the Secretary accepts</li><li><b>CERCLA amount added (138.100):</b> the greater of <b>$5,000,000 or $300 per GT</b> for vessels over 300 GT carrying a hazardous substance as cargo. For any other vessel over 300 GT the greater of <b>$500,000 or $300 per GT</b>. The total is the OPA 90 amount plus the CERCLA amount</li><li><b>Direct action</b> lies against the guarantor. The guarantor can use the responsible party's defences and wilful misconduct, and is liable only up to the amount it provided</li><li><b>Sanctions:</b> Treasury withholds or revokes <b>clearance</b>. The ship may be refused entry or <b>detained</b>, and one found without evidence in navigable waters is liable to <b>seizure and forfeiture</b></li><li><b>Civil penalty (2716a):</b> up to <b>$25,000 per day</b> of violation</li></ul><p><b>The certificate (COFR, 33 CFR 138.80)</b></p><ul><li>The <b>COFR operator</b> applies to the Director <b>at least 21 days</b> before the certificate is needed, with the fee, and names a US <b>agent for service of process</b></li><li>Changes are reported in writing within <b>5 business days</b> of discovery</li><li><b>Fleet certificate:</b> evidence covers the largest unmanned non-tank barge. <b>Master certificate:</b> the largest tank vessel and the largest non-tank vessel. A master certificate holder reports at application and every <b>6 months</b></li><li>Before operating a vessel above the gross tonnage on the certificate, the operator files a <b>new or amended application</b> with new evidence</li></ul><h2>Prevention rules</h2><p><b>Double hull (46 U.S.C. 3703a)</b></p><ul><li>A tank vessel built or adapted to carry oil in bulk must have a <b>double hull</b> to operate in US waters, including the EEZ</li><li><b>Not covered:</b> spill response vessels, vessels <b>under 5,000 GT</b> with double containment judged as effective, certain Alaska barges under <b>1,500 GT</b>, and vessels in the National Defense Reserve Fleet</li><li><b>Phase-out of older ships</b> (contracted before 30 Jun 1990 and delivered before 1 Jan 1994): a <b>single-hull vessel may not operate after 1 Jan 2010</b>, and one with a <b>double bottom or double sides after 1 Jan 2015</b></li><li>Earlier age limits for a single hull: <b>25 years</b> (5,000 to under 30,000 GT) and <b>23 years</b> (30,000 GT or more)</li></ul><p><b>Vessel response plans (33 CFR 155 subpart D)</b></p><ul><li>The plan covers <b>all US areas</b> where the vessel handles, stores or carries oil, including offshore transit areas. It is in <b>English</b>, and in a language the crew with plan duties understands</li><li><b>11 sections:</b> general information, notification, shipboard mitigation, shore-based response, contacts, training, exercises, plan review and update, on-board notification checklist (unmanned tank barges only), a geographic appendix for each Captain of the Port zone, and a vessel-specific appendix</li><li>A <b>qualified individual</b> and an alternate are named. Each holds a <b>full current copy</b>. The vessel carries an English copy of the specified sections and the <b>USCG approval letter</b> (an electronic copy is allowed)</li><li>The plan agrees with the National Contingency Plan (40 CFR 300) and the Area Contingency Plans</li><li><b>Annual review</b> within <b>1 month</b> of the approval anniversary, recorded on the changes page. <b>Resubmit</b> the whole plan <b>6 months</b> before the approval period ends</li><li><b>Revisions</b> for significant change go in at least <b>30 days</b> before operation. Phone list changes need no prior approval</li><li>A petition on a deficiency notice is due within the compliance period or <b>7 days</b>, whichever is less. An appeal of non-approval is due within <b>21 days</b></li></ul><p><b>Prince William Sound (2737):</b> a tank vessel that has spilled <b>over 1,000,000 gallons</b> of oil into the sea after 22 Mar 1989 may not operate in Prince William Sound, Alaska.</p><h2>State law</h2><p>OPA 90 does <b>not preempt</b> State law (2718). A State may impose <b>additional liability, requirements or penalties</b> for discharges within its waters, and keep its own oil pollution funds. A State may also enforce the financial responsibility rule in its own waters (2719).</p><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>CLC and Fund 1992 the international scheme that OPA 90 sits beside</li><li>MARPOL 73/78 the international discharge rules</li><li>US Maritime Regulations Guide other US rules a ship meets</li><li>Salvage 1989 salvage of ship and cargo after an incident</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The <b>$1 billion</b> per-incident limit of the Fund and its <b>$500 million</b> natural resource sub-limit (they are in the Internal Revenue Code, not the text checked)</li><li><b>33 CFR 138.110</b> forms of guaranty in detail, the <b>fees</b>, and <b>self-insurer</b> conditions</li><li><b>Vessel response plan</b> approval details for tank vessels (33 CFR 155.1035 to 155.1050), worst-case discharge figures and response resource tiers</li><li><b>Tank vessel manning</b>, licensing and <b>alcohol and drug</b> rules under Title IV</li><li>The <b>National Contingency Plan</b> (40 CFR 300) and the role of the <b>Federal On-Scene Coordinator</b></li><li><b>Civil and criminal penalties</b> for the spill itself under the Clean Water Act section 311</li><li><b>State laws</b>, for example California and Washington</li><li>The <b>latest inflation adjustment</b>. Figures are from the 2025 CFR print and may have been updated since</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>33 U.S.C. chapter 40, Oil Pollution (2023 US Code): https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title33/pdf/USCODE-2023-title33-chap40.pdf</li><li>33 U.S.C. 2704 Limits on liability: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/33/2704</li><li>33 U.S.C. 2703 Defenses: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/33/2703</li><li>33 U.S.C. 2702 Liability: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/33/2702</li><li>33 U.S.C. 2701 Definitions: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/33/2701</li><li>33 CFR 138.230 Limits of liability (2025 CFR): https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CFR-2025-title33-vol2/pdf/CFR-2025-title33-vol2-sec138-230.pdf</li><li>33 CFR 138.20, 138.80, 138.100: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/138.20 , https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/138.80 , https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/138.100</li><li>33 CFR 155.1030 and 155.1070 (response plans): https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/155.1030 , https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/155.1070</li><li>33 CFR 153.203 Procedure for notice of discharge: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/153.203</li><li>46 U.S.C. 3703a Tank vessel construction standards: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/46/3703a</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-unclos-1982","v":187995547,"title":"UNCLOS 1982","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["unclos","law-of-the-sea","un","maritime-zones","flag-state","piracy","itlos","convention","imo","topic"],"body":"<h1>UNCLOS 1982 — United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>10 Dec 1982</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>16 Nov 1994</b> (12 months after the 60th ratification, Art 308)</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td><b>170</b> (UN status table, 31 Mar 2025)</td></tr><tr><td>Implementing agreements</td><td><b>1994 Part XI Agreement</b> (153 parties, in force 28 Jul 1996). <b>1995 Fish Stocks Agreement</b> (93 parties, in force 11 Dec 2001). <b>BBNJ Agreement</b> (adopted 19 Jun 2023, in force <b>17 Jan 2026</b>, 83 parties)</td></tr><tr><td>Reservations</td><td>None allowed unless another article expressly permits them (Art 309)</td></tr><tr><td>Why it matters to ships</td><td>It sets where a coastal State's power ends and the flag State's begins. IMO conventions work inside this frame</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Maritime zones</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"unclos-zones\"><figcaption>Maritime zones measured from the baselines</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Limit from baselines</th><th>What the coastal State has</th><th>Foreign ships</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Territorial sea</b> (Arts 2–3)</td><td>Up to <b>12 nm</b></td><td>Sovereignty</td><td><b>Innocent passage</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Contiguous zone</b> (Art 33)</td><td>Up to <b>24 nm</b></td><td>Control to prevent and punish breaches of its laws</td><td>Freedoms of navigation</td></tr><tr><td><b>EEZ</b> (Arts 55–58)</td><td>Up to <b>200 nm</b></td><td>Sovereign rights over resources (Art 56)</td><td>Freedoms of navigation and overflight (Art 58)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Continental shelf</b> (Art 76)</td><td><b>200 nm</b>, or the edge of the margin. Fixed points not beyond <b>350 nm</b>, or <b>100 nm</b> from the 2,500 m isobath</td><td>Sovereign rights over the seabed's natural resources (Art 77)</td><td>Water above is EEZ or high seas</td></tr><tr><td><b>High seas</b> (Arts 86–87)</td><td>All sea outside the above</td><td>None</td><td>Open to all States, coastal or land-locked</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Artificial islands and installations</b> in the EEZ: the coastal State has the exclusive right to build or authorize them. They do not have the status of islands (Art 60)</li><li><b>Archipelagic States</b> (Arts 46–53): baselines not longer than <b>100 nm</b> (with limited exceptions). All ships have the right of <b>archipelagic sea lanes passage</b></li></ul><h2>Passage</h2><ul><li><b>Innocent passage</b> (Arts 17, 19, 25): all ships have it in the territorial sea, as long as it is \"not prejudicial to the peace, good order or security of the coastal State\". The coastal State may take necessary steps to prevent passage that is not innocent</li><li><b>Transit passage</b> (Arts 37–39): in straits used for international navigation, all ships and aircraft have it and it \"shall not be impeded\". Ships must proceed without delay</li></ul><h2>Flag State and the high seas</h2><ul><li><b>Genuine link</b> between the State and the ship (Art 91). A ship sails under <b>one flag</b> and is generally under that State's exclusive jurisdiction on the high seas (Art 92)</li><li><b>Flag State duties</b> (Art 94): effectively exercise jurisdiction and control in administrative, technical and social matters. Keep a ship register. Deal with manning, labour conditions and crew training, signals, communications and collision prevention, and safety at sea</li><li>After certain high seas casualties the flag State holds an inquiry by a suitably qualified person (Art 94(7))</li><li><b>Rescue</b> (Art 98(1)(a)): the master must render assistance to any person found at sea in danger of being lost</li><li><b>Piracy</b> (Art 101): illegal violence, detention or depredation for private ends by the crew or passengers of a private ship or aircraft. Any State may seize a pirate ship on the high seas (Art 105)</li><li><b>Drugs</b> (Art 108): all States cooperate to suppress illicit drug traffic by ships on the high seas</li><li><b>Right of visit</b> (Art 110): a warship may board a foreign ship only with reasonable grounds to suspect piracy, slave trade, unauthorized broadcasting, statelessness, or that the ship has the warship's own nationality</li><li><b>Hot pursuit</b> (Art 111): may start when the coastal State has good reason to believe the ship broke its laws, and extends to the EEZ and continental shelf</li></ul><h2>Pollution from ships (Part XII)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"unclos-pollution\"><figcaption>Who enforces against ship pollution</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General</td><td>States protect and preserve the marine environment (Art 192), with the best practicable means (Art 194)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Flag State</b> (Arts 211(2), 217)</td><td>Make laws for its ships, ensure they comply with applicable international rules and standards, and investigate at once and prosecute where appropriate</td></tr><tr><td><b>Port State</b> (Arts 218–219)</td><td>May investigate when a ship is voluntarily in port. Takes administrative measures to stop a substandard ship sailing, as far as practicable</td></tr><tr><td><b>Coastal State</b> (Art 211(4), 220)</td><td>May make laws for foreign ships in its territorial sea, but they must not hamper innocent passage. May start proceedings for violations in its territorial sea or EEZ</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Safeguards for the ship</b></p><ul><li>Only <b>monetary penalties</b> may be imposed on foreign ships for violations beyond the territorial sea (Art 230)</li><li>Proceedings by the coastal State stop if the flag State starts its own penalty proceedings within <b>6 months</b>, unless exceptions apply (Art 228)</li><li>States are <b>liable</b> for loss caused by unlawful enforcement (Art 232)</li><li>Art 233: none of this changes the regime of straits used for international navigation</li><li>MARPOL and the other IMO conventions supply the \"applicable international rules and standards\" (see MARPOL 73/78)</li></ul><h2>Settling disputes</h2><ul><li>States settle disputes by peaceful means (Art 279) and may choose any means (Art 280). Any party may submit a dispute to the competent court or tribunal (Art 286)</li><li><b>Art 287 choices:</b> the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea (<b>ITLOS</b>), the International Court of Justice, an arbitral tribunal (Annex VII), or a special arbitral tribunal (Annex VIII). A State with no declaration is deemed to accept <b>Annex VII arbitration</b></li><li><b>Provisional measures</b> (Art 290) can be ordered</li><li><b>Prompt release</b> (Art 292): applies where a State detains another State's vessel</li><li><b>ITLOS</b> has <b>21</b> independent members (Annex VI, Art 2). Its Seabed Disputes Chamber has 11 (Annex VI, Art 35)</li><li>Optional exceptions (Art 298) and limits (Art 297, for example marine scientific research) apply to some disputes</li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>SOLAS 1974 flag State safety duties</li><li>MARPOL 73/78 pollution rules</li><li>SUA 1988 offences against ships</li><li>Salvage 1989 help at sea</li><li>Intervention 1969 action beyond the territorial sea after casualties</li><li>SAR 1979 rescue duties</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The 500 m safety zones around installations (Art 60(5))</li><li>Art 18 (meaning of passage) and Art 21 (coastal State laws on innocent passage)</li><li>Straits detail beyond Arts 37–39 (duties of ships in transit, Art 39(1)(b))</li><li>Master's full duty to proceed to rescue, and the duty to render assistance to ships in collision (Art 98 in full)</li><li>Part XI Area and the International Seabed Authority (structure, Art 156)</li><li>Marine scientific research rules and the exact text of Art 246</li><li>Land-locked State rights, islands and maritime boundary delimitation (Arts 15, 74, 83)</li><li>The current number of parties (170 is at 31 Mar 2025)</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>UN, Convention text (via University of Oslo mirror): https://www.jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/08/8-01/unclos.xml</li><li>UN, UNCLOS status tables (as at 31 Mar 2025): https://www.un.org/Depts/los/reference_files/StatusTablesEnglish.pdf</li><li>IISD, BBNJ Agreement enters into force: https://sdg.iisd.org/news/bbnj-agreement-enters-into-force/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-us-maritime-regulations-guide","v":134698439,"title":"US Maritime Regulations Guide","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["usa","opa-90","vgp","vida","eca","sewage","ballast-water","uscg","cfr","notice-of-arrival","cofr","port-state-control"],"body":"<h1>US Maritime Regulations Guide — What a ship must know before it calls at a US port</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Law and regulation</th><th>Enforced by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oil spill liability</td><td>OPA 90, 33 U.S.C. 2701 ff., with <b>33 CFR 138, 153 and 155</b></td><td>USCG</td></tr><tr><td>Other discharges (graywater, bilge, scrubbers and more)</td><td>VGP and VIDA</td><td>EPA, USCG</td></tr><tr><td>Air emissions</td><td><b>MARPOL Annex VI</b> and the North American and US Caribbean Sea <b>ECAs</b>. Engines under <b>40 CFR 1042 and 1043</b></td><td>EPA, USCG</td></tr><tr><td>Sewage</td><td>Clean Water Act <b>section 312</b>. EPA standards in <b>40 CFR 140</b>. USCG design rules in <b>33 CFR 159</b></td><td>USCG</td></tr><tr><td>Ballast water</td><td><b>33 CFR 151 subpart D</b> and <b>46 CFR 162</b> (type approval)</td><td>USCG</td></tr><tr><td>Arrival and port state control</td><td><b>33 CFR 160 subpart C</b> (Notice of Arrival). USCG port state control exams</td><td>USCG</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Many US rules go beyond the IMO conventions. They apply to foreign ships in US waters. A ship that follows MARPOL and BWM only can still break a US rule.</blockquote><h2>Where the rules are written</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Title of the CFR</th><th>Subject</th><th>Used here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>33 CFR</b></td><td>Navigation and navigable waters</td><td>Parts 138 (financial responsibility), 151 (ballast and pollution), 155 (oil pollution prevention), 159 (sanitation devices), 160 (notice of arrival)</td></tr><tr><td><b>40 CFR</b></td><td>Protection of environment (EPA)</td><td>Part 140 (sanitation standard), parts 1042 and 1043 (marine engines and fuel)</td></tr><tr><td><b>46 CFR</b></td><td>Shipping</td><td>Part 162 (ballast water system approval)</td></tr><tr><td><b>U.S.C.</b></td><td>The statutes the CFR rests on</td><td>33 U.S.C. 2701 ff. (OPA 90)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Oil Pollution Act 1990 (OPA 90)</h2><p>Liability, defences, limits, COFR, claims, double hull and response plans are in OPA 90.</p><h2>Vessel discharges: VGP and VIDA</h2><p>The 2013 permit, the replacement law and the 20 discharge standards are in VGP and VIDA.</p><h2>Emission Control Areas (ECA)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>ECAs near the US</b></td><td><b>North American</b> area (coasts of the US and Canada, and Saint-Pierre-et-Miquelon) and <b>US Caribbean Sea</b> area (Puerto Rico and the US Virgin Islands)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sulphur in an ECA</b></td><td><b>1.00% m/m</b> from 1 Jul 2010. <b>0.10% m/m</b> from <b>1 Jan 2015</b> (MARPOL Annex VI reg 14.4)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sulphur elsewhere</b></td><td><b>3.50%</b> from 1 Jan 2012. <b>0.50%</b> from <b>1 Jan 2020</b> (reg 14.1)</td></tr><tr><td><b>NOx Tier III</b></td><td>Ships constructed (keel laid) on or after <b>1 Jan 2016</b>. Emergency-only engines such as emergency generators, emergency fire pumps and lifeboat engines are excepted</td></tr><tr><td><b>Changeover</b></td><td>A <b>written changeover procedure</b> on board. Enough time to flush before entering. Log the <b>volume of low-sulphur fuel in each tank</b>, and the <b>date, time and position</b> of the changeover, in the logbook the flag State prescribes (reg 14.6)</td></tr><tr><td><b>US engine rules</b></td><td>US-flag engines meet <b>40 CFR 1042 and 1043</b>. Foreign ships must show that engines that are not Tier III were not and will not be used in the ECA</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pilot fuel</b></td><td>Within the US ECAs it meets the <b>0.10%</b> limit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Scrubber waste</b></td><td>Under the 2013 VGP, sludge and residue from washwater treatment is <b>not discharged</b> within 3 nm. It goes ashore. Monitoring equipment is calibrated <b>at least yearly</b> or as the maker says</td></tr><tr><td><b>Dual-fuel engines</b></td><td>Switching fuel mode happens <b>outside</b> the ECA. Averaging of NOx is not allowed</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Sewage</h2><p>Under Clean Water Act section 312, a vessel with an <b>installed toilet</b> on US navigable waters, including the 3 nm territorial sea, uses a <b>USCG-certified marine sanitation device (MSD)</b>. A portable toilet is not covered. EPA sets the performance standard. The USCG sets design, certification and installation.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>Standard</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Type I</b></td><td>Flow-through, usually macerate and disinfect. <b>Vessels 65 ft (about 20 m) or less only</b></td><td>Fecal coliform <b>not above 1,000 per 100 mL</b> and <b>no visible floating solids</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Type II</b></td><td>Flow-through, often biological treatment. Any length</td><td>Fecal coliform <b>not above 200 per 100 mL</b> and suspended solids <b>not above 150 mg/L</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Type III</b></td><td>Holding tank. Disposal ashore, or beyond 3 nm</td><td>No numeric standard. Must <b>prevent any overboard discharge</b> of sewage</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Discharge is banned even when treated</b> in a designated <b>no-discharge zone</b>, in freshwater lakes or impoundments with an entrance too shallow for vessels with installed toilets, and in rivers that do not carry interstate traffic of such vessels</li><li>A State cannot set its own MSD design or use rules (houseboats excepted), but can ask EPA for a <b>no-discharge zone</b></li></ul><h2>Ballast water (33 CFR 151 subpart D)</h2><p>One of <b>five</b> methods must be used by a vessel with ballast tanks operating in US waters (151.2025):</p><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>1. Approved BWMS</b></td><td>A system approved by the Coast Guard under <b>46 CFR 162</b>. It meets the discharge standard at every discharge</td></tr><tr><td><b>2. US public water</b></td><td>Only water from a US public system that meets 40 CFR 141 and 143. Tanks cleaned, or never holding ambient water. Source records and an invoice kept</td></tr><tr><td><b>3. Exchange</b></td><td>A <b>complete exchange at least 200 nm from any shore</b>. Only if the vessel is not yet required to have an approved BWMS</td></tr><tr><td><b>4. No discharge</b></td><td>No ballast discharged into US waters</td></tr><tr><td><b>5. Transfer</b></td><td>To an onshore facility or another vessel for treatment</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>An <b>alternate management system (AMS)</b>, fitted before the vessel's compliance date, may be used for <b>up to 5 years</b> after that date</li><li>A ship with a BWMS in independent laboratory testing is deemed compliant with method 1</li></ul><p><b>Discharge standard (151.2030)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Organisms <b>50 µm or larger</b></td><td>Fewer than <b>10 per m³</b></td></tr><tr><td>Organisms <b>10 to under 50 µm</b></td><td>Fewer than <b>10 per mL</b></td></tr><tr><td>*Vibrio cholerae* (O1 and O139)</td><td>Less than <b>1 cfu per 100 mL</b></td></tr><tr><td>*Escherichia coli*</td><td>Fewer than <b>250 cfu per 100 mL</b></td></tr><tr><td>Intestinal enterococci</td><td>Fewer than <b>100 cfu per 100 mL</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>When an approved system is required (151.2035)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ballast capacity</th><th>Built</th><th>Compliance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All</td><td>On or after <b>1 Dec 2013</b></td><td>On delivery</td></tr><tr><td>Under 1,500 m³</td><td>Before 1 Dec 2013</td><td>First scheduled drydocking after <b>1 Jan 2016</b></td></tr><tr><td>1,500 to 5,000 m³</td><td>Before 1 Dec 2013</td><td>First scheduled drydocking after <b>1 Jan 2014</b></td></tr><tr><td>Over 5,000 m³</td><td>Before 1 Dec 2013</td><td>First scheduled drydocking after <b>1 Jan 2016</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Alaskan North Slope crude exporters also do the mandatory deep-water exchange under 15 CFR 754.2(j) unless it would endanger the vessel or crew</li><li>Rule 151.2036 lets the Coast Guard <b>extend a compliance date</b>. Rule 151.2060 sets the <b>reporting</b> and 151.2070 the <b>recordkeeping</b></li></ul><h2>Coming into a US port</h2><h3>Notice of Arrival, NOA (33 CFR 160 subpart C)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Voyage time</th><th>Submit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>96 hours or more</b></td><td>At least <b>96 hours</b> before arrival</td></tr><tr><td><b>Less than 96 hours</b></td><td>Before departure, and at least <b>24 hours</b> before arrival</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Updates</b> are due as soon as practicable and at least <b>24 hours</b> before arrival (voyage of 24 h or more remaining), or <b>12 hours</b> before arrival (under 24 h remaining).</p><ul><li>Applies to <b>US and foreign vessels</b> bound for or leaving a US port or place. It is <b>not</b> required of US recreational vessels</li><li>Exempt unless carrying certain dangerous cargo: vessels of <b>300 GT or less</b> (except foreign vessels entering the 7th Coast Guard District), vessels in one Captain of the Port zone, force majeure arrivals, public vessels, US Great Lakes vessels other than tankers</li><li>Two or more consecutive US calls on one voyage can use <b>one NOA</b>, at least 96 hours before the first port</li><li>Submit through the <b>National Vessel Movement Center</b>, by <b>eNOAD</b> (website, XML or InfoPath), email, fax or telephone</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Information</th><th>Includes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Vessel</b></td><td>Name, owner, flag, call sign, IMO number, operator, charterer, class society</td></tr><tr><td><b>Voyage</b></td><td>Last <b>five</b> ports and dates, US destinations, ETA, ETD, current position, 24-hour contact</td></tr><tr><td><b>Cargo</b></td><td>General description, dangerous cargo names, UN numbers, amounts</td></tr><tr><td><b>Crew and others</b></td><td>Full name, date of birth, nationality, passport or mariner document number, position, embarkation point</td></tr><tr><td><b>Equipment</b></td><td>Operational condition under 33 CFR 164.35</td></tr><tr><td><b>ISM and ISPS</b></td><td>Issue dates of the DOC and SMC, and of the ISSC with the plan and company officer details. Under 500 GT need not give the ISM notice</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Hazardous conditions (160.215):</b> any condition on board, or caused by the vessel, that may hurt the vessel, a bridge, a structure, a shore area, or the environment of the port is reported <b>immediately</b> to the nearest Coast Guard Marine Safety Office or Group Office. A written report under 46 CFR 4.05-10 follows.</p><h3>Port state control by the Coast Guard</h3><ul><li>The USCG examines foreign ships under SOLAS and MARPOL. <b>2025 figures:</b> <b>8,999 SOLAS safety exams</b>, <b>77 detentions</b>, an annual detention rate of <b>0.85%</b> (0.94% in the year before)</li><li>Fire safety and life-saving appliance detentions fell again in 2025. Recognised organizations linked to detentions fell from <b>eight to seven</b></li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>MARPOL 73/78 the international base for the discharge, sewage and air rules</li><li>BWM 2004 and BWMS Code the IMO ballast convention and system approval</li><li>CLC and Fund 1992 the international oil spill liability scheme that OPA 90 sits beside</li><li>ISM Code, ISPS Code certificates reported in the NOA</li><li>SOLAS 1974 the safety rules checked in a port state control exam</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li><b>Penalties</b> under the Clean Water Act, APPS and the ballast rules</li><li>The ballast water <b>reporting form</b>, the time before arrival, the <b>record-keeping period</b>, and exemptions (151.2060 to 151.2070). Their text could not be retrieved</li><li><b>Small vessel</b> and Great Lakes ballast rules, and state rules such as <b>California</b> and <b>Washington</b></li><li><b>MARPOL Annex IV</b> inside the US (cruise ship rules in Alaska) and the <b>Annex V</b> garbage rules</li><li>The <b>200 nm</b> extent of the North American ECA and its <b>coordinates</b></li><li>The <b>Clean Air Act</b> fuel rules and fines, bunker delivery notes, and the <b>exhaust gas cleaning</b> approvals</li><li>The USCG <b>targeting matrix</b>, <b>QUALSHIP 21</b>, flag lists and the exam booklet</li><li><b>Section 301 port fees</b> on certain ships, which were suspended to 9 Nov 2026 and may have changed</li><li><b>Jones Act</b>, <b>TWIC</b>, <b>CBP and customs</b> rules, and <b>MTSA</b> security</li><li><b>46 CFR</b> manning, inspection and licensing rules</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>MARPOL Annex VI reg 14 text (EGCSA copy): https://www.egcsa.com/regulatory/marpol-annex-vi-regulation-14/</li><li>IMO FAQ, sulphur limits in ECAs (West P&amp;I copy): https://www.westpandi.com/globalassets/news/imo-frequenty-asked-questions---sulphur-limits-in-emission-control-areas-from-1-january-2015.pdf</li><li>ABS, Emission Control Areas: https://ww2.eagle.org/en/rules-and-resources/regulatory-updates/emission-control-areas.html</li><li>EPA, Guidance on ECA and Tier III engines: https://19january2017snapshot.epa.gov/sites/production/files/2017-01/documents/420f16055.pdf</li><li>33 CFR 159.3 and 159.53 (MSD types): https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/159.3 and https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/159.53</li><li>EPA, Marine sanitation devices: https://www.epa.gov/node/113769</li><li>33 CFR 151.2025, 151.2030, 151.2035 (ballast): https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/151.2025</li><li>33 CFR 151 subpart D contents: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/part-151/subpart-D</li><li>33 CFR 160 subpart C (NOA), 160.212: https://chanrobles.com/usa/uslaws/cfr/title33/33-2.0.1.6.28.3.php and https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/33/160.212</li><li>USCG, 2025 Port State Control annual report: https://www.news.uscg.mil/maritime-commons/Article/4495805/2025-port-state-control-annual-report-posted/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-vgp-and-vida","v":2932860,"title":"VGP and VIDA","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["vgp","vida","vessel-general-permit","incidental-discharge","epa","uscg","clean-water-act","usa","graywater","bilge","scrubber","topic"],"body":"<h1>VGP and VIDA — US rules for normal ship discharges</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><b>2013 Vessel General Permit (VGP)</b></th><th><b>VIDA national standards</b></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What</td><td>An EPA permit under the Clean Water Act for discharges <b>incidental to normal operation</b></td><td>A 2018 law (Vessel Incidental Discharge Act). EPA sets the standards, the <b>Coast Guard</b> enforces them</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td><b>In force now</b></td><td>EPA rule published <b>9 Oct 2024</b>, effective <b>8 Nov 2024</b>. The standards <b>start only when the Coast Guard's rules are final</b></td></tr><tr><td>Waters</td><td>US waters out to <b>3 nm</b>, including the Great Lakes</td><td>US waters and the contiguous zone</td></tr><tr><td>Vessels</td><td>Non-recreational, non-military commercial vessels. Most are over 79 ft</td><td>Non-military, non-recreational vessels <b>79 ft and longer</b>. About <b>85,000</b> vessels</td></tr><tr><td>Not covered</td><td>Recreational and Armed Forces vessels</td><td>Armed Forces, recreational, permanently moored craft. <b>Vessels under 79 ft and fishing vessels are excluded except for ballast water</b></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Until the Coast Guard publishes its rule, the <b>2013 VGP</b> and the <b>Coast Guard ballast rules (33 CFR 151)</b> still apply. The VGP expired on 19 Dec 2018 on paper, but it stays in force until VIDA replaces it.</blockquote><h2>How VIDA replaces the VGP</h2><ul><li><b>Congress</b> passed VIDA in <b>2018</b>, adding <b>section 312(p)</b> to the Clean Water Act</li><li><b>EPA</b> published *Vessel Incidental Discharge National Standards of Performance* on <b>9 Oct 2024</b>, effective <b>8 Nov 2024</b></li><li>The <b>Coast Guard</b> has <b>two years</b> from EPA's publication to make its implementing, compliance and enforcement rules</li><li>On the date those rules are <b>final, effective and enforceable</b>, the VGP and the Coast Guard's older NANPCA ballast rules (as of 3 Dec 2018) are repealed. The small vessel permit was repealed when VIDA became law</li><li><b>States lose their own ballast rules.</b> Seven states had extra ballast rules (California, Michigan, Minnesota, Ohio, Oregon, Washington, Wisconsin). They stop applying when the national standards are in place. A State can still petition EPA for an <b>emergency order</b>, a discharge ban or a <b>no-discharge zone</b></li><li><b>Ballast water</b> becomes an incidental discharge under section 312(p), with the VGP as the minimum. The Coast Guard sets the compliance dates. Before a vessel's date it must still exchange ballast water under 40 CFR 139.10(e) or the regional rules in 139.10(f) and (g)</li><li>EPA reviews the standards <b>every 5 years</b></li></ul><h2>The 2013 VGP in detail</h2><h3>Who files what</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Effective</b></td><td>19 Dec 2013 (expiry 19 Dec 2018, kept in force)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Notice of Intent (NOI)</b></td><td>Vessels of <b>300 GT or more</b>, or able to hold or discharge <b>over 8 m³</b> of ballast water. Filed through EPA's <b>eNOI</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>When</b></td><td>New or newly delivered vessels file at least <b>7 days</b> before discharge (paper: <b>30 days</b>)</td></tr><tr><td><b>No NOI</b></td><td>Vessels under 300 GT with ballast of 8 m³ or less complete a <b>PARI form</b> (Appendix K) and keep it on board</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lifeboats, rescue boats and barges</b></td><td>Covered by the larger vessel's NOI</td></tr><tr><td><b>Annual report</b></td><td>Due by <b>28 Feb</b> of the next year (paper postmarked by <b>21 Feb</b>). Appendix H form, all analytical results, and all noncompliance. Filed electronically. Barges and vessels under 300 GT may file a combined report if conditions are met</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>General limits for every vessel (Part 2.1)</h3><ul><li><b>Material storage:</b> minimize exposure to rain and spray. Handle oily drainage by dry cleanup, onshore storage or an oily water separator</li><li><b>Toxic and hazardous materials:</b> protected, sealed, labelled, secured, not overfilled. <b>Jettisoning containers is not allowed</b></li><li><b>Fuel spills:</b> no overfilling. Spill containment on vent lines. Clean sheens at once</li><li><b>Oil:</b> MARPOL vessels discharge below <b>15 ppm</b> and hold a current <b>IOPP certificate</b>. Others discharge no harmful quantities</li><li><b>Other laws</b> still apply: CWA 311, the Act to Prevent Pollution from Ships, the Marine Sanctuaries Act, FIFRA, OPA</li><li><b>Training:</b> crew who manage or affect discharges are trained, including fuel spill response</li></ul><h3>Specific discharges (Part 2.2)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Discharge</th><th>Key rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Deck washdown (2.2.1)</b></td><td>Broom-clean first. Drip pans under leaking machinery. Phosphate-free, minimally toxic cleaners. Unused paint is not discharged</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bilgewater (2.2.2)</b></td><td>Over <b>400 GT</b>: no untreated bilgewater over <b>15 ppm</b>. If sailing beyond the territorial sea, avoid discharge within <b>1 nm</b>, and discharge underway (over <b>6 knots</b>) when possible. No dispersants to hide a sheen. New builds over 400 GT after 19 Dec 2013 sample oil content yearly, and may stop after two years below <b>5 ppm</b>. Data by 28 Feb</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ballast water (2.2.3)</b></td><td>See the next table</td></tr><tr><td><b>Anti-fouling coatings (2.2.4)</b></td><td><b>No TBT or other organotins</b>. Follow the FIFRA label</td></tr><tr><td><b>AFFF (2.2.5)</b></td><td>Emergencies. Maintenance and training discharges restricted</td></tr><tr><td><b>Boiler blowdown (2.2.6)</b></td><td>Minimize in port</td></tr><tr><td><b>Chain locker (2.2.8)</b></td><td>Wash the chain as it comes up. Clean the locker in drydock</td></tr><tr><td><b>Distillation brine (2.2.10)</b></td><td>Must not contact machinery or toxic materials</td></tr><tr><td><b>Elevator pit (2.2.11)</b></td><td>Untreated discharge only in emergencies. Otherwise treat as bilgewater</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fire main (2.2.12)</b></td><td>Emergencies and limited testing</td></tr><tr><td><b>Gas turbine washwater (2.2.14)</b></td><td>No direct discharge in permit waters</td></tr><tr><td><b>Motor gasoline (2.2.16)</b></td><td>No harmful oil, no sheen, <b>15 ppm</b> limit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Non-oily machinery (2.2.17)</b></td><td>No harmful oils or toxic additives. Packing gland effluent must not make a sheen</td></tr><tr><td><b>Refrigeration condensate (2.2.18)</b></td><td>Must not contact oily or toxic materials</td></tr><tr><td><b>Seawater cooling and piping (2.2.19, 2.2.20)</b></td><td>Discharge underway where possible. FIFRA-registered chemicals only, minimum amounts</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sonar dome (2.2.22)</b></td><td>No sonar dome water discharge for maintenance</td></tr><tr><td><b>Underwater ship husbandry (2.2.23)</b></td><td>Clean in drydock where possible. Control paint and debris</td></tr><tr><td><b>Graywater (2.2.15)</b></td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td><b>Scrubber washwater (2.2.26)</b></td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fish hold (2.2.27)</b></td><td>Screen large solids. Use shore facilities where available</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Ballast water (2.2.3)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Training and plan</b></td><td>Crew trained. A written training plan and records. A vessel-specific <b>ballast water management plan</b> available to EPA</td></tr><tr><td><b>Practices</b></td><td>Avoid uptake or discharge in sanctuaries and reefs. Minimize uptake in high-risk areas. Clean tanks only mid-ocean or under control. <b>No sediment discharge</b> in permit waters. High sea suction when feasible</td></tr><tr><td><b>Numeric limit</b></td><td>Organisms <b>50 µm or larger</b>: fewer than <b>10 per m³</b>. <b>10 to 50 µm</b>: fewer than <b>10 per mL</b>. *V. cholerae*: <b>under 1 cfu per 100 mL</b>. *E. coli*: <b>under 250</b>. Enterococci: <b>under 100</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Schedule</b></td><td>Built after <b>1 Dec 2013</b>: on delivery. Existing under 1,500 m³ and over 5,000 m³: first drydocking after <b>1 Jan 2016</b>. Existing 1,500 to 5,000 m³: after <b>1 Jan 2014</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Treatment limits</b></td><td>pH <b>6.5 to 9</b>. Residual biocides: chlorine dioxide <b>200 µg/L</b>, chlorine or ozone <b>100 µg/L</b>, peracetic acid <b>500 µg/L</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Exemptions</b></td><td>Short voyages (within one Captain of the Port zone, or <b>10 nm</b> or less), unpowered barges, pre-2009 Great Lakes ships, vessels under <b>1,600 GRT</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Before treatment is required</b></td><td>Exchange beyond the EEZ and more than <b>200 nm</b> from shore. Pacific nearshore voyages: <b>50 nm</b> and <b>200 m</b> depth. Empty tanks flushed or sealed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Records</b></td><td>Kept on board <b>3 years</b>. Great Lakes ships also follow 33 CFR 151 subpart C</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Graywater (2.2.15)</b></p><ul><li>Minimize discharge in port</li><li>Vessels over <b>400 GT</b> more than <b>1 nm</b> from shore discharge beyond 1 nm while underway, unless they treat it</li><li>Phosphate-free, minimally toxic soaps. pH not below <b>6.0</b> or above <b>9.0</b></li><li><b>Great Lakes:</b> hold onshore, or meet fecal coliform <b>200 per 100 mL</b> and suspended solids <b>150 mg/L</b></li><li><b>Monitoring:</b> two samples a year at least <b>14 days</b> apart (BOD, fecal coliform or *E. coli*, suspended solids, pH, residual chlorine). Records <b>3 years</b></li></ul><p><b>Scrubber washwater (2.2.26)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>pH</b></td><td>At least <b>6.0</b> at overboard discharge. Up to <b>2.0</b> units below the inlet allowed during maneuvering and transit</td></tr><tr><td><b>PAHs</b></td><td>Up to <b>50 µg/L</b> above inlet at 45 t/MWh. The table runs from <b>2,250 µg/L</b> at 0 to 1 t/MWh down to <b>25 µg/L</b> at 90 t/MWh. A 100% excursion for <b>15 min per 12 h</b> at startup</td></tr><tr><td><b>Turbidity</b></td><td>Up to <b>25 FNU/NTU</b> above inlet, 15-minute rolling average. One 15-minute excursion of up to 20% per 12 h</td></tr><tr><td><b>Nitrates and nitrites</b></td><td>The greater of <b>12% NOx removal</b> or <b>60 mg/L</b> at 45 t/MWh. The table runs from <b>2,700 mg/L</b> to <b>30 mg/L</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Monitoring</b></td><td>Continuous pH, PAH (where available), turbidity, temperature. Calibrate <b>yearly</b>. Two samples in year one, at least 14 days apart, then yearly. Data by <b>28 Feb</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Sludge</b></td><td>Not discharged within 3 nm. Delivered ashore</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Inspections and records (Parts 3 and 4)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Routine visual inspection</b></td><td>At least <b>weekly</b>, or each voyage if more often. Ships making several voyages a day: at least <b>daily</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Record</b></td><td>Date, time, locations, inspector, findings, signature</td></tr><tr><td><b>Long layup (13 days or more unmanned)</b></td><td>Pre-layup inspection, every <b>2 weeks</b>, and a post-layup inspection before re-entering service</td></tr><tr><td><b>Annual comprehensive inspection</b></td><td>At least every <b>12 months</b>, by qualified personnel. Hull, ballast tanks, bilges, oil systems, pollution controls</td></tr><tr><td><b>Drydock report</b></td><td>Confirms chain locker cleaning, hull inspection, coating compliance, anode maintenance</td></tr><tr><td><b>Corrective action</b></td><td>Triggered by a limit violation or an ineffective control. Simple fixes within <b>2 weeks</b>. Parts or equipment within <b>3 months</b>. Drydock-only work before the ship relaunches. The fix does <b>not</b> excuse the original violation</td></tr><tr><td><b>Records kept</b></td><td><b>3 years</b>: voyage log, violations, inspection logs, analytical results, drydock findings, operational logs, training</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ballast records (4.3)</b></td><td>Capacity and tank status, management, origin and exchange of discharged ballast. The ballast report goes to the National Ballast Information Clearinghouse before arrival. Not required for a vessel operating in one Captain of the Port zone</td></tr><tr><td><b>Noncompliance</b></td><td>Oral notice to the EPA Regional Office within <b>24 hours</b>, written follow-up within <b>5 days</b>. A report to the National Response Center (800-424-8802) satisfies it</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Special vessel classes (Part 5)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Main rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Large cruise ships</b> (500 or more people)</td><td>Treated graywater: fecal coliform <b>20 per 100 mL</b> (geometric mean), <b>40</b> for no more than 10% of samples, residual chlorine <b>10 µg/L</b>. No untreated graywater. Pools and spas dechlorinated. Initial monitoring: <b>5 samples over 30 days</b>, then quarterly. Crew and passenger training</td></tr><tr><td><b>Medium cruise ships</b> (100 to 499)</td><td>Similar, with a <b>1 nm</b> trigger</td></tr><tr><td><b>Large ferries</b></td><td>Carrying <b>100 tons</b> of vehicles or more: deck water through an oily water separator. Authorized for <b>250 passengers</b> or more: graywater rules</td></tr><tr><td><b>Barges</b></td><td>Scuppers plugged during cargo or fueling. Broom-clean before washing. Visual sheen test</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tankers and chemical carriers</b></td><td>Inert gas scrubber effluent and deck seal water authorized. Scuppers plugged during loading. Sheen tests</td></tr><tr><td><b>Research, emergency and rescue vessels</b></td><td>Limited discharges for research, training and tests</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>State conditions (Part 6)</h3><p>Each State's water quality certification adds conditions that become enforceable. Examples: <b>California</b> bans large passenger vessel graywater in State waters and sets oil and grease at <b>15 mg/L</b> or less. <b>Connecticut</b> bans bilgewater, graywater and scrubber washwater discharge in certain waters. <b>Arkansas</b> needs phone notice within <b>24 hours</b> and written notice in <b>3 days</b>.</p><h2>VIDA national standards in detail</h2><h3>Three general standards (all vessels)</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Operation and maintenance</b> (40 CFR 139.4)</td><td>Minimize discharges with available and practicable measures. No additives except for treatment. Contain cargo. Store hazardous materials properly. Discharge while underway and as far from shore as possible</td></tr><tr><td><b>Biofouling management</b> (139.5)</td><td>A <b>biofouling management plan</b> for anti-fouling systems and practices, with hull and niche area cleaning rules. In-water cleaning is restricted. <b>Annual inspections</b> and drydock reports</td></tr><tr><td><b>Oil management</b> (139.6)</td><td>Used or spent oil is not discharged. <b>Environmentally acceptable lubricants</b> at oil-to-sea interfaces unless technically infeasible</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Twenty specific discharges</h3><ul><li><b>Ballast tanks, bilges, boilers, cathodic protection, chain lockers, decks, desalination and purification systems, elevator pits, exhaust gas emission control systems, fire protection equipment, gas turbines, graywater systems, hulls and niche areas, inert gas systems, motor gasoline and compensating systems, non-oily machinery, pools and spas, refrigeration and air conditioning, seawater piping, sonar domes</b></li><li><b>13 are substantially unchanged</b> from the VGP, <b>2 slightly changed</b> (bilges, desalination), and <b>5 changed most</b>: ballast tanks, exhaust gas emission control systems, graywater systems, hulls and niche areas, seawater piping</li><li><b>Federally protected waters:</b> slightly changed rules for chain lockers, decks, hulls, pools and seawater piping. Extra rules for the Great Lakes and the Pacific region</li></ul><h3>Key numbers</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Ballast discharge limit</b> (139.10)</td><td>Same numbers as the VGP: fewer than <b>10 per m³</b> (50 µm or larger), <b>10 per mL</b> (10 to 50 µm). *V. cholerae* under <b>1 cfu per 100 mL</b>, *E. coli* under <b>250</b>, enterococci under <b>100</b>. Instantaneous maximums</td></tr><tr><td><b>Biocide limits</b></td><td>Chlorine dioxide <b>200 µg/L</b>, chlorine or ozone <b>100 µg/L</b>, peracetic acid <b>500 µg/L</b>, hydrogen peroxide <b>1,000 µg/L</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Ballast exchange</b></td><td><b>200 nm</b> from shore for certain voyages. <b>50 nm</b> for voyages starting in the US or Canadian EEZ</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ballast exclusions</b></td><td>Flow-through systems, vessels using only Safe Drinking Water Act water, permanent sealed ballast, discharge only to a reception facility, National Defense Reserve Fleet vessels for disposal. Coastwise crude oil tankers are <b>not</b> exempt</td></tr><tr><td><b>New Great Lakes ships</b></td><td>Of <b>3,000 GT or more</b>, operating only in the Great Lakes and St. Lawrence River, built after the Coast Guard rule: a USCG type-approved system. <b>Existing Lakers</b> get no equipment standard</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bilges</b> (139.11)</td><td>Vessels <b>400 GT and above</b>: discharge underway, at least <b>1 nm</b> from shore where feasible, at <b>15 ppm</b> oil or less</td></tr><tr><td><b>Chain lockers</b> (139.14)</td><td>Rinse chain and anchor of organisms and sediment on retrieval. No discharge in port</td></tr><tr><td><b>Exhaust gas emission control</b> (139.18)</td><td>Limits on pH, PAHs, turbidity, suspended particles, nitrates plus nitrites. No sludge or residue discharge</td></tr><tr><td><b>Records</b></td><td>Measures to minimize discharges are written in shipboard logs and plans. Inspectors can check records, sample discharges and verify equipment</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>OPA 90 liability for oil spills</li><li>MARPOL 73/78 and BWM 2004 the international rules behind bilge, sewage and ballast limits</li><li>US Maritime Regulations Guide other US rules a ship meets</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The <b>Coast Guard VIDA rule</b>. No proposed or final rule was found. Its two-year period from EPA's 9 Oct 2024 publication ended in October 2026. The Coast Guard's own page is old, so check the current status</li><li><b>Appendix G</b> federally protected waters list, the VGP <b>Appendix E</b> NOI contents, and the <b>Notice of Termination</b></li><li><b>Part 6</b> conditions for States after Hawaii, and Tribal conditions</li><li>The full <b>PAH and nitrate tables</b> between 1 and 90 t/MWh</li><li>The 2013 VGP <b>Part 1.14</b> electronic filing waiver rules</li><li>The text of <b>40 CFR 139</b> itself. The VIDA section above follows Federal Register summaries and trade-press summaries, which agree with each other, not the codified rule</li><li><b>Penalties</b> under the Clean Water Act for permit or VIDA violations</li><li><b>Section 301 port fees</b> and other trade measures</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>EPA, 2013 Vessel General Permit (full text): https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2025-07/2013-vessel-general-permit.pdf</li><li>EPA, Vessel Incidental Discharge National Standards of Performance, 89 FR 82074 (9 Oct 2024): https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2024-10-09/html/2024-22013.htm</li><li>USCG, role in implementing VIDA: https://news.uscg.mil/maritime-commons/Article/3932088/the-coast-guards-role-in-implementing-the-vessel-incidental-discharge-act</li><li>USCG, VIDA page (interim requirements): https://www.dco.uscg.mil/Our-Organization/Assistant-Commandant-for-Prevention-Policy-CG-5P/Commercial-Regulations-standards-CG-5PS/Office-of-Operating-and-Environmental-Standards/Environmental-Standards/VIDA/</li><li>ABS, VIDA regulatory requirements (secondary): https://ww2.eagle.org/en/rules-and-resources/regulatory-updates/vida-regulatory-rqmts.html</li><li>Holland &amp; Knight, EPA final VIDA rule (secondary): https://hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2024/10/epa-to-cruise-ships-cargo-ships-barges-tankers-tugs-and-tows</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-york-antwerp-arrest-and-collision-rules","v":247588216,"title":"York-Antwerp, Arrest and Collision Rules","category":"Maritime › Topics","tags":["general-average","york-antwerp","arrest-of-ships","collision","maritime-law","convention","imo","topic"],"body":"<h1>York-Antwerp, Arrest and Collision Rules — Claims between ship interests</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><b>York-Antwerp Rules 2016</b></th><th><b>Arrest of Ships 1999</b></th><th><b>Collision Convention 1910</b></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kind</td><td>Private rules, apply only if the contract says so</td><td>Treaty</td><td>Treaty (Brussels)</td></tr><tr><td>Question answered</td><td>Who shares the cost of a sacrifice made to save ship and cargo</td><td>When a court may arrest a ship for a debt</td><td>Who pays when ships collide</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Used through bills of lading and charterparties</td><td>In force <b>14 Sep 2011</b></td><td>Not verified</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The York-Antwerp Rules are not a treaty. They bind only when written into the contract, usually by a bill of lading clause.</blockquote><h2>York-Antwerp Rules 2016 (general average)</h2><p><b>Basic rules</b></p><ul><li><b>Rule Paramount:</b> nothing is allowed unless the sacrifice or expense was <b>reasonably</b> made or incurred</li><li><b>Rule A:</b> a general average act is an <b>extraordinary</b> sacrifice or expense made <b>intentionally and reasonably</b> for the common safety, to save the property in a common maritime adventure from peril</li><li><b>Rule B:</b> a common maritime adventure also exists when one vessel tows or pushes another, if all are in commercial activity and none is in a salvage operation</li><li><b>Rule C:</b> only the <b>direct consequence</b> of the act is allowed. Environmental damage, demurrage, loss of market and delay are not</li><li><b>Rule D:</b> fault of one party does not stop the claim for contribution. Remedies against the party at fault remain</li><li><b>Rule E:</b> the party claiming bears the <b>burden of proof</b> and must notify the average adjuster. The adjuster may estimate the figures if particulars are not supplied within <b>12 months</b></li><li><b>Rule F:</b> an extra expense incurred instead of an allowable one is allowed, up to the amount saved</li><li><b>Rule G:</b> loss and contribution are worked out on values at the <b>time and place the adventure ends</b></li></ul><p><b>Numbered rules</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Subject</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>Jettison of cargo</td><td>Allowed only if carried in line with the custom of the trade</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Sacrifices for the common safety</td><td>Allowed, including water entering through hatches opened to jettison</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Extinguishing fire on board</td><td>Allowed (water damage, beaching, scuttling). <b>Smoke and heat damage not</b></td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Cutting away wreck</td><td><b>Not allowed</b></td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Voluntary stranding</td><td>Allowed if run ashore for the common safety</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Salvage</td><td>Allowed if it saved the property. <b>SCOPIC</b> and Salvage Convention Art 14 special compensation excluded</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Machinery and boilers</td><td>Allowed when trying to refloat a stranded ship. <b>Never</b> when working afloat</td></tr><tr><td>IX</td><td>Cargo or ship's materials as fuel</td><td>Allowed in time of peril. Credit given for fuel otherwise used</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>Port of refuge</td><td>Entry and exit expenses allowed. Discharge, handling and storage allowed when necessary</td></tr><tr><td>XI</td><td>Crew wages, fuel, stores, port charges</td><td>Allowed during the extra detention or longer voyage. <b>Not</b> for repairs to damage found with no accident on the voyage</td></tr><tr><td>XIV</td><td>Temporary repairs</td><td>Common safety or sacrifice damage: in full. Accidental damage: up to the saving in general average expense. <b>No</b> new-for-old deduction</td></tr><tr><td>XVII</td><td>Contributory values</td><td>Cargo at discharge (invoice value, with insurance and freight). Ship without regard to charters</td></tr><tr><td>XVIII</td><td>Damage to the ship</td><td>Repaired: reasonable actual cost. Not repaired: reasonable depreciation, not above the repair cost</td></tr><tr><td>XX</td><td>Provision of funds</td><td>Capital loss on goods sold to raise funds, and insurance of disbursements. <b>No commission figure</b> in the 2016 text</td></tr><tr><td>XXI</td><td>Interest</td><td>Until <b>3 months</b> after the adjustment. Rate: 12-month ICE LIBOR for the currency, set on the first banking day of the year, <b>plus 4 points</b></td></tr><tr><td>XXII</td><td>Cash deposits</td><td>Held by the adjuster in a separate account. If the depositor does not answer within <b>90 days</b>, the adjuster may deduct the amount</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Arrest of Ships Convention 1999</h2><ul><li><b>Arrest</b> means a court detaining or restricting a ship to <b>secure a maritime claim</b>. Seizure to carry out a judgment is not arrest (Art 1)</li><li><b>Maritime claims</b> are a <b>closed</b> list, items (a) to (v). Examples: damage from the ship's operation, salvage, wreck removal, supplies and services, port dues, <b>crew wages</b>, insurance premiums, mortgages, disputes over sale of the ship</li><li><b>Art 2:</b> only a <b>court</b> of the State where the ship is can arrest or release it, and only for a maritime claim. The procedure follows the law of that State</li><li><b>Art 3, whose ship:</b> the ship may be arrested if the liable person owns it (at the time of the claim and of the arrest) or is its <b>demise charterer</b>. Mortgage claims, ownership claims and maritime liens are other grounds</li><li><b>Art 3, sister ships:</b> another ship owned by the liable person may be arrested, if that person owned, demise chartered, time chartered or voyage chartered the first ship when the claim arose. Not for ownership or possession claims</li><li><b>Art 4, release:</b> the ship <b>must</b> be released once sufficient security is given. If the parties disagree, the court sets it, <b>never more than the ship's value</b>. Asking for release is not an admission of liability</li><li><b>Art 5, re-arrest:</b> not allowed for the same claim, unless the security is inadequate (total capped at the ship's value), the security provider is or is likely to be unable to pay, or the release was at the claimant's request or beyond the claimant's control</li><li><b>Art 6, wrongful arrest:</b> the court may require the claimant to give security against loss from a wrongful or unjustified arrest, or from excessive security demanded</li><li><b>Art 7, merits:</b> the courts of the arresting State may hear the case, unless the parties agreed on another court or arbitration. A court may set a deadline for proceedings elsewhere, and if none are brought, release the ship</li><li><b>Art 8, scope:</b> any ship in a State Party's jurisdiction, whatever its flag. <b>Warships, naval auxiliaries and non-commercial government ships are excluded</b></li></ul><h2>Collision Convention 1910 (Brussels)</h2><ul><li><b>Art 1:</b> applies to collisions between sea-going vessels, or between sea-going and inland vessels, in any waters</li><li><b>Art 2:</b> accidental collision, force majeure, or unexplained cause: each side <b>bears its own loss</b></li><li><b>Art 3:</b> if one vessel alone is at fault, that vessel pays</li><li><b>Art 4:</b> if more than one is at fault, liability is <b>in proportion to the fault</b>. If the degrees cannot be shown, or are equal, liability is <b>shared equally</b>. For death or injury, the vessels at fault are <b>jointly and severally</b> liable to third parties</li><li><b>Art 5:</b> liability remains when the fault is a compulsory pilot's</li><li><b>Art 7:</b> claims are barred after <b>2 years</b> from the casualty. A claim for contribution between the vessels must be made within <b>1 year</b> of payment</li><li><b>Art 8:</b> after a collision each master must help the other ship, crew and passengers if this does not seriously endanger his own ship. He must also give his ship's name, port of registry, last and next ports. Breach does not by itself make the owner liable</li><li><b>Art 11:</b> warships and government ships used only for public service are excluded</li><li><b>Art 13:</b> also covers damage by manoeuvre or breach of regulations with <b>no actual contact</b></li></ul><h2>Related notes</h2><ul><li>COLREG 1972 the collision regulations that decide who was at fault</li><li>Salvage 1989 salvage and special compensation (excluded by Rule VI)</li><li>LLMC 1976 the shipowner's limit of liability</li><li>Hague-Visby, Hamburg and Rotterdam Rules cargo claims against the carrier</li><li>MLC 2006 (ILO) seafarers' wages (a maritime claim under the Arrest Convention)</li></ul><h2>Not included (not verified against the original text)</h2><ul><li>The <b>Arrest Convention 1952</b>, its list of claims and which States still apply it</li><li>Which States are parties to the 1999 and 1910 Conventions, and the date the 1910 Convention came into force</li><li><b>York-Antwerp Rules 2024</b> (a newer version may exist) and the earlier 1974, 1994 and 2004 versions</li><li>York-Antwerp Rules XII, XIII, XV, XVI and XIX, and the general-average guarantee and bond</li><li>Arrest procedure in the Philippines or any other country</li></ul><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>York-Antwerp Rules 2016, full text: https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/dea35ba0c18145edb901009dabdb9334/york-antwerp-rules-2016.pdf</li><li>Arrest of Ships Convention 1999, original text (Univ. of Oslo treaty library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/arrest-ships.html</li><li>UN Audiovisual Library commentary on the 1999 Convention: https://legal.un.org/avl/pdf/ha/icas/icas_e.pdf</li><li>Collision Convention 1910, text (Admiralty and Maritime Law Guide): https://admiraltylawguide.com/conven/collisions1910.html</li></ul>"}];
